window.ACE_RC_BANK = {"version":"2026-10-01","types":{"detail":"Detail","inference":"Inference","mainidea":"Main idea / purpose","function":"Function of a paragraph","except":"EXCEPT / NOT","tone":"Tone / attitude","application":"Application","vocab":"Vocabulary in context"},"passages":{"rc-o01":{"id":"rc-o01","title":"The Slow Work of Thin Air","topic":"high-altitude acclimatization","field":"physiology","source":"original","words":1370,"paragraphs":[["A traveler who arrives in La Paz, Bolivia, roughly 3,600 meters above sea level, is breathing air that still holds about 21 percent oxygen; what has changed is the pressure pushing those molecules into the lungs.","Barometric pressure falls with altitude, and because the partial pressure of a gas is its fraction times the total pressure, the oxygen available to the body falls with it.","At sea level the partial pressure of oxygen in dry air is about 160 millimeters of mercury; near the summit of Mount Everest, at nearly 8,850 meters, it is roughly one third of that.","The French physiologist Paul Bert proved this in 1878 with pressure chambers, showing that the misery of altitude came from a scarcity of oxygen molecules rather than from low pressure itself."],["The body cannot change the air, so it changes itself, and the adjustments it makes over hours, days, and weeks are called acclimatization.","The word is often confused with adaptation, and the difference matters.","Acclimatization is reversible and happens within one lifetime; adaptation is the work of natural selection across many generations and is written into a population's genes.","A climber from Chicago acclimatizes; a Tibetan herder is adapted."],["The first response arrives within minutes.","Small clusters of cells called the carotid bodies, lodged where each common carotid artery divides in the neck, monitor the oxygen in arterial blood.","When the partial pressure of oxygen drops, they fire more rapidly along the glossopharyngeal nerve to the brainstem, which answers by driving deeper and faster breathing.","Physiologists call this the hypoxic ventilatory response; it is why a newcomer feels breathless even while sitting still.","Extra ventilation pulls more fresh air into the alveoli and raises the oxygen pressure there, a real but partial repair."],["The repair has a cost.","Breathing hard blows off carbon dioxide faster than the tissues make it, and because carbon dioxide forms carbonic acid in blood, losing it leaves the blood more alkaline.","Chemoreceptors in the brainstem that respond to acidity sense this respiratory alkalosis and read it as a sign that breathing should slow.","Two signals now compete: the carotid bodies urging more breaths and the brainstem's acid sensors urging fewer.","In the first day or two the second signal acts as a brake, and ventilation rises less than the oxygen shortage alone would call for."],["The kidneys release the brake, but slowly.","Over the next several days they excrete bicarbonate, the main base in blood, into the urine, pulling the pH back toward normal.","As the alkalosis fades the brainstem stops objecting and ventilation climbs further, which is why the heaviest breathing often comes several days after arrival rather than on the first night.","The drug acetazolamide exploits this chemistry: developed as a diuretic, it blocks the enzyme carbonic anhydrase in the kidney, forcing bicarbonate out in the urine and producing a mild acidosis within hours.","The drug thus imitates several days of renal work: ventilation rises sooner and oxygen levels during sleep improve.","Its side effects are mostly harmless but memorable: tingling in the fingers and toes and a flat, metallic taste in carbonated drinks."],["Acclimatization matters because its absence hurts.","Acute mountain sickness, which commonly appears above about 2,500 meters in those who ascend too quickly, announces itself with a headache plus some mix of nausea, loss of appetite, fatigue, dizziness, and poor sleep.","Occasionally it progresses to fluid in the lungs or swelling of the brain, and both can kill.","The standard advice is therefore a schedule rather than a drug: above about 3,000 meters, raise the sleeping elevation by no more than 300 to 500 meters a day, rest every third or fourth day, and descend if symptoms worsen.","The schedule works because it gives the kidneys and the slower processes described below the time they need."],["A second adjustment takes shape inside the red cells, where hemoglobin's grip on oxygen is tuned by 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate, or 2,3-BPG, a small molecule the cells make as a side branch of their sugar metabolism.","When 2,3-BPG binds hemoglobin it loosens the grip, so oxygen unloads more readily in the tissues; on a graph of saturation against oxygen pressure, the curve shifts right.","Red cell 2,3-BPG rises within a day or two at altitude, and textbooks long presented this as a clean benefit.","The story is less tidy than that.","The alkalosis of early acclimatization shifts the same curve left, tightening the grip, and at extreme altitude the leftward pull wins.","That may be no accident: near a Himalayan summit the harder problem is loading oxygen in the lungs, not unloading it in the tissues, and a tighter grip helps with loading."],["The slowest adjustment is the growth of the red cell mass.","Specialized cells in the kidney sense low oxygen and release the hormone erythropoietin, or EPO, which tells the bone marrow to make more red cells.","EPO rises within hours of arrival and peaks within a day or two, then drifts back toward normal while the marrow keeps working; a measurable rise in circulating red cells takes weeks.","Travelers are told that their blood thickens within days, and in a sense it does, though not for the reason they imagine: plasma volume contracts early, so the same cells sit in less fluid.","The true expansion comes later and continues for months.","The oxygen-sensing switch behind EPO, a protein called hypoxia-inducible factor, earned William Kaelin, Peter Ratcliffe, and Gregg Semenza the 2019 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],["It is tempting to conclude that more red cells are simply better, and I think that is mistaken.","Each added cell carries oxygen, but it also thickens the blood, and thick blood moves sluggishly through small vessels and taxes the heart.","Past some point the slower flow cancels the extra carrying capacity, and oxygen delivery stops improving.","Native highlanders of the Andes have lived above 3,500 meters for thousands of years and carry noticeably more hemoglobin than sea-level populations.","A meaningful fraction develop chronic mountain sickness, first described in Peru in the 1920s by the physician Carlos Monge, in which the red cell count climbs so high that the blood becomes a burden.","Headaches, fatigue, bluish lips, and strain on the right side of the heart follow.","By a widely used definition, a hemoglobin concentration of 21 grams per deciliter or more in a man living at altitude marks the condition; a typical sea-level value is closer to 15."],["The Tibetan Plateau tells a different story at a similar height.","Tibetan highlanders above 4,000 meters tend to have hemoglobin concentrations close to sea-level values, well below those of Andeans at the same altitude, and chronic mountain sickness is comparatively rare among them.","They breathe more at rest and move more blood through their tissues, partly because they make more nitric oxide, a gas that relaxes vessel walls.","In 2010 several research groups reported that a variant of the gene EPAS1, which encodes one of the hypoxia-inducible factor proteins, is unusually common in Tibetans and linked to their lower hemoglobin.","Four years later a comparison of genomes showed that this stretch of DNA most likely entered the Tibetan lineage from Denisovans, an extinct group of archaic humans.","In effect, Tibetan bodies have turned down the very response that sea-level visitors turn up."],["The anthropologist Cynthia Beall, whose fieldwork spans both ranges, has called the two patterns distinct solutions to the same problem, and the contrast is instructive.","Andean physiology looks like a visitor's acclimatization carried on indefinitely: more red cells, then still more.","Tibetan physiology looks like something else, a settlement reached over a long time in which the body stops fighting the thin air and lives with it.","Which is better is not a question physiology alone can answer, and I am wary of any tidy verdict that one population won.","But the contrast should make us cautious about a popular assumption: that acclimatization is a short preview of what evolution would eventually deliver.","The visitor's body does what it can in the time it has, and some of what it does, pursued long enough, becomes a disease; the system was tuned for a journey of days and weeks, not for the generations of a home."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o01-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the amount of oxygen available to the body falls at high altitude because","choices":["the fraction of oxygen in the air drops as the air thins with elevation","total air pressure falls while the oxygen fraction stays the same","oxygen molecules are less able to cross into the blood in cold, dry air","low pressure acts on the body in ways unrelated to oxygen","the lungs take in less air with each breath at high elevation"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"the partial pressure of a gas is its fraction times the total pressure, the oxygen available to the body falls with it"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the air still holds about 21 percent oxygen and that barometric pressure falls with altitude; since partial pressure is fraction times total pressure, it is the pressure, not the fraction, that drops.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 1 says the fraction stays at about 21 percent.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing is said about cold air or the blood's ability to absorb oxygen.","This is the idea Paul Bert ruled out at the end of paragraph 1.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says breathing becomes deeper and faster, not shallower."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage credits Paul Bert with","choices":["discovering that the fraction of oxygen in air is about 21 percent","measuring the barometric pressure near the summit of Mount Everest","inventing the pressure chamber as a tool for studying altitude","showing that altitude sickness stems from too few oxygen molecules","proving that low air pressure itself, not oxygen, harms the body at elevation"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"the misery of altitude came from a scarcity of oxygen molecules rather than from low pressure itself"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says Bert used pressure chambers in 1878 to show that the misery of altitude came from a scarcity of oxygen molecules, not from low pressure itself.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: the 21 percent figure is stated as a fact, not as Bert's discovery.","Wrong detail: the Everest figure in paragraph 1 is not attributed to Bert.","Half right: Bert used pressure chambers, but the passage never says he invented them.",null,"Reversed: this is exactly the explanation Bert's work rejected."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author distinguishes acclimatization from adaptation in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["suggest that the two words are interchangeable in everyday speech","establish a contrast that the rest of the passage will develop","argue that a climber's adjustments are more impressive than a herder's","introduce the genetic evidence that will be presented in the following paragraph","explain why natural selection works faster at high altitude than at sea level"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"The word is often confused with adaptation, and the difference matters."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"acclimatization is a short preview of what evolution would eventually deliver"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says the difference matters, and the passage returns to that difference at the end, when it warns against treating acclimatization as a preview of evolution. The definition sets up the contrast the whole passage builds toward.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author says the words are often confused, which is a reason to separate them.",null,"Outside the passage: the author never ranks the climber's adjustments against the herder's.","Wrong paragraph: the genetic evidence does not appear until paragraph 10, and paragraph 3 is about the carotid bodies.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about the speed of natural selection."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the carotid bodies","choices":["are located in the brainstem, where they regulate the depth of breathing","take several days to begin responding to a drop in oxygen pressure","release a hormone that signals the bone marrow to make more red cells","respond to a rise in the acidity of the blood by slowing the rate of breathing","sit where each common carotid artery divides and monitor arterial oxygen"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"lodged where each common carotid artery divides in the neck, monitor the oxygen in arterial blood"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 places the carotid bodies where each common carotid artery divides in the neck and says they monitor the oxygen in arterial blood.","distractorNotes":["Wrong location: paragraph 3 puts them in the neck and says they signal to the brainstem.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says the first response arrives within minutes.","Wrong organ: paragraph 8 says cells in the kidney release EPO.","Wrong sensor: paragraph 4 gives that job to chemoreceptors in the brainstem.",null]},{"id":"rc-o01-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if a newcomer's kidneys could not excrete bicarbonate, the newcomer would most likely","choices":["continue to have ventilation held back by the alkaline brake","experience the heaviest breathing on the first night rather than later","develop a more severe metabolic acidosis than usual","stop breathing faster altogether within the first day or two of the stay","show a larger rise in 2,3-BPG inside the red cells"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"the second signal acts as a brake"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"The kidneys release the brake, but slowly."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"As the alkalosis fades the brainstem stops objecting and ventilation climbs further"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the kidneys release the brake by excreting bicarbonate, and only as the alkalosis fades does ventilation climb further. Take away the bicarbonate excretion and the alkalosis, and the brake, would remain.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: paragraph 5 says the heaviest breathing comes after the brake is released, not before.","Reversed: the problem in paragraph 4 is alkalosis, and keeping bicarbonate would keep the blood alkaline.","Too extreme: the carotid bodies still drive extra breathing; the brake only limits how much ventilation rises.","Unrelated: 2,3-BPG in paragraph 7 is not tied to the kidneys' handling of bicarbonate."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q06","type":"function","stem":"The author describes acetazolamide in paragraph 5 primarily to","choices":["show how a drug can hasten a correction the kidneys make on their own","argue that drugs are a poor substitute for a careful ascent schedule","warn readers that the drug's side effects outweigh its benefits","explain why diuretics were abandoned as a treatment for acute mountain sickness","introduce the role of carbonic acid in the respiratory alkalosis"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"The drug thus imitates several days of renal work"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the drug exploits the same chemistry and imitates several days of renal work, so it is there as an example of speeding up what the kidneys already do.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong paragraph and too strong: paragraph 6 prefers a schedule, but paragraph 5 presents the drug favorably.","Reversed: paragraph 5 calls the side effects mostly harmless.","Outside the passage: the drug was developed as a diuretic, and nothing says diuretics were abandoned.","Wrong paragraph: carbonic acid is explained in paragraph 4, before the drug appears."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q07","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are named in the passage as features of acute mountain sickness EXCEPT:","choices":["headache","nausea","loss of appetite","dizziness","tingling"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"a headache","choice":0},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"loss of appetite","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"dizziness","choice":3},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"nausea","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":6,"quote":"tingling in the fingers and toes"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 lists headache, nausea, loss of appetite, fatigue, dizziness, and poor sleep. Tingling belongs to paragraph 5, where it is a side effect of acetazolamide.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 6 as the symptom that announces the illness.","Stated in paragraph 6 in the list of accompanying symptoms.","Stated in paragraph 6 in the list of accompanying symptoms.","Stated in paragraph 6 in the list of accompanying symptoms.",null]},{"id":"rc-o01-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the standard advice for climbers above about 3,000 meters includes","choices":["taking acetazolamide at the first sign that symptoms are worsening","descending by at least 500 meters after every third or fourth day","raising the sleeping elevation by no more than 300 to 500 meters a day","remaining at 2,500 meters until the heaviest breathing has passed","raising the sleeping elevation by about 300 meters every third or fourth day"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"raise the sleeping elevation by no more than 300 to 500 meters a day"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 spells out the schedule: no more than 300 to 500 meters of sleeping elevation a day, a rest every third or fourth day, and descent if symptoms worsen.","distractorNotes":["Mash-up: the advice is a schedule rather than a drug, and the response to worsening symptoms is descent.","Mash-up: the rest day comes every third or fourth day; no descent distance is given.",null,"Outside the passage: 2,500 meters is where mountain sickness commonly appears, not a holding point.","Mash-up: the limit is per day, and the third or fourth day is a rest day."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when 2,3-BPG binds to hemoglobin, it","choices":["tightens hemoglobin's grip on oxygen and shifts the dissociation curve to the left","speeds the kidneys' excretion of bicarbonate into the urine","helps the lungs load oxygen more effectively near a Himalayan summit","loosens hemoglobin's grip so oxygen unloads more readily in the tissues","raises the partial pressure of oxygen inside the alveoli"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"it loosens the grip, so oxygen unloads more readily in the tissues"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says that when 2,3-BPG binds hemoglobin it loosens the grip, so oxygen unloads more readily in the tissues, and the curve shifts right.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: that is what the alkalosis does, not 2,3-BPG.","Wrong paragraph: bicarbonate excretion belongs to the kidneys in paragraph 5.","Reversed: paragraph 7 says a tighter grip helps with loading, and 2,3-BPG loosens it.",null,"Wrong paragraph: raising alveolar oxygen is the job of extra ventilation in paragraph 3."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q10","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, near a Himalayan summit, a drug that shifted the oxygen curve further to the right would most likely","choices":["reverse the alkalosis that develops early in acclimatization","make it harder for the blood to pick up oxygen in the lungs","improve oxygen delivery by helping the tissues unload oxygen more readily","raise 2,3-BPG levels inside the red cells within a day or two","have no effect, because 2,3-BPG already dominates at extreme altitude"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"the harder problem is loading oxygen in the lungs, not unloading it in the tissues, and a tighter grip helps with loading"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"it loosens the grip"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says a rightward shift means a looser grip, and that near a summit the harder problem is loading oxygen in the lungs, which a tighter grip helps. A further right shift would loosen the grip and work against loading.","distractorNotes":["Unrelated: the curve's position does not change blood pH; the passage runs the cause the other way.",null,"Half right: unloading would be easier, but paragraph 7 says unloading is not the harder problem at extreme altitude.","Reversed cause and effect: 2,3-BPG shifts the curve; the curve does not raise 2,3-BPG.","Contradicted: paragraph 7 says the leftward pull wins at extreme altitude."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q11","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the early thickening of a traveler's blood at altitude is mainly due to","choices":["a drop in plasma volume that packs the same cells into less fluid","the peak in erythropoietin, which arrives within a day or two of reaching altitude","a slowing of blood flow through the small vessels","a surge of new red cells released by the bone marrow","the rise in 2,3-BPG inside the circulating red cells"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"plasma volume contracts early, so the same cells sit in less fluid"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the blood thickens within days, but not for the reason travelers imagine: plasma volume contracts early, so the same cells sit in less fluid.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong cause: EPO does peak early, but paragraph 8 attributes the thickening to plasma volume.","Reversed: in paragraph 9, slower flow is a result of thick blood, not its cause.","Reversed: paragraph 8 says a measurable rise in circulating red cells takes weeks.","Wrong paragraph: 2,3-BPG in paragraph 7 changes hemoglobin's grip, not the blood's thickness."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q12","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a blood sample drawn three weeks after a traveler arrives at altitude would most likely show","choices":["a red cell count that has returned to its sea-level value","a red cell count that has reached its final size, since EPO has stopped","an EPO level below its early peak and a red cell count that has risen","an EPO level near its peak and a red cell count that has not yet changed","an EPO level higher than on the second day and about as many red cells"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"then drifts back toward normal while the marrow keeps working; a measurable rise in circulating red cells takes weeks"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"The true expansion comes later and continues for months."}],"why":"Paragraph 8 gives the timeline: EPO peaks within a day or two and drifts back toward normal, while a measurable rise in red cells takes weeks. At three weeks, EPO is past its peak and the count has started to climb.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: the red cell mass is growing at that point, not returning to baseline.","Too strong: paragraph 8 says the expansion continues for months.",null,"Wrong timing: EPO is past its peak by three weeks, and the count has had weeks to rise.","Reversed: EPO peaks within a day or two and then drifts back down."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the idea that more red cells are simply better is best described as","choices":["skeptical","resigned","enthusiastic","indifferent","bewildered"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"I think that is mistaken"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"Past some point the slower flow cancels the extra carrying capacity"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 opens by calling the idea tempting and then says plainly, I think that is mistaken, before explaining why thicker blood stops helping. That is a skeptic's voice, not a believer's.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 9 suggests reluctant acceptance.","Reversed: the author rejects the idea rather than championing it.","Too weak: the author takes a clear position and argues for it.","Wrong register: the author explains the problem confidently, not in confusion."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q14","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle described in paragraph 9?","choices":["A farmer who plants more seeds in a field harvests more grain every year without limit","A library buys more books than its shelves can hold and stores the surplus in a basement","A runner who trains harder each week improves steadily until a sprained ankle forces a month of rest","A city adds buses to a route until the extra buses jam the streets and riders arrive later","A factory hires additional workers, and its output rises in direct proportion to the size of its staff"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"Past some point the slower flow cancels the extra carrying capacity, and oxygen delivery stops improving."},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"it also thickens the blood, and thick blood moves sluggishly"}],"why":"Paragraph 9's principle is that each added carrier also clogs the system, so past some point the extra capacity is cancelled by slower flow. Extra buses that jam the streets and slow riders down are the same pattern.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: the principle is that benefit stops past some point, not that it grows without limit.","Wrong mechanism: the surplus is stored, not turned into a cost that cancels the benefit.","Wrong mechanism: the setback is a separate injury, not the added effort itself slowing the whole system.",null,"Contradicted: output rising in direct proportion is exactly what paragraph 9 says does not happen."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q15","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of Tibetan highlanders EXCEPT:","choices":["They make more nitric oxide, which relaxes vessel walls.","Part of their DNA most likely came from Denisovans.","They carry far more hemoglobin than sea-level populations.","A variant of the gene EPAS1 is unusually common among them and linked to their hemoglobin.","They breathe more at rest."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"They breathe more at rest","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"they make more nitric oxide, a gas that relaxes vessel walls","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"is unusually common in Tibetans and linked to their lower hemoglobin","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"most likely entered the Tibetan lineage from Denisovans","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"hemoglobin concentrations close to sea-level values"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says Tibetan hemoglobin concentrations are close to sea-level values and well below those of Andeans. The other four statements all appear in paragraph 10.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 10, sentence 3 says they make more nitric oxide, a gas that relaxes vessel walls.","Paragraph 10, sentence 5 says this stretch of DNA most likely came from Denisovans.",null,"Paragraph 10, sentence 4 reports the EPAS1 variant and links it to their lower hemoglobin.","Paragraph 10, sentence 3 says this directly: they breathe more at rest."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q16","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 11, the word \"settlement\" most nearly means","choices":["the gradual sinking of material to the bottom of a liquid","an accommodation reached between two opposing pressures","a payment that ends a legal dispute between two parties","a final decision imposed from outside by an authority","a village founded by newcomers in a remote place"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"a settlement reached over a long time in which the body stops fighting the thin air and lives with it"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 describes Tibetan physiology as a settlement in which the body stops fighting the thin air and lives with it: an accommodation between the body and its surroundings, not a place or a payment.","distractorNotes":["Settling of particles is a lab meaning of the word that has nothing to do with this sentence.",null,"A legal settlement is another everyday meaning; nothing in the sentence involves a dispute between parties or money.","Nothing is imposed from outside; the body reaches the arrangement itself, over a long time.","This is the common sense of the word, and the Tibetans do live in villages, but the sentence describes a state of the body, not a place."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Producing more red blood cells is the single most important change that lets a person survive at high altitude","Chronic mountain sickness shows that people who live high in the Andes are poorly suited to their home and should move lower","Climbers should take acetazolamide before every ascent because the kidneys correct the blood's chemistry too slowly","Tibetan highlanders prove that natural selection always finds the same solution that acclimatization reaches","The body's short-term answers to thin air are real but limited, and they differ from long-term genetic adaptation"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"Acclimatization is reversible and happens within one lifetime; adaptation is the work of natural selection"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"that acclimatization is a short preview of what evolution would eventually deliver"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"the system was tuned for a journey of days and weeks, not for the generations of a home"}],"why":"The passage walks through the body's responses to thin air over hours, days and weeks, warns that more red cells is not simply better, and closes by separating acclimatization from adaptation. Only the answer covers both halves.","distractorNotes":["The author is openly skeptical that more red cells is simply better; that is the point of paragraph 9.","Too extreme and never said: the author refuses any tidy verdict about which population did better.","Acetazolamide is one detail in the passage, and the passage never says every climber should take it.","Reversed: paragraph 10 says Tibetans turned down the very response visitors turn up, a different solution.",null]}]},"rc-o02":{"id":"rc-o02","title":"The Chain and Its Weakest Link","topic":"trophic cascades and the difficulty of proving them","field":"ecology","source":"original","words":1356,"paragraphs":[["Remove a predator from a community and the effects should ripple down through everything it ate and everything its prey ate.","Ecologists call this a trophic cascade: a top predator's influence tumbles down through two or more feeding levels, the sign of the effect flipping at each step.","More predators mean fewer grazers, and fewer grazers mean more plants.","It is one of ecology's most satisfying ideas, simple to state and, in a few famous cases, demonstrated with something close to proof.","Yet the cleaner a cascade story sounds, the more tempting it is to tell it without checking whether every link actually held."],["The first of those cases began in 1963 on a rocky shore at Mukkaw Bay, on the outer coast of Washington's Olympic Peninsula.","Robert Paine, a zoologist at the University of Washington, began removing every ochre sea star, Pisaster ochraceus, from a section of intertidal rock, prying them loose and flinging them into deeper water.","A neighboring stretch of shore, left alone, served as the control.","The sea star is the main predator of the California mussel, and without it the mussels spread downward across rock that had held barnacles, limpets, chitons, and algae.","Within a few years the mussels had crowded most of these out, and the species count on the cleared plot fell from about fifteen to eight.","Paine published the result in 1966."],["In 1969 Paine gave the pattern a name that has stuck: the keystone species.","The metaphor comes from the wedge-shaped block at the top of an arch, small relative to the whole but holding every other stone in place.","A keystone predator is one whose effect on its community is far out of proportion to its abundance.","The sea stars were never numerous, but their steady predation arrested the mussels' advance and kept space open for everything else.","The method mattered more than the metaphor: Paine had not merely observed a correlation; he had removed the predator, watched the response, and compared it against an untouched plot a few meters away.","In 1980 Paine also supplied the phrase \"trophic cascade\" itself."],["The second case came from the Aleutian Islands of Alaska, where James Estes began studying sea otters in the early 1970s.","The fur trade had hunted otters nearly to extinction, and when an international treaty protected them in 1911 only scattered remnant colonies survived.","By the 1970s the otters had recovered around some islands but not others, which gave Estes a natural comparison.","Around islands with otters, the sea floor was a forest of kelp.","Around islands without them, the kelp was gone and the rock was carpeted with sea urchins, a condition divers call an urchin barren.","Sea otters eat urchins, urchins eat kelp, and so a predator two links away determines whether a kelp forest exists at all.","Estes and a colleague published the comparison in Science in 1974."],["The otter story later acquired a fourth link nobody had planned for.","In the 1990s, otter numbers around the western Aleutians collapsed, and in 1998 Estes and his colleagues reported that killer whales had apparently begun eating otters, possibly because their usual prey of seals and sea lions had declined.","As the otters vanished, urchins returned and the kelp thinned again.","This is perhaps the most instructive part of the story: a cascade is not a closed loop but an open chain, and a disturbance anywhere along it can be felt on the rocks near shore."],["No cascade story is told more often than the Yellowstone wolf story, and none is harder to pin down.","Gray wolves had been eliminated from Yellowstone National Park by the 1920s.","In 1995 biologists released fourteen wolves captured in Alberta, and in 1996 they added seventeen more from British Columbia, for a total of thirty-one founders.","By then the northern range elk herd numbered nearly twenty thousand, and the willows, aspen, and cottonwoods along the streams had been browsed to stubs for decades."],["Within a few years, William Ripple and Robert Beschta of Oregon State University reported that young willows and aspen in parts of the northern range were taller than they had been in decades.","Their explanation had two parts.","First, wolves were killing elk, so there were fewer mouths to browse the shoots.","Second, the surviving elk were afraid, avoiding streamside thickets and valley bottoms where wolves could ambush them, so plants in those risky places got a reprieve.","From there the story grew: taller willows would bring back beavers, beaver dams would raise water tables and create wetlands, songbirds would return, and stabilized banks would change the shape of the rivers.","In 2014 a short video titled \"How Wolves Change Rivers,\" narrated by the British writer George Monbiot, carried this version to tens of millions of viewers."],["Almost every piece of that account has since been contested, and the contest is where the lessons lie.","Elk numbers did fall, from nearly twenty thousand in the mid-1990s to a few thousand by the 2010s.","But wolves were far from the only new pressure.","Hunters in Montana took more than a thousand elk in some winters as the herd migrated north out of the park.","Grizzly bears and black bears, both increasing, took a large share of newborn calves each spring.","Cougars had quietly recolonized the park in the 1980s.","A severe drought in the early 2000s reduced the forage elk depend on.","Sorting the wolves' share out of that list is an accounting problem no one has fully solved."],["The second half of the hypothesis, the idea that fear alone reshaped elk behavior, has fared worse.","The phrase \"landscape of fear\" was introduced in 2001 to describe how Yellowstone's elk and bison might read the terrain for danger, and it was quickly adopted as the mechanism behind the plant recovery.","But when Matthew Kauffman and colleagues tested it in 2010, aspen stands where elk faced the highest risk from wolves were recovering no better than stands in safer places.","Tracking-collar studies found that an individual elk met wolves only occasionally and went back to feeding soon afterward, hardly the behavior of an animal too frightened to eat."],["Then there is the matter of water, which the tidy version leaves out entirely.","Willows along Yellowstone's small streams need a high water table, and the beavers whose dams once maintained it had been nearly absent for decades, partly because there were no tall willows for them to eat and build with.","A decade-long experiment by Colorado State University researchers fenced willow plots to keep elk out and, in some, built small dams to mimic the work of beavers.","Willows protected from browsing but left with a low water table did not reach the heights of the past, while those given both protection and water did.","Removing the browsers is not enough: the willows need the beavers and the beavers need the willows, a loop that wolves alone cannot restart."],["The claim that wolves changed the course of rivers is the boldest in the chain and rests on the thinnest evidence.","Stream channels were cut deep by decades of erosion while the willows were gone, and channel form responds to vegetation over spans far longer than the twenty years since the wolves returned.","The change in the rivers that the video describes remains a projection more than a measured result."],["None of this means the wolves did nothing.","Elk are fewer, some willow stands have recovered, and biologists still argue over how much credit the wolves deserve, which is exactly how a live scientific question should look.","What separates Yellowstone from Mukkaw Bay or the Aleutians is not the strength of the idea but the shape of the evidence.","Paine had a control plot, and Estes had islands with and without otters.","Yellowstone is a single reintroduction in a single park, with bears, cougars, hunters, drought, and beavers all changing at once, and nothing to compare it against.","The trophic cascade remains one of ecology's best ideas, and the honest way to admire it is to keep asking, at every link, whether the chain was tested or merely assumed."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o02-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, what happened on the plot at Mukkaw Bay after the sea stars were removed?","choices":["Barnacles replaced the mussels as the dominant animal on the rock","The number of species fell from about fifteen to eight","Kelp spread across rock that the mussels had once held","Sea urchins carpeted the rock once the sea stars were gone","The number of species roughly doubled as the mussels spread"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"the species count on the cleared plot fell from about fifteen to eight"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says that once the mussels crowded the other species out, the species count on the cleared plot fell from about fifteen to eight.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the mussels crowded out the barnacles, not the other way around.",null,"Kelp belongs to the Aleutian otter story in paragraph 4, not to Mukkaw Bay.","Urchin barrens are the Aleutian result in paragraph 4; at Mukkaw Bay the mussels took over.","Reversed: diversity fell by about half, it did not double."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q02","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, the word 'arrested' most nearly means","choices":["detained","captured","halted","attracted","punished"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"their steady predation arrested the mussels' advance"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the predation 'arrested the mussels' advance and kept space open,' so the sea stars were stopping the mussels from spreading. The police meaning of the word is the trap.","distractorNotes":["The police sense of the word; sea stars do not detain mussels.","Sea stars do eat mussels, but the sentence is about stopping the mussels' advance, not seizing individuals.",null,"Opposite in spirit; the predation held the mussels back rather than drawing them in.","Imports a moral sense the sentence does not carry."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions 'an untouched plot a few meters away' in paragraph 3 primarily to","choices":["show how small the area Paine studied actually was","explain why the mussels spread downward across the rock","suggest that the sea stars soon recolonized the cleared rock","stress that Paine's result rested on an experiment with a control","argue that sea star removal had effects well beyond the boundaries of the cleared plot"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"compared it against an untouched plot a few meters away"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the method mattered more than the metaphor: Paine had not merely observed a correlation; he removed the predator and compared the result against an untouched plot. The untouched plot is the control, and that is the point being made.","distractorNotes":["The distance is not there to describe the study's size; the sentence is about method.","The mussels' spread is explained in paragraph 2 by the missing predator, not by the control plot.","The passage never says the sea stars recolonized the plot.",null,"Nothing in the passage describes effects spilling outside the cleared plot."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, which of the following characterized Aleutian islands that lacked sea otters?","choices":["Rock carpeted with sea urchins and stripped of kelp","Kelp forests that had grown unusually dense in the otters' absence","Remnant otter colonies protected by the 1911 treaty","Killer whales feeding on seals and sea lions","Mussel beds that crowded out other species"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"the kelp was gone and the rock was carpeted with sea urchins"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says that around islands without otters 'the kelp was gone and the rock was carpeted with sea urchins,' which divers call an urchin barren.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: kelp forests were found around islands with otters, not without them.","Remnant colonies are where otters survived; the question asks about islands without them.","Killer whales enter in paragraph 5, and their switch to otters is a 1990s event, not a feature of otter-free islands in the 1970s.","Mussel beds are the Mukkaw Bay result in paragraph 2."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that Estes's study differed from Paine's in that Estes","choices":["removed the predator from the study area himself","worked without any site to compare against","studied a predator that was more numerous than the sea star","published his results before the term keystone species existed","relied on a comparison he had not set up himself"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"which gave Estes a natural comparison"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"Paine had a control plot, and Estes had islands with and without otters"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says uneven otter recovery 'gave Estes a natural comparison,' and paragraph 12 contrasts Paine's control plot with Estes's islands. Paine created his comparison; nature handed Estes his.","distractorNotes":["That describes Paine, who pried the sea stars loose by hand.","The islands with and without otters were his comparison.","The passage says nothing about how numerous otters were relative to sea stars.","Keystone species was named in 1969; Estes published in 1974.",null]},{"id":"rc-o02-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Estes and his colleagues reported in 1998 that","choices":["sea otters had been protected by a new international treaty","urchin barrens around the western Aleutians had turned back into kelp forest","killer whales had apparently begun to eat sea otters","seals and sea lions had recovered around the western Aleutians","sea otters had spread to the shore at Mukkaw Bay"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"killer whales had apparently begun eating otters"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the 1998 report was that 'killer whales had apparently begun eating otters,' possibly because seals and sea lions had declined.","distractorNotes":["The treaty dates to 1911, in paragraph 4.","Reversed: as otters vanished, urchins returned and the kelp thinned.",null,"Reversed: the passage suggests seals and sea lions had declined, not recovered.","Mukkaw Bay is Paine's sea star site; otters are never placed there."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q07","type":"function","stem":"The author describes the killer whale episode in paragraph 5 primarily to","choices":["show that the sea otter cascade had been mistaken all along","explain why the fur trade nearly drove sea otters to extinction","argue that killer whales deserve the label keystone species","show that a cascade is an open chain, not a closed loop","demonstrate that kelp forests recover quickly once urchin numbers fall"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"a cascade is not a closed loop but an open chain"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 calls the episode 'the most instructive part of the story' because it shows 'a cascade is not a closed loop but an open chain' that a disturbance from elsewhere can reach.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the episode extends the cascade by a link; it does not overturn it.","The fur trade is in paragraph 4 and has nothing to do with killer whales.","The passage never applies the keystone label to killer whales.",null,"Reversed: when the otters vanished, the kelp thinned."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, how many wolves in total were released in Yellowstone in 1995 and 1996?","choices":["fourteen","seventeen","twenty","thirty-one","nearly twenty thousand"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"for a total of thirty-one founders"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 gives fourteen wolves in 1995 and seventeen in 1996, 'for a total of thirty-one founders.'","distractorNotes":["Fourteen is the 1995 release alone.","Seventeen is the 1996 release alone.","Twenty does not appear in the passage; it is a rough mental sum that misses by eleven.",null,"Nearly twenty thousand is the size of the elk herd, not the number of wolves."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Ripple and Beschta's explanation for the taller willows and aspen had which two parts?","choices":["Fewer elk overall, and surviving elk that avoided risky places","Drought on the northern range and the return of beavers to its streams","Human hunting north of the park and rising bear predation on calves","Higher water tables and fewer cougars in the valley bottoms","Beaver dams and the return of songbirds to the thickets"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"wolves were killing elk, so there were fewer mouths to browse the shoots"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"the surviving elk were afraid, avoiding streamside thickets and valley bottoms"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lays out the two parts: wolves killed elk so there were fewer mouths to browse, and the surviving elk were afraid and avoided streamside thickets and valley bottoms.","distractorNotes":[null,"Drought and beavers belong to the critics' side in paragraphs 8 and 10, not to Ripple and Beschta's explanation.","Hunting and bears are alternative pressures from paragraph 8, raised by critics, not by Ripple and Beschta.","Water tables are part of the Colorado State experiment in paragraph 10; cougars appear in paragraph 8.","These are downstream predictions in the extended story, not the two-part explanation itself."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q10","type":"except","stem":"According to paragraph 7, the extended version of the Yellowstone story predicted all of the following EXCEPT","choices":["the return of beavers to the streams","the return of songbirds","stabilized stream banks","rivers changing shape","a decline in grizzly bears"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"taller willows would bring back beavers","choice":0},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"songbirds would return","choice":1},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"stabilized banks","choice":2},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"change the shape of the rivers","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lists beavers, wetlands, songbirds, stabilized banks, and reshaped rivers. Bears appear only in paragraph 8, and there they are increasing, not declining.","distractorNotes":["Stated: 'taller willows would bring back beavers.'","Stated: 'songbirds would return.'","Stated: 'stabilized banks would change the shape of the rivers.'","Stated: the banks 'would change the shape of the rivers.'",null]},{"id":"rc-o02-q11","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the popular version of the Yellowstone story is best described as","choices":["admiring","skeptical","indifferent","hostile","bewildered"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"which the tidy version leaves out entirely"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"the boldest in the chain and rests on the thinnest evidence"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"without checking whether every link actually held"}],"why":"The judging words are 'tidy version,' 'boldest in the chain,' 'thinnest evidence,' and 'a projection more than a measured result.' That is doubt with reasons, not dismissal.","distractorNotes":["The author admires the cascade idea and Paine's method, not the popular Yellowstone retelling.",null,"An indifferent writer would not spend five paragraphs testing the story link by link.","Too strong: the author says 'none of this means the wolves did nothing.'","The author is not confused; the critique is organized and specific."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q12","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are mentioned in the passage as pressures on Yellowstone's elk other than wolves EXCEPT","choices":["hunting by people north of the park in Montana","predation on newborn calves by bears","the recolonization of the park by cougars","a severe drought in the early 2000s","disease spreading from domestic cattle"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"Hunters in Montana took more than a thousand elk","choice":0},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"took a large share of newborn calves","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"Cougars had quietly recolonized the park","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":7,"quote":"A severe drought in the early 2000s","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 8 lists hunters, bears, cougars, and drought. Disease never appears anywhere in the passage.","distractorNotes":["Stated: hunters in Montana took more than a thousand elk in some winters.","Stated: grizzly and black bears took a large share of newborn calves.","Stated: cougars had recolonized the park in the 1980s.","Stated: a severe drought in the early 2000s reduced forage.",null]},{"id":"rc-o02-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that if the 'landscape of fear' had been the main driver of plant recovery, then","choices":["aspen in risky places should have outgrown aspen in safe ones","elk numbers would have needed to fall below a few thousand","beavers would have returned before the willows grew tall","the wolves would have had to come from Alberta rather than British Columbia","the drought of the early 2000s would have had no effect on elk"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"aspen stands where elk faced the highest risk from wolves were recovering no better than stands in safer places"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 treats Kauffman's finding, that high-risk aspen stands were 'recovering no better than stands in safer places,' as evidence against the fear hypothesis. That only counts against it if the hypothesis predicted the risky stands would do better.","distractorNotes":[null,"The fear hypothesis is about behavior, not a threshold in elk numbers.","The passage says beavers need tall willows first, the opposite order.","Where the wolves came from has no bearing on the fear mechanism.","Nothing links the fear hypothesis to drought having no effect."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q14","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Colorado State University experiment found that willows grew to the heights of the past only when they were","choices":["left unfenced but given a raised water table","fenced and also given a raised water table","fenced after live beavers had been returned to the streams","browsed lightly by elk during the winter months","fenced from elk but left with a low water table"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"those given both protection and water did"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says willows protected from browsing but left with a low water table did not reach the heights of the past, 'while those given both protection and water did.'","distractorNotes":["Half right: water alone is not what the passage credits; the successful plots had fences as well.",null,"The experiment used small built dams to mimic beavers, not live beavers.","Light browsing is never mentioned; the fences kept elk out.","This is the group that did not reach the past heights."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the claim about rivers is weak chiefly because","choices":["the rivers in the northern range have changed in the opposite direction","beavers have never returned to any Yellowstone stream","stream channels respond more slowly than twenty years","the video that popularized it was narrated by a writer rather than a scientist","elk never browsed close enough to the rivers to affect them"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"channel form responds to vegetation over spans far longer than the twenty years since the wolves returned"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says channel form 'responds to vegetation over spans far longer than the twenty years since the wolves returned,' which is why the river change is called a projection rather than a measured result.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the change is unmeasured, not that it ran the other way.","Too strong and unsupported: the passage never says beavers are absent from every stream.",null,"The author never attacks Monbiot's credentials; the objection is about evidence and time.","Reversed: the passage says willows along the streams had been browsed to stubs for decades."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q16","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the kind of evidence the author says Yellowstone lacks?","choices":["Removing a fish from some ponds and leaving matched ponds untouched","Noting that deer declined in a park during the decade after coyotes arrived there","Filming a documentary about how sharks protect seagrass meadows","Counting the birds in a single forest over one summer","Building a computer model of plant growth after predators return"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"Paine had a control plot, and Estes had islands with and without otters"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"nothing to compare it against"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says what Yellowstone lacks is a comparison: Paine had a control plot and Estes had islands with and without otters. Ponds with the predator removed beside matched untouched ponds is exactly that design.","distractorNotes":[null,"This is the Yellowstone problem itself: a single before-and-after with nothing to compare it against.","A documentary is a retelling, the very thing the author warns can outrun the evidence.","One forest, one summer, and no manipulation gives nothing to compare.","A model predicts; the author wants a tested comparison, not a projection."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Wolves restored Yellowstone's willows, aspen, beavers, and rivers, confirming the cascade that Paine and Estes first described","The experiments at Mukkaw Bay and in the Aleutians showed that predators matter less than ecologists once believed","The cascade idea is sound, but Yellowstone's popular version outruns its evidence","Ecologists should abandon the term keystone species because the public misunderstands it","Elk declines in Yellowstone were caused entirely by hunters, bears, and drought rather than by wolves"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"the cleaner a cascade story sounds, the more tempting it is to tell it without checking whether every link actually held"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"not the strength of the idea but the shape of the evidence"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":6,"quote":"whether the chain was tested or merely assumed"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 sets the thesis, that clean cascade stories tempt us to skip checking the links, and paragraph 12 closes it: the idea is strong, Yellowstone's evidence is not shaped like Paine's or Estes's, and the honest way to admire the idea is to keep testing each link.","distractorNotes":["This is the popular version the passage spends five paragraphs contesting.","Reversed: both experiments showed that predators matter enormously.",null,"The author never argues against the term; it is presented approvingly.","Too strong: paragraph 12 says the wolves did not do nothing, and sorting out their share is unsolved."]}]},"rc-o03":{"id":"rc-o03","title":"Insulin and the Quarrel over Credit","topic":"the discovery of insulin and the dispute over credit","field":"history of science","source":"original","words":1385,"paragraphs":[["When news of the 1923 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine reached Toronto, one of the two winners reacted not with joy but with anger.","Frederick Banting learned that he would share the prize with John James Rickard Macleod, the professor of physiology in whose Toronto department the work had been done.","Banting considered refusing it.","Instead he announced that he would split his prize money with Charles Best, the student who had worked beside him, and Macleod responded by splitting his with James Collip, the biochemist who had purified the extract.","The record supports a less dramatic conclusion than the legend: insulin was the product of a team, and the instruments we use to assign credit were never built to describe one."],["The trail begins in 1869, when Paul Langerhans, a medical student in Berlin, described small clusters of cells scattered through the pancreas that looked nothing like the surrounding tissue, which makes digestive juice for the gut.","He did not know what the clusters did, and he did not guess.","In 1889, at Strasbourg, Oskar Minkowski and Joseph von Mering removed the pancreas from a dog and found that the animal developed every sign of diabetes, including urine so rich in sugar that it drew flies.","In 1893 the French histologist Edouard Laguesse named the clusters the islets of Langerhans and proposed that they released something directly into the blood, an internal secretion in the language of the day."],["A name came before a substance: the Belgian Jean de Meyer proposed insuline in 1909, and the English physiologist Edward Sharpey-Schafer proposed it again in 1916, both from the Latin insula, meaning island.","Extracting the hormone was harder than naming it, because injected pancreas extracts often caused fever, abscesses, and toxic reactions.","Georg Zuelzer in Berlin treated diabetic patients with a pancreatic extract around 1908 and saw their sugar fall before side effects forced him to stop.","Ernest Scott in Chicago in 1912 and Israel Kleiner in New York in 1919 each lowered the blood sugar of depancreatized dogs with extracts, and each stopped short of treating a patient.","Nicolae Paulescu in Bucharest reported in 1921 that his extract lowered the blood sugar of diabetic dogs, publishing in French months before the Toronto results appeared.","It is sobering that the substance sat in impure form in several laboratories for more than a decade before anyone could keep a patient alive with it."],["In the autumn of 1920 Banting was a struggling surgeon in London, Ontario, preparing a lecture on the pancreas, when he read an article about a patient whose pancreatic duct had been blocked by a stone.","In such cases the enzyme-producing tissue of the gland withers while the islets survive.","Early on October 31 he wrote himself a note: tie off the pancreatic ducts of dogs, wait for the digestive tissue to degenerate, and extract whatever the surviving islets contained.","His reasoning was that earlier extracts had failed because the gland's own digestive enzymes destroyed the hormone during preparation.","He took the idea to Macleod, an authority on carbohydrate metabolism, who was skeptical but agreed to provide laboratory space, ten dogs, and a student assistant for the summer of 1921."],["The assistant was Charles Best, a twenty-two-year-old with a fresh degree in physiology and biochemistry who could measure blood sugar in small samples, a skill Banting lacked.","The two began on May 17, 1921; Macleod left for Scotland in June, an absence Banting never let him forget.","On July 30, 1921, an extract of duct-ligated pancreas was injected into a depancreatized dog, and its blood sugar fell.","Banting and Best called the material isletin.","Over the following months they found that extracts of fetal calf pancreas worked, and then that whole adult beef pancreas soaked in alcohol worked too; it was the alcohol method that made the slow business of duct ligation unnecessary.","Banting's hypothesis had been the doorway rather than the destination; the method that finally mattered was close to what Scott had tried a decade earlier."],["When Macleod returned in September he pressed for better controls and repeated experiments, advice that Banting took as interference.","In December Macleod brought in Collip, a biochemist from the University of Alberta on sabbatical in Toronto.","Collip's contribution was purification.","By precipitating the extract in rising concentrations of alcohol he found that the active substance came out of solution near ninety percent while most contaminating protein stayed in solution.","He also showed that the extract made the liver store glycogen and that an overdose in rabbits produced convulsions that a dose of sugar could reverse, an early description of what would be called insulin shock."],["The first patient was Leonard Thompson, a fourteen-year-old boy who weighed about sixty-five pounds and was dying of diabetes in Toronto General Hospital.","On January 11, 1922, he received an injection of the Banting and Best extract.","His blood sugar fell somewhat, but sugar remained in his urine and a sterile abscess formed at the injection site; by any honest reading, the first trial failed.","On January 23 he received Collip's purified extract.","His blood sugar dropped sharply, the sugar in his urine nearly vanished, and the ketones that mark a body consuming its own fat disappeared.","Thompson lived another thirteen years."],["Success did not bring peace.","Within days of the second injection Collip told Banting and Best that he would not share his purification method, and an argument followed that, by the participants' later accounts, turned physical.","On January 25 the four signed an agreement to work together under Macleod's direction and to seek no patents independently.","Then the method failed: when Collip tried to make larger batches the extract lost its potency, and for weeks no one in Toronto could produce usable insulin.","Best recovered a working process in May.","That same month the university reached an agreement with Eli Lilly and Company of Indianapolis to produce insulin at scale."],["The patent question showed the group at its best.","Banting at first refused to put his name on any patent, holding that a physician should not profit from a remedy, so Best and Collip filed the application and his name was added later.","In 1923 the three assigned the United States patent to the Board of Governors of the University of Toronto for one dollar each.","The purpose was not income but control: the university could license manufacturers, require that every batch meet a standard of potency and purity, and keep any one company from cornering a drug that patients would need every day for life.","Whatever one makes of the men's quarrels, this decision was made with patients in view."],["The Nobel committee acted quickly, partly because the Danish physiologist August Krogh, himself a laureate, visited Toronto in late 1922, carried the method home, and nominated Banting and Macleod.","The prize was announced in October 1923.","Banting, who turned thirty-two that November, remains the youngest laureate in that category and was the first Canadian to win a Nobel Prize.","None of that softened his conviction that Macleod had taken credit for his idea, nor his insistence that Best belonged on the citation.","Macleod regarded the award as recognition of a department's achievement and quietly shared his money with the man who had made the extract safe to inject.","The two laureates never reconciled, and Macleod left Toronto for Scotland in 1928."],["What, then, was discovered, and by whom?","Banting supplied the idea and the stubbornness to test it, Best the daily laboratory work, Collip the purification that turned a toxic soup into a medicine, and Macleod the laboratory, the discipline, and the standing that got the work heard.","The historian Michael Bliss, whose 1982 study remains the standard account, reached much the same verdict from the notebooks and was not gentle with the summer experiments, which contain errors a more experienced investigator would have caught.","Yet the error-prone summer led somewhere, and the careful investigators before it had not.","Prizes honor names, and history remembers names; the discovery itself was made by a laboratory.","That is less a criticism of the Nobel committee than a limit of its instrument, and the Toronto quarrel is best read not as a contest with a rightful winner but as four men learning that a shared achievement cannot be divided."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o03-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, after Banting announced that he would split his prize money with Best, Macleod","choices":["refused any share of the prize","divided his own share of the prize money with Best","split his share of the money with Collip","briefly considered declining the award","publicly disputed Banting's claim to the discovery"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"Macleod responded by splitting his with James Collip"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says Macleod responded by splitting his prize money with James Collip, the biochemist who had purified the extract.","distractorNotes":["Macleod accepted the prize; nothing in the passage says he refused a share.","Best received money from Banting, not from Macleod.",null,"It was Banting who considered refusing the prize, not Macleod.","The passage never says Macleod publicly disputed Banting's claim."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the 1889 experiment of Minkowski and von Mering showed that","choices":["a dog without its pancreas developed diabetes","the islets release an internal secretion","the pancreas makes digestive juice","sugar in the urine could be reversed by a pancreatic extract","a stone blocking the pancreatic duct destroys the islets"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"removed the pancreas from a dog and found that the animal developed every sign of diabetes"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says that at Strasbourg in 1889 Minkowski and von Mering removed a dog's pancreas and the animal developed every sign of diabetes.","distractorNotes":[null,"The islet idea was Laguesse's proposal in 1893, not a result of the 1889 experiment.","Digestive juice is mentioned in describing Langerhans's work, not as a finding of this experiment.","No extract was given in 1889; the experiment removed the organ and watched diabetes appear.","The duct stone appears in paragraph 4, and there the islets survive rather than being destroyed."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the flies in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["explain Laguesse's internal secretion idea","suggest the laboratory was poorly kept","show that Langerhans's clusters had a digestive function","make vivid how much sugar the dog's urine carried","contrast the dog's condition with that of Leonard Thompson"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"including urine so rich in sugar that it drew flies"}],"why":"The flies are a concrete picture of the sign named in the same sentence: urine so rich in sugar that it drew flies. The detail makes the dog's diabetes visible.","distractorNotes":["Laguesse's 1893 proposal is a separate sentence and is not tied to the flies.","The passage says nothing about the state of the laboratory.","The clusters are set apart from the digestive tissue, and the flies illustrate sugar loss, not digestion.",null,"Thompson appears in paragraph 7 and is never compared with the dog."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q04","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are named in the passage as investigators whose pancreatic extracts lowered sugar before 1922 EXCEPT:","choices":["Georg Zuelzer","Ernest Scott","Israel Kleiner","Nicolae Paulescu","Paul Langerhans"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"Georg Zuelzer in Berlin treated diabetic patients with a pancreatic extract around 1908 and saw their sugar fall","choice":0},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Ernest Scott in Chicago in 1912 and Israel Kleiner in New York in 1919 each lowered the blood sugar of depancreatized dogs with extracts","choice":1},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Israel Kleiner in New York in 1919 each lowered the blood sugar of depancreatized dogs with extracts","choice":2},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"Nicolae Paulescu in Bucharest reported in 1921 that his extract lowered the blood sugar of diabetic dogs","choice":3},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"He did not know what the clusters did, and he did not guess.","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 3 confirms Zuelzer, Scott, Kleiner, and Paulescu one by one. Langerhans, in paragraph 2, described the clusters in 1869 and did not even guess what they did; he made no extract.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 says Zuelzer treated patients with an extract and saw their sugar fall.","Paragraph 3 says Scott lowered the blood sugar of depancreatized dogs in 1912.","Paragraph 3 says Kleiner did the same in New York in 1919.","Paragraph 3 says Paulescu reported his extract lowered blood sugar in diabetic dogs in 1921.",null]},{"id":"rc-o03-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, by 1921, the chief obstacle to treating diabetes with a pancreatic extract was","choices":["ignorance of which organ controlled blood sugar","the impurity of the extracts rather than their absence","the lack of a name for the hormone being sought","the refusal of physicians to test extracts on patients","the absence of a reliable way to make a diabetic animal"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"injected pancreas extracts often caused fever, abscesses, and toxic reactions"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"the substance sat in impure form in several laboratories for more than a decade"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the extracts caused fever, abscesses, and toxic reactions, and the author adds that the substance sat in impure form in several laboratories for more than a decade. The problem was purity, not existence.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 says the 1889 experiment had already tied the pancreas to diabetes.",null,"The name insuline existed by 1909, before the substance was isolated.","Zuelzer did treat patients; what stopped him was side effects, not refusal.","Removing the pancreas was already a known way to produce diabetes in a dog."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Banting proposed tying off the pancreatic ducts of dogs because he believed that","choices":["the islets would multiply once the ducts closed","Macleod had suggested the procedure","stones in the pancreatic duct were the usual cause of diabetes in people","the gland's own enzymes were destroying the hormone","duct ligation was the only way to obtain a usable extract"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"earlier extracts had failed because the gland's own digestive enzymes destroyed the hormone during preparation"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 gives his reasoning directly: earlier extracts had failed because the gland's own digestive enzymes destroyed the hormone during preparation, so letting that tissue degenerate first should protect it.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the islets survive when the duct is blocked, not that they multiply.","Macleod was skeptical of the idea; it was Banting's, not his.","The stone case was the article that gave Banting the idea; the passage never calls stones the usual cause of diabetes.",null,"Paragraph 5 says duct ligation turned out to be unnecessary, so it was not the only way."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q07","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, which finding made duct ligation unnecessary?","choices":["Whole adult beef pancreas soaked in alcohol gave a working extract","Extracts of fetal calf pancreas lowered blood sugar in dogs","Collip's alcohol precipitation removed the contaminating protein","Blood sugar could be measured in small samples","Macleod's demand for repeated experiments confirmed the summer results"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"it was the alcohol method that made the slow business of duct ligation unnecessary"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says whole adult beef pancreas soaked in alcohol worked, and that it was the alcohol method that made duct ligation unnecessary.","distractorNotes":[null,"Fetal calf pancreas worked, but the passage credits the alcohol method, not the fetal extracts, with ending duct ligation.","Collip's precipitation is in paragraph 6 and concerns purifying the extract, not how the gland was prepared.","Measuring blood sugar was Best's skill; it does not touch the duct question.","Macleod's demands are in paragraph 6 and are never linked to duct ligation."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q08","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the rabbit experiments in paragraph 6 primarily to","choices":["explain why Collip refused to share his method","show that Collip's work went beyond purification","argue that the extract was too dangerous to give to patients","illustrate why the first Thompson injection failed","demonstrate that Macleod's demand for controls was justified"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"Collip's contribution was purification."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"He also showed that the extract made the liver store glycogen and that an overdose in rabbits produced convulsions"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 states that Collip's contribution was purification, then opens the next sentence with \"He also showed,\" adding the glycogen and overdose findings. The rabbits extend his contribution past purification.","distractorNotes":["Collip's refusal is in paragraph 8 and is never explained by the rabbits.",null,"The passage goes straight on to treat a patient; it does not argue the extract was too dangerous.","The first injection failed through impurity and an abscess, not an overdose.","Macleod's demand for controls is a separate sentence about Banting's resentment."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the injection Leonard Thompson received on January 11, 1922","choices":["used the extract that Collip had purified","cleared the ketones from his urine within a day","lowered his blood sugar sharply but produced convulsions and coma","had no measurable effect on his blood sugar","lowered his blood sugar only somewhat and caused an abscess"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"His blood sugar fell somewhat, but sugar remained in his urine and a sterile abscess formed at the injection site"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says his blood sugar fell somewhat, sugar remained in his urine, and a sterile abscess formed at the injection site. That was January 11; the sharp drop came on January 23.","distractorNotes":["Collip's purified extract was the second injection, on January 23.","The ketones disappeared after the January 23 injection, not the first.","The sharp drop belongs to January 23, and no convulsions are reported in Thompson.","The passage says his blood sugar fell somewhat, so there was a measurable effect.",null]},{"id":"rc-o03-q10","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that the agreement signed on January 25, 1922 was a response to","choices":["Macleod's return from Scotland the previous September","the loss of potency in Collip's larger batches","the dispute over Collip's purification method","the university's negotiations with Eli Lilly","Banting's refusal to put his name on a patent"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"Collip told Banting and Best that he would not share his purification method"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"signed an agreement to work together under Macleod's direction and to seek no patents independently"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 places the agreement right after Collip's refusal to share his method and the argument that followed, and its terms, working together and seeking no patents independently, answer exactly that dispute.","distractorNotes":["Macleod's return is in paragraph 6, months earlier, and is not connected to the agreement.","The batches lost potency after the agreement; the passage says \"Then the method failed.\"",null,"The Lilly arrangement came in May, after the agreement.","Banting's refusal concerns the patent application in paragraph 9, which came later."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q11","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, the inventors assigned the patent to the University of Toronto for all of the following reasons EXCEPT to","choices":["keep control over the drug in the university's hands","license the companies that manufactured insulin","require that every batch meet a standard of potency and purity","provide the university with a steady source of income","prevent any one company from cornering the drug"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"The purpose was not income but control","choice":0},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"the university could license manufacturers","choice":1},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"require that every batch meet a standard of potency and purity","choice":2},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"keep any one company from cornering a drug","choice":4},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"The purpose was not income but control","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the purpose was not income but control, then lists licensing manufacturers, batch standards of potency and purity, and keeping any one company from cornering the drug. Income is the one the passage rules out.","distractorNotes":["Control is named outright as the purpose in paragraph 9.","Licensing manufacturers is the first item in the list.","The potency and purity standard is the second item.",null,"Keeping any one company from cornering the drug is the third item."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q12","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 9, the word \"cornering\" most nearly means","choices":["trapping","monopolizing","turning","cheapening","discontinuing"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"keep any one company from cornering a drug that patients would need every day for life"}],"why":"The sentence is about keeping any one company from controlling a drug that patients need every day. To corner a market is to monopolize it, which fits; trapping is the word's everyday sense and does not.","distractorNotes":["Trapping is the everyday meaning of corner, but a company cannot trap a drug.",null,"Turning a corner is a different idiom and makes no sense here.","Cheapening is the opposite worry; the fear was control of supply, not low prices.","Discontinuing is not suggested; the concern is one company holding the supply."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q13","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, August Krogh","choices":["nominated Banting and Macleod after visiting Toronto","was the first Canadian to win a Nobel Prize","shared his prize money with Collip","served as the Nobel committee's Danish chairman in 1923","published the standard historical account of the discovery"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"visited Toronto in late 1922, carried the method home, and nominated Banting and Macleod"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says Krogh, a Danish physiologist and laureate, visited Toronto in late 1922, carried the method home, and nominated Banting and Macleod.","distractorNotes":[null,"Banting was the first Canadian laureate; Krogh was Danish.","Macleod shared his money with Collip, not Krogh.","The passage calls him a laureate who nominated the pair, not a committee chairman.","The standard account is Michael Bliss's 1982 study, in paragraph 11."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the Nobel committee's decision to honor Banting and Macleod is best described as","choices":["openly contemptuous of its unfairness","wholehearted agreement with its choice","measured, blaming the instrument rather than the committee","indifferent to who received the money","nostalgic for an era when scientific prizes were less bitterly contested"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"That is less a criticism of the Nobel committee than a limit of its instrument"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"Prizes honor names, and history remembers names; the discovery itself was made by a laboratory."}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the problem is less a criticism of the Nobel committee than a limit of its instrument, right after noting that prizes honor names while the discovery was made by a laboratory. That is a measured judgment, not an attack.","distractorNotes":["The author explicitly declines to criticize the committee; contempt is far too strong.","The author says a prize cannot represent a team discovery, so this is not wholehearted agreement.",null,"The whole final paragraph weighs who deserves credit; the author is not indifferent.","Nothing in the passage looks back to an earlier era of prizes."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q15","type":"application","stem":"Which of the following situations best illustrates the author's point in the final paragraph about prizes and shared achievements?","choices":["Two chemists working independently in different countries isolate the same compound and are jointly honored by a scientific society","A runner who wins a marathon gives her medal to the coach who trained her and to the club that sponsored her","A company lists every one of its three hundred employees as co-inventors on a single patent application","A student submits a teammate's results under his own name and is later exposed by the laboratory director","An award names two project leaders, though the result depended equally on an unnamed tester and designer"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"Prizes honor names, and history remembers names; the discovery itself was made by a laboratory."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"a shared achievement cannot be divided"}],"why":"The final paragraph says prizes honor names while the discovery was made by a laboratory. An award that names two leaders and leaves out people the result depended on is the same mismatch in a new setting.","distractorNotes":["Two independent discoverers are not a team, and here the honor names both of them.","Voluntarily passing a medal along is a personal gesture, not a limit of the award itself.","Naming everyone is the opposite of the problem; the author's point is that prizes leave essential contributors out.","Taking credit for another's work is misconduct, which the passage never discusses.",null]},{"id":"rc-o03-q16","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, compared with Banting and Best, earlier investigators such as Scott and Kleiner were","choices":["less honest in reporting their results","more careful in method but less successful in the end","more interested in naming the hormone than in extracting it","equally successful but denied credit by the Nobel committee","hampered mainly by the lack of a surgical assistant"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"Yet the error-prone summer led somewhere, and the careful investigators before it had not."},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"each stopped short of treating a patient"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 contrasts the error-prone summer, which led somewhere, with the careful investigators before it, who had not gotten there; paragraph 3 says Scott and Kleiner each stopped short of treating a patient.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in the passage questions anyone's honesty.",null,"The naming was done by de Meyer and Sharpey-Schafer, not by Scott or Kleiner.","They stopped short of treating a patient, so they were not equally successful, and the committee is never said to have denied them.","The passage gives no reason tied to assistants; Best's role was blood sugar measurement, not surgery."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Banting deserved the Nobel Prize alone, and Macleod's share of it was an injustice","Collip's purification was the real discovery of insulin, and the quarrel obscured it","The discovery of insulin was delayed for decades by investigators who lacked the courage to test their extracts on patients","Insulin was a team achievement, and the quarrel over credit shows how poorly prizes represent such work","The University of Toronto's handling of the insulin patent set the standard for how universities should manage medical discoveries"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"insulin was the product of a team, and the instruments we use to assign credit were never built to describe one"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"the discovery itself was made by a laboratory"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"a shared achievement cannot be divided"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 states the thesis outright, that insulin was the product of a team and that our instruments for assigning credit were never built to describe one, and paragraph 11 returns to it: the discovery was made by a laboratory, and a shared achievement cannot be divided.","distractorNotes":["The author sides with no single claimant and calls the quarrel a contest without a rightful winner.","Collip's purification is one of four contributions the passage weighs, not the whole discovery.","The passage blames impurity and side effects for the delay, not a lack of courage, and Zuelzer did treat patients.",null,"The patent is one paragraph and is praised, but it is not what the passage is about."]}]},"rc-o04":{"id":"rc-o04","title":"The Hand the Body Wants","topic":"handedness (chirality) in drug molecules","field":"chemistry","source":"original","words":1318,"paragraphs":[["Your two hands look nearly identical, yet no amount of turning will make a left hand fit a right glove.","Molecules can have the same property: one that cannot be superimposed on its own mirror image is called chiral, from the Greek word for hand, a term the physicist Lord Kelvin introduced in the 1890s.","In medicine, handedness turned out to be a matter of life and death, and later, a matter of marketing."],["The story begins in Paris in 1848 with Louis Pasteur, then in his mid-twenties.","Chemists already knew that tartaric acid, obtained from the crust inside wine casks, rotated plane-polarized light, light filtered so that its waves vibrate in a single plane.","A second form, chemically identical in every test, did not rotate the light at all, and no one could say why.","Pasteur crystallized a sodium ammonium salt of the inactive acid and, examining the crystals under a magnifying lens, noticed that they came in two shapes that were mirror images of each other.","With tweezers he sorted them into two piles, dissolved each separately, and found that one solution rotated light to the right and the other to the left by the same amount.","The inactive acid was not inactive at all; it was an equal mixture of two opposite forms whose effects cancelled."],["Such a fifty-fifty mixture is now called racemic.","The explanation came in 1874, when Jacobus van 't Hoff and Joseph Le Bel independently proposed that the four bonds of a carbon atom point toward the corners of a tetrahedron.","If the four attached groups are all different, they can be arranged in two ways that are mirror images and cannot be interconverted without breaking a bond.","Such a carbon is called a stereocenter, and the two mirror-image molecules are called enantiomers."],["Enantiomers are strange twins.","They share the same melting point, boiling point, solubility, and density, and ordinary laboratory methods cannot tell them apart.","They differ in exactly two circumstances: in the direction they rotate polarized light, and in how they interact with other chiral things.","The nose makes the point vividly: one enantiomer of carvone smells of spearmint while its mirror image smells of caraway."],["This matters in medicine because the body is itself overwhelmingly one-handed.","Proteins are built from amino acids of a single handedness, so every enzyme and every receptor is a chiral pocket, as particular about shape as a glove.","In 1933 the researchers Easson and Stedman offered a model still taught today: if a drug must touch its receptor at three points, only one enantiomer can line up all three at once, while its mirror image can manage two at best.","Pharmacologists call the better-fitting enantiomer the eutomer and its partner the distomer.","A distomer may do nothing, may do something milder, may do something different, or, in the worst case, may do harm."],["The worst case has a name.","Thalidomide went on sale in West Germany in 1957 as a sedative and was soon taken by pregnant women for morning sickness.","By 1961 physicians had linked it to a wave of severe birth defects, often malformed or missing limbs, and it was withdrawn late that year.","Thousands of children were affected worldwide, by common estimates near ten thousand.","The United States was largely spared because Frances Kelsey, a reviewer at the Food and Drug Administration, refused to approve the drug for lack of adequate safety data; a 1962 law then strengthened the agency's demands for evidence."],["Thalidomide has one stereocenter, and later animal studies indicated that one enantiomer carries the sedative effect while the other causes the birth defects.","It is tempting to conclude that selling only the safe hand would have averted the tragedy, but that conclusion is wrong.","The hydrogen on thalidomide's stereocenter is easily removed and replaced under the mild conditions of the bloodstream, so either pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both within hours of being swallowed.","A single-enantiomer thalidomide would not have been a safe drug, because the body would have manufactured the harmful form on its own.","The drug has since returned under strict controls, approved by the FDA in 1998 for a painful complication of leprosy and later for a cancer of the bone marrow."],["Ibuprofen tells a gentler version of the same story.","Developed at the Boots company in Britain in the 1960s, it has mainly been sold as a racemate.","Only the S enantiomer blocks the enzyme that produces the signaling molecules behind pain and inflammation; the R enantiomer is nearly inert at that target.","Instead the body attaches the R form to a carrier molecule, inverts its stereocenter, and releases it as the active S form, a conversion that runs in only one direction and captures a large share of the R form, by many estimates more than half.","The supposedly inactive half of the tablet is in effect a slow-release supply of the active one.","A pure S version is sold in some countries, but the racemate remains the standard."],["In 1992 the FDA issued a policy statement encouraging developers to study each enantiomer of a new chiral drug separately, and single enantiomers became the norm for new chiral medicines.","The more interesting development was commercial.","A company whose racemic drug was nearing the end of its patent could isolate the eutomer and launch it as a new product under a new patent, a maneuver the industry calls a chiral switch.","The most famous example is esomeprazole, the S enantiomer of the stomach-acid drug omeprazole, approved in the United States in 2001 as the older drug's patent protection was running out.","Advertised directly to consumers as the purple pill, it went on to sell billions of dollars' worth a year."],["Here is where a careful reader should slow down.","Omeprazole is a prodrug: it does nothing until it reaches the acid-secreting cells of the stomach lining, where acid converts it into an active form that has no stereocenter at all.","At the site of action, in other words, the two enantiomers become the same molecule.","The difference lies in the liver, which breaks down the S enantiomer more slowly, so a given dose of esomeprazole delivers somewhat more drug to the stomach than the same dose of omeprazole.","That is a real difference in how the body handles the drug, but several widely cited trials compared 40 milligrams of the new product with 20 milligrams of the old one, and critics argued that much of the advantage could be had by simply raising the omeprazole dose.","I find it hard to read the esomeprazole episode as anything but a commercial achievement first and a scientific one a distant second."],["It would be unfair to dismiss every switch on that basis.","Bupivacaine, a local anesthetic, is dangerous to the heart in overdose, and the danger is greater for its R enantiomer.","The S enantiomer, sold alone as levobupivacaine, provides comparable numbing with a wider margin of safety, which is exactly the sort of benefit the three-point model would predict.","A chiral switch is neither a scientific advance nor a marketing trick by definition; each one must be judged by whether patients do measurably better on the single enantiomer than on the mixture, at matched doses."],["None of this should obscure how remarkable the underlying chemistry is.","Pasteur sorted crystals with tweezers because no other method existed.","Today chemists can build one enantiomer and not the other on an industrial scale using catalysts that are themselves chiral, work recognized by the 2001 Nobel Prize in Chemistry awarded to William Knowles, Ryoji Noyori, and Barry Sharpless.","For any new medicine it is now possible to ask which hand the body wants and to supply that hand alone.","Whether supplying it alone is worth paying for is a different question, and it should be answered by evidence rather than by the color of the pill."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o04-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, what did Pasteur notice about the crystals of the sodium ammonium salt?","choices":["They rotated light more strongly than tartaric acid did.","They were chemically different from ordinary tartaric acid.","They formed only when the solution was kept warm.","They could not be dissolved once they had been sorted.","They came in two mirror-image shapes."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"noticed that they came in two shapes that were mirror images of each other"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says Pasteur, looking through a magnifying lens, noticed that the crystals came in two shapes that were mirror images of each other. That observation is the whole starting point of the passage.","distractorNotes":["The crystals themselves were from the inactive acid; the passage says the solutions rotated light equally in opposite directions, not more strongly than tartaric acid.","Paragraph 2 says the inactive acid was chemically identical to tartaric acid in every test, the reverse of this choice.","The passage never mentions temperature or warming; this is outside the passage.","Paragraph 2 says he dissolved each pile after sorting, so this contradicts the text.",null]},{"id":"rc-o04-q02","type":"except","stem":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as part of Pasteur's work in 1848?","choices":["heating the crystals to separate the two forms","crystallizing a sodium ammonium salt of the inactive acid","sorting the crystals by hand with tweezers","measuring the rotation produced by each solution","examining the crystals under a magnifying lens"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"crystallized a sodium ammonium salt of the inactive acid","choice":1},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"With tweezers he sorted them into two piles","choice":2},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"found that one solution rotated light to the right and the other to the left","choice":3},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"examining the crystals under a magnifying lens","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 2 confirms the salt, the lens, the tweezers, and the measured rotations, but it never mentions heating. The separation was done by eye and by hand, so heating is the one you cannot confirm.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 2 says Pasteur crystallized a sodium ammonium salt of the inactive acid.","Paragraph 2 says he sorted the crystals into two piles with tweezers.","Paragraph 2 says he found one solution rotated light to the right and the other to the left.","Paragraph 2 says he examined the crystals under a magnifying lens."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, two enantiomers of the same compound differ in","choices":["their melting points and boiling points","the direction they rotate polarized light","the number of stereocenters they each contain","their solubility and density in water","the strength of their bonds to carbon"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"in the direction they rotate polarized light"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 lists exactly two circumstances in which enantiomers differ: the direction they rotate polarized light and how they interact with other chiral things. Everything else on this list is something they share.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 says enantiomers share the same melting point and boiling point.",null,"Enantiomers are mirror images of the same molecule; the passage never says they differ in number of stereocenters.","Paragraph 4 says they share the same solubility and density.","Bond strength is never discussed; this is outside the passage."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q04","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \"particular\" most nearly means","choices":["specific","individual","unusual","detailed","selective"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"as particular about shape as a glove"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 calls a receptor a chiral pocket that is as particular about shape as a glove. A glove is choosy about which hand it accepts, so here \"particular\" means selective or fussy, not specific or individual.","distractorNotes":["\"Specific\" is the common meaning of the word, but a glove is not specific about shape; it is choosy about shape.","\"Individual\" is another everyday sense of \"particular\" that does not fit a glove being picky.","Nothing in the sentence suggests the receptor is unusual; the comparison is about pickiness.","\"Detailed\" does not describe a glove's relationship to a hand.",null]},{"id":"rc-o04-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the three-point attachment model explains why","choices":["enzymes are built from amino acids of a single handedness","a drug's two enantiomers share the same melting point","receptors change shape when a drug binds to them","only one enantiomer can fully fit a receptor","the distomer of every drug is harmful to the body"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"only one enantiomer can line up all three at once, while its mirror image can manage two at best"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 describes the model: if a drug must touch its receptor at three points, only one enantiomer can line up all three, while the mirror image manages two at best. The model is about fit, not about what the distomer does.","distractorNotes":["That proteins are built from one-handed amino acids is stated earlier in the paragraph as a fact, not something the model explains.","Shared melting point is a physical property from paragraph 4, unrelated to the model.","The passage never says receptors change shape; this is outside the passage.",null,"Paragraph 5 says a distomer may do nothing, something milder, something different, or harm; \"every\" is far too strong."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q06","type":"function","stem":"The author ends paragraph 5 with the words \"may do harm\" primarily to","choices":["argue that racemic mixtures should never be approved for sale","introduce the thalidomide case that follows","explain why the body's proteins are one-handed","contrast ibuprofen's distomer with esomeprazole's","summarize the model proposed by Easson and Stedman"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"in the worst case, may do harm"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"The worst case has a name."}],"why":"The last line of paragraph 5 names the worst case a distomer can be, and paragraph 6 opens with \"The worst case has a name\" and tells the thalidomide story. The phrase is a bridge into that example.","distractorNotes":["The author later praises racemic ibuprofen and asks for case-by-case judgment, so a blanket ban is far too strong.",null,"One-handed proteins are explained at the start of paragraph 5, not by its closing words.","Ibuprofen and esomeprazole come in paragraphs 8 to 10, and the passage never contrasts their distomers.","The model is summarized in the third sentence; the closing sentence lists what a distomer can do, which goes beyond the model."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q07","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the United States was largely spared the thalidomide disaster because","choices":["the drug was never manufactured or sold anywhere outside West Germany","American physicians connected it to birth defects before it went on sale","a 1962 law banned the use of sedatives during pregnancy","the drug's single-enantiomer form was the only version sold there","an FDA reviewer withheld approval over inadequate safety data"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"refused to approve the drug for lack of adequate safety data"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says Frances Kelsey, a reviewer at the FDA, refused to approve the drug for lack of adequate safety data. The 1962 law came afterward and strengthened the agency's demands; it was not the reason.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 6 says thousands of children were affected worldwide, so the drug was clearly sold beyond West Germany.","The passage credits an FDA reviewer, not American physicians, and says the drug was never approved there.","The 1962 law strengthened demands for evidence; the passage never says it banned sedatives in pregnancy, and it came after the refusal.","Paragraph 7 says a single-enantiomer version would not have been safe, and the passage never says one was sold anywhere.",null]},{"id":"rc-o04-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if thalidomide had been sold only as its sedative enantiomer,","choices":["the harmful form would still have formed inside patients","the sedative effect would have been lost entirely","the FDA would have approved it for sale in 1957","the birth defects would have been prevented","the drug could not have returned for leprosy complications in 1998"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"either pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both within hours of being swallowed"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"the body would have manufactured the harmful form on its own"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says either pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both within hours, and that the body would have manufactured the harmful form on its own. So the sedative-only version would have become the dangerous mixture anyway.","distractorNotes":[null,"Nothing suggests the sedative effect disappears; the problem is that the harmful form reappears alongside it.","Kelsey's refusal was about missing safety data; the passage never ties FDA approval to which enantiomer was sold.","Paragraph 7 calls exactly this conclusion wrong.","The 1998 return is about strict controls for a leprosy complication; the passage never links it to the enantiomer sold."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q09","type":"application","stem":"Which of the following new situations best illustrates the problem described in paragraph 7?","choices":["A heartburn drug whose two enantiomers become the same active molecule once acid converts them","A painkiller whose inactive enantiomer is steadily converted by the body into the active enantiomer over several hours","An antibiotic sold as one pure enantiomer that becomes a fifty-fifty mixture in patients' blood","A crystalline drug whose harmful enantiomer can be removed by sorting its crystals by hand","A fragrance compound whose two enantiomers produce entirely different smells"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"either pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both within hours of being swallowed"}],"why":"The problem in paragraph 7 is interconversion: a pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both inside the body, so selling the safe hand alone does not help. An antibiotic that becomes a fifty-fifty mixture in the blood is the same situation with a new drug.","distractorNotes":["Two enantiomers becoming one achiral active form is the omeprazole case from paragraph 10, not interconversion between enantiomers.","One-way conversion of the inactive form into the active one is the ibuprofen case from paragraph 8, which the passage presents as helpful rather than a problem.",null,"Sorting crystals by hand is Pasteur's method from paragraph 2 and has nothing to do with what happens inside the body.","Different smells are the carvone example from paragraph 4, which illustrates that enantiomers interact differently with chiral things."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q10","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are stated in the passage about ibuprofen EXCEPT:","choices":["It was developed at the Boots company in Britain.","Only its S enantiomer blocks the target enzyme.","The body converts part of the R form into the S form.","The conversion of R to S runs in both directions.","A pure S version is sold in some countries."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"Developed at the Boots company in Britain in the 1960s","choice":0},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"Only the S enantiomer blocks the enzyme","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"releases it as the active S form","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"A pure S version is sold in some countries","choice":4},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"a conversion that runs in only one direction"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 confirms Boots, the S enantiomer as the active one, the R-to-S conversion, and the pure S product, but it says the conversion runs in only one direction. A two-way conversion is the opposite of what the passage states.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 8 says ibuprofen was developed at the Boots company in Britain in the 1960s.","Paragraph 8 says only the S enantiomer blocks the enzyme behind pain and inflammation.","Paragraph 8 says the body inverts the R form and releases it as the active S form.",null,"Paragraph 8 says a pure S version is sold in some countries."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q11","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the FDA's 1992 policy statement","choices":["required that all new drugs be sold as single enantiomers","banned the sale of racemic mixtures in the United States","approved esomeprazole for sale as a new product","introduced the term chiral switch into the industry","encouraged separate study of each enantiomer"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"encouraging developers to study each enantiomer of a new chiral drug separately"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the 1992 statement encouraged developers to study each enantiomer of a new chiral drug separately. Encouraging is softer than requiring, and the passage says nothing about bans.","distractorNotes":["\"Required\" and \"all\" are too strong; the passage says the statement encouraged study of each enantiomer.","Racemic ibuprofen remains the standard according to paragraph 8, so no ban occurred.","Esomeprazole was approved in 2001, nine years later, and the policy statement is not credited with that approval.","The passage says the industry calls the maneuver a chiral switch; it never attributes the term to the FDA.",null]},{"id":"rc-o04-q12","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if the liver broke down both enantiomers of omeprazole at the same rate,","choices":["omeprazole would no longer act as a prodrug","esomeprazole would offer little or no advantage","esomeprazole would deliver more drug to the stomach than before","the active form of the drug would regain its stereocenter","the 40 milligram trials would have shown a larger effect"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"the two enantiomers become the same molecule"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"The difference lies in the liver, which breaks down the S enantiomer more slowly"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the two enantiomers become the same molecule at the site of action and that the difference lies in the liver, which breaks down the S enantiomer more slowly. Take away the liver difference and nothing is left to separate the two drugs.","distractorNotes":["Omeprazole is a prodrug because stomach acid activates it, according to paragraph 10; liver rates have nothing to do with that.",null,"Reversed: esomeprazole delivers more drug only because it is broken down more slowly, so equal breakdown would remove that extra delivery.","The active form lacks a stereocenter because of what acid does to it, not because of the liver.","Outside the passage, and backwards: with no liver difference, the trials would have shown less advantage, not more."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q13","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, critics of esomeprazole argued that","choices":["a higher dose of omeprazole could match much of its advantage","it was broken down by the liver faster than omeprazole","it caused more side effects than the older drug","its patent protection had already expired before the drug was approved","it failed to reach the acid-secreting cells of the stomach"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"critics argued that much of the advantage could be had by simply raising the omeprazole dose"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says widely cited trials compared 40 milligrams of the new drug with 20 milligrams of the old one, and critics argued that much of the advantage could be had by simply raising the omeprazole dose.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: paragraph 10 says the liver breaks down the S enantiomer more slowly, not faster.","Side effects are never mentioned; this is outside the passage.","It was omeprazole's patent that was running out, according to paragraph 9, not esomeprazole's.","Paragraph 10 says esomeprazole delivers somewhat more drug to the stomach, not less."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the esomeprazole chiral switch is best described as","choices":["enthusiastic approval","neutral indifference","measured skepticism","outright hostility","puzzled confusion"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"a commercial achievement first and a scientific one a distant second"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"That is a real difference in how the body handles the drug, but"}],"why":"The author grants that there is a real difference in how the body handles the drug, then calls the episode a commercial achievement first and a scientific one a distant second. Conceding the point and still doubting the value is measured skepticism.","distractorNotes":["The author doubts the scientific value of the switch; nothing in paragraph 10 is enthusiastic.","An author who tells the reader to slow down and then renders a judgment is not indifferent.",null,"Hostility is too strong; the author concedes a real difference and says in paragraph 11 that not every switch should be dismissed.","The author is not confused; the mechanism is laid out clearly and a verdict is given."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q15","type":"function","stem":"The author discusses bupivacaine in paragraph 11 primarily to","choices":["illustrate how the liver breaks down enantiomers at different rates","argue that local anesthetics should always be sold as racemates","prove that the three-point attachment model is incorrect","show that some chiral switches deliver a real benefit","compare the sales of levobupivacaine with those of esomeprazole"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"It would be unfair to dismiss every switch on that basis."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"provides comparable numbing with a wider margin of safety"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 opens by saying it would be unfair to dismiss every switch, then offers levobupivacaine as a single enantiomer with a wider margin of safety. The example exists to balance the skepticism of paragraph 10.","distractorNotes":["Liver metabolism is the omeprazole story in paragraph 10; paragraph 11 is about heart toxicity.","Reversed: the passage presents the single enantiomer, not the racemate, as the safer anesthetic.","Reversed: the passage says the benefit is exactly what the three-point model would predict.",null,"Sales figures are given only for esomeprazole; the passage never compares the two products commercially."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q16","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from the passage that before the development of chiral catalysts,","choices":["no method existed that could distinguish one enantiomer from the other","producing a single enantiomer at industrial scale was difficult","Pasteur's method of sorting crystals was used in factories","drugs were never sold as single enantiomers","racemic mixtures were considered unsafe by regulators"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"Pasteur sorted crystals with tweezers because no other method existed."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"Today chemists can build one enantiomer and not the other on an industrial scale using catalysts that are themselves chiral"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 contrasts Pasteur's tweezers, used because no other method existed, with today's chiral catalysts that build one enantiomer on an industrial scale. The natural inference is that industrial-scale production of one hand was hard before those catalysts.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: paragraph 2 shows that polarized light could tell the two apart as early as 1848.",null,"The passage never says Pasteur's hand-sorting was used in factories; this is outside the passage.","Too strong: the passage never says single-enantiomer drugs were impossible before the catalysts, only that producing them at scale is now possible.","Paragraph 9 says the 1992 statement encouraged separate study; nothing says regulators considered racemates unsafe."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Pasteur's discovery of mirror-image crystals was the most important event in the history of chemistry.","Chiral switches are marketing maneuvers that regulators should prohibit.","Handedness shapes how drugs act, but each single-enantiomer product must be judged on evidence.","Thalidomide and ibuprofen show that racemic drugs are always more dangerous than single enantiomers.","Enzymes and receptors are chiral because they are built from amino acids of a single handedness."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"a matter of life and death, and later, a matter of marketing"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"each one must be judged by whether patients do measurably better on the single enantiomer than on the mixture"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"it should be answered by evidence rather than by the color of the pill"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 frames handedness as a matter of life and death and later of marketing; paragraph 11 says each switch must be judged by whether patients do better; paragraph 12 closes by asking for evidence rather than the color of the pill. The passage is about handedness mattering and the value being an evidence question.","distractorNotes":["Pasteur is the opening story, not the thesis, and the passage never ranks his discovery against all of chemistry.","Too strong: paragraph 11 says a switch is neither an advance nor a trick by definition, and the author praises levobupivacaine.",null,"Reversed and too strong: racemic ibuprofen is presented as perfectly good, and \"always\" contradicts paragraph 11.","That is a supporting fact from paragraph 5, not the main idea of the whole passage."]}]},"rc-o05":{"id":"rc-o05","title":"Eaters of Bacteria","topic":"bacteriophage therapy, its eclipse and its return","field":"microbiology","source":"original","words":1367,"paragraphs":[["In 1915, the English bacteriologist Frederick Twort noticed something odd on his culture plates.","Some bacterial colonies had turned glassy, as if dissolving, and material from a dissolved colony passed the effect to a fresh one.","Twort reported this in The Lancet, suggested a virus might be responsible, and then, short of money and distracted by war, set the question aside.","Two years later, at the Pasteur Institute in Paris, Felix d'Herelle described an invisible agent that destroyed the bacteria of dysentery and named it: bacteriophage, the eater of bacteria.","A long quarrel over credit followed, but the discovery itself was secure."],["A bacteriophage, or phage for short, is a virus that infects only bacteria.","They are thought to be the most abundant biological entities on Earth.","The classic phage looks like a tiny lunar lander: a protein head holding its genes, a tail, and gripping fibers.","Infection follows a sequence that microbiologists call the lytic cycle.","The phage attaches to a specific receptor molecule on the bacterial surface, injects its genome into the cell, and redirects the cell's own machinery to copy that genome and build new phage parts.","Within as little as half an hour, new viruses fill the cell, and phage enzymes break open the cell wall from the inside.","The cell bursts, releasing tens to hundreds of phages, each ready to begin again; a dense culture can clear within hours."],["Not every phage kills on arrival.","Some, called temperate phages, can instead insert their genome into the bacterial chromosome and lie quiet, copied with the host at each division, a state called lysogeny.","Such a dormant phage, a prophage, may wake after many generations, often when its host is damaged, and turn lytic.","Lysogeny has a darker side for medicine.","A prophage can carry genes that change its host's behavior, and the toxins behind cholera and diphtheria are encoded on genes acquired this way.","A temperate phage can also shuttle antibiotic resistance genes from one bacterium to another.","So a phage chosen for therapy should be strictly lytic: it must kill reliably, and it must not be able to settle in and hand its host new tricks."],["Phages are also choosy, and their choosiness cuts both ways.","Because a phage must recognize a particular receptor, it usually infects only certain strains within a single species, sometimes only a handful.","For the patient, this is a gift: a phage aimed at a wound infection will leave the trillions of helpful bacteria in the gut untouched, while a broad-spectrum antibiotic scorches friend and foe alike.","For the physician, it is a burden.","The infecting strain must be isolated, grown, and tested against a library of phages, which can take days a very sick patient may not have.","An antibiotic can be pulled from a shelf; a phage, in most cases, must be matched."],["The decades after d'Herelle's report were giddy ones.","He was treating patients by 1919, companies in Europe and the United States sold phage preparations for everything from dysentery to boils, and Sinclair Lewis built his 1925 novel Arrowsmith around a young doctor testing phages against plague.","The evidence, however, did not keep pace.","Many early trials had no control groups, some commercial products were found to contain little or no active phage, and the lysogeny problem was not yet understood.","In 1934, a long review in the Journal of the American Medical Association found most claims unsupported, and the field's reputation in the West never recovered.","Then came penicillin.","By the late 1940s, antibiotics were cheap, stable, broad, and backed by controlled trials, and most Western physicians saw no reason to look back."],["The story did not end everywhere.","In 1923, the Georgian microbiologist George Eliava, who had worked with d'Herelle in Paris, founded an institute in Tbilisi that became a center of phage work, and d'Herelle himself came to work there in the 1930s.","Eliava was executed in Stalin's purges in 1937, but the institute that now bears his name survived, and phage therapy became routine in Soviet medicine.","Short of antibiotics, Soviet physicians prescribed phages for wound and intestinal infections for decades, and the institute built one of the world's largest phage collections.","Most of it appeared in Russian journals Western scientists did not read, and much of it lacked the controlled design that Western medicine had come to demand, so it was easy to ignore."],["There was a scientific reason for caution too, the same one now troubling antibiotics: evolution.","Bacteria exposed to a phage soon produce mutants that the phage cannot infect, most often by altering or shedding the surface receptor the phage uses to attach.","Bacteria also carry internal defenses that chop up foreign DNA; CRISPR, now a gene-editing tool, began as a bacterial memory of past phage attacks.","Phage therapists answer with cocktails: mixtures of several phages that attack the same bacterium through different receptors, so that a mutant which escapes one phage is still caught by another.","There is a further, more hopeful twist.","Resisting a phage often costs the bacterium something: the discarded receptor may have been needed to cause disease or to pump out antibiotics, so a resistant mutant can end up newly vulnerable to drugs that had failed."],["That twist played out in the case that revived Western interest.","In late 2015, Tom Patterson, a professor at the University of California, San Diego, fell ill in Egypt with an infection of Acinetobacter baumannii, a bacterium notorious for resisting nearly every antibiotic.","By early 2016 he was in a coma and out of antibiotics, and his wife, the epidemiologist Steffanie Strathdee, began calling laboratories about phage therapy.","Within weeks, researchers at Texas A&M University and at a United States Navy laboratory had each screened their collections against his isolate and assembled cocktails that killed it.","With emergency permission from the Food and Drug Administration, his physicians infused phages into his abscess and then into his bloodstream in March 2016.","Patterson woke within days, and over the following months, with phages and antibiotics given together, the infection cleared.","Tellingly, the bacterium did evolve resistance to the phages, but the resistant form appeared more sensitive to an antibiotic in his regimen."],["The case was published in 2017, and the effect was immediate: UC San Diego opened a phage therapy center in 2018, requests poured into phage laboratories worldwide, and compassionate-use treatments have since been reported for lung infections, infected implants, and other infections antibiotics could no longer touch.","Yet an honest reader should notice what these reports are: single cases, tried because nothing else remained, and often published because they succeeded.","The patients who did not improve rarely make the news, and a therapy cannot be judged on its best days."],["The deepest obstacle may be regulatory, and it follows from the biology.","Drug approval normally rests on a fixed, well-characterized product made the same way every time.","A phage cocktail is none of those things: it is alive, it reproduces inside the patient, it may need reformulating as the bacterium changes, and the ideal mixture for one patient is wrong for the next.","United States regulators have so far granted case-by-case emergency permission rather than approving any phage as a medicine, while Belgium lets pharmacists prepare phages for individual patients under a defined quality standard.","The first phage products accepted by American regulators were not drugs at all but food sprays, the earliest approved in 2006 against Listeria on ready-to-eat meats."],["Whether phage therapy becomes ordinary medicine depends on work far less dramatic than a bedside rescue.","It will require randomized trials, of which few have been run, with mixed results; phage banks that match an isolate in hours, not days; and manufacturing standards for a product that evolves.","The need is not in doubt: antibiotic-resistant infections now cause more than a million deaths a year worldwide, and few new antibiotics are coming.","A century after Twort watched his colonies dissolve, the eaters of bacteria are back on the table.","They are not a miracle, and anyone who promises one has not read the history, but for the first time since the 1930s they have a fair chance to prove what they can do."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o05-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the agent d'Herelle described in 1917 destroyed the bacteria responsible for","choices":["dysentery","cholera","plague","boils","diphtheria"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"destroyed the bacteria of dysentery"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says d'Herelle described an invisible agent that destroyed the bacteria of dysentery. The other diseases do appear in the passage, but each is attached to a different idea.","distractorNotes":[null,"Cholera appears in paragraph 3 as a toxin carried on phage genes, not as d'Herelle's target.","Plague is the disease in Sinclair Lewis's novel in paragraph 5.","Boils are among the conditions commercial phage products were sold for in paragraph 5.","Diphtheria is paired with cholera in paragraph 3 as a phage-encoded toxin."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q02","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 1, the word 'secure' most nearly means","choices":["kept under guard","safe from theft","tightly fastened","emotionally confident","beyond dispute"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"but the discovery itself was secure"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 sets the quarrel over credit against the discovery itself, which was 'secure': the finding stood firm no matter who got the credit. The everyday sense of safety or locks does not fit a discovery.","distractorNotes":["Being guarded is the physical sense again; nothing in the sentence is being protected.","Safety from theft is the everyday sense of 'secure' and cannot describe a scientific finding.","Fastening is the physical sense of the word, which does not apply to a discovery.","Confidence describes a person, and the sentence is about the discovery, not the people.",null]},{"id":"rc-o05-q03","type":"except","stem":"Which of the following is NOT described in paragraph 2 as part of the lytic cycle?","choices":["The phage injects its genome into the cell.","The phage genome is inserted into the bacterial chromosome.","Phage enzymes break open the cell wall from the inside.","The cell's own machinery is redirected to build new phage parts.","The phage attaches to a receptor on the bacterial surface."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"attaches to a specific receptor molecule on the bacterial surface","choice":4},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"injects its genome into the cell","choice":0},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"redirects the cell's own machinery to copy that genome and build new phage parts","choice":3},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"phage enzymes break open the cell wall from the inside","choice":2},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"insert their genome into the bacterial chromosome"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 lists attachment, injection, hijacking of the cell's machinery, and bursting out. Inserting the genome into the chromosome is lysogeny, which paragraph 3 presents as the alternative to the lytic cycle.","distractorNotes":["Injection of the genome is stated in the same sentence of paragraph 2.",null,"Enzymes breaking the wall open from the inside is in paragraph 2, sentence 6.","Redirecting the cell's own machinery is stated in paragraph 2, sentence 5.","Attachment is the first step named in paragraph 2, sentence 5."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q04","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a temperate phage would be a poor choice for therapy even if it could kill the target bacterium because it","choices":["cannot recognize receptors on most bacterial strains","reproduces too slowly to clear a dense infection","might give the bacterium harmful new genes","has never been tested in a controlled clinical trial","would be destroyed by the bacterium's CRISPR defenses"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"A prophage can carry genes that change its host's behavior"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":7,"quote":"it must not be able to settle in and hand its host new tricks"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says a prophage can carry toxin genes and shuttle antibiotic resistance between bacteria, then concludes that a therapeutic phage must not 'settle in and hand its host new tricks.' The danger is gene transfer, not killing power.","distractorNotes":["Receptor specificity belongs to paragraph 4 and applies to all phages, not temperate ones in particular.","Speed of reproduction is never raised as a weakness of temperate phages.",null,"Trial history is discussed in paragraphs 5 and 11 and is never tied to temperate phages.","CRISPR appears in paragraph 7 as a general bacterial defense, not a special weakness of temperate phages."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a phage's narrow host range benefits the patient because the phage","choices":["can be pulled from a shelf like an antibiotic","spares the helpful bacteria in the gut","cannot be resisted by the infecting bacterium","kills a wider range of strains than an antibiotic does","can be matched to the infecting strain within hours"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"will leave the trillions of helpful bacteria in the gut untouched"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 calls specificity a gift for the patient because a targeted phage leaves the helpful gut bacteria untouched, while a broad-spectrum antibiotic 'scorches friend and foe alike.'","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 says the opposite: an antibiotic comes off a shelf, while a phage must be matched.",null,"Paragraph 7 says bacteria do evolve resistance to phages.","Reversed: the phage's range is narrower than an antibiotic's, not wider.","Matching takes days in paragraph 4; hours is the goal paragraph 11 hopes for."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q06","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions Sinclair Lewis's novel Arrowsmith in paragraph 5 primarily to","choices":["explain why the 1934 review was so harshly critical","suggest that phage therapy was effective against plague","give an example of a controlled trial of phage therapy","show that novelists understood phage biology better than physicians did","illustrate how far early excitement about phages had spread"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"The decades after d'Herelle's report were giddy ones."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"Sinclair Lewis built his 1925 novel Arrowsmith around a young doctor testing phages against plague"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 opens by calling the decades after d'Herelle 'giddy ones' and then stacks up examples: d'Herelle's patients, commercial products, and a popular novel. The novel is there to show that the excitement reached beyond the laboratory into popular culture.","distractorNotes":["The review is introduced three sentences later and is explained by the weak evidence, not by the novel.","The author never claims phages worked against plague; the novel is fiction.","A novel is not a trial, and the passage says early trials lacked control groups.","The passage makes no comparison between novelists and physicians.",null]},{"id":"rc-o05-q07","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following contributed to the decline of phage therapy in the West EXCEPT","choices":["the arrival of cheap, stable antibiotics","a critical review published in 1934","trials that lacked control groups","evidence that phages carried toxin genes","commercial products that contained little or no active phage"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"Many early trials had no control groups","choice":2},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"some commercial products were found to contain little or no active phage","choice":4},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"In 1934, a long review","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":7,"quote":"antibiotics were cheap, stable","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"the lysogeny problem was not yet understood"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 names uncontrolled trials, inactive products, the 1934 review, and the arrival of antibiotics. It says the lysogeny problem was 'not yet understood' at the time, so toxin genes could not have driven the decline.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 ends with penicillin and the cheap, stable antibiotics of the late 1940s.","The 1934 review is in paragraph 5, sentence 5.","Stated in paragraph 5, sentence 4.",null,"Stated in the same sentence of paragraph 5."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the institute in Tbilisi","choices":["was founded by d'Herelle himself in the 1930s","was the first to test phage therapy in controlled clinical trials","closed after its founder was executed in 1937","was founded by a scientist who had worked with d'Herelle","published most of its findings in Western journals"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"George Eliava, who had worked with d'Herelle in Paris, founded an institute in Tbilisi"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says George Eliava, who had worked with d'Herelle in Paris, founded the Tbilisi institute in 1923. d'Herelle only visited, in the 1930s.","distractorNotes":["d'Herelle visited in the 1930s, but Eliava founded it in 1923.","Paragraph 6 says much of the Soviet work lacked controlled design.","Paragraph 6 says the institute survived Eliava's execution.",null,"Paragraph 6 says most of the work was published in Russian, in journals Western scientists did not read."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Western scientists ignored the Soviet phage work mainly because","choices":["it was out of their sight and lacked rigorous design","the Eliava Institute refused to share its phage collection","it had been shown to be fraudulent after Eliava's death","the 1934 review had already proven that phages could not work","Soviet physicians used phages only for intestinal infections"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"much of it lacked the controlled design that Western medicine had come to demand, so it was easy to ignore"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 closes by saying the work was in Russian, in journals Western scientists did not read, and lacked controlled design, 'so it was easy to ignore.' Language and method, not disproof, kept it out of view.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage says the institute built a large collection, not that it refused to share it.","Nothing in the passage calls the Soviet work fraudulent.","The 1934 review found Western claims unsupported, which is not the same as proving phages cannot work.","Paragraph 6 names wound infections as well as intestinal ones."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, bacteria most often become resistant to a phage by","choices":["producing enzymes that destroy the phage's protein head","altering or losing the receptor the phage attaches to","using CRISPR to chop up the phage's DNA","pumping the phage out of the cell before it can replicate","acquiring resistance genes from a prophage"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"most often by altering or shedding the surface receptor the phage uses to attach"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says resistant mutants arise 'most often by altering or shedding the surface receptor the phage uses to attach.' DNA-chopping defenses and CRISPR are mentioned next as additional defenses, not the most common route.","distractorNotes":["The passage never mentions enzymes attacking the phage head.",null,"CRISPR is named in paragraph 7 as a further defense, not the most common one.","Pumps appear in paragraph 7 as a way bacteria expel antibiotics, not phages.","Prophages shuttle antibiotic resistance genes in paragraph 3, not resistance to phages."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q11","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle behind the phage cocktails described in paragraph 7?","choices":["A farmer rotates crops from year to year so that a pest adapted to one crop starves the next year","A hospital switches to a different antibiotic each month so that no single drug is overused","A laboratory screens fifty candidate phages against a patient's bacterial strain to find the single one that kills it fastest","A patient takes three drugs that hit a virus at different steps, so a mutant escaping one is stopped by the others","A doctor doubles the dose of an antibiotic when the first course fails to clear an infection"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"so that a mutant which escapes one phage is still caught by another"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 describes cocktails as several attackers used at once, through different routes, so that an escape from one is caught by another. Three antiviral drugs given together at different steps follow the same logic. Rotation and bigger doses are different strategies.","distractorNotes":["Crop rotation changes the target over time; a cocktail attacks the same target several ways at once.","Rotating one drug at a time is sequential, not several attackers at once.","Screening for the single best phage is the matching problem of paragraph 4, not a cocktail.",null,"A higher dose of the same agent gives a mutant nothing new to escape from."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the phages used to treat Tom Patterson were supplied by","choices":["his wife's own laboratory at UC San Diego","a university in Texas and a Navy laboratory","a company that sold phage products in the 1930s","the Food and Drug Administration's phage bank","the Eliava Institute in Tbilisi and a Belgian pharmacy"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"researchers at Texas A&M University and at a United States Navy laboratory"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says researchers at Texas A&M University and at a United States Navy laboratory each screened their collections against Patterson's isolate and assembled cocktails that killed it.","distractorNotes":["Strathdee is an epidemiologist who called laboratories; the passage never gives her a phage lab.",null,"Commercial products of the 1930s belong to paragraph 5, not to the 2016 case.","The FDA gave emergency permission; the passage never says it holds phages.","The Eliava Institute and Belgium appear in other paragraphs and had no part in this case."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that Patterson's recovery, by itself, cannot settle whether phage therapy works because","choices":["the Food and Drug Administration refused to approve the treatment","the bacterium never developed resistance to the cocktail","he also received antibiotics and his case was one patient","the phages were given only into the abscess and never reached his blood","his case was published without peer review"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"with phages and antibiotics given together"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"single cases, tried because nothing else remained, and often published because they succeeded"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 notes the phages and antibiotics were given together, and paragraph 9 warns that such reports are single cases, often published because they succeeded. One patient on two treatments cannot show which one worked.","distractorNotes":["The FDA granted emergency permission; it did not refuse.","Paragraph 8 says the bacterium did evolve resistance.",null,"Paragraph 8 says the phages went into his abscess and then into his bloodstream.","The passage says the case was published in 2017 and says nothing about review."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the future of phage therapy is best described as","choices":["cautiously optimistic","uncritically enthusiastic","nostalgic and regretful","openly dismissive","coldly indifferent"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"They are not a miracle, and anyone who promises one has not read the history, but for the first time since the 1930s they have a fair chance to prove what they can do."},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"a therapy cannot be judged on its best days"}],"why":"The last paragraph says phages are 'not a miracle' and warns against anyone who promises one, yet gives them 'a fair chance to prove what they can do.' Paragraph 9 adds that a therapy cannot be judged on its best days. Hope with the brakes on is cautious optimism.","distractorNotes":[null,"The author explicitly warns against promises of a miracle and against judging on best days.","The author looks forward to trials, not backward with regret.","A dismissive author would not say the need is not in doubt or give phages a fair chance.","Indifference does not fit an author who calls the need beyond doubt."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q15","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions food sprays in paragraph 10 primarily to","choices":["praise the Food and Drug Administration for its early approval in 2006","argue that phages are safer in food than in patients","highlight how little ground phages have gained as approved medicines","show that Listeria is the bacterium phages kill best","explain why Belgium adopted a different regulatory approach from the United States"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"rather than approving any phage as a medicine"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"were not drugs at all but food sprays"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 is about the regulatory obstacle. Right after saying no phage has been approved as a medicine in the United States, the author notes that the first accepted phage products were food sprays, 'not drugs at all,' which underlines how far phages still are from approval as medicine.","distractorNotes":["The sentence is pointed, not congratulatory; it stresses what has not happened.","The passage never compares safety in food with safety in patients.",null,"Listeria is simply the target of the spray; no claim about 'best' is made.","Belgium's approach is stated in the previous sentence and is not explained by the food sprays."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q16","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Belgium differs from the United States in that Belgium","choices":["lets pharmacists prepare phages for single patients","grants emergency permission on a case-by-case basis","requires a randomized trial before any phage may be used","has approved a phage cocktail as a licensed medicine","restricts phages to use on ready-to-eat foods"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"Belgium lets pharmacists prepare phages for individual patients under a defined quality standard"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 contrasts the United States, which grants case-by-case emergency permission, with Belgium, which lets pharmacists prepare phages for individual patients under a defined quality standard.","distractorNotes":[null,"Case-by-case emergency permission is the United States approach in the same sentence.","The passage calls for randomized trials in paragraph 11 but attributes no such requirement to Belgium.","Neither country is said to have approved a phage as a licensed medicine.","Food sprays are the United States example in the next sentence."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Regulators should refuse to approve any medicine that is alive and changes inside the patient.","Bacteriophages are the most abundant organisms on Earth and will soon replace antibiotics.","Tom Patterson's recovery proved that phage cocktails can cure any antibiotic-resistant infection.","Phage therapy was abandoned in the West because the Soviet Union controlled the only phage collections.","Phage therapy, eclipsed by antibiotics, is returning with real promise and unresolved problems."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"bacteriophage, the eater of bacteria"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":7,"quote":"most Western physicians saw no reason to look back"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"the case that revived Western interest"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"they have a fair chance to prove what they can do"}],"why":"The passage walks from the discovery (paragraph 1) through the eclipse by antibiotics (paragraph 5), the revival after Patterson's case (paragraph 8), and the trials and regulatory problems still ahead (paragraphs 10 and 11). The answer captures the whole arc without overreaching.","distractorNotes":["The passage describes the regulatory problem but never argues that regulators should refuse.","Abundance is a detail of paragraph 2, and the passage never predicts phages will replace antibiotics.","Too strong: paragraph 9 says single cases cannot judge a therapy, and 'any' infection goes far beyond the passage.","Reverses the reason: the West turned to antibiotics, and the Soviet work was ignored, not withheld.",null]}]},"rc-o06":{"id":"rc-o06","title":"The Stripes That Moved the Continents","topic":"seafloor magnetic stripes and the acceptance of plate tectonics","field":"geology","source":"original","words":1369,"paragraphs":[["Few ideas in the Earth sciences have had a stranger career than continental drift.","In January 1912 the German meteorologist Alfred Wegener proposed that the continents had once been joined in a single landmass, which he later called Pangaea, and had since moved apart.","His book, The Origin of Continents and Oceans, appeared in 1915.","Wegener drew on several independent lines of evidence: the jigsaw fit of the South American and African coastlines, matching rock formations across the Atlantic, identical fossils on continents now an ocean apart, and signs of ancient glaciation in regions that today sit near the equator.","Any one of these could be explained away, but together they deserved a serious hearing."],["They did not get one.","Wegener could describe what had happened but not how, and the mechanism he offered, continents plowing through the ocean floor under tidal forces and a push away from the poles, did not survive scrutiny.","The Cambridge geophysicist Harold Jeffreys showed that these forces were far too weak to move anything.","At a 1926 symposium in New York, American geologists lined up to reject the hypothesis, some in openly mocking terms.","Wegener died on the Greenland ice sheet in 1930, and for three decades drift was treated in most English-speaking departments as a cautionary tale.","In hindsight the reaction looks less like healthy caution than a refusal to take good evidence seriously because it lacked a tidy explanation."],["The evidence that settled the matter came from the ocean floor, which in 1950 was almost entirely unmapped.","At the Lamont Geological Observatory in New York, Marie Tharp turned echo-sounding records into profiles of the Atlantic floor while Bruce Heezen collected the data at sea.","In 1952, plotting six profiles across the North Atlantic, Tharp noticed a deep notch at the crest of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge in every one and argued that it was a continuous rift valley down the center of the ridge.","Heezen at first dismissed the idea, but when earthquake epicenters from the ocean basins were plotted on the same map, they fell along the valley Tharp had drawn.","By 1957 the two had published a physiographic diagram of the North Atlantic floor, and later work traced the ridge and its rift around the globe as a mountain system roughly 65,000 kilometers long.","A rift valley is where crust is being pulled apart, and a rift that circles the planet is hard to reconcile with a static Earth."],["Harry Hess of Princeton supplied the idea that gave the rift its meaning.","By 1960 he had collected several puzzles: the ocean floor carried only a thin blanket of sediment, nothing truly ancient had been recovered from it, and heat flow was unusually high at the ridges.","His answer, circulated in 1960 and published in 1962, was that mantle convection brings hot material up beneath the ridges, where it forms new floor that spreads outward in both directions and is eventually carried back into the mantle at the deep trenches.","Hess called his paper an essay in geopoetry, and Robert Dietz, publishing independently in 1961, gave the process its lasting name: seafloor spreading."],["Spreading was still a hypothesis without a test, and the test came from an unlikely direction: the magnetism frozen into volcanic rock.","Basalt, the rock of the upper ocean crust, contains small grains of the iron oxide magnetite.","Above a threshold known as the Curie temperature, about 580 degrees Celsius for magnetite, the grains cannot hold a magnetic direction.","As a lava cools below that threshold, the grains lock in the direction and polarity of Earth's field at that moment, a property called thermoremanent magnetization.","Every basalt flow is therefore a compass reading taken on the day it solidified and kept ever since."],["Those readings revealed something odd.","In 1906 the French physicist Bernard Brunhes reported lavas in central France magnetized roughly opposite to the present field, and in 1929 Motonori Matuyama showed that reversed lavas in Japan and Manchuria were consistently older than normal ones.","The obvious conclusion, that Earth's field flips its polarity from time to time, was resisted for decades.","In the early 1960s Allan Cox, Richard Doell and Brent Dalrymple of the United States Geological Survey paired magnetic measurements of lava flows with potassium-argon dating of the same flows and built a calendar of reversals.","They named the most recent intervals after pioneers of geomagnetism: the Brunhes normal epoch, in which we live, the Matuyama reversed epoch before it, then the Gauss normal and Gilbert reversed epochs.","The Brunhes-Matuyama boundary is now placed at about 780,000 years ago, and reversals proved to come irregularly, typically a few hundred thousand years apart."],["In 1955 and 1956 a Coast and Geodetic Survey ship towing a magnetometer ran closely spaced lines off Washington, Oregon and California, and the map that Arthur Raff and Ronald Mason published in 1961 showed something nobody expected.","The field strength varied in long parallel bands, alternately slightly stronger and slightly weaker than the regional average, so that the floor looked striped like a zebra.","No one could say what had drawn the stripes."],["The explanation arrived in September 1963 in a short paper in Nature by Fred Vine, a graduate student at Cambridge, and his supervisor Drummond Matthews.","New basalt at a ridge records the field of the day, and spreading carries each strip away from the axis in both directions, so the floor should be a tape recording of the reversal history: alternating bands symmetric about the ridge crest, normal bands adding to the present field and reversed bands subtracting from it.","Lawrence Morley of the Geological Survey of Canada had reached the same conclusion independently earlier that year, but Nature and then an American journal rejected his manuscript, one reviewer remarking that the idea belonged at a cocktail party rather than in print."],["Even then, the reception was cool.","Lamont, which held an enormous archive of marine magnetic data, remained unconvinced.","Two developments in 1966 changed that.","First, Doell and Dalrymple identified a short normal interval inside the Matuyama epoch, the Jaramillo event, and once it was added to the calendar, seafloor profiles that had fit the model only roughly now fit it closely.","Second, in 1965 the research vessel Eltanin had crossed the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge on its nineteenth cruise towing a magnetometer, and when Walter Pitman and James Heirtzler plotted the profile, the anomalies on one side mirrored those on the other for hundreds of kilometers.","Folded about the ridge crest, the two halves of the Eltanin-19 profile matched peak for peak, and their spacing fit the reversal calendar if the floor spread at a few centimeters a year.","Shown at a conference in New York that November, the profile changed the minds of several prominent skeptics in the room."],["Once the stripes were accepted, the rest followed fast.","The distance of a dated reversal from the ridge divided by its age gave a spreading rate, and the rates measured in ocean after ocean were slow, steady and sufficient to carry Wegener's continents.","In 1967 and 1968 Jason Morgan, Dan McKenzie and Robert Parker, and Xavier Le Pichon described Earth's surface as a small number of rigid plates moving over a sphere, and the theory acquired its modern name, plate tectonics.","Drilling by the Glomar Challenger in the South Atlantic in 1968 and 1969 then found sediment on the basalt growing older with distance from the ridge at very nearly the predicted rate."],["The stripes are a direct physical record, written by the planet in its own rocks, of a process argued about for half a century.","The symmetry of the Eltanin-19 profile is, to my mind, among the most beautiful results in the Earth sciences: a single line of data that confirms spreading, reversals and drift at once, and that anyone can check with a ruler.","The rift valley was on Tharp's drafting table in 1952, Hess's essay was circulating in 1960, and the stripes were explained in 1963, yet most geologists did not come around until 1966 or later.","Caution has its place, but when the evidence is this clear, the delay says more about the habits of a profession than about the strength of the case."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o06-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, which of the following was among the lines of evidence Wegener drew on for continental drift?","choices":["The unusually high heat flow measured at the mid-ocean ridges","Signs of ancient glaciation in regions now near the equator","Earthquake epicenters lined up along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge","The thin blanket of sediment covering the ocean floor","Parallel magnetic stripes on the floor of the Pacific Ocean"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"signs of ancient glaciation in regions that today sit near the equator"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 lists Wegener's evidence: the coastline fit, matching rocks, identical fossils, and signs of ancient glaciation in regions that now sit near the equator. The other choices are real details from the passage, but they belong to later discoveries by other people.","distractorNotes":["Heat flow at the ridges is one of Hess's puzzles in paragraph 4, not part of Wegener's case.",null,"The epicenters belong to Tharp and Heezen's rift valley in paragraph 3, decades after Wegener.","Thin sediment is a Hess puzzle from paragraph 4, not evidence Wegener used.","The stripes were found in the 1950s survey of paragraph 7; Wegener never saw them."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q02","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 1, the word 'hearing' most nearly means","choices":["the physical sense by which sound is perceived","a formal proceeding before a judge","a fair chance to be considered","a lecture delivered to an audience","a rumor passed from person to person"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"together they deserved a serious hearing"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the evidence 'deserved a serious hearing,' and paragraph 2 opens with 'They did not get one' and then describes geologists refusing the idea. So a hearing here is a fair chance to be considered, not the sense of hearing.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday meaning of the word, but a body of evidence cannot literally be listened to.","No court or judge appears anywhere in the passage; the hearing is figurative.",null,"Wegener did give lectures, but the sentence is about how his evidence was received, not how it was delivered.","A rumor is unverified talk; the sentence is about evidence deserving serious consideration."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Harold Jeffreys's role in the debate over continental drift was to","choices":["show that the forces Wegener proposed were far too weak","organize the 1926 symposium that rejected the hypothesis","propose that convection in the mantle could carry the continents","argue that the fossil evidence could be explained away","supply the mechanism that Wegener's proposal had lacked"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"showed that these forces were far too weak to move anything"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says Jeffreys 'showed that these forces were far too weak to move anything.' He attacked Wegener's mechanism; he did not provide one of his own.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage names no organizer for the 1926 symposium and never places Jeffreys there.","Mantle convection is Hess's answer in paragraph 4, not anything Jeffreys proposed.","Paragraph 1 says any single line could be explained away, but that is the author's remark, not Jeffreys's argument.","Reversed: Jeffreys knocked down Wegener's mechanism rather than supplying a better one."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q04","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions earthquake epicenters in paragraph 3 primarily to","choices":["explain why Tharp was not allowed to collect data at sea","suggest that the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is volcanically active","show that the ocean floor was almost entirely unmapped in 1950","show what persuaded Heezen that the rift valley was real","demonstrate that the ridge system is 65,000 kilometers long"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"they fell along the valley Tharp had drawn"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says Heezen 'at first dismissed the idea, but when earthquake epicenters from the ocean basins were plotted on the same map, they fell along the valley Tharp had drawn.' The epicenters are there to show what changed his mind.","distractorNotes":["The passage never says why Tharp stayed ashore; it only says Heezen collected the data at sea.","Volcanic activity is not mentioned in paragraph 3; the epicenters tie to the rift, not to volcanoes.","The unmapped floor is the paragraph's opening claim and has nothing to do with the epicenters.",null,"The length of the ridge comes from later work in the next sentence, not from the epicenters."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the mid-ocean ridge and its central rift","choices":["were first noticed during the Eltanin's nineteenth cruise","form a mountain system roughly 65,000 kilometers long","were discovered by Harry Hess using a naval echo sounder","appear only in the six profiles Tharp plotted in 1952","were mapped completely before the Second World War"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"a mountain system roughly 65,000 kilometers long"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says later work 'traced the ridge and its rift around the globe as a mountain system roughly 65,000 kilometers long.'","distractorNotes":["The Eltanin crossed the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge in 1965 (paragraph 9); the rift was recognized in 1952.",null,"Hess supplied the idea that explained the rift (paragraph 4); Tharp and Heezen found it, and no echo sounder is tied to Hess.","Too narrow: the rift showed in all six profiles and was later traced around the globe.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says the floor was almost entirely unmapped in 1950."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q06","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Hess's hypothesis accounted for the thin blanket of sediment on the ocean floor because","choices":["the deep trenches swallow sediment faster than it accumulates","the high heat flow at the ridges bakes the sediment away","the sediment settles mainly on the continents instead","the floor is continually renewed and so is never very old","Dietz had already shown that the sediment was thin in 1961"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"nothing truly ancient had been recovered from it"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"it forms new floor that spreads outward in both directions and is eventually carried back into the mantle at the deep trenches"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 pairs the puzzles (thin sediment, nothing ancient) with Hess's answer: new floor forms at the ridges and is carried back into the mantle at the trenches. If the floor is constantly made and destroyed it is young, and young floor has had little time to collect sediment.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the floor itself is carried into the trenches; it never says trenches remove sediment faster than it forms.","Heat flow is listed as a puzzle, not as a process that destroys sediment.","Nothing in the passage says sediment settles on the continents.",null,"Dietz is credited with the name seafloor spreading, not with any measurement of sediment."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q07","type":"except","stem":"According to paragraph 4, all of the following are true of Hess's proposal EXCEPT:","choices":["It was circulated in 1960, two years before it was published.","It invoked mantle convection beneath the mid-ocean ridges.","It had new floor carried back into the mantle at the trenches.","Hess himself described the paper as an essay in geopoetry.","Hess gave the process its lasting name, seafloor spreading."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"circulated in 1960 and published in 1962","choice":0},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"mantle convection brings hot material up beneath the ridges","choice":1},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"carried back into the mantle at the deep trenches","choice":2},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"Hess called his paper an essay in geopoetry","choice":3},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"Robert Dietz, publishing independently in 1961, gave the process its lasting name: seafloor spreading"}],"why":"Four choices come straight from paragraph 4. Its last sentence says it was Robert Dietz, not Hess, who gave the process the name seafloor spreading.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 4: 'circulated in 1960 and published in 1962.'","Stated in paragraph 4: 'mantle convection brings hot material up beneath the ridges.'","Stated in paragraph 4: new floor is 'eventually carried back into the mantle at the deep trenches.'","Stated in paragraph 4: 'Hess called his paper an essay in geopoetry.'",null]},{"id":"rc-o06-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Curie temperature is the temperature","choices":["at which basalt begins to melt into lava","at which Earth's magnetic field reverses its polarity","above which magnetite grains cannot hold a magnetic direction","below which potassium-argon dating of a lava flow becomes unreliable","at which hot material begins to rise beneath the ridges"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"the grains cannot hold a magnetic direction"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 defines it directly: above the Curie temperature, about 580 degrees Celsius for magnetite, 'the grains cannot hold a magnetic direction.'","distractorNotes":["Melting is never mentioned; the Curie temperature is about magnetism, not melting.","Reversals are a behavior of Earth's field (paragraph 6), not a temperature.",null,"Potassium-argon dating appears in paragraph 6 with no temperature attached to it.","That describes mantle convection from paragraph 4, not the Curie temperature."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if an ancient basalt flow were reheated well above 580 degrees Celsius and then allowed to cool again, it would","choices":["record the field as it was at the time of the reheating","preserve its original compass reading unchanged","lose its magnetite grains entirely","record both the original and the new field at once","become impossible to date by the potassium-argon dating method"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"the grains cannot hold a magnetic direction"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"lock in the direction and polarity of Earth's field at that moment"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says that above the Curie temperature the grains cannot hold a direction, and that on cooling below it they lock in the field 'at that moment.' Reheating erases the old reading; cooling again writes a new one.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: above the Curie temperature the grains cannot hold a direction, so the old reading is lost.","The passage says the grains lose their magnetic direction, not that they disappear.","The grains lock in the field 'at that moment,' a single reading, not two.","Nothing in the passage links the Curie temperature to potassium-argon dating."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Cox, Doell and Dalrymple built their calendar of reversals by","choices":["towing a magnetometer behind a research vessel across the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge","comparing the lavas of central France with those of Japan and Manchuria","folding seafloor magnetic profiles about the crest of the ridge","pairing magnetic measurements of lava flows with potassium-argon dates","drilling into the sediment that rests on the basalt of the South Atlantic"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"paired magnetic measurements of lava flows with potassium-argon dating of the same flows"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says they 'paired magnetic measurements of lava flows with potassium-argon dating of the same flows and built a calendar of reversals.'","distractorNotes":["That is the Eltanin survey in paragraph 9, worked up by Pitman and Heirtzler.","Brunhes and Matuyama studied those lavas decades earlier; the calendar came from dated flows.","Folding the profile is what revealed the Eltanin-19 symmetry in paragraph 9.",null,"Drilling is the Glomar Challenger work described in paragraph 10."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q11","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following are true of geomagnetic reversals EXCEPT:","choices":["Brunhes reported reversely magnetized lavas in France in 1906.","Matuyama found that reversed lavas were older than normal ones.","The idea that the field flips was resisted for decades.","The Brunhes-Matuyama boundary is now placed near 780,000 years ago.","Reversals recur at a regular interval of about 780,000 years."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"In 1906 the French physicist Bernard Brunhes reported lavas in central France magnetized roughly opposite to the present field","choice":0},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"reversed lavas in Japan and Manchuria were consistently older than normal ones","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"was resisted for decades","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"now placed at about 780,000 years ago","choice":3},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"reversals proved to come irregularly, typically a few hundred thousand years apart"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 confirms the other four choices. Its last sentence says reversals 'come irregularly, typically a few hundred thousand years apart,' so a fixed 780,000-year interval is contradicted; 780,000 years is the age of one boundary, not a repeating period.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 6: Brunhes, 1906, lavas in central France magnetized opposite to the present field.","Stated in paragraph 6: reversed lavas in Japan and Manchuria were 'consistently older than normal ones.'","Stated in paragraph 6: the conclusion 'was resisted for decades.'","Stated in paragraph 6: the boundary is 'now placed at about 780,000 years ago.'",null]},{"id":"rc-o06-q12","type":"function","stem":"The author describes Lawrence Morley's experience in paragraph 8 primarily to","choices":["suggest that Vine and Matthews had taken the idea from his rejected manuscript","explain why Nature was the most prestigious journal in the field","show that the magnetic stripes were first discovered in Canada","illustrate how unwelcome the idea was even when it was right","argue that cocktail parties are where good science is first discussed"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"one reviewer remarking that the idea belonged at a cocktail party rather than in print"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says Morley reached the same conclusion independently, yet two journals rejected it and a reviewer mocked it. The episode shows the field dismissing a correct idea, which feeds the author's larger point about slow acceptance.","distractorNotes":["The passage says Morley reached the conclusion 'independently,' which is the opposite of anyone borrowing it.","Nothing is said about Nature's prestige; it is simply where the Vine and Matthews paper appeared.","The stripes were found off Washington, Oregon and California (paragraph 7), not in Canada.",null,"The cocktail party remark was a reviewer's insult, not a recommendation from the author."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a band of seafloor where the measured field is slightly stronger than the regional average most likely","choices":["formed when the field had the same polarity as today","lies directly beneath the crest of the mid-ocean ridge","is older than the reversed bands on either side of it","contains more magnetite than the neighboring bands","formed during the Matuyama reversed epoch"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"normal bands adding to the present field and reversed bands subtracting from it"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 explains that normal bands add to the present field and reversed bands subtract from it. A slightly stronger reading therefore marks floor that cooled when the field pointed the way it does now.","distractorNotes":[null,"Position relative to the crest tells you age, not polarity; stronger bands occur at many distances from it.","Age depends on distance from the ridge (paragraph 10), not on polarity; one neighbor is older and one is younger.","The passage ties the stripes to polarity, not to how much magnetite a band contains.","Reversed: the Matuyama epoch was reversed, and a reversed band subtracts from the present field."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q14","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Eltanin-19 profile was striking because","choices":["it was the first magnetic survey ever run across an ocean ridge","it revealed the Jaramillo event for the first time","it showed the ocean floor spreading far faster than anyone had believed possible","it was gathered off the Pacific coast of North America","its anomalies on one side of the ridge mirrored those on the other"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"the anomalies on one side mirrored those on the other for hundreds of kilometers"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says that when Pitman and Heirtzler plotted the profile, 'the anomalies on one side mirrored those on the other for hundreds of kilometers,' and that folded about the crest the two halves matched peak for peak.","distractorNotes":["The passage never calls it the first; an earlier magnetometer survey appears in paragraph 7.","Doell and Dalrymple identified the Jaramillo event from lava flows, not from this profile.","The passage gives a rate of a few centimeters a year and calls spreading slow and steady (paragraph 10).","That is the Raff and Mason survey of paragraph 7; the Eltanin crossed the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge.",null]},{"id":"rc-o06-q15","type":"application","stem":"Which of the following best illustrates the principle described in paragraph 5?","choices":["A compass needle continuously swings to follow the field wherever it is carried","Wet cement poured over a leaf hardens and keeps the leaf's imprint for decades","A thermometer on a wall rises and falls with the temperature of the room","Iron filings line up along the field lines when a magnet is held beneath the paper","A weather vane turns to point into whatever wind is blowing at the moment"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"the grains lock in the direction and polarity of Earth's field at that moment"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"a compass reading taken on the day it solidified and kept ever since"}],"why":"Paragraph 5's principle is that a material locks in a record of conditions at the moment it solidifies and keeps that record afterward. Cement hardening around a leaf does exactly that; the other cases respond to present conditions and keep no record.","distractorNotes":["A compass tracks the current field and keeps no record, the opposite of locking in a reading.",null,"A thermometer responds to the present temperature and remembers nothing once it changes.","Filings show the field at this moment; move the magnet and the pattern changes with it.","A weather vane follows the present wind and keeps no record of past winds."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the geological profession's handling of continental drift is best described as","choices":["unreserved admiration","puzzled curiosity","measured criticism","bitter contempt","complete indifference"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"a refusal to take good evidence seriously because it lacked a tidy explanation"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"the delay says more about the habits of a profession than about the strength of the case"}],"why":"The judging words are in paragraph 2 ('less like healthy caution than a refusal to take good evidence seriously') and paragraph 11 ('says more about the habits of a profession'). The author faults the delay but grants that 'caution has its place,' so the criticism is measured rather than bitter.","distractorNotes":["Admiration is reserved for the evidence, especially the Eltanin-19 profile, not for the profession's response.","The author is not puzzled; the delay is explained as a habit of the profession.",null,"Too strong: 'caution has its place' and the respectful account of the scientists rule out contempt.","The author plainly cares, calling the evidence beautiful and the delay telling."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Seafloor magnetic stripes supplied the evidence that turned continental drift into plate tectonics","Alfred Wegener was the first scientist to propose that the continents had once been joined","Marie Tharp's discovery of the rift valley was unfairly credited to Bruce Heezen","Earth's magnetic field reverses its polarity at irregular intervals of a few hundred thousand years","The symmetric Eltanin-19 profile was the single piece of evidence that persuaded geologists to accept seafloor spreading"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"the test came from an unlikely direction: the magnetism frozen into volcanic rock"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"the floor should be a tape recording of the reversal history"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"The stripes are a direct physical record, written by the planet in its own rocks, of a process argued about for half a century"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"a single line of data that confirms spreading, reversals and drift at once"}],"why":"The passage opens with drift rejected for lack of a mechanism, then builds to the magnetic test (paragraph 5), the stripe prediction (paragraph 8) and the author's verdict in paragraph 11 that the stripes are the record that settled the argument. The answer covers that whole arc.","distractorNotes":[null,"True, but it is only paragraph 1; the passage is about how drift was finally proven.","The passage never says credit was taken from Tharp; it says Heezen was at first unconvinced.","A detail from paragraph 6, one piece of the story rather than its point.","Too strong: the Jaramillo event and the Glomar Challenger drilling also mattered."]}]},"rc-o07":{"id":"rc-o07","title":"What the Train Cannot Copy","topic":"telomeres and telomerase","field":"molecular biology","source":"original","words":1361,"paragraphs":[["In 1912 the surgeon Alexis Carrel, a Nobel laureate that same year, began growing cells from a chick embryo heart in glass flasks.","The culture was fed and divided for 34 years, outliving Carrel himself, and it became the standard proof that cells freed from the body could divide forever.","Then, in 1961, Leonard Hayflick and Paul Moorhead at the Wistar Institute in Philadelphia reported that normal human fetal cells divided roughly 50 times and then stopped, however carefully they were fed.","The cells did not die; they entered a state now called replicative senescence, alive and metabolically active but finished with division.","The ceiling came to be known as the Hayflick limit, and the chick heart was explained away: the embryo extract that fed the culture had most likely carried fresh cells into the flask with every feeding.","I find something admirable in a young scientist telling his field that its favorite experiment was wrong."],["A decade later the limit acquired a possible mechanism, found by thinking about copying rather than aging.","DNA polymerase, the enzyme that duplicates a chromosome, can only add to the end of an existing strand, so it needs a short RNA primer to start, and it builds in one direction only.","On one strand this causes no trouble, but on the other the enzyme works backward in short pieces called Okazaki fragments, each begun by its own primer.","When the primer at the very tip is removed, there is no strand upstream to extend into the gap, and a short stretch at the end goes uncopied.","Every round of replication should therefore leave the chromosome slightly shorter.","The Russian biologist Alexey Olovnikov is said to have grasped this in 1971 while waiting for a Moscow subway train, picturing the polymerase as a train that cannot copy the track beneath it, and he proposed that the steady loss of the ends might be the counter behind Hayflick's limit."],["Chromosome ends had been singled out as special a generation earlier.","In 1938 the geneticist Hermann Muller, who had been breaking fruit fly chromosomes with X-rays, noted that broken ends were sticky and tended to fuse with other broken ends, while the natural ends never did.","He coined the word telomere for the natural end, from the Greek for end and part.","Around the same time Barbara McClintock, working with maize, watched broken ends fuse and then tear apart at the next division, a cycle that natural ends were somehow spared.","Something at the tip was protecting it, but nobody knew what the tip was made of."],["The answer came from a pond.","Tetrahymena thermophila is a single-celled ciliate that swims in fresh water and carries, in one of its two nuclei, many thousands of tiny linear chromosomes, so every cell holds an enormous number of chromosome ends.","In the late 1970s Elizabeth Blackburn, a postdoctoral researcher in Joseph Gall's laboratory at Yale, sequenced the ends of these molecules; her 1978 paper reported a simple sequence, TTGGGG, repeated over and over.","A decade later the human version turned up: the same kind of repeat, TTAGGG, copied hundreds to thousands of times at the end of every chromosome."],["Jack Szostak, at Harvard Medical School, had found that linear DNA put into yeast cells was chewed up or stitched onto other chromosomes.","After hearing Blackburn speak at a 1980 meeting, he proposed capping his linear yeast DNA with the Tetrahymena repeats to see whether it survived.","It did.","Their 1982 paper showed that a ciliate's chromosome ends could protect DNA inside a yeast, the first sign that telomeres worked by a mechanism shared across kingdoms.","The experiment held a quieter clue: over time the yeast cells added their own repeats onto the borrowed Tetrahymena ends, which no known copying mechanism could explain.","An enzyme that built telomeres from scratch, Blackburn and Szostak reasoned, had to exist."],["Finding it fell to Carol Greider, a graduate student who joined Blackburn's laboratory at Berkeley in 1984.","She mixed Tetrahymena extracts with a synthetic DNA ending in the telomere repeat, added nucleotides, and looked for anything that lengthened it.","On December 25, 1984, she developed an X-ray film and saw a ladder of bands climbing the gel in steps of six: the extract was adding TTGGGG repeats one after another.","They published the finding in 1985 and named the activity telomere terminal transferase, soon shortened to telomerase.","Two further results made the enzyme stranger and more elegant: its activity vanished when RNA was destroyed, so it was part protein and part RNA, and that RNA carried a stretch complementary to the repeat, a built-in template the enzyme reads to decide what to add."],["The picture that emerged is of a reverse transcriptase, an enzyme that makes DNA by reading RNA, carrying its own instructions.","Telomerase has two essential components: a protein subunit, now called TERT, that performs the chemistry, and an RNA subunit, TERC, that supplies the template.","The RNA pairs with the chromosome's overhanging end, the protein extends that end by copying the template, the enzyme slides forward, and the cycle repeats.","The end-replication problem is not abolished by this arrangement; it is paid for in advance, with a buffer of disposable repeats that can be topped up when a cell has the enzyme to do it."],["The two stories met in 1990, when Calvin Harley, Bruce Futcher, and Greider measured telomeres in cultured human fibroblasts and found that they shrank with every division, a loss now put at roughly 50 to 200 base pairs per division.","Most ordinary cells make little or no telomerase, so their ends run down with use; the cells that must divide indefinitely, including germ cells and some stem cells, keep the enzyme switched on.","In 1998 researchers reported that adding the TERT gene to normal human cells let them sail past the Hayflick limit with no sign of senescence, the strongest evidence yet that the shrinking ends were the counter Olovnikov had imagined."],["Too little telomerase ends a cell's working life; too much has consequences of its own.","Roughly 85 to 90 percent of human cancers show telomerase activity, having switched the enzyme back on to become immortal; most of the rest maintain their ends by a recombination route called alternative lengthening of telomeres.","The opposite extreme is dyskeratosis congenita, a rare inherited disorder marked by abnormal skin pigmentation, misshapen nails, white patches in the mouth, and, most dangerously, failure of the bone marrow; its known causes are mutations in genes that build or maintain telomeres, including the components of telomerase, and patients' telomeres are abnormally short.","The enzyme is therefore both a target to inhibit, in tumors, and a deficiency to correct, in a few inherited diseases, and a treatment aimed at one must be watched for the other."],["It is a short step from these facts to the claim that telomeres are the clock of human aging, a step taken constantly, often by people with something to sell.","I am wary of it.","Laboratory mice carry telomeres several times longer than ours and live about two years, and mice engineered to lack telomerase stayed largely healthy for several generations before defects appeared.","Telomere length also varies widely among healthy people of the same age, which makes it a poor ruler for any one individual.","Replicative senescence is real, and it very likely protects us from cancer by retiring overworked cells; whether it also drives the decline of a whole organism is a genuinely open question, not a settled one."],["In 2009 the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine went to Blackburn, Greider, and Szostak for the discovery of how chromosomes are protected by telomeres and the enzyme telomerase.","The prize had the shape of the work: a theorist on a subway platform saw the flaw, a pond ciliate supplied the sequence, a yeast showed the fix was ancient, and a graduate student working through a holiday caught the enzyme in the act.","What strikes me is how little of this was aimed at aging or cancer and how much of both it explained; the question that mattered was simply how a chromosome keeps its ends."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o07-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when the cells studied by Hayflick and Moorhead reached their limit, they","choices":["died within a few days of their final division","fused with one another and tore apart at the next division","stayed alive and active but no longer divided","resumed dividing only when fresh embryo extract was added","transformed into cancer cells that switched telomerase on"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"alive and metabolically active but finished with division"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the cells did not die; they entered replicative senescence, alive and metabolically active but finished with division.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 1 says plainly that the cells did not die.","Wrong paragraph: fusing and tearing apart is McClintock's maize observation in paragraph 3.",null,"Reversed: the cells stopped no matter how carefully they were fed, and embryo extract belongs to Carrel's story.","Outside the passage: nothing says the senescent cells became cancerous; telomerase in cancer is a separate point in paragraph 9."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q02","type":"function","stem":"The author describes Alexis Carrel's chick heart culture in paragraph 1 primarily to","choices":["present the accepted belief that Hayflick's finding overturned","show that cells from birds divide more times than cells from humans","credit Carrel with the first measurement of the Hayflick limit","argue that Nobel Prize winners are rarely questioned by younger scientists","explain how embryo extract keeps cultured cells alive and dividing"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"it became the standard proof that cells freed from the body could divide forever"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"Then, in 1961, Leonard Hayflick and Paul Moorhead"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the chick heart became the standard proof that cells could divide forever, and the very next sentence opens with 'Then, in 1961' and Hayflick's contrary result. Carrel is there as the belief being overturned.","distractorNotes":[null,"Outside the passage: the chick culture is explained by fresh cells in the extract, not by any difference between birds and humans.","Wrong person: paragraph 1 credits Hayflick and Moorhead, and Carrel's result was the opposite of a limit.","Too strong: the author admires Hayflick for questioning his field but never generalizes about Nobel winners.","Half right: the extract matters, but as the suspected source of fresh cells, not as something the author sets out to explain."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q03","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if Carrel's culture had been fed an extract containing no living cells, the culture would most likely have","choices":["continued dividing for 34 years just as it did","produced abnormally short telomeres within a few divisions","fused its chromosome ends into a destructive cycle","entered a state of senescence and kept dividing slowly","stopped dividing after a limited number of doublings"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"carried fresh cells into the flask with every feeding"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"divided roughly 50 times and then stopped"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 explains the chick heart's apparent immortality by fresh cells arriving with every feeding of embryo extract. Take those away and the culture is just ordinary cells, which, as Hayflick found, divide a limited number of times and then stop.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the 34 years depended on the fresh cells the extract carried in.","Outside the passage: no telomere measurement is tied to Carrel's culture, and the shortening in paragraph 8 is gradual.","Wrong paragraph: fusion of broken ends is McClintock's observation in paragraph 3, not a result of changing a culture's food.","Half wrong: paragraph 1 says senescent cells are finished with division, not dividing slowly.",null]},{"id":"rc-o07-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a short stretch of DNA at the chromosome tip goes uncopied because","choices":["the enzyme that removes RNA primers also digests the DNA beneath them","no strand lies upstream of the last primer's gap for the enzyme to extend","the polymerase adds nucleotides only to the beginning of a strand, never its end","the polymerase builds in both directions and its two copies collide at the tip","the RNA primer at the tip is never removed and blocks the final copy"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"there is no strand upstream to extend into the gap"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says that when the primer at the very tip is removed, there is no strand upstream to extend into the gap, so that stretch goes uncopied.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: nothing says primer removal digests DNA; the problem is the gap left behind.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 2 says the polymerase can only add to the end of an existing strand.","Reversed: paragraph 2 says the polymerase can build in only one direction.","Reversed: the trouble begins precisely when the final primer is removed."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q05","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 2, the word 'counter' most nearly means","choices":["a flat surface across which goods are sold","a reply made in order to oppose an argument","a small disc used as a token in a game","a mechanism that keeps a running tally","a cook's work surface in a kitchen"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"the steady loss of the ends might be the counter behind Hayflick's limit"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says the steady loss of the ends might be the counter behind Hayflick's limit: each division removes a little more, so the shrinking ends keep count of how many divisions have happened. The shop or kitchen surface is the everyday meaning and makes no sense here.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday meaning, but nothing in the sentence is being sold or served.","The word can mean a rebuttal, but Olovnikov is proposing a mechanism for the limit, not arguing against it.","A game token is another meaning, but the sentence describes a process that tallies divisions, not a piece.",null,"Another everyday meaning that has no place in a sentence about chromosome ends."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Hermann Muller observed that","choices":["broken ends fused with other broken ends, while natural ends did not","natural ends of chromosomes turned sticky after exposure to X-rays","broken ends in maize fused and then tore apart at the following division","the natural ends of fruit fly chromosomes carried a short repeated sequence","chromosomes that lost their natural ends could no longer be copied"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"broken ends were sticky and tended to fuse with other broken ends, while the natural ends never did"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says Muller, breaking fruit fly chromosomes with X-rays, noted that broken ends were sticky and tended to fuse with other broken ends, while the natural ends never did.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: it was the broken ends that were sticky, and the natural ends never fused.","Wrong person: the fuse-and-tear cycle in maize is McClintock's observation.","Wrong era: the repeated sequence was not found until Blackburn's work in paragraph 4, and Muller did not know what the tip was made of.","Outside the passage: the copying problem belongs to paragraph 2 and was not Muller's observation."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q07","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are stated in the passage about Tetrahymena thermophila EXCEPT:","choices":["It is a single-celled organism that lives in fresh water.","Each of its cells holds an enormous number of chromosome ends.","Its chromosome ends consist of the sequence TTGGGG repeated over and over.","It added its own repeats onto the ends of borrowed yeast DNA.","Its chromosome ends protected linear DNA that was placed inside yeast cells."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"single-celled ciliate that swims in fresh water","choice":0},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"every cell holds an enormous number of chromosome ends","choice":1},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"TTGGGG, repeated over and over","choice":2},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"a ciliate's chromosome ends could protect DNA inside a yeast","choice":4},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"the yeast cells added their own repeats onto the borrowed Tetrahymena ends"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says it was the yeast cells that added their own repeats onto the borrowed Tetrahymena ends, not the other way around. The other four statements come straight from paragraphs 4 and 5.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4, sentence 2 calls it a single-celled ciliate that swims in fresh water.","Paragraph 4, sentence 2 says every cell holds an enormous number of chromosome ends.","Paragraph 4, sentence 3 reports the sequence TTGGGG repeated over and over.",null,"Paragraph 5, sentence 4 says a ciliate's chromosome ends could protect DNA inside a yeast."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, what Carol Greider saw on December 25, 1984, was","choices":["a synthetic DNA strand that had been digested into fragments of six","a ladder of bands climbing up the gel in steps of six","an RNA molecule containing a stretch complementary to the repeat","yeast colonies whose chromosomes carried Tetrahymena repeats","a protein subunit that lengthened DNA without any template"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"saw a ladder of bands climbing the gel in steps of six"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says that on December 25, 1984, Greider developed an X-ray film and saw a ladder of bands climbing the gel in steps of six, the sign that something was adding TTGGGG repeats one after another.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the DNA was being lengthened, not digested.",null,"Wrong timing: the RNA template was one of the two further results described later in paragraph 6.","Wrong paragraph: the yeast experiment belongs to Szostak in paragraph 5.","Contradicted: paragraph 6 says the enzyme is part RNA and reads a built-in template."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q09","type":"application","stem":"Suppose a researcher alters the RNA subunit of telomerase in a yeast cell so that part of its template region carries a different sequence. Based on the passage, the repeats newly added to that cell's chromosome ends would most likely","choices":["be unchanged, because the protein subunit decides what sequence is added","stop being added, because the protein subunit can only read DNA","switch to the Tetrahymena sequence TTGGGG regardless of the change","shrink by about 50 to 200 base pairs with each cell division","match the new sequence written into the altered template"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"a built-in template the enzyme reads to decide what to add"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"an RNA subunit, TERC, that supplies the template"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the RNA carries a built-in template that the enzyme reads to decide what to add, and paragraph 7 says the RNA subunit supplies the template. Change the template and the enzyme copies the new sequence into the chromosome ends.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 7 gives the protein the chemistry and the RNA the template.","Contradicted: paragraph 7 calls telomerase a reverse transcriptase, an enzyme that makes DNA by reading RNA.","Wrong organism: TTGGGG is the Tetrahymena repeat, and the enzyme copies whatever template it carries.","Wrong paragraph: shrinking is what happens to human fibroblasts without telomerase in paragraph 8.",null]},{"id":"rc-o07-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the 1990 study by Harley, Futcher, and Greider found that","choices":["human fibroblasts with added TERT divided past the Hayflick limit","germ cells and stem cells keep telomerase switched on throughout life","telomeres in cultured human fibroblasts shrank with every division","roughly 85 to 90 percent of human tumors contain active telomerase","Tetrahymena extracts added TTGGGG repeats to synthetic DNA"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"measured telomeres in cultured human fibroblasts and found that they shrank with every division"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says that in 1990 Harley, Futcher, and Greider measured telomeres in cultured human fibroblasts and found that they shrank with every division.","distractorNotes":["Wrong year: the TERT experiment is the 1998 result later in paragraph 8.","Stated in paragraph 8, but as general background, not as the 1990 study's finding.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the cancer figure is in paragraph 9 and is not credited to this study.","Wrong study: that is Greider's 1984 observation in paragraph 6."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q11","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a line of germ cells, compared with a line of ordinary skin cells, would most likely","choices":["maintain the length of its chromosome ends across many divisions","lose about 50 to 200 base pairs from each chromosome end per division","reach the Hayflick limit after roughly 50 divisions","require an added TERT gene in order to keep dividing","rely on recombination to maintain its chromosome ends"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"the cells that must divide indefinitely, including germ cells and some stem cells, keep the enzyme switched on"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"a buffer of disposable repeats that can be topped up when a cell has the enzyme to do it"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says most ordinary cells make little or no telomerase, so their ends run down, while germ cells keep the enzyme switched on. Paragraph 7 says the enzyme tops up the disposable repeats, so germ cells should hold their ends steady where skin cells lose ground.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: that steady loss is what paragraph 8 describes for cells without telomerase.","Reversed: the limit belongs to the ordinary cells whose ends run down.","Reversed: germ cells already have telomerase switched on, so nothing needs to be added.","Wrong paragraph: recombination is the route some cancers use in paragraph 9, not germ cells."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, approximately what share of human cancers show telomerase activity?","choices":["fewer than 10 percent","about one third","roughly half","somewhere between 50 and 60 percent","roughly 85 to 90 percent"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"Roughly 85 to 90 percent of human cancers show telomerase activity"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 gives the figure directly: roughly 85 to 90 percent of human cancers show telomerase activity, and most of the rest use a recombination-based route.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the minority without telomerase, not the majority, is about this small.","Not in the passage: no one-third figure appears anywhere.","Too low: paragraph 9 says the large majority, not half.","Not in the passage: the only cancer figure given is 85 to 90 percent.",null]},{"id":"rc-o07-q13","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of dyskeratosis congenita EXCEPT:","choices":["It is marked by white patches in the mouth.","Patients' telomeres are unusually long.","Its most dangerous feature is failure of the bone marrow.","It is a rare inherited disorder.","Its causes include mutations in telomerase components."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"white patches in the mouth","choice":0},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"most dangerously, failure of the bone marrow","choice":2},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"a rare inherited disorder","choice":3},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"including the components of telomerase","choice":4},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"patients' telomeres are abnormally short"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says patients' telomeres are abnormally short, the opposite of long. The other four statements all appear in that same sentence.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 9, sentence 3 lists white patches in the mouth among its marks.",null,"Paragraph 9, sentence 3 names bone marrow failure as the most dangerous feature.","Paragraph 9, sentence 3 calls it a rare inherited disorder.","Paragraph 9, sentence 3 traces its known causes to genes including the components of telomerase."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q14","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions laboratory mice in paragraph 10 primarily to","choices":["show that telomerase is unnecessary for the health of mammals","explain why mice are the preferred animal for telomere research","propose that mice age more slowly than their telomeres would predict","cast doubt on the claim that telomere length sets the pace of aging","argue that human telomeres should be lengthened to match those of mice"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"the claim that telomeres are the clock of human aging"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"Laboratory mice carry telomeres several times longer than ours and live about two years"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 opens with the claim that telomeres are the clock of aging, the author says 'I am wary of it,' and the mice come next: long telomeres, short lives, and health without telomerase for generations. They are evidence against the clock claim.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the mice stayed largely healthy for several generations before defects appeared, so the enzyme is not unnecessary.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about which animal researchers prefer.","Reversed: the mice live about two years despite long telomeres, which is faster, not slower, than a telomere clock would predict.",null,"Outside the passage: the author never recommends lengthening human telomeres and is wary of such claims."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that mice engineered to lack telomerase remained largely healthy for several generations most likely because","choices":["mice rely on recombination rather than telomerase to maintain their ends","mouse cells do not shorten their chromosome ends when they divide","their unusually long telomeres took several generations to run down","defects in mice appear only after the Hayflick limit of 50 divisions","replicative senescence does not occur in animals that live two years"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"Laboratory mice carry telomeres several times longer than ours"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"a buffer of disposable repeats"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"make little or no telomerase, so their ends run down with use"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says mouse telomeres are several times longer than ours, and paragraphs 7 and 8 describe telomeres as a buffer that runs down when a cell lacks the enzyme. A much bigger buffer takes much longer to spend, which is why the defects took generations to show.","distractorNotes":["Wrong paragraph: recombination is the route some cancers use in paragraph 9; nothing attributes it to mice.","Contradicted: paragraph 2 says every round of replication shortens the chromosome, and the defects did eventually appear.",null,"Mash-up: the Hayflick limit counts divisions of cultured human cells, not generations of mice.","Too strong and unsupported: the passage never says senescence is absent in short-lived animals."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the claim that telomeres are the clock of human aging is best described as","choices":["approving","skeptical","detached","nostalgic","alarmed"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"often by people with something to sell"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"I am wary of it."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"a genuinely open question, not a settled one"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the step to the aging-clock claim is taken constantly, often by people with something to sell, then states 'I am wary of it' and closes by calling the question open, not settled. That is a skeptic's voice: doubting without dismissing.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author resists the claim rather than endorsing it.",null,"Too weak: the author takes a clear position in the first person.","Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 10 looks back fondly on anything.","Too strong: the author is measured and calls the question open, not dangerous."]},{"id":"rc-o07-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Carol Greider's Christmas Day discovery of telomerase was the most important single event in the history of molecular biology","The end-replication problem proves that telomere shortening is the clock that drives human aging and that switching on telomerase would reverse it","Alexis Carrel's chick heart culture misled biologists for decades until Hayflick's experiment showed that cells can divide only a limited number of times","A long inquiry into how chromosomes keep their ends explained both the division limit and cancer's immortality, while its link to aging stays open","Tetrahymena thermophila was chosen for telomere research because its cells carry more chromosome ends than those of any other organism on Earth"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"The ceiling came to be known as the Hayflick limit"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"the strongest evidence yet that the shrinking ends were the counter Olovnikov had imagined"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"a genuinely open question, not a settled one"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"the question that mattered was simply how a chromosome keeps its ends"}],"why":"The passage follows one question, how a chromosome keeps its ends, from Hayflick's limit through telomerase to cancer, and paragraphs 10 and 11 close by separating what the work explained from the aging claim the author calls open.","distractorNotes":["Too strong and never said: the author admires Greider's experiment but ranks nothing as the most important event in the field.","Reversed: paragraph 10 says the author is wary of the aging-clock claim and calls it an open question.","Too narrow: Carrel and Hayflick fill only the first of eleven paragraphs.",null,"Too narrow and too strong: the passage says Tetrahymena holds an enormous number of ends, not more than any other organism, and that is one detail among many."]}]},"rc-o08":{"id":"rc-o08","title":"The Long Hunt for the Last Case","topic":"the eradication of smallpox","field":"history of medicine","source":"original","words":1374,"paragraphs":[["On May 8, 1980, the World Health Assembly, meeting in Geneva, declared that smallpox had been eradicated from the earth.","No other human disease has been deliberately driven to extinction in nature, and the achievement deserves a close look, because it was not inevitable.","The variola virus passed from person to person, usually by droplets from a sick person's nose and mouth.","After roughly twelve days of incubation came fever, then a rash that ripened into deep pustules on the face, arms, and legs.","The severe form, variola major, killed about 30 percent of the people it infected, and many survivors were left deeply scarred or blind.","In the twentieth century alone the disease killed an estimated 300 million people."],["Long before anyone understood viruses, people noticed that survivors of smallpox never caught it again, and some drew a bold conclusion: a mild case given on purpose might buy lifelong protection at a bearable price.","The practice, later called variolation after variola, the Latin name of the disease, meant scratching matter from a smallpox patient's pustules into the skin of a healthy person.","It was in use in China, India, the Ottoman Empire, and parts of Africa well before Europeans adopted it.","In Boston in 1721, the minister Cotton Mather, who had learned of the practice from Onesimus, an enslaved West African man, persuaded the physician Zabdiel Boylston to try it.","About one in forty of Boylston's inoculated patients died, compared with about one in seven of the Bostonians who caught smallpox naturally.","The improvement was real, but variolation gave a person a genuine case of smallpox, and that person could pass the disease to neighbors who had not agreed to any risk."],["Edward Jenner, a country physician in Gloucestershire, took the next step by testing a piece of rural folklore: that milkmaids who had caught cowpox, a mild infection of the hands, did not catch smallpox.","On May 14, 1796, he took matter from a cowpox sore on the hand of a milkmaid, Sarah Nelmes, and inoculated an eight-year-old boy, James Phipps.","That July he variolated the boy with smallpox matter, and no disease followed.","Jenner published his findings in 1798, and the Latin word for cow, vacca, gave the procedure its name, vaccination.","He was not the first to try cowpox: a Dorset farmer named Benjamin Jesty had inoculated his wife and sons with it in 1774.","What Jenner added, and what history rightly credits him for, was a deliberate test, a written account, and years of argument for the method against ridicule."],["Vaccination spread quickly across Europe and the Americas, and in 1853 England made it compulsory for infants.","By the mid twentieth century smallpox was gone from Europe and North America, yet it remained endemic across much of Asia, Africa, and South America.","In 1958 Viktor Zhdanov, a Soviet deputy health minister, proposed to the World Health Assembly that the disease be eradicated worldwide, and the Assembly agreed the following year.","The program that followed had good intentions and almost no money, and for eight years it drifted."],["In 1967 the World Health Organization launched what it called the Intensified Smallpox Eradication Programme, with about 2.4 million dollars a year from its own budget.","Smallpox was then endemic in more than thirty countries, with an estimated 10 to 15 million cases and some two million deaths every year.","The American epidemiologist Donald A. Henderson was sent to Geneva to direct the effort, and he led it until 1977.","Ten years, he was told, was the goal, and almost no one in public health thought it could be met."],["Two pieces of technology made the field work possible.","The first was a freeze-dried vaccine, developed by Leslie Collier in London in the 1950s, that stayed potent for weeks in tropical heat without refrigeration.","The second was the bifurcated needle, invented in 1965 by Benjamin Rubin of Wyeth Laboratories, who ground the eye of a sewing needle into two prongs.","Dipped into a vial, the prongs held a single dose, and fifteen quick pricks into the skin gave a reliable vaccination with about a quarter of the vaccine older methods required.","A villager could learn to use it in minutes, and the needles could be boiled and used again."],["Smallpox was also, in a sense, an easy target, and I think any honest account must say so.","The virus infected only humans, so there was no animal reservoir to bring it back.","There were no silent carriers: everyone infected developed the rash, and the rash was so distinctive that a trained searcher could spot it across a room.","A patient became infectious only around the time the rash appeared, and by then was usually too ill to travel far.","Survivors were immune for life, and their scars recorded the fact.","A disease with hidden infections or an animal host would have demanded a different campaign altogether."],["The original plan was mass vaccination: reach at least 80 percent of every population and the disease should starve.","Experience in eastern Nigeria in late 1966 changed the plan.","William Foege, an American physician working there, ran short of vaccine during an outbreak, so instead of vaccinating everyone he used a missionary radio network to locate cases, isolated the patients, and vaccinated the households and villages around them.","The outbreak ended within months, with a fraction of the vaccine a blanket campaign would have consumed.","The method became known as surveillance and containment, or ring vaccination, and its logic is simple: a virus that cannot live outside people dies out when every chain of transmission runs into immune hosts.","Rather than immunize a whole country, the program drew a ring of immune people around each case and let the virus burn out inside it."],["Surveillance meant finding cases, and finding cases meant persuading ordinary people to report them.","Teams carried recognition cards showing a child covered in the rash, and governments offered cash rewards for reporting a case.","In India, from 1973 to 1975, more than 100,000 health workers went house to house across the country in repeated searches.","India recorded its last case in May 1975, and the last case of variola major anywhere was a small girl, Rahima Banu, on Bhola Island in Bangladesh, that October."],["The disease made its final stand in the Horn of Africa.","In October 1977 a hospital cook in Merca, Somalia, named Ali Maow Maalin caught the milder form, variola minor, after riding in a vehicle with two infected children.","He recovered, and he turned out to be the last person on earth to catch smallpox naturally.","The last death came a year later and far from any endemic zone: in 1978 Janet Parker, a medical photographer in Birmingham, England, was infected by virus from a laboratory in her building and died.","After two more years of searching turned up nothing, a global commission certified in December 1979 that the disease was gone, and the Assembly's declaration followed the next spring."],["The whole program cost roughly 300 million dollars, with the endemic countries themselves paying the larger share.","Set against the savings it was one of the great bargains in medicine: countries could stop vaccinating, as the United States had done for children in 1972.","That mattered because the vaccine was not harmless and caused about one death for every million people vaccinated.","Eradication retired that risk along with the disease.","The virus itself survives in two guarded laboratories, one in Atlanta and one in Russia, and whether to destroy those stocks has been debated for decades."],["It is tempting to read the campaign as a template, and I would resist that reading.","Smallpox was a vivid, slow-moving, human-only disease with a cheap and durable vaccine, and the campaigns against polio and measles, which lack some of those advantages, have found the last mile longer.","The lesson that does carry over is the one Foege stumbled on in Nigeria: that finding the disease is worth more than blanketing the population, and that a small team with good information can beat a large one without it.","Jenner predicted in 1801 that his practice would one day annihilate smallpox, and it took 179 years of unglamorous searching to prove him right."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o08-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, variola major","choices":["was the milder of the two forms and rarely left scars","spread mainly through contaminated water and food","was first distinguished from variola minor in 1977","produced a rash only on the trunk of the body","killed about 30 percent of the people it infected"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"The severe form, variola major, killed about 30 percent of the people it infected"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the severe form, variola major, killed about 30 percent of the people it infected and left many survivors scarred or blind.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 1 calls variola major the severe form, and paragraph 10 names variola minor as the milder one.","Wrong mechanism: paragraph 1 says the virus passed by droplets from a sick person's nose and mouth.","Outside the passage: 1977 is the year of the last natural case, not of any distinction between the two forms.","Reversed: paragraph 1 places the pustules over the face, arms, and legs.",null]},{"id":"rc-o08-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, one drawback of variolation was that","choices":["it protected a person for only a few years","it required matter taken from a cow rather than a patient","an inoculated person could pass smallpox to others","it killed about one in seven of those who received it","it had never been tried outside of Europe"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"that person could pass the disease to neighbors who had not agreed to any risk"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 closes by saying variolation gave a person a genuine case of smallpox, and that person could pass the disease to neighbors who had not agreed to any risk.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 2 opens with the observation that survivors never caught smallpox again, and the goal was lifelong protection.","Reversed: cowpox matter was Jenner's innovation in paragraph 3; variolation used matter from a smallpox patient.",null,"Wrong figure: one in seven was the death rate among Bostonians who caught smallpox naturally, not among the inoculated.","Reversed: paragraph 2 says the practice was in use in China, India, the Ottoman Empire, and Africa before Europeans adopted it."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author cites the Boston figures of one in forty and one in seven in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["show that variolation cut the risk of death but did not remove it","prove that Boylston was a more skilled physician than Jenner","explain why the practice originated in China and the Ottoman Empire","demonstrate that smallpox was less deadly in Boston than in London","argue that Mather should have ignored the advice of Onesimus"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"About one in forty of Boylston's inoculated patients died, compared with about one in seven"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"The improvement was real, but variolation gave a person a genuine case of smallpox"}],"why":"The numbers in paragraph 2 are followed at once by the verdict: the improvement was real, but variolation still gave a person a genuine case of smallpox. The figures show a smaller risk, not a vanished one.","distractorNotes":[null,"Outside the passage: the author never compares Boylston's skill with Jenner's.","Wrong purpose: the practice's origins are stated earlier in paragraph 2 and have nothing to do with the Boston numbers.","Outside the passage: London is never mentioned, and no death rates are compared between cities.","Reversed: the figures show that Mather's borrowed idea worked, which is the opposite of a case against it."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, in Jenner's experiment of 1796, James Phipps","choices":["caught smallpox after being inoculated with cowpox","was a milkmaid who had already recovered from cowpox","was inoculated with cowpox by the farmer Benjamin Jesty","received cowpox matter and later resisted smallpox","developed a severe case of cowpox that scarred his hands"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"inoculated an eight-year-old boy, James Phipps"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"That July he variolated the boy with smallpox matter, and no disease followed."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says Jenner inoculated the boy with matter from a cowpox sore in May 1796, then variolated him with smallpox matter that July, and no disease followed.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 3 says no disease followed the smallpox test.","Wrong person: Sarah Nelmes was the milkmaid; Phipps was an eight-year-old boy.","Wrong person: Jesty inoculated his own wife and sons in 1774, not Phipps.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing is said about the severity of the boy's cowpox or any scarring."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Benjamin Jesty receives less credit than Jenner mainly because Jesty","choices":["neither tested the method on purpose nor published an account","inoculated his family with smallpox matter rather than cowpox","performed his inoculations more than twenty years after Jenner","was a physician whose work was rejected by the Royal Society","used a virus that was later shown to be unrelated to cowpox"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"What Jenner added, and what history rightly credits him for, was a deliberate test, a written account"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"a Dorset farmer named Benjamin Jesty had inoculated his wife and sons with it in 1774"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says Jesty tried cowpox first, then names what Jenner added and is rightly credited for: a deliberate test and a written account. The step past the line is that Jesty is remembered less because he supplied neither.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: paragraph 3 says Jesty inoculated his family with cowpox, not smallpox.","Reversed timing: Jesty's 1774 inoculations came 22 years before Jenner's experiment.","Wrong detail: paragraph 3 calls Jesty a farmer, and the Royal Society never appears in the passage.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about the identity of the virus Jesty used, and paragraph 3 says it was cowpox."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 4, the word 'drifted' most nearly means","choices":["was carried along slowly by a current of water","piled up slowly in heaps, like windblown snow","made little progress for lack of direction","moved steadily and purposefully toward its goal","was abandoned entirely by its sponsors"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"had good intentions and almost no money, and for eight years it drifted"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 pairs the word with good intentions and almost no money, and the next paragraph describes the 1967 relaunch as a fresh start. A program that drifted was one that made little headway for lack of money and direction.","distractorNotes":["This is the literal sense of the word, moving with a current, and nothing in the sentence involves water.","Snow and sand drift into heaps; a program does not.",null,"Reversed: a drifting program is one that is not moving steadily toward anything.","Too strong: the program was underfunded and aimless, but the passage never says its sponsors abandoned it, and the Assembly relaunched it in 1967."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q07","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of the eradication program launched in 1967 EXCEPT:","choices":["It was given about 2.4 million dollars a year from the organization's own budget.","It was directed from Geneva by Donald A. Henderson.","It was launched by the World Health Organization.","It was set a goal of ten years.","It was expected by most public health experts to succeed."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"about 2.4 million dollars a year from its own budget","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"sent to Geneva to direct the effort","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"the World Health Organization launched","choice":2},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"Ten years, he was told, was the goal","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"almost no one in public health thought it could be met"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 confirms the budget, the leader, the launching organization, and the ten-year goal. It also says almost no one in public health thought the goal could be met, which rules out the idea that experts expected success.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5, sentence 1 gives the figure of about 2.4 million dollars a year from the organization's own budget.","Paragraph 5, sentence 3 says Henderson was sent to Geneva to direct the effort.","Paragraph 5, sentence 1 names the World Health Organization as the launcher.","Paragraph 5, sentence 4 says ten years was the goal.",null]},{"id":"rc-o08-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, before the freeze-dried vaccine became available, vaccination campaigns in hot climates would most likely have","choices":["relied on the bifurcated needle to conserve doses","struggled to keep the vaccine potent without refrigeration","been unnecessary because smallpox was absent from the tropics","used variolation in place of vaccination","succeeded wherever the recognition cards were distributed"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"stayed potent for weeks in tropical heat without refrigeration"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"Two pieces of technology made the field work possible."}],"why":"Paragraph 6 presents the freeze-dried vaccine as one of two technologies that made field work possible, because it stayed potent for weeks in tropical heat without refrigeration. The step past the line is that earlier vaccine, lacking that property, would have lost potency in the heat unless it could be kept cold.","distractorNotes":["Wrong timing: paragraph 6 dates the needle to 1965, after the freeze-dried vaccine, and the needle conserves doses rather than preserving them.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 4 says smallpox remained endemic across much of Asia, Africa, and South America.","Outside the passage: variolation is never offered as a fallback once vaccination existed.","Wrong paragraph: recognition cards in paragraph 9 helped find cases; they say nothing about keeping vaccine potent."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the bifurcated needle","choices":["was developed by Leslie Collier at a laboratory in London","injected a full dose of vaccine beneath the skin in one stroke","could be used safely only by physicians with special training","needed about a quarter of the vaccine that older methods used","had to be thrown away after one use to prevent infection"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"with about a quarter of the vaccine older methods required"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says fifteen quick pricks with the bifurcated needle produced a reliable vaccination with about a quarter of the vaccine older methods required.","distractorNotes":["Wrong person: Collier developed the freeze-dried vaccine; Benjamin Rubin invented the needle.","Wrong mechanism: paragraph 6 says the prongs held a dose that went in by fifteen quick pricks, not a single injection.","Reversed: paragraph 6 says a villager could learn to use it in minutes.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 6 says the needles could be boiled and used again."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q10","type":"function","stem":"The author calls smallpox an easy target in paragraph 7 primarily to","choices":["argue that the campaign deserved less praise than it received","qualify the achievement by noting the disease's own vulnerabilities","explain why the freeze-dried vaccine survived for weeks in tropical heat","suggest that eradication could have been finished well before 1977","introduce the recognition cards used to spot the rash"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"an easy target, and I think any honest account must say so"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"would have demanded a different campaign altogether"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 opens in the author's own voice, insisting that an honest account admit the disease's weaknesses, then lists them. The paragraph tempers the triumph by crediting the virus's biology, not only the campaign.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the author tempers the praise but never says the campaign was overpraised, and paragraph 11 calls it one of the great bargains in medicine.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the vaccine's heat tolerance is explained in paragraph 6 and has nothing to do with the virus's biology.","Outside the passage: the author never suggests the job could have been finished sooner.","Wrong paragraph: recognition cards appear in paragraph 9, and the easy-target remark is about the disease, not the tools."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q11","type":"except","stem":"The passage names all of the following as features that made smallpox vulnerable to eradication EXCEPT:","choices":["The virus infected only humans.","Survivors were immune for life.","A patient became infectious only around the time the rash appeared.","The rash was distinctive enough to spot at a distance.","The virus was destroyed within hours by tropical heat."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"The virus infected only humans","choice":0},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"Survivors were immune for life","choice":1},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"A patient became infectious only around the time the rash appeared","choice":2},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"the rash was so distinctive that a trained searcher could spot it across a room","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lists the human-only host, the unmistakable rash, the late onset of infectiousness, and lifelong immunity. Heat is mentioned in paragraph 6, but it is the vaccine that survives heat; nothing says the virus was destroyed by it.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 7, sentence 2 says the virus infected only humans.","Paragraph 7, sentence 5 says survivors were immune for life.","Paragraph 7, sentence 4 says a patient became infectious only around the time the rash appeared.","Paragraph 7, sentence 3 says a trained searcher could spot the rash across a room.",null]},{"id":"rc-o08-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when William Foege ran short of vaccine in eastern Nigeria, he","choices":["postponed the campaign until more vaccine arrived from Geneva","vaccinated at least 80 percent of the surrounding population","used jet injectors to stretch the remaining supply","isolated patients and vaccinated the villages around them","distributed recognition cards and offered cash rewards"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"isolated the patients, and vaccinated the households and villages around them"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says Foege used a radio network of missionaries to locate cases, isolated the patients, and vaccinated the households and villages around them.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: no delay or shipment from Geneva is mentioned; Foege acted with what he had.","Reversed: 80 percent coverage was the original mass vaccination plan that Foege's experience replaced.","Outside the passage: jet injectors are never mentioned.",null,"Wrong paragraph: recognition cards and cash rewards belong to the surveillance methods in paragraph 9."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q13","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle of surveillance and containment described in paragraph 8?","choices":["A city sprays insecticide along every street each spring to hold down mosquitoes","A fire crew clears a strip of bare ground around a blaze so it runs out of fuel","A school sends every student home for a week whenever one child has the flu","A hospital gives all of its staff a flu shot at the start of each winter","A farmer plants a different crop in each field every year to keep the soil fertile"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"drew a ring of immune people around each case and let the virus burn out inside it"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"dies out when every chain of transmission runs into immune hosts"}],"why":"Paragraph 8's rule is to surround each outbreak with a barrier it cannot cross and let it burn out inside, rather than treating the whole population. A firebreak cleared around a blaze so the fire runs out of fuel is the same move.","distractorNotes":["This is the blanket approach paragraph 8 contrasts with containment: treating every street rather than ringing an outbreak.",null,"Wrong mechanism: sending everyone home is a shutdown of the whole group, not a ring drawn around the one case.","This is mass vaccination of everyone in advance, the original plan paragraph 8 says was replaced.","Unrelated: crop rotation protects soil fertility and involves no outbreak and no ring."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q14","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the campaign in India between 1973 and 1975","choices":["relied chiefly on vaccinating 80 percent of the population","recorded the last case of variola major anywhere on earth in 1975","sent over 100,000 workers house to house in repeated searches","cost roughly 300 million dollars, paid mostly by India itself","was directed in the field by William Foege"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"more than 100,000 health workers went house to house across the country in repeated searches"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says that in India, from 1973 to 1975, more than 100,000 health workers went house to house across the country in repeated searches.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: 80 percent coverage was the original mass plan in paragraph 8; the Indian campaign was a search.","Wrong country: paragraph 9 places the last case of variola major on Bhola Island in Bangladesh.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the 300 million dollar figure in paragraph 11 is the cost of the whole global program.","Outside the passage: Foege's work is placed in eastern Nigeria in paragraph 8."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Janet Parker's death in 1978 did not undermine the claim that smallpox had been eradicated because","choices":["her infection came from a laboratory, not from natural spread","she recovered, as Ali Maow Maalin had recovered the year before","her illness was the milder form, variola minor","the global commission had already certified eradication","she lived in England, where vaccination had long been compulsory"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"was infected by virus from a laboratory in her building and died"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"the last person on earth to catch smallpox naturally"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 calls Maalin the last person to catch smallpox naturally, then describes Parker's infection as coming from a laboratory, far from any endemic zone. The step past the line is that a laboratory infection is not natural transmission, so it did not restart the count.","distractorNotes":[null,"Contradicted: paragraph 10 says Parker died.","Unsupported: the passage never says which form Parker had; variola minor is attached to Maalin.","Reversed timing: the commission certified eradication in December 1979, more than a year after Parker's death in 1978.","Wrong reasoning: compulsory vaccination in England is mentioned in paragraph 4 about 1853, and the passage never links it to Parker's case."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the idea that the smallpox campaign is a template for other diseases is best described as","choices":["contemptuous","admiring","cautious","indifferent","resigned"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"I would resist that reading"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"The lesson that does carry over"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says the author would resist reading the campaign as a template, then concedes the one lesson that does carry over. Resisting while granting a part is a cautious stance, not a hostile or an admiring one.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the author resists the idea politely and still salvages a lesson from it.","Reversed: the author admires the campaign, not the idea that it is a template.",null,"Too weak: the author takes a clear position and explains it.","Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 12 suggests reluctant acceptance of the idea."]},{"id":"rc-o08-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Edward Jenner's cowpox experiment of 1796 made the eventual disappearance of smallpox inevitable, whatever came after it","Smallpox was ended by a long campaign in which surveillance and the virus's own biology mattered as much as the vaccine","Variolation deserves more credit than vaccination, since it protected people for centuries before Jenner was born","The methods that ended smallpox can be transferred without change to the campaigns against polio and measles","The laboratory stocks of smallpox virus in Atlanta and Russia should be destroyed before an accident releases them"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"the achievement deserves a close look, because it was not inevitable"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"Smallpox was also, in a sense, an easy target"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"finding the disease is worth more than blanketing the population"}],"why":"The passage traces smallpox from variolation and Jenner through the 1967 program, then gives equal weight to the disease's own vulnerabilities in paragraph 7 and to the surveillance strategy in paragraphs 8 and 9, closing with the lesson that finding cases beats blanket coverage. Only the answer holds all of that together.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 1 says the achievement was not inevitable, and it took 179 more years of work.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 2 ends with variolation's serious drawback, and the passage never ranks it above vaccination.","Contradicted: paragraph 12 explicitly resists reading the campaign as a template for polio and measles.","Outside the passage: paragraph 11 reports the debate over the stocks without taking a side."]}]},"rc-o09":{"id":"rc-o09","title":"A Better Room for an Old Enzyme","topic":"c4 and cam photosynthesis","field":"botany","source":"original","words":1390,"paragraphs":[["Every leaf on Earth depends on an enzyme that is, by the standards of its profession, both slow and careless.","Ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase, mercifully shortened to rubisco, attaches carbon dioxide from the air to a five-carbon sugar in the chloroplast.","The product splits at once into two molecules of a three-carbon acid, 3-phosphoglycerate, which is why the ordinary pathway of carbon fixation is called C3 photosynthesis.","Rubisco manages only a few reactions per second, a sluggish pace for an enzyme, and plants compensate by making enormous amounts of it; it is generally thought to be the most abundant protein on the planet."],["Slowness is a nuisance; the carelessness is worse.","Rubisco cannot reliably tell carbon dioxide from oxygen, and when an oxygen molecule arrives instead, it attaches that to the same sugar.","That reaction, the oxygenase half of the name, yields one useful 3-phosphoglycerate and one two-carbon compound, phosphoglycolate, which the Calvin cycle cannot use.","Salvaging that carbon takes a roundabout route through the chloroplast, the peroxisome, and the mitochondrion, costs energy, and returns some carbon to the air.","Botanists call the whole wasteful detour photorespiration, because a leaf running it consumes oxygen and releases carbon dioxide in the light, as if it were breathing.","On a hot, bright day photorespiration can undo a quarter or more of what a C3 leaf has fixed."],["It is natural to ask why natural selection has tolerated such a flaw for so long, and the likeliest answer is history.","Rubisco arose in microbes more than two billion years ago, in air rich in carbon dioxide and nearly empty of oxygen, so there was no rival gas to discriminate against.","By the time photosynthesis had filled the air with oxygen the design was set.","The flaw bites hardest when a plant can least afford it: heat lowers rubisco's preference for carbon dioxide, and warm water holds less dissolved carbon dioxide relative to oxygen.","Drought makes matters worse, because a leaf conserves water by closing its stomata, the pores on its surface, and a closed leaf runs down its internal carbon dioxide while photosynthesis keeps generating oxygen.","A C3 plant in a hot, dry field is therefore trapped: open the stomata and lose water, or close them and feed rubisco the wrong gas."],["The way out was found, fittingly, in a crop that thrives in those conditions.","In 1965 Hugo Kortschak and colleagues at a sugar research station in Hawaii reported that when sugarcane leaves were fed radioactive carbon dioxide, the label appeared first not in 3-phosphoglycerate but in the four-carbon acids malate and aspartate.","The next year Marshall Hatch and Roger Slack, biochemists in the Australian sugar industry, confirmed the finding and traced the carbon from those acids into the ordinary Calvin cycle, showing that sugarcane ran a second carboxylation in front of rubisco.","It is now called C4 photosynthesis, after the carbons in its first product, though for years it was simply the Hatch-Slack pathway."],["The chemistry depends on a second enzyme, phosphoenolpyruvate carboxylase, or PEP carboxylase, which attaches carbon to a three-carbon acceptor, phosphoenolpyruvate, to make four-carbon oxaloacetate, quickly converted to malate.","Two properties make PEP carboxylase an ideal front end for rubisco.","First, it takes its carbon not as dissolved gas but as bicarbonate ion, which the leaf's carbonic anhydrase makes from carbon dioxide almost instantly, so it gathers carbon even when little is present.","Second, it has no oxygenase activity at all; oxygen simply does not fit.","The four-carbon acid is a parcel, and its job is delivery."],["The delivery requires a floor plan that the botanist Gottlieb Haberlandt had described in the 1880s, decades before anyone understood it: in certain grasses the cells around each leaf vein form a conspicuous ring, which he called Kranz anatomy, German for wreath.","In a C4 leaf the outer cells of the wreath, the mesophyll, hold PEP carboxylase but no rubisco; the inner ring of bundle sheath cells, pressed against the vein, holds rubisco and the Calvin cycle, often behind thickened walls that resist the leakage of gas.","Malate made in the mesophyll diffuses through channels into the bundle sheath, where an enzyme strips off its carbon dioxide and hands the gas to rubisco.","Because parcels keep arriving and the gas cannot easily escape, carbon dioxide in the bundle sheath climbs to several times its level in an ordinary leaf, and there rubisco almost never mistakes oxygen for its substrate.","The three-carbon leftover, pyruvate, returns to the mesophyll to be rebuilt into phosphoenolpyruvate."],["The pump is not free.","Rebuilding phosphoenolpyruvate costs roughly two extra ATP for every carbon dioxide delivered, so a C4 leaf spends about five ATP per carbon fixed where a C3 leaf spends three.","In cool, dim, or humid conditions, where rubisco's confusion is mild, that surcharge outweighs the savings and C3 plants win; in heat and strong light the balance tips the other way.","The distribution of C4 plants follows that arithmetic.","They are only about three percent of flowering plant species, yet they include maize, sugarcane, sorghum, and millet, dominate the tropical grasslands, and account for roughly a quarter of the primary productivity on land.","Most are grasses, but not all: amaranths and some saltbushes use the trick, and the pathway has evolved independently more than sixty times.","A few desert plants of Central Asia even run the whole cycle inside single cells, with no wreath at all, which shows that the anatomy serves the chemistry rather than the reverse."],["Succulents solved the same problem with the same enzyme and a different arrangement: they separate the two carboxylations in time rather than in space.","The pattern is called crassulacean acid metabolism, or CAM, after the stonecrop family, and it occurs in cacti, agaves, pineapple, and many orchids.","A CAM plant opens its stomata at night, when the air is cool and an open pore costs the least water.","PEP carboxylase fixes the carbon dioxide that enters into malate, which the plant stores as malic acid in the large vacuoles of its cells.","By dawn the tissue is markedly sour.","Then the stomata close for the day, the stored acid is broken down, and the released carbon dioxide reaches rubisco behind sealed pores, in the daylight the Calvin cycle needs."],["The sourness had been noticed long before the chemistry.","In 1815 Benjamin Heyne, a physician in India, reported to the Linnean Society of London that the leaves of a succulent tasted as acid as sorrel in the morning and lost the taste as the day wore on, a rhythm he could not explain.","What the rhythm buys is water: a C3 crop commonly transpires several hundred grams of water per gram of carbon dioxide fixed, and a CAM plant manages on a small fraction of that.","The price is speed, since a night's worth of stored acid is a small ration of carbon, and CAM plants are among the slowest growers in the plant kingdom."],["The textbook summary, that C4 answers heat and CAM answers drought, is tidy, and I think slightly too tidy.","Some quillworts, small plants rooted in shallow ponds, run CAM entirely underwater, where no water is being conserved.","There the nighttime uptake serves a different shortage: by day the pond's other plants strip the water of carbon dioxide, and only at night does the supply recover, so the quillwort gathers carbon when it can and spends it later.","What CAM and C4 share is not a climate but a strategy, which is to keep rubisco away from the open air and feed it from a concentrated store."],["Rice, a staple for about half of humanity, is a C3 plant, and since 2008 an international consortium has been trying to install the C4 pathway in it, one of the most ambitious projects in plant science.","I admire the ambition, and I doubt the timetable.","Moving a pathway means moving not just a handful of enzymes but a floor plan, a set of cell-specific switches, and a transport system, and each of the sixty-odd natural inventions of C4 took a very long time.","Still, rubisco's lesson is worth remembering whenever we are tempted to call a piece of biology badly designed.","The enzyme is not broken; it is old, and plants have twice found ways to build a better room around it rather than a better worker."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o09-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the ordinary pathway of carbon fixation is called C3 photosynthesis because","choices":["rubisco attaches carbon dioxide to a three-carbon sugar inside the chloroplast","the enzyme completes only about three reactions per second","its first product is a three-carbon acid, 3-phosphoglycerate","three organelles cooperate to salvage the carbon that rubisco misplaces","the pathway was the third form of photosynthesis to be discovered"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"two molecules of a three-carbon acid, 3-phosphoglycerate, which is why the ordinary pathway of carbon fixation is called C3 photosynthesis"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the product splits into two molecules of a three-carbon acid, 3-phosphoglycerate, and that this is why the ordinary pathway is called C3 photosynthesis. The name comes from the first product.","distractorNotes":["Wrong detail: paragraph 1 says the acceptor sugar has five carbons, not three.","Outside the passage: paragraph 1 says only a few reactions per second and never ties the name to that pace.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the three organelles in paragraph 2 belong to photorespiration, not to the pathway's name.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about the order in which pathways were discovered."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, photorespiration gets its name from the fact that","choices":["the salvage route passes through the mitochondrion, the organelle of respiration","rubisco attaches oxygen rather than carbon dioxide to its five-carbon sugar","the detour costs the leaf energy, much as breathing costs an animal","phosphoglycolate is broken down only at night, when the leaf is not photosynthesizing","the leaf consumes oxygen and gives off carbon dioxide while in the light"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"because a leaf running it consumes oxygen and releases carbon dioxide in the light, as if it were breathing"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 explains the name directly: a leaf running the detour consumes oxygen and releases carbon dioxide in the light, as if it were breathing.","distractorNotes":["Half right: the mitochondrion is on the salvage route in paragraph 2, but the passage never ties the name to it.","Wrong step: that is the oxygenase reaction that starts the problem, not the reason given for the name.","Wrong reason: the energy cost is real, but paragraph 2 names the gas exchange, not the cost, as the source of the name.","Contradicted: paragraph 2 says the exchange happens in the light; nothing says the breakdown waits for night.",null]},{"id":"rc-o09-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the atmosphere of more than two billion years ago in paragraph 3 primarily to","choices":["show that rubisco was once a faster enzyme than it is today","explain why a flawed enzyme could persist despite natural selection","argue that oxygen entered the atmosphere more recently than carbon dioxide did","establish that microbes rather than plants were the first organisms to use rubisco","prove that heat and drought were common on the early Earth"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":1,"quote":"why natural selection has tolerated such a flaw for so long, and the likeliest answer is history"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"so there was no rival gas to discriminate against"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 opens by asking why selection tolerated the flaw and answers with history. The ancient atmosphere is the evidence: with no oxygen around, there was no rival gas to discriminate against, so the design was set before the flaw mattered.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: nothing says rubisco was ever faster than it is now.",null,"Half right: the passage implies oxygen came later, but as support for the point about history, not as the point itself.","Wrong emphasis: microbes are mentioned in passing; the paragraph's question is why the flaw survived.","Wrong detail: heat and drought appear later in the paragraph as present-day conditions, not ancient ones."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q04","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a C3 plant that closes its stomata during a hot afternoon will most likely","choices":["stop producing oxygen until the pores reopen","lose more water than a neighboring plant whose stomata stay open","shift its carbon fixation to PEP carboxylase","see photorespiration rise as its internal carbon dioxide falls","fix carbon more quickly because it is no longer losing water"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"a closed leaf runs down its internal carbon dioxide while photosynthesis keeps generating oxygen"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"close them and feed rubisco the wrong gas"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says a closed leaf runs down its internal carbon dioxide while oxygen keeps building, which means feeding rubisco the wrong gas. Paragraph 2 told us what that produces: the oxygenase reaction and photorespiration.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 3 says photosynthesis keeps generating oxygen behind closed pores.","Reversed: closing the stomata is how the leaf conserves water, so it loses less, not more.","Outside the passage: PEP carboxylase belongs to C4 and CAM plants, not to a C3 plant.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 3 says the closed leaf feeds rubisco the wrong gas, the opposite of faster fixation."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Hatch and Slack","choices":["traced carbon from four-carbon acids into the Calvin cycle","were the first to report that radioactive carbon appeared in malate and aspartate","worked at a sugar planters' research station in Hawaii","named the pathway C4 photosynthesis after its first product","discovered the enzyme PEP carboxylase in maize leaves"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"traced the carbon from those acids into the ordinary Calvin cycle"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says Hatch and Slack confirmed the Hawaiian finding and traced the carbon from the four-carbon acids into the ordinary Calvin cycle, showing a second carboxylation in front of rubisco.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong team: paragraph 4 credits the first report, in 1965, to Kortschak's group in Hawaii.","Wrong place: Hatch and Slack worked in the Australian sugar industry; the Hawaiian station was Kortschak's.","Outside the passage: paragraph 4 says what the pathway is now called but never says who named it.","Outside the passage: nothing says they discovered the enzyme, and their plant was sugarcane, not maize."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, one reason PEP carboxylase works well as a front end for rubisco is that it","choices":["runs several times faster than rubisco at the same temperature","operates only in the bundle sheath, where oxygen is scarce","converts carbon dioxide into bicarbonate ion on its own","has no oxygenase activity, since oxygen does not fit it","releases its carbon directly into the Calvin cycle without a carrier"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"it has no oxygenase activity at all; oxygen simply does not fit"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 lists two properties, and the second is stated plainly: PEP carboxylase has no oxygenase activity at all, because oxygen simply does not fit.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: the passage never compares the speeds of the two enzymes.","Wrong cell: paragraph 6 places PEP carboxylase in the mesophyll, the outer ring, not the bundle sheath.","Half right: it uses bicarbonate, but paragraph 5 says carbonic anhydrase makes the bicarbonate, not PEP carboxylase.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 5 says its product is a parcel whose job is delivery, carried to rubisco rather than released directly."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q07","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following are true of the bundle sheath cells of a C4 leaf EXCEPT:","choices":["They hold rubisco and the Calvin cycle.","They contain PEP carboxylase but no rubisco.","They are pressed against the leaf vein.","They receive malate that diffuses in from the mesophyll.","They are often wrapped in thickened walls."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"holds rubisco and the Calvin cycle","choice":0},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"pressed against the vein","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"Malate made in the mesophyll diffuses through channels into the bundle sheath","choice":3},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"often behind thickened walls that resist the leakage of gas","choice":4},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"the mesophyll, hold PEP carboxylase but no rubisco"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 assigns PEP carboxylase, and no rubisco, to the outer mesophyll cells. The bundle sheath is the inner ring that holds rubisco and the Calvin cycle; the other four statements all come from paragraph 6.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 6, sentence 2 says the bundle sheath holds rubisco and the Calvin cycle.",null,"Paragraph 6, sentence 2 says the inner ring is pressed against the vein.","Paragraph 6, sentence 3 says malate made in the mesophyll diffuses into the bundle sheath.","Paragraph 6, sentence 2 says the Calvin cycle sits behind thickened walls that resist leakage."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q08","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, the word 'arithmetic' most nearly means","choices":["a weighing of costs against benefits","the branch of mathematics dealing with numbers","a count of the species in a family","a sum computed by adding figures together","an exact measurement taken in the field"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"The distribution of C4 plants follows that arithmetic."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"that surcharge outweighs the savings and C3 plants win; in heat and strong light the balance tips the other way"}],"why":"The sentence before explains a surcharge weighed against savings, with the balance tipping one way in cool conditions and the other in heat. The arithmetic that C4 distribution follows is that cost-against-benefit comparison.","distractorNotes":[null,"The everyday meaning; no computation with numbers is being performed in the sentence.","Wrong referent: the species figure comes after this sentence as a consequence, not as what the word refers to.","Too literal: the sentence points back to a comparison of cost and savings, not to an addition problem.","Outside the passage: no measurement is described; the word refers to the reasoning of the previous sentence."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, C4 plants","choices":["make up about a quarter of all flowering plant species on Earth","are almost entirely limited to members of the grass family","account for about three percent of the primary productivity on land","include rice, maize, and sorghum among their number","dominate the tropical grasslands despite being few in species"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"They are only about three percent of flowering plant species, yet they include maize, sugarcane, sorghum, and millet, dominate the tropical grasslands"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says C4 plants are only about three percent of flowering plant species, yet they dominate the tropical grasslands and account for roughly a quarter of productivity on land.","distractorNotes":["Swapped numbers: a quarter is their share of productivity, not of species.","Too strong: paragraph 7 says most are grasses but names amaranths and saltbushes as well.","Swapped numbers: three percent is their share of species; their share of productivity is about a quarter.","Wrong plant: paragraph 11 says rice is a C3 plant.",null]},{"id":"rc-o09-q10","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the desert plants of Central Asia in paragraph 7 primarily to","choices":["show that C4 photosynthesis can evolve even in plants that are not grasses","introduce the succulents that will be discussed in the next paragraph","show that the anatomy serves the chemistry rather than the reverse","argue that the C4 pathway first arose in deserts and only later spread to grasslands","illustrate how many separate times the pathway has arisen"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":7,"quote":"which shows that the anatomy serves the chemistry rather than the reverse"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says these plants run the whole cycle inside single cells with no wreath at all, and spells out the point: the anatomy serves the chemistry rather than the reverse.","distractorNotes":["Wrong detail: the non-grass point is made with amaranths and saltbushes in the sentence before.","Wrong link: the succulents of paragraph 8 are introduced on their own and are not these desert plants.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing is said about where the pathway first arose.","Wrong detail: the count of more than sixty origins is a separate point in the previous sentence."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q11","type":"application","stem":"A farmer must choose between a C3 grass and a C4 grass for a pasture in a cool, cloudy, humid valley. Based on the reasoning in paragraph 7, the passage would predict that","choices":["the C4 grass will outgrow the C3 grass because its pump never shuts off","the two grasses will grow equally well, because that climate removes rubisco's flaw entirely","the C4 grass will do better only if the valley also suffers from drought","the C4 grass will switch to the C3 pathway once the surcharge becomes too high","the C3 grass will do better, since the surcharge outweighs its savings there"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"In cool, dim, or humid conditions, where rubisco's confusion is mild, that surcharge outweighs the savings and C3 plants win"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 gives the rule: in cool, dim, or humid conditions rubisco's confusion is mild, so the C4 surcharge outweighs the savings and C3 plants win. A cool, cloudy, humid valley is exactly that case.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 7 says the C4 surcharge is a cost that pays off only in heat and strong light.","Too strong: paragraph 7 says rubisco's confusion is mild in such conditions, not absent, and still names a winner.","Wrong rule: paragraph 7 ties the C4 advantage to heat and strong light, and drought is not part of the stated rule.","Outside the passage: nothing in the passage describes a plant trading one pathway for the other.",null]},{"id":"rc-o09-q12","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the leaves of a CAM plant would taste","choices":["sour at dusk and bland at dawn","most sour at dawn and less sour by the end of the day","equally sour at every hour, since acid is stored permanently","sour only during a drought, when the stomata stay closed","sweet in the morning, once the stored malate has become sugar"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"By dawn the tissue is markedly sour."},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"Then the stomata close for the day, the stored acid is broken down"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the acid is stored through the night, so by dawn the tissue is markedly sour, and then the stored acid is broken down during the day. Sourness peaks at dawn and fades by evening.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 8 says the acid builds overnight and is broken down by day.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 8 says the stored acid is broken down during the day.","Outside the passage: the night-and-day rhythm is routine for a CAM plant, not a drought response.","Outside the passage: nothing says the malate becomes sugar or that the leaf turns sweet."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q13","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Benjamin Heyne","choices":["worked out the chemistry behind the daily change in taste","was the first to study the stonecrop family closely","measured how much water a succulent lost at night","reported a daily change in a succulent's taste","showed that quillworts take up carbon dioxide at night"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"the leaves of a succulent tasted as acid as sorrel in the morning and lost the taste as the day wore on"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says Heyne reported in 1815 that a succulent's leaves tasted as acid as sorrel in the morning and lost the taste as the day wore on, a rhythm he could not explain.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 9 says the rhythm was one he could not explain.","Outside the passage: the stonecrop family is mentioned in paragraph 8 without any researcher attached.","Outside the passage: the water figures in paragraph 9 are not attributed to Heyne.",null,"Wrong detail: the quillworts in paragraph 10 are described with no researcher named."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q14","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of CAM plants EXCEPT:","choices":["They grow faster than most C3 plants do.","They open their stomata during the night.","They store malic acid in large vacuoles.","They include cacti, agaves, and pineapple.","They fix carbon dioxide using PEP carboxylase."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"A CAM plant opens its stomata at night","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"stores as malic acid in the large vacuoles","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"cacti, agaves, pineapple","choice":3},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"PEP carboxylase fixes the carbon dioxide","choice":4},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"CAM plants are among the slowest growers in the plant kingdom"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the price of CAM is speed and that CAM plants are among the slowest growers in the plant kingdom. The other four statements all come from paragraph 8.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 8, sentence 3 says a CAM plant opens its stomata at night.","Paragraph 8, sentence 4 says the malate is stored as malic acid in large vacuoles.","Paragraph 8, sentence 2 lists cacti, agaves, and pineapple among CAM plants.","Paragraph 8, sentence 4 says PEP carboxylase fixes the carbon dioxide that enters."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the carbon dioxide dissolved in a shallow pond inhabited by quillworts is most likely","choices":["constant throughout the day, because the quillworts keep it in balance","highest in the afternoon, when the pond's plants are most active","unaffected by the plants rooted in the pond","lowest at the end of the day and highest late at night","depleted at night, when the quillworts take it up"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"by day the pond's other plants strip the water of carbon dioxide, and only at night does the supply recover"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the pond's other plants strip the water of carbon dioxide by day and the supply recovers only at night. So the supply is at its lowest when the day ends and at its highest after a night of recovery.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 10 describes a daily fall and recovery in the supply.","Reversed: the plants' daytime activity strips the water of carbon dioxide rather than adding to it.","Contradicted: the pond's other plants are exactly what deplete it by day.",null,"Half right: the quillwort does take it up at night, but paragraph 10 says night is when the supply recovers."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the project to install the C4 pathway in rice is best described as","choices":["openly dismissive","admiring but doubtful","unreservedly enthusiastic","largely indifferent","deeply alarmed"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"I admire the ambition, and I doubt the timetable."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"each of the sixty-odd natural inventions of C4 took a very long time"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 states it in one line: the author admires the ambition and doubts the timetable, then lists why moving a pathway is hard. Admiration plus doubt is the whole attitude.","distractorNotes":["Too negative: the author says plainly that the ambition is admirable.",null,"Too positive: the author doubts the timetable and lists the obstacles.","Wrong register: the author takes a clear position on both the aim and the schedule.","Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 11 expresses fear or warning about the project."]},{"id":"rc-o09-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Rubisco is the slowest and least accurate enzyme in the plant kingdom, and photorespiration is the price of that failing","C4 and CAM plants have replaced rubisco with PEP carboxylase, an enzyme that cannot mistake oxygen for carbon dioxide","Plants have twice evolved ways of feeding rubisco concentrated carbon dioxide rather than replacing the flawed enzyme itself","Because C4 plants dominate the tropics and account for a quarter of land productivity, engineering the pathway into rice should be straightforward","The discovery of C4 photosynthesis in sugarcane during the 1960s overturned the long-held belief that all plants fix carbon the same way"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"Succulents solved the same problem with the same enzyme and a different arrangement"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"What CAM and C4 share is not a climate but a strategy, which is to keep rubisco away from the open air and feed it from a concentrated store"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"plants have twice found ways to build a better room around it rather than a better worker"}],"why":"The passage sets up rubisco's flaw, then shows C4 and CAM as two arrangements that feed the same enzyme from a concentrated store, and closes by saying plants built a better room around the enzyme rather than a better worker. The answer covers both halves.","distractorNotes":["Too narrow and too strong: the flaw is only the setup, and the passage never ranks rubisco against every enzyme.","Contradicted: PEP carboxylase is a front end that delivers carbon to rubisco; paragraph 6 says rubisco still does the fixing.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 11 doubts the timetable and lists why moving the pathway is hard.","Too narrow: the discovery is one episode in paragraph 4, and the passage never mentions such a belief."]}]},"rc-o10":{"id":"rc-o10","title":"The Pump and the Proof","topic":"john snow and cholera in london","field":"epidemiology","source":"original","words":1400,"paragraphs":[["Cholera reached Britain for the first time in the autumn of 1831, at the port of Sunderland, and spread across the country over the following year.","It was terrifying in its speed: a healthy adult could be seized by violent diarrhea and vomiting in the morning and be dead of dehydration by nightfall.","The physicians of the day had an old explanation ready: miasma theory held that epidemic disease arose from foul air, the invisible emanations of rotting matter, stagnant drains, and overcrowded slums.","The theory matched experience, since the districts that smelled worst were usually those where the poor died fastest.","Edwin Chadwick, whose 1842 report launched Britain's public health movement, was a convinced miasmatist, and so was William Farr, the statistician of the General Register Office."],["John Snow was an unlikely challenger.","Born in York in 1813, he had met cholera as a teenage apprentice treating miners at Killingworth colliery during that first epidemic.","By the late 1840s he was a London physician best known for anesthesia; in 1853 he would give chloroform to Queen Victoria at the birth of Prince Leopold.","If cholera were inhaled, he reasoned, it should first injure the lungs or poison the blood and announce itself with fever.","Instead it began in the gut.","The simplest explanation was that the agent had been swallowed, had multiplied in the intestine, and had left the body in the stools, ready to reach other mouths through soiled hands, soiled linen, and above all water."],["Snow set out this case in 1849 in a short pamphlet, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera, with examples of local outbreaks that a single contaminated supply seemed to explain.","He could not name the agent.","Filippo Pacini would describe a comma-shaped bacterium in cholera victims in Florence in 1854, in work that went almost unnoticed, and Robert Koch would not isolate the organism and convince the medical world until the 1880s.","Snow was proposing a living, self-reproducing poison on the strength of its behavior alone, and his critics did not find that persuasive."],["At the end of August 1854, cholera erupted in Soho around Broad Street, and in roughly ten days more than five hundred people died within a few hundred yards of a single public pump.","Snow obtained the addresses of the dead from the General Register Office and walked the streets asking survivors where their households had drawn water.","The deaths clustered around the Broad Street pump, whose cool, clear water was popular.","On the evening of September 7 he put his findings to the parish Board of Guardians, and on the following day the pump's handle was removed."],["What persuaded Snow was not only the cluster but the exceptions.","A workhouse near the pump, holding more than five hundred inmates, recorded only a handful of deaths; it had its own well.","A brewery on Broad Street employing about seventy men lost none of them; the workers drank the beer they made, and the brewery had its own well.","A widow in Hampstead, miles from Soho, died of cholera; she had liked the Broad Street water so much that a cart brought her a bottle of it each day.","Her niece, who visited and drank from the same supply, died as well.","People who shared Soho's air but not its water escaped; people who drank the water escaped nothing, however far away they lived."],["Snow's most valuable local ally began as a doubter.","Henry Whitehead, a young curate in the parish, set out to disprove the water theory by his own door-to-door inquiry, and ended by confirming it.","He identified what now looks like the first case: an infant at 40 Broad Street who fell ill with diarrhea a few days before the outbreak, whose mother had rinsed the soiled napkins and emptied the water into a cesspool in front of the house.","A surveyor who opened the ground found the cesspool's brickwork decayed and the well of the pump only a few feet away."],["The removal of the handle has become the founding legend of epidemiology, and like most legends it is tidier than the record.","Snow himself acknowledged that the outbreak was already subsiding when the handle came off, largely because so many residents had fled.","His celebrated map, with black bars marking the dead street by street, was drawn afterward as an instrument of persuasion rather than discovery; the theory came first.","None of this diminishes the work.","It means the pump was a demonstration, not the proof, and that the proof lay south of the river."],["In south London two private companies, the Southwark and Vauxhall Company and the Lambeth Company, sold Thames water through pipes that ran down the same streets, often to houses side by side.","Both had once drawn from the river in the heart of the city, downstream of its sewer outfalls, but in 1852 the Lambeth Company moved its intake upstream to Thames Ditton, above the tidal reach and above the sewage; the Southwark and Vauxhall Company went on pumping from the tidal river at Battersea.","Hundreds of thousands of people, breathing the same air and often unaware of which company's pipe entered their house, had been divided without their knowledge into two groups that differed in one respect only.","No experiment could have been designed more cleanly, and this one had arranged itself on a grand scale."],["The difficulty was practical: survivors in a stricken household frequently did not know which company served it.","Snow found a chemical answer: water from the tidal river carried far more salt, and a few drops of silver nitrate solution threw down a visibly heavier white precipitate of silver chloride when the sample came from the Southwark and Vauxhall Company.","With a bottle of the reagent and the addresses supplied by Farr's office, Snow traced the supply of house after house.","The figures he published in 1855 were stark: in the first seven weeks, cholera killed about 315 people for every ten thousand houses supplied by the Southwark and Vauxhall Company, against 37 per ten thousand houses supplied by the Lambeth Company, and 59 in the rest of London.","A customer of the polluted supply was more than eight times as likely to die as a neighbor who drank from the clean one."],["The reception was cool.","The General Board of Health's scientific committee, reporting in 1855, declined to accept Snow's conclusion and attributed the Soho outbreak to the state of the atmosphere.","When Snow told a parliamentary committee that same year that the city's offensive trades did not spread disease, The Lancet mocked him in an editorial.","The parish restored the pump handle once the outbreak had passed.","When Snow died of a stroke in 1858, at forty-five, the same journal's obituary ran to a few lines and praised his work on chloroform without mentioning cholera at all."],["Vindication arrived by routes Snow did not live to see, some of them ironic.","Farr, who had long explained cholera's pattern by elevation and the cleaner air of higher ground, came to allow that the river water itself might be the link.","In 1866, when London's last cholera epidemic struck the East End, Farr traced it to a company that had supplied unfiltered water from the River Lea.","Meanwhile the hot summer of 1858 had produced the Great Stink, when the smell of the Thames drove members of Parliament from their chambers, and Parliament promptly funded Joseph Bazalgette's intercepting sewers to carry the city's waste downstream.","The sewers were built to abolish a smell.","In doing so they removed sewage from the stretch of river that the water companies drank from, and once they were complete cholera never returned to the capital."],["It is tempting to tell this as one clear-eyed man against a blind establishment, and I think that framing does a disservice to both sides.","The miasmatists were wrong about the mechanism, but their drains and sewers saved lives, and Snow's opponents had a reasonable objection: he was asking them to believe in an organism no one had seen.","What Snow had, and what his critics lacked, was a method for deciding between explanations when the cause itself could not be observed.","Hold everything constant but the one suspected factor, count the dead on each side, and let the ratio speak.","That method, more than the pump handle, is his inheritance."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o10-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, miasma theory attributed epidemic disease to","choices":["contaminated drinking water drawn from the tidal Thames","a living organism that multiplied in the intestine","foul air given off by decaying matter and crowded slums","the violent diarrhea and vomiting that spread from the sick","the low elevation of the poorest districts above the river"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"miasma theory held that epidemic disease arose from foul air, the invisible emanations of rotting matter, stagnant drains, and overcrowded slums"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 defines the theory directly: epidemic disease arose from foul air, the emanations of rotting matter, stagnant drains, and overcrowded slums.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: contaminated water is Snow's explanation in paragraph 2, the rival of miasma theory.","Reversed: a living organism multiplying in the gut is Snow's proposal, not the miasmatists' view.",null,"Outside the passage: the symptoms are described in paragraph 1, but nothing says the miasmatists blamed them for spreading disease.","Wrong paragraph: elevation is Farr's pattern in paragraph 11, and there too the explanation is cleaner air, not elevation itself."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Snow concluded that the cholera agent was swallowed rather than inhaled because","choices":["the illness began in the gut, not with fever in the blood","the agent had been observed multiplying in the intestine","miners at Killingworth had all drunk from one contaminated well","the stools of victims were found to hold a living bacterium","fever and lung damage appeared only late in the course of the illness"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"it should first injure the lungs or poison the blood and announce itself with fever"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"Instead it began in the gut."}],"why":"Paragraph 2 lays out the reasoning: an inhaled poison should injure the lungs or blood and announce itself with fever, but instead cholera began in the gut, so the agent must have been swallowed.","distractorNotes":[null,"Contradicted: paragraph 3 says Snow could not name the agent and argued from its behavior alone.","Outside the passage: Killingworth in paragraph 2 is where Snow first met cholera; no well is mentioned.","Wrong timing: paragraph 3 says the bacterium was not described until 1854, after Snow's 1849 argument.","Distortion: paragraph 2 says the illness began in the gut, not that fever and lung damage came later."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions Filippo Pacini and Robert Koch in paragraph 3 primarily to","choices":["credit the scientists who first proved that Snow's theory was correct","show that the microscope had already made miasma theory untenable by 1849","argue that Snow deserves less credit than the men who found the organism","emphasize that Snow was arguing for an agent that no one had yet seen","explain why the Board of Health rejected Snow's conclusions in 1855"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"He could not name the agent."},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Snow was proposing a living, self-reproducing poison on the strength of its behavior alone"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says Snow could not name the agent, then dates the organism's discovery to 1854 and the 1880s, and concludes that he was proposing a living poison on the strength of its behavior alone. The two names mark how far ahead of the evidence he was working.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: paragraph 11 credits Farr and the sewers with vindication, and nothing says Koch proved Snow right.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says the organism was not seen until 1854 and not accepted until the 1880s, well after 1849.","Outside the passage: the author never ranks Snow against Pacini or Koch.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the Board of Health's rejection is in paragraph 10 and is tied to the atmosphere, not to Pacini or Koch."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the handle of the Broad Street pump was removed","choices":["on the evening Snow addressed the Board of Guardians","the day after Snow presented his findings to the parish","roughly ten days after the first deaths, by Henry Whitehead","after a surveyor found the cesspool leaking into the well","once the General Register Office supplied the addresses"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"on the following day the pump's handle was removed"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says Snow put his findings to the parish Board of Guardians on the evening of September 7, and on the following day the handle was removed.","distractorNotes":["Wrong timing: paragraph 4 says the handle came off the following day, not the same evening.",null,"Wrong person: paragraph 6 introduces Whitehead as an investigator, not as the one who removed the handle.","Reversed order: the surveyor's inspection in paragraph 6 came after the handle was already off.","Half right: the addresses let Snow begin his inquiry, but the handle was removed only after he reported to the parish."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q05","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are cited in paragraph 5 as cases that persuaded Snow EXCEPT:","choices":["a workhouse near the pump with its own well and few deaths","a brewery whose seventy workers drank beer and all survived","a widow in Hampstead who had the pump's water delivered","a niece who drank from the same supply and died","an infant whose soiled napkins went into a cesspool"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"it had its own well","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"lost none of them","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"a cart brought her a bottle of it each day","choice":2},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"Her niece, who visited and drank from the same supply, died as well.","choice":3},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"an infant at 40 Broad Street who fell ill with diarrhea a few days before the outbreak"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 lists the workhouse, the brewery, the Hampstead widow, and her niece. The infant belongs to paragraph 6, where it is Henry Whitehead's finding, not one of Snow's exceptions.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5, sentence 2 gives the workhouse with its own well and only a handful of deaths.","Paragraph 5, sentence 3 gives the brewery that lost none of its seventy men.","Paragraph 5, sentence 4 gives the widow who had a bottle of the water brought by cart each day.","Paragraph 5, sentence 5 says the niece drank from the same supply and died as well.",null]},{"id":"rc-o10-q06","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the brewery workers on Broad Street escaped cholera because they","choices":["did not drink the water that the public pump supplied","worked in air that was cleaner than the air of the street","had fled the neighborhood as soon as the outbreak began","lived outside Soho and came in only during the day","had survived an earlier attack and were immune"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"the workers drank the beer they made, and the brewery had its own well"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":6,"quote":"People who shared Soho's air but not its water escaped"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the brewery workers drank the beer they made and the brewery had its own well, then sums up: people who shared Soho's air but not its water escaped. The workers were spared because they never drank from the pump.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: paragraph 5 says the survivors shared Soho's air; it was the water they did not share.","Wrong paragraph: fleeing residents appear in paragraph 7, and nothing links them to the brewery.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about where the workers lived.","Outside the passage: the passage never mentions immunity from a previous attack."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q07","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Henry Whitehead","choices":["was the physician who first proposed that cholera was waterborne","removed the handle from the Broad Street pump on Snow's advice","began his inquiry intending to refute the water theory","drew the map of deaths that Snow published in 1855","was the surveyor who found the decayed brickwork of the cesspool"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"set out to disprove the water theory by his own door-to-door inquiry, and ended by confirming it"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says Whitehead set out to disprove the water theory by his own door-to-door inquiry and ended by confirming it; he began as a doubter.","distractorNotes":["Wrong person: paragraphs 2 and 3 give that role to Snow, and Whitehead was a curate, not a physician.","Wrong person: paragraph 4 says the parish removed the handle after Snow reported to the Board of Guardians.",null,"Wrong person: paragraph 7 calls the map Snow's celebrated map.","Wrong person: paragraph 6 says a surveyor opened the ground; Whitehead identified the first case."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q08","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, the word 'instrument' most nearly means","choices":["a device for taking measurements","a means of achieving a purpose","a formal legal document","a tool with a sharp edge","an object that produces music"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"as an instrument of persuasion rather than discovery"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 calls the map an instrument of persuasion: something used to accomplish an end, in this case convincing others. The map was a means, not a measuring device or a musical object.","distractorNotes":["A measuring device is a common meaning of the word, but the map measured nothing; it persuaded.",null,"A legal instrument is another everyday meaning, and nothing in the sentence involves a document with legal force.","A cutting tool has nothing to do with a map of deaths.","The musical sense is the most familiar meaning, and it makes no sense applied to a map."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q09","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the popular story of the pump handle is best described as","choices":["scornful and openly dismissive","uncritically admiring","entirely indifferent","admiring but corrective","bitterly disappointed"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"like most legends it is tidier than the record"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"None of this diminishes the work."}],"why":"Paragraph 7 calls the story a legend that is tidier than the record and sets the record straight, then adds that none of this diminishes the work. The author corrects the story while still admiring what Snow did.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the author says plainly that none of this diminishes the work.","Reversed: the author spends paragraph 7 correcting the legend, which is the opposite of uncritical.","Too weak: the author cares enough to correct the record in detail.",null,"Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 7 expresses disappointment; the correction is calm and measured."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, in 1852 the Lambeth Company","choices":["began selling filtered water drawn from the River Lea","laid new pipes in streets its rival did not serve","moved its intake downstream to Battersea, below the sewer outfalls","merged its supply with that of the Southwark and Vauxhall Company","shifted its intake upstream, above the tide and the sewage"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"the Lambeth Company moved its intake upstream to Thames Ditton, above the tidal reach and above the sewage"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says that in 1852 the Lambeth Company moved its intake upstream to Thames Ditton, above the tidal reach and above the sewage.","distractorNotes":["Wrong paragraph: the River Lea belongs to the 1866 East End epidemic in paragraph 11, and the Lambeth Company drew from the Thames.","Reversed: paragraph 8 says the two companies' pipes ran down the same streets.","Reversed: Battersea is where the Southwark and Vauxhall Company kept pumping; Lambeth moved upstream.","Outside the passage: the two companies are described as rivals serving neighboring houses, not as a merged supply.",null]},{"id":"rc-o10-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author notes in paragraph 8 that the two companies' pipes ran down the same streets primarily to","choices":["criticize the wastefulness of competing private water companies","show that the two groups of customers differed in their water alone","explain why customers so often did not know which company served them","suggest that the two supplies were sometimes mixed in the pipes","contrast south London with the crowded streets of Soho"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"had been divided without their knowledge into two groups that differed in one respect only"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"No experiment could have been designed more cleanly"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 builds to the point that the customers of the two companies breathed the same air and lived in the same streets, so they differed in one respect only: their water. The shared streets are what make the comparison clean.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: the author never complains about waste or competition.",null,"Answers a different question: customers' ignorance of their supplier is mentioned, but it is a side effect, not the reason the shared streets matter.","Outside the passage: nothing suggests the supplies mixed; the whole comparison depends on their staying separate.","Wrong purpose: paragraph 8 is not comparing neighborhoods; it is setting up a comparison between two water supplies."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Snow used silver nitrate to","choices":["tell which company had supplied a house's water","measure how much sewage the tidal river carried","purify samples of water taken from the Broad Street pump","detect the cholera organism in the stools of victims","confirm that the Lambeth Company filtered its supply"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"a few drops of silver nitrate solution threw down a visibly heavier white precipitate of silver chloride when the sample came from the Southwark and Vauxhall Company"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 explains that the saltier tidal water gave a heavier precipitate of silver chloride, which told Snow a sample came from the Southwark and Vauxhall Company when the household did not know.","distractorNotes":[null,"Half right: the test detected salt, not sewage, and it sorted samples by company rather than measuring pollution.","Wrong paragraph and wrong purpose: the Broad Street pump is in paragraphs 4 to 6, and the reagent sorted water, it did not purify it.","Contradicted: paragraph 3 says Snow could not name the agent, and the test was applied to water, not stools.","Outside the passage: filtering is never attributed to the Lambeth Company; the difference was where its intake sat."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, during the first seven weeks of the 1854 epidemic, a household supplied by the Lambeth Company was","choices":["at greater risk than a household elsewhere in London","at no risk of cholera at all","as likely to lose a member as a household supplied by its rival","safer than the typical household in the rest of London","at risk only if it also drew water from a public pump"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"against 37 per ten thousand houses supplied by the Lambeth Company, and 59 in the rest of London"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 gives 37 deaths per ten thousand Lambeth houses against 59 in the rest of London, so a Lambeth household did better than the London average, not just better than its rival's customers.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: 37 per ten thousand is lower than the 59 for the rest of London, not higher.","Too extreme: 37 deaths per ten thousand houses is low, but it is not zero.","Contradicted: the rival's customers died at 315 per ten thousand, more than eight times the Lambeth rate.",null,"Outside the passage: paragraph 9 says nothing about public pumps in south London."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q14","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following was part of the reception of Snow's work EXCEPT:","choices":["A scientific committee attributed the Soho outbreak to the atmosphere.","A journal mocked him in an editorial.","Parliament ordered the pump sealed permanently.","The parish put the pump handle back after the outbreak.","His obituary praised his anesthesia work and omitted cholera."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"attributed the Soho outbreak to the state of the atmosphere","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"The Lancet mocked him in an editorial","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"The parish restored the pump handle once the outbreak had passed.","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"praised his work on chloroform without mentioning cholera at all","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"The parish restored the pump handle"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives the committee's verdict, the journal's mockery, the restored handle, and the obituary. Nothing says Parliament sealed the pump; the parish put the handle back.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 10, sentence 2 says the committee attributed the outbreak to the state of the atmosphere.","Paragraph 10, sentence 3 says The Lancet mocked him in an editorial.",null,"Paragraph 10, sentence 4 says the parish restored the pump handle once the outbreak had passed.","Paragraph 10, sentence 5 says the obituary praised his chloroform work without mentioning cholera."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the sewers built after the Great Stink succeeded against cholera","choices":["because Parliament had finally accepted Snow's theory","for a reason their builders had not set out to address at all","because the Lambeth Company had already moved its intake upstream","by filtering the water of the River Lea before it reached the East End","by driving the smell of the river away from the Houses of Parliament"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"The sewers were built to abolish a smell."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"they removed sewage from the stretch of river that the water companies drank from"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the sewers were built to abolish a smell, and that in doing so they removed sewage from the river the water companies drank from. The builders aimed at the air; the benefit came through the water.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 11 says the sewers were built to abolish a smell, which is miasma reasoning, not Snow's.",null,"Wrong link: the Lambeth move in paragraph 8 concerned one company's intake, not the sewers.","Wrong paragraph: the River Lea is tied to the 1866 East End epidemic and to Farr, not to Bazalgette's sewers.","Half right: driving away the smell was the goal, but the passage credits the result to removing sewage from the water supply."]},{"id":"rc-o10-q16","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the method the passage credits to Snow in paragraph 12?","choices":["A chemist isolates a bacterium from a sample of river water and grows it in culture","A city builds new sewers after a hot summer makes the river's smell unbearable","A physician notes that patients with a fever recover faster in rooms with open windows","An inspector condemns a well because its brickwork is cracked and a cesspool lies nearby","A farmer sows two fields with the same seed, waters only one, and compares the yields"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"Hold everything constant but the one suspected factor, count the dead on each side, and let the ratio speak."},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"two groups that differed in one respect only"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 states the method: hold everything constant but the one suspected factor, count the outcome on each side, and let the ratio speak. Two fields alike in everything but watering, with the yields compared, is that method in a new setting.","distractorNotes":["Wrong method: isolating the organism is what Koch did in paragraph 3, and it is exactly what Snow could not do.","Wrong method: that is the Great Stink response from paragraph 11, action without a comparison.","Wrong method: nothing is held constant; the physician is noticing a pattern, not controlling for other differences.","Wrong method: that is the surveyor's inspection from paragraph 6, a finding about one site rather than a comparison between groups.",null]},{"id":"rc-o10-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["The removal of the Broad Street pump handle ended cholera in London and proved that miasma theory was false","Victorian physicians rejected Snow's theory because they were unwilling to abandon the sanitary reforms it threatened","Koch's isolation of the cholera bacterium finally gave Snow's followers the proof he had lacked","Snow's lasting contribution was a method for testing an unseen cause by comparison, not the famous pump","The miasma theory was harmless in practice because the sewers it inspired happened to remove cholera's true source"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"the pump was a demonstration, not the proof, and that the proof lay south of the river"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"What Snow had, and what his critics lacked, was a method for deciding between explanations when the cause itself could not be observed."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"That method, more than the pump handle, is his inheritance."}],"why":"The passage sets up miasma theory, tells the pump story and then deflates it as a demonstration rather than the proof, walks through the south London comparison, and closes by naming the comparative method as Snow's inheritance. Only the answer captures that arc.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 7 says the outbreak was already subsiding when the handle came off, and paragraph 11 says cholera returned in 1866.","Outside the passage: the author never gives that motive; paragraph 12 says the critics' objection was the unseen organism.","Outside the passage: Koch appears only in paragraph 3, and paragraph 11 credits vindication to Farr and the sewers.",null,"Too strong and one detail only: paragraph 12 calls the miasmatists wrong about the mechanism, and the sewers are one irony in paragraph 11, not the thesis."]}]},"rc-o11":{"id":"rc-o11","title":"Zero, and the Conditions Attached","topic":"superconductivity","field":"physics","source":"original","words":1359,"paragraphs":[["In April 1911 the Dutch physicist Heike Kamerlingh Onnes, working in Leiden, cooled a thread of mercury to a few degrees above absolute zero and watched its electrical resistance vanish.","He could do this because in 1908 he had become the first person to liquefy helium, which boils at about 4.2 kelvin.","Physicists had long argued about what a metal's resistance would do near absolute zero: Lord Kelvin expected it to climb toward infinity as the electrons froze in place, others expected a smooth slide to zero, and still others expected a floor set by impurities.","Mercury did none of these things: its resistance fell gradually and then, within a narrow band near 4.2 kelvin, dropped abruptly to a value too small to measure.","Onnes named the state superconductivity and received the 1913 Nobel Prize in Physics, mainly for the low-temperature work behind it."],["The word zero is meant literally.","A current started in a copper ring dies away in a fraction of a second as collisions turn its energy into heat.","A current started in a superconducting ring has been followed for years with no measurable decay.","The temperature below which this happens is called the critical temperature, and for the metals Onnes knew it is painfully low: about 4.2 kelvin for mercury, 7.2 for lead, and 9.2 for niobium, the highest of any pure element at ordinary pressure.","The best ordinary conductors never join the club: copper, silver, and gold have not been seen to superconduct at any temperature yet reached.","Superconductivity is thus not good conduction carried to an extreme but a different condition of matter."],["Onnes saw the practical prize at once.","A coil with no resistance could carry a huge current without heating and produce a field far stronger than any iron magnet, and he spoke openly of building such magnets.","The hope collapsed almost immediately.","A magnetic field above a modest threshold, now called the critical field, destroyed the superconducting state, and so did a current above a critical value, since a current produces a field of its own.","For lead and mercury the critical field is a small fraction of a tesla, far below what a useful magnet would need."],["The second surprise came in 1933, when Walther Meissner and Robert Ochsenfeld in Berlin measured the magnetic field around tin and lead samples cooled through their transitions.","The field was pushed out of the interior: a superconductor is not merely a perfect conductor but a perfect diamagnet, one that expels magnetic flux.","The distinction is subtle, and to my mind it is the deeper of the two discoveries.","A perfect conductor cooled in a magnetic field would simply trap whatever flux was already inside it, because currents induced at its surface would oppose any change.","A superconductor does something a perfect conductor cannot: it expels the field even when it was cooled with the field already present."],["The expulsion is not always total.","In pure metals such as lead the field is kept out completely until the critical field is reached, and then superconductivity collapses all at once; these are type I superconductors.","In many alloys and compounds, by contrast, the field enters above a lower critical field, but only as thin threads, each carrying one quantum of flux, and superconductivity survives between the threads up to an upper critical field of many teslas; these are type II.","Type II behavior is why hospital MRI magnets are wound from a niobium-titanium alloy, which stays superconducting in fields of several teslas at about 4 kelvin, the temperature of liquid helium."],["Explaining any of this took forty-six years.","Albert Einstein, Niels Bohr, and Werner Heisenberg each tried and failed, and by the 1950s the problem had a reputation for embarrassing theorists.","The decisive clue came in 1950 from experiments on isotopes of mercury: samples with heavier nuclei had a slightly lower critical temperature.","Electrons should not care about nuclear mass unless the nuclei are moving, so the result pointed to vibrations of the crystal lattice.","In 1957 John Bardeen, Leon Cooper, and John Robert Schrieffer assembled the theory that now carries their initials.","In the BCS picture an electron moving through the lattice tugs nearby positive ions toward it, leaving a brief patch of extra positive charge that draws in a second electron, so two electrons that should repel each other end up weakly bound in a Cooper pair.","The pairs settle into one collective quantum state, an energy gap opens above it, and because scattering a single electron would require breaking a pair, the current flows undisturbed.","The three shared the 1972 Nobel Prize in Physics, Bardeen's second; he remains the only person to have won it twice."],["The theory proved sound for every superconductor then known, explaining the isotope effect, the energy gap, and the Meissner effect at once.","It also seemed to set a ceiling.","Pairing by lattice vibrations is fragile, and most theorists concluded that it could not survive much above 30 kelvin, so the slow climb of the record, to about 23 kelvin in a niobium-germanium compound by 1973, looked like the approach to a wall.","Liquid helium, scarce and expensive, would always be required."],["In 1986 Georg Bednorz and Alex Muller, at an IBM laboratory near Zurich, reported superconductivity at about 35 kelvin in a ceramic oxide of lanthanum, barium, and copper.","Ceramics are usually insulators, and few had thought to look there, so the result met disbelief until other groups reproduced it.","The pair received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1987, the year after the discovery, unusually fast.","Early in 1987 Paul Chu of the University of Houston and Maw-Kuen Wu of the University of Alabama in Huntsville replaced lanthanum with the smaller atom yttrium and found a transition above 90 kelvin.","The number mattered because liquid nitrogen boils at 77 kelvin and is cheap, costing about what milk does.","A hastily arranged session at the American Physical Society's March 1987 meeting drew some two thousand physicists and ran past three in the morning; it is still called the Woodstock of physics."],["What followed was a wave of prediction: levitating trains, power grids with no losses, and cheap medical magnets were all said to be a few years away.","Looking back, I think the excitement was earned and the timetables were not.","The copper oxide superconductors, or cuprates, are brittle ceramics that cannot be drawn into wire as niobium-titanium can, and current is weakened where it crosses the boundaries between crystal grains.","Some promises have been kept in modest form: superconducting cables now carry power beneath a few cities, and cuprate filters have been used in cellular base stations.","The record at ordinary pressure, about 133 kelvin in a mercury-based cuprate found in 1993, has not moved since."],["There is a deeper embarrassment.","The cuprate electrons do form pairs, but nearly four decades on there is still no agreed account of what holds them together, and few believe lattice vibrations alone can do it at such temperatures.","Meanwhile the old BCS mechanism has staged a comeback.","In 2015 a group in Mainz, Germany, squeezed hydrogen sulfide to about 150 gigapascals, roughly a million and a half atmospheres, and found a critical temperature near 203 kelvin; a lanthanum hydride reached about 250 kelvin a few years later.","These hydrides appear to be conventional BCS superconductors in which light hydrogen atoms vibrate fast enough to bind pairs tightly, the loophole the 30 kelvin argument overlooked.","They are also useless outside a diamond anvil, and I would urge caution about the headlines that greet each new record: several recent claims of room-temperature superconductivity have been withdrawn after other laboratories could not reproduce them and questions arose about the data."],["More than a century after Leiden, the pattern is hard to miss.","The experiments came first, usually in a cold corner of a laboratory, and theory followed, sometimes by decades.","Each advance arrived from an unlikely direction: a poor conductor like mercury, an insulating ceramic, a gas crushed between diamonds.","That is worth remembering the next time someone announces that the search for a room-temperature superconductor has reached its limit."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o11-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Lord Kelvin expected that near absolute zero a metal's electrical resistance would","choices":["fall smoothly to zero as the metal cooled","rise toward infinity as the electrons froze in place","level off at a floor set by impurities","vanish abruptly within a narrow band of temperature near 4.2 kelvin","depend on the mass of the atoms' nuclei"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"Lord Kelvin expected it to climb toward infinity as the electrons froze in place"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 lays out three old predictions and attaches Kelvin's name to one of them: resistance climbing toward infinity as the electrons froze in place.","distractorNotes":["Wrong camp: paragraph 1 gives the smooth slide to zero to unnamed others, not to Kelvin.",null,"Wrong camp: the floor set by impurities is the third prediction in paragraph 1, not Kelvin's.","This is what mercury actually did, which paragraph 1 says matched none of the predictions.","Wrong paragraph: the dependence on nuclear mass is the 1950 isotope result in paragraph 6."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, which pure element has the highest critical temperature at ordinary pressure?","choices":["mercury","lead","copper","niobium","tin"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"9.2 for niobium, the highest of any pure element at ordinary pressure"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 lists the metals Onnes knew and names niobium, at 9.2 kelvin, as the highest of any pure element at ordinary pressure.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 puts mercury at about 4.2 kelvin, the lowest of the three listed.","Paragraph 2 puts lead at 7.2 kelvin, below niobium.","Paragraph 2 says copper has not been seen to superconduct at all.",null,"Tin appears in paragraph 4 as a Meissner sample; no critical temperature is given for it."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions copper, silver, and gold in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["show that superconductivity is not simply ordinary conduction carried further","explain why Onnes chose mercury rather than a more familiar metal","list the metals whose critical temperatures had been measured by 1911","argue that the best conductors will eventually be found to superconduct","illustrate how impurities set a floor beneath the resistance of ordinary metals"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"copper, silver, and gold have not been seen to superconduct"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"not good conduction carried to an extreme but a different condition of matter"}],"why":"Right after naming the three best ordinary conductors, paragraph 2 draws the lesson: superconductivity is not good conduction carried to an extreme but a different condition of matter. The three metals are there to make that point.","distractorNotes":[null,"Outside the passage: paragraph 1 never says why Onnes chose mercury.","Reversed: these are metals that have not been seen to superconduct, so they have no critical temperature to list.","Too strong and reversed: the passage says they have never been seen to superconduct and makes no prediction that they will.","Wrong paragraph: the impurity floor is one of the pre-1911 predictions in paragraph 1."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q04","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a wire of pure niobium would","choices":["superconduct in liquid nitrogen but not in liquid helium","lose its resistance at a higher temperature than any cuprate","carry a persistent current only if its crystal contains defects","stay superconducting in a field of several teslas","superconduct in liquid helium but not in liquid nitrogen"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"9.2 for niobium"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"helium, which boils at about 4.2 kelvin"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"liquid nitrogen boils at 77 kelvin"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 puts niobium's critical temperature at 9.2 kelvin. Paragraph 1 says liquid helium boils at about 4.2 kelvin, cold enough, and paragraph 8 says liquid nitrogen boils at 77 kelvin, far too warm.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: 77 kelvin is well above niobium's 9.2 kelvin, while 4.2 kelvin is below it.","Reversed: paragraphs 8 and 9 put cuprate transitions above 90 kelvin and up to about 133 kelvin, far above 9.2.","Outside the passage: nothing links persistent currents to crystal defects.","Unsupported: paragraph 3 says pure metals lose superconductivity in modest fields; several teslas is credited only to the niobium-titanium alloy in paragraph 5.",null]},{"id":"rc-o11-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Onnes's hope of building powerful magnets collapsed because","choices":["liquid helium could not be produced in quantities large enough to cool a coil","the mercury he used could not be drawn into a wire","modest magnetic fields destroyed the superconducting state","the current in his coils died away within a fraction of a second","iron magnets turned out to be stronger than any coil he could build"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"A magnetic field above a modest threshold, now called the critical field, destroyed the superconducting state"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the hope collapsed almost immediately because a field above a modest threshold destroyed the superconducting state, and a coil's own current makes a field.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: nothing is said about a shortage of liquid helium in Leiden.","Wrong paragraph: trouble drawing wire belongs to the cuprates in paragraph 9, not to Onnes's magnets.",null,"Wrong paragraph: a current dying away in a fraction of a second is the copper ring of paragraph 2.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says the coil would be far stronger than any iron magnet; the problem was the field, not the iron."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a superconductor differs from a perfect conductor in that a superconductor","choices":["expels a field even when cooled with that field already inside","traps whatever magnetic flux was present when it was cooled","allows a magnetic field to die away gradually within its interior","carries a persistent current for years with no measurable decay","keeps a magnetic field out only until a critical field is reached"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"it expels the field even when it was cooled with the field already present"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"A perfect conductor cooled in a magnetic field would simply trap whatever flux was already inside it"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says a perfect conductor cooled in a field would simply trap the flux inside it, while a superconductor expels the field even when it was cooled with the field already present. That is the difference Meissner and Ochsenfeld found.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: paragraph 4 says trapping the flux is what a perfect conductor would do.","Outside the passage: nothing says the field fades gradually inside the material.","Not a difference: zero resistance is shared by both, and paragraph 4 says a superconductor is not merely a perfect conductor.","Half right: paragraph 5 says type I materials keep the field out until the critical field, but that is not what separates them from a perfect conductor."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q07","type":"application","stem":"A laboratory cools a new material below the temperature at which its resistance vanishes while a weak magnetic field, well below any critical field, passes through it, and finds the field inside the sample unchanged after cooling. Based on the reasoning in paragraph 4, the laboratory should conclude that the material","choices":["is a type II superconductor in which flux has entered as thin threads","has a critical temperature above the boiling point of liquid helium","has reached its upper critical field and lost superconductivity","is behaving as a perfect conductor rather than as a superconductor","is a type I superconductor cooled below its critical temperature"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"A perfect conductor cooled in a magnetic field would simply trap whatever flux was already inside it"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"it expels the field even when it was cooled with the field already present"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 gives the test: a perfect conductor cooled in a field traps the flux, while a superconductor expels it even when cooled with the field present. A field that stays unchanged is the perfect-conductor result, so the sample has not shown the Meissner effect.","distractorNotes":["Wrong paragraph: paragraph 5 says the field enters a type II material only above a lower critical field, and the stem says the field is well below any critical field, so a superconductor should have expelled it.","Unrelated: nothing in the case says what coolant was used or where the transition sits.","Contradicted: the stem says the field is well below any critical field, and losing superconductivity would mean the resistance returned.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 5 says a type I superconductor keeps the field out completely below its critical field, not unchanged inside."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that MRI magnets are wound from a niobium-titanium alloy rather than from pure lead or mercury because","choices":["the alloy has a critical temperature well above the boiling point of liquid nitrogen","the alloy remains superconducting in fields that would destroy the pure metals","lead and mercury cannot be cooled below their critical temperatures with helium","the alloy is a type I superconductor that expels fields completely","pure metals have too few crystal defects to carry a persistent current"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"For lead and mercury the critical field is a small fraction of a tesla, far below what a useful magnet would need"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"stays superconducting in fields of several teslas"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says lead and mercury lose superconductivity in a small fraction of a tesla, far below what a useful magnet needs, and paragraph 5 says the niobium-titanium alloy stays superconducting in fields of several teslas. The field tolerance is the difference.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 5 says the alloy works at about 4 kelvin, the temperature of liquid helium, far below nitrogen's 77 kelvin.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 2 puts lead and mercury at 7.2 and 4.2 kelvin, and Onnes reached those temperatures with liquid helium.","Reversed: paragraph 5 says the alloys and compounds that tolerate high fields are type II, not type I.","Outside the passage: nothing links persistent currents to crystal defects."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q09","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions Einstein, Bohr, and Heisenberg in paragraph 6 primarily to","choices":["credit the physicists whose early ideas led to the BCS theory","show that the isotope experiments of 1950 confirmed their predictions","suggest that theorists had lost all interest in superconductivity by the 1950s","contrast the fame of these physicists with the obscurity of Bardeen","convey how difficult the problem of explaining superconductivity had proved"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"each tried and failed, and by the 1950s the problem had a reputation for embarrassing theorists"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"Explaining any of this took forty-six years."}],"why":"Paragraph 6 opens by saying the explanation took forty-six years, then names three of the most famous physicists of the century and says each tried and failed. The names measure the difficulty.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the passage says they failed; it does not connect their ideas to BCS.","Wrong link: paragraph 6 says the isotope effect pointed to lattice vibrations, not to anything these three predicted.","Too strong: a problem that embarrasses theorists is one they keep attacking, and BCS arrived in 1957.","Outside the passage: Bardeen is presented as a two-time Nobel winner, hardly obscure.",null]},{"id":"rc-o11-q10","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are described in the passage as part of the BCS picture EXCEPT:","choices":["a passing electron tugs nearby positive ions toward itself","two electrons become weakly bound into a Cooper pair","the two electrons attract each other through their charges","an energy gap separates the paired state from any excited state","scattering a single electron would require breaking a pair"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"tugs nearby positive ions toward it","choice":0},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"weakly bound in a Cooper pair","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":7,"quote":"an energy gap opens above it","choice":3},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":7,"quote":"scattering a single electron would require breaking a pair","choice":4},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"two electrons that should repel each other"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the two electrons should repel each other; what binds them is the patch of positive charge left in the lattice, not any attraction between their own charges. The other four statements are all in paragraph 6.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 6 says the electron tugs nearby positive ions toward it.","Paragraph 6 ends its description with the electrons weakly bound in a Cooper pair.",null,"Paragraph 6 says an energy gap opens above the collective state.","Paragraph 6 says scattering a single electron would require breaking a pair."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q11","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, the word 'sound' most nearly means","choices":["audible","well founded","straightforward","popular","undamaged"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The theory proved sound for every superconductor then known"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the theory proved sound and then lists what it explained at once; the sentence is about the theory holding up, so sound means well founded.","distractorNotes":["Audible is the noise meaning of the word, and nothing in the sentence is heard.",null,"Simplicity is not what the sentence tests; it tests whether the theory held up against every known case.","Popularity is not mentioned; the sentence is about the theory explaining the evidence.","Undamaged is a physical sense of the word, as in sound timber, and does not fit a theory."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the 1987 transition above 90 kelvin was reached by","choices":["squeezing the lanthanum compound under enormous pressure","reproducing the Zurich result in several laboratories","cooling the lanthanum compound in liquid nitrogen rather than helium","substituting copper for barium in the Zurich ceramic","replacing lanthanum with the smaller atom yttrium"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"replaced lanthanum with the smaller atom yttrium and found a transition above 90 kelvin"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says Chu and Wu replaced lanthanum with the smaller atom yttrium and found a transition above 90 kelvin.","distractorNotes":["Wrong paragraph: squeezing under enormous pressure is the hydride work of paragraph 10.","Reversed sequence: reproduction confirmed the 35 kelvin result; it did not raise the transition.","Reversed cause and effect: the transition above 90 kelvin is what made liquid nitrogen usable, not the other way around.","Mash-up: copper was already part of the Zurich oxide; the substitution in paragraph 8 was yttrium for lanthanum.",null]},{"id":"rc-o11-q13","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of the discovery reported by Bednorz and Muller EXCEPT:","choices":["It occurred above the boiling point of liquid nitrogen.","It was made at an IBM laboratory near Zurich.","It involved a ceramic oxide containing copper.","It was met with disbelief when first reported.","It brought the two physicists a Nobel Prize the following year."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"at an IBM laboratory near Zurich","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"a ceramic oxide of lanthanum, barium, and copper","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"the result met disbelief","choice":3},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1987, the year after the discovery","choice":4},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"about 35 kelvin"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"liquid nitrogen boils at 77 kelvin"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 puts the Zurich transition at about 35 kelvin and says liquid nitrogen boils at 77 kelvin; it was the yttrium material of 1987 that crossed that line. The other four statements are all in paragraph 8.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 8 opens with the IBM laboratory near Zurich.","Paragraph 8 names a ceramic oxide of lanthanum, barium, and copper.","Paragraph 8 says the result met disbelief until other groups reproduced it.","Paragraph 8 says the pair received the prize in 1987, the year after the discovery."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the predictions that followed the 1987 discoveries is best described as","choices":["scornful","nostalgic","measured","indifferent","credulous"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"I think the excitement was earned and the timetables were not"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"Some promises have been kept in modest form"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 gives the predictions their due, calling the excitement earned, then faults the timetables and tallies what was and was not delivered. That is a measured verdict, neither mocking nor starry-eyed.","distractorNotes":["Too harsh: the author says the excitement was earned and credits promises kept in modest form.","Wrong register: the author is weighing the predictions against results, not longing for the moment.",null,"Too weak: the author takes a clear position on both the excitement and the timetables.","Reversed: the author says the timetables were not earned and urges caution about new headlines in paragraph 10."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q15","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the hydrogen sulfide experiment of 2015","choices":["produced the first superconductor with a critical temperature above 77 kelvin","was carried out at an IBM laboratory in Switzerland","confirmed that lattice vibrations cannot bind pairs above 30 kelvin","required a pressure of roughly a million and a half atmospheres","was later withdrawn when other laboratories could not reproduce it"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"about 150 gigapascals, roughly a million and a half atmospheres"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the Mainz group squeezed hydrogen sulfide to about 150 gigapascals, roughly a million and a half atmospheres, before finding a transition near 203 kelvin.","distractorNotes":["Wrong paragraph: the first material above 77 kelvin was the yttrium cuprate of 1987 in paragraph 8.","Wrong place: paragraph 10 puts the work in Mainz, Germany; the IBM laboratory near Zurich belongs to 1986.","Reversed: paragraph 10 says the hydrides are the loophole the 30 kelvin argument overlooked.",null,"Wrong result: the withdrawn claims in paragraph 10 are room-temperature claims, not the 203 kelvin measurement."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q16","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the hydride superconductors matter to theorists mainly because they","choices":["show that the cuprates must also be held together by lattice vibrations","show that lattice-mediated pairing can work far above 30 kelvin","prove that a room-temperature superconductor is within reach","confirm that heavier nuclei raise the critical temperature","demonstrate that the Meissner effect disappears under extreme pressure"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"conventional BCS superconductors in which light hydrogen atoms vibrate fast enough to bind pairs tightly, the loophole the 30 kelvin argument overlooked"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"most theorists concluded that it could not survive much above 30 kelvin"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says theorists thought pairing by lattice vibrations could not survive much above 30 kelvin. Paragraph 10 then calls the hydrides conventional BCS superconductors near 203 and 250 kelvin and names them the loophole that argument overlooked.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 10 says few believe lattice vibrations alone can explain the cuprates.",null,"Too strong: paragraph 10 says the hydrides are useless outside a diamond anvil and urges caution about room-temperature claims.","Reversed: paragraph 6 says heavier nuclei lowered the critical temperature, and the hydrides work because hydrogen is light.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about the Meissner effect under pressure."]},{"id":"rc-o11-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["The BCS theory of 1957 explained every superconductor then known, and the discoveries that followed have confirmed rather than challenged its central mechanism","Superconductivity became a practical technology only after the 1987 cuprates made cooling with inexpensive liquid nitrogen possible for the first time","Superconductors have remained laboratory curiosities because they must be kept extremely cold, shielded from strong fields, and limited to small currents","Superconductivity has advanced by experimental surprises that theory struggled to follow, and its promise has arrived more slowly than predicted","Room-temperature superconductivity is now within reach because hydrides under pressure have shown that lattice vibrations can bind pairs at high temperatures"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"The experiments came first, usually in a cold corner of a laboratory, and theory followed, sometimes by decades."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"Each advance arrived from an unlikely direction"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"the excitement was earned and the timetables were not"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"Explaining any of this took forty-six years."}],"why":"The passage runs from Leiden to the hydrides and closes by naming the pattern: experiments first, theory following by decades, each advance from an unlikely direction. Along the way it keeps weighing promise against delivery, from Onnes's magnets to the 1987 timetables.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 10 says there is still no agreed account of the cuprates, so later discoveries did challenge the mechanism.","Too narrow and contradicted: the MRI magnets in paragraph 5 use a helium-cooled alloy, and paragraph 9 says the cuprates are still hard to use.","Too extreme: paragraphs 5 and 9 describe MRI magnets, power cables, and filters in real use.",null,"Too strong: paragraph 10 says the hydrides are useless outside a diamond anvil and that room-temperature claims have been withdrawn."]}]},"rc-o12":{"id":"rc-o12","title":"The Sterile Stomach","topic":"helicobacter pylori and peptic ulcers","field":"medicine","source":"original","words":1367,"paragraphs":[["For most of the twentieth century, a patient with a peptic ulcer, a raw crater in the lining of the stomach or the duodenum just beyond it, was told a story about acid and nerves, with coffee, alcohol, tobacco, and spicy food cast as accomplices.","In 1910 the surgeon Karl Schwarz compressed that story into a maxim, \"no acid, no ulcer,\" and for seventy years it held.","In 1915 the Chicago physician Bertram Sippy prescribed hourly feedings of milk and cream to soak up the stomach's acid.","When rest and diet failed, surgeons cut the vagus nerve to reduce acid output or removed part of the stomach."],["The theory produced one genuine triumph.","In 1976 the pharmacologist James Black and his colleagues introduced cimetidine, which blocks the histamine receptors on the stomach's acid-making cells and so turns acid production down.","Ulcers that had festered for years healed within weeks, and Black shared the 1988 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine partly for this work.","Yet the triumph had a hollow center.","Cimetidine healed ulcers but did not cure the disease: once the drug was stopped, most ulcers returned within a year, and many patients simply took it indefinitely."],["One reason nobody looked for a germ was that everybody knew the stomach was sterile: its contents can reach a pH below 2, and acid that strong kills most bacteria quickly.","In the 1890s the Italian pathologist Giulio Bizzozero described spiral organisms in the stomachs of dogs, and others later reported similar shapes in human stomachs.","But in 1954 the American gastroenterologist Eddy Palmer examined more than a thousand stomach biopsies, found no bacteria in any of them, and concluded that the earlier sightings were contamination from the mouth.","His stains, it was later noted, were not the silver stains that show the organisms best, but his conclusion was what the field expected, and it stuck."],["The sighting that mattered came from Perth, in Western Australia.","In 1979 Robin Warren, a pathologist at Royal Perth Hospital, noticed curved bacteria in a biopsy from an inflamed stomach, and with a silver stain he kept finding them, always in the mucus near the surface cells and always beside inflamed tissue.","In 1981 Barry Marshall, a trainee in internal medicine looking for a research project, joined him.","Together they examined biopsies from 100 consecutive patients and found the bacteria in nearly every patient with active gastritis, the inflammation that precedes an ulcer, and nearly every patient with a duodenal or gastric ulcer.","Patients whose stomach lining was normal seldom carried them."],["Seeing the organism was one thing; growing it was another.","For months Marshall tried to culture it, treating it like Campylobacter, a known gut bacterium of similar shape, and routine called for discarding any plate that showed nothing after two days.","Over the Easter weekend of 1982 a batch of plates was left in the incubator for five days instead, and when it was checked, tiny colonies had appeared.","The organism was simply slow.","It was spiral, bore several whip-like flagella at one end, and grew only in air with reduced oxygen, a requirement microbiologists call microaerophilic.","It was first named Campylobacter pyloridis, after the pylorus, the muscular outlet where the stomach empties into the duodenum; in 1989, once it was clearly a genus of its own, it became Helicobacter pylori, the helical bacterium of the pylorus."],["How does it live where nothing was supposed to live?","The answer is an enzyme called urease, which the bacterium makes in abundance.","Urease splits urea, a waste compound present in gastric juice, into ammonia and carbon dioxide, and the ammonia neutralizes the acid in a small cloud around each cell.","Protected this way, the bacterium swims through the thick mucus that coats the stomach lining and settles against the surface cells, where the pH is close to neutral."],["The medical world did not rush to agree.","In 1983 the Gastroenterological Society of Australia rejected Marshall's summary of the findings, and a presentation abroad met with open skepticism.","The objections were serious ones.","The stomach was sterile, so the organisms were probably contaminants; the bacteria might simply be colonizing tissue that something else had damaged, so correlation was not cause; and the germ, it soon became clear, lived in many people with no ulcer at all.","Behind the arguments, I suspect, stood something less rigorous: a profitable industry built on acid suppression, and a profession that had learned its ulcer theory as settled fact.","Warren and Marshall's full paper, in The Lancet in 1984, concluded cautiously that the bacterium might be an important factor in the diseases."],["Marshall chose to answer the causation objection the oldest way.","In the 1880s the German bacteriologist Robert Koch had set out the tests for blaming a microbe for a disease, now called Koch's postulates: find it in every case, grow it in pure culture, produce the disease by giving it to a healthy host, and recover it from that host.","The organism had been found and grown; the third step required a healthy host, and attempts to infect laboratory animals had failed.","So in 1984, after an endoscopy confirmed that his own stomach was healthy, Marshall drank a culture of the bacterium taken from a patient.","Within about a week he was nauseated and vomiting, and a biopsy on the tenth day showed a stomach lining crowded with the organism and acutely inflamed.","After two weeks he took antibiotics and recovered; the account appeared in the Medical Journal of Australia in 1985.","I admire the nerve and distrust the method.","One volunteer who develops gastritis but no ulcer, which is what happened, does not prove that the germ causes ulcers; it showed that the organism could colonize a healthy stomach and inflame it, and that made the question impossible to dismiss."],["What settled the matter was duller and better: clinical trials.","Through the late 1980s and early 1990s, groups in Perth, Houston, Vienna, and elsewhere gave ulcer patients antibiotics, with or without acid suppression, and followed them for a year or more.","The pattern was consistent: ulcers healed with acid suppression alone came back in most patients within a year, while ulcers in patients whose bacteria had been eradicated almost never returned.","In 1994 a consensus panel convened by the National Institutes of Health concluded that ulcer patients carrying the bacterium should receive antibiotics, and later that year the World Health Organization's cancer agency classified it as a cause of stomach cancer."],["It would be satisfying to say the old theory was simply wrong, but the truth is more tangled, and more interesting.","Acid still matters.","When the bacterium settles in the antrum, the lower part of the stomach, it disturbs the hormonal signals that regulate acid and drives output up, which is one reason ulcers form downstream in the duodenum.","Schwarz's maxim was right about the mechanism and silent about the cause.","Nor is the germ the only culprit: aspirin and related painkillers damage the stomach lining directly, and most ulcers in people free of the bacterium are traced to those drugs.","Most puzzling of all, the bacterium infects roughly half the people on Earth, usually from childhood and usually for life, yet most carriers never develop an ulcer; lifetime estimates run between one in ten and one in five."],["In 2005 Warren and Marshall shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for discovering the bacterium and its role in gastritis and peptic ulcer disease.","The organism was not hiding; it had been seen in the 1890s, and anyone with a silver stain and a stubborn disposition could have found it in 1954.","What hid it was a sentence everyone could recite, and a treatment that worked just well enough to keep anyone from asking why the disease kept coming back.","Consensus is not evidence, and a theory that explains the mechanism of a disease has not necessarily found its cause.","The story is usually told as two outsiders against the establishment, and that is true as far as it goes, but I would put it differently: the establishment was not stupid, it was merely finished, and a finished question is the most dangerous kind."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o12-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Bertram Sippy's treatment for ulcers consisted of","choices":["cutting the vagus nerve to lower the stomach's output of acid","hourly feedings of milk and cream to soak up stomach acid","a drug that blocks histamine receptors on acid-making cells","removing the lower part of the stomach, where acid is produced","antibiotics combined with a drug that suppresses stomach acid"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"Bertram Sippy prescribed hourly feedings of milk and cream to soak up the stomach's acid"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says it directly: in 1915 Sippy prescribed hourly feedings of milk and cream to soak up the stomach's acid. The surgery and the drugs belong to other people and other years.","distractorNotes":["Wrong treatment: paragraph 1 gives the vagus nerve to surgeons, not to Sippy.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the histamine blocker is cimetidine, introduced by James Black in paragraph 2.","Wrong treatment: removing part of the stomach was the surgeons' last resort, and nothing says acid is made in the lower part.","Wrong era: antibiotics with acid suppression are the trials of paragraph 9, decades after Sippy."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q02","type":"function","stem":"The author describes cimetidine in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["argue that acid suppression should be abandoned as a treatment for ulcers","show that James Black deserved his Nobel Prize more than Warren and Marshall did","explain the chemistry by which histamine triggers the stomach's acid-making cells","present the acid theory's greatest success and expose its limit","introduce the clinical trials that later compared antibiotics with acid suppression"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"The theory produced one genuine triumph."},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"Cimetidine healed ulcers but did not cure the disease"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 opens by calling cimetidine the theory's one genuine triumph and closes by saying it healed ulcers but did not cure the disease. The drug is there to show how far the acid theory could go and where it stopped.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: paragraph 10 says acid still matters, so the author never argues for abandoning acid suppression.","Outside the passage: the author never compares the two prizes or ranks the winners.","Not the purpose: the paragraph names the receptor in passing and explains no chemistry.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the trials arrive in paragraph 9, and paragraph 2 does not mention them."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Eddy Palmer concluded in 1954 that","choices":["earlier sightings of stomach bacteria were contamination from the mouth","spiral organisms lived in the stomachs of dogs but not of humans","silver stains were too unreliable to detect bacteria in biopsies of the stomach","the stomach's acid was not strong enough to kill most bacteria","biopsies had to be examined within hours or any bacteria would die"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"concluded that the earlier sightings were contamination from the mouth"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says Palmer examined more than a thousand biopsies, found no bacteria, and concluded that the earlier sightings were contamination from the mouth.","distractorNotes":[null,"Mash-up: the dogs belong to Bizzozero in the 1890s, and others reported the shapes in humans too.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says Palmer did not use silver stains, not that he judged them unreliable.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says acid that strong kills most bacteria quickly, which is why the stomach was thought sterile.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about how quickly biopsies had to be examined."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q04","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, the word 'sterile' most nearly means","choices":["unable to produce offspring","lacking imagination or vitality","cleaned with heat or chemicals","barren and unproductive","free of living microbes"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":1,"quote":"everybody knew the stomach was sterile: its contents can reach a pH below 2, and acid that strong kills most bacteria quickly"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 explains the word in the same sentence: the acid kills most bacteria quickly, so sterile here means free of living microbes.","distractorNotes":["This is the reproductive sense of the word, and nothing in the sentence concerns offspring.","This is the figurative sense, as in a sterile debate, and the sentence is about acid and bacteria.","This describes a process done to instruments; the stomach is not cleaned, it is simply too acidic for microbes.","This is the sense used of land or soil, and the sentence is about germs, not productivity.",null]},{"id":"rc-o12-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if Palmer had used a silver stain in 1954, he would most likely have","choices":["found the bacteria only in patients who already had ulcers","confirmed that the organisms Bizzozero saw were oral contaminants","seen the organisms in at least some of the biopsies he examined","cultured the organism decades before Marshall managed to","been believed, since the field was ready for a new theory"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"were not the silver stains that show the organisms best"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"anyone with a silver stain and a stubborn disposition could have found it in 1954"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 notes that Palmer's stains were not the silver stains that show the organisms best, and paragraph 11 says outright that anyone with a silver stain could have found the bacterium in 1954. With the right stain, some of his thousand biopsies would have shown it.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: paragraph 4 finds the bacteria in gastritis as well as ulcers, and paragraph 10 says half the world carries it.","Reversed: a stain that shows the organisms would have undercut the contamination explanation, not confirmed it.",null,"Wrong step: a stain makes organisms visible under a microscope; growing them on plates is the separate problem of paragraph 5.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says his conclusion stuck because it was what the field expected, so a new finding would have met resistance."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, in Warren and Marshall's study of 100 patients, the bacteria were","choices":["present in nearly every patient who had active gastritis or an ulcer","present in every patient, including those whose stomach lining was normal","found only in patients with duodenal ulcers, not in those with gastric ulcers","cultured successfully from the biopsies of most of the patients","seen in dogs as well as in humans, confirming Bizzozero's report"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"found the bacteria in nearly every patient with active gastritis, the inflammation that precedes an ulcer, and nearly every patient with a duodenal or gastric ulcer"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the bacteria turned up in nearly every patient with active gastritis and nearly every patient with a duodenal or gastric ulcer, while patients with normal stomach lining seldom carried them.","distractorNotes":[null,"Contradicted: paragraph 4 says patients with normal stomach lining seldom carried the bacteria.","Contradicted: paragraph 4 names duodenal and gastric ulcers together.","Wrong paragraph: this study was about seeing the bacteria in biopsies; culturing came later and is the subject of paragraph 5.","Wrong paragraph: the dogs belong to Bizzozero in paragraph 3, and this study examined only human patients."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q07","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the organism was finally cultured in 1982 because","choices":["Marshall stopped treating it like Campylobacter and changed the growth medium","the incubator was set to a lower oxygen level over the holiday","the biopsies were taken from patients with ulcers rather than gastritis","a batch of plates was left in the incubator longer than routine allowed","the laboratory began using silver stains to check the plates for colonies"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"a batch of plates was left in the incubator for five days instead, and when it was checked, tiny colonies had appeared"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says routine called for discarding plates after two days, but over Easter 1982 a batch sat for five days, and that was when colonies appeared. The organism was simply slow.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 5 says he was treating it like Campylobacter, and no change of medium is mentioned.","Mash-up: the bacterium does need reduced oxygen, but paragraph 5 credits the extra time, not a change in the incubator.","Outside the passage: nothing links the breakthrough to which patients the biopsies came from.",null,"Wrong paragraph: silver stains are for seeing bacteria in tissue (paragraphs 3 and 4), not for checking plates."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q08","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are stated in the passage as features of Helicobacter pylori EXCEPT:","choices":["It has a spiral shape.","It carries flagella at one end.","It grows only in air with reduced oxygen.","It makes the enzyme urease in abundance.","It grows quickly on culture plates."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"It was spiral","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"bore several whip-like flagella at one end","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"grew only in air with reduced oxygen","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"which the bacterium makes in abundance","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"The organism was simply slow."}],"why":"Paragraph 5 gives the shape, the flagella, and the reduced-oxygen requirement, and paragraph 6 gives the urease. The one thing the passage says the opposite of is speed: the organism was simply slow, which is why two-day plates showed nothing.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 5: it was spiral.","Stated in paragraph 5: several whip-like flagella at one end.","Stated in paragraph 5: it grew only in air with reduced oxygen.","Stated in paragraph 6: the bacterium makes urease in abundance.",null]},{"id":"rc-o12-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, urease protects the bacterium by","choices":["thickening the mucus layer that coats the stomach lining","producing ammonia that neutralizes the acid around each cell","breaking down the acid itself into carbon dioxide and water","lowering the pH at the surface cells to a level the bacterium tolerates","propelling the bacterium through the mucus toward the surface cells"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"the ammonia neutralizes the acid in a small cloud around each cell"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, and the ammonia neutralizes the acid in a small cloud around each cell.","distractorNotes":["Wrong role: the mucus is the stomach's own coating, and paragraph 6 never says the enzyme changes it.",null,"Wrong chemistry: paragraph 6 says urease splits urea, not acid, and the products are ammonia and carbon dioxide.","Reversed: lowering pH means more acid; the enzyme's ammonia makes the surroundings less acidic.","Wrong part: swimming through the mucus is the job of the flagella in paragraph 5, not of an enzyme."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q10","type":"function","stem":"The author lists the objections in paragraph 7 primarily to","choices":["demonstrate that the skeptics had misread the Lancet paper","show that the scientific community was motivated purely by profit","grant that the doubters had real arguments before suggesting other motives","explain why Marshall's summary was rejected by the society in 1983","prepare the reader for the clinical trials that would answer each objection in turn"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"The objections were serious ones."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"Behind the arguments, I suspect, stood something less rigorous"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 introduces the list by calling the objections serious ones, and only after laying them out does the author add, I suspect, that something less rigorous stood behind them. The list gives the skeptics their due before the author's own suspicion.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: the Lancet paper is mentioned after the objections, and nothing says the skeptics misread it.","Too strong: the author calls the objections serious and offers profit only as a suspicion, not the whole explanation.",null,"Half right: the rejection is mentioned earlier in the paragraph, but the list is about why the field doubted, not about one society's decision.","Wrong paragraph: the trials in paragraph 9 settle the question overall; they are not matched to each objection in turn."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q11","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Marshall's self-experiment fell short of fulfilling Koch's third postulate for ulcers because","choices":["he never confirmed his stomach was healthy before drinking the culture","the bacterium he swallowed had been grown from a laboratory animal rather than a patient","he took antibiotics before the organism could be recovered from him","no endoscopy was performed until after the symptoms had already cleared","the disease the postulate required, a peptic ulcer, never developed in him"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"produce the disease by giving it to a healthy host"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":8,"quote":"One volunteer who develops gastritis but no ulcer, which is what happened, does not prove that the germ causes ulcers"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 states the third postulate as producing the disease in a healthy host, and then says Marshall developed gastritis but no ulcer. For ulcers, the disease in question never appeared, so the step was not completed.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 8 says an endoscopy confirmed his stomach was healthy before he drank the culture.","Contradicted: paragraph 8 says the culture was taken from a patient, and animals could not be infected at all.","Contradicted: the biopsy on the tenth day found the organism before he took antibiotics after two weeks.","Contradicted: the tenth-day biopsy showed a lining crowded with the organism and acutely inflamed, so it came during the illness.",null]},{"id":"rc-o12-q12","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward Marshall's self-experiment is best described as","choices":["admiring yet doubtful","openly contemptuous","coolly indifferent","wholly approving","bewildered and dismissive"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":7,"quote":"I admire the nerve and distrust the method."},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":8,"quote":"does not prove that the germ causes ulcers"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says it in one line: I admire the nerve and distrust the method. The author then explains why one volunteer proves little while still crediting the experiment with keeping the question alive.","distractorNotes":[null,"Too harsh: an author who admires the nerve is not contemptuous.","Too cool: the author takes a clear position and argues it in two directions.","Half right: the admiration is there, but the author also distrusts the method and says the experiment proved little.","Wrong register: the author explains exactly what the experiment did and did not show, with no confusion."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q13","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the clinical trials of the late 1980s and early 1990s showed that","choices":["antibiotics healed ulcers faster than acid suppression did","ulcers treated with antibiotics alone returned in most patients within a year","ulcers seldom returned once the bacteria had been eradicated","acid suppression was unnecessary once antibiotics were given","patients in Perth, Houston, and Vienna responded differently to treatment"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"ulcers in patients whose bacteria had been eradicated almost never returned"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 gives the pattern: ulcers healed with acid suppression alone came back in most patients within a year, while ulcers in patients whose bacteria had been eradicated almost never returned.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: the trials measured whether ulcers came back, and paragraph 9 says nothing about speed of healing.","Reversed: it was acid suppression alone that led to relapse in most patients.",null,"Too strong: paragraph 9 says antibiotics were given with or without acid suppression and never calls acid suppression unnecessary.","Reversed: paragraph 9 says the pattern across the groups was consistent."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q14","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that cimetidine failed to cure ulcers because it","choices":["was taken for too short a time to heal the crater completely","lowered acid without removing the organism that drove the disease","blocked histamine receptors on the bacterium rather than on the stomach cells","thickened the mucus layer in which the bacterium lives","suppressed acid so completely that the bacterium spread more easily"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"Cimetidine healed ulcers but did not cure the disease"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"ulcers healed with acid suppression alone came back in most patients within a year, while ulcers in patients whose bacteria had been eradicated almost never returned"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says cimetidine healed ulcers but did not cure the disease, and paragraph 9 explains the difference: acid suppression alone led to relapse, while eradicating the bacteria did not. Put together, the drug treated the acid and left the cause in place.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 2 says ulcers healed within weeks, and many patients took the drug indefinitely.",null,"Wrong target: paragraph 2 says the receptors are on the stomach's acid-making cells, not on any bacterium.","Outside the passage: nothing says cimetidine changes the mucus.","Outside the passage: no link between acid suppression and the spread of the bacterium is ever made."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q15","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of Helicobacter pylori infection EXCEPT:","choices":["It is found in roughly half the people on Earth.","It is usually acquired in childhood.","It usually lasts for life.","It leads to an ulcer in most carriers.","It can drive the stomach's acid output up."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"infects roughly half the people on Earth","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"usually from childhood","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"usually for life","choice":2},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"drives output up","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"most carriers never develop an ulcer"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the bacterium infects roughly half the people on Earth, usually from childhood and usually for life, yet most carriers never develop an ulcer, with lifetime risk between one in ten and one in five. The acid rise comes earlier in the same paragraph.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 10: roughly half the people on Earth.","Stated in paragraph 10: usually from childhood.","Stated in paragraph 10: usually for life.",null,"Stated in paragraph 10: in the antrum it disturbs the hormonal signals and drives acid output up."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q16","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle described in paragraph 11?","choices":["A homeowner whose basement floods each spring pumps it dry every time and never asks where the water comes from","A gardener whose roses keep wilting tests the soil, finds a fungus, and treats the roots before replanting them","A coach benches a star player after one bad game, certain that the player's talent has suddenly vanished","A pilot rechecks the fuel gauge before every flight even though it has never once given a false reading","A student memorizes the answers to last year's exam and is surprised to fail this year's entirely different test"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"a treatment that worked just well enough to keep anyone from asking why the disease kept coming back"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"a theory that explains the mechanism of a disease has not necessarily found its cause"}],"why":"Paragraph 11's principle is that a fix which manages the mechanism well enough can stop anyone from asking about the cause. Pumping out the water every spring is cimetidine; never asking where it comes from is the ulcer field before Warren and Marshall.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: the gardener hunts for the cause and treats it, which is what the ulcer field failed to do.","Wrong principle: this is a hasty judgment from one event, not a working fix that hides a cause.","Wrong principle: this is caution about a problem that has never occurred, with nothing recurring and nothing hidden.","Wrong principle: this is a failure to prepare for something new, not a recurring problem patched over without asking why."]},{"id":"rc-o12-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Barry Marshall's self-experiment was the decisive proof that bacteria cause peptic ulcers","The acid theory of ulcers was a mistake that delayed effective treatment for seventy years","A settled theory kept medicine from seeing the bacterial cause of ulcers that was in plain view","Peptic ulcers are now cured with antibiotics because Helicobacter pylori lives in the stomach's mucus","Warren and Marshall succeeded because they ignored the scientific establishment entirely"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":1,"quote":"One reason nobody looked for a germ was that everybody knew the stomach was sterile"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"The organism was not hiding"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"What hid it was a sentence everyone could recite"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"Consensus is not evidence"}],"why":"The passage opens with a theory everyone could recite, shows that the organism had been seen and ignored, follows the discovery through to the trials, and closes by saying that what hid the germ was consensus itself. Only one choice carries both the discovery and the lesson.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 8 says the self-experiment proved little, and paragraph 9 says the clinical trials settled the matter.","Too strong: paragraph 10 says acid still matters and that Schwarz's maxim was right about the mechanism.",null,"Too narrow: the mucus and the antibiotics are details from paragraphs 6 and 9, not the argument of the whole passage.","Outside the passage: they published in The Lancet and presented to the societies; nothing says they ignored the establishment."]}]},"rc-o13":{"id":"rc-o13","title":"The Bat Had Been Shouting","topic":"echolocation in bats","field":"zoology","source":"original","words":1348,"paragraphs":[["A small bat hunting over a pond may catch hundreds of insects on a night too dark to see a hand before the face, and its eyes play almost no part in it.","The explanation, that the bat shouts and listens for echoes, is taught to schoolchildren now, but it took roughly 150 years to travel from a strong suspicion to an accepted fact.","The story of that delay is, to my mind, as instructive as the biology, because the answer was essentially in hand in the 1790s and nobody could hear it."],["The first careful experiments were made in 1793 and 1794 by Lazzaro Spallanzani, an Italian priest and professor at the University of Pavia.","He began with an owl, which flew well in dim light but struck the walls once the room was fully dark.","Bats, by contrast, flew freely in total darkness, so he blinded several, covering their eyes or removing them outright.","The blinded animals still swerved around obstacles, and some that he later recaptured had stomachs full of freshly caught insects.","Whatever guided them, it was not sight."],["The next step came from Geneva, where the naturalist Louis Jurine learned of the work and, in 1794, plugged the ears of bats with wax.","Deafened, the animals blundered into obstacles they had cleared minutes earlier, and when the plugs came out they flew normally again.","Spallanzani repeated the experiment and confirmed it, concluding that the ears somehow did the work of the eyes, though he could not say how, since a flying bat seemed to make no sound at all.","The idea found few takers.","In 1800 the French anatomist Georges Cuvier judged the ear experiments unconvincing and proposed instead that the wings felt the air pushed back from nearby objects.","Cuvier's authority was enormous, and the touch theory held the field for more than a century."],["Two guesses in the early twentieth century came closer.","In 1912, shortly after the Titanic struck an iceberg, the inventor Hiram Maxim suggested that bats sensed obstacles with low-frequency sound from their wingbeats, below human hearing, and proposed a similar device for ships.","In 1920 the English physiologist Hamilton Hartridge argued the reverse: that bats emitted sounds above human hearing and steered by their echoes.","Hartridge had the physics right, but he had no instrument that could detect such sounds, and the suggestion sat in the literature untested for nearly two decades."],["The instrument turned up at Harvard.","The physicist George W. Pierce had built an apparatus that made high-frequency sound audible and was using it to study the songs of insects.","In 1938 Donald Griffin, then an undergraduate who had been banding bats to study their migrations, carried a cage of them into Pierce's laboratory.","Held in front of the detector, the silent-seeming animals were pouring out sound far above the roughly 20 kilohertz ceiling of human hearing.","Griffin and Robert Galambos, a graduate student working on hearing, then ran the decisive tests.","They strung fine wires across a darkened room and counted how often flying bats touched them.","Bats whose mouths were tied shut, and bats whose ears were plugged, struck the wires far more often than untouched animals.","The results were published in 1941, and in 1944 Griffin coined a word for the sense: echolocation."],["The physics explains why the sounds must be so high.","Sound moves through air at about 340 meters per second, so a 50 kilohertz call has a wavelength of roughly 7 millimeters, and sound reflects cleanly only from objects at least about that size.","A mosquito or a fine wire is invisible to a low note and vivid to a high one.","The price is range.","Air absorbs high frequencies far more strongly than low ones, so the higher a bat pitches its call, the sooner the echo fades into silence.","Every species therefore lives with a trade, sharper detail for shorter reach, and most bats first detect a small insect only a few meters away.","Distance itself comes from timing: an echo from a target 1.7 meters away returns 10 milliseconds after the call."],["Call design varies, and two broad patterns stand out.","Many bats use frequency-modulated calls: brief sweeps that start high and plunge downward through many pitches in a few thousandths of a second.","Horseshoe bats, which emit through their nostrils past a fleshy nose-leaf, do something different.","They hold a long, nearly constant note, a little above 80 kilohertz in the greater horseshoe bat, and listen for small changes in the echo's pitch.","The changes come from the Doppler shift, the effect that raises an approaching siren's pitch: an echo from something the bat flies toward comes back higher than the note it sent.","In 1968 Hans-Ulrich Schnitzler showed that a flying horseshoe bat lowers its outgoing note by just enough that the raised echo lands at the frequency where its hearing is sharpest, a trick now called Doppler shift compensation."],["While searching, a bat calls perhaps ten times a second.","Once it detects prey the rate climbs, and in the last fraction of a second before contact it rises to as many as 200 calls per second, a burst that researchers call the feeding buzz.","The calls are not whispers; the loudest species exceed 110 decibels close to the mouth, an obvious problem, since the bat must hear a faint echo a moment after shouting.","A small muscle in the middle ear contracts just before each call, damping its sensitivity, and relaxes within milliseconds so that the echo arrives at an ear that is open again."],["A sound that loud is also a broadcast, and some of the intended victims are listening.","In the late 1950s Kenneth Roeder and Asher Treat showed that many night-flying moths carry a pair of simple ears, each with only two sound-sensitive cells.","The more sensitive cell fires when a bat is still tens of meters away, well before the bat can detect the moth, and the moth responds by turning and flying directly away.","The second cell fires only when the sound grows loud, meaning the bat is close, and that sets off something quite different: the moth folds its wings and drops, or breaks into loops and spirals that no echo can predict.","Roeder worked much of this out at night in a field with a loudspeaker and a floodlight, and I admire the plainness of it: cheap equipment and patience exposed a contest no one had suspected."],["A few moths do more than listen.","Tiger moths carry tymbals, ridged plates on the thorax that buckle and snap back, and when a bat closes in, some species answer with bursts of ultrasonic clicks.","In 1965 Dorothy Dunning and Roeder played recorded clicks to bats trained to catch tossed mealworms, and the bats veered away from food they would otherwise have taken.","Three explanations have been offered, and they are not exclusive.","The clicks might startle a bat; they might warn it, since many tiger moths feed as caterpillars on plants that leave them foul-tasting; or they might jam its sonar by cluttering the moment the echo is due.","In 2007 Jesse Barber and William Conner found support for the warning idea: bats learned to avoid clicking moths that tasted bad, and then avoided palatable moths that clicked as well.","In 2009 Aaron Corcoran, Barber, and Conner showed that one species, Bertholdia trigona, clicks up to about 4,500 times per second, and that big brown bats attacking it kept trying and missing, a pattern that fits jamming rather than warning or startle."],["It is tempting to call all of this an arms race, and the metaphor is useful, but I distrust how neat it makes things.","Plenty of eared moths are eaten anyway, and clicking tiger moths are a small minority.","What the history shows more clearly than any metaphor is how much a science depends on its instruments.","Spallanzani and Jurine had the answer within reach in 1794, and it waited for a physicist's device for listening to insects.","The bat had been shouting the whole time."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o13-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Spallanzani's experiment with an owl showed that the owl","choices":["could navigate in total darkness as well as any bat","relied on its ears rather than its eyes to avoid walls","flew well in dim light but hit the walls in full darkness","refused to fly at all once the room was fully dark","caught insects in the dark as long as its eyes were uncovered"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"flew well in dim light but struck the walls once the room was fully dark"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says the owl flew well in dim light but struck the walls once the room was fully dark. That contrast with the bats is what sent Spallanzani further.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 2 says the owl struck the walls in full darkness while the bats flew freely.","Wrong animal: the ear finding belongs to the bats in paragraph 3, not to the owl.",null,"Too strong: the owl flew, and the passage says it struck the walls, not that it refused to fly.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about the owl catching insects."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q02","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of Spallanzani EXCEPT:","choices":["He first plugged bats' ears with wax.","He held a professorship at Pavia.","He removed or covered the eyes of several bats.","He recaptured blinded bats with full stomachs.","He confirmed Jurine's result by repeating it."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"professor at the University of Pavia","choice":1},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"covering their eyes or removing them outright","choice":2},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"some that he later recaptured had stomachs full of freshly caught insects","choice":3},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"Spallanzani repeated the experiment and confirmed it","choice":4},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":1,"quote":"Louis Jurine learned of the work and, in 1794, plugged the ears of bats with wax"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 gives the wax plugs to Louis Jurine of Geneva; Spallanzani repeated that experiment afterward. Pavia, the blinding, the recaptured bats, and the confirmation are all in paragraphs 2 and 3.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 2 calls him a professor at the University of Pavia.","Paragraph 2 says he covered the bats' eyes or removed them outright.","Paragraph 2 says some recaptured bats had stomachs full of freshly caught insects.","Paragraph 3 says Spallanzani repeated Jurine's experiment and confirmed it."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when Jurine plugged the ears of bats with wax, the bats","choices":["flew normally but stopped catching insects","made audible sounds for the first time","struck walls only when the room was also dark","collided with obstacles they had just avoided","refused to leave the ground until the plugs were removed"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"blundered into obstacles they had cleared minutes earlier"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the deafened bats blundered into obstacles they had cleared minutes earlier, and flew normally again once the plugs came out.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: paragraph 3 says nothing about the plugged bats' feeding, and they did not fly normally.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says a flying bat seemed to make no sound at all.","Mash-up: the dark room and the wall belong to the owl in paragraph 2.",null,"Too strong: paragraph 3 says the bats flew and blundered into obstacles; it never says they stayed on the ground."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q04","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, the word 'field' most nearly means","choices":["an open stretch of farmland","the dominant position in a dispute","a branch of scientific study","the area a sense can reach","the ground on which a battle is fought"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"the touch theory held the field for more than a century"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the touch theory held the field for more than a century right after noting Cuvier's enormous authority. To hold the field here is to remain the accepted view in the dispute.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday meaning of the word, but no land is involved in the sentence.",null,"A field of study is a tempting reading, but the sentence says a theory held the field against rivals, not that it was a discipline.","The sentence is about a theory's standing, not about how far a sense reaches.","The idiom comes from battle, but the sentence describes a scientific debate, not combat."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q05","type":"function","stem":"The author discusses Maxim and Hartridge in paragraph 4 primarily to","choices":["credit Maxim with the first correct account of how bats avoid obstacles","explain how the sinking of the Titanic led directly to the discovery of echolocation","contrast a physiologist's careful reasoning with an inventor's reckless guess","show that Cuvier's touch theory had been abandoned by 1912","show that the correct idea surfaced before an instrument could test it"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"Two guesses in the early twentieth century came closer."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"he had no instrument that could detect such sounds, and the suggestion sat in the literature untested"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 opens by saying two guesses came closer and closes by saying Hartridge's correct idea sat untested for nearly two decades because no instrument could detect the sounds. The paragraph is a bridge from suspicion to the instrument.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 4 says Hartridge, not Maxim, had the physics right.","Too strong: the Titanic prompted Maxim's suggestion, but paragraph 5 credits the discovery to a detector built for insect songs.","Outside the passage: the author never calls Maxim's guess reckless, and both men are presented as guessing.","Outside the passage: paragraph 4 never says the touch theory had been given up.",null]},{"id":"rc-o13-q06","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that bats seemed silent to Spallanzani and Jurine because","choices":["the bats they studied called only while hunting insects outdoors","bats produce sound only when their eyes are covered","the wax plugs muffled the sounds the bats produced","the bats fell quiet whenever they were handled by people","the calls were pitched above the range of human hearing"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"a flying bat seemed to make no sound at all"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"far above the roughly 20 kilohertz ceiling of human hearing"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says a flying bat seemed to make no sound, and paragraph 5 reveals why: the sound was far above the roughly 20 kilohertz ceiling of human hearing. Put the two together and the silence was in the listeners, not the bats.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: nothing suggests the bats called only outdoors; paragraph 5 says they called in a laboratory.","Outside the passage: the calls had nothing to do with whether the eyes were covered.","Reversed: the plugs went in the bats' ears, not their mouths, and the bats seemed silent even without plugs.","Outside the passage: paragraph 5 shows handled bats calling in front of the detector.",null]},{"id":"rc-o13-q07","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, in Griffin and Galambos's wire experiments, which bats struck the wires far more often than untouched animals?","choices":["Those with their mouths tied shut and those with their ears plugged","Those with their eyes covered and those with their ears plugged","Those flying in a lighted room and those flying in a fully darkened one","Those with their mouths tied shut and those with their eyes covered","Those held in front of the detector and those released in the room"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":7,"quote":"Bats whose mouths were tied shut, and bats whose ears were plugged, struck the wires far more often than untouched animals."}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says bats whose mouths were tied shut, and bats whose ears were plugged, struck the wires far more often than untouched animals: silencing the call or deafening the ear both broke the sense.","distractorNotes":[null,"Half right: plugged ears is one group, but paragraph 5 names tied mouths, not covered eyes, as the other.","Outside the passage: paragraph 5 describes only a darkened room.","Half right: tied mouths is one group, but the other had plugged ears; covered eyes belong to Spallanzani in paragraph 2.","Wrong detail: the detector in paragraph 5 revealed the calls; it was not a wire-test condition."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q08","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of Donald Griffin EXCEPT:","choices":["He banded bats as an undergraduate to study their migrations.","He carried bats into Pierce's laboratory in 1938.","He built the apparatus that first detected the bats' calls.","He published the wire experiments with Galambos in 1941.","He coined the word echolocation in 1944."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"an undergraduate who had been banding bats to study their migrations","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"carried a cage of them into Pierce's laboratory","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"Griffin and Robert Galambos, a graduate student working on hearing, then ran the decisive tests.","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":8,"quote":"The results were published in 1941, and in 1944 Griffin coined a word for the sense: echolocation.","choice":4},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"The physicist George W. Pierce had built an apparatus"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 credits the detector to the physicist George W. Pierce, who built it to study insect songs; Griffin only carried the bats into the laboratory. The other four statements are all in paragraph 5.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 says Griffin had been banding bats to study their migrations as an undergraduate.","Paragraph 5 says he carried a cage of bats into Pierce's laboratory in 1938.",null,"Paragraph 5 says Griffin and Galambos ran the tests and the results were published in 1941.","Paragraph 5 says that in 1944 Griffin coined the word echolocation."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, raising the pitch of an echolocation call","choices":["lets the call travel farther before its echo fades","reveals smaller objects but shortens the call's reach","lowers the speed at which the sound travels through air","makes the echo return sooner from a target at a given distance","increases the wavelength so fine wires reflect it more cleanly"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"the higher a bat pitches its call, the sooner the echo fades into silence"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"sharper detail for shorter reach"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 lays out the trade: a short wavelength makes a fine wire vivid, but air absorbs high frequencies faster, so the echo fades sooner. Sharper detail for shorter reach.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 6 says the higher the pitch, the sooner the echo fades.",null,"Outside the passage: the speed of sound in paragraph 6 is given as a constant, not something pitch changes.","Wrong mechanism: paragraph 6 ties echo timing to distance, not to pitch.","Reversed: paragraph 6 pairs the 50 kilohertz call with a short wavelength, and higher pitch means a shorter wave."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Schnitzler showed in 1968 that a flying horseshoe bat","choices":["raises its outgoing note so that the echo returns at a lower pitch","switches from a constant note to a downward sweep as it nears prey","emits its calls through the mouth rather than the nostrils","lowers its call so the echo lands where its hearing is sharpest","finds insects by timing the gap between its call and the echo"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"lowers its outgoing note by just enough that the raised echo lands at the frequency where its hearing is sharpest"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says Schnitzler showed that a flying horseshoe bat lowers its outgoing note by just enough that the raised echo lands where its hearing is sharpest, the trick called Doppler shift compensation.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 7 says the bat lowers its note, and the echo comes back higher.","Mash-up: downward sweeps are the frequency-modulated calls of other bats earlier in paragraph 7.","Reversed: paragraph 7 says horseshoe bats emit through their nostrils.",null,"Mash-up: timing the gap between call and echo is the distance method of paragraph 6, and Schnitzler's finding in paragraph 7 is about the pitch of the echo, not its timing."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q11","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if the small muscle in a bat's middle ear failed to relax after a call, the bat would most likely","choices":["be deafened by the loudness of its own call","produce its next call at a lower pitch than usual","miss the faint echo that returns a moment later","produce a feeding buzz earlier than usual","receive echoes shifted higher in pitch than the call"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"relaxes within milliseconds so that the echo arrives at an ear that is open again"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"the bat must hear a faint echo a moment after shouting"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the muscle contracts just before each call, damping the ear, and relaxes within milliseconds so the echo arrives at an ear that is open again. If it stayed contracted, the faint echo would arrive at a damped ear.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: a muscle stuck in contraction keeps the ear damped, so the bat would hear less, not more.","Unrelated: paragraph 8 ties the muscle to hearing, not to the pitch of the next call.",null,"Unrelated: the feeding buzz in paragraph 8 is driven by closing on prey, not by the ear muscle.","Wrong paragraph: the pitch shift is the Doppler effect of paragraph 7 and has nothing to do with the ear muscle."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the feeding buzz is","choices":["a call rate of about ten per second used while a bat searches for prey","the loudest call a bat produces, exceeding 110 decibels","a burst of clicks that a tiger moth produces as a bat closes in","a steady note that a horseshoe bat holds while listening for Doppler shifts","a burst of up to 200 calls per second just before a bat reaches its prey"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"it rises to as many as 200 calls per second, a burst that researchers call the feeding buzz"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says that in the last fraction of a second before contact the rate rises to as many as 200 calls per second, a burst that researchers call the feeding buzz.","distractorNotes":["Wrong phase: ten calls a second is the searching rate in paragraph 8, before prey is detected.","Mash-up: the 110 decibel figure in paragraph 8 is about loudness, not the rate of calls.","Wrong paragraph: clicks from tiger moths belong to paragraph 10.","Wrong paragraph: the steady note belongs to horseshoe bats in paragraph 7.",null]},{"id":"rc-o13-q13","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle described in paragraph 9?","choices":["A deer that walks away from a faint wolf scent but bolts in wild zigzags when the scent grows strong","A rabbit that freezes in exactly the same way whether the sound it hears is faint or loud","A flock of birds that takes off together the instant a hawk appears, however far away it is","A fish that swims toward a faint vibration to investigate and flees only when the predator is in view","A squirrel that chatters loudly at a cat to warn the other squirrels before climbing a tree"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"the moth responds by turning and flying directly away"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"sets off something quite different: the moth folds its wings and drops, or breaks into loops and spirals that no echo can predict"}],"why":"Paragraph 9's rule is a sensor with two thresholds: a faint signal means a distant threat and triggers an orderly move away, while a loud signal means a close threat and triggers wild, unpredictable evasion. The deer's calm retreat followed by zigzags is the same design.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong mechanism: paragraph 9 describes two different responses to faint and loud signals, not one response to everything.","Wrong mechanism: a single all-or-nothing response ignores the graded, two-threshold design of the moth's ear.","Reversed: the moth moves away from even a faint signal; it never approaches to investigate.","Wrong paragraph: signaling at a predator is the tiger moth idea of paragraph 10, not the two-cell ear of paragraph 9."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q14","type":"function","stem":"The author describes the 2009 experiment with Bertholdia trigona in paragraph 10 primarily to","choices":["prove that tiger moth clicks serve only to jam a bat's sonar","show that Dunning and Roeder's 1965 result could not be repeated","explain how tiger moths produce clicks with their tymbals","offer evidence that fits jamming better than the other explanations","argue that big brown bats never learn to avoid moths that taste foul to them"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":7,"quote":"a pattern that fits jamming rather than warning or startle"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"Three explanations have been offered, and they are not exclusive."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 presents three explanations and then says the Bertholdia trigona result, bats that kept trying and kept missing, fits jamming rather than warning or startle. The experiment is there to sort the explanations for that species.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: paragraph 10 says the three explanations are not exclusive, and the finding is tied to one species.","Outside the passage: nothing says the 1965 result failed; the 2009 work asks why clicks work, not whether they do.","Wrong detail: how tymbals make clicks is explained earlier in paragraph 10, before the experiment.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 10 says bats learned to avoid clicking moths that tasted bad."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a palatable tiger moth that clicks is protected from bats because","choices":["its clicks are too rapid for a bat to hear as separate sounds","its clicks startle bats, which never grow used to the sound","bats have come to associate clicking with a foul taste","it feeds as a caterpillar on plants that make it foul-tasting","its clicks clutter the moment when the echo is due"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"bats learned to avoid clicking moths that tasted bad, and then avoided palatable moths that clicked as well"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says bats learned to avoid clicking moths that tasted bad and then avoided palatable moths that clicked as well. The palatable moth is borrowing the lesson the bats learned from its foul-tasting relatives.","distractorNotes":["Wrong mechanism: that is the jamming idea, which paragraph 10 ties to Bertholdia trigona, not to the palatable mimics.","Too extreme and wrong mechanism: the passage never says bats fail to get used to clicks, and the 2007 finding rests on learning.",null,"Contradicted: the stem describes a palatable moth, so a foul taste cannot be the reason.","Wrong mechanism: jamming belongs to the 2009 Bertholdia trigona experiment, not to the 2007 learning result about palatable moths."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward describing the relationship between bats and moths as an arms race is best described as","choices":["qualified acceptance","open hostility","unreserved enthusiasm","complete indifference","amused confusion"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"the metaphor is useful, but I distrust how neat it makes things"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the arms race metaphor is useful but that the author distrusts how neat it makes things. That is a yes with a reservation attached: qualified acceptance.","distractorNotes":[null,"Too strong: the author calls the metaphor useful, which is not hostility.","Reversed: the author distrusts how neat the metaphor makes things, so the enthusiasm is anything but unreserved.","Wrong register: the author takes a position and explains it, which is the opposite of indifference.","Wrong register: there is no confusion in paragraph 11; the author names exactly what the metaphor leaves out."]},{"id":"rc-o13-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Spallanzani's blinded bats proved in 1794 that bats navigate by sound, and later scientists merely confirmed what he had found","Echolocation in bats was suspected long before it could be shown, and its history reveals how much science depends on its instruments","Georges Cuvier's touch theory delayed the discovery of echolocation by more than a century and should serve as a warning against trusting authority","The defenses of eared moths and clicking tiger moths show that bats are steadily losing an evolutionary arms race with their prey","Donald Griffin's coining of the word echolocation in 1944 was the single most important event in the scientific study of bats"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"it took roughly 150 years to travel from a strong suspicion to an accepted fact"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"the answer was essentially in hand in the 1790s and nobody could hear it"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"how much a science depends on its instruments"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the answer was essentially in hand in the 1790s and nobody could hear it, and paragraph 11 closes on how much a science depends on its instruments. The passage walks from suspicion to proof to mechanism and back to that lesson.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: paragraph 3 says Spallanzani could not say how the ears worked, and paragraphs 5 to 10 are far more than confirmation.",null,"Too narrow: Cuvier's theory is one episode in paragraph 3, and paragraph 11 draws the lesson about instruments, not a warning about authority.","Contradicted: paragraph 11 distrusts the arms race framing and says plenty of eared moths are eaten anyway.","Too extreme: coining the word is one event in paragraph 5, and the passage never ranks it above the rest."]}]},"rc-o14":{"id":"rc-o14","title":"The Guests That Stayed","topic":"endosymbiotic origin of mitochondria and chloroplasts","field":"cell biology","source":"original","words":1363,"paragraphs":[["Nearly every cell of a plant, animal, or fungus carries mitochondria, small compartments that burn sugar with oxygen to power the cell, and plant and algal cells also carry chloroplasts, which capture sunlight.","Both, by the account now in every textbook, began as free-living bacteria that were swallowed by another cell more than a billion years ago and never left.","That account, the endosymbiotic theory, is so settled today that it is easy to forget how long it spent as a joke.","How it was proposed, mocked, forgotten, revived, and finally proved is a lesson in how evidence, rather than argument, moves a science."],["The first hints came from the microscope.","In 1883 the German botanist Andreas Schimper observed that chloroplasts are never built from scratch but arise only when an existing chloroplast splits in two, and he remarked in passing that green plants might have begun as a partnership between a colorless organism and a chlorophyll-bearing one.","The Russian botanist Konstantin Mereschkowski took the remark seriously.","He had spent years studying lichens, in which a fungus and an alga live as one body, and the lichen gave him a model for how two organisms might merge into one.","In 1905 he proposed that chloroplasts had begun as cyanobacteria taken in by a host cell, and in 1910 he coined the word symbiogenesis for the making of new life forms by merger."],["The idea for mitochondria arrived a generation later, and in worse company.","In the 1920s the American anatomist Ivan Wallin argued that mitochondria were bacteria and reported that he had grown them in culture outside the cell.","No one else could repeat the result, and the failure tainted the larger claim with the smaller.","Few biologists were willing to entertain the proposal after that.","In 1925 the American cell biologist Edmund Beecher Wilson wrote that such speculations seemed too fantastic for polite biological society, though he allowed that they might someday deserve serious thought.","For nearly four decades they did not get it."],["What changed the mood was not an argument but a finding.","In 1962 Hans Ris and Walter Plaut showed with the electron microscope that the chloroplast of the green alga Chlamydomonas contains DNA of its own.","The following year Margit Nass and Sylvan Nass reported fibers with the properties of DNA inside the mitochondria of chick embryos.","An organelle with its own genes looked less like a part than a resident, and the old speculation had something to stand on."],["The biologist who built on that footing was Lynn Margulis.","In 1967, not yet thirty and publishing under her married name, Lynn Sagan, she placed a long paper, On the Origin of Mitosing Cells, in the Journal of Theoretical Biology.","It is said to have been turned down by about fifteen journals first.","It argued that mitochondria descended from oxygen-breathing bacteria, that chloroplasts descended from cyanobacteria, and, more boldly, that the whiplike tails many eukaryotic cells swim with had descended from spirochetes, a group of corkscrew-shaped bacteria.","She expanded the case in a 1970 book, Origin of Eukaryotic Cells, and the package was named serial endosymbiosis, because the mergers were supposed to have happened one after another."],["The reception was cool, and the coolness was not entirely unfair.","A theory of ancient mergers predicts that the organelles should resemble particular bacteria in their molecules, and in 1967 there was no good way to check.","The tools arrived over the next two decades, largely through Carl Woese, who showed that ribosomal RNA, a molecule every cell carries, could be used to trace kinship among microbes.","When the organelles' own ribosomal RNA was read, mitochondria fell among the alpha-proteobacteria, a group that includes the typhus bacterium Rickettsia, and chloroplasts fell squarely among the cyanobacteria.","By the mid-1980s the organelle half of Margulis's theory was textbook material; she was elected to the National Academy of Sciences in 1983 and received the National Medal of Science in 1999.","The spirochete half never found comparable support, and I think the two halves deserve different grades: filing them under one name does her real achievement no favor."],["The classic case rests on four lines of evidence.","First, both carry DNA of their own, typically a circular molecule like a bacterial chromosome, not the linear kind in the nucleus.","Second, both are enclosed by two membranes rather than one.","Third, both make some of their own proteins on ribosomes that resemble bacterial ribosomes more than those in the surrounding cytoplasm.","Fourth, both reproduce by pinching in two, the way bacteria divide, and a cell that loses them cannot build new ones."],["The genomes are small, because most of the original bacterial genes have moved to the nucleus or been lost.","Human mitochondrial DNA is 16,569 base pairs long and holds 37 genes, of which 13 encode proteins; the rest encode the RNAs needed to make those 13.","A typical land plant chloroplast genome is larger, roughly 120,000 to 160,000 base pairs and a hundred or so genes, but it too is a remnant beside the several thousand genes of a free-living cyanobacterium.","The missing proteins are made in the cytoplasm and imported: a human mitochondrion takes in more than a thousand different proteins and makes only 13 itself."],["To my mind the ribosome evidence is the most persuasive, because it can be tested with a bottle of antibiotics.","Chloramphenicol, which halts protein synthesis in bacteria by binding their ribosomes, also halts protein synthesis inside mitochondria and chloroplasts while leaving the cell's cytoplasmic ribosomes untouched.","Cycloheximide does the reverse: it stops the cytoplasmic ribosomes and spares both the organelles and the bacteria.","A borrowed machine keeps the habits of its maker."],["The membranes deserve more caution than they usually get.","The tidy textbook drawing makes the inner membrane the bacterium's own and the outer one the pouch of host membrane that closed around it when it was swallowed.","But a mitochondrion's outer membrane is studded with porins, barrel-shaped channels of the kind found in the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria, and the bacteria mitochondria descend from are gram-negative, with two membranes of their own.","On that evidence the outer membrane may be bacterial as well, and the host's pouch, if there ever was one, may have been lost rather than kept."],["Division tells a similar story of inheritance with revision.","Chloroplasts split using FtsZ, the same protein that assembles into a ring at the middle of a dividing bacterium and draws it in two.","The mitochondria of animals and fungi have lost FtsZ and are pinched instead by a protein borrowed from the host cell's membrane-cutting toolkit, though a few algae still divide their mitochondria with the bacterial ring.","The guest, in other words, has been remodeled by the house, and the extent of remodeling records how long the arrangement has lasted."],["The experiment has also been run again, which is the strongest kind of evidence a historical theory can get.","Several groups of algae got their chloroplasts by swallowing not a cyanobacterium but a eukaryotic alga that already had one, and their chloroplasts sit inside three or four membranes rather than two, the extra layers being the remains of the engulfed cell.","More striking is a freshwater amoeba called Paulinella, which took in a cyanobacterium on its own, independently of the plant lineage and much more recently, on the order of a hundred million years ago.","Its partner still has a genome several times larger than any chloroplast's, as if the merger had been caught midway."],["Why did a correct idea wait sixty years for a hearing?","It is tempting to cast Mereschkowski as a prophet wronged by a timid profession, and I do not think that is quite right.","His proposal rested on resemblance, and resemblance is a weak witness: plenty of things divide by splitting without being bacteria.","Wallin's irreproducible cultures made matters worse, and until 1962 there was no fact in hand that resemblance alone could not explain.","The lesson is less flattering and more useful: a right answer can sit unproven for a lifetime, and the credit is fairly shared with whoever builds the instrument that finally asks the question properly."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o14-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Schimper observed in 1883 that chloroplasts","choices":["contain DNA of their own, like a bacterial chromosome","arise only when an existing chloroplast splits","resemble the alga that lives inside a lichen","can be grown in culture apart from the cell","are enclosed by two membranes rather than one"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"arise only when an existing chloroplast splits in two"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says Schimper observed that chloroplasts are never assembled from scratch but arise only when an existing chloroplast splits in two. That continuity was the first hint.","distractorNotes":["Wrong era: DNA inside chloroplasts was not shown until Ris and Plaut in 1962, in paragraph 4.",null,"Mash-up: lichens belong to Mereschkowski in paragraph 2, and no one says chloroplasts resemble the lichen's alga.","Wrong person and organelle: culturing outside the cell was Wallin's claim about mitochondria in paragraph 3.","Wrong paragraph: the double membrane is listed as evidence in paragraph 7, not as Schimper's observation."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q02","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions lichens in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["show that Schimper and Mereschkowski studied the same organisms","introduce the first organism known to contain a chloroplast","explain why cyanobacteria could survive inside a host cell","identify where Mereschkowski found a model for a merger","argue that fungi, like plants, descended from a bacterial partner"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"the lichen gave him a model for how two organisms might merge into one"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says Mereschkowski had spent years studying lichens, in which a fungus and an alga live as a single body, and that the lichen gave him a model for how two organisms might merge. The lichen is there to explain where his idea came from.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: Schimper is tied to chloroplasts in paragraph 2, not to lichens.","Outside the passage: nothing says lichens were the first organisms known to hold chloroplasts.","Wrong function: the lichen is a model for partnership, not an explanation of how a cyanobacterium survives inside a host.",null,"Too strong: the passage never claims that fungi descended from a bacterial partnership."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the claim by Ivan Wallin that no one else could repeat was that he had","choices":["grown mitochondria in culture outside the cell","found DNA fibers inside the mitochondria of chick embryos","traced mitochondria to the alpha-proteobacteria","shown that mitochondria reproduce by pinching in two","persuaded Wilson that mitochondria were bacteria"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"reported that he had grown them in culture outside the cell"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"No one else could repeat the result"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says Wallin reported that he had grown mitochondria in culture outside the cell, and that no one else could repeat the result.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong people and decade: the DNA fibers were reported by Margit and Sylvan Nass in 1963, in paragraph 4.","Wrong era: ribosomal RNA sequencing placed mitochondria there decades later, in paragraph 6.","Wrong source: division by pinching is one of the four lines of evidence in paragraph 7, not Wallin's experiment.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says Wilson found such speculations too fantastic for polite biological society."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q04","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Wilson's remark in 1925 was","choices":["a direct reply to the paper Margulis would later publish","the main reason the idea stayed dormant for decades","a defense of Wallin's attempts to culture mitochondria","an endorsement of Mereschkowski's proposal about chloroplasts","a dismissal that left room for a later change of mind"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"though he allowed that they might someday deserve serious thought"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says Wilson called such speculations too fantastic for polite biological society, though he allowed that they might someday deserve serious thought. He dismissed the idea for the present without closing the door on it.","distractorNotes":["Impossible timing: Margulis's paper came in 1967, more than forty years after the remark.","Too strong: paragraph 3 blames Wallin's unrepeatable result, and paragraph 13 blames the lack of any testable fact, not Wilson's remark.","Reversed: Wilson's remark belittled such speculations rather than defending them.","Reversed: calling an idea too fantastic for polite society is not an endorsement.",null]},{"id":"rc-o14-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Margit Nass and Sylvan Nass reported","choices":["that the chloroplast of Chlamydomonas contains DNA of its own","that mitochondria could be cultured apart from the cell","fibers with the properties of DNA inside mitochondria","that organelle ribosomes are blocked by chloramphenicol","that mitochondrial DNA forms a single circular molecule"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"reported fibers with the properties of DNA inside the mitochondria of chick embryos"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says that in 1963 Margit Nass and Sylvan Nass reported fibers with the properties of DNA inside the mitochondria of chick embryos.","distractorNotes":["Wrong team: the Chlamydomonas chloroplast finding belongs to Ris and Plaut in 1962.","Wrong person: that was Wallin's unrepeatable claim in paragraph 3.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the chloramphenicol test appears in paragraph 9 and is not credited to the Nasses.","Wrong paragraph: the circular shape is described in paragraph 7, not as the Nass finding."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Margulis's 1967 paper","choices":["appeared under the name Lynn Sagan","was published in a book titled Origin of Eukaryotic Cells","was accepted by the first journal that considered it","argued that chloroplasts descended from spirochetes","was written after she joined the National Academy"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"publishing under her married name, Lynn Sagan"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says she published the 1967 paper under her married name, Lynn Sagan, in the Journal of Theoretical Biology.","distractorNotes":[null,"Mash-up: the book came in 1970; the 1967 paper appeared in the Journal of Theoretical Biology.","Reversed: paragraph 5 says it was turned down by about fifteen journals first.","Mash-up: spirochetes were proposed as the source of swimming tails and hairs, not of chloroplasts.","Reversed chronology: paragraph 6 says she was elected in 1983, long after the paper."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q07","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are stated in the passage about Margulis's theory EXCEPT:","choices":["It held that mitochondria descended from oxygen-breathing bacteria.","It held that chloroplasts descended from cyanobacteria.","Its mergers were supposed to have happened one after another.","Its organelle half was textbook material by the mid-1980s.","Its spirochete half was confirmed by ribosomal RNA sequencing."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"mitochondria descended from oxygen-breathing bacteria","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"chloroplasts descended from cyanobacteria","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"the mergers were supposed to have happened one after another","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"By the mid-1980s the organelle half of Margulis's theory was textbook material","choice":3},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"The spirochete half never found comparable support"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the spirochete half never found comparable support. The other four statements come straight from paragraphs 5 and 6.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 5 as the first of the paper's three claims.","Stated in paragraph 5 as the second of the paper's claims.","Stated in paragraph 5 as the reason for the name serial endosymbiosis.","Stated in paragraph 6, in the same sentence as her election to the National Academy.",null]},{"id":"rc-o14-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the organelle half of Margulis's theory won acceptance mainly because","choices":["Margulis had been elected to the National Academy of Sciences","tools arriving after 1967 let its prediction be tested","Rickettsia was shown to be the agent of typhus","Carl Woese publicly endorsed the serial endosymbiosis theory","the spirochete half of the theory had already been confirmed"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"in 1967 there was no good way to check"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"The tools arrived over the next two decades"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"By the mid-1980s the organelle half of Margulis's theory was textbook material"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the theory made a prediction that could not be checked in 1967, that the tools arrived over the next two decades, and that once the organelles' ribosomal RNA was read the organelle half became textbook material. Acceptance followed the test.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the election in 1983 came as the theory was being accepted, not as its cause.",null,"Irrelevant detail: Rickettsia is mentioned only as a relative of mitochondria, not as a reason for acceptance.","Outside the passage: Woese is credited with the ribosomal RNA method, not with endorsing the theory.","Contradicted: paragraph 6 says the spirochete half never found comparable support."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q09","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the spirochete half of Margulis's theory is best described as","choices":["admiring","indignant","unconvinced","disappointed","ambivalent"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"The spirochete half never found comparable support, and I think the two halves deserve different grades"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the spirochete half never found comparable support and that the two halves deserve different grades. The author is not persuaded by that half, and says so plainly.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author's admiration goes to the organelle half, which the spirochete half is set against.","Wrong register: the author is calm and measured, not angry.",null,"Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 6 expresses regret or let-down, only a verdict on the evidence.","Too weak: the author gives a clear verdict rather than wavering between views."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q10","type":"except","stem":"According to paragraph 7, all of the following are among the classic lines of evidence for the theory EXCEPT:","choices":["DNA of their own, typically circular","ribosomes that resemble bacterial ribosomes","reproduction by pinching in two","enclosure by two membranes rather than one","a genome larger than a bacterium's"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"both carry DNA of their own, typically a circular molecule","choice":0},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"ribosomes that resemble bacterial ribosomes","choice":1},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"both reproduce by pinching in two","choice":2},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"both are enclosed by two membranes rather than one","choice":3},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"The genomes are small"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lists four lines: their own DNA, two membranes, bacteria-like ribosomes, and division by pinching in two. Paragraph 8 says the genomes are small, remnants beside a free-living bacterium's, so a large genome is the opposite of what the passage says.","distractorNotes":["Listed first in paragraph 7.","Listed third in paragraph 7.","Listed fourth in paragraph 7.","Listed second in paragraph 7.",null]},{"id":"rc-o14-q11","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, human mitochondrial DNA","choices":["is roughly 120,000 to 160,000 base pairs long","encodes more than a thousand different proteins","carries a hundred or so genes","holds 37 genes, of which 13 encode proteins","is several times larger than a chloroplast genome"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"holds 37 genes, of which 13 encode proteins"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 gives the figures: human mitochondrial DNA is 16,569 base pairs long and holds 37 genes, of which 13 encode proteins.","distractorNotes":["Wrong organelle: that range is the land plant chloroplast genome in paragraph 8.","Reversed: paragraph 8 says the mitochondrion imports more than a thousand proteins and makes only 13 itself.","Wrong organelle: a hundred or so genes is the chloroplast figure in paragraph 8.",null,"Reversed: the chloroplast genome is the larger one; the several-times-larger genome belongs to Paulinella's partner in paragraph 12."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q12","type":"application","stem":"A newly discovered antibiotic halts protein synthesis in bacteria by binding to their ribosomes. If the antibiotic were added to living plant cells, which outcome does the rule in paragraph 9 predict?","choices":["Synthesis would halt everywhere in the cell, because all of its ribosomes are the same kind","Synthesis would halt inside the organelles but continue in the cytoplasm","Synthesis would halt in the cytoplasm but continue inside the organelles","The organelles would stop dividing, because division depends on bacterial ribosomes","Nothing would change, because antibiotics act only on free-living bacteria"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"also halts protein synthesis inside mitochondria and chloroplasts while leaving the cell's cytoplasmic ribosomes untouched"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"A borrowed machine keeps the habits of its maker."}],"why":"Paragraph 9's rule is that a drug which binds bacterial ribosomes, like chloramphenicol, also halts protein synthesis inside mitochondria and chloroplasts while leaving the cytoplasmic ribosomes untouched. A new antibiotic that works the same way should sort the cell the same way.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 9 says the cytoplasmic ribosomes are left untouched because they are a different kind.",null,"Reversed: that is the cycloheximide pattern, the opposite of a bacterial ribosome blocker.","Outside the passage: paragraph 9 is about protein synthesis, and division is handled by FtsZ and its substitutes in paragraph 11.","Contradicted: the whole point of paragraph 9 is that the organelles respond like bacteria."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q13","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, the word 'entertain' most nearly means","choices":["give serious consideration to","amuse with a performance","host as a guest in one's home","defend in public against critics","keep alive for a long time"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Few biologists were willing to entertain the proposal after that."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says few biologists were willing to entertain the proposal after Wallin's result failed. To entertain a proposal is to consider it seriously; the sentence is about ideas, not amusement.","distractorNotes":[null,"This is the everyday meaning, but a proposal is not a show, and the sentence is about taking an idea seriously.","Hosting a guest is another common meaning that makes no sense with a proposal as the object.","Too strong: the sentence is about mere willingness to consider the idea, not public defense of it.","Wrong sense: the passage says the idea went unconsidered, not that it was kept alive."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q14","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, chloroplasts divide using","choices":["a protein borrowed from the host cell's membrane-cutting toolkit","porins of the kind found in gram-negative bacteria","the same protein that forms a ring in a dividing bacterium","a mechanism that has been lost in every alga","ribosomes that resemble those of bacteria"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"Chloroplasts split using FtsZ, the same protein that assembles into a ring at the middle of a dividing bacterium"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says chloroplasts split using FtsZ, the same protein that assembles into a ring at the middle of a dividing bacterium and draws it in two.","distractorNotes":["Wrong organelle: the host's membrane-cutting protein pinches the mitochondria of animals and fungi.","Wrong paragraph: porins sit in the mitochondrial outer membrane in paragraph 10 and have nothing to do with division.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 11 says a few algae still use the bacterial ring even for their mitochondria.","Wrong line: ribosomes are evidence about protein synthesis, not the machinery of division."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that an alga whose chloroplast is wrapped in four membranes most likely","choices":["took in a cyanobacterium more recently than plants did","is a close relative of the amoeba Paulinella","has a chloroplast genome several times larger than usual","acquired its chloroplast by engulfing another eukaryote","lost the two membranes that its ancestors originally had"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"their chloroplasts sit inside three or four membranes rather than two, the extra layers being the remains of the engulfed cell"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says some algae got their chloroplasts by swallowing a whole eukaryotic alga that already had one, and that such chloroplasts sit inside three or four membranes, the extra layers being the remains of the engulfed cell. Four membranes point to that second route.","distractorNotes":["Wrong case: the recent, independent uptake of a cyanobacterium is Paulinella's story, and it involves no extra membranes.","Outside the passage: nothing links the four-membrane algae to Paulinella.","Wrong case: the oversized genome belongs to Paulinella's partner, not to secondhand chloroplasts.",null,"Reversed: the four-membrane chloroplast has gained layers, not lost them."]},{"id":"rc-o14-q16","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions Paulinella in paragraph 12 primarily to","choices":["show that amoebas, like plants, can carry out photosynthesis","argue that chloroplasts arose more recently than mitochondria","explain why some chloroplasts sit inside three or four membranes","show that a merger can be reversed once it has begun","offer an independent and more recent instance of the same event"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"The experiment has also been run again, which is the strongest kind of evidence a historical theory can get."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"which took in a cyanobacterium on its own, independently of the plant lineage and much more recently"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 opens by saying the experiment has been run again, the strongest evidence a historical theory can get, and Paulinella is its most striking example: a cyanobacterium taken in independently of plants and much more recently.","distractorNotes":["Half right at best: Paulinella's partner is a cyanobacterium, but the paragraph is about repetition of the merger, not about which organisms photosynthesize.","Outside the passage: no comparison of the ages of chloroplasts and mitochondria is made.","Wrong sentence: the extra membranes are explained by the swallowing of a eukaryotic alga, before Paulinella appears.","Contradicted: the merger is described as caught midway, not reversed.",null]},{"id":"rc-o14-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Lynn Margulis was the first scientist to propose that mitochondria and chloroplasts had descended from free-living bacteria","An idea long dismissed, that organelles began as swallowed bacteria, was vindicated once evidence could test it","Mitochondria and chloroplasts are the only parts of the cell that contain DNA of their own","Biologists of the early twentieth century were too timid to accept a correct idea about the cell","Antibiotics provide the only reliable test of whether an organelle was once a free-living bacterium"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"began as free-living bacteria that were swallowed by another cell more than a billion years ago and never left"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"a lesson in how evidence, rather than argument, moves a science"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"a right answer can sit unproven for a lifetime"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 states the theory and frames the passage as a lesson in how evidence, rather than argument, moves a science; paragraph 13 closes on the same note. The body traces the dismissal, the revival, and the molecular proof.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 2 credits Mereschkowski in 1905, and paragraph 3 credits Wallin for mitochondria, both before Margulis.",null,"Too narrow and never claimed: the nucleus also holds DNA, and the organelles' DNA is one of several lines of evidence.","Reversed: paragraph 13 says the author does not think the profession's hesitation was simply timidity.","Too strong: paragraph 9 calls the antibiotic test the most persuasive, not the only reliable one."]}]},"rc-o15":{"id":"rc-o15","title":"What Photo 51 Could Say","topic":"x-ray evidence and the structure of dna","field":"history of science","source":"original","words":1366,"paragraphs":[["The most famous image in molecular biology is a dark field crossed by a blurred X of dashes; to an untrained eye, Photo 51 could pass for a flaw in the film.","It was recorded at King's College London in May 1952, and within a year it had helped settle the shape of DNA.","The story is usually told as the triumph of James Watson and Francis Crick in Cambridge, and their model deserves its fame.","But the model rested on measurements made by other people, and how they traveled between laboratories has been argued over ever since."],["Friedrich Miescher isolated DNA from the nuclei of white blood cells in 1869, but for decades nobody took it seriously.","The biochemist Phoebus Levene later identified its parts: a sugar, a phosphate group, and the four bases adenine, guanine, cytosine, and thymine.","Levene proposed that the four bases simply repeated in a fixed order along the chain, the tetranucleotide hypothesis, and a molecule that monotonous seemed too dull to carry hereditary instructions.","Proteins, built from some twenty different amino acids, looked a far better candidate.","The mood changed in 1944, when Oswald Avery and his colleagues showed that purified DNA could pass a heritable trait between strains of bacteria."],["The tetranucleotide hypothesis fell to careful chemistry.","In the late 1940s Erwin Chargaff at Columbia University used paper chromatography to separate and measure the four bases in the DNA of many species.","First, the proportions of the bases differed from one species to another, which a fixed repeating unit could not allow.","Second, in every sample the amount of adenine closely matched the amount of thymine, and guanine matched cytosine.","These became known as Chargaff's ratios; he reported them without explaining them."],["Shape was a question for X-rays: when a beam passes through a fiber of roughly parallel long molecules, the regularly spaced atoms scatter it into a pattern of spots and arcs on film.","The pattern is not an image of the molecule; it is a map of the distances that repeat inside it, and turning it back into a structure takes mathematics and judgment.","In 1938 William Astbury and Florence Bell at Leeds recorded the first X-ray patterns from DNA fibers and found a strong repeat of 3.4 angstroms along the fiber axis, but their fibers were too poorly ordered to say more."],["The problem passed to King's College London, where the physicist John Randall ran a biophysics unit.","His deputy, Maurice Wilkins, and a graduate student, Raymond Gosling, had already obtained sharper DNA patterns than Astbury's.","In January 1951 Rosalind Franklin joined the unit.","Trained as a physical chemist at Cambridge, she had spent three years in Paris learning to apply X-ray methods, and Randall assigned her the DNA problem, with Gosling as her student.","Wilkins seems to have expected her to work as his assistant; she understood from Randall's letter that the problem was now hers.","The misunderstanding was never repaired."],["Franklin found that DNA fibers take two forms: at lower humidity the molecule packs into an ordered, crystalline arrangement she called the A form, and with more water it loosens into the less ordered B form of living cells.","Controlling the moisture, she and Gosling photographed each form alone, and in May 1952 Gosling, under her direction, recorded the B form image that became Photo 51.","To anyone trained in the new diffraction theory of helices, the picture was eloquent.","The X of dashes meant a helix; the spacing between the dashes gave the distance the helix climbs in one full turn, 34 angstroms; the heavy smudges at top and bottom gave the 3.4 angstrom spacing between stacked bases; and the width of the pattern gave the molecule's diameter, about 20 angstroms."],["Franklin did not rush to build a model.","The B form was plainly helical, but the A form promised more exact numbers, and she set out to extract them by Patterson analysis, a slow method that assumes nothing in advance.","She distrusted guesswork, and in July 1952 she and Gosling circulated a mock death notice for the crystalline DNA helix, a joke often read, unfairly, as denying that DNA was helical at all.","What she lacked was not insight but a reason to hurry."],["Watson and Crick, at Cambridge's Cavendish Laboratory, worked in the opposite spirit.","Following the example of Linus Pauling, who had found the alpha helix of proteins in 1951 by building models, they guessed the structure first and tested it against the X-ray data afterward.","Their first attempt, in November 1951, was a three-chain helix with the phosphates on the inside.","Franklin, visiting from London, pointed out that it allowed for only a fraction of the water her measurements showed the fibers to contain, and the model was abandoned.","Lawrence Bragg, the laboratory's director, ordered the pair to leave DNA to King's.","They were let back in early in 1953, after Pauling produced a three-chain model with the same flaw and seemed likely to correct it."],["Three pieces of evidence then reached Watson and Crick.","In January 1953 Wilkins showed Watson Photo 51 without telling Franklin, and Watson, who knew the helical theory, read the X at a glance.","In February Max Perutz of the Cavendish passed Crick a committee report with Franklin's measurements, among them the symmetry of the A form crystals, from which Crick deduced that the two chains ran in opposite directions.","Finally Jerry Donohue, a chemist sharing their office, corrected the textbook forms of the bases Watson had been using.","With the right forms, Watson found in late February that an adenine-thymine pair and a guanine-cytosine pair were the same width, so any sequence fit between two evenly spaced backbones.","Chargaff's ratios fell out of the geometry at once: if adenine always faces thymine, the two must be equally abundant."],["The result appeared in Nature on April 25, 1953, in about nine hundred words, alongside papers from Wilkins's group and from Franklin and Gosling.","Watson and Crick described two chains wound around a common axis, backbones outside and paired bases inside, joined by hydrogen bonds between the bases, and added a sentence quoted ever since: the specific pairing immediately suggested a possible copying mechanism for the genetic material.","They acknowledged being stimulated by a general knowledge of unpublished results from King's; they did not say how much they had seen.","Franklin had left for Birkbeck College in London, where she turned to the structure of viruses.","She died of ovarian cancer in April 1958, at the age of thirty-seven.","In 1962 the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine went to Watson, Crick, and Wilkins; a prize is shared by at most three people and is not given to anyone who died before being nominated, so the question of whether she would have been included was never put."],["The argument over credit began in 1968 with Watson's memoir The Double Helix, which treated Franklin as an obstacle.","Anne Sayre's biography of 1975 answered it, and a story took shape in which Franklin's photograph was stolen and her discovery taken from her.","That story is too simple, and I think it does her a disservice.","Her Birkbeck colleague Aaron Klug, reading her notebooks, showed that she had worked out several features of the double helix herself and was, in his judgment, close to the full structure.","She was less a victim of theft than a contributor whose share was quietly forgotten, a harder wrong to put right."],["What strikes me most is how little a single image could settle.","Photo 51 said helix and gave three numbers; it did not say how many chains there were, which way they ran, or how the bases fit together.","Those answers came from the symmetry in Franklin's crystallographic report, from Chargaff's ratios, from Donohue's chemistry, and from two impatient men who kept building wrong models until one was right.","The double helix was the meeting point of two methods, the patient measurement that Franklin practiced and the bold guessing that she distrusted, and neither would have finished the job alone.","That is a less satisfying story than a theft or a triumph, but I suspect it is the true one."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o15-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the tetranucleotide hypothesis made DNA seem an unlikely carrier of hereditary instructions because","choices":["it suggested that DNA occurred only in the nuclei of blood cells","it held that DNA contained far fewer parts than the proteins it was compared with","it pictured the four bases repeating in a fixed order along the chain","it showed that the proportions of the bases differed from species to species","it proposed that the molecule was a single short chain rather than a long one"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"Levene proposed that the four bases simply repeated in a fixed order along the chain"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says Levene proposed that the four bases simply repeated in a fixed order along the chain, and that a molecule that monotonous seemed too dull to carry hereditary instructions.","distractorNotes":["Wrong detail: white blood cells are where Miescher isolated DNA in paragraph 2, not part of Levene's hypothesis.","Half right: proteins had more building blocks, but the hypothesis was about the order of the bases, not a count of parts.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 3 says Chargaff's finding that the proportions differ is exactly what the hypothesis could not allow.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about the length of the chain."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Chargaff's measurements showed that","choices":["the amount of adenine in a sample closely matched the amount of thymine","the four bases were present in equal amounts in the DNA of every species","the DNA of all species contained the same proportions of the four bases","adenine and thymine together made up a fixed fraction of every sample","the bases could be separated only after the sugar had been removed"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"the amount of adenine closely matched the amount of thymine"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 lists his two findings, and the second is that in every sample the amount of adenine closely matched the amount of thymine, and guanine matched cytosine.","distractorNotes":[null,"Too strong: only the paired bases matched each other; nothing says all four were present in equal amounts.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says the proportions differed from one species to another.","Outside the passage: no fixed fraction for adenine plus thymine is mentioned.","Outside the passage: paper chromatography separated the bases, and nothing is said about removing the sugar first."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author describes how X-ray diffraction works in paragraph 4 primarily to","choices":["argue that Astbury's photographs were more accurate than those taken later at King's","show that the technique was invented specifically to study the shape of DNA","explain why Franklin preferred to study the A form rather than the B form","establish that a diffraction pattern must be interpreted rather than simply viewed","demonstrate that Astbury's fibers were too poorly ordered to reveal the structure"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"The pattern is not an image of the molecule; it is a map of the distances that repeat inside it"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the pattern is not an image of the molecule but a map of repeating distances, and that turning it back into a structure takes mathematics and judgment. The description is there so the reader understands why reading Photo 51 took training.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 5 says Wilkins and Gosling obtained sharper patterns than Astbury's.","Outside the passage: the technique is described in general terms, and nothing says it was invented for DNA.","Wrong paragraph: Franklin's choice of the A form is explained in paragraph 7, and for a different reason.",null,"Wrong purpose: the poor order of Astbury's fibers is a detail added after the technique is explained, not the reason for explaining it."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the friction between Franklin and Wilkins arose because","choices":["Randall had removed Wilkins from the biophysics unit before Franklin arrived","each of them understood a different person to be in charge of the DNA work","Franklin refused to share Gosling's earlier photographs with Wilkins","Wilkins had been trained in Paris and distrusted her Cambridge methods","Franklin had publicly criticized the quality of Wilkins's diffraction patterns"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"Wilkins seems to have expected her to work as his assistant; she understood from Randall's letter that the problem was now hers."}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says Wilkins expected her to work as his assistant while she understood from Randall's letter that the problem was hers, and that this misunderstanding was never repaired.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: Wilkins is described as Randall's deputy and stayed in the unit.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing is said about Franklin withholding photographs.","Reversed: it was Franklin, not Wilkins, who had trained in Paris.","Outside the passage: no criticism of Wilkins's patterns, public or private, is mentioned."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q05","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following could be read from Photo 51 EXCEPT:","choices":["the distance the helix climbs in one full turn","the spacing between stacked bases","the diameter of the molecule","the fact that the molecule is a helix","the number of chains in the molecule"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"the spacing between the dashes gave the distance the helix climbs in one full turn","choice":0},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"the 3.4 angstrom spacing between stacked bases","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"the width of the pattern gave the molecule's diameter","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"The X of dashes meant a helix","choice":3},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"it did not say how many chains there were"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 lists what the picture gave: a helix, the 34 angstrom rise per turn, the 3.4 angstrom spacing between bases, and the 20 angstrom diameter. Paragraph 12 says outright that it did not say how many chains there were.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 6 says the spacing between the dashes gave the distance the helix climbs in one full turn.","Paragraph 6 says the smudges at top and bottom gave the spacing between stacked bases.","Paragraph 6 says the width of the pattern gave the molecule's diameter.","Paragraph 6 says the X of dashes meant a helix.",null]},{"id":"rc-o15-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, the word 'eloquent' most nearly means","choices":["fluently spoken","full of meaning","forcefully argued","pleasing to look at","hard to interpret"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"the picture was eloquent"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the picture was eloquent to anyone trained in the diffraction theory of helices, and the next sentence lists everything it told such a reader. The image spoke volumes in the sense of carrying meaning, not in the sense of speech.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday sense of the word, but a photograph does not speak.",null,"The picture is not making an argument; it is yielding information to a trained reader.","Paragraph 1 says the image could pass for a flaw in the film, so beauty is not the point.","Reversed: the sentence says the picture was clear to a trained reader, not hard to read."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q07","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the mock death notice circulated in July 1952 referred to","choices":["the A form of the molecule rather than DNA in general","the three-chain model built in Cambridge the previous year","the B form, which Franklin had not yet photographed","Astbury's early patterns from poorly ordered fibers","the end of Franklin's collaboration with Gosling"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"a mock death notice for the crystalline DNA helix"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"ordered, crystalline arrangement she called the A form"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the notice was for the crystalline DNA helix, and paragraph 6 identifies the crystalline arrangement as the A form. Put the two together and the joke was about the A form, which is why the author calls the broader reading unfair.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong target: the Cambridge model is in paragraph 8, and the notice concerns a form of DNA, not a model.","Contradicted: Photo 51, the image of the B form, was taken in May 1952, two months earlier.","Wrong paragraph: Astbury's patterns are in paragraph 4 and have nothing to do with the notice.","Contradicted: Gosling circulated the notice with her, so the collaboration continued."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Franklin objected to the first Cambridge model because it","choices":["put the phosphate groups on the outside of the helix","contained two chains when her data pointed to three","copied the three-chain structure that Pauling had already published","allowed for far less water than her measurements showed","placed the bases ten to a turn instead of eleven"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"it allowed for only a fraction of the water her measurements showed the fibers to contain"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says Franklin pointed out that the model allowed for only a fraction of the water her measurements showed the fibers to contain, and that the model was abandoned.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the first model had the phosphates on the inside.","Reversed: the model had three chains, and the passage never says her data pointed to three.","Wrong timing: Pauling's model came in early 1953, after the Cambridge attempt of November 1951.",null,"Outside the passage: no count of bases per turn figures in her objection."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q09","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions Linus Pauling's alpha helix in paragraph 8 primarily to","choices":["show that proteins had already been proven to be the true carriers of heredity","explain why Bragg ordered Watson and Crick to stop working on DNA","identify the method of model building that Watson and Crick were imitating","suggest that Pauling had already solved the structure of DNA by 1951","contrast the alpha helix of proteins with the double helix of DNA"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"Following the example of Linus Pauling, who had found the alpha helix of proteins in 1951 by building models"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 introduces Pauling as the example Watson and Crick were following: guess the structure by building a model, then test it against the data. The alpha helix is there as proof that the method could work.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 2 says Avery's experiment of 1944 changed the mood in favor of DNA over proteins.","Wrong cause: Bragg's order followed the failed model of November 1951, not Pauling's protein work.",null,"Contradicted: Pauling's DNA model of 1953 shared the flaw of the first Cambridge attempt.","Outside the passage: the author never compares the two helices."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Crick concluded that the two chains of DNA run in opposite directions from","choices":["the X-shaped pattern of dashes in Photo 51","the equal widths of the two kinds of base pair","Chargaff's finding that adenine always matched thymine","Donohue's correction of the forms of the bases","the crystal symmetry recorded in Franklin's report"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"the symmetry of the A form crystals, from which Crick deduced that the two chains ran in opposite directions"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the report Perutz passed on contained the symmetry of the A form crystals, from which Crick deduced that the two chains ran in opposite directions.","distractorNotes":["Wrong evidence: Photo 51 told Watson the molecule was a helix, and paragraph 12 says it did not say which way the chains ran.","Wrong step: the equal widths explained how any sequence fit between the backbones, not their direction.","Wrong step: Chargaff's ratios were explained by the pairing, not used to find the chain direction.","Wrong step: Donohue's correction led to the base pairing, not to the direction of the chains.",null]},{"id":"rc-o15-q11","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a species whose DNA contained noticeably more adenine than thymine would","choices":["have a correspondingly lower proportion of guanine than of cytosine","confirm the tetranucleotide hypothesis proposed by Levene","conflict with the base pairing in the Watson and Crick model","have a double helix whose diameter is wider than 20 angstroms","support Pauling's three-chain structure for the molecule"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"if adenine always faces thymine, the two must be equally abundant"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says that if adenine always faces thymine, the two must be equally abundant. A species with more adenine than thymine would break that rule, so it would be at odds with the model's pairing.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: nothing links an adenine surplus to the amounts of guanine and cytosine.","Wrong paragraph: the hypothesis was about a fixed repeating order and had already fallen to Chargaff's data in paragraph 3.",null,"Unrelated: the diameter came from Photo 51 and has nothing to do with base proportions.","Outside the passage: Pauling's model appears only as a flawed three-chain attempt; nothing links it to base proportions."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Watson and Crick paper of April 1953","choices":["acknowledged unpublished King's results without saying how much had been seen","was published several months before the papers from King's College London","described two chains held together by bonds between the sugar and phosphate backbones","ran to several thousand words and included Franklin's measurements in full","made no mention of any possible mechanism for copying the genetic material"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"They acknowledged being stimulated by a general knowledge of unpublished results from King's; they did not say how much they had seen."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says they acknowledged being stimulated by a general knowledge of unpublished results from King's, and that they did not say how much they had seen.","distractorNotes":[null,"Contradicted: paragraph 10 says the King's papers appeared alongside it.","Wrong detail: the chains were held together by hydrogen bonds between the paired bases, with the backbones outside.","Reversed: the paper was about nine hundred words, and the King's results were acknowledged only in general terms.","Contradicted: the paper's famous sentence proposed exactly such a copying mechanism."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that even if the Nobel committee of 1962 had wished to honor Franklin, it","choices":["would have had to remove Wilkins from the list to make room for her","would have been blocked by the standing rules of the prize itself","would have needed the consent of Watson and Crick to do so","would have waited until her notebooks had been examined by Klug","would have awarded the prize in chemistry instead of in medicine"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"a prize is shared by at most three people and is not given to anyone who died before being nominated, so the question of whether she would have been included was never put"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says a prize is shared by at most three people and is not given to anyone who died before being nominated, and the previous sentence says Franklin died in 1958. Both rules stood in the way, whatever the committee thought of her work.","distractorNotes":["Half right: the three-person limit is real, but she had died in 1958, so no reshuffling of the list could have included her.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing about consent appears.","Wrong timing: Klug's reading of her notebooks is in paragraph 11 and has no connection to the committee's rules.","Outside the passage: the field of the prize is never raised as an issue."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q14","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of Rosalind Franklin EXCEPT:","choices":["She trained as a physical chemist at Cambridge.","She spent three years in Paris learning X-ray methods.","She moved to Birkbeck College and worked on viruses.","She died in 1958 at the age of thirty-seven.","She shared the 1962 Nobel Prize with Wilkins."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"Trained as a physical chemist at Cambridge","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"she had spent three years in Paris learning to apply X-ray methods","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"left for Birkbeck College in London, where she turned to the structure of viruses","choice":2},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"She died of ovarian cancer in April 1958, at the age of thirty-seven.","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine went to Watson, Crick, and Wilkins"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the 1962 prize went to Watson, Crick, and Wilkins, and that Franklin, who had died in 1958, could not have been included. The other four statements come from paragraphs 5 and 10.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 says she trained as a physical chemist at Cambridge.","Paragraph 5 says she spent three years in Paris learning to apply X-ray methods.","Paragraph 10 says she left for Birkbeck College and turned to the structure of viruses.","Paragraph 10 says she died in April 1958 at the age of thirty-seven.",null]},{"id":"rc-o15-q15","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the story in which Franklin's photograph was stolen and her discovery taken is best described as","choices":["amused","unconvinced","outraged","uninterested","approving"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"That story is too simple, and I think it does her a disservice."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"That is a less satisfying story than a theft or a triumph, but I suspect it is the true one."}],"why":"Paragraph 11 calls the story too simple and says it does her a disservice, and paragraph 12 prefers a less satisfying account to a theft or a triumph. The author does not accept the stolen-photograph version.","distractorNotes":["Wrong register: the author treats the question of credit seriously throughout.",null,"Too strong: the author rejects the story calmly and offers a different reading, without anger.","Reversed: the author devotes two paragraphs to the question of credit.","Reversed: the author says the story is too simple and does Franklin a disservice."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q16","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle described in paragraph 12?","choices":["A detective solves a case from a single photograph that happens to show the culprit's face clearly","Two rival laboratories race toward the same result, and only the faster one is remembered","A surveyor refuses to publish a map until every hill has been measured twice","A search team finds a wreck where a careful sonar survey and a historian's bold guess overlap","A theorist's guess about a planet's orbit is accepted without any measurement"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"The double helix was the meeting point of two methods, the patient measurement that Franklin practiced and the bold guessing that she distrusted, and neither would have finished the job alone."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"how little a single image could settle"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says the structure came from two methods meeting, patient measurement and bold guessing, and that neither would have finished the job alone. A wreck found where a careful survey and a bold guess overlap is the same pattern.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 12 opens by saying how little a single image could settle.","Wrong principle: a race between rivals is the story the author argues against, not the meeting of two methods.","Half right: patient measurement alone is one of the two methods, and the author says neither finished the job alone.",null,"Half right: bold guessing alone is the other method, and the passage says it also could not finish the job by itself."]},{"id":"rc-o15-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Watson and Crick deserve sole credit for the double helix because they alone were willing to build and test models","Rosalind Franklin solved the structure of DNA on her own, and her photograph was simply stolen by her Cambridge rivals","The structure of DNA emerged from several people's evidence and from two methods that each needed the other","X-ray diffraction alone settled the shape of DNA once the technique had been perfected at King's","The Nobel committee of 1962 wronged Franklin by applying its rules too strictly to a case that deserved an exception"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"But the model rested on measurements made by other people"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"Those answers came from the symmetry in Franklin's crystallographic report, from Chargaff's ratios, from Donohue's chemistry"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"neither would have finished the job alone"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the model rested on measurements made by other people, and paragraph 12 names those contributions and calls the double helix the meeting point of two methods, neither of which would have finished the job alone. One choice holds both halves.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 1 says the model rested on other people's measurements, and paragraph 12 names them.","This is the story paragraph 11 calls too simple; the passage says she was close, not finished.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 12 says how little a single image could settle.","Unsupported and too narrow: the Nobel rules are stated without blame, and the credit debate is only part of the passage."]}]},"rc-o16":{"id":"rc-o16","title":"Colder Than Their Own Blood","topic":"antifreeze proteins in polar fishes","field":"biochemistry","source":"original","words":1347,"paragraphs":[["Seawater does not freeze at 0 degrees Celsius.","Its dissolved salts, about 35 grams per kilogram, push the freezing point down to roughly minus 1.9 degrees, and the coastal waters around Antarctica sit near that temperature for much of the year.","The blood of a typical marine bony fish is far less salty than the sea, about one third as concentrated, and freezes near minus 0.7 degrees.","A fish swimming in polar water is therefore colder than the freezing point of its own blood, a condition physicists call supercooling.","Supercooled liquid is stable only so long as nothing seeds it: a single ice crystal, touching the gills or swallowed with a mouthful of water, can freeze the whole fish."],["That the danger is real was shown in the 1950s by the physiologist Per Scholander and his colleagues, who studied fish in a deep Labrador fjord.","Fish living at the bottom, in water below the freezing point of their blood, were supercooled and perfectly healthy, because no ice reached that depth to seed them.","Brought up and touched with a sliver of ice, the same fish froze at once.","Fish near the surface in winter, where ice was everywhere, had blood that froze at a lower temperature than the deep fish's, and their salts did not explain all of the difference.","Something else in the blood was lowering its freezing point, and no one yet knew what."],["The answer came from the far end of the planet.","In the early 1960s Arthur DeVries, a Stanford graduate student, began working at McMurdo Station in Antarctica, where the water stays near minus 1.9 degrees all year and often holds ice.","Most fish there are notothenioids, a single group that dominates both the species and the biomass of the Antarctic shelf.","One of them, a small fish living directly under the sea ice, had blood that froze close to minus 2 degrees, and its salts explained only about half of that protection.","In 1969 DeVries and his adviser Donald Wohlschlag reported that when the serum was dialyzed, so that small molecules left through a membrane while large ones stayed behind, the extra freezing resistance stayed with the large molecules.","The agents were glycoproteins, proteins with sugars attached, and DeVries called them antifreeze glycoproteins."],["Their structure is absurdly simple: the protein backbone is a short motif of three amino acids, alanine, alanine, threonine, repeated over and over, and a pair of sugars hangs from each threonine.","What they do is stranger than what they are.","Salt lowers the freezing point of water in proportion to the number of dissolved particles, an effect chemists call colligative, and by that rule the glycoproteins, which are present in modest numbers of relatively heavy molecules, should barely register.","Instead they lower the temperature at which the blood freezes hundreds of times more than their numbers would predict."],["The clue to how lies in a quantity called thermal hysteresis.","Add a glycoprotein to water holding a small ice crystal and the temperature at which that crystal melts does not move; the temperature at which it grows drops by about a degree in the concentrations found in fish blood.","Between those two temperatures the crystal simply sits, neither growing nor shrinking, and that gap is the hysteresis.","The name antifreeze has always struck me as slightly misleading, and the hysteresis shows why.","The proteins do not prevent ice from forming the way salt on a winter road does; they prevent ice that already exists from getting any bigger, which for a fish that swallows the occasional crystal is the protection that matters."],["In 1977 DeVries and James Raymond proposed the mechanism that, in its essentials, still stands.","The glycoproteins adsorb, or stick, to particular faces of a growing ice crystal and stay there.","Water can still join the crystal in the gaps between bound proteins, but only as a surface that bulges outward, and a sharply curved ice front must be colder before it can advance.","A crystal held this way looks like two six-sided pyramids joined at their bases.","When the temperature finally falls below the hysteresis point, the restraint fails all at once, and ice shoots out of the pyramids' tips as fine needles."],["How a protein holds onto ice is a question the field answered once and then had to answer again.","The early picture was intuitive: hydroxyl groups on the threonines and sugars were thought to form hydrogen bonds with water molecules locked in the ice lattice, their spacing matching that of the lattice.","Later work, much of it from Peter Davies and his colleagues at Queen's University in Canada, found that the ice-binding face is flat and surprisingly water-repellent, and seems to order a layer of water into an ice-like arrangement that merges with the crystal.","The current account is more subtle than the first, and I suspect it is not the last."],["Notothenioids are not the only fish with antifreeze, and the other solutions look nothing like theirs.","The winter flounder of the North American Atlantic coast makes a small, alanine-rich protein coiled into a single helix; the sea raven makes a globular protein resembling a sugar-binding lectin; the ocean pout makes a compact protein unlike either.","Each binds ice and produces hysteresis, and each is built on a different fold.","The surprise was the Arctic cod.","Arctic cods carry a glycoprotein nearly indistinguishable in composition from the notothenioid one: the same three-amino-acid repeat, the same pair of sugars on each threonine."],["Were the two glycoproteins inherited from a shared ancestor?","In 1997 Liangbiao Chen, DeVries, and Chi-Hing Christina Cheng, all at the University of Illinois, answered no.","The notothenioid gene, they showed, had grown out of a gene for trypsinogen, a digestive enzyme precursor: it still carries pieces of the trypsinogen gene at both ends, while a nine-nucleotide stretch in between has been copied over and over to form the repeats.","Their molecular clock placed the gene's origin at roughly 5 to 14 million years ago, as the Southern Ocean cooled toward freezing.","The cod gene has no such pieces and no relation to trypsinogen, and later work from Cheng's laboratory traced it to DNA that had never coded for a protein.","Two lineages, starting from different raw material, had arrived at nearly the same molecule: convergent evolution at the level of a single protein."],["The solution came with obligations.","The glycoproteins are small enough to be lost through an ordinary fish kidney, and most Antarctic notothenioids have kidneys without glomeruli, the filtering units that would otherwise strain the proteins into the urine.","Because marine fish drink seawater to replace water lost to their saltier surroundings, ice can enter by the gut as well, and the intestinal fluid carries antifreeze too.","There is a stranger cost.","In 2014 a team including Cheng and DeVries reported that fish from McMurdo Sound carried ice crystals inside their bodies, including the spleen, and that the proteins that keep those crystals from growing also keep them from melting, even slightly above their normal melting point.","Summer water never warms enough to clear them, so a fish may carry its ice for life.","I find that a sobering footnote to a story usually told as a triumph: the proteins are an arrangement with ice, not a victory over it."],["The proteins have found work outside the fish.","One manufacturer uses a yeast-made version of the ocean pout protein to keep ice crystals small in ice cream, which gives low-fat varieties a smooth texture.","There are reasonable hopes for preserving transplant organs and frozen cells, where large crystals do the damage.","The promise of frost-proof crops, floated for decades, has delivered much less than advertised, and I would treat each new announcement in that line with patience rather than excitement.","What endures is the original observation: a graduate student on the ice, puzzled that fish lived in water colder than their blood should allow, found a protein that holds ice still, and six decades later it remains among the clearest examples of a molecule meeting a climate."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o16-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a marine bony fish swimming in polar seawater is described as supercooled because","choices":["its blood holds roughly three times as much salt as the surrounding sea","the sea around it has begun to turn into ice","it is colder than the point at which its own blood freezes","ice crystals touch its gills with every mouthful of water it takes in","its blood, unlike seawater, freezes at exactly 0 degrees Celsius"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"colder than the freezing point of its own blood, a condition physicists call supercooling"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says a fish in polar water is colder than the freezing point of its own blood, and that this condition is what physicists call supercooling.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 1 says fish blood is about one third as concentrated as seawater, not three times saltier.","Not the definition: supercooling describes the fish's own state, and paragraph 1 treats ice in the water as the danger that seeds freezing.",null,"Too strong: paragraph 1 says a single crystal can seed freezing, not that ice arrives with every mouthful.","Contradicted: paragraph 1 puts the freezing point of fish blood near minus 0.7 degrees; 0 degrees is the figure seawater is compared against."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the fish living at the bottom of the Labrador fjord survived in a supercooled state because","choices":["their blood froze at a lower temperature than that of fish near the surface","no ice reached the depth at which they lived to seed them","the salts in their blood matched those of the sea","an unidentified substance in their blood lowered its freezing point","the water at the bottom of the fjord was warmer than their blood's freezing point"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"because no ice reached that depth to seed them"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says the bottom-dwelling fish were supercooled and healthy because no ice reached that depth to seed them; the danger was ice, not the cold itself.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 2 says it was the surface fish whose blood froze at the lower temperature.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing says the deep fish had seawater-strength blood, and paragraph 1 says fish blood is far less salty.","Wrong fish: the unexplained extra protection belonged to the fish living near the surface in winter.","Contradicted: paragraph 2 says the deep fish lived in water below the freezing point of their blood."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author ends paragraph 2 with the remark that 'no one yet knew what' primarily to","choices":["cast doubt on the accuracy of Scholander's measurements","argue that salt alone explains a fish's resistance to freezing","suggest that the deep-water fish were not truly supercooled","set up the discovery described in the following paragraphs","explain why the surface fish froze more readily than the deep fish"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"Something else in the blood was lowering its freezing point, and no one yet knew what."},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":1,"quote":"The answer came from the far end of the planet."}],"why":"Paragraph 2 closes on an open question, and paragraph 3 opens with the words 'The answer came from the far end of the planet.' The remark is a hinge: it names the mystery that DeVries's work will solve.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author presents Scholander's results as reliable evidence that the danger is real.","Contradicted: the previous sentence says the salts did not explain all of the difference.","Contradicted: paragraph 2 says the deep fish were supercooled and perfectly healthy.",null,"Reversed: the surface fish were the better protected ones; their blood froze at a lower temperature."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the 1969 report by DeVries and Wohlschlag showed that","choices":["notothenioids make up most of the fish species on the Antarctic shelf","the blood of the fish beneath the ice froze at about minus 0.7 degrees","the small molecules that passed through the dialysis membrane carried the resistance","the freezing resistance stayed with the large molecules after dialysis","salts accounted for all of the blood's protection against freezing"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"the extra freezing resistance stayed with the large molecules"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says that when the serum was dialyzed, letting small molecules out through a membrane, the extra freezing resistance stayed with the large molecules; that is what the 1969 report showed.","distractorNotes":["Wrong attribution: the dominance of notothenioids is background in paragraph 3, not a finding of the 1969 paper.","Wrong number: minus 0.7 degrees is the figure for a typical marine fish in paragraph 1; the Antarctic fish's blood froze close to minus 2.","Reversed: the small molecules left and the resistance stayed behind with the large ones.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 3 says the salts explained only about half of the protection."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the antifreeze glycoproteins of notothenioids are built from","choices":["a single long helix rich in alanine with sugars attached at one end","a three-amino-acid motif, repeated, with a pair of sugars on each threonine","a globular protein that closely resembles a sugar-binding lectin","a chain of dissolved salts bound tightly to a protein backbone","a repeated motif of three sugars with a single amino acid attached to each one"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"a short motif of three amino acids, alanine, alanine, threonine, repeated over and over, and a pair of sugars hangs from each threonine"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 describes the backbone as a short motif of three amino acids, alanine, alanine, threonine, repeated over and over, with a pair of sugars hanging from each threonine.","distractorNotes":["Wrong fish: the alanine-rich single helix belongs to the winter flounder in paragraph 8.",null,"Wrong fish: the lectin-like protein is the sea raven's, in paragraph 8.","Outside the passage: salts are dissolved in the blood, and nothing binds them to the protein.","Reversed: the repeat is three amino acids, and the sugars come as a pair on each threonine."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 4, the word 'register' most nearly means","choices":["enroll on an official list of names","dissolve fully in the blood","show a feeling on one's face","be recorded by a measuring instrument","produce any noticeable effect"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"should barely register"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"Instead they lower the temperature at which the blood freezes hundreds of times more than their numbers would predict."}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says that by the colligative rule the few heavy glycoproteins should barely register, and the next sentence says that instead they lower the freezing point hundreds of times more than predicted. The word means to make a noticeable difference, not to enroll.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday meaning of the word, but no list or enrollment appears in the sentence.","Nothing in the sentence concerns whether the proteins dissolve; it concerns how much they change the freezing point.","A face registering emotion is another common use, but the sentence is about molecules in blood.","A gauge can register a reading, but the sentence is about the proteins' effect on the blood, not about any measurement.",null]},{"id":"rc-o16-q07","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if the glycoproteins worked only by the colligative rule, they would","choices":["lower the freezing point of the blood far less than they actually do","have no effect whatsoever on the freezing point of the blood","hold an ice crystal between its melting and growth temperatures","lower the freezing point of the blood in strict proportion to their weight","force a growing ice crystal into a shape like two joined pyramids"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"by that rule the glycoproteins, which are present in modest numbers of relatively heavy molecules, should barely register"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"hundreds of times more than their numbers would predict"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the colligative rule depends on the number of dissolved particles, that by that rule the few heavy glycoproteins should barely register, and that instead they do hundreds of times more. Take away the extra and only the small colligative share would remain.","distractorNotes":[null,"Too extreme: paragraph 4 says they should barely register under that rule, which is a small effect, not none.","Reversed: holding a crystal between two temperatures is the non-colligative behavior described in paragraph 5, not what the counting rule predicts.","Reversed: paragraph 4 says the colligative rule counts dissolved particles, not weight, and that the heavy molecules are too few to register.","Wrong mechanism: the pyramid shape in paragraph 6 comes from proteins sticking to crystal faces, not from the number of dissolved particles."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, adding antifreeze glycoprotein to water that holds a small ice crystal","choices":["lowers the temperatures at which the crystal melts and grows by equal amounts","raises the temperature at which the crystal melts by about a degree","lowers the growth temperature but not the melting point of the crystal","causes the crystal to shrink slowly until it disappears","lowers the melting point of the crystal while leaving its growth temperature alone"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"the temperature at which that crystal melts does not move; the temperature at which it grows drops"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the temperature at which the crystal melts does not move, while the temperature at which it grows drops by about a degree at fish-blood concentrations. That one-sided shift is the thermal hysteresis.","distractorNotes":["Half right: only the growth temperature falls; the melting point stays put.","Wrong direction: paragraph 5 says the melting point does not move at all.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 5 says the crystal sits between the two temperatures, neither growing nor shrinking.","Reversed: it is the growth temperature that drops and the melting point that stays fixed."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q09","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions salt on a winter road in paragraph 5 primarily to","choices":["contrast what ordinary antifreeze does with what the proteins actually do","show that salt is a cheaper and more practical antifreeze than protein","explain why fish cannot simply raise the salt content of their blood","illustrate the colligative rule that was introduced in the previous paragraph","argue that the proteins deserve the name antifreeze after all"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"The proteins do not prevent ice from forming the way salt on a winter road does; they prevent ice that already exists from getting any bigger"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"The name antifreeze has always struck me as slightly misleading"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 calls the name antifreeze slightly misleading and then explains: the proteins do not prevent ice from forming the way salt on a winter road does, they stop existing ice from growing. The road salt is the familiar case set against the fish's.","distractorNotes":[null,"Outside the passage: no comparison of cost or practicality is made anywhere.","Outside the passage: the passage never discusses why fish do not raise their blood salt; the sentence is about how the proteins differ from salt.","Wrong purpose: the colligative rule belongs to paragraph 4, and this sentence is about preventing versus halting ice, not about counting particles.","Reversed: the author says the name is slightly misleading, and the road salt shows why."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q10","type":"application","stem":"A laboratory adds notothenioid antifreeze glycoprotein, at the concentration found in the fish's blood, to a tube of fresh water that contains one small ice crystal, and holds the tube at minus 0.5 degrees Celsius. Based on paragraph 5, the crystal would most likely","choices":["melt away completely within a few minutes","grow slowly along the faces the proteins have not covered","remain the same size while it is held there","shoot out fine needles of ice from its tips","dissolve as the protein molecules bind to its faces"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"the temperature at which that crystal melts does not move; the temperature at which it grows drops by about a degree in the concentrations found in fish blood"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"Between those two temperatures the crystal simply sits, neither growing nor shrinking"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 gives the rule: the melting point stays where it was, which for fresh water is 0 degrees, and the growth temperature drops by about a degree at fish-blood concentration, to about minus 1. Minus 0.5 falls in the gap, where the crystal simply sits, neither growing nor shrinking.","distractorNotes":["Wrong side of the gap: the crystal would melt only above its unchanged melting point of 0 degrees.","Wrong side of the gap: growth needs a temperature below about minus 1, and paragraph 6 says the gaps are exactly where growth is held back.",null,"Wrong temperature: paragraph 6 says the needles burst out only when the temperature falls below the hysteresis point.","Wrong mechanism: paragraph 6 says bound proteins stop growth; nothing says binding dissolves the crystal."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q11","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when the temperature falls below the hysteresis point, a crystal coated with antifreeze protein","choices":["melts from the tips of its pyramids inward","sheds the proteins bound to its faces and stops growing","grows evenly on all faces at the same slow rate","shrinks until it reaches a size the proteins can hold","sends out fine needles of ice from its tips"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"ice shoots out of the pyramids' tips as fine needles"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 ends by saying that when the temperature finally falls below the hysteresis point the restraint fails all at once, and ice shoots out of the pyramids' tips as fine needles.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the crystal grows, not melts, when the temperature falls.","Outside the passage: nothing says the proteins come off, and the crystal grows rather than stops.","Contradicted: paragraph 6 says the restraint fails all at once and growth comes as needles from the tips, not evenly.","Reversed: falling below the hysteresis point lets the crystal grow, not shrink.",null]},{"id":"rc-o16-q12","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the water-repellent character of the ice-binding face was 'surprising' because","choices":["the earlier picture had the proteins bonding directly to water in the ice","the proteins had been thought to bind ice through their sugars alone","a flat surface was expected to slide off a crystal rather than stick to it","Davies and his colleagues had set out to confirm the earlier picture","the spacing of the proteins had been found not to match the ice lattice"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"were thought to form hydrogen bonds with water molecules locked in the ice lattice"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"flat and surprisingly water-repellent"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the early picture had the hydroxyl groups forming hydrogen bonds with water molecules in the ice lattice. A face that bonds to water is the opposite of one that repels it, so a water-repellent face overturns that expectation.","distractorNotes":[null,"Half right: the early picture involved the hydroxyls on the threonines as well as the sugars, and it is the bonding to water that the new finding upset.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about flat surfaces sliding off crystals.","Outside the passage: the passage does not say what Davies's group set out to show.","Contradicted: the matching of spacing was part of the early picture, and the passage never says it failed."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q13","type":"except","stem":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as a fish that carries an antifreeze protein?","choices":["winter flounder","sea raven","ocean pout","Arctic cod","herring"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"The winter flounder of the North American Atlantic coast makes a small, alanine-rich protein","choice":0},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"the sea raven makes a globular protein","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"the ocean pout makes a compact protein","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"The surprise was the Arctic cod.","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 8 names the winter flounder, the sea raven, and the ocean pout, and then introduces the Arctic cod. Herring appear nowhere in the passage.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 8 names the winter flounder and its alanine-rich helix.","Paragraph 8 names the sea raven and its lectin-like protein.","Paragraph 8 names the ocean pout, which returns in paragraph 11.","Paragraph 8 introduces the Arctic cod as the surprise, and paragraph 9 traces its gene.",null]},{"id":"rc-o16-q14","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following were part of the 1997 findings of Chen, DeVries, and Cheng EXCEPT:","choices":["The notothenioid gene grew out of a gene for trypsinogen.","The Arctic cod gene had grown out of a trypsinogen gene of its own.","A molecular clock placed the gene's origin at roughly 5 to 14 million years ago.","The antifreeze gene still carries pieces of the trypsinogen gene at both ends.","A short nine-nucleotide stretch had been copied over and over to form the repeats."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"had grown out of a gene for trypsinogen","choice":0},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"Their molecular clock placed the gene's origin at roughly 5 to 14 million years ago","choice":2},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"still carries pieces of the trypsinogen gene at both ends","choice":3},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"has been copied over and over to form the repeats","choice":4},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"The cod gene has no such pieces and no relation to trypsinogen"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the cod gene has no such pieces and no relation to trypsinogen; that is the whole point of the convergence story. The other four statements all appear in paragraph 9.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 9, sentence 3 says the notothenioid gene had grown out of a gene for trypsinogen.",null,"Paragraph 9, sentence 4 gives the molecular clock estimate of roughly 5 to 14 million years.","Paragraph 9, sentence 3 says the gene still carries pieces of trypsinogen at both ends.","Paragraph 9, sentence 3 describes the nine-nucleotide stretch copied over and over."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a notothenioid whose kidneys had ordinary filtering units would most likely","choices":["produce larger glycoproteins that the kidney could not strain out","take in less ice through its gut, since it would drink less seawater","accumulate more ice crystals in its spleen over its lifetime","lose part of its antifreeze into its urine and be less protected","have blood that froze at a lower temperature than other notothenioids"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"The glycoproteins are small enough to be lost through an ordinary fish kidney"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"the filtering units that would otherwise strain the proteins into the urine"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the glycoproteins are small enough to be lost through an ordinary fish kidney, and that most notothenioids lack glomeruli, the filtering units that would otherwise strain the proteins into the urine. Give the fish those units back and the proteins would drain away.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: nothing suggests the fish could change the size of its proteins in response.","Unrelated: drinking in paragraph 10 is driven by water loss to the salty sea, not by the kidney.","Wrong link: the ice in the spleen in paragraph 10 is tied to the proteins preventing melting, not to kidney filtering.",null,"Reversed: losing antifreeze would raise the temperature at which the blood freezes, not lower it."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward announcements of frost-proof crops is best described as","choices":["hostile","cautious","enthusiastic","indifferent","amused"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"I would treat each new announcement in that line with patience rather than excitement"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"has delivered much less than advertised"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the promise of frost-proof crops has delivered much less than advertised and that the author would treat each new announcement with patience rather than excitement. That is caution: not rejection, not enthusiasm.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the author counsels patience, not opposition.",null,"Reversed: excitement is exactly what the author declines.","Too weak: the author cares enough to warn the reader and to weigh the record.","Wrong register: nothing in the sentence treats the announcements as funny."]},{"id":"rc-o16-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Polar fishes survive in freezing water because their blood carries more salt than the sea around them","Antarctic and Arctic fishes inherited one antifreeze gene from a common ancestor that lived in cold seas","Antifreeze proteins have transformed food manufacturing and organ preservation","Proteins that hold ice still let polar fishes live below the freezing point of their own blood","Scientists still cannot explain how a protein can bind to the surface of a growing ice crystal"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"colder than the freezing point of its own blood"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"they prevent ice that already exists from getting any bigger"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"found a protein that holds ice still"}],"why":"The passage opens with fish living below the freezing point of their own blood, spends its middle on how the glycoproteins keep existing ice from growing, and closes by calling that the enduring observation. The answer names both the problem and the solution; the others pick one detail or get it wrong.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 1 says fish blood is far less salty than the sea, and paragraph 3 says salts explain only about half of the protection.","Contradicted: paragraph 9 says the two glycoproteins arose independently from different raw material.","Too narrow: the applications occupy one paragraph, and the author is cautious about them.",null,"Too strong: paragraph 7 says the binding question was answered, then revised, and may be revised again, not that it is unexplained."]}]},"rc-o17":{"id":"rc-o17","title":"The Hole Over Halley Bay","topic":"the ozone hole and CFCs","field":"atmospheric chemistry","source":"original","words":1373,"paragraphs":[["Most of Earth's ozone sits in a band of the stratosphere between roughly 15 and 35 kilometers up, and there is surprisingly little of it.","If every ozone molecule above a given spot were brought down to sea-level pressure and temperature, the layer would be about three millimeters thick.","That thickness is written as 300 Dobson units, after Gordon Dobson, the Oxford physicist whose instrument became the standard for measuring ozone in the 1920s, and it is close to the global average.","The layer matters because ozone absorbs most of the ultraviolet wavelengths that damage DNA and cause skin cancers and cataracts."],["In 1930 the American engineer Thomas Midgley Jr. stood before a meeting of the American Chemical Society, inhaled a lungful of a new gas, and blew out a candle with it.","The gas was dichlorodifluoromethane, a chlorofluorocarbon, or CFC, developed at General Motors as a refrigerant.","The refrigerants of the day, ammonia, sulfur dioxide, and methyl chloride, were toxic, flammable, or both.","The new compounds were neither; stable, odorless, and cheap, they spread under the trade name Freon from refrigerators into air conditioners, aerosol spray cans, foam insulation, and solvents for cleaning electronics."],["The first to look for CFCs in the open air was the British scientist James Lovelock, who had invented an extremely sensitive detector for such compounds.","In the early 1970s he carried it aboard a research ship bound for Antarctica and found CFC-11 in every sample, at only parts per trillion but present over the whole ocean.","Because nothing seemed to be removing the gas, Lovelock concluded that nearly all the CFCs ever released were still in the air, and he wrote that they posed no conceivable hazard.","It was not a careless remark; by every test then known, the gases were harmless."],["Harmless at the surface is not harmless everywhere, and in 1974 Mario Molina and F. Sherwood Rowland, at the University of California, Irvine, asked where the gases would finally go.","Their answer, published in Nature that June, was the stratosphere.","CFCs do not dissolve in rain and are not attacked by the reactive molecules that scrub most pollutants from the lower atmosphere, so they persist for decades and drift up past the ozone layer.","There they meet the hard ultraviolet light that ozone blocks for the rest of us, and it breaks the carbon-chlorine bond, releasing a free chlorine atom.","A chlorine atom pulls one oxygen atom from ozone, forming chlorine monoxide; chlorine monoxide then meets a free oxygen atom, hands over its oxygen, and the chlorine atom is back.","The chlorine is a catalyst, consumed by the first step and restored by the second, and one atom can repeat the cycle roughly a hundred thousand times before something else removes it.","Paul Crutzen had shown in 1970 that nitrogen oxides destroy ozone by a similar cycle, so the idea was not new, but the scale was."],["The paper met resistance, and some of it was reasonable.","The manufacturers promised to stop production if a real threat were shown and argued that a calculation did not count.","Public opinion moved first: in 1978 the United States banned CFCs as propellants in most aerosol sprays, and Canada, Norway, and Sweden did the same.","Spray cans were only one use among several, however, and as refrigeration and foam production grew through the 1980s, total CFC output climbed back toward its peak."],["The sign, when it came, came from the ground rather than from space.","The British Antarctic Survey had measured total ozone from Halley Bay, on the Brunt Ice Shelf, with a Dobson spectrophotometer since 1957.","Joseph Farman, Brian Gardiner, and Jonathan Shanklin noticed that the October readings, taken in the Antarctic spring, had been sliding since the late 1970s and by 1984 stood roughly a third below their earlier values.","Their report in Nature in May 1985 tied the loss to the growing burden of chlorine and suggested that something peculiar to the polar spring was at work.","An American satellite had measured ozone over Antarctica all along, but its software flagged values below a set threshold as probable error, and nobody examined the flagged data until the British paper appeared.","I find that sobering: the costliest instrument in the field had seen the hole and set it aside."],["The finding fit the 1974 prediction in one way and contradicted it in others.","Chlorine was implicated, but Molina and Rowland had expected a slow, global loss of a few percent high in the stratosphere, not a collapse of a third lower down, confined to one continent and a few weeks each spring.","Within a year three explanations competed: a chemical one driven by chlorine, a dynamical one in which shifting winds lifted ozone-poor air from below, and a solar one blaming nitrogen oxides from a recent peak in solar activity.","The chemists had to admit that chlorine alone did not explain why the loss came only over the pole and only in spring."],["The missing piece was ice.","In 1986 Susan Solomon of the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration proposed, with Rowland and two colleagues, that the answer lay in polar stratospheric clouds.","Through the polar night a ring of winds called the polar vortex seals off the air above Antarctica, and without sunlight the stratosphere inside it cools below about minus 78 degrees Celsius, cold enough for thin clouds of nitric acid and water to form.","Elsewhere in the world most stratospheric chlorine is locked in two reservoir compounds, hydrogen chloride and chlorine nitrate, that do not attack ozone.","On the cloud particles, however, the reservoirs react with each other and release molecular chlorine, while the nitric acid stays in the particles, which can grow heavy enough to fall away.","When the sun returns in September it splits the chlorine molecules into atoms, and because free oxygen atoms are scarce at those altitudes the destruction runs by a second route, in which two chlorine monoxide molecules pair up and are split by sunlight.","Ozone over the continent falls by half or more within a few weeks."],["The hypothesis was tested quickly.","That same spring Solomon led a ground expedition to McMurdo Station that measured unusually high levels of chlorine oxides.","The following year a high-altitude ER-2 aircraft flew from Punta Arenas, Chile, into the vortex, and its instruments showed chlorine monoxide climbing sharply exactly where ozone fell, a mirror image as close to a smoking gun as atmospheric science usually gets.","The dynamical and solar explanations faded; neither could account for the chlorine."],["Diplomacy did not wait for the aircraft.","The Montreal Protocol on Substances that Deplete the Ozone Layer was signed in September 1987 and took effect at the start of 1989.","Its original terms were modest: a freeze in production of the main CFCs, then a cut of 50 percent by 1999.","A clause required the parties to revisit the targets as the science changed, and amendments adopted in London in 1990 and Copenhagen in 1992 turned the half measure into a halt: production in developed countries ended in 1996, and developing countries, given more time and a fund for substitutes, followed by 2010.","The treaty has since been ratified by every member state of the United Nations, and a 2016 amendment adopted in Kigali, Rwanda, extended it to hydrofluorocarbons, substitutes that spare ozone but trap heat powerfully.","It is fashionable to call Montreal the most successful environmental treaty ever written, and I think the praise is earned, with one reservation: the problem was comparatively easy, since substitutes existed and the manufacturers were few."],["In 1995 Crutzen, Molina, and Rowland shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry.","Stratospheric chlorine peaked in the late 1990s and has fallen slowly since, because the gases that carry it live for decades to a century.","In 2016 Solomon and her colleagues reported the first firm evidence that the September hole was shrinking, and current assessments expect Antarctic ozone to recover to its 1980 state in the 2060s.","The lag is the lesson I would draw from the whole affair.","Because CFCs are so stable, the atmosphere answers a decision only slowly: the hole was found a decade after the warning and will close a lifetime after the ban."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o17-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, 300 Dobson units corresponds to","choices":["the altitude at which stratospheric ozone is most concentrated","ozone about three millimeters thick at sea-level conditions","the fraction of harmful ultraviolet light the layer absorbs","the thickness of the stratospheric band that holds the ozone","the amount of ozone lost over Antarctica in a typical spring"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"the layer would be about three millimeters thick"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"That thickness is written as 300 Dobson units"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says that if all the ozone above a spot were brought down to sea-level pressure and temperature the layer would be about three millimeters thick, and the next sentence names that thickness 300 Dobson units.","distractorNotes":["Wrong quantity: paragraph 1 gives 15 to 35 kilometers as where the ozone sits, not what a Dobson unit measures.",null,"Outside the passage: the unit measures the amount of ozone, not the light it absorbs.","Wrong quantity: the band is 15 to 35 kilometers deep; the Dobson unit describes the ozone squeezed to sea-level conditions.","Wrong paragraph: the spring loss belongs to paragraph 6 and is not what the unit defines."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the CFCs developed at General Motors replaced earlier refrigerants that","choices":["were too expensive to produce in large quantities","left an unpleasant odor that lingered in kitchens","broke down quickly inside sealed machinery","were poisonous, flammable, or sometimes both","could not be adapted for use in air conditioners"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"were toxic, flammable, or both"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 names the refrigerants of the day, ammonia, sulfur dioxide, and methyl chloride, and says they were toxic, flammable, or both; the new compounds were neither.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: cheapness is listed as a virtue of the new compounds, not as a fault of the old.","Reversed: odorless is a virtue of the new gas; nothing says the old ones smelled.","Outside the passage: stability is credited to the new compounds, but the old ones are never said to break down.",null,"Outside the passage: air conditioners are a place the new compounds spread to, not a failing of the old ones."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author's comment in paragraph 3 that Lovelock's judgment 'was not a careless remark' serves primarily to","choices":["show that the danger was invisible to the tests of the time","criticize Lovelock for ignoring the warning of Molina and Rowland","argue that his detector was too crude to measure CFCs reliably","support the manufacturers' later claim that no threat had been demonstrated","suggest that CFCs are in fact harmless to the ozone layer"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"by every test then known, the gases were harmless"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 follows the remark with its reason: by every test then known, the gases were harmless. The author is explaining how a careful scientist could reach the wrong conclusion, not blaming him.","distractorNotes":[null,"Anachronism: Molina and Rowland's warning in paragraph 4 came after Lovelock wrote, so he could not have ignored it.","Reversed: paragraph 3 calls his detector extremely sensitive.","Wrong paragraph: the manufacturers' argument belongs to paragraph 5, and the author does not use Lovelock to support it.","Contradicted: paragraph 4 opens by saying harmless at the surface is not harmless everywhere."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, after a chlorine atom takes an oxygen atom from ozone, the resulting chlorine monoxide","choices":["dissolves in rain and is washed out of the stratosphere","absorbs hard ultraviolet light and splits into chlorine and oxygen","combines with nitrogen oxides to form a stable compound","reacts with a second ozone molecule to rebuild the ozone it destroyed","hands its oxygen to a free oxygen atom and frees the chlorine"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"chlorine monoxide then meets a free oxygen atom, hands over its oxygen, and the chlorine atom is back"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 lays out both steps: the chlorine atom takes an oxygen from ozone to form chlorine monoxide, and chlorine monoxide then meets a free oxygen atom, hands over its oxygen, and the chlorine atom is back.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 4 says CFCs do not dissolve in rain, and nothing is washed out of the stratosphere.","Wrong paragraph: sunlight splitting a chlorine compound appears in paragraph 8, and there it is molecular chlorine, not chlorine monoxide.","Outside the passage: paragraph 8 names chlorine nitrate as a reservoir but never says chlorine monoxide forms it in this cycle.","Reversed: the cycle destroys ozone; nothing in it rebuilds ozone.",null]},{"id":"rc-o17-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if CFCs dissolved readily in rainwater, they would most likely","choices":["release their chlorine atoms more quickly in the lower atmosphere","form polar stratospheric clouds at much warmer temperatures","be removed from the air before reaching the ozone layer","destroy ozone through a different catalytic cycle","persist in the atmosphere for even longer than they do now"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"CFCs do not dissolve in rain and are not attacked by the reactive molecules that scrub most pollutants from the lower atmosphere, so they persist for decades and drift up past the ozone layer"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says CFCs persist and drift up past the ozone layer because they do not dissolve in rain and are not scrubbed out like most pollutants. Take away that resistance and rain would remove them before they climbed.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: chlorine is freed only by hard ultraviolet light in the stratosphere, and rain is never said to release it.","Unrelated: the clouds in paragraph 8 form from nitric acid and water, not from CFCs.",null,"Outside the passage: no alternative cycle for dissolved CFCs is described.","Reversed: being washed out is the opposite of persisting."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q06","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are mentioned in the passage as uses of CFCs EXCEPT:","choices":["fire extinguishers","refrigerators","aerosol spray cans","foam insulation","solvents for cleaning electronics"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"refrigerators","choice":1},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"aerosol spray cans","choice":2},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"foam insulation","choice":3},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"solvents for cleaning electronics","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 2 lists refrigerators, air conditioners, aerosol spray cans, foam insulation, and solvents for cleaning electronics. Fire extinguishers never appear anywhere in the passage.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 2 says the compounds spread from refrigerators into their other uses.","Paragraph 2 lists aerosol spray cans, and paragraph 5 returns to them.","Paragraph 2 lists foam insulation.","Paragraph 2 lists solvents for cleaning electronics."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q07","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the measurements that first revealed the Antarctic ozone loss were made","choices":["by an American satellite launched in 1957","from a high-altitude aircraft flying out of Chile","at McMurdo Station during the Antarctic spring of 1986","from a ground station on the Brunt Ice Shelf","aboard a research ship bound for Antarctica"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"measured total ozone from Halley Bay, on the Brunt Ice Shelf, with a Dobson spectrophotometer since 1957"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"came from the ground rather than from space"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the sign came from the ground rather than from space: the British Antarctic Survey had measured total ozone from Halley Bay, on the Brunt Ice Shelf, since 1957.","distractorNotes":["Mash-up: 1957 is when the ground record began, and paragraph 6 says the satellite data were set aside, not that they revealed the loss.","Wrong paragraph: the aircraft flights in paragraph 9 confirmed the chlorine two years after the loss was reported.","Wrong paragraph: the McMurdo expedition in paragraph 9 came a year after the report.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the ship in paragraph 3 carried Lovelock's detector for CFCs, not ozone."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the satellite failed to reveal the ozone loss because","choices":["its sensor could not detect ozone values below a certain minimum level","its data-handling rules set aside the very readings that mattered","it was launched too late to record the decline that began in the late 1970s","it measured ozone only in the upper stratosphere","its orbit did not carry it over Antarctica in October"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"its software flagged values below a set threshold as probable error, and nobody examined the flagged data"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"had seen the hole and set it aside"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the satellite had measured ozone all along; what failed was the rule that flagged low values as probable error and the fact that nobody examined the flagged data. The author sums it up: the instrument had seen the hole and set it aside.","distractorNotes":["Half right: the readings were low, but the sensor recorded them; it was the software that flagged them as suspect.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 6 says the satellite had measured ozone over Antarctica all along.","Outside the passage: nothing limits the satellite to the upper stratosphere.","Contradicted: the satellite measured over Antarctica; the problem was what happened to the readings afterward."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q09","type":"function","stem":"The author presents three competing explanations in paragraph 7 primarily to","choices":["argue that shifting winds were the most plausible explanation for the loss","show that Molina and Rowland had been mistaken about the role of chlorine","suggest that the solar cycle had been overlooked by chemists","introduce the satellite data that would settle the dispute","show that chlorine alone did not yet explain the pattern of the loss"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"chlorine alone did not explain why the loss came only over the pole and only in spring"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"contradicted it in others"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 opens by saying the finding contradicted the 1974 prediction in several ways and closes by admitting that chlorine alone did not explain why the loss came only over the pole and only in spring. The three explanations show the question was still open.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 9 says the dynamical explanation faded, and the author never favors it.","Too strong: paragraph 7 says chlorine was implicated, as predicted; the prediction was incomplete, not wrong.","Outside the passage: the solar idea is listed as one competitor, not as something chemists overlooked.","Wrong paragraph and reversed: satellite data appear in paragraph 6, and the dispute was settled by aircraft in paragraph 9.",null]},{"id":"rc-o17-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, polar stratospheric clouds form over Antarctica because","choices":["chlorine nitrate condenses as soon as it meets hydrogen chloride","the returning spring sunlight lifts moisture into the stratosphere","nitric acid falling from the particles seeds new clouds below","winds carry ozone-poor air upward from the lower atmosphere","sealed-off air cools far below freezing during the polar night"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"the polar vortex seals off the air above Antarctica, and without sunlight the stratosphere inside it cools below about minus 78 degrees Celsius, cold enough for thin clouds of nitric acid and water to form"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the polar vortex seals off the air above Antarctica, and without sunlight the stratosphere inside it cools below about minus 78 degrees Celsius, cold enough for the clouds to form.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 8 says the reservoirs react on the cloud particles; they do not make the clouds.","Reversed: the clouds form during the sunless winter, and the spring sun is what activates the chlorine.","Reversed: paragraph 8 says the nitric acid stays in the particles and can fall away; it does not seed clouds.","Wrong paragraph: rising ozone-poor air is the dynamical hypothesis from paragraph 7, which paragraph 9 says faded.",null]},{"id":"rc-o17-q11","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 8, the word 'reservoir' most nearly means","choices":["a form in which something is held out of use","an artificial lake that stores water for a city","an extra supply kept back for use in emergencies","a container that collects runoff","a source that steadily releases its contents"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"two reservoir compounds, hydrogen chloride and chlorine nitrate, that do not attack ozone"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 calls hydrogen chloride and chlorine nitrate reservoir compounds that do not attack ozone: the chlorine is held in them, inactive, until the cloud particles release it.","distractorNotes":[null,"This is the everyday meaning of the word, but no water or lake is involved in the sentence.","Close but off: the chlorine is not being saved for later use; it is locked up and inactive.","Nothing in the sentence collects anything; the compounds hold chlorine chemically.","Reversed: the reservoirs hold chlorine and do not attack ozone; release happens only on the cloud particles."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the 1987 aircraft flights into the polar vortex showed that","choices":["ozone-poor air was being lifted from the lower atmosphere","chlorine monoxide rose sharply where ozone dropped","nitrogen oxides had built up after the peak in solar activity","polar stratospheric clouds were made of nitric acid and water","the ozone loss extended well beyond the edge of the vortex"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"its instruments showed chlorine monoxide climbing sharply exactly where ozone fell"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the ER-2's instruments showed chlorine monoxide climbing sharply exactly where ozone fell, a mirror image the author calls a smoking gun.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: that is the dynamical hypothesis, which paragraph 9 says faded after the flights.",null,"Reversed: that is the solar hypothesis, which also faded; the flights measured chlorine, not nitrogen oxides.","Wrong paragraph: the makeup of the clouds is given in paragraph 8 as part of Solomon's proposal, not as an aircraft result.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about ozone beyond the vortex."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the Montreal Protocol is best described as","choices":["entirely dismissive","unreservedly enthusiastic","approving but qualified","studiously neutral","deeply anxious"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"I think the praise is earned, with one reservation"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says plainly that the praise for Montreal is earned, then adds one reservation: the problem was comparatively easy. That is approval with a qualification attached.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author calls the praise earned.","Too strong: the author attaches a reservation to the praise.",null,"Too weak: the author takes a clear position in the first person.","Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 10 expresses worry about the treaty."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q14","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of the Montreal Protocol EXCEPT:","choices":["It took effect at the beginning of 1989.","Its original terms called for a 50 percent cut by 1999.","Every member state of the United Nations has ratified it.","Its original terms halted all CFC production at once.","An amendment adopted in 2016 extended it to hydrofluorocarbons."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"took effect at the start of 1989","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"then a cut of 50 percent by 1999","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"ratified by every member state of the United Nations","choice":2},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"a 2016 amendment adopted in Kigali, Rwanda, extended it to hydrofluorocarbons","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"Its original terms were modest: a freeze in production of the main CFCs"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 calls the original terms modest: a freeze, then a 50 percent cut by 1999. The full halt came only with the London and Copenhagen amendments, and even then production in developed countries ran until 1996.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 10 says the treaty took effect at the start of 1989.","Paragraph 10 gives the original target as a cut of 50 percent by 1999.","Paragraph 10 says every member state of the United Nations has ratified it.",null,"Paragraph 10 describes the 2016 Kigali amendment covering hydrofluorocarbons."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the ozone hole kept appearing each spring for years after CFC production ended in developed countries because","choices":["the polar vortex grew stronger each winter after the ban","hydrofluorocarbons released as substitutes destroy ozone as well","the CFCs already released remained in the atmosphere for decades","the Montreal Protocol did not take effect until 2010","polar stratospheric clouds persist for decades once they have formed"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"because the gases that carry it live for decades to a century"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"Because CFCs are so stable, the atmosphere answers a decision only slowly"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"they persist for decades"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says stratospheric chlorine has fallen only slowly because the gases that carry it live for decades to a century, and that the atmosphere answers a decision only slowly because CFCs are so stable. The gas released before the ban was still up there.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: nothing is said about the vortex changing from year to year.","Contradicted: paragraph 10 says hydrofluorocarbons spare ozone.",null,"Wrong date: paragraph 10 says the treaty took effect in 1989; 2010 was the developing countries' deadline.","Outside the passage: the clouds form each polar night in paragraph 8; nothing says they last for decades."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q16","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle described in paragraph 11?","choices":["A city bans a pesticide that breaks down within days, and its river is clean by the following summer.","A town stops burying a chemical that lasts for centuries, and its wells stay tainted for decades.","A factory switches to a cleaner fuel on Monday, and the haze over the town is gone by Friday.","A village plants trees on a bare slope, and the first storm washes the seedlings away.","A farmer stops applying fertilizer, and the very next harvest is noticeably smaller than the last."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"Because CFCs are so stable, the atmosphere answers a decision only slowly"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"the gases that carry it live for decades to a century"}],"why":"Paragraph 11's principle is that when a pollutant is very stable, stopping its release pays off only slowly, because what was already released lingers. Wells that stay tainted for decades after the burials stop follow the same rule.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: a pollutant that breaks down within days brings a fast recovery, the opposite of the lag.",null,"Reversed: the haze clears within days, so there is no lag.","Wrong mechanism: the seedlings are lost to a storm, not to a lingering pollutant.","Wrong direction: this is an immediate cost of stopping, not a delayed benefit."]},{"id":"rc-o17-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["The discovery of the Antarctic ozone hole proved that satellites are less reliable than ground-based instruments for monitoring the atmosphere","Thomas Midgley's refrigerants caused more environmental damage than any other invention of the twentieth century","The Montreal Protocol succeeded only because the chemical industry had already developed cheap substitutes for the CFCs it gave up","A chemical threat predicted on paper was confirmed by a surprise Antarctic discovery and answered by a treaty whose effects unfold slowly","Polar stratospheric clouds, not CFCs, are the true cause of the seasonal ozone loss over Antarctica"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"asked where the gases would finally go"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"The sign, when it came, came from the ground rather than from space."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"The lag is the lesson I would draw from the whole affair."}],"why":"The passage moves from a prediction on paper in 1974, through the unexpected Antarctic discovery and the chemistry that explained it, to a treaty and a recovery that will take decades. Only the answer carries all three parts.","distractorNotes":["Too narrow: the satellite story is one sobering detail in paragraph 6, not the point of the passage.","Too narrow and outside the passage: Midgley appears only in paragraph 2, and the author never ranks his invention against others.","Too strong: the author calls substitutes one reason the problem was easier, not the only reason the treaty succeeded.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 8 says the clouds activate chlorine that came from CFCs; they are the mechanism, not a rival cause."]}]},"rc-o18":{"id":"rc-o18","title":"A Code Written in a Thousand Genes","topic":"the sense of smell","field":"neuroscience","source":"original","words":1372,"paragraphs":[["The eye solves an enormous problem with a small kit: three kinds of cone cell, each tuned to a different band of wavelengths, let a person tell apart millions of colors.","The ear sorts sound by frequency along a single membrane in the inner ear.","Smell offers no such shortcut: odor molecules come in tens of thousands of shapes, and there is no single property, nothing like wavelength or pitch, along which they can be lined up.","How the nose makes sense of that crowd was, until 1991, one of the largest open questions in sensory biology."],["The olfactory epithelium is a patch of tissue high in the nasal cavity, and in a human it holds millions of olfactory sensory neurons.","Each is a slender bipolar cell: a tuft of cilia at one end sits in the mucus lining the nose, and a thin axon at the other climbs through the cribriform plate, a perforated sheet of bone, into the olfactory bulb.","These neurons are unusual in that they die and are replaced throughout life, each lasting weeks to months before a new one grows from a stem cell below.","By the late 1980s researchers had shown that odorants raise the level of cyclic AMP in the cilia, and in 1989 Randall Reed's group at Johns Hopkins identified a G protein, named G-olf, concentrated in olfactory neurons.","Both findings pointed toward a known class of proteins, the G protein-coupled receptors, which cross the cell membrane seven times and hand their signal to a G protein inside the cell."],["Linda Buck, then a postdoctoral researcher in Richard Axel's laboratory at Columbia University, built her search on three assumptions.","The receptors would be G protein-coupled receptors; they would be made in the olfactory epithelium and nowhere else; and because odorants are so varied, they would form a large family of related genes rather than one gene with a broad appetite.","She used the polymerase chain reaction with deliberately loose primers, matched to stretches shared by known receptors of the class, so that unknown relatives would be copied too.","The decisive trick was to look not for one new gene but for amplified fragments that turned out to be mixtures of many closely related sequences, exactly what a large family would produce.","Buck and Axel published the result in the journal Cell in 1991, and in 2004 they shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for the discovery and for the picture of the olfactory system that grew from it."],["The family turned out to be vast.","Mice and rats carry roughly 1,000 functional odorant receptor genes, a few percent of all their genes, which makes this the largest gene family in the mammalian genome.","Humans carry a smaller working set, roughly 400, alongside hundreds of pseudogenes, copies that have accumulated mutations and are no longer translated into working receptors.","It is tempting to read those counts as a ranking of noses, rat at the top and human far below, but I think the gene tally is a crude yardstick, for reasons I will return to."],["A second discovery gave the family its logic.","Each olfactory sensory neuron expresses only one of the thousand or so receptor genes, and it uses only one of its two copies of that gene, the maternal or the paternal.","The choice looks random within broad zones of the epithelium, and once a neuron has settled on a receptor, a feedback signal from the receptor protein itself appears to lock out the alternatives.","Every neuron is therefore a specialist, and the brain can read its identity from the receptor it carries."],["That identity is preserved in the wiring.","Neurons expressing the same receptor are scattered across the epithelium, yet their axons gather at the same small knots of nerve endings in the bulb, called glomeruli, typically two per bulb out of roughly 2,000 in a mouse.","In 1996 Peter Mombaerts and colleagues in Axel's laboratory made the convergence visible by attaching a marker gene to one receptor gene in mice and following the stained axons to their glomerulus.","Two years later the same laboratory swapped one receptor's coding sequence for another's and saw the axons change destination, evidence that the receptor protein steers the axon as well as detecting odorants.","The bulb is therefore a map, though a map of receptors rather than of space: a given smell becomes a particular pattern of active glomeruli."],["The deepest question remained: with hundreds of receptor types and far more odorants, how is identity encoded?","In 1999 Buck's own laboratory, by then at Harvard Medical School, answered it by recording from individual mouse neurons as odorants washed over them, then fishing out the receptor gene each responsive neuron carried.","Three rules emerged.","A single receptor recognizes several odorants; a single odorant is recognized by several receptors; and different odorants are recognized by different combinations of receptors.","The scheme is a combinatorial code, the olfactory equivalent of spelling words from a fixed alphabet, and with hundreds of letters the number of possible words is beyond counting.","The same study found that a small change to a molecule, such as lengthening its carbon chain by one unit, changes which receptors respond, and that raising the concentration of an odorant recruits additional receptors.","That last finding is a plausible explanation for an old puzzle: indole, which smells of flowers when dilute, smells of decay when concentrated."],["How many smells can such a code distinguish?","For most of the twentieth century the standard answer was about 10,000, a figure that rested on very little evidence.","In 2014 a group at Rockefeller University asked volunteers to pick the odd one out among mixtures drawn from 128 odorants and extrapolated from their success to an estimate of more than one trillion distinguishable stimuli.","Soon afterward other researchers showed that the extrapolation was extremely sensitive to its assumptions and that modest changes in method moved the estimate by many orders of magnitude.","I find neither number persuasive as a number.","What the episode did establish is more modest and more durable: people can tell apart mixtures that differ in only a fraction of their components, which is not the mark of a weak sense."],["That brings me back to the gene count.","The belief that humans smell poorly is older than the gene family; it goes back at least to the nineteenth-century anatomist Paul Broca, who noted that the human olfactory bulb is small relative to the rest of the brain and classed our species among the weak smellers.","In 2017 the neuroscientist John McGann of Rutgers University reviewed the evidence and argued that this reputation is a myth.","Counted by the neurons it contains rather than by its share of the brain, the human bulb is not unusual among mammals, and in direct tests humans are more sensitive than dogs and rodents to some odorants, though less to others.","The 400 working genes, in other words, support a far better instrument than the tally suggests; I suspect that what differs most between species is not the nose but what each animal does with the information."],["One more feature is often invoked.","From the glomeruli, the bulb's output cells send their axons directly to the olfactory cortex, and from there the signal reaches the amygdala and the hippocampus, regions tied to emotion and memory.","Every other major sense passes through the thalamus before reaching its cortex; smell, on its main path, does not.","This anatomy is often offered as the reason a smell can summon a childhood kitchen so abruptly, and it may be right, but I would hold it loosely: the directness of a pathway and the vividness of a memory are not the same kind of fact."],["What Buck and Axel found, in the end, was not a receptor for each smell but a way of doing without one.","A thousand genes, one per neuron, wired to a map of glomeruli and read as combinations, let a finite set of detectors cover an open-ended world of molecules.","That is a different kind of answer from the eye's three cones, and a more generous one: the nose did not need to know in advance which molecules would exist."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o18-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, smell differs from vision and hearing in that","choices":["it relies on far more sensory neurons than either of the other two senses","its stimuli are processed in the inner ear rather than in the brain","it depends on three kinds of receptor cell rather than on one","its stimuli cannot be ordered along a single physical property","it was the only sense whose receptors were understood before 1991"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"there is no single property, nothing like wavelength or pitch, along which they can be lined up"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 lines up the three senses: the eye uses wavelength, the ear uses frequency, and for smell there is no single property along which odor molecules can be lined up. That missing axis is the difference.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: paragraph 2 gives a count for olfactory neurons but never compares it with the other senses.","Wrong sense: paragraph 1 places the membrane in the inner ear for hearing, and smell is handled in the olfactory bulb and cortex.","Reversed: the three cone types belong to the eye, and paragraph 1 presents them as the shortcut smell lacks.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 1 says the question of how the nose works was still open until 1991."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, olfactory sensory neurons are unusual among neurons because they","choices":["send their axons through the thalamus before reaching the olfactory cortex","are replaced throughout life as older cells die off and new ones grow","carry cilia that reach into the mucus lining the nose","cross a bone on their way from the nose to the brain","hand their signal to a G protein inside the cell"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"These neurons are unusual in that they die and are replaced throughout life"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says these neurons are unusual in that they die and are replaced throughout life, with a new one growing from a stem cell beneath. Only that feature is labeled unusual.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 10 says smell is the sense that does not pass through the thalamus on its main path.",null,"True but answers a different question: paragraph 2 describes the cilia without calling them unusual.","True but answers a different question: the cribriform plate is part of the route, not the feature the passage calls unusual.","Wrong level: paragraph 2 says this about G protein-coupled receptors in general, not about what makes these neurons unusual."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions cyclic AMP and the G protein G-olf in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["show that the chemistry of smell was fully understood before 1991","contrast the human olfactory system with the rat's","explain how a single neuron chooses one receptor gene from the family","introduce the molecule that each odorant must bind before it is sensed","show what evidence pointed to the kind of protein Buck would seek"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"Both findings pointed toward a known class of proteins, the G protein-coupled receptors"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"The receptors would be G protein-coupled receptors"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 closes by saying both findings pointed toward the G protein-coupled receptors, and paragraph 3 opens with Buck assuming exactly that class. The two details are there to set up her first assumption.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: paragraph 1 says how the nose works was still an open question until 1991.","Outside the passage: paragraph 2 does not compare species.","Wrong paragraph: the one-receptor-per-neuron rule is paragraph 5, and it is not explained by cyclic AMP.","Wrong molecule: an odorant binds its receptor; cyclic AMP is a signal made inside the cell afterward.",null]},{"id":"rc-o18-q04","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, the word 'appetite' most nearly means","choices":["a craving for food that grows with time","the range of molecules a receptor accepts","the amount of energy a cell consumes","a receptor's speed in handing its signal to a G protein","a researcher's eagerness to find a gene"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"a large family of related genes rather than one gene with a broad appetite"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 contrasts a large family of genes with one gene with a broad appetite, so the appetite is how many different odorants a single receptor would have to take in. The everyday meaning, hunger, does not fit a gene.","distractorNotes":["This is the common meaning of the word, and it is wrong here: a gene does not crave food.",null,"Nothing in the sentence concerns energy use; the contrast is about how many odorants one receptor would handle.","Speed is not at issue in the sentence, which is about breadth of recognition.","The sentence describes a hypothetical gene, not a researcher's feelings."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the decisive step in Buck's search was","choices":["looking for amplified fragments that were mixtures of related sequences","designing primers that matched one receptor gene and no other","sequencing the complete genome of the rat to count receptor genes","comparing the olfactory epithelium with tissue from the olfactory bulb","attaching a marker gene to a receptor gene so its product could be traced"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"The decisive trick was to look not for one new gene but for amplified fragments that turned out to be mixtures of many closely related sequences"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 names the decisive trick directly: looking for amplified fragments that were mixtures of many closely related sequences, which is what a large family would produce.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: paragraph 3 says the primers were deliberately loose so that unknown relatives would be copied too.","Outside the passage: no genome sequencing is described.","Outside the passage: the search was in olfactory tissue; no comparison with the bulb is mentioned.","Wrong paragraph: the marker gene experiment is Mombaerts' work in paragraph 6, five years later."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q06","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the pseudogenes in the human odorant receptor family","choices":["are expressed in the olfactory epithelium and nowhere else","outnumber the functional receptor genes of mice and rats","descend from copies that once encoded working receptors","were first described by Buck and Axel in 1991","are the reason humans smell poorly compared with rats"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"copies that have accumulated mutations and are no longer translated into working receptors"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 defines pseudogenes as copies that have accumulated mutations and are no longer translated into working receptors. No longer means they once were, so each pseudogene descends from a copy that used to work.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: the location of expression is an assumption about the receptors in paragraph 3, and pseudogenes are not translated at all.","Contradicted: paragraph 4 puts human pseudogenes in the hundreds and rodent functional genes at roughly 1,000.",null,"Outside the passage: the 1991 paper is credited with finding the family, and nothing ties it to pseudogenes.","Contradicted: paragraphs 4 and 9 argue that the gene tally is a crude yardstick and that the poor-smell reputation is a myth."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q07","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a single olfactory sensory neuron","choices":["expresses both the maternal and the paternal copy of one receptor gene","expresses a receptor chosen for it by the glomerulus its axon reaches","switches to a new receptor gene whenever one is locked out","expresses several receptor genes drawn from one zone of the epithelium","uses just one receptor gene and only one of that gene's two copies"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"expresses only one of the thousand or so receptor genes, and it uses only one of its two copies of that gene"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 states the rule in one line: each neuron expresses only one receptor gene, and only one of its two copies of that gene, the maternal or the paternal.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 5 says only one of the two copies is used.","Reversed: paragraph 6 says the receptor helps steer the axon to its glomerulus, not the other way around.","Contradicted: paragraph 5 says that once a neuron settles on a receptor, the alternatives are locked out.","Contradicted: the zone is where the choice is made, and the neuron expresses one gene, not several.",null]},{"id":"rc-o18-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the number of glomeruli in a mouse olfactory bulb is best explained by","choices":["each receptor type being assigned about two glomeruli","each odorant being assigned a glomerulus of its own","each neuron sending its axon to a separate glomerulus","each zone of the epithelium having its own glomerulus","each glomerulus holding axons from several receptor types"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"typically two per bulb out of roughly 2,000 in a mouse"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"Mice and rats carry roughly 1,000 functional odorant receptor genes"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the axons for one receptor gather at typically two glomeruli per bulb, out of roughly 2,000, and paragraph 4 gives roughly 1,000 receptor genes in a mouse. Two glomeruli for each of 1,000 receptors is about 2,000.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong unit: paragraph 6 says the bulb is a map of receptors rather than of odorants, and odorants far outnumber glomeruli.","Contradicted: millions of neurons converge on about 2,000 glomeruli, so many axons share each one.","Wrong paragraph: zones in paragraph 5 are where the receptor choice is made, not units of the bulb.","Reversed: paragraph 6 says axons from the same receptor gather together, which ties a glomerulus to one receptor type."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q09","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, the 1999 study from Buck's laboratory found all of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["A single receptor can recognize several different odorants.","Raising an odorant's concentration recruits additional receptors.","A small change in a molecule's structure changes which receptors respond.","Odorants that smell alike share most of the receptors they activate.","Different odorants are recognized by different combinations of receptors."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"A single receptor recognizes several odorants","choice":0},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"raising the concentration of an odorant recruits additional receptors","choice":1},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"a small change to a molecule, such as lengthening its carbon chain by one unit, changes which receptors respond","choice":2},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"different odorants are recognized by different combinations of receptors","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lists the three rules and then two more findings about structure and concentration. Nothing in the passage links similar smells to shared receptors; that statement cannot be confirmed anywhere.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 7 as the first of the three rules.","Stated in paragraph 7 as the finding that explains the indole puzzle.","Stated in paragraph 7, with the carbon chain as the example.",null,"Stated in paragraph 7 as the third of the three rules."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q10","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions indole in paragraph 7 primarily to","choices":["show that a single receptor can recognize several chemically unrelated odorants","prove that the 1999 study's findings apply to humans as well as mice","suggest how recruiting more receptors could alter a smell's character","give an example of an odorant whose receptor has not been identified","illustrate how lengthening a carbon chain alters a molecule's smell"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":7,"quote":"That last finding is a plausible explanation for an old puzzle"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"raising the concentration of an odorant recruits additional receptors"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 introduces indole right after the finding that raising concentration recruits additional receptors, calling it a plausible explanation for an old puzzle. Indole is the illustration of what a changing receptor set might do to a smell.","distractorNotes":["Wrong rule: one receptor recognizing several odorants is a separate finding, and indole is a single odorant.","Too strong and outside: the author calls the link plausible, not proven, and says nothing about human subjects here.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing is said about which receptors respond to indole.","Wrong example: the carbon chain illustrates structural change; indole illustrates concentration."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q11","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the combinatorial code described in paragraph 7?","choices":["A panel of ten lamps whose lit-and-unlit pattern names any one of hundreds of rooms","A hotel that gives every guest a key that opens that guest's room and no other room","A smoke alarm that sounds the same tone no matter what kind of smoke reaches it","A thermometer whose single reading climbs steadily as the temperature of a room rises","A phone directory in which each name appears beside one number assigned to it alone"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"different odorants are recognized by different combinations of receptors"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"the olfactory equivalent of spelling words from a fixed alphabet"}],"why":"Paragraph 7's rule is that identity is carried by the combination of detectors that respond, like spelling words from a fixed alphabet. A small set of lamps whose on-and-off pattern names hundreds of rooms is the same idea: few detectors, many combinations.","distractorNotes":[null,"One key for one room is a one-to-one code, the opposite of the passage's scheme, in which each receptor serves several odorants.","A single detector with a broad response, but no combination of detectors to read.","A single graded signal, not a pattern across many detectors.","One-to-one pairing again: each name has its own number, so nothing is read in combination."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the 2014 study at Rockefeller University","choices":["tested 128 volunteers on their ability to name single odorants","confirmed the long-standing estimate of about 10,000 smells","was accepted without objection by other researchers","showed that humans are more sensitive than dogs to certain odorants","extrapolated from an odd-one-out task to over a trillion stimuli"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"asked volunteers to pick the odd one out among mixtures drawn from 128 odorants and extrapolated from their success to an estimate of more than one trillion distinguishable stimuli"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 describes the study: volunteers picked the odd one out among mixtures, and the researchers extrapolated from that success to more than one trillion distinguishable stimuli.","distractorNotes":["Number swap: 128 is the count of odorants, not volunteers, and the task was comparing mixtures, not naming.","Reversed: the trillion estimate replaced the old figure of about 10,000.","Contradicted: paragraph 8 says other researchers soon showed the extrapolation was extremely sensitive to its assumptions.","Wrong paragraph: the comparison with dogs is McGann's point in paragraph 9.",null]},{"id":"rc-o18-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the published estimates of how many smells people can distinguish is best described as","choices":["admiring","skeptical","indifferent","hostile","amused"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"I find neither number persuasive as a number."},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"a figure that rested on very little evidence"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the old figure rested on very little evidence and then, of both numbers, I find neither persuasive as a number. Doubting both while crediting what the work did establish is a skeptic's stance.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author doubts both figures rather than praising them.",null,"Too weak: the author takes a clear position and then says what the episode did establish.","Too strong: the author grants that the study established something modest and durable.","Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 8 is playful."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q14","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Paul Broca judged human smell to be weak on the basis of","choices":["the small number of functional receptor genes in humans","the number of neurons in the human olfactory bulb","head-to-head tests of humans against dogs and rodents","the olfactory bulb's small size relative to the brain","the absence of a relay through the thalamus"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"who noted that the human olfactory bulb is small relative to the rest of the brain and classed our species among the weak smellers"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says Broca noted that the human olfactory bulb is small relative to the rest of the brain and on that basis classed our species among the weak smellers.","distractorNotes":["Anachronism: Broca worked in the nineteenth century, and the gene family was found in 1991.","Reversed: counting neurons is the measure McGann used to answer Broca.","Wrong source: the direct tests are part of McGann's case in paragraph 9, not Broca's.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the thalamus appears in paragraph 10 and is never tied to Broca."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a rat, with roughly 1,000 working receptor genes, compared with a human, who has about 400,","choices":["can detect every odorant at a lower concentration","has an olfactory bulb that holds more than twice as many neurons","does not necessarily possess the more capable sense of smell","distinguishes roughly two and a half times as many distinct smells","uses a larger share of its glomeruli for each odorant"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"I think the gene tally is a crude yardstick"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"humans are more sensitive than dogs and rodents to some odorants, though less to others"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 calls the gene tally a crude yardstick for ranking noses, and paragraph 9 reports that humans beat rodents on some odorants and lose on others. Put together, the larger gene count does not settle which nose is better.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 9 says humans are more sensitive to some odorants.","Outside the passage: paragraph 9 says bulb neuron counts are not unusual across mammals, and never ties them to gene counts.",null,"Outside the passage: no such arithmetic appears, and the author rejects simple counts.","Outside the passage: nothing links the number of receptor genes to how many glomeruli an odorant uses."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q16","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of the olfactory bulb EXCEPT:","choices":["Its signals relay through the thalamus before reaching cortex.","Its output cells send axons directly to the olfactory cortex.","In the mouse it contains roughly 2,000 glomeruli.","Broca noted that in humans it is small relative to the rest of the brain.","Counted by neurons, the human bulb is not unusual among mammals."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"send their axons directly to the olfactory cortex","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"roughly 2,000 in a mouse","choice":2},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"the human olfactory bulb is small relative to the rest of the brain","choice":3},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"the human bulb is not unusual among mammals","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"smell, on its main path, does not"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says every other major sense passes through the thalamus, and smell, on its main path, does not. The other four statements appear in paragraphs 6, 9 and 10.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 10, sentence 2 says the output cells send their axons directly to the olfactory cortex.","Paragraph 6, sentence 2 gives roughly 2,000 glomeruli in a mouse bulb.","Paragraph 9, sentence 2 reports Broca's observation about the bulb's relative size.","Paragraph 9, sentence 4 says the human bulb, counted by neurons, is not unusual among mammals."]},{"id":"rc-o18-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Humans can distinguish more than a trillion smells, far more than scientists believed for most of the twentieth century","The sense of smell is poorly developed in humans because so many of their odorant receptor genes have become pseudogenes","A large family of receptor genes, one per neuron and read in combination, lets the nose identify countless molecules","Buck and Axel's method of loose primers and mixed fragments remains the standard way to find new gene families","The olfactory bulb's direct connection to the amygdala and hippocampus explains why smells trigger vivid memories"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"How the nose makes sense of that crowd"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"The scheme is a combinatorial code"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"let a finite set of detectors cover an open-ended world of molecules"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 poses the question of how the nose handles a crowd of molecules, paragraph 7 names the combinatorial code, and paragraph 11 sums it up: a finite set of detectors covers an open-ended world. The gene family, the one-per-neuron rule and the code are the spine of the passage.","distractorNotes":["One disputed estimate from paragraph 8, and the author does not find it persuasive.","Reversed: paragraph 9 argues that the poor-smell reputation is a myth.",null,"Too narrow and outside: the method is one paragraph, and the passage never calls it a standard.","One detail from paragraph 10, and one the author says to hold loosely."]}]},"rc-o19":{"id":"rc-o19","title":"The Skeleton That Listens","topic":"bone remodeling and mechanical load","field":"physiology","source":"original","words":1370,"paragraphs":[["A dry bone on a museum shelf looks like stone, and the look is wrong.","In a living body the skeleton is tissue: supplied with blood, served by nerves, and taken apart and rebuilt without pause.","By a common estimate roughly a tenth of the adult skeleton is replaced each year.","The material is collagen, the protein of tendon, stiffened by crystals of a calcium phosphate mineral called hydroxyapatite that make up roughly two thirds of its weight.","The mineral supplies hardness, the collagen keeps the bone from shattering, and the whole structure doubles as a reserve holding about 99 percent of the body's calcium."],["Three kinds of cell do the work.","Osteoblasts build, laying down a soft collagen matrix called osteoid that hardens as mineral forms inside it.","Osteoclasts demolish: large cells with many nuclei, drawn from the same blood-forming lineage as macrophages, they seal themselves against a bone surface, pump acid into the sealed space to dissolve the mineral, and release enzymes to digest the collagen.","The third cell is the least famous and the most numerous: when osteoblasts finish building, some are walled in by their own product and become osteocytes, each living in a small chamber called a lacuna and reaching its neighbors through hair-thin channels called canaliculi.","Osteocytes make up more than 90 percent of the cells in bone and form a network threading the entire skeleton."],["Building and demolishing follow a sequence that the American orthopedic surgeon Harold Frost named the basic multicellular unit in the 1960s: a team of cells moving through bone like a road crew.","Osteoclasts arrive first and carve a cavity, a job that takes roughly three weeks.","After a brief pause, osteoblasts fill the cavity with new osteoid over about three months, and the fresh matrix then spends further months hardening.","A full cycle at one site runs four to six months, and in a healthy young adult the amounts removed and replaced closely match, a balance physiologists call coupling.","Trabecular bone, the lattice inside the vertebrae and the ends of long bones, holds about a fifth of the skeleton's mass but turns over several times faster than the dense cortical bone of the shafts."],["Why rebuild a structure that already works?","Remodeling repairs the microscopic cracks of daily loading, releases calcium when the blood runs short, and lets the skeleton adjust its mass to the loads it carries.","The balance does not hold forever: bone mass peaks around age 30 and then declines slowly, because each cycle replaces slightly less than it removes, and in women the loss speeds up after menopause, when falling estrogen frees osteoclasts.","Over decades the deficits add up, and the porous, brittle bone of osteoporosis results."],["That bone answers to load was noticed before anyone could explain it: in 1867 the anatomist Georg Hermann von Meyer published drawings of the lattice inside the upper end of the thigh bone, and Karl Culmann, an engineer in Zurich, remarked that the lattice followed the same curves as the stress lines he had drawn for a crane.","The German surgeon Julius Wolff took up the idea and in 1892 published a book arguing that bone reshapes itself by mathematical rules until its architecture matches the forces it bears.","The claim has been known ever since as Wolff's law, and I think the title flatters it.","Later critics faulted his mathematics, and his tidy right-angle lattices are not quite what one finds in a real femur.","What survives is the plainer observation underneath: bone is laid down where it is stressed and removed where it is not, and that observation has held up remarkably well."],["Frost gave that observation its modern form in the 1980s with the mechanostat, named by analogy with a thermostat: the osteocyte network measures strain, the slight stretching and squeezing of bone under load, against two set points.","Strain below the lower set point marks bone as surplus to be removed, strain above the upper one calls for more bone, and between the two the tissue is maintained.","The model predicts that bone responds locally rather than throughout the body, and the evidence agrees.","A 1977 study of professional tennis players found the cortex of the upper arm bone in the playing arm roughly a third thicker than in the other arm.","Swimmers and cyclists, whose sports spare the skeleton, tend to have lighter bones than runners and weight lifters."],["How does a cell buried in rock feel a load?","The leading explanation is that bending a bone squeezes fluid through the canaliculi past the osteocytes' processes, and the drag of that flow is what the cells detect.","They answer with chemistry: osteocytes secrete a protein called sclerostin that acts as a brake on osteoblasts; loading turns the secretion down and unloading turns it up.","People born without working sclerostin, a rare condition called sclerosteosis, grow bone so dense that the skull can pinch the nerves passing through it; an antibody against sclerostin is now an approved osteoporosis drug.","Osteocytes also govern demolition, releasing a signal called RANKL that osteoclast precursors must receive before they can mature into working osteoclasts, and releasing more of it when neighbors die, as happens in damaged or unloaded bone."],["The sharpest demonstration is removing load altogether.","In orbit, where nothing presses on the skeleton, astronauts on the International Space Station have lost bone mineral from the hip and lower spine at roughly 1 to 1.5 percent a month.","The bones of the forearm and skull, which carry no body weight even on Earth, are largely spared.","The calcium freed by the extra demolition leaves in the urine, raising the risk of kidney stones.","Recovery after landing is slower than the loss, and a year back on the ground is often not enough to restore what six months in orbit removed.","There is evidence, too, that the trabecular lattice, once lost, is not fully rebuilt even when overall density looks normal again."],["One need not leave the planet to see the same effect.","Space agencies mimic it by paying healthy volunteers to lie for weeks or months with the head tilted six degrees below the feet, which shifts fluid toward the head as weightlessness does and takes all weight off the legs.","Their bones thin at rates close to those seen in orbit, and the bedridden patient and the person paralyzed below the waist show the same pattern, losing bone from exactly the limbs that have stopped bearing weight.","The skeleton, in other words, runs a strict economy.","Tissue that is not loaded is treated as surplus and spent, and the osteocytes, not gravity or the vacuum of space, are the accountants."],["If unloading drains bone, loading should refill it, but what kind?","Experiments in the 1980s on turkey wing bones showed that a steady, unchanging load did nothing to prevent bone loss, while a load applied and released a few dozen times a day was enough to maintain the bone and sometimes to add to it.","What bone seems to want is loading that is dynamic rather than static, forceful rather than prolonged, and unusual in its direction.","That rule explains a finding I find sobering: walking, the exercise most often recommended to older adults, does less for the hip and spine than most assume, because it loads the skeleton in exactly the way it is used to.","Since late 2008 the space station has carried a resistance machine built around vacuum cylinders, and crews who train hard on it lose markedly less bone by standard density scans, though finer measures of bone structure still record a loss."],["The picture that emerges is of a tissue in permanent negotiation with its own use.","Osteoblasts and osteoclasts are the parties at the table, but the osteocytes, listening for the push of fluid, set the terms.","I would not go as far as the enthusiasts who promise that the right exercise can rebuild any bone at any age; an old skeleton answers more faintly than a young one.","But the lesson of the astronauts and the bed rest volunteers runs both ways, and a skeleton that is asked, often and forcefully, to do its job will generally do it."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o19-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the hydroxyapatite in bone","choices":["is a form of collagen that keeps bone from shattering under load","makes up about 99 percent of the tissue's weight","is a calcium phosphate mineral that gives bone its hardness","is dissolved by the enzymes that osteoclasts release","is produced by osteoclasts as they dissolve older bone surfaces"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"a calcium phosphate mineral called hydroxyapatite"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"The mineral supplies hardness"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 names hydroxyapatite as a calcium phosphate mineral and then says the mineral supplies hardness while the collagen keeps the bone from shattering.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 1 says collagen, not the mineral, keeps bone from shattering.","Wrong number: 99 percent is the share of the body's calcium that bone holds; the mineral is about two thirds of the tissue's weight.",null,"Mash-up: paragraph 2 says the acid dissolves the mineral, while the enzymes digest the collagen.","Reversed: paragraph 2 says osteoclasts dissolve mineral; osteoblasts lay down the matrix that mineralizes."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, osteoclasts break down bone by","choices":["pumping acid into a sealed space and releasing enzymes that digest collagen","walling themselves into chambers from which they dissolve the surrounding mineral","laying down a soft matrix that draws calcium out of the hardened bone around it","cutting hair-thin channels through the mineral so that fluid can wash it away","secreting a protein called sclerostin that dissolves the mineral crystals"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"pump acid into the sealed space to dissolve the mineral, and release enzymes to digest the collagen"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says osteoclasts seal themselves against a bone surface, pump acid into the sealed space to dissolve the mineral, and release enzymes to digest the collagen.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong cell: being walled into chambers is how osteoblasts become osteocytes, later in paragraph 2.","Reversed: laying down soft matrix is the osteoblasts' job, and that matrix hardens rather than dissolving anything.","Wrong detail: the hair-thin canaliculi are channels that connect osteocytes, not a demolition method.","Wrong paragraph: sclerostin in paragraph 7 is an osteocyte protein that brakes osteoblasts; it dissolves nothing."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q03","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are stated in the passage about osteocytes EXCEPT:","choices":["They begin as osteoblasts that become walled in by their own product.","They make up more than 90 percent of the cells in bone.","They live in small chambers called lacunae.","They reach their neighbors through channels called canaliculi.","They are descended from the same lineage as macrophages."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"walled in by their own product and become osteocytes","choice":0},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"more than 90 percent of the cells in bone","choice":1},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"a small chamber called a lacuna","choice":2},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"reaching its neighbors through hair-thin channels called canaliculi","choice":3},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"drawn from the same blood-forming lineage as macrophages"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 gives the macrophage lineage to osteoclasts, not osteocytes. The other four statements all come from the last two sentences of paragraph 2.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2, sentence 4 says some osteoblasts are walled in by their own product and become osteocytes.","Paragraph 2, sentence 5 says osteocytes make up more than 90 percent of the cells in bone.","Paragraph 2, sentence 4 says each lives in a small chamber called a lacuna.","Paragraph 2, sentence 4 says they reach their neighbors through hair-thin channels called canaliculi.",null]},{"id":"rc-o19-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, in a single remodeling cycle","choices":["osteoblasts arrive first and prepare the surface that osteoclasts later clear","resorption takes about three weeks and formation about three months","osteoclasts and osteoblasts work on the same cavity at the same time","the whole sequence from start to finish lasts roughly three weeks","the new matrix hardens fully before the osteoblasts have finished filling the cavity"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"a job that takes roughly three weeks"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"over about three months"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 gives the timeline: osteoclasts carve the cavity in roughly three weeks, then osteoblasts fill it over about three months, with further months of hardening after that.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 3 says osteoclasts arrive first.",null,"Contradicted: osteoblasts follow after a brief pause, so the two phases are sequential.","Wrong number: three weeks is the resorption phase alone; the full cycle runs four to six months.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says the fresh matrix spends further months hardening after the cavity is filled."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Karl Culmann contributed to the idea behind Wolff's law by","choices":["publishing detailed drawings of the lattice inside the upper end of the thigh bone","arguing in an 1892 book that bone follows mathematical rules","measuring the forces that act on a crane during lifting","noticing that the bone's lattice matched the stress lines of a crane","proving that trabeculae meet at right angles in a real femur"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"remarked that the lattice followed the same curves as the stress lines he had drawn for a crane"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says Culmann, an engineer in Zurich, remarked that von Meyer's lattice followed the same curves as the stress lines he had drawn for a crane.","distractorNotes":["Wrong person: the drawings were published by von Meyer.","Wrong person: the 1892 book was Wolff's.","Outside the passage: Culmann drew stress lines for a crane on paper; nothing says he measured forces during lifting.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 5 says Wolff's tidy right-angle lattices are not quite what one finds in a real femur."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q06","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward Wolff's law is best described as","choices":["respectful but qualified","openly contemptuous","enthusiastically uncritical","detached and neutral","puzzled and uncertain"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"I think the title flatters it"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"that observation has held up remarkably well"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the title of law flatters the claim and that critics faulted Wolff's mathematics, yet it credits the plainer observation underneath as having held up remarkably well. That is respect with reservations.","distractorNotes":[null,"Too strong: the author faults the mathematics but keeps and praises the core observation.","Reversed: the author openly criticizes the law's title and its mathematics.","Too weak: the author takes a personal position with the words I think.","Wrong register: the author states clearly what fails and what survives."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q07","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the study of tennis players in paragraph 6 primarily to","choices":["show that diet and hormones have little effect on the adult skeleton","argue that racket sports are the best exercise for building bone","illustrate that bone responds to load locally rather than throughout the body","explain why swimmers and cyclists tend to have lighter bones than runners","demonstrate that the two set points of the mechanostat can be measured directly"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"The model predicts that bone responds locally rather than throughout the body, and the evidence agrees."}],"why":"Paragraph 6 sets up the study with a prediction, that bone responds locally rather than throughout the body, and the one-arm difference in the players is offered as the evidence that agrees.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: diet and hormones are never discussed in connection with the players.","Outside the passage: the author never ranks sports or recommends tennis.",null,"Wrong detail: the swimmers and cyclists are a separate sentence making a related point, not what the tennis study explains.","Outside the passage: nothing says the set points were measured in the players."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, mechanical loading of a bone causes osteocytes to","choices":["release more sclerostin, which speeds the work of osteoblasts","raise the RANKL signal so that osteoclast precursors mature","die and summon a demolition crew to the site","secrete an antibody that neutralizes sclerostin in the matrix","secrete less sclerostin, easing a brake on osteoblasts"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"acts as a brake on osteoblasts; loading turns the secretion down and unloading turns it up"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says sclerostin is a brake on osteoblasts and that loading turns its secretion down while unloading turns it up.","distractorNotes":["Reversed twice: loading lowers sclerostin, and sclerostin brakes osteoblasts rather than speeding them.","Reversed: paragraph 7 ties more RANKL to osteocytes dying in damaged or unloaded bone, not to loading.","Reversed: osteocytes die in bone that is damaged or starved of load.","Wrong source: the antibody against sclerostin is a drug, not something the cells secrete.",null]},{"id":"rc-o19-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a drug blocking the RANKL signal would most likely","choices":["prevent osteoblasts from being walled into the matrix as osteocytes","reduce the number of osteoclasts able to resorb bone","increase the amount of sclerostin released by osteocytes","raise urinary calcium by freeing mineral from the skeleton","shorten the time osteoblasts need to fill a cavity"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"a signal called RANKL that osteoclast precursors must receive before they can mature into working osteoclasts"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says osteoclast precursors must receive RANKL before they can mature into working osteoclasts. Block the signal and fewer precursors mature, so less bone is resorbed.","distractorNotes":["Unrelated: the walling-in of osteoblasts in paragraph 2 has nothing to do with RANKL.",null,"Unrelated: the passage ties sclerostin to loading, not to RANKL.","Reversed: fewer osteoclasts would mean less mineral freed, not more; the urinary calcium rise in paragraph 8 comes from extra demolition.","Unrelated: the passage ties RANKL to osteoclasts, and nothing connects it to how fast osteoblasts work."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q10","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if the fluid in the canaliculi could not move, a loaded bone would most likely","choices":["be sensed as overloaded and gain mass above the upper set point","shift its remodeling from the cortical bone to the trabecular lattice","respond with a stronger RANKL signal from the osteoclasts themselves","behave as if it were unloaded, with sclerostin secretion rising","harden faster, since the osteoid would no longer be disturbed"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"the drag of that flow is what the cells detect"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"loading turns the secretion down and unloading turns it up"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the drag of fluid flow is what osteocytes detect, and that unloading turns sclerostin up. If the fluid cannot move, the cells would detect no load, and the chemistry would follow the unloaded path.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: without the flow the cells would detect less load, not more.","Unrelated: the cortical and trabecular contrast in paragraph 3 is about turnover rate, not sensing.","Wrong cell: RANKL in paragraph 7 comes from osteocytes, not osteoclasts.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing links osteoid hardening to the movement of canalicular fluid."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q11","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following are true of bone loss during long spaceflights EXCEPT:","choices":["It affects the skull and forearm about as much as the hip.","It proceeds at roughly 1 to 1.5 percent a month in the hip and lower spine.","It raises the risk of kidney stones.","It is not fully reversed within a year of landing in many cases.","It may leave the trabecular lattice incompletely rebuilt even after density recovers."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"1 to 1.5 percent a month","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"raising the risk of kidney stones","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"a year back on the ground is often not enough","choice":3},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"not fully rebuilt even when overall density looks normal again","choice":4},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"The bones of the forearm and skull, which carry no body weight even on Earth, are largely spared."}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the forearm and skull, which carry no body weight even on Earth, are largely spared. The other four statements are all in paragraph 8.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 8, sentence 2 gives the rate of 1 to 1.5 percent a month for the hip and lower spine.","Paragraph 8, sentence 4 says the freed calcium raises the risk of kidney stones.","Paragraph 8, sentence 5 says a year back on the ground is often not enough.","Paragraph 8, sentence 6 says the trabecular lattice is not fully rebuilt even when density looks normal."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q12","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the earliest measurable bone loss in a newly arrived astronaut would most likely appear in","choices":["the shafts of the long bones, where dense cortical bone makes up most of the mass","the skull, where the fluid shift of weightlessness is greatest","the forearm, which carries no body weight even on Earth","the collagen fibers rather than the mineral crystals","the vertebrae, where fast-turning trabecular bone is concentrated"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"the lattice inside the vertebrae and the ends of long bones"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"turns over several times faster than the dense cortical bone of the shafts"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"lost bone mineral from the hip and lower spine"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says trabecular bone, the lattice inside the vertebrae and the ends of long bones, turns over several times faster than cortical bone, and paragraph 8 reports the losses from the hip and lower spine. The fastest-cycling sites are where a shift in the balance would show first.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 3 says the cortical bone of the shafts turns over several times more slowly.","Contradicted: paragraph 8 says the skull is largely spared.","Contradicted: paragraph 8 says the forearm is largely spared because it carries no body weight.","Outside the passage: the losses are reported as bone mineral, and nothing suggests collagen goes first.",null]},{"id":"rc-o19-q13","type":"function","stem":"The author describes bed rest studies in paragraph 9 primarily to","choices":["warn that prolonged hospital stays cause even more bone loss than long spaceflights","show that the loss in orbit comes from unloading rather than from space itself","explain how space agencies select the volunteers for their missions","introduce the vacuum-cylinder machine that astronauts use to exercise","argue that fluid shifting toward the head is the main cause of bone loss"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"One need not leave the planet to see the same effect."},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"the osteocytes, not gravity or the vacuum of space, are the accountants"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 opens by saying one need not leave the planet to see the same effect and closes by crediting the osteocytes, not gravity or the vacuum of space. The bed rest studies isolate unloading as the cause.","distractorNotes":["Wrong comparison: paragraph 9 says the rates are close to those in orbit, not greater.",null,"Outside the passage: the volunteers are paid to lie in bed, and nothing about mission selection is said.","Wrong paragraph: the resistance machine appears in paragraph 10.","Wrong emphasis: the fluid shift is mentioned as a way the posture mimics weightlessness; the paragraph blames the absence of load."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q14","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 9, the word 'economy' most nearly means","choices":["the system of trade and production in a nation","a period of financial hardship or scarcity","a careful and thrifty management of resources","the cheapest class of service available","a branch of knowledge about money and markets"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"The skeleton, in other words, runs a strict economy."},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"Tissue that is not loaded is treated as surplus and spent"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the skeleton runs a strict economy and then explains what that means: tissue that is not loaded is treated as surplus and spent, with the osteocytes as the accountants. The sense is thrift, not a nation's markets.","distractorNotes":["This is the common meaning, but the sentence is about a single skeleton managing its own tissue, not a nation.","Nothing in the sentence describes hardship; it describes a policy of not keeping what is unused.",null,"The travel sense of economy class has no bearing on a sentence about bone.","The academic subject is a different sense; the skeleton is practicing thrift, not studying it."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q15","type":"application","stem":"Based on the passage, which of the following people would the author most likely expect to add the most bone to the legs over several months?","choices":["A swimmer who covers two miles in the pool every morning","A person who stands for an hour each day holding a heavy pack without moving","A cyclist who rides long distances on flat roads several times a week","A person who walks briskly for an hour every evening on familiar streets","A person who performs a short daily set of hard, varied jumps"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"loading that is dynamic rather than static, forceful rather than prolonged, and unusual in its direction"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"a load applied and released a few dozen times a day was enough to maintain the bone and sometimes to add to it"}],"why":"Paragraph 10's rule is that bone wants loading that is dynamic rather than static, forceful rather than prolonged, and unusual in direction, and that a few dozen loading cycles a day can be enough. A short set of hard, varied jumps fits every part of that rule.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 6 says swimming spares the skeleton and leaves swimmers with lighter bones.","Contradicted: the turkey experiments in paragraph 10 showed a steady, unchanging load did nothing to prevent bone loss.","Contradicted: paragraph 6 lists cyclists among athletes with lighter bones because their sport spares the skeleton.","Wrong fit: paragraph 10 says walking does less than assumed because it loads the skeleton in exactly the way it is used to.",null]},{"id":"rc-o19-q16","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, astronauts who train hard on the station's resistance machine","choices":["lose no bone at all, provided they train on it every day of the flight","show losses on standard density scans but none on finer measurements","have used it ever since the station's first long missions back in the late 1990s","lose far less bone by standard scans, though finer measures show some loss","no longer excrete extra calcium in their urine during the flight"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"lose markedly less bone by standard density scans, though finer measures of bone structure still record a loss"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says crews who train hard on the machine lose markedly less bone by standard density scans, though finer measures of bone structure still record a loss.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the passage says markedly less loss, not none, and finer measures still record a loss.","Reversed: it is the finer measures that still show loss while the standard scans look much better.","Wrong date: paragraph 10 says the machine has been aboard since late 2008.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing says the machine removes the urinary calcium rise described in paragraph 8."]},{"id":"rc-o19-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Wolff's law, despite flaws in its mathematics, remains the most accurate description of how bone grows","Bone is living tissue that its cells constantly rebuild in response to the loads it carries","Spaceflight is the single most damaging thing that can happen to the adult human skeleton","Resistance exercise can restore the bone that any person has lost at any age if it is forceful enough","Osteocytes are more important to the skeleton than osteoblasts and osteoclasts combined"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"taken apart and rebuilt without pause"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"bone is laid down where it is stressed and removed where it is not"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"a tissue in permanent negotiation with its own use"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 introduces bone as tissue taken apart and rebuilt without pause, paragraph 5 states the rule that bone is laid down where stressed and removed where not, and paragraph 11 sums the skeleton up as a tissue in permanent negotiation with its own use. The spaceflight, bed rest, and exercise sections are all cases of that one idea.","distractorNotes":["Too narrow and too strong: the author says the title of law flatters the claim, and Wolff is one historical thread.",null,"Too extreme and never said: spaceflight is the sharpest demonstration, not the most damaging event.","Contradicted: paragraph 11 refuses to go as far as the enthusiasts who promise that any bone can be rebuilt at any age.","Too narrow: osteocytes set the terms, but the passage is about the whole remodeling system and what drives it."]}]},"rc-o20":{"id":"rc-o20","title":"Life from Nothing, Tested","topic":"the spontaneous generation debate","field":"history of science","source":"original","words":1376,"paragraphs":[["For most of recorded history, the idea that living things could arise from nonliving matter was not a superstition but an observation.","Aristotle taught that certain insects and worms sprang from decaying matter, and maggots on meat, frogs in spring mud, and mice in granaries seemed to prove him right.","In the early seventeenth century the Flemish physician Jan Baptist van Helmont, a serious chemist, offered a recipe: wheat sealed in a jar with a sweaty shirt would yield mice in about three weeks.","The doctrine, called spontaneous generation, rested on the oldest evidence there is: seeing something happen and not seeing its cause."],["The first real test came in 1668 from Francesco Redi, a physician at the Medici court in Florence.","Redi put pieces of meat, among them snake, fish, eel, and veal, into flasks: some left open, some sealed, and some covered with a fine gauze that let in air but kept out insects.","Maggots appeared in the meat of the open flasks and never in the sealed ones.","The gauze-covered flasks were the telling case: flies, drawn by the smell, laid their eggs on the cloth, and maggots hatched on the gauze but never in the meat below.","Redi concluded that maggots were the young of flies, not the children of rot.","He did not abandon the old doctrine altogether; he allowed that certain worms found inside plants and animals might still arise on their own."],["Within a decade the question had changed scale.","In the 1670s Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, a Delft cloth merchant who ground superb lenses, reported to the Royal Society that pond water and pepper water swarmed with creatures too small for the eye.","An infusion, in the language of the time, was simply water in which some organic material had been steeped, and such liquids reliably filled with these animalcules within days.","Flies might have parents, but no one had seen these creatures lay eggs, and the doctrine retreated to the microscopic world and made a comfortable home there."],["In the 1740s John Needham, an English priest and a Fellow of the Royal Society, tested the matter directly.","He heated mutton gravy, corked it in a flask, and warmed the flask in hot ashes to kill anything the first heating had missed.","After some days the gravy was teeming.","Needham, working alongside the French naturalist Buffon, concluded that heat had destroyed whatever life the broth once held and that a vegetative force in the matter itself had produced the new creatures."],["Lazzaro Spallanzani, an Italian priest and one of the most careful experimenters of his century, did not believe it.","In the 1760s he repeated Needham's work with two changes.","He boiled his infusions far longer, for about an hour, and instead of a cork he sealed each flask by melting its glass neck shut.","Nothing grew.","When he cracked a seal and let air reach the broth, the animalcules returned within days."],["Needham objected that an hour of boiling had destroyed the vegetative force, and that the air in a sealed, heated flask had been spoiled, so the experiment proved nothing about ordinary air.","His objections are often presented as the excuses of a loser, and I think that is unfair: it is easy to smile at the first, but the second was sound, and it took the better part of a century to answer.","In 1837 Theodor Schwann passed air through a heated tube before letting it reach a boiled broth, and the broth stayed clear.","In the 1850s Heinrich Schroeder and Theodor von Dusch plugged flask necks with cotton wool, which passed air but trapped dust, and most boiled broths stayed clear.","Milk and some meat infusions spoiled anyway, and the doctrine's defenders noticed."],["The decisive round opened in 1859, when Felix Pouchet of Rouen published a long treatise defending spontaneous generation with experiments of his own.","The French Academy of Sciences offered a prize for experiments that would settle the question, and Louis Pasteur, fresh from his work on fermentation, entered.","His best known device was the swan-neck flask: boiled broth in a flask whose neck had been drawn out into a long curve that dipped and rose again, open to the air at its far end.","Air could move freely in and out, but the dust it carried settled in the bend and never reached the liquid.","Such broths stayed clear for months; some remain unspoiled today.","Tilt one so the broth washed into the bend, or snap off the neck, and it spoiled within days; the only change was whether dust touched the liquid."],["Pasteur also examined the air itself, drawing it through guncotton and finding in the dissolved plug organized bodies that looked like the germs of the microbes spoiling his broths.","Then he carried sealed flasks of boiled broth to places with different air and briefly opened a set in each: in the still air of a cellar beneath the Paris Observatory, on a hill in the Jura, and on the Mer de Glace glacier at about 2,000 meters.","The cleaner the air, the fewer flasks spoiled; on the glacier only one flask in twenty spoiled.","The Academy awarded him its prize in 1862, and in a lecture at the Sorbonne in 1864 he declared that the doctrine would never recover from the blow."],["It was a fine piece of theater, and I think it was premature.","Pouchet had worked with hay infusions, while Pasteur worked mostly with sugared yeast water.","Pouchet's boiled hay flasks spoiled repeatedly, and Pasteur, who could not reproduce the result with his own broths, assumed carelessness.","A commission of the Academy set terms for a direct comparison in 1864, but Pouchet withdrew in protest, and the two broths were never tested side by side.","We now know that hay carries bacterial spores that survive boiling at 100 degrees Celsius, so Pouchet's flasks were spoiling for reasons that had nothing to do with carelessness.","In 1870 Thomas Henry Huxley gave the two positions their modern labels, biogenesis for life from life and abiogenesis for life from nonliving matter, and the English physician Henry Charlton Bastian kept defending abiogenesis with boiled infusions of his own."],["The man who finished the argument was a physicist.","John Tyndall of the Royal Institution in London had spent years studying how light scatters from dust in the air, and he knew that a beam through a closed box would show the dust as a glowing track until it settled.","In the mid-1870s he built a cabinet with a glass front, coated its inner walls with glycerin to hold whatever landed on them, and waited until a beam showed nothing at all.","Boiled broths in this optically empty air stayed clear for months, while identical broths in ordinary air spoiled within days.","Then the experiment failed.","After he brought old hay into the laboratory, boiled broths began to spoil even in the cabinet, and a single boiling no longer protected them.","Tyndall concluded that some bacteria pass through a resting phase that survives boiling and later wakes, which fit the spores Ferdinand Cohn had described in the hay bacterium in 1876.","His remedy was patient rather than violent: boil briefly, let the broth stand so the resting forms could wake into their vulnerable state, boil again, and repeat several times.","The method, now called discontinuous heating, sterilized broths that an hour of continuous boiling could not."],["Looking back over two centuries, what strikes me is how many times the question was answered and how reliably it returned at a smaller scale.","Redi settled it for flies and the microscope reopened it; Spallanzani settled it for sealed flasks and the air objection reopened it; Pasteur settled it for his broths and Pouchet's hay reopened it, whatever he said at the Sorbonne.","Each answer was correct within its limits, and each limit was invisible until someone tested the next case.","The lesson is not that the doctrine's defenders were fools; many were careful, and Needham's complaint about spoiled air was better than the first answers to it.","It is that an experiment proves no more than the hardiest contaminant allows it to prove, and the final word belonged to the person who found that contaminant."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o20-q01","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions van Helmont's recipe for mice in paragraph 1 primarily to","choices":["mock the state of chemistry in the early seventeenth century as hopelessly naive","explain why mice were so often discovered in granaries","show that a serious scientist could treat the doctrine as plain observation","introduce the series of flask experiments that Redi performed in 1668","argue that the doctrine was a superstition rather than an observation"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"a serious chemist, offered a recipe"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":1,"quote":"was not a superstition but an observation"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 opens by saying the idea was not a superstition but an observation, then presents van Helmont, a serious chemist, offering a recipe as if it were a fact of nature. The recipe shows how literally and respectably the doctrine was once held.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the author calls van Helmont a serious chemist and never mocks the period.","Wrong focus: the granary mice are one of the observations the doctrine rested on, not something the recipe explains.",null,"Wrong paragraph: Redi's experiment is introduced in paragraph 2 as the first real test, not by the recipe.","Reversed: paragraph 1 says the idea was not a superstition but an observation."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, in Redi's gauze-covered flasks","choices":["maggots appeared in the meat because the gauze let in air","flies laid eggs on the cloth and maggots hatched there, not in the meat","no flies came near because the gauze held in the smell of the meat","the meat stayed free of maggots only because the flasks had been sealed shut","maggots appeared in the meat only after the gauze was removed"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"laid their eggs on the cloth, and maggots hatched on the gauze but never in the meat below"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 calls the gauze-covered flasks the telling case: flies, drawn by the smell, laid their eggs on the cloth, and maggots hatched on the gauze but never in the meat below.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the gauze let in air, but paragraph 2 says the maggots never reached the meat.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 2 says flies were drawn by the smell and laid eggs on the cloth.","Wrong flasks: the sealed flasks were a separate group; the gauze flasks were open to the air.","Outside the passage: Redi never removes the gauze in the account given."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q03","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Redi would most likely have agreed with which of the following statements?","choices":["Every living creature descends from parents of its own kind.","Maggots can arise from rotting meat whenever flies are kept away from it.","Gauze is a more reliable barrier against insects than a sealed flask is.","The microscope would eventually overturn his conclusion about flies.","Some worms living inside plants or animals may arise without parents."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"he allowed that certain worms found inside plants and animals might still arise on their own"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says Redi did not abandon the old doctrine altogether and allowed that certain worms found inside plants and animals might still arise on their own. That is the one statement he is on record as accepting.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: Redi kept an exception for worms inside plants and animals, so he did not hold the universal claim.","Reversed: Redi's sealed and gauze-covered flasks showed that keeping flies away prevents maggots.","Outside the passage: Redi used both barriers, and the passage never ranks one above the other.","Outside the passage: the microscope reopened the question in paragraph 3, but nothing says Redi expected it, and his conclusion about flies was never overturned.",null]},{"id":"rc-o20-q04","type":"detail","stem":"As described in paragraph 3, an infusion was","choices":["water in which organic material had been steeped","a drop of pond water examined under a hand-ground lens","a broth that had been boiled and sealed inside a glass flask","a liquid corked in a flask and then warmed in hot ashes","Leeuwenhoek's own name for the creatures he saw in water"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"was simply water in which some organic material had been steeped"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 defines the word directly: an infusion, in the language of the time, was simply water in which some organic material had been steeped.","distractorNotes":[null,"Half right: pond water was one of the liquids Leeuwenhoek examined, but the definition in paragraph 3 is about steeping organic material.","Wrong paragraph: sealed, boiled broths belong to Spallanzani in paragraph 5.","Wrong paragraph: corking and hot ashes describe Needham's method in paragraph 4.","Wrong word: the creatures were called animalcules; an infusion is the liquid."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Needham concluded from his experiment with mutton gravy that","choices":["the cork had failed to keep outside air away from the gravy","the heating had been too brief to kill everything in the broth","the creatures had entered the flask along with the hot ashes","a vegetative force in the matter itself had produced the creatures","flies had laid their eggs in the gravy before the flask had been corked"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"a vegetative force in the matter itself had produced the new creatures"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says Needham concluded that heat had destroyed whatever life the broth once held and that a vegetative force in the matter itself had produced the new creatures.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: Needham believed his cork and his heating had excluded outside life; the complaint about air came later and in a different form.","Reversed: Needham believed the heat had destroyed whatever life the broth held.","Outside the passage: the ashes were used to heat the corked flask, and no one blamed them.",null,"Wrong paragraph: flies and eggs belong to Redi in paragraph 2; Needham's creatures were microscopic."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q06","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of Spallanzani's experiments EXCEPT:","choices":["He boiled his infusions for about an hour.","He passed heated air into each flask before sealing it.","He sealed each flask by melting its glass neck shut.","He found that nothing grew in the boiled, sealed flasks.","He saw animalcules return after cracking a seal."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"He boiled his infusions far longer, for about an hour","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"he sealed each flask by melting its glass neck shut","choice":2},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"Nothing grew.","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"the animalcules returned within days","choice":4},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"In 1837 Theodor Schwann passed air through a heated tube"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 gives Spallanzani's two changes, longer boiling and a melted seal, and his two results, nothing grew and the animalcules returned when a seal was cracked. Heated air belongs to Schwann in paragraph 6, seventy years later.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 says he boiled his infusions far longer, for about an hour.",null,"Paragraph 5 says he sealed each flask by melting its glass neck shut.","Paragraph 5 says plainly that nothing grew.","Paragraph 5 says that when he cracked a seal the animalcules returned within days."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, the word 'sound' most nearly means","choices":["loud enough to be heard clearly","deep and unbroken, as sleep can be","undamaged by the heat of boiling","stated at length and with feeling","well reasoned and hard to dismiss"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"the second was sound, and it took the better part of a century to answer"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the second objection was sound and took the better part of a century to answer: an objection that is valid and hard to dismiss. The everyday meanings of the word, noise or deep sleep, do not fit a sentence about an argument.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday noise meaning of the word, and nothing in the sentence is about hearing.","A sound sleep is another common sense, but the sentence is about an objection, not rest.","Too literal: the sentence is about the quality of an argument, not about what heat did to anything.","Nothing in paragraph 6 says how forcefully or at what length Needham made the objection.",null]},{"id":"rc-o20-q08","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward Needham's objections in paragraph 6 is best described as","choices":["dismissive","amused","respectful","alarmed","indifferent"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"I think that is unfair"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"Needham's complaint about spoiled air was better than the first answers to it"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the objections are often presented as the excuses of a loser and that the author finds that unfair, then credits the second objection as sound. Paragraph 11 adds that Needham's complaint was better than the first answers to it. That is respect for an opponent, not scorn.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author objects to dismissing Needham and calls one objection sound.","Wrong register: the author admits it is easy to smile at the first objection and then declines to.",null,"Wrong register: the author's judgment is calm, and no danger is involved.","Too weak: the author takes a clear position in Needham's defense."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a swan-neck flask kept its broth clear because","choices":["dust in the air that entered settled in the bend of the neck","the curved neck was melted shut once the broth had been boiled","the broth was boiled far longer than in any earlier experiment","a plug of cotton wool in the neck trapped the dust in the air","air was unable to pass through the long curve of the neck"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"the dust it carried settled in the bend and never reached the liquid"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says air could move freely in and out of the swan-neck flask, but the dust it carried settled in the bend and never reached the liquid.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong paragraph: melting the neck shut was Spallanzani's method in paragraph 5; the swan neck stayed open at its far end.","Outside the passage: paragraph 7 says nothing about how long Pasteur boiled, and boiling time was Spallanzani's variable.","Wrong paragraph: cotton wool plugs belong to Schroeder and von Dusch in paragraph 6.","Reversed: paragraph 7 says air could move freely in and out."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when Pasteur opened flasks of boiled broth on the Mer de Glace,","choices":["every flask spoiled within days","about half of the flasks spoiled","none of the flasks spoiled at all","only one flask in twenty spoiled","all but one of the flasks spoiled"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"on the glacier only one flask in twenty spoiled"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the cleaner the air, the fewer flasks spoiled, and that on the glacier only one flask in twenty spoiled.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the glacier had the cleanest air and the fewest spoiled flasks.","Wrong number: paragraph 8 gives one in twenty, not half.","Too strong: one flask did spoil.",null,"Reversed: one spoiled and the rest stayed clear."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author contrasts Pouchet's hay infusions with Pasteur's yeast water in paragraph 9 primarily to","choices":["show that Pasteur's broths spoiled more easily than Pouchet's","explain how both men could have reported their results honestly","argue that the Academy should have awarded its prize to Pouchet","demonstrate that hay is a poor material for preparing an infusion","suggest that Pasteur chose a broth he knew in advance would not spoil"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"Pouchet's flasks were spoiling for reasons that had nothing to do with carelessness"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"assumed carelessness"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says Pasteur assumed carelessness, then explains that hay carries spores that survive boiling, so Pouchet's flasks were spoiling for reasons that had nothing to do with carelessness. The contrast in broths accounts for the contradictory results without calling either man dishonest.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: it was Pouchet's hay flasks that spoiled repeatedly.",null,"Outside the passage: the author calls Pasteur's declaration premature but never says the prize was wrongly awarded.","Wrong focus: hay's spores explain Pouchet's results; the author is not giving advice on making infusions.","Too strong: paragraph 9 says Pasteur could not reproduce the result and assumed carelessness, not that he chose his broth to win."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q12","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if Pouchet had boiled sugared yeast water instead of hay infusions, his flasks would most likely have","choices":["stayed clear, as Pasteur's own flasks did","spoiled even sooner than his hay flasks did","spoiled only when opened at low altitude","been accepted by the commission in 1864","shown spores under the microscope"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"hay carries bacterial spores that survive boiling"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"Pasteur, who could not reproduce the result with his own broths"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 traces Pouchet's spoiled flasks to spores in hay that survive boiling, and says Pasteur could not reproduce the spoiling with his own broths. Take away the hay and the spores go with it.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: the hay was the source of the spoiling.","Mixed up: altitude belongs to Pasteur's field trip in paragraph 8, not to Pouchet's broths.","Outside the passage: Pouchet withdrew before any comparison, and the commission never judged his flasks.","Outside the passage: nothing says Pouchet examined spores, and yeast water is the broth without them."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q13","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Thomas Henry Huxley","choices":["defended abiogenesis with boiled infusions of his own","served on the Academy commission that Pouchet refused","first described the spores of the hay bacterium","gave the two sides of the debate their modern names","awarded Pasteur the Academy's prize in 1862"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"In 1870 Thomas Henry Huxley gave the two positions their modern labels"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says that in 1870 Huxley gave the two positions their modern labels, biogenesis and abiogenesis.","distractorNotes":["Wrong person: paragraph 9 gives that role to Henry Charlton Bastian.","Wrong country and paragraph: the commission was French and met in 1864; Huxley is not connected to it.","Wrong person: paragraph 10 credits Ferdinand Cohn with describing the spores.",null,"Wrong body: the French Academy of Sciences awarded the prize."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q14","type":"except","stem":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage in connection with Tyndall's cabinet?","choices":["a glass front","inner walls coated with glycerin","a beam of light used to test the air inside","boiled broths that stayed clear for months","flask necks plugged with cotton wool"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"a cabinet with a glass front","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"coated its inner walls with glycerin","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"waited until a beam showed nothing at all","choice":2},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"stayed clear for months","choice":3},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"plugged flask necks with cotton wool"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 describes the cabinet: a glass front, glycerin on the inner walls, a beam of light to test the air, and broths that stayed clear for months inside it. Cotton wool plugs belong to Schroeder and von Dusch in paragraph 6.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 10 says he built a cabinet with a glass front.","Paragraph 10 says he coated its inner walls with glycerin.","Paragraph 10 says he waited until a beam of light showed nothing at all.","Paragraph 10 says broths in the optically empty air stayed clear for months.",null]},{"id":"rc-o20-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Tyndall's broths began to spoil inside the cabinet after the hay arrived because","choices":["the glycerin on the walls had lost its grip on the settling dust","the beam of light could no longer be seen clearly through the glass front","the air now carried resting forms that one boiling could not kill","the hay had been steeped in water for too short a time","the glass front of the cabinet had been left open to the room"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"a single boiling no longer protected them"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":7,"quote":"some bacteria pass through a resting phase that survives boiling and later wakes"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says that after the hay arrived, boiled broths spoiled even in the cabinet and a single boiling no longer protected them, and Tyndall concluded that some bacteria pass through a resting phase that survives boiling. The hay had brought those forms into the room.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: nothing says the glycerin stopped working.","Outside the passage: the beam test is never said to fail.",null,"Outside the passage: no steeping time is given, and the problem was in the broths, not the hay.","Outside the passage: no door or opening is mentioned, and the broths spoiled even in the cabinet."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q16","type":"application","stem":"A cannery seals jars of vegetables after one continuous hour of boiling, yet some jars still spoil. Which change does the reasoning in paragraph 10 most strongly support?","choices":["Boil the jars for three continuous hours instead of one before sealing them.","Boil the jars briefly, let them stand, and boil again, repeating the cycle.","Filter the air of the cannery through cotton wool before sealing the jars.","Coat the inner walls of the cannery with glycerin so dust settles out of the air.","Move the cannery to a high mountain where the air carries fewer germs."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":8,"quote":"boil briefly, let the broth stand so the resting forms could wake into their vulnerable state, boil again, and repeat several times"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":9,"quote":"sterilized broths that an hour of continuous boiling could not"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says Tyndall's remedy was to boil briefly, let the broth stand so the resting forms could wake into their vulnerable state, boil again, and repeat, and that this sterilized broths that an hour of continuous boiling could not. Jars that survive an hour of boiling and still spoil are the same problem.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 10 says the resting forms survive an hour of continuous boiling, so more of the same is not the remedy.",null,"Wrong mechanism: cotton wool in paragraph 6 keeps dust out of the air, but the problem here is a form already inside the jar that survives boiling.","Wrong mechanism: the glycerin in paragraph 10 settles dust from the air; it does not kill what is already in the broth.","Wrong mechanism: cleaner air in paragraph 8 means fewer flasks get contaminated, but these jars are sealed and already hold forms that survive boiling."]},{"id":"rc-o20-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Pasteur's swan-neck flasks were the single decisive experiment that ended belief in spontaneous generation once and for all.","The defenders of spontaneous generation were careless experimenters whose results could never be reproduced by others.","The microscope made the question of spontaneous generation impossible to answer by experiment alone.","The question was answered repeatedly at one scale and returned at a smaller one until heat-resistant spores were found.","Tyndall's discontinuous heating mattered more to the practice of medicine than any of Pasteur's experiments with flasks."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"how many times the question was answered and how reliably it returned at a smaller scale"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"an experiment proves no more than the hardiest contaminant allows it to prove"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"I think it was premature"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 states it outright: the question was answered many times and reliably returned at a smaller scale, and the final word belonged to the person who found the hardiest contaminant. The whole passage, from Redi to Tyndall, is built to make that case.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 9 calls Pasteur's declaration premature, and paragraph 11 says Pouchet's hay reopened the question.","Contradicted: paragraph 11 says many defenders were careful, and paragraph 9 clears Pouchet of carelessness.","Too strong: the microscope reopened the question in paragraph 3, but experiments went on answering it.",null,"Outside the passage: the author admires Tyndall's result but never ranks it against Pasteur for medicine."]}]},"rc-o21":{"id":"rc-o21","title":"The Skin That Paints Itself","topic":"camouflage in cephalopods","field":"zoology","source":"original","words":1359,"paragraphs":[["An octopus resting on a patch of weed can be invisible until it moves, and then it is simply there, a pale animal jetting away from what looked like bare algae.","Aristotle noticed the trick more than two thousand years ago, writing that the octopus changes its color to resemble the stones around it.","A chameleon or a flounder takes seconds to minutes to shift its color; a cuttlefish can switch its entire body pattern in under a second.","The system behind this belongs to the coleoid cephalopods, the octopuses, cuttlefishes, and squids, and not to their shelled relative the nautilus."],["The engine of the change is the chromatophore, a word that means something different here than in a fish or a frog.","In those vertebrates a chromatophore is a single cell that darkens or pales by moving pigment granules within itself, a process that takes seconds to minutes and is often driven by hormones.","In a cephalopod a chromatophore is a small organ: an elastic sac of yellow, orange, red, brown, or black pigment ringed by radial muscle fibers, usually fifteen to twenty-five of them, attached like the spokes of a wheel.","When the fibers contract they pull the sac outward into a flat disc, spreading the pigment into view; when they relax, the sac's own elasticity snaps it back to a dot.","Fully opened, a chromatophore in a large octopus can be more than a millimeter across; retracted, it is nearly invisible."],["What makes the organ fast is the wiring.","Each radial muscle is driven directly by motor neurons whose cell bodies lie in the chromatophore lobes of the brain, so a decision made there reaches the skin along a nerve, with no hormone in the loop.","Cut the nerve to a patch of skin and the patch goes pale and still, its chromatophores slack while the skin around it keeps flickering.","An adult common octopus carries on the order of a million of these organs, and the brain drives them in groups, so the skin holds a repertoire of patterns rather than a photograph.","The British zoologist John Messenger summarized the system in a 2001 review that remains a standard reference, and I know of no better example of a nervous system reaching the surface of the body."],["Pigment alone cannot produce the blues and greens that cuttlefish and squid display; no chromatophore holds a blue pigment.","Those colors come from a layer of cells beneath the chromatophores called iridophores, each holding stacks of thin plates of a protein called reflectin, identified in 2004 in the Hawaiian bobtail squid.","Light reflecting from the successive plates interferes with itself, canceling some wavelengths and reinforcing others, so the color depends on the spacing of the plates rather than on any pigment.","In some squid the spacing is adjustable: the nerve transmitter acetylcholine makes the reflectin condense, shifting the reflected color toward the blue over several seconds.","A third cell type, the leucophore, scatters all wavelengths about equally, so it looks white in white light, yellowish under yellow light, and takes on whatever color the surroundings supply.","Leucophores are generally thought to be passive, though a few squid can switch theirs on and off, and the matter is not settled."],["The patterns the animals produce with all this equipment are not infinite.","Working with the European cuttlefish, Roger Hanlon and his colleagues sorted its body patterns into three classes: uniform, evenly light or dark; mottle, a scatter of small light and dark patches; and disruptive, a few large, high-contrast components that break the body's outline into pieces that do not add up to an animal.","The best-known disruptive component is the white square, a pale patch on the mantle.","Hanlon's group, at the Marine Biological Laboratory in Massachusetts, placed cuttlefish on checkerboards and found that they produced disruptive patterns when the checks were roughly the size of their own white square, and other patterns when the checks were much smaller.","The animal, in other words, measures the scene and selects a pattern from a limited set rather than copying what it sees."],["Here the story runs into a puzzle that has occupied biologists for half a century.","Cephalopod camouflage looks colored, and to a diver it often seems to match the hue of sand or weed, yet the eyes that direct it appear to be color-blind.","Color vision in the usual sense requires at least two kinds of photoreceptor tuned to different wavelengths, so the brain can compare their responses, and the retinas of almost every cephalopod examined contain a single visual pigment.","In 1977 Messenger reported that octopuses could learn to tell a brighter target from a dimmer one but not two colors apart, and in 1996 he and Justin Marshall showed that cuttlefish on backgrounds of two colors matched in brightness responded as if the backgrounds were plain.","The only clear exception found so far is the firefly squid, a small bioluminescent species of Japanese waters that carries three visual pigments and lives in open water rather than on a seabed."],["The first explanation is that the match is less exact than it looks.","The leucophores and iridophores reflect whatever light reaches them, so a cuttlefish on yellow sand under yellowish light returns yellow from its skin without having to see it.","Spectral measurements of cuttlefish on natural backgrounds have generally found the match good but not perfect, and for a fish searching a seabed a mismatch in brightness or pattern is probably far more revealing than one in hue.","I think this explanation carries much of the weight, but a passive reflector can only return the light it is given, and the chromatophores laid over it still have to be the right colors."],["In 2016 Alexander Stubbs, a graduate student at the University of California, Berkeley, and his father Christopher Stubbs, a Harvard physicist, proposed a bolder idea: that cephalopods might extract color from a flaw most eyes suppress.","A lens bends blue light more strongly than red, so the two come to focus at slightly different distances, an effect called chromatic aberration.","The odd pupils of cephalopods, the W shape of the cuttlefish and the horizontal slot of the octopus, admit light through the edges of the lens rather than its center, which worsens the aberration.","Their calculations showed that an animal with a single pigment could in principle work out an object's color by noting the focus setting at which it becomes sharp.","The idea is elegant, and it would explain why an animal keeps a pupil shape that costs it sharpness, but it remains a calculation: no one has yet shown a cuttlefish doing it, and I would hold the hypothesis loosely until someone does."],["A third line of evidence moves the question from the eye to the skin.","In 2015 Desmond Ramirez and Todd Oakley showed that patches of skin cut from the California two-spot octopus expand their chromatophores when light falls on them, with no eye or brain attached, and that the skin contains the same opsin, or light-catching protein, found in the retina.","The response is slow, taking seconds, and because the skin uses the one pigment the eye uses, it cannot by itself add color discrimination; what it may add is local information about brightness.","Cephalopods also detect the polarization of light, which we cannot see at all, and which gives them another channel of contrast underwater.","Whether any of these channels combine into something like color vision is unknown; the honest summary is that the animals produce colored camouflage by means we do not fully understand."],["What is certain is simpler than any of these hypotheses.","The chromatophore is a muscle-driven organ wired straight to the brain; the reflecting cells beneath it supply the colors pigment cannot; the brain selects patterns by the size and contrast of what it sees; and all of this happens in less than a second.","The rest, the question of how a color-blind animal paints itself to suit a colored world, is open, and I would rather leave it open than close it with a tidy answer the evidence does not yet support."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o21-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a chromatophore in a fish or a frog differs from one in a cephalopod in that the vertebrate chromatophore","choices":["is a small organ whose pigment sac is pulled open by a ring of muscles","contains a blue pigment that cephalopod chromatophores lack","is a single cell that moves pigment granules within itself","changes color in a fraction of a second under direct nerve control","reflects light from stacked plates rather than holding any pigment"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"a chromatophore is a single cell that darkens or pales by moving pigment granules within itself"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says that in fish and frogs a chromatophore is a single cell that darkens or pales by moving pigment granules within itself, and then contrasts that with the cephalopod version, which is a small organ.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 2 describes the muscle-ringed sac as the cephalopod chromatophore, not the vertebrate one.","Contradicted: paragraph 4 says no chromatophore holds a blue pigment.",null,"Reversed: speed and direct nerve control are what paragraph 3 attributes to the cephalopod organ.","Wrong cell type: stacked reflecting plates belong to the iridophores of paragraph 4."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the pigment in a cephalopod chromatophore comes into view when","choices":["radial muscle fibers contract and pull the elastic sac into a flat disc","the elastic sac relaxes and snaps outward into a dot","hormones carried in the blood signal the sac to release its granules","acetylcholine makes the pigment inside the sac condense","the muscle fibers relax and allow the sac to spread under its own elasticity"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"When the fibers contract they pull the sac outward into a flat disc, spreading the pigment into view"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says that when the fibers contract they pull the sac outward into a flat disc, spreading the pigment into view; relaxing does the opposite.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: the dot is the retracted state, which the sac snaps back to when the fibers relax.","Wrong mechanism: hormones are tied to the vertebrate chromatophore in paragraph 2, and paragraph 3 says there is no hormone in the loop.","Wrong cell: acetylcholine acts on reflectin in iridophores, in paragraph 4.","Reversed: paragraph 2 says relaxation lets the sac's elasticity pull it back to a dot, not spread it."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q03","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if the radial muscles of a cephalopod's chromatophores were temporarily paralyzed, the animal's skin would most likely","choices":["darken, since the pigment sacs would spread without resistance","keep its current pattern frozen in place until the muscles recovered","shift toward blue as the iridophores took over","turn pale, since each sac would retract on its own","flicker more rapidly as the brain tried to restore control"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"when they relax, the sac's own elasticity snaps it back to a dot"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"the patch goes pale and still, its chromatophores slack"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says the sac retracts on its own elasticity whenever the fibers relax, and paragraph 3 shows what that looks like when the nerve is cut: the patch goes pale and still. Paralyzed muscles cannot contract, so every sac would retract.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: spreading requires the muscles to contract; without them the sacs retract.","Contradicted: the sac does not hold its shape on its own; paragraph 2 says elasticity snaps it back to a dot.","Unrelated: nothing in the passage says iridophores compensate when chromatophores fail.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 3 says a patch without working control goes still, not more active."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q04","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions cutting the nerve to a patch of skin in paragraph 3 primarily to","choices":["warn that the chromatophore system is easily damaged","show that the chromatophores depend on direct control from the brain","explain why an adult octopus needs so many chromatophores","introduce the role of hormones in regulating the skin's pattern","describe how scientists first counted the organs in a single patch of skin"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"a decision made there reaches the skin along a nerve, with no hormone in the loop"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"Cut the nerve to a patch of skin and the patch goes pale and still"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 is about the wiring: the brain reaches the skin along a nerve with no hormone in the loop. The cut-nerve example demonstrates that dependence, since the patch stops working the moment its nerve is gone.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: the author never says the system is fragile; the example is about control, not damage.",null,"Wrong detail: the million-organ count comes later in the paragraph and is not explained by the nerve cut.","Reversed: the same paragraph says there is no hormone in the loop.","Outside the passage: no counting method is described."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the color produced by an iridophore depends on","choices":["which of the five pigments its elastic sac contains","how brightly the water around the animal is lit","the number of radial muscle fibers attached around it","the opsin it shares with the animal's retina","the spacing of its stacked plates of reflectin"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"the color depends on the spacing of the plates rather than on any pigment"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says light reflecting from the successive plates interferes with itself, so the color depends on the spacing of the plates rather than on any pigment.","distractorNotes":["Wrong cell: the five pigments and the elastic sac belong to the chromatophore in paragraph 2.","Wrong cell: taking on the surrounding light is what paragraph 4 says the leucophore does.","Wrong cell: radial muscles ring the chromatophore sac, not the iridophore.","Wrong paragraph: the shared opsin is in the skin study of paragraph 9 and has nothing to do with iridophore color.",null]},{"id":"rc-o21-q06","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of iridophores EXCEPT:","choices":["They hold a blue pigment that the chromatophores lack.","They lie in a layer beneath the chromatophores.","Their plates are built from a protein called reflectin.","They produce the blues and greens that pigment cannot.","In some squid their color shifts over several seconds."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"a layer of cells beneath the chromatophores called iridophores","choice":1},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"stacks of thin plates of a protein called reflectin","choice":2},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"Those colors come from a layer of cells beneath the chromatophores called iridophores","choice":3},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"shifting the reflected color toward the blue over several seconds","choice":4},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"the color depends on the spacing of the plates rather than on any pigment"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says iridophore color depends on the spacing of the plates rather than on any pigment, and the paragraph opens by saying no chromatophore holds a blue pigment either. The other four statements are all in paragraph 4.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 4, sentence 2 places iridophores in a layer beneath the chromatophores.","Paragraph 4, sentence 2 says the plates are made of a protein called reflectin.","Paragraph 4 opens by saying pigment cannot make the blues and greens and that those colors come from the iridophores.","Paragraph 4, sentence 4 says the reflected color shifts toward the blue over several seconds in some squid."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q07","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a cuttlefish leucophore viewed under blue light would most likely appear","choices":["white, because it scatters every wavelength about equally","dark, because it absorbs the blue light it cannot reflect","bluish, because it takes on the color of the light reaching it","yellowish, because its stacked plates condense in blue light","red, because blue light is reinforced and the other colors canceled"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"it looks white in white light, yellowish under yellow light, and takes on whatever color the surroundings supply"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says a leucophore looks white in white light, yellowish under yellow light, and takes on whatever color the surroundings supply. Run that rule with blue light and the cell looks bluish.","distractorNotes":["Half right: it does scatter all wavelengths equally, but that is exactly why it returns the color it is given, and the light given here is blue.","Reversed: paragraph 4 says the leucophore scatters light, not that it absorbs it.",null,"Wrong cell and wrong light: condensing plates belong to the iridophore, and yellow comes from yellow light.","Wrong cell: canceling and reinforcing wavelengths is the iridophore mechanism, and it would not turn blue light red."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, cuttlefish placed on checkerboards produced disruptive patterns when","choices":["the checks were a great deal smaller than the animal's own white square","the checks were roughly the size of the animal's white square","the board was uniformly light or uniformly dark in tone","the checks were in two colors matched for brightness","the board was lit from above by yellowish light"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"they produced disruptive patterns when the checks were roughly the size of their own white square"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says Hanlon's group found disruptive patterns when the checks were roughly the size of the animal's own white square, and other patterns when the checks were much smaller.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: much smaller checks produced other patterns, according to paragraph 5.",null,"Wrong detail: a uniform board is not described; uniform is one of the animal's pattern classes.","Wrong paragraph: brightness-matched colors are the 1996 color-blindness test in paragraph 6, where the animals responded as if the board were plain.","Outside the passage: yellowish light appears in paragraph 7 and has nothing to do with checkerboards."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q09","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle described in the last sentence of paragraph 5?","choices":["A photocopier reproduces a document line by line so that the copy matches the original exactly","A painter mixes a fresh shade for every patch of sky until the canvas matches the view","A mirror shows whatever object is placed in front of it, in every detail","A musician with three songs picks the one whose tempo fits the mood of the room","A student memorizes a list of answers and repeats them regardless of the question"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"measures the scene and selects a pattern from a limited set rather than copying what it sees"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"The patterns the animals produce with all this equipment are not infinite."}],"why":"The rule in paragraph 5 is that the animal measures the scene and selects from a limited set rather than copying. A musician who reads the room and picks one of three songs is doing the same thing: measuring, then choosing from a small repertoire.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: a photocopier copies exactly, which is what the passage says the animal does not do.","Reversed: mixing a new shade for every patch is copying with an unlimited palette, not selecting from a limited set.","Reversed: a mirror copies the scene; it makes no selection at all.",null,"Half right: the set is limited, but the student ignores the scene instead of measuring it."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q10","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the firefly squid in paragraph 6 primarily to","choices":["offer evidence that cephalopod camouflage relies on bioluminescent light","show that color vision is common among squid that live in open water","introduce the species used in the 1996 brightness experiments","argue that the puzzle of color-blind camouflage has been solved","note an exception to the claim that cephalopods have one pigment"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"The only clear exception found so far is the firefly squid"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"the retinas of almost every cephalopod examined contain a single visual pigment"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says almost every cephalopod examined has a single visual pigment, then calls the firefly squid the only clear exception found so far. It is there to qualify the generalization honestly, not to overturn it.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: the squid's bioluminescence is mentioned in passing and never tied to camouflage.","Too strong: the passage calls it the only clear exception, not a common case.","Wrong species: the 1996 experiments in paragraph 6 used cuttlefish.","Reversed: paragraphs 9 and 10 say the puzzle remains open.",null]},{"id":"rc-o21-q11","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Messenger's 1977 experiments showed that octopuses","choices":["learned to tell a brighter target from a dimmer one but not two colors apart","could be trained to distinguish two colors but not two levels of brightness","produced disruptive patterns only when the checks matched their white square","treated backgrounds of two colors matched in brightness as though they were plain backgrounds","expanded their chromatophores when light was shone on patches of their skin"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"octopuses could learn to tell a brighter target from a dimmer one but not two colors apart"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says that in 1977 Messenger reported octopuses could learn to tell a brighter target from a dimmer one but not two colors apart.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: brightness was learnable and color was not.","Wrong paragraph and wrong animal: the checkerboard work in paragraph 5 was done with cuttlefish by Hanlon's group.","Wrong experiment: that is the 1996 cuttlefish result with Justin Marshall, later in the same sentence.","Wrong paragraph: the skin-and-light finding is the 2015 work of Ramirez and Oakley in paragraph 9."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q12","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, the word 'plain' most nearly means","choices":["simple to understand","unattractive","honest and direct","unpatterned","obvious at a glance"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"responded as if the backgrounds were plain"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 contrasts a background of two colors with one the cuttlefish treated as plain: the animals could not see the difference between the squares, so to them the board had no pattern. Plain here means unpatterned.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday sense of plain speech or a plain explanation, but a background is not something the cuttlefish understands.","Plain can mean homely, but the sentence is about what the cuttlefish could see, not about looks.","Plain speaking is another common meaning, and nothing in the sentence involves honesty.",null,"Reversed: the point is that the two colors were not obvious to the animal at all."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a fish hunting over a seabed would be most likely to notice a cuttlefish whose","choices":["hue differed slightly from the sand beneath it","brightness or pattern differed from the background","leucophores reflected the color of the surrounding light","chromatophores were the right colors for the place","iridophores had shifted toward the blue over several seconds"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"for a fish searching a seabed a mismatch in brightness or pattern is probably far more revealing than one in hue"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says that for a fish searching a seabed, a mismatch in brightness or pattern is probably far more revealing than one in hue. So a brightness or pattern mismatch is what would give the cuttlefish away.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 7 says a hue mismatch is the less revealing kind.",null,"Reversed: reflecting the surrounding light is part of how the match is made, not how it fails.","Reversed: the right colors would help the cuttlefish hide, not expose it.","Unrelated: the iridophore color shift in paragraph 4 is not connected to being noticed."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q14","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are stated in the passage about the Stubbs hypothesis EXCEPT:","choices":["It was proposed in 2016 by a graduate student and his physicist father.","It relies on chromatic aberration, the lens's unequal bending of colors.","It would account for a pupil shape that costs the animal sharpness.","It rests on calculations rather than on observed behavior.","It has been confirmed by tests with cuttlefish in the laboratory."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"In 2016 Alexander Stubbs, a graduate student at the University of California, Berkeley, and his father Christopher Stubbs, a Harvard physicist, proposed","choice":0},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"A lens bends blue light more strongly than red, so the two come to focus at slightly different distances, an effect called chromatic aberration.","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"it would explain why an animal keeps a pupil shape that costs it sharpness","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"it remains a calculation","choice":3},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"no one has yet shown a cuttlefish doing it"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the idea remains a calculation and that no one has yet shown a cuttlefish doing it, so it has not been confirmed. The other four statements are all in paragraph 8.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 8, sentence 1 gives the year, the graduate student, and his physicist father.","Paragraph 8, sentence 2 defines chromatic aberration as the lens bending blue more strongly than red.","Paragraph 8, sentence 5 says the idea would explain why an animal keeps a pupil shape that costs it sharpness.","Paragraph 8, sentence 5 says it remains a calculation, with no cuttlefish yet shown doing it.",null]},{"id":"rc-o21-q15","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the Stubbs hypothesis is best described as","choices":["openly dismissive","entirely convinced","admiring but unconvinced","indifferent to its outcome","puzzled by its reasoning"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"The idea is elegant"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"I would hold the hypothesis loosely until someone does"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 calls the idea elegant and credits what it would explain, then says it remains a calculation and that the author would hold it loosely until someone shows a cuttlefish doing it. That is admiration paired with reserved judgment.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: an author who calls an idea elegant is not dismissing it.","Reversed: the author explicitly withholds belief until there is behavioral evidence.",null,"Wrong register: the author cares enough to weigh the idea and say what would settle it.","Wrong register: the author lays out the reasoning clearly and finds it elegant, not confusing."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q16","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Ramirez and Oakley found that patches of skin from the California two-spot octopus","choices":["turned pale and still as soon as their nerve supply was cut away","expanded their chromatophores in light with no eye or brain attached","contained an opsin that differed from the one found in the animal's retina","could tell colors apart even though the animal's retina could not","responded to light within a fraction of a second, faster than the eye does"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"expand their chromatophores when light falls on them, with no eye or brain attached"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the skin patches expand their chromatophores when light falls on them, with no eye or brain attached, and that they contain the same opsin found in the retina.","distractorNotes":["Wrong paragraph: the cut-nerve observation is in paragraph 3 and is not credited to Ramirez and Oakley.",null,"Reversed: paragraph 9 says the skin contains the same opsin found in the retina.","Reversed: paragraph 9 says the skin uses the one pigment the eye uses and cannot by itself add color discrimination.","Reversed: paragraph 9 says the response is slow, taking seconds."]},{"id":"rc-o21-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Cephalopods change color by means of hormones acting on single pigment cells, in much the same way that fish and frogs change theirs","The Stubbs hypothesis has settled the question of how color-blind cephalopods manage to see color","Cephalopod skin contains three kinds of cells, and the leucophore is the most important of them for camouflage","Cephalopod color change is a fast, well-understood neural system, but how a color-blind animal matches colors is still unresolved","Because cephalopods have only a single visual pigment, their camouflage cannot genuinely match the colors of their surroundings"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"a cuttlefish can switch its entire body pattern in under a second"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"yet the eyes that direct it appear to be color-blind"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"The rest, the question of how a color-blind animal paints itself to suit a colored world, is open"}],"why":"The first half of the passage explains the fast, nerve-driven machinery of the skin; the second half sets out a puzzle, that the eyes directing it appear color-blind, and weighs three explanations without settling on one. Paragraph 10 says plainly what is certain and what is still open.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraphs 2 and 3 say the cephalopod system is muscular and neural, with no hormone in the loop, unlike the vertebrate cell.","Contradicted: paragraph 8 says the hypothesis remains a calculation that no one has yet demonstrated.","Too narrow and unsupported: the three cell types are one part of the passage, and the author never ranks the leucophore first.",null,"Too strong: paragraph 7 says spectral measurements found the match good but not perfect, and the author treats the question as open rather than closed."]}]},"rc-o22":{"id":"rc-o22","title":"What the Clumps Said","topic":"blood groups and safe transfusion","field":"physiology","source":"original","words":1360,"paragraphs":[["In 1667 a Paris physician, Jean-Baptiste Denis, ran lamb's blood into the veins of a feverish boy, who lived; a later patient died, and within a few years France forbade transfusion.","The practice returned in 1818, when the London obstetrician James Blundell began moving blood from one person into another, often to rescue women bleeding after childbirth.","Some patients rallied; others grew feverish, passed dark urine, and died, and no one could say why the same procedure saved one and killed the next."],["The answer came in 1901, from Vienna, through an experiment simple enough to repeat on a glass slide.","Karl Landsteiner drew blood from himself and a handful of colleagues, separated the fluid serum from the red cells, and mixed each person's serum with each person's cells.","Some mixtures stayed smooth and others clumped, and the samples sorted into three groups, which Landsteiner called A, B, and C; group C, whose cells clumped with no one's serum, was later renamed O.","A fourth group, AB, turned up the following year when two of his colleagues tested more people.","Landsteiner received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for the discovery in 1930."],["Red cells carry molecular markers called antigens; in the ABO system these are short chains of sugars, and a cell may display the A sugar, the B sugar, both, or neither.","Plasma carries antibodies, proteins that bind to antigens the body treats as foreign, and the pattern is a mirror image: A cells come with anti-B, B cells with anti-A, AB cells with neither, and O cells with both.","Because an antibody has more than one binding arm, it can bridge neighboring cells into a lattice, and that lattice is agglutination, the clumping Landsteiner saw.","Curiously, these antibodies appear in the first months of life with no transfusion to provoke them, probably because common gut bacteria carry similar sugars and the infant's immune system learns its targets from them."],["The rule for safe transfusion follows directly: a recipient's plasma must contain no antibody against the donor's cells.","Group O cells, carrying neither sugar, can therefore go to anyone, and a group AB recipient, carrying neither antibody, can accept cells of any group.","Break the rule and the donor's cells are agglutinated and then torn open inside the recipient's vessels, spilling hemoglobin into the plasma.","The patient develops fever, chills, back pain, and dark urine; the free hemoglobin can injure the kidneys, and a large mismatch can kill within hours.","This is almost certainly what Blundell was seeing.","I would add one caution about the phrase 'universal donor': it is true of O cells but not of O plasma, which is loaded with anti-A and anti-B, and the phrase has encouraged more confidence than it deserves."],["Knowing the groups did not make transfusion practical, because blood begins to clot the moment it leaves the body.","For more than a decade afterward, surgeons still had to connect donor to recipient directly, sometimes artery to vein, with no way to measure how much blood had passed.","The remedy was chemical.","Clotting depends on calcium ions at several steps, and sodium citrate binds calcium, so a small amount of citrate added to freshly drawn blood keeps it liquid.","Between 1914 and 1915 the idea was put to work independently by Albert Hustin in Brussels, Luis Agote in Buenos Aires, and Richard Lewisohn in New York, who found a dose low enough to be safe and high enough to stop clotting.","By 1917 Oswald Robertson, an American physician serving with the British, was keeping bottles of citrated blood on ice for the wounded of the Western Front."],["Once blood could wait, it could be stockpiled.","In 1937 Bernard Fantus, a physician at Cook County Hospital in Chicago, opened a laboratory where donated blood was typed, citrated, and refrigerated against future need, and he gave it the name that stuck: a blood bank.","The metaphor was deliberate: families deposited blood that the patient could later withdraw, and the idea spread quickly to hospitals across the United States."],["The ABO groups explained most transfusion disasters but not all of them.","In 1939 Philip Levine and Rufus Stetson described a woman who, after a stillbirth, reacted violently to blood from her husband that matched her ABO group; her serum clumped his cells, pointing to an antigen outside the ABO system.","The following year Landsteiner, by then at the Rockefeller Institute in New York, and Alexander Wiener injected red cells from rhesus monkeys into rabbits and guinea pigs.","Their antiserum clumped the red cells of about 85 percent of the New Yorkers tested, and the two men named the factor Rh, after the monkey; people whose cells reacted were Rh positive, the rest Rh negative.","The name has outlived its accuracy: the rabbit antibody and the human antibody turned out to recognize different molecules, and the rabbit target is now called LW, for Landsteiner and Wiener, while the human antigen keeps the name Rh."],["The Rh factor mattered less for transfusion, where it could simply be matched, than for pregnancy.","An Rh negative woman carrying an Rh positive fetus is usually safe in that first pregnancy, because fetal red cells enter her circulation mainly at delivery.","Once they do, she can form anti-Rh antibodies, and in a later pregnancy those antibodies cross the placenta and destroy the red cells of an Rh positive fetus, causing hemolytic disease of the newborn.","Levine connected the disease to the Rh factor in 1941.","The prevention, introduced in the late 1960s, is elegant: an Rh negative mother receives ready-made anti-Rh antibody shortly after delivery, which clears the fetal cells from her blood before her own immune system notices them.","Where it is used, a disease that once commonly killed infants has become rare, and I count it among the cleanest victories of preventive medicine."],["Storing blood for a hospital is one problem; moving it across an ocean in wartime is another, and Charles Drew solved the second.","Drew, a surgeon whose 1940 doctoral dissertation at Columbia University was titled Banked Blood, was asked that year to supervise Blood for Britain, which collected blood in New York and shipped it to a country under bombardment.","He chose to ship plasma rather than whole blood.","Plasma restores blood volume, which is what a bleeding soldier most needs; it keeps longer than red cells; and plasma pooled from many donors can be given with less regard to group, since any one donor's antibodies are diluted.","Drew also pushed the drying of plasma to a powder that could travel without refrigeration and be reconstituted with sterile water near the front.","In 1941 he became the first director of the American Red Cross blood bank in New York and sent out the mobile collection units that today's blood drives descend from."],["The program's rules were less rational than its chemistry.","The Red Cross, at the request of the armed forces, at first refused blood from Black donors, and when the policy changed in 1942 it accepted that blood only if it was stored and labeled separately.","Drew, who was Black, had by then returned to Howard University to teach surgery, and he said plainly that the practice had no basis in science, since nothing in the plasma marks the race of the donor.","He died in 1950 after a car accident in North Carolina, at the age of forty five.","It is hard to read this episode without anger: the decade that produced the Rh test and the dried-plasma bottle also produced a labeling rule that the science of the labels flatly contradicted."],["Landsteiner's experiment is, in the end, a lesson in which questions to ask.","He did not ask why transfusion sometimes killed; he asked whether blood from different people behaved differently in a dish, and the dish answered in clumps.","Every advance that followed had the same shape, a rule read off the blood and then a tool that put it to use: the citrate bottle, the bank, the Rh test, the plasma shipment.","Where the story went wrong, it went wrong by adding a category that the blood did not contain."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o22-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, James Blundell's transfusions in the early nineteenth century","choices":["were forbidden in France within a few years of his first attempt","used the blood of lambs when human donors could not be found","saved some patients and killed others for reasons no one could explain","were performed mainly on soldiers wounded on the battlefield","were abandoned once fevers and dark urine were traced to the use of lamb's blood"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"no one could say why the same procedure saved one and killed the next"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says some of Blundell's patients rallied while others grew feverish and died, and no one could say why the same procedure saved one and killed the next.","distractorNotes":["Wrong person: the ban in France followed Denis's work in the 1660s, not Blundell's.","Wrong person: lamb's blood belongs to Denis in 1667; Blundell moved blood from one person into another.",null,"Outside the passage: paragraph 1 says he often treated women bleeding after childbirth, not soldiers.","Outside the passage: nothing says the practice was abandoned, and lamb's blood had nothing to do with Blundell's patients."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Landsteiner identified the blood groups by","choices":["mixing the serum of each person with the red cells of each other person","injecting red cells from monkeys into rabbits and guinea pigs","adding sodium citrate to freshly drawn blood and watching it clot","testing a larger set of people the year after his first paper appeared","comparing the blood of patients who survived transfusion with those who died"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"mixed each person's serum with each person's cells"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 describes the experiment: Landsteiner separated serum from red cells and mixed each person's serum with each person's cells, and the clumping sorted the samples into groups.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong paragraph: the monkey experiment is the Rh work of 1940 in paragraph 7.","Wrong paragraph and reversed: citrate in paragraph 5 stops clotting, and it came more than a decade later.","Wrong people: paragraph 2 says two of his colleagues tested more people the following year and found AB.","Outside the passage: Landsteiner mixed blood from healthy colleagues, not from transfusion patients."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions gut bacteria in paragraph 3 primarily to","choices":["warn that infections can trigger a dangerous transfusion reaction","explain why infants are unable to receive transfusions safely","argue that the ABO antigens originally evolved in bacteria","show that antibodies against blood sugars require a prior transfusion","account for antibodies that form without any transfusion"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"these antibodies appear in the first months of life with no transfusion to provoke them, probably because common gut bacteria carry similar sugars"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 calls it curious that the antibodies appear with no transfusion to provoke them, then offers gut bacteria with similar sugars as the probable explanation.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: nothing links infection to transfusion reactions.","Outside the passage: the passage says nothing about transfusing infants.","Too strong: the passage says the bacteria carry similar sugars, not that the antigens evolved in them.","Reversed: the whole point is that these antibodies form without any transfusion.",null]},{"id":"rc-o22-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a person whose red cells carry the B sugar and no A sugar has plasma that contains","choices":["anti-B only","anti-A only","both anti-A and anti-B","neither anti-A nor anti-B","anti-A until the first months of life, then neither"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"B cells with anti-A"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 gives the mirror-image pattern: A cells come with anti-B, B cells with anti-A, AB cells with neither, and O cells with both.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: anti-B belongs to a person with A cells, and a person's plasma never attacks the sugar their own cells carry.",null,"Wrong group: both antibodies belong to a person with O cells.","Wrong group: neither antibody belongs to a person with AB cells.","Reversed timing: paragraph 3 says the antibodies appear in the first months of life, not that they disappear then."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if a patient with group A blood were given group B red cells, the most likely result would be that","choices":["the recipient's cells would be agglutinated by the donor's anti-A","the donor's cells would be accepted because the A and B sugars are nearly identical","the recipient's immune system would need several months to form anti-B","the recipient's anti-B would clump the donor's cells and tear them open","the recipient would form anti-B only after a second transfusion"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"A cells come with anti-B"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"a recipient's plasma must contain no antibody against the donor's cells"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"the donor's cells are agglutinated and then torn open inside the recipient's vessels"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says a person with A cells already carries anti-B, and paragraph 4 says that when the rule is broken the donor's cells are agglutinated and then torn open. Put those together and the recipient's anti-B attacks the incoming B cells.","distractorNotes":["Reversed direction: red cells were given, not plasma, and paragraph 4 says it is the donor's cells that are attacked.","Contradicted: paragraph 3 treats A and B as different sugars with different antibodies against them.","Wrong timing: paragraph 3 says the antibodies are already present from the first months of life.",null,"Wrong timing: anti-B is already there in group A plasma, so no first transfusion is needed to create it."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 4, the word 'loaded' most nearly means","choices":["filled with","weighed down by","armed with","biased toward","wealthy from"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"O plasma, which is loaded with anti-A and anti-B"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says O plasma is loaded with anti-A and anti-B, and the point is that the plasma holds plenty of both antibodies. Loaded here means full of.","distractorNotes":[null,"Burdened is a common sense of loaded, but the sentence is about what the plasma contains, not about a weight it carries.","A loaded gun is armed, but plasma is not a weapon being readied.","A loaded question is biased, but the sentence describes contents, not a slant.","Loaded can mean rich, but nothing in the sentence concerns money."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q07","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following are true of sodium citrate EXCEPT:","choices":["It binds calcium ions.","It keeps freshly drawn blood liquid.","It was first used in transfusion by Oswald Robertson.","It was put to work independently in Brussels, Buenos Aires, and New York.","Its safe dose was worked out by Richard Lewisohn."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"sodium citrate binds calcium","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"keeps it liquid","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"put to work independently by Albert Hustin in Brussels, Luis Agote in Buenos Aires, and Richard Lewisohn in New York","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"who found a dose low enough to be safe and high enough to stop clotting","choice":4},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":6,"quote":"By 1917 Oswald Robertson, an American physician serving with the British, was keeping bottles of citrated blood on ice"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 credits Hustin, Agote, and Lewisohn with putting citrate to work between 1914 and 1915. Robertson appears later, by 1917, storing bottles of citrated blood on ice; he did not introduce it.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 5: sodium citrate binds calcium.","Stated in paragraph 5: a small amount of citrate keeps freshly drawn blood liquid.",null,"Stated in paragraph 5, which names the three men and their three cities.","Stated in paragraph 5: Lewisohn found a dose low enough to be safe and high enough to stop clotting."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Bernard Fantus","choices":["coined the term 'blood bank' to describe a program for shipping plasma overseas","first demonstrated that citrated blood could be kept on ice for the wounded","opened the first blood bank at the Rockefeller Institute in New York","discovered that donated blood could be refrigerated for only a few days","opened a Chicago laboratory where blood was typed, citrated, and stored"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"opened a laboratory where donated blood was typed, citrated, and refrigerated against future need"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says Fantus, at Cook County Hospital in Chicago, opened a laboratory where donated blood was typed, citrated, and refrigerated against future need, and named it a blood bank.","distractorNotes":["Mash-up: Fantus did coin the term, but the plasma shipments belong to Drew in paragraph 9.","Wrong person: paragraph 5 credits Oswald Robertson with keeping citrated blood on ice for the wounded.","Wrong place: paragraph 6 puts the bank at Cook County Hospital in Chicago; the Rockefeller Institute is where Landsteiner worked.","Outside the passage: no storage limit is given for Fantus's bank.",null]},{"id":"rc-o22-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that adding calcium to a bottle of citrated blood would most likely","choices":["tear the red cells open","let the blood begin to clot once again","make the blood safe for a recipient of any group","extend the time the blood could be stored on ice","lower the dose of citrate needed to keep the blood liquid"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"Clotting depends on calcium ions at several steps, and sodium citrate binds calcium"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says clotting depends on calcium ions and that citrate works by binding calcium. Supplying calcium again would undo that and let the blood clot.","distractorNotes":["Wrong mechanism: in paragraph 4 cells are torn open by an antibody mismatch, not by calcium.",null,"Unrelated: group compatibility in paragraph 4 depends on sugars and antibodies, not on calcium.","Reversed: restoring clotting would ruin the bottle, not preserve it longer.","Reversed: more calcium would call for more citrate to bind it, not less."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Rh factor got its name from","choices":["the woman whose serum first revealed an antigen outside the ABO system","the two researchers who identified it, Landsteiner and Wiener","the Rockefeller Institute, where the experiment was carried out","the rhesus monkeys whose red cells were used to produce the antiserum","the roughly 85 percent of New Yorkers whose cells reacted to the antiserum"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"named the factor Rh, after the monkey"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says Landsteiner and Wiener injected red cells from rhesus monkeys into rabbits and guinea pigs and named the factor Rh, after the monkey.","distractorNotes":["Wrong detail: the Levine and Stetson patient pointed to the antigen but gave it no name.","Wrong name: paragraph 7 says the researchers' initials went to LW, the rabbit target, not to Rh.","Outside the passage: the institute is where Landsteiner worked, not the source of the name.",null,"Wrong detail: the 85 percent figure describes how many people were Rh positive, not where the name came from."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the LW antigen in paragraph 7 primarily to","choices":["credit Landsteiner and Wiener for a second discovery as important as the first","argue that the Rh test should be replaced by a more accurate one","note that the Rh name no longer matches what the original antibody detected","explain why about 85 percent of people tested were Rh positive","show that the human antibody and the rabbit antibody are identical"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"The name has outlived its accuracy"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 introduces LW with the words 'the name has outlived its accuracy': the rabbit antibody and the human antibody turned out to recognize different molecules, so the name Rh stuck to an antigen the original experiment did not actually detect.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the sentence is about a mismatch in naming, not a second major discovery.","Outside the passage: the author never questions the test, only the name.",null,"Wrong detail: the 85 percent figure comes from the antiserum result, and LW does not explain it.","Reversed: paragraph 7 says the two antibodies recognize different molecules."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q12","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that an Rh positive woman carrying an Rh negative fetus would most likely","choices":["form no anti-Rh antibodies against the fetal cells","need ready-made anti-Rh antibody after delivery","form anti-Rh antibodies at delivery, as an Rh negative woman would","deliver a child with hemolytic disease of the newborn","be unharmed only until a second pregnancy"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"An Rh negative woman carrying an Rh positive fetus"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"Once they do, she can form anti-Rh antibodies"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"proteins that bind to antigens the body treats as foreign"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 builds the danger on an Rh negative mother meeting Rh positive fetal cells, and paragraph 3 says antibodies form against antigens the body treats as foreign. Flip the pairing and the fetal cells carry no Rh antigen for her to react to, so no anti-Rh forms.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: paragraph 8 gives the ready-made antibody to Rh negative mothers, who are the ones at risk of forming their own.","Reversed: the antibody forms in a mother who lacks the antigen her fetus carries; here the fetus lacks it.","Reversed: paragraph 8 ties the disease to anti-Rh antibodies destroying an Rh positive fetus's cells.","Reversed: the second-pregnancy risk in paragraph 8 belongs to the Rh negative mother."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q13","type":"application","stem":"A patient with group B blood needs red cells, and the hospital has cells of only one group on hand. According to the rule in paragraph 4, which of the following supplies could be given safely?","choices":["group A cells, because the recipient's plasma carries only anti-A","group AB cells, because they carry both sugars and so match any recipient","group A or AB cells, because the recipient's anti-A is diluted by the donor's plasma","group O cells, because they carry neither sugar for the recipient's anti-A to bind","cells of any group, because the recipient's antibodies attack only foreign plasma"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"a recipient's plasma must contain no antibody against the donor's cells"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"Group O cells, carrying neither sugar, can therefore go to anyone"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"B cells with anti-A"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says a group B person carries anti-A, and paragraph 4's rule is that the recipient's plasma must hold no antibody against the donor's cells. Group O cells carry neither sugar, so there is nothing for the anti-A to bind.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: carrying anti-A is exactly why group A cells would be agglutinated.","Reversed: AB cells carry the A sugar, which the recipient's anti-A attacks; carrying both sugars makes AB a universal recipient, not a universal donor.","Wrong mechanism: dilution of antibodies in paragraph 9 concerns pooled plasma, and the recipient's own anti-A is not diluted by a transfusion of cells.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 4 says antibodies attack the donor's cells, not plasma."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q14","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Drew chose to ship plasma rather than whole blood partly because plasma","choices":["carries the red cells that a bleeding soldier most urgently needs","could be shipped only after being dried to a powder","can be stored for weeks without any refrigeration","contains no antibodies against the ABO sugars","keeps longer than red cells and restores blood volume"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"it keeps longer than red cells"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 lists three reasons: plasma restores blood volume, it keeps longer than red cells, and pooled plasma dilutes any one donor's antibodies.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: plasma is what remains without the red cells; paragraph 9 says what a bleeding soldier most needs is blood volume.","Too strong: paragraph 9 presents drying as an additional technique Drew pushed, not a requirement for shipping.","Half right: paragraph 9 says the dried powder travels without refrigeration, not plasma in general.","Contradicted: paragraph 9 says pooling dilutes the donors' antibodies, which means plasma still contains them.",null]},{"id":"rc-o22-q15","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of Charles Drew EXCEPT:","choices":["He wrote a doctoral dissertation titled Banked Blood.","He resigned from the Red Cross in protest at its donor policy.","He was the first director of the American Red Cross blood bank in New York.","He died at forty five after a car accident in North Carolina.","He said that separating blood by race had no basis in science."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"doctoral dissertation at Columbia University was titled Banked Blood","choice":0},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"the first director of the American Red Cross blood bank in New York","choice":2},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"He died in 1950 after a car accident in North Carolina, at the age of forty five","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"the practice had no basis in science","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"had by then returned to Howard University to teach surgery"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says Drew had already returned to Howard University by the time the policy changed, and it never says he resigned in protest. The other four statements appear in paragraphs 9 and 10.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 9: his 1940 doctoral dissertation at Columbia was titled Banked Blood.",null,"Stated in paragraph 9: in 1941 he became the first director of the American Red Cross blood bank in New York.","Stated in paragraph 10: he died in 1950 after a car accident in North Carolina, at forty five.","Stated in paragraph 10: he said plainly that the practice had no basis in science."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the policy of labeling blood by the donor's race is best described as","choices":["amused","neutral","indignant","ambivalent","resigned"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"It is hard to read this episode without anger"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"The program's rules were less rational than its chemistry."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 opens by calling the rules less rational than the chemistry and closes with 'It is hard to read this episode without anger.' That is indignation, a controlled anger at something unjust.","distractorNotes":["Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 10 is treated as funny.","Too weak: the author states anger openly rather than withholding judgment.",null,"Too weak: the author takes one clear side; there is no wavering between two views.","Wrong register: resignation accepts a wrong quietly, and this author calls it out."]},{"id":"rc-o22-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Karl Landsteiner's 1930 Nobel Prize was the most important award ever given for research on human blood.","Safe transfusion grew from rules read off the blood itself, each put to use by a practical tool.","Charles Drew's plasma program proved that whole blood should never be shipped in wartime.","The Rh factor, not the ABO system, explains most of the deaths from early transfusion.","Blood banking succeeded in the United States because families were willing to deposit blood."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"a rule read off the blood and then a tool that put it to use"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"Landsteiner's experiment is, in the end, a lesson in which questions to ask"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"The rule for safe transfusion follows directly"}],"why":"The passage moves from Landsteiner's clumps to the citrate bottle, the bank, the Rh test, and the plasma shipment, and paragraph 11 names the pattern: a rule read off the blood and then a tool that put it to use.","distractorNotes":["Too extreme and never said: the Nobel Prize is a one-sentence detail in paragraph 2.",null,"Too extreme: paragraph 9 gives Drew's reasons for plasma, but never says whole blood should never be shipped.","Reversed: paragraph 7 says the ABO groups explained most transfusion disasters, and Rh the rest.","A detail: the deposit idea is one sentence in paragraph 6, not the passage's point."]}]},"rc-o23":{"id":"rc-o23","title":"The Barium That Should Not Have Been There","topic":"the discovery of nuclear fission","field":"history of science","source":"original","words":1333,"paragraphs":[["In 1934 Enrico Fermi's group in Rome, bombarding one element after another with neutrons, reached uranium, the heaviest element then known, and found something new.","The irradiated uranium gave off radioactivities whose half-lives matched no known substance, and Fermi suggested, cautiously, that a uranium nucleus had captured a neutron and then decayed into an element beyond uranium, number 93.","The idea was reasonable: in lighter elements a captured neutron moved the nucleus at most a step or two along the periodic table.","Over the next four years laboratories in Rome, Paris, and Berlin reported a growing family of supposed transuranic elements, and Fermi's 1938 Nobel Prize in Physics credited him with new radioactive elements made by neutron irradiation."],["One chemist objected almost at once.","Ida Noddack, a German chemist who had helped discover rhenium, pointed out in 1934 that Fermi had ruled out only the elements near uranium, and that the nucleus might instead have broken into several large fragments of elements far down the table.","She offered no mechanism, and physicists, who had every reason to think a nucleus could shed only a small particle at a time, set the suggestion aside.","In hindsight the paper reads as prophecy; at the time it read as a chemist straying outside her field, and I think the second reading, though wrong, was not unreasonable."],["Otto Hahn, a radiochemist, and Lise Meitner, a physicist from Vienna, had worked together in Berlin since 1907, and at the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute for Chemistry the analytical chemist Fritz Strassmann joined their uranium work in the 1930s.","Their method was the radiochemist's oldest trick: add a known element as a carrier, precipitate it out of solution, and see whether the unknown radioactivity comes down with it.","By 1938 the team had sorted the uranium products into supposed chains of transuranic elements and, separately, a few isotopes of radium, element 88, which they thought arose when the nucleus shed two alpha particles."],["Then the team came apart.","Meitner was of Jewish descent, and her Austrian citizenship had shielded her from the laws that drove other Jewish scientists from German institutions; when Germany annexed Austria in March 1938, that shield vanished.","In July she slipped out of Germany with the help of the Dutch physicist Dirk Coster and settled in Stockholm, where she had a place to work but little equipment and few colleagues."],["The radium was the problem.","Radium and barium occupy the same column of the periodic table and behave alike in solution, so the Berlin chemists had been using barium as the carrier for their radium isotopes.","To prove that the activity really was radium, they had to separate it from the barium, as Marie Curie had done decades earlier by fractional crystallization, which works because radium salts crystallize out of a solution slightly ahead of barium salts.","In December 1938 Hahn and Strassmann found that the separation would not work: the activity stayed with the barium through every cycle, while known radium isotopes added to the same solutions as controls separated cleanly.","Only one conclusion fit the chemistry: the product was not radium at all but barium, element 56, with barely more than half the mass of uranium.","In the paper they submitted to the journal Naturwissenschaften on December 22, they wrote that as chemists they had to call the substance barium, but that as nuclear chemists they could not yet bring themselves to a step that contradicted all previous experience of nuclear physics."],["Hahn had already described the result to Meitner in a letter dated December 19 and asked whether she could think of any explanation.","She was spending Christmas in a small town on the Swedish west coast, and her nephew Otto Robert Frisch, a physicist at Niels Bohr's institute in Copenhagen, came to join her.","Frisch later recalled that they worked the problem through on a walk in the snow, he on skis and she on foot, stopping to do the arithmetic on scraps of paper."],["The tool they reached for was a picture of the nucleus that Bohr had been developing in Copenhagen: the liquid drop.","In this picture the nucleus is not a rigid structure but something like a droplet of water, held together by a short-range attraction that acts like surface tension and pulled apart by the electrical repulsion among its protons.","For a small nucleus the surface tension wins easily; as the charge grows, the repulsion grows faster, and Meitner and Frisch estimated that for a nucleus with a charge of about 100 the surface tension would vanish altogether.","Uranium, with 92 protons, sits uncomfortably close to that limit.","An arriving neutron adds energy and sets the drop oscillating; it stretches, develops a waist, and, if the stretch goes far enough, snaps into two smaller drops that fly apart.","On this view barium was an expected fragment, and its partner, to bring the charges to 92, would be krypton, with 36 protons."],["Two calculations clinched the argument.","The repulsion between two freshly separated fragments of roughly equal charge would drive them apart with about 200 million electron volts, far beyond anything seen in ordinary radioactive decay.","Meitner, who remembered how to compute nuclear masses from a formula then in use, asked where that energy would come from: the two fragments together weighed about one fifth of a proton mass less than the uranium nucleus and its captured neutron, and by Einstein's relation between mass and energy that missing fifth came to about 200 million electron volts.","The two numbers matched.","Back in Copenhagen in January, Frisch detected the energetic fragments directly in an ionization chamber and borrowed from a visiting biologist the word for a dividing cell: fission.","Their paper appeared in Nature in February 1939; by then Bohr, who had heard the news from Frisch before sailing, had carried it to the United States."],["By September 1939 Bohr and the American physicist John Wheeler had turned the drop into a quantitative theory and argued that it was the rare isotope uranium-235, not the common uranium-238, that split when struck by a slow neutron.","What followed, the chain reaction and the weapons built on it, lies outside this account, except to note that Meitner declined to work on the bomb."],["The prize came after the war.","The Nobel Prize in Chemistry for 1944, which the committee had held in reserve, was awarded in November 1945 to Hahn alone, for the discovery of the fission of heavy nuclei; he learned of it from a newspaper while interned at Farm Hall in England.","Strassmann, who had done much of the work at the bench, was not named, and neither was Meitner, though she was nominated many times for both the chemistry and the physics prize.","Hahn's own view, held to the end of his life, was that fission had been a chemical discovery, made by chemists, after the physicist had left."],["I find that account true in its particulars and misleading as a whole.","It was the chemistry of Hahn and Strassmann, careful and beautifully controlled, that forced the word barium onto the page, and nothing here should diminish that.","But it was Meitner who had guided the physics of the Berlin program for years, who urged Hahn to recheck the radium, and who, with Frisch, turned an impossible result into a mechanism, a pair of fragments, and an energy.","Historians who examined the Nobel committee's records once they were opened concluded that its evaluators, chemists judging a chemical prize, underrated the interpretation and never grasped how thoroughly the work had crossed disciplinary lines.","I am wary of recasting the past as a simple tale of theft; the committee worked from the papers in front of it, in a year when little else was clear.","Still, the barium that should not have been there was discovered twice: once in a Berlin laboratory, by chemists who could not believe it, and once on a walk in the snow, by two physicists who could."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o23-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Fermi proposed in 1934 that the new radioactivities from irradiated uranium came from","choices":["the splitting of a uranium nucleus into two lighter fragments of similar size","a nucleus that had captured a neutron and then decayed into element 93","isotopes of radium formed when the nucleus shed two alpha particles","a contaminant that had entered the samples during the neutron bombardment","slow neutrons that had been absorbed by lighter elements near the sample"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"a uranium nucleus had captured a neutron and then decayed into an element beyond uranium, number 93"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says Fermi suggested that a uranium nucleus had captured a neutron and then decayed into an element beyond uranium, number 93. The other choices are later ideas or ideas nobody in the passage proposed.","distractorNotes":["Wrong time: splitting is the interpretation Meitner and Frisch reach in paragraphs 7 and 8, not Fermi's 1934 proposal.",null,"Wrong group: the radium isotopes are the Berlin team's belief in paragraph 3, not Fermi's.","Outside the passage: no contamination is ever mentioned.","Mash-up: slow neutrons appear only in paragraph 9, and nothing says lighter elements absorbed them."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q02","type":"function","stem":"The author discusses Ida Noddack in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["prove that chemists understood the nucleus better than physicists did","credit her with the first direct detection of fission fragments","argue that Fermi should have shared his Nobel Prize with her","show that the right idea had surfaced early and been set aside","explain the chemical method by which rhenium had been discovered"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"set the suggestion aside"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"In hindsight the paper reads as prophecy"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 presents Noddack's 1934 suggestion that the nucleus had broken into large fragments, says physicists set it aside, and calls the paper prophecy in hindsight. She is there to show that the right idea arrived early and went unheeded.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the author also says the physicists' reading, though wrong, was not unreasonable.","Wrong person: the fragments were detected by Frisch in paragraph 8; Noddack offered no mechanism and no measurement.","Outside the passage: the author never says who should have shared Fermi's prize.",null,"Wrong focus: rhenium is a passing credential, and the method of its discovery is never described."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Berlin group's basic method was to","choices":["measure the half-life of each new radioactivity and match it to a known element","bombard a sample with slow neutrons and count the fragments in an ionization chamber","compute the masses of the products from a formula and compare them with uranium","crystallize the products repeatedly until only the heaviest element remained","add a known element as a carrier and see whether the activity followed it"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"add a known element as a carrier, precipitate it out of solution, and see whether the unknown radioactivity comes down with it"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 calls it the radiochemist's oldest trick: add a known element as a carrier, precipitate it out of solution, and see whether the unknown radioactivity comes down with it.","distractorNotes":["Wrong paragraph: half-lives belong to Fermi's work in paragraph 1, and the passage never says the Berlin team matched them to elements.","Wrong person: the ionization chamber is Frisch's experiment in paragraph 8.","Wrong person: computing masses from a formula is Meitner's step in paragraph 8.","Half right: fractional crystallization appears in paragraph 5, but it was used to separate radium from barium, not to isolate the heaviest element.",null]},{"id":"rc-o23-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Meitner lost her protection from the laws aimed at Jewish scientists when","choices":["Germany annexed Austria in March 1938","the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute dismissed its Jewish staff","she published the barium result with Hahn and Strassmann","Dirk Coster arrived in Berlin from the Netherlands","Hahn and Strassmann found the barium in December 1938"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"when Germany annexed Austria in March 1938, that shield vanished"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says her Austrian citizenship had shielded her, and that when Germany annexed Austria in March 1938 that shield vanished.","distractorNotes":[null,"Outside the passage: no dismissal by the institute is described.","Wrong fact: she was not an author of that paper, which came five months after she left.","Reversed role: Coster helped her escape; his arrival did not change her legal standing.","Wrong time: the barium came in December 1938, well after she had fled in July."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Berlin chemists used barium as a carrier for radium because the two elements","choices":["have nearly identical half-lives when they are radioactive","had been separated by Marie Curie decades earlier","occupy the same column and behave alike in solution","are both produced when a uranium nucleus sheds two alpha particles","crystallize out of a solution at exactly the same rate"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"Radium and barium occupy the same column of the periodic table and behave alike in solution"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 explains that radium and barium occupy the same column of the periodic table and behave alike in solution, which is exactly what a carrier needs to do.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: no half-lives are given for either element.","Half right: Curie did separate them, but that explains why a separation was possible, not why barium was chosen as the carrier.",null,"Wrong fact: paragraph 3 says the team thought radium arose that way; barium was the carrier they added, not a product.","Reversed: paragraph 5 says radium salts crystallize slightly ahead of barium salts, which is how the two can be told apart."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q06","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if the activity in the December 1938 solutions had really been radium, Hahn and Strassmann would have observed that","choices":["the activity stayed with the barium through every cycle of crystallization","the activity crystallized out slightly ahead of the barium","the known radium isotopes added as controls failed to separate","the barium carrier lost its radioactivity as soon as it dissolved","the product weighed barely more than half as much as uranium"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"radium salts crystallize out of a solution slightly ahead of barium salts"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"known radium isotopes added to the same solutions as controls separated cleanly"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says radium salts crystallize slightly ahead of barium salts, and that known radium isotopes added as controls separated cleanly. Real radium would have pulled ahead of the barium; the activity never did.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: that is what they actually saw, and it is the evidence that the activity was not radium.",null,"Reversed: the controls did separate, and nothing about the unknown would change that.","Outside the passage: nothing says a carrier loses its activity on dissolving.","Wrong element: that is the mass of barium, the conclusion the chemistry forced, not a sign of radium."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q07","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following are true of Hahn and Strassmann's December 1938 work EXCEPT:","choices":["The activity stayed with the barium through every cycle of separation.","Known radium isotopes added as controls separated from the barium cleanly.","The paper was submitted to Naturwissenschaften on December 22.","The authors wrote that, as chemists, they were obliged to call the substance barium.","The authors concluded at once that the nucleus had split in two."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"the activity stayed with the barium through every cycle","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"known radium isotopes added to the same solutions as controls separated cleanly","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":6,"quote":"submitted to the journal Naturwissenschaften on December 22","choice":2},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":6,"quote":"as chemists they had to call the substance barium","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":6,"quote":"could not yet bring themselves to a step that contradicted all previous experience of nuclear physics"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 ends with the authors saying they could not yet bring themselves to a step that contradicted all previous experience of nuclear physics; the idea that the nucleus had split came from Meitner and Frisch in paragraphs 7 and 8. The other four statements are all in paragraph 5.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 5: the activity stayed with the barium through every cycle.","Stated in paragraph 5: the radium controls separated cleanly.","Stated in paragraph 5: submitted to Naturwissenschaften on December 22.","Stated in paragraph 5: as chemists they had to call the substance barium.",null]},{"id":"rc-o23-q08","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, the word 'picture' most nearly means","choices":["a photograph taken with a powerful instrument","a painting of a scene from nature","a simplified model of how something behaves","an exact record of every measurement made","a vivid story told mainly to entertain"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"a picture of the nucleus that Bohr had been developing in Copenhagen: the liquid drop"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 calls the liquid drop a picture of the nucleus and then describes how such a drop behaves, so the word means a model or way of thinking about the nucleus, not an actual image.","distractorNotes":["The everyday meaning, but no instrument or photograph of a nucleus appears anywhere in the passage.","Another common meaning of the word that has nothing to do with the sentence.",null,"Too strong: the liquid drop is a simplification that compares the nucleus to a droplet; it records no measurements.","Wrong register: the drop is a scientific tool used to explain a result, not entertainment."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Meitner and Frisch estimated that a nucleus would have no surface tension left once its charge reached about","choices":["thirty-six","fifty-six","eighty-eight","one hundred","ninety-two"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"for a nucleus with a charge of about 100 the surface tension would vanish altogether"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says Meitner and Frisch estimated that for a nucleus with a charge of about 100 the surface tension would vanish altogether; uranium, at 92, sits close to that limit but not at it.","distractorNotes":["Wrong number: 36 is krypton's proton count in paragraph 7.","Wrong number: 56 is barium's atomic number in paragraph 5.","Wrong number: 88 is radium's atomic number in paragraph 3.",null,"Wrong number: 92 is uranium, which paragraph 7 says sits close to the limit, not at it."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q10","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, compared with uranium, a nucleus with far fewer protons would be","choices":["much less likely to split when a neutron arrives","more strongly pulled apart by electrical repulsion","unable to capture a neutron in the first place","likely to break into more than two fragments","held together by a stronger attraction between its protons"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"For a small nucleus the surface tension wins easily; as the charge grows, the repulsion grows faster"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"Uranium, with 92 protons, sits uncomfortably close to that limit."}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says that for a small nucleus the surface tension wins easily and that repulsion grows faster than cohesion as the charge grows. A nucleus far from the limit would hold together where uranium barely does.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: repulsion grows with charge, so fewer protons means less repulsion, not more.","Too extreme: paragraph 1 says lighter elements did capture neutrons; they simply moved a step along the table.","Outside the passage: nothing links fewer protons to more fragments.","Wrong force: paragraph 7 says the protons repel one another; what holds the nucleus together is a separate short-range attraction."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q11","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the two fragments together weighed less than the uranium nucleus and its captured neutron by about","choices":["the mass of one neutron","the mass of two alpha particles","one fifth of a proton mass","half the mass of uranium","200 proton masses"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"about one fifth of a proton mass less than the uranium nucleus and its captured neutron"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the two fragments together weighed about one fifth of a proton mass less than the uranium nucleus and its captured neutron, and that this missing fifth came to about 200 million electron volts.","distractorNotes":["Wrong figure: the neutron is part of what was weighed, not the amount that went missing.","Wrong paragraph: two alpha particles are the Berlin team's earlier idea about radium in paragraph 3.",null,"Confused: barium has barely more than half the mass of uranium, but that is a fragment's mass, not the missing mass.","Confused: 200 is the energy in million electron volts, not a count of proton masses."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q12","type":"function","stem":"The author's remark in paragraph 8 that 'the two numbers matched' serves primarily to","choices":["suggest that the agreement was too neat to be taken at face value","show that two independent calculations gave the same answer","explain why Frisch chose the word fission for the process","contrast Meitner's arithmetic with Hahn's chemistry","indicate that Bohr had already carried the news abroad"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"Two calculations clinched the argument."},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"The two numbers matched."}],"why":"Paragraph 8 opens by saying two calculations clinched the argument: one from the repulsion of the fragments, one from the missing mass. That the two numbers matched is what made the argument convincing.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author presents the match as clinching the argument, not as suspicious.",null,"Wrong detail: the word fission came from a biologist's term for a dividing cell, not from the energy calculation.","Wrong contrast: both numbers in paragraph 8 are physics calculations by Meitner and Frisch.","Wrong sentence: Bohr's trip is mentioned at the end of the paragraph and is not what the match shows."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q13","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle described in paragraph 7?","choices":["A rubber band keeps stretching the harder it is pulled and never snaps","A boulder splits only after it is struck many times with a heavy sledgehammer","A magnet lifts more iron filings the closer it is brought to the pile","A bubble blown almost to bursting pops at a puff a small bubble survives","A sand pile grows taller and taller and never collapses under its own weight"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"For a small nucleus the surface tension wins easily"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"Uranium, with 92 protons, sits uncomfortably close to that limit."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"An arriving neutron adds energy and sets the drop oscillating"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 describes a system near its limit of stability: a small nucleus holds together easily, but uranium sits so close to the point where cohesion fails that one arriving neutron is enough to split it. A bubble near bursting that pops at a puff a small bubble survives is the same pattern.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: the principle is that past some point the system snaps, not that it stretches without limit.","Wrong mechanism: the nucleus splits from a small disturbance because it is near the limit, not from repeated heavy blows.","Unrelated: this is attraction growing with closeness, with no limit and no splitting.",null,"Contradicted: a pile that never collapses is the opposite of a drop at the edge of stability."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q14","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that when natural uranium is exposed to slow neutrons,","choices":["every nucleus in the sample eventually splits into two fragments","uranium-238 splits more readily than uranium-235 does","the fragments produced are always barium and krypton","the theory of Bohr and Wheeler fails to predict the outcome","only a minority of the nuclei are of the kind that splits"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"it was the rare isotope uranium-235, not the common uranium-238, that split when struck by a slow neutron"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the isotope that splits under slow neutrons, uranium-235, is the rare one, while uranium-238 is the common one. So in natural uranium only a minority of nuclei are the kind that splits.","distractorNotes":["Too extreme: the passage says only the rare isotope splits under slow neutrons.","Reversed: paragraph 9 names uranium-235, not 238, as the isotope that splits.","Too strong: paragraph 7 gives barium and krypton as one pairing whose charges add to 92, not the only one.","Reversed: the Bohr and Wheeler theory is what identified the isotope.",null]},{"id":"rc-o23-q15","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for 1944 EXCEPT:","choices":["It cited the discovery of the chain reaction.","It was awarded in November 1945.","Hahn learned of it from a newspaper.","Strassmann was not named.","It had been held in reserve by the Nobel committee."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"was awarded in November 1945","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"he learned of it from a newspaper","choice":2},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"Strassmann, who had done much of the work at the bench, was not named","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"which the committee had held in reserve","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"for the discovery of the fission of heavy nuclei"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the prize went to Hahn for the discovery of the fission of heavy nuclei. The chain reaction appears only in paragraph 9, as something that followed and lies outside the account.","distractorNotes":[null,"Stated in paragraph 10: awarded in November 1945.","Stated in paragraph 10: he learned of it from a newspaper at Farm Hall.","Stated in paragraph 10: Strassmann was not named.","Stated in paragraph 10: the committee had held it in reserve."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward Hahn's claim that fission was a chemical discovery is best described as","choices":["openly contemptuous","appreciative but unconvinced","wholly persuaded by it","coolly indifferent to it","resentful on Meitner's behalf"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"I find that account true in its particulars and misleading as a whole."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"careful and beautifully controlled"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"I am wary of recasting the past as a simple tale of theft"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 calls Hahn's account true in its particulars and misleading as a whole, praises the chemistry as careful and beautifully controlled, and refuses to call the episode a theft. That is appreciation paired with disagreement.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the author praises the chemistry and says nothing should diminish it.",null,"Reversed: the author calls the account misleading as a whole.","Too weak: the author takes a clear position and argues it across a full paragraph.","Too strong: the author says plainly that they are wary of recasting the past as a tale of theft."]},{"id":"rc-o23-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Ida Noddack deserves to be remembered as the true discoverer of nuclear fission because she proposed the idea first","The liquid-drop model of the nucleus was the most important theoretical advance in physics during the 1930s","The chemistry of Hahn and Strassmann was so badly flawed that physicists had to step in and rescue the result","Fission was found by chemists who could not believe their result and explained by physicists the prize left out","The Nobel committee acted in bad faith when it gave the 1944 chemistry prize to Hahn alone and not to Meitner"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":6,"quote":"could not yet bring themselves to a step"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"true in its particulars and misleading as a whole"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"the barium that should not have been there was discovered twice"}],"why":"The passage follows the barium from the Berlin bench to the walk in the snow and closes by saying the discovery happened twice, once by chemists who could not believe it and once by physicists who could, while the prize recognized only the first. That is the whole arc.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: paragraph 2 calls Noddack's idea prophecy but also says it offered no mechanism and was reasonably set aside.","Too narrow: the liquid drop is the tool of paragraph 7, and the passage never ranks it against other advances.","Reversed: paragraph 11 calls the chemistry careful and beautifully controlled.",null,"Too strong: paragraph 11 says the author is wary of calling the episode a theft and notes the committee worked from the papers in front of it."]}]},"rc-o24":{"id":"rc-o24","title":"The Key That Turns Halfway","topic":"enzyme recognition and catalysis","field":"biochemistry","source":"original","words":1321,"paragraphs":[["Table sugar dissolved in water can sit for years with little change, because the bond joining its two halves breaks far too slowly to notice.","A pinch of yeast begins splitting that bond at once, using a protein called invertase, one of thousands of enzymes that make slow chemistry fast.","Every enzyme raises two questions that are easy to confuse: how it recognizes one molecule among thousands drifting past, and how it then makes a reaction run millions of times faster than it otherwise would.","Both questions turn on the active site, a small cleft on the enzyme's surface where the reacting molecule, called the substrate, binds and is transformed."],["In 1894 the German chemist Emil Fischer, working in Berlin, was studying enzymes that split glycosides, compounds in which glucose is joined to a partner by a bond that can point one of two ways, called alpha and beta.","A crude preparation from yeast split the alpha form and left the beta form untouched, while a preparation from almonds did exactly the reverse.","Fischer concluded that enzyme and substrate must fit together like a lock and a key: a key cut for the wrong lock, however similar, will not turn.","The proposal is remarkable for how little he had to go on: no one would show that an enzyme is a protein until James Sumner crystallized urease in 1926, so Fischer was describing the shape of something no one had seen."],["The lock and key picture explained selectivity well, and it still does.","But a rigid lock has an awkward consequence: any smaller key should slip into the keyhole, and the smallest molecule in the cell is water, which fits nearly any pocket.","Consider hexokinase, the enzyme that begins the breakdown of glucose by moving a phosphate group from ATP onto the sugar.","Water carries the same reactive hydroxyl group as glucose, is far smaller, and is thousands of times more abundant, so a rigid lock would let the enzyme hand its phosphate to water almost constantly.","It does not: hexokinase transfers phosphate to water at a tiny fraction of the rate at which it transfers phosphate to glucose.","Something about glucose, beyond merely fitting, switches the enzyme on."],["In 1958 the American biochemist Daniel Koshland, then at Brookhaven National Laboratory in New York, proposed the answer he called induced fit.","An enzyme, in his view, is less a lock than a glove: the hand goes in, and the glove changes shape to meet it.","The substrate's arrival pulls the enzyme into a new shape that brings the catalytic groups of the active site into working position, and only a substrate able to cause that shift gets a reaction.","A molecule that fits the pocket but cannot move the protein may bind and simply sit there; water is ignored not because it fails to fit but because it is too small to move anything."],["Koshland had inferred all this indirectly; it took twenty years to see it.","In 1978 Thomas Steitz and his colleagues at Yale University compared X-ray structures of yeast hexokinase with and without glucose bound.","The empty enzyme has two lobes with a deep cleft between them, and the sugar settles at the bottom of the cleft.","Once it is there, the two lobes swing together and close over it, so that the glucose is nearly buried and the water that had filled the cleft is squeezed out.","The closing brings the catalytic machinery to bear on the sugar's sixth carbon, where the phosphate will attach, and removes the water that might otherwise have accepted it."],["Textbooks like to set the two models against each other, as though Koshland had overthrown Fischer, and I think that framing does both men a disservice.","Fischer's claim that recognition depends on complementary shape has never been in doubt; what Koshland added was that the complementary shape need not exist until the substrate helps create it.","Many enzymes barely move when their substrates bind, and for them the old key still turns the old lock.","The deeper limitation of both models is that they concern recognition, and recognition by itself does not make a reaction faster."],["Picture a reaction as a journey over a hill: reactants rest in one valley and products in another, and between them the atoms must pass through a strained, fleeting arrangement, old bonds half broken and new ones half formed, that chemists call the transition state.","The height of the hill above the starting valley is the activation energy, a term that goes back to the Swedish chemist Svante Arrhenius in the 1880s.","Only molecules with enough energy to reach the top can cross, and because their number falls off steeply as the hill grows higher, even a modest reduction in its height greatly increases the rate.","Lowering the hill is the whole business of catalysis, and the question becomes how an enzyme manages it."],["The answer was sketched in 1946 by Linus Pauling, who noticed a flaw in the idea of a perfect fit.","An enzyme shaped exactly to match its substrate would bind it and then hold it, since every contact would reward the substrate for staying as it is.","Pauling argued instead that the active site is complementary not to the substrate but to the transition state.","It grips the substrate loosely and the awkward halfway arrangement tightly, so that part of the climb is paid for by contacts the enzyme makes along the way.","On this account the perfect lock would be the worst possible catalyst; an enzyme succeeds because its key does not fit well until it is halfway through turning."],["Pauling's proposal carried a prediction: a stable molecule shaped like the transition state should bind an enzyme far more tightly than the substrate does.","Such molecules, called transition-state analogs, do exist, and many bind thousands or even millions of times more tightly than the real substrate.","The most striking test came in 1986, when groups led by Richard Lerner in La Jolla, California, and Peter Schultz in Berkeley independently raised antibodies against a mimic of the transition state of an ester bond being split by water.","The antibodies, which have no history as catalysts, split the ester.","I find the result elegant and its limits instructive: the antibodies worked far more slowly than natural enzymes, so a snug fit to the transition state is clearly part of what an enzyme does and just as clearly not all of it."],["In 1995 Richard Wolfenden and Anna Radzicka at the University of North Carolina measured how fast a step in nucleotide synthesis, the loss of carbon dioxide from a molecule called orotidine monophosphate, proceeds in water with no enzyme present.","They had to heat sealed samples and extrapolate downward, because at room temperature the reaction has a half-life of about 78 million years.","The enzyme that performs the step in cells finishes it in a few hundredths of a second.","The speeding factor is roughly a hundred million billion, among the largest ever measured, achieved without heat, pressure, or harsh chemicals."],["One thing an enzyme cannot do deserves equal emphasis, because students, and occasionally textbooks, get it wrong: an enzyme does not change where a reaction ends up.","The ratio of products to reactants at equilibrium is fixed by the energy difference between the two valleys, and the enzyme, unchanged when the reaction is over, leaves that difference as it found it.","What it lowers is the hill between the valleys, and a lower hill is lower from both sides, so the reverse reaction is accelerated by precisely the same factor as the forward one.","A tunnel bored through a mountain shortens the trip without moving either town, and traffic uses it in both directions.","The enzyme's gift is time: it brings the cell to a destination chemistry had already chosen, within the lifetime of a cell rather than of a mountain range."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o24-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the active site of an enzyme is","choices":["the bond in a sugar molecule that breaks too slowly to notice","a protein in yeast that splits table sugar into its two halves","a small cleft on the enzyme's surface where the substrate binds","the part of the substrate that the enzyme recognizes by its shape","the strained, fleeting arrangement of atoms at the top of the energy hill"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"a small cleft on the enzyme's surface where the reacting molecule, called the substrate, binds"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 defines the active site directly: a small cleft on the enzyme's surface where the reacting molecule, called the substrate, binds and is transformed.","distractorNotes":["Wrong object: the slow-breaking bond in paragraph 1 belongs to table sugar, not to the enzyme.","Wrong object: that describes invertase, the enzyme itself, not a site on it.",null,"Reversed: the active site is on the enzyme, and the substrate is the molecule that binds to it.","Wrong paragraph: that is the transition state, defined in paragraph 7."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Fischer's preparation from yeast","choices":["split both the alpha and the beta forms of the glycoside","split the beta form of the glycoside but not the alpha form","split neither form until a preparation from almonds was added","was shown to be a protein when it was crystallized in 1926","split the alpha form of the glycoside but not the beta form"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"A crude preparation from yeast split the alpha form and left the beta form untouched"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says the crude preparation from yeast split the alpha form and left the beta form untouched, while the almond preparation did the reverse.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 2 says the yeast preparation left the beta form untouched.","Reversed: that is what the almond preparation did, not the yeast preparation.","Outside the passage: nothing says the two preparations were ever combined.","Wrong enzyme: the protein crystallized in 1926 was urease, by James Sumner.",null]},{"id":"rc-o24-q03","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that when Fischer proposed the lock and key model, he","choices":["could not have known the chemical nature of the enzymes he studied","had already seen the three-dimensional shape of an enzyme's active site","based the model on crystals of urease that he had prepared himself","believed that the enzymes from yeast and from almonds were the same","relied on the X-ray structures that were available to him at the time"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"no one would show that an enzyme is a protein until James Sumner crystallized urease in 1926, so Fischer was describing the shape of something no one had seen"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says no one would show that an enzyme is a protein until 1926, thirty years after Fischer's proposal, and that he was describing the shape of something no one had seen. So in 1894 he could not have known what enzymes were made of.","distractorNotes":[null,"Contradicted: paragraph 2 says Fischer was describing the shape of something no one had seen.","Wrong person: it was James Sumner who crystallized urease, and not until 1926.","Contradicted: the two preparations behaved in opposite ways, which is the whole point of the experiment.","Anachronism: the first X-ray structures the passage mentions date from 1978."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q04","type":"function","stem":"The author discusses water in paragraph 3 primarily to","choices":["explain how hexokinase begins the breakdown of glucose inside the cell","show that water is the most abundant molecule in a living cell","argue that the lock and key model fails to explain selectivity","expose a prediction of the rigid lock that the enzyme does not obey","introduce the two lobes that close around glucose in hexokinase"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"so a rigid lock would let the enzyme hand its phosphate to water almost constantly"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"It does not"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 sets up what a rigid lock would predict, that the enzyme would hand its phosphate to water almost constantly, and then says flatly that it does not. Water is there to show the prediction failing.","distractorNotes":["Too narrow: hexokinase is described only as a vehicle for the water problem, not for its own sake.","Wrong purpose: the passage mentions water's abundance to sharpen the prediction, not to rank molecules in the cell.","Contradicted: paragraph 3 opens by saying the model explained selectivity well, and still does.",null,"Wrong paragraph: the closing lobes do not appear until paragraph 5."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to Koshland's model as the passage describes it, a molecule receives a reaction from an enzyme only if it","choices":["is smaller than the pocket of the active site and fits inside it loosely","pulls the enzyme into a shape that puts its catalytic groups in place","binds the active site more tightly than the transition state does","is present at a concentration thousands of times higher than water","matches the active site exactly, the way a key matches a rigid lock"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"only a substrate able to cause that shift gets a reaction"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the substrate's arrival pulls the enzyme into a new shape that brings the catalytic groups into working position, and only a substrate able to cause that shift gets a reaction.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 4 says a molecule that fits but cannot move the protein may bind and simply sit there.",null,"Wrong paragraph: tight binding of the transition state is Pauling's idea in paragraph 8.","Reversed and irrelevant: water is the abundant one, and abundance earns it nothing.","Wrong model: an exact match to a rigid lock is Fischer's picture, the one Koshland revised."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the 1978 structures of yeast hexokinase showed that when glucose binds,","choices":["the sugar remains loosely held at the mouth of the cleft between the two lobes","water is drawn into the cleft so that it can accept the phosphate","the enzyme's two lobes separate to expose the sugar's sixth carbon atom","the phosphate group is transferred from the sugar back onto ATP","the two lobes close over the sugar and squeeze water out of the cleft"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"the two lobes swing together and close over it, so that the glucose is nearly buried and the water that had filled the cleft is squeezed out"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says that once the sugar settles at the bottom of the cleft, the two lobes swing together and close over it, burying the glucose and squeezing out the water that had filled the cleft.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: the sugar settles at the bottom of the cleft and is nearly buried, not held loosely at the mouth.","Reversed: water is squeezed out of the cleft precisely so it cannot accept the phosphate.","Reversed: the lobes close rather than separate.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says the phosphate moves from ATP onto the sugar.",null]},{"id":"rc-o24-q07","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are stated in the passage about hexokinase EXCEPT:","choices":["Without the enzyme its reaction takes about 78 million years.","It moves a phosphate group from ATP onto glucose.","Its empty form has two lobes with a deep cleft between them.","It transfers phosphate to water far more slowly than to glucose.","Its structure was examined by X-ray methods at Yale University."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"moving a phosphate group from ATP onto the sugar","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"The empty enzyme has two lobes with a deep cleft between them","choice":2},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"hexokinase transfers phosphate to water at a tiny fraction of the rate at which it transfers phosphate to glucose","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"Thomas Steitz and his colleagues at Yale University compared X-ray structures of yeast hexokinase","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"the reaction has a half-life of about 78 million years"}],"why":"The 78 million year half-life in paragraph 10 belongs to the orotidine monophosphate reaction studied by Wolfenden and Radzicka, not to hexokinase. The other four statements are all in paragraphs 3 and 5.","distractorNotes":[null,"Stated in paragraph 3: hexokinase begins the breakdown of glucose by moving a phosphate group from ATP onto the sugar.","Stated in paragraph 5: the empty enzyme has two lobes with a deep cleft between them.","Stated in paragraph 3: it transfers phosphate to water at a tiny fraction of the rate for glucose.","Stated in paragraph 5: Steitz and colleagues at Yale compared its X-ray structures."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q08","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward textbooks that present lock and key and induced fit as rival models is best described as","choices":["amused","critical","indifferent","admiring","bewildered"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"I think that framing does both men a disservice"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says plainly that the textbook framing does both men a disservice, and then explains what each man actually contributed. That is a critic correcting a habit, not a reader enjoying it or puzzled by it.","distractorNotes":["Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 6 treats the framing as funny.",null,"Too weak: the author takes a clear position and argues for it.","Reversed: the author objects to the framing rather than praising it.","Wrong register: the author explains the problem confidently, not in confusion."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q09","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, the word 'modest' most nearly means","choices":["humble about one's own achievements","reluctant to draw attention to oneself","small in size or amount","unpretentious or simple in style","decent and proper in behavior"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"even a modest reduction in its height greatly increases the rate"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 contrasts a modest reduction in the hill's height with a large increase in rate, so the word is sizing the reduction: a small one. The everyday sense of being humble has nothing to do with a hill.","distractorNotes":["This is the common meaning of the word, but a reduction in height cannot be humble.","Same problem: the sentence is about the size of a change, not anyone's personality.",null,"Style is not at issue; the sentence is comparing a small cause with a large effect.","Another everyday sense of the word, and it cannot apply to the height of a hill."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Pauling argued that an enzyme's active site is complementary to","choices":["the substrate in its resting, unstrained form","the products that rest in the second valley","any molecule small enough to fit the pocket","the transition state rather than the substrate","a stable analog that mimics the transition state"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"the active site is complementary not to the substrate but to the transition state"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 states Pauling's argument in one line: the active site is complementary not to the substrate but to the transition state.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: a site matched to the resting substrate is exactly the perfect fit Pauling faulted, one that would hold the substrate as it is.","Outside the passage: Pauling's argument says nothing about matching the products.","Wrong paragraph: the small-key problem belongs to the rigid lock in paragraph 3.",null,"Half right: analogs appear in paragraph 9 as a later test of the idea, not as what Pauling said the site matches."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q11","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that an enzyme makes its tightest contacts with a reacting molecule when that molecule","choices":["has just settled into the active site in its original form","has been fully converted into the product and is about to leave the site","is present at a concentration many thousands of times higher than usual","is held in the enzyme's pocket without having triggered any shift","is midway through the change, its bonds half broken and half formed"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"It grips the substrate loosely and the awkward halfway arrangement tightly"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"old bonds half broken and new ones half formed, that chemists call the transition state"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the enzyme grips the substrate loosely and the awkward halfway arrangement tightly, and paragraph 7 defines that halfway arrangement as the transition state, with old bonds half broken and new ones half formed. Put the two lines together and the tightest grip comes midway through the change.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 8 says the substrate in its original form is gripped loosely.","Outside the passage: nothing describes the enzyme's grip on the finished product.","Unrelated: concentration appears in paragraph 3 about water, not about the tightness of binding.","Wrong paragraph: a molecule that binds without triggering a shift simply sits there, in Koshland's account.",null]},{"id":"rc-o24-q12","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the catalytic antibodies of 1986 in paragraph 9 primarily to","choices":["present a test of Pauling's idea and mark the limit of what it shows","argue that antibodies will eventually replace natural enzymes in industry","show that transition-state analogs bind more tightly than substrates","demonstrate that Koshland's induced fit also applies to antibodies","prove that a snug fit to the transition state fully explains enzyme speed"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"The most striking test came in 1986"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"clearly part of what an enzyme does and just as clearly not all of it"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 calls the antibodies the most striking test of Pauling's prediction, then uses their slowness to say that fit to the transition state is clearly part of what an enzyme does and just as clearly not all of it. The example is there to confirm the idea and to bound it.","distractorNotes":[null,"Outside the passage: nothing is said about industry or about replacing enzymes.","Wrong sentence: that is the point of the transition-state analogs earlier in paragraph 9, not of the antibodies.","Outside the passage: Koshland's model is never connected to antibodies.","Reversed: the author says the fit is not all of it, because the antibodies worked far more slowly than natural enzymes."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q13","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Wolfenden and Radzicka determined the uncatalyzed rate of the orotidine monophosphate reaction by","choices":["measuring the reaction directly over a period of 78 million years","raising antibodies against a mimic of its transition state","comparing X-ray structures with and without the enzyme bound","heating sealed samples and extrapolating to room temperature","timing the enzyme as it finished the step in a fraction of a second"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"They had to heat sealed samples and extrapolate downward"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says they had to heat sealed samples and extrapolate downward, because at room temperature the reaction has a half-life of about 78 million years.","distractorNotes":["Impossible by the passage's own numbers: a 78 million year half-life is why they could not measure it directly.","Wrong paragraph: raising antibodies is the 1986 experiment in paragraph 9.","Wrong paragraph: X-ray structures belong to the hexokinase work in paragraph 5.",null,"Wrong measurement: the question asks about the rate without the enzyme, not with it."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q14","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of an enzyme EXCEPT:","choices":["It is unchanged when the reaction is over.","It lowers the hill between the reactant and product valleys.","It shifts the equilibrium ratio of products to reactants.","It speeds the reverse reaction as much as the forward one.","It brings a reaction to its destination far sooner."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"unchanged when the reaction is over","choice":0},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"What it lowers is the hill between the valleys","choice":1},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"the reverse reaction is accelerated by precisely the same factor as the forward one","choice":3},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"within the lifetime of a cell rather than of a mountain range","choice":4},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"an enzyme does not change where a reaction ends up"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says an enzyme does not change where a reaction ends up and leaves the energy difference between the valleys as it found it, so the equilibrium ratio is the one thing it cannot shift. The other four statements are all in paragraph 11.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 11: the enzyme is unchanged when the reaction is over.","Stated in paragraph 11: what it lowers is the hill between the valleys.",null,"Stated in paragraph 11: the reverse reaction is accelerated by precisely the same factor as the forward one.","Stated in paragraph 11: it brings the cell to its destination within the lifetime of a cell rather than of a mountain range."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q15","type":"application","stem":"A chemist adds an enzyme to a flask in which its reaction has already reached equilibrium. Which outcome does the principle described in paragraph 11 predict?","choices":["The amount of product rises, because the enzyme lowers the hill","The amounts of reactant and product remain where they were","The reaction runs backward, because the enzyme favors the lower valley","The amount of product falls as the enzyme is consumed","The amount of product rises slowly, then returns to its old value"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"an enzyme does not change where a reaction ends up"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"a lower hill is lower from both sides, so the reverse reaction is accelerated by precisely the same factor as the forward one"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"shortens the trip without moving either town"}],"why":"Paragraph 11's rule is that an enzyme does not change where a reaction ends up: it speeds both directions by the same factor and moves neither town. A flask already at its endpoint has nowhere to go, so the amounts stay put.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: lowering the hill speeds both directions equally, so it cannot push the mixture toward product.",null,"Contradicted: the enzyme does not favor either valley; it leaves the energy difference as it found it.","Contradicted: paragraph 11 says the enzyme is unchanged when the reaction is over.","Contradicted: nothing in the rule allows even a temporary departure from the endpoint."]},{"id":"rc-o24-q16","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if an enzyme speeds a forward reaction by a factor of one million, the reverse reaction would be","choices":["slowed by a factor of one million","unaffected, since the enzyme acts only in one direction","sped up, but by a smaller factor than the forward reaction","prevented entirely, since the product is removed as it forms","sped up by a factor of one million as well"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"the reverse reaction is accelerated by precisely the same factor as the forward one"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says a lower hill is lower from both sides, so the reverse reaction is accelerated by precisely the same factor as the forward one. Apply that to a factor of one million and the reverse gets the same million.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the reverse reaction is accelerated, not slowed.","Contradicted: the tunnel in paragraph 11 carries traffic in both directions.","Contradicted: the passage says precisely the same factor, not a smaller one.","Outside the passage: nothing in paragraph 11 says the product is removed.",null]},{"id":"rc-o24-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Koshland's induced fit model replaced Fischer's lock and key model, which biochemists have since abandoned as mistaken","Hexokinase is the best understood of all enzymes because its closing lobes were seen directly in X-ray structures in 1978","The catalytic antibodies of 1986 prove that any protein shaped to fit a transition state will work as well as a natural enzyme","Enzymes recognize substrates by shape and speed reactions by binding the transition state, without moving the endpoint","Enzymes speed reactions chiefly by shifting the equilibrium toward products, and they emerge unchanged when the reaction ends"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"Every enzyme raises two questions that are easy to confuse"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"complementary not to the substrate but to the transition state"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"an enzyme does not change where a reaction ends up"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 sets up two questions, recognition and speed; the middle paragraphs answer recognition with shape and speed with the transition state; paragraph 11 adds what an enzyme cannot do. Only one choice covers all three parts without overstating any of them.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 6 says Fischer's claim has never been in doubt and that the old key still turns for many enzymes.","Too narrow: hexokinase is one example, and the passage never ranks it against other enzymes.","Contradicted: paragraph 9 says the antibodies worked far more slowly than natural enzymes.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 11 says an enzyme does not shift the equilibrium at all."]}]},"rc-o25":{"id":"rc-o25","title":"An Island's Running Balance","topic":"island biogeography","field":"ecology","source":"original","words":1350,"paragraphs":[["Any naturalist who has worked on islands knows two rules of thumb: large islands hold more species than small ones, and remote islands hold fewer than islands near a coast.","The first rule was given mathematical shape in 1921 by the Swedish botanist Olof Arrhenius, who found that species number rises with area as a power law: the count is proportional to area raised to a fixed exponent.","For true islands that exponent usually falls between about 0.2 and 0.35, which means that multiplying an island's area by ten roughly doubles its number of species.","Ecologists call this the species-area relationship, and for four decades it remained a description without a mechanism: it said what islands held, not why."],["The why arrived in 1963, in a paper in the journal Evolution by Robert MacArthur and Edward O. Wilson, and in fuller form in their 1967 book, The Theory of Island Biogeography.","Wilson was a Harvard entomologist who had spent years collecting ants on islands of the western Pacific; MacArthur, at Princeton, was a mathematically minded ecologist who would die of cancer in 1972 at the age of 42.","Their shared idea was simple enough to sketch on a napkin: the number of species on an island is not a fixed property but a running balance between arrival and loss."],["Immigration, in the theory's special sense, is the rate at which species from a mainland source pool arrive on an island where they are not already present.","That rate is highest at the start and falls as the island fills, because the pool of absent species shrinks and because the best dispersers tend to arrive first.","Extinction is the rate at which resident species disappear, and it rises as the island fills, because there are more species to lose and each has a smaller population.","Plot the falling immigration curve and the rising extinction curve against the number of species present, and the two lines cross.","MacArthur and Wilson called the species number at that crossing the equilibrium, and argued that every island drifts toward its own."],["The elegance of the model lies in what it does with the two old rules of thumb.","Distance pulls the immigration curve down, since fewer colonists survive a long crossing; area pulls the extinction curve down, since a large island supports large populations that are hard to wipe out.","Four combinations follow: a large island near the mainland holds the most species, a small island far from it holds the fewest, and the other two cases fall between.","The species-area relationship stops being a bare description and becomes a consequence.","More striking, the model predicts that an island at equilibrium is not at rest: species keep arriving and keep vanishing at matching rates, so the roster turns over while the total stays put."],["That last claim, turnover, is the part of the theory I find most impressive, because it was a risky prediction.","A theory that merely said big islands are rich could never be wrong.","A theory that said the names on an island's list should change from census to census, while the list's length held steady, could be checked, and could fail.","MacArthur and Wilson leaned on the record from Krakatau, the Indonesian volcanic island that erupted in 1883 and was left all but lifeless: over the following decades the number of resident bird species climbed and then leveled off while individual species kept coming and going."],["A record kept by chance is not an experiment, and in 1966 Wilson and his graduate student Daniel Simberloff set out to run one.","Their islands were small clumps of red mangrove off the Florida Keys, each rooted in shallow water and small enough to be covered by a tent.","Mangrove islets suited the purpose because their animals were almost entirely arthropods, insects, spiders, and their relatives, which colonize quickly and can be counted on a few trees.","Simberloff censused every species on each islet, then hired a pest-control crew to tent the islet and fumigate it with methyl bromide, the method then used to rid houses of termites.","The islets came out nearly empty of animal life but structurally intact, so the experiment had changed nothing except the species count.","Over the next two years Simberloff returned repeatedly to record what had come back."],["The results, published in the journal Ecology in 1969, fit the model better than anyone had a right to expect.","Within roughly a year, the number of species on most islets had climbed back to about its pre-fumigation level.","Islets closer to a source of colonists refilled faster and reached higher counts, while the most isolated islet lagged and settled below its original number.","The lists themselves were different: many returning species were not the ones that had been killed, and the membership kept shifting from visit to visit even after the totals had stabilized.","Here was turnover, observed rather than inferred.","The experiment had its limits, and it is worth being candid about them: the islets were minute, the animals were arthropods only, and two years is a short time."],["Later work added texture without overturning the frame.","In 1977 James Brown and Astrid Kodric-Brown pointed out that distance affects extinction as well: on a near island, a dwindling population is regularly topped up by fresh arrivals of its own species, an idea they called the rescue effect.","And not every island sits at an equilibrium at all.","In 1971 Brown had examined small mammals on isolated mountaintops in the Great Basin of the western United States, where cool forest survives above desert; with the desert impassable, immigration had effectively stopped since the last ice age, and the faunas were simply losing species, a one-way slide ecologists call relaxation."],["Jared Diamond carried the theory out of the academy in 1975, arguing that a nature reserve surrounded by cleared land is an island too, and should be designed like one.","His rules are still reproduced in textbooks: a large reserve beats a small one, one large reserve beats several small ones of the same total area, reserves close together beat reserves far apart, a compact shape beats a long thin one, and corridors between reserves beat none.","The second rule set off a quarrel that ran for years and acquired its own acronym, SLOSS, for single large or several small.","Simberloff himself, writing with Lawrence Abele in 1976, objected that several small reserves spread across different habitats might together hold more species than one large block, and that the theory gave no clean verdict."],["The largest experiment built to settle such questions began in 1979 north of Manaus, in the Brazilian Amazon, where Thomas Lovejoy arranged with ranchers clearing forest to leave standing patches of 1, 10, and 100 hectares.","The Biological Dynamics of Forest Fragments Project has followed those patches for more than four decades.","The small fragments lost species quickly, the understory birds that follow army ant swarms among the first to go, and their edges dried and brightened as wind and sun pushed in, until a 1-hectare patch was in effect all edge.","But the project also showed how a fragment differs from an islet: when cleared land around a patch grew back into young secondary forest, birds began to cross it, and the patch behaved as if it had moved closer to the mainland."],["That last finding is the one I would keep in front of anyone tempted to treat the theory as a blueprint.","An island is bounded by water that no beetle can walk across; a fragment is bounded by pasture, roads, and regrowth, a matrix that is sometimes a wall and sometimes a bridge, and one that people can change.","The equilibrium model earns its place because it turned two rules of thumb into a mechanism and then survived a real test on real islands.","It does not follow that a reserve can be sized by an exponent.","The honest use of the theory is as a scaffold for sharper questions about area, isolation, and loss, not as a formula that answers them."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o25-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the exponent in the species-area relationship for true islands implies that","choices":["doubling an island's area roughly multiplies its species count by ten","a tenfold increase in island area roughly doubles the number of species","the number of species rises in direct proportion to an island's area","remote islands hold about one third as many species as islands near a coast","species number stops rising once an island exceeds a certain area"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"multiplying an island's area by ten roughly doubles its number of species"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the exponent usually sits between about 0.2 and 0.35, which means that multiplying an island's area by ten roughly doubles its number of species. Ten times the area, twice the species.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the passage multiplies area by ten and gets twice the species, not the other way around.",null,"Contradicted: a power law with an exponent well below one is not direct proportion; ten times the area gives only twice the species.","Mash-up: 0.35 is the upper end of the exponent, and the remoteness rule in paragraph 1 has no number attached.","Outside the passage: nothing says the species count ever stops rising with area."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage states which of the following about Robert MacArthur?","choices":["He spent years collecting ants on islands of the western Pacific.","He published the equilibrium theory alone in 1963 before Wilson joined him.","He was a Harvard entomologist with a gift for field collecting.","He was a mathematically minded ecologist who worked at Princeton.","He fumigated mangrove islets in the Florida Keys with Simberloff."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"MacArthur, at Princeton, was a mathematically minded ecologist"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 places MacArthur at Princeton and calls him a mathematically minded ecologist, in contrast to Wilson the Harvard entomologist.","distractorNotes":["Wrong person: paragraph 2 gives the ant collecting to Wilson.","Contradicted: paragraph 2 says the 1963 paper was by MacArthur and Wilson together.","Wrong person: Wilson was the Harvard entomologist.",null,"Wrong person: paragraph 6 says Wilson and his student Simberloff ran the fumigation experiment."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the immigration rate falls as an island fills because","choices":["fewer absent species remain and the best dispersers have already arrived","the resident species crowd out newcomers through sharper competition","the mainland source pool itself shrinks as species leave it for the island","each resident species has a smaller population and is easier to lose","longer crossings become necessary once the nearest sources are used up"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"because the pool of absent species shrinks and because the best dispersers tend to arrive first"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 gives two reasons the immigration rate falls: the pool of species still absent from the island shrinks, and the best dispersers tend to arrive first, leaving slower travelers for later.","distractorNotes":[null,"Outside the passage: competition among residents is never offered as a reason for falling immigration.","Misread: the pool that shrinks is the set of species not yet on the island; the passage never says the mainland loses species.","Wrong process: a smaller population per species is paragraph 3's reason that extinction rises, not that immigration falls.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about crossings growing longer as an island fills."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q04","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if an island's extinction curve were lowered while its immigration curve stayed the same, the island's equilibrium number of species would","choices":["fall, because fewer new species would arrive to replace those that were lost","stay the same, because the equilibrium depends only on immigration","fall, because the two curves would no longer cross at any point","stay the same, because turnover holds the total fixed regardless","rise, because the curves would cross at a higher species count"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"called the species number at that crossing the equilibrium"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"area pulls the extinction curve down, since a large island supports large populations that are hard to wipe out"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"a large island near the mainland holds the most species"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 defines the equilibrium as the crossing of the two curves, and paragraph 4 says a large island pulls the extinction curve down and holds more species. Lowering the extinction curve moves the crossing to a higher species count.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the immigration curve is unchanged, and a lower extinction curve means fewer losses, not fewer arrivals.","Contradicted: paragraph 3 makes the equilibrium depend on both curves, which is why area matters in paragraph 4.","Contradicted: a lower extinction curve still rises as the island fills, so it still crosses the falling immigration curve.","Wrong idea: turnover in paragraph 4 describes what happens at a given equilibrium, not what sets its size.",null]},{"id":"rc-o25-q05","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions Krakatau in paragraph 5 primarily to","choices":["show that volcanic islands recover their species faster than other islands","describe the method that Simberloff and Wilson would later copy","point to a real-world record that bore out the turnover prediction","argue that bird species are better dispersers than arthropods","demonstrate that the species-area relationship fails on remote islands"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"could be checked, and could fail"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"the number of resident bird species climbed and then leveled off while individual species kept coming and going"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 sets up turnover as a prediction that could be checked, then says MacArthur and Wilson leaned on the Krakatau record, where the bird count leveled off while individual species kept coming and going. Krakatau is there as evidence for turnover.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: no comparison of recovery speed between volcanic and other islands is made.","Contradicted: paragraph 6 says a record kept by chance is not an experiment, and the islets were fumigated, not left to a volcano.",null,"Outside the passage: birds and arthropods are never compared as dispersers.","Wrong idea: Krakatau illustrates turnover, not a failure of the species-area relationship."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, mangrove islets were well suited to the experiment because","choices":["their animals were mainly arthropods, which colonize fast and are easily counted","red mangroves regrow rapidly after fumigation strips them of their leaves and bark","they were the only islands near a mainland source pool of colonists","their arthropods were resistant to methyl bromide and survived fumigation","they had been censused by naturalists for decades before the study began"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"their animals were almost entirely arthropods, insects, spiders, and their relatives, which colonize quickly and can be counted on a few trees"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the islets suited the purpose because their animals were almost entirely arthropods, which colonize quickly and can be counted on a few trees.","distractorNotes":[null,"Outside the passage: paragraph 6 says the islets came out structurally intact, and nothing is said about regrowth.","Too extreme and never said: the passage does not call them the only islands near a source.","Reversed: the fumigation left the islets nearly empty of animal life, so the arthropods did not survive it.","Wrong detail: the decades-long record belongs to Krakatau in paragraph 5; Simberloff did the censusing himself."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q07","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are stated in the passage about the Florida Keys experiment EXCEPT:","choices":["The islets were fumigated with methyl bromide.","Simberloff counted every species on each islet before the fumigation.","Most islets regained roughly their earlier number of species within about a year.","The most isolated islet eventually exceeded its original species count.","Many of the species that returned differed from those that had been killed."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"fumigate it with methyl bromide","choice":0},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"Simberloff censused every species on each islet","choice":1},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"Within roughly a year, the number of species on most islets had climbed back","choice":2},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"many returning species were not the ones that had been killed","choice":4},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"the most isolated islet lagged and settled below its original number"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the most isolated islet lagged and settled below its original number, the opposite of exceeding it. The other four statements are all in paragraphs 6 and 7.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 6: the crew tented each islet and fumigated it with methyl bromide.","Stated in paragraph 6: Simberloff censused every species on each islet, then hired the crew.","Stated in paragraph 7: within roughly a year most islets had climbed back to about their earlier level.",null,"Stated in paragraph 7: many returning species were not the ones that had been killed."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q08","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, the word 'minute' most nearly means","choices":["brief","tiny","exact","few","remote"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"the islets were minute"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lists the experiment's limits, and the first is that the islets were minute: paragraph 6 has already called them small clumps small enough to cover with a tent. The word here means very small, not a unit of time.","distractorNotes":["This leans on the sixty-second meaning of the word; the sentence is about the islets' size, and the time limit is a separate item in the same list.",null,"Minute can mean precise, as in a minute examination, but an islet cannot be exact; the sentence is about how small it is.","The sentence says nothing about how many islets there were; it describes each islet's size.","Isolation is discussed elsewhere in paragraph 7, but this word describes size, not distance."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the most isolated islet in the experiment","choices":["was never fully emptied of animal life by the fumigation","refilled faster than the others because it faced less competition","reached a higher count than before because turnover added species","received colonists only after two years had passed","refilled more slowly and leveled off below its earlier number"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"the most isolated islet lagged and settled below its original number"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says islets closer to a source refilled faster and higher, while the most isolated islet lagged and settled below its original number.","distractorNotes":["Wrong detail: paragraph 6 says the islets came out nearly empty, and no islet is singled out as an exception.","Reversed: paragraph 7 says the most isolated islet lagged behind the others.","Reversed: it settled below its original number, not above it.","Too extreme: the passage says it lagged, not that it received nothing for two years.",null]},{"id":"rc-o25-q10","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, once the rescue effect is taken into account, an island near the mainland should","choices":["receive fewer new species than a far island of the same size","have a higher extinction rate than a far island of the same size","lose resident species less often than a far island of the same size","reach equilibrium only after its dwindling populations have died out","hold the same number of species as a far island of the same size"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"distance affects extinction as well: on a near island, a dwindling population is regularly topped up by fresh arrivals of its own species"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says that on a near island a dwindling population is regularly topped up by fresh arrivals of its own species, so distance affects extinction too. A population that keeps getting topped up is harder to lose, which means fewer extinctions on the near island.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 4 says distance pulls immigration down, so the near island receives more new species, not fewer.","Reversed: the rescue effect lowers extinction on the near island.",null,"Contradicted: the rescue effect keeps dwindling populations alive rather than waiting for them to die.","Contradicted: paragraphs 4 and 8 both give the near island more species, not the same number."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q11","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the mammal faunas on Great Basin mountaintops","choices":["reached equilibrium soon after the last ice age ended","gain new species whenever the desert between peaks is crossed","were rescued from extinction by arrivals from nearby mountains","turned over steadily while their totals stayed roughly constant","were losing species with almost no immigration to replace them"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"immigration had effectively stopped since the last ice age, and the faunas were simply losing species"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says that with the desert impassable, immigration had effectively stopped since the last ice age and the faunas were simply losing species, a slide the passage calls relaxation.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 8 introduces the mountaintops as islands that do not sit at an equilibrium.","Contradicted: the desert is described as impassable, so it is not being crossed.","Reversed: the rescue effect in paragraph 8 belongs to near islands, and these mountaintops get no arrivals.","Wrong idea: turnover with a steady total is the equilibrium pattern of paragraph 4, which these faunas do not show.",null]},{"id":"rc-o25-q12","type":"function","stem":"The author lists Diamond's reserve design rules in paragraph 9 primarily to","choices":["show how the theory was turned into practical guidance for reserves","endorse the rules as the best available method for sizing a reserve","prove that Simberloff had abandoned the theory he had once helped to test","explain why the Amazon fragments were cut to 1, 10, and 100 hectares","demonstrate that the species-area exponent applies to cleared land"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"a nature reserve surrounded by cleared land is an island too, and should be designed like one"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"The second rule set off a quarrel"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 opens with Diamond carrying the theory out of the academy and arguing that a reserve should be designed like an island; the rules are the concrete form that guidance took, and the quarrel that follows grows out of one of them.","distractorNotes":[null,"Too strong: the author reports that the rules are still reproduced in textbooks, then immediately turns to the quarrel over one of them, which is not an endorsement.","Outside the passage: paragraph 9 says Simberloff objected to one rule, not that he abandoned the theory.","Wrong paragraph: the fragment sizes in paragraph 10 are never tied to Diamond's rules.","Outside the passage: the exponent appears in paragraph 1 for true islands, and paragraph 11 doubts it can size a reserve."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Simberloff's view of the equilibrium theory was that it","choices":["had failed its test on the mangrove islets and should be set aside","proved that a single large reserve always outperforms several small ones","applied to arthropods on islets but never to birds or mammals","held up on islets but did not settle how reserves should be built","was sound in principle but had never been tested in the field"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"fit the model better than anyone had a right to expect"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"the theory gave no clean verdict"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says Simberloff's own results fit the model better than anyone expected, and paragraph 9 says that in the reserve debate he objected that the theory gave no clean verdict. Put together, he saw it confirmed on islets but not as a ruling on reserve design.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: paragraph 7 says the results fit the model unusually well.","Reversed: paragraph 9 says Simberloff objected to the one-large rule.","Too extreme and never said: the passage lists arthropods as a limit of the experiment, not as the theory's boundary.",null,"Contradicted: Simberloff ran the field test himself, as paragraphs 6 and 7 describe."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q14","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of the Biological Dynamics of Forest Fragments Project EXCEPT:","choices":["It began in 1979 north of Manaus in the Brazilian Amazon.","Its patches were cut in sizes of 1, 10, and 1,000 hectares.","Understory birds that follow army ant swarms were among the first species lost.","It has followed its forest patches for more than four decades.","Birds began crossing cleared land once secondary forest grew back."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"began in 1979 north of Manaus, in the Brazilian Amazon","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"the understory birds that follow army ant swarms among the first to go","choice":2},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"has followed those patches for more than four decades","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"when cleared land around a patch grew back into young secondary forest, birds began to cross it","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"patches of 1, 10, and 100 hectares"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives the patch sizes as 1, 10, and 100 hectares; the largest is 100, not 1,000. Every other statement is in paragraph 10.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 10: the experiment began in 1979 north of Manaus, in the Brazilian Amazon.",null,"Stated in paragraph 10: the understory birds that follow army ant swarms were among the first to go.","Stated in paragraph 10: the project has followed the patches for more than four decades.","Stated in paragraph 10: once cleared land grew back into young secondary forest, birds began to cross it."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q15","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the point the author draws from the Amazon fragments in paragraphs 10 and 11?","choices":["A pond that dries up each summer loses its fish and is restocked every spring by a state hatchery","A mountaintop forest keeps losing species because the desert around it cannot be crossed","A city park gains new butterfly species after nearby lots are planted with wildflowers","An offshore island gains species when a larger island nearby is set aside as a reserve","A small reserve holds fewer bird species than a large reserve of the same shape"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"when cleared land around a patch grew back into young secondary forest, birds began to cross it, and the patch behaved as if it had moved closer to the mainland"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"a matrix that is sometimes a wall and sometimes a bridge, and one that people can change"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says that when the land around a fragment grew back, birds crossed it and the fragment behaved as if it were closer to its source, and paragraph 11 calls that surrounding matrix a wall or a bridge that people can change. A park that gains species once its surroundings become crossable is the same pattern.","distractorNotes":["Wrong mechanism: the hatchery delivers the fish directly; nothing about the pond's surroundings has become easier to cross.","Wrong paragraph: this is the relaxation case from paragraph 8, where the matrix stays a wall.",null,"Wrong mechanism: the water between the islands is unchanged, so the island has not become easier to reach.","Wrong rule: this illustrates the area effect, not the idea that the surrounding matrix can change."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward using the equilibrium theory as a blueprint for reserve design is best described as","choices":["admiring","hostile","indifferent","cautious","bewildered"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"anyone tempted to treat the theory as a blueprint"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"It does not follow that a reserve can be sized by an exponent."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"not as a formula that answers them"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 warns anyone tempted to treat the theory as a blueprint, says a reserve cannot be sized by an exponent, and offers the theory as a scaffold for questions rather than a formula. That is a careful, hedging voice, not a dismissive or an admiring one.","distractorNotes":["Wrong target: the author admires the theory itself in paragraphs 5 and 11, but the question asks about using it as a blueprint, which the author warns against.","Too strong: paragraph 11 says the model earns its place and is a useful scaffold, so the author is not against it.","Too weak: the author takes a clear position and spends the final paragraph defending it.",null,"Wrong register: the author explains the limits of the theory confidently, not in confusion."]},{"id":"rc-o25-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Simberloff's mangrove experiment proved that an island's species count never changes once its equilibrium has been reached","The equilibrium theory explained island species counts and passed a field test, but it is a guide to questions about fragments, not a formula","Nature reserves should always be designed as single large blocks because the island theory shows that large islands hold the most species","The species-area relationship is a dependable law that allows ecologists to predict exactly how many species a reserve of any size will hold","The Amazon fragments project overturned the equilibrium theory by showing that habitat fragments do not behave like true islands in any respect"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"a running balance between arrival and loss"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"it turned two rules of thumb into a mechanism and then survived a real test on real islands"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"a scaffold for sharper questions about area, isolation, and loss, not as a formula that answers them"}],"why":"The passage explains the equilibrium theory, follows it through the Krakatau record and the mangrove experiment, then turns to reserves and fragments and closes by calling the theory a scaffold for questions rather than a formula. Only one choice covers both the theory's success and its limits.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 7 found turnover, meaning the membership kept changing; it was the total, not the list, that held steady.",null,"Too extreme and contradicted: paragraph 9 says the single-large rule set off a quarrel and the theory gave no clean verdict.","Contradicted: paragraph 11 says it does not follow that a reserve can be sized by an exponent.","Too strong: paragraph 10 shows fragments differ from islets in one respect, but paragraph 11 still says the model earns its place."]}]},"rc-o26":{"id":"rc-o26","title":"The Room That Was Measured","topic":"clair patterson, the age of the earth, and lead","field":"geochemistry","source":"original","words":1330,"paragraphs":[["The age of the Earth is a number most people have heard and almost no one can trace.","It is about 4.55 billion years, published in 1956 by Clair Patterson, a geochemist at the California Institute of Technology then in his early thirties.","What makes the story worth telling is not the number but the obstacle in its way: lead, the element Patterson needed to measure, turned out to be smeared over everything he touched, and clearing it away changed his career and eventually the air of the whole country."],["Before radioactivity was understood, the best physics gave a much younger planet.","In the nineteenth century Lord Kelvin treated the Earth as a cooling ball of once-molten rock and concluded from the heat still leaking out of the ground that it could be at most a few tens of millions of years old.","Radioactivity, discovered in 1896, undid that argument twice over: decaying uranium and thorium in the crust supply heat Kelvin had not known about, and they also offer a clock.","Uranium-238 decays to lead-206 with a half-life of about 4.5 billion years, and uranium-235 to lead-207 with one of about 700 million.","Lead-204, by contrast, is produced by no decay at all, so it serves as a fixed reference against which the growing radiogenic isotopes, the ones born of decay, can be measured."],["The clock has a catch.","To read a rock's age from its lead you must know how much of each isotope was there at the start, and the Earth's surface rocks have been melted, mixed, and reworked so many times that their starting lead is lost.","Patterson's doctoral adviser at the University of Chicago, Harrison Brown, saw a way around this.","Meteorites are leftovers from the formation of the solar system, and if the Earth formed at the same time, their primordial lead should be the Earth's as well.","Iron meteorites in particular contain almost no uranium, so whatever lead they hold has sat unchanged since they solidified."],["The job proved far harder than anyone expected.","Patterson had learned mass spectrometry at Oak Ridge, Tennessee, during the Manhattan Project, and the instrument was not the difficulty.","It appeared when he and a fellow student, George Tilton, measured traces of uranium and lead in zircon crystals from a granite: the uranium numbers were steady, but the lead came out wildly high and different on every run.","A sample might hold only billionths of a gram of lead, and the laboratory was adding more than that from the air, the dust, the paint, the plumbing solder, and the bottled acids themselves.","Patterson was not measuring the rock.","He was measuring the room."],["His answer was a laboratory cleaner than any yet built.","At Chicago and then at Caltech, where he moved in 1952, he sealed the walls, boiled his glassware in acid, redistilled his water and reagents in quartz vessels, kept the air in the room at a slightly higher pressure than the hallway, and required everyone who entered to change into clean garments.","Above all he measured the blank: a run through the whole procedure with no sample at all, which reveals how much lead the method itself contributes.","It was slow, unglamorous work, and I think it was the most important thing he ever did, because every measurement that followed rested on it."],["Patterson's primordial lead came from the Canyon Diablo meteorite, the iron mass that dug Meteor Crater in Arizona, which he compared with stony meteorites that did contain uranium and so had been accumulating radiogenic lead since they formed.","Plotted as lead-207 against lead-206, each relative to lead-204, the samples fell on a straight line, an isochron, whose slope depends only on the time since they shared the same starting lead.","The slope gave 4.55 billion years, give or take about 70 million, and the value accepted today, about 4.54 billion, sits inside that range.","Lead from modern ocean sediment, standing in for the Earth as a whole, fell on the same line, which meant the planet and the meteorites were the same age."],["Patterson spent his next two decades asking where all that lead had come from.","The largest single source was gasoline.","In 1921 Thomas Midgley Jr., a chemist at General Motors, found that a small dose of tetraethyl lead stopped engines from knocking, and the additive went on sale in 1923.","To keep lead from fouling the engine, refiners also blended in halogen compounds that carried it out of the tailpipe as fine particles.","After refinery workers died of lead poisoning in 1924, the Surgeon General called a conference and sales paused, but a brief study found no evidence of public harm and the additive returned to the market in 1926."],["In the early 1960s he and his collaborators found far more lead in surface seawater than in the deep water beneath it, a pattern that pointed to recent fallout from the air rather than to any natural source from below.","Greenland snow, which preserves each year's atmosphere, told the same story in 1969: lead in the ice had risen roughly two-hundredfold from the oldest layers to the mid-1960s, most of it after leaded gasoline entered wide use.","In a 1965 paper, 'Contaminated and Natural Lead Environments of Man,' he drew the conclusion that mattered: the amount of lead in a typical modern body was not the natural amount but something like a hundred times higher, and calling it normal confused what is common with what is safe."],["For four decades the established view belonged to Robert Kehoe, a physician whose laboratory at the University of Cincinnati was funded largely by the makers of the additive.","Kehoe held that lead was natural in the environment, that the body excreted what it absorbed, and that below a threshold it did no harm, and he could point to thousands of measurements showing that ordinary people carried lead without obvious illness.","Patterson's reply was that every one of those measurements had been compared against the wrong baseline, since the 'normal' people were themselves contaminated.","He said so to a Senate subcommittee under Edmund Muskie in 1966.","It is tempting to tell what followed as one stubborn man against an industry, and I resist that telling only partly: the man was stubborn and the industry was powerful, but the laws that changed were written by other hands and rested on other evidence too."],["The Clean Air Act of 1970 gave the new Environmental Protection Agency authority over fuel additives, and in 1973 the agency ordered a gradual reduction of lead in gasoline.","From the 1975 model year, new cars carried catalytic converters, and because lead ruins a catalyst, those cars required unleaded fuel.","In 1979 the pediatrician Herbert Needleman reported that children with more lead in their shed baby teeth scored lower on tests of intelligence and attention, harm at levels Kehoe's threshold had called safe.","In 1985 the agency cut the lead allowed in gasoline by roughly ninety percent, and under amendments passed in 1990 the sale of leaded gasoline for road vehicles ended on the first day of 1996.","Patterson died in December 1995, a few weeks before the ban took full effect."],["Patterson's two careers used one tool.","The same discipline that let him trust a few billionths of a gram of lead from a meteorite let him see the lead in seawater, in Greenland snow, and in canned tuna, where most of the metal, he showed in 1980, came from the solder.","A measurement is only as good as its blank, and a judgment about what is normal is only as good as the baseline it is compared to.","I do not think the second lesson has been fully learned.","Lead left gasoline, paint, and solder, but each generation inherits an altered world and calls it natural, and the hard part, as Patterson found, is not the instrument but the willingness to measure what everyone else has agreed to assume."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o26-q01","type":"function","stem":"The author discusses Lord Kelvin in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["show that nineteenth-century physicists ignored the heat escaping from the ground","contrast an older estimate with the clock that radioactivity made possible","argue that Kelvin's method was sound and only his data were wrong","explain why Patterson chose meteorites rather than rocks from the Earth's surface","demonstrate that the Earth has been cooling steadily since it formed"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"Before radioactivity was understood, the best physics gave a much younger planet."},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"undid that argument twice over"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 opens by saying the best physics before radioactivity gave a much younger planet, then shows how radioactivity undid Kelvin's argument and offered a clock. Kelvin is there as the before picture that the uranium clock replaces.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: Kelvin built his estimate on the heat leaking from the ground; he did not ignore it.",null,"Outside the passage: the author never defends Kelvin's method, and the problem was a missing heat source, not bad data.","Wrong paragraph: the choice of meteorites is explained in paragraph 3, and Kelvin has nothing to do with it.","This is Kelvin's own assumption, not the author's purpose, and paragraph 2 says radioactive decay adds heat he did not know about."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, lead-204 is useful as a reference isotope because it","choices":["has the longest half-life of any isotope of lead","is the final product of the decay of uranium-235","is found only in iron meteorites and not in Earth rocks","is not created by the decay of any other element","accumulates at a fixed rate as uranium decays"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"Lead-204, by contrast, is produced by no decay at all, so it serves as a fixed reference"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says lead-204 is produced by no decay at all, which is exactly why it can serve as a fixed reference while the radiogenic isotopes grow.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: no half-life is given for any lead isotope, and lead-204 is described as stable, not decaying.","Wrong isotope: paragraph 2 says uranium-235 decays to lead-207.","Outside the passage: lead-204 is used as a reference in every sample, including Earth sediment in paragraph 6.",null,"Reversed: the isotopes that accumulate as uranium decays are lead-206 and lead-207, not lead-204."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q03","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if the Earth's surface rocks had never been melted and reworked, Patterson would most likely have","choices":["been able to find the starting lead in rocks from the Earth itself","found that uranium-238 decays more slowly than it does in meteorites","still needed iron meteorites, since Earth rocks contain no lead-204","measured an age for the Earth considerably greater than 4.55 billion years","avoided the contamination that plagued his zircon measurements"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"the Earth's surface rocks have been melted, mixed, and reworked so many times that their starting lead is lost"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"their primordial lead should be the Earth's as well"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the starting lead is lost precisely because surface rocks have been melted, mixed, and reworked, and that meteorites are the way around this. Remove the reworking and the reason for going to meteorites disappears.","distractorNotes":[null,"Outside the passage: a half-life is a fixed property of the isotope; nothing suggests it differs by location.","Contradicted: paragraph 6 uses lead-204 ratios for Earth sediment, so Earth rocks do contain it.","Unsupported: the passage gives no reason the age itself would change; only the route to the starting lead would.","Wrong paragraph: the contamination in paragraph 4 came from the laboratory, not from the history of the rocks."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when Patterson and Tilton measured zircon crystals as students, they found that","choices":["the uranium readings varied wildly while the lead readings stayed steady","the crystals held far less lead than the uranium clock predicted","the mass spectrometer itself was adding lead to every sample","the granite had been melted and reworked so its starting lead was lost","the lead readings were too high and changed from one run to the next"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"the uranium numbers were steady, but the lead came out wildly high and different on every run"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the uranium numbers were steady but the lead came out wildly high and different on every run, which is what revealed the contamination.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 4 says the uranium was steady and the lead varied.","Reversed: the lead came out too high, not too low.","Contradicted: paragraph 4 says the instrument itself was not the difficulty; the room was.","Wrong paragraph: melting and reworking is the problem with starting lead in paragraph 3, not the zircon result.",null]},{"id":"rc-o26-q05","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are mentioned in paragraph 5 as steps Patterson took to keep lead out of his laboratory EXCEPT:","choices":["boiling the glassware in acid before use","keeping the room's air at a higher pressure than the hallway","storing the samples in sealed metal containers","redistilling water and reagents in quartz vessels","requiring anyone who entered to change into clean garments"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"boiled his glassware in acid","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"kept the air in the room at a slightly higher pressure than the hallway","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"redistilled his water and reagents in quartz vessels","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"required everyone who entered to change into clean garments","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 5 lists sealing the walls, boiling glassware in acid, redistilling water and reagents in quartz, positive air pressure, and clean garments. Sealed metal containers appear nowhere, and metal is the last thing a lead-free lab would want near a sample.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 5: he boiled his glassware in acid.","Stated in paragraph 5: the room's air was kept at a slightly higher pressure than the hallway.",null,"Stated in paragraph 5: he redistilled his water and reagents in quartz vessels.","Stated in paragraph 5: everyone who entered had to change into clean garments."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 5, the word 'blank' most nearly means","choices":["a trial of the method with no sample in it","an empty space on a form where a result goes","a cartridge that fires without a bullet","a moment in which the memory fails","a sample so pure that it contains no lead"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"a run through the whole procedure with no sample at all, which reveals how much lead the method itself contributes"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 defines the word in the same sentence: a run through the whole procedure with no sample at all, done to learn how much lead the method itself adds.","distractorNotes":[null,"This is the everyday meaning of the word, but the sentence describes a procedure, not a space on paper.","Another common meaning, with no connection to a laboratory procedure.","A figurative meaning that has nothing to do with the sentence.","Half right: the point of a blank is that there is no sample at all, not a very pure one."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q07","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the clean-laboratory work described in paragraph 5 is best described as","choices":["amused","skeptical","indifferent","admiring","nostalgic"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"I think it was the most important thing he ever did"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 calls the work slow and unglamorous and then says, I think it was the most important thing he ever did, because every later measurement rested on it. That is admiration.","distractorNotes":["Wrong register: the author takes the drudgery seriously rather than finding it funny.","Reversed: the author endorses the work without reservation.","Too weak: the author makes a strong personal judgment about its importance.",null,"Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 5 looks back fondly on a lost time; the work is praised for what it made possible."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, compared with the Canyon Diablo meteorite, a stony meteorite containing uranium would show","choices":["lower ratios of lead-206 and lead-207 to lead-204","higher ratios of lead-206 and lead-207 to lead-204","the same lead ratios, since both formed at the same time","a larger amount of lead-204 relative to its uranium","a slope on the isochron plot that gives a younger age"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"had been accumulating radiogenic lead since they formed"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"Iron meteorites in particular contain almost no uranium, so whatever lead they hold has sat unchanged"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"the growing radiogenic isotopes, the ones born of decay"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says lead-206 and lead-207 grow by decay while lead-204 does not, paragraph 3 says the iron meteorite's lead has sat unchanged, and paragraph 6 says the stony meteorites had been accumulating radiogenic lead. So their ratios to lead-204 must be higher.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: uranium decay adds lead-206 and lead-207, so the ratios rise, not fall.",null,"Contradicted: paragraph 6 says the stony meteorites had been accumulating radiogenic lead, so their ratios moved while the iron meteorite's stayed put.","Reversed: lead-204 is not produced by decay, so it is the radiogenic isotopes, not lead-204, that are more abundant.","Confused: paragraph 6 says all the samples fall on one line with one slope; a single sample does not have its own age-giving slope."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, lead from modern ocean sediment was included in the 1956 analysis in order to","choices":["supply the primordial lead composition that the meteorites lacked","show that leaded gasoline had already reached the deep sea","correct the meteorite data for lead added by the laboratory","measure how much uranium the Earth's crust had lost over time","test whether the Earth and the meteorites were the same age"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"standing in for the Earth as a whole, fell on the same line, which meant the planet and the meteorites were the same age"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the ocean sediment stood in for the Earth as a whole and fell on the same line, which meant the planet and the meteorites were the same age.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the primordial lead came from the Canyon Diablo iron meteorite, not from the sediment.","Wrong paragraph: the seawater work on gasoline lead comes in paragraph 8, years later.","Wrong tool: the laboratory's added lead was measured with the blank in paragraph 5.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about uranium lost from the crust.",null]},{"id":"rc-o26-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, refiners added halogen compounds to leaded gasoline in order to","choices":["stop engines from knocking at high compression","keep the lead particles from escaping into the air","carry the lead out of the engine through the exhaust","satisfy the Surgeon General's conditions for resuming sales","protect the catalytic converters in later cars"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"To keep lead from fouling the engine, refiners also blended in halogen compounds that carried it out of the tailpipe as fine particles"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the halogen compounds were blended in to keep lead from fouling the engine, carrying it out of the tailpipe as fine particles.","distractorNotes":["Wrong additive: stopping knock was the job of tetraethyl lead itself.","Reversed: the halogen compounds sent the lead out of the tailpipe as fine particles.",null,"Outside the passage: no conditions from the Surgeon General are described.","Wrong paragraph and reversed: paragraph 10 says lead ruins a catalyst, so nothing in the fuel protected converters."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q11","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if the lead in the ocean had come mainly from natural sources on the sea floor, Patterson would have expected","choices":["surface and deep water to hold no measurable lead at all","the surface water to hold far more lead than the deep water below it","the Greenland ice to show a steady decline in lead","the deep water to hold at least as much lead as the surface","the lead in the surface water to have come from leaded gasoline"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"a pattern that pointed to recent fallout from the air rather than to any natural source from below"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the surface excess pointed to fallout from the air rather than a natural source from below. A source on the sea floor would feed the deep water first, so the deep water should hold at least as much lead as the surface.","distractorNotes":["Too extreme: a natural source would still put lead in the water; the question is where it would be concentrated.","Reversed: this is the pattern Patterson actually found, and it is what pointed away from the sea floor.","Wrong evidence: the Greenland ice is a separate record of the atmosphere, and the passage says it rose, not fell.",null,"Reversed: gasoline fallout is the conclusion the surface excess supported, not what a sea-floor source would predict."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Patterson's 1965 paper argued that","choices":["a typical modern body held about a hundred times the natural amount of lead","the body excretes nearly all of the lead that it absorbs from food and air","lead in Greenland ice had risen about two-hundredfold since ancient times","children with more lead in their baby teeth scored lower on tests of intelligence","ordinary people carried lead without any obvious signs of illness"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"the amount of lead in a typical modern body was not the natural amount but something like a hundred times higher"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the 1965 paper concluded that the lead in a typical modern body was not the natural amount but something like a hundred times higher.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong side of the debate: this was Kehoe's position in paragraph 9, not Patterson's.","Wrong year and wrong study: the Greenland ice result in paragraph 8 came in 1969.","Wrong person: this was Needleman's 1979 finding in paragraph 10.","Wrong side of the debate: paragraph 9 attributes this to Kehoe's thousands of measurements."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q13","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions Kehoe's thousands of measurements in paragraph 9 primarily to","choices":["show that Kehoe had far more data than Patterson and was therefore more credible","prove that lead below a certain threshold causes no harm","explain why the Senate subcommittee rejected Patterson's testimony","illustrate the kind of blank that Patterson ran in his own laboratory","present the evidence that Patterson said rested on a mistaken baseline"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"he could point to thousands of measurements showing that ordinary people carried lead without obvious illness"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"every one of those measurements had been compared against the wrong baseline"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 sets up Kehoe's thousands of measurements and then, in the very next sentence, gives Patterson's reply: every one of them had been compared against the wrong baseline. The measurements are there so the reply has something to answer.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the author never says Kehoe was more credible; the next sentence undercuts his data.","Reversed: the author reports the threshold claim as Kehoe's view, not as proven, and paragraph 10 says Needleman found harm below it.","Outside the passage: the passage never says the subcommittee rejected Patterson.","Wrong paragraph: the blank belongs to paragraph 5 and has nothing to do with Kehoe.",null]},{"id":"rc-o26-q14","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the error the author describes in paragraph 8, confusing what is common with what is safe?","choices":["A chemist discards a measurement because the blank came out higher than the sample itself","A town calls its water normal because its levels match those of nearby towns on the same river","A clinic flags a patient's blood pressure because it is higher than that of healthy adults","A factory reduces its emissions after a study finds harm at levels it had considered acceptable","A geologist estimates a rock's age from its lead without first measuring its starting composition"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"calling it normal confused what is common with what is safe"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"a judgment about what is normal is only as good as the baseline it is compared to"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says calling a contaminated level normal confuses what is common with what is safe, and paragraph 11 adds that a judgment about normal is only as good as its baseline. A town that judges its water by neighbors on the same contaminated river is using a contaminated baseline and calling the result normal.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: throwing out a result when the blank is too high is the correct practice from paragraph 5, not the error.",null,"Not the error: the patient is compared against healthy people, an uncontaminated baseline, and the high reading is flagged rather than called normal.","Not the error: this is the response to new evidence of harm, like the changes in paragraph 10.","A different mistake: skipping the starting composition is the catch in paragraph 3, not a confusion of common with safe."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q15","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following played a part in the removal of lead from gasoline between 1970 and 1996 EXCEPT:","choices":["the Clean Air Act of 1970","catalytic converters on new cars","the Surgeon General's conference","Needleman's study of children's teeth","the Clean Air Act amendments of 1990"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"The Clean Air Act of 1970 gave the new Environmental Protection Agency authority over fuel additives","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"because lead ruins a catalyst, those cars required unleaded fuel","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"children with more lead in their shed baby teeth scored lower on tests of intelligence and attention","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"under amendments passed in 1990 the sale of leaded gasoline for road vehicles ended","choice":4},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"the additive returned to the market in 1926"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 names the 1970 act, the catalytic converters, Needleman's teeth study, and the 1990 amendments as parts of the phase-out. The Surgeon General's conference belongs to paragraph 7 and the 1920s, and it ended with the additive returning to the market.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 10: the 1970 act gave the new agency authority over fuel additives.","Stated in paragraph 10: lead ruins a catalyst, so the new cars needed unleaded fuel.",null,"Stated in paragraph 10: Needleman's 1979 study found harm at levels once called safe.","Stated in paragraph 10: the 1990 amendments ended sales for road vehicles in 1996."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q16","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the sale of leaded gasoline for road vehicles in the United States ended","choices":["at the start of 1996, a few weeks after Patterson's death","in 1985, when the EPA cut the permitted lead content by ninety percent","in 1975, when catalytic converters became standard on new cars","in 1973, when the EPA first ordered a gradual reduction","in 1990, when Congress passed amendments to the Clean Air Act"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"the sale of leaded gasoline for road vehicles ended on the first day of 1996"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"Patterson died in December 1995, a few weeks before the ban took full effect."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the sale ended on the first day of 1996 and that Patterson died in December 1995, a few weeks before the ban took full effect.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong step: 1985 was a steep cut in the allowed amount, not the end of sales.","Wrong step: 1975 brought the converters and unleaded pumps, but leaded fuel was still sold.","Wrong step: 1973 was the start of a gradual reduction, not the end.","Half right: the 1990 amendments ordered the end, but the passage says it took effect in 1996."]},{"id":"rc-o26-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Patterson's age for the Earth was wrong by less than one percent, which proves that his clean-room methods were unnecessary","Leaded gasoline was banned in the United States mainly because it ruined the catalytic converters on new cars","Robert Kehoe's threshold theory was correct for adults but failed to account for the vulnerability of children","The rigorous measurement Patterson developed to date the Earth also exposed how much lead humans had added to the environment","Meteorites are the only reliable source of primordial lead, so the age of the Earth can never be measured from terrestrial rocks"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"clearing it away changed his career and eventually the air of the whole country"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"Patterson's two careers used one tool."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"The same discipline that let him trust a few billionths of a gram of lead from a meteorite let him see the lead in seawater"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 promises that clearing lead from the lab changed both Patterson's career and the country's air, and paragraph 11 closes by saying his two careers used one tool. The passage is about one habit of measurement that answered two questions.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author says every later measurement rested on the clean-room work, and the near-accuracy is presented as its reward.","Too narrow: the converters are one step among several in paragraph 10, and the passage is about far more than the ban.","Outside the passage: the author never endorses Kehoe's threshold for anyone, and paragraph 10 presents evidence against it.",null,"Too extreme and too narrow: this is one step in paragraph 3, and the passage never says never."]}]},"rc-p01":{"id":"rc-p01","title":"How Tardigrades Survive Drying Out","topic":"cryptobiosis in tardigrades","field":"zoology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1381,"paragraphs":[["A square of moss on a north facing roof tile spends much of the summer as a brittle gray crust.","Crumble a pinch into a watch glass, add rainwater, and wait twenty minutes.","Under a dissecting scope the crust becomes a crowd.","Nothing in the dry crust looked alive.","Nothing in it was dead, either."],["Tardigrades are a phylum of their own, mostly under half a millimeter long.","They live in ocean sediment, in soil, and in the thin films of water on moss and lichen.","A tardigrade is an aquatic animal.","It walks, feeds, and reproduces only while surrounded by water, and the water in a moss cushion is gone by noon."],["The answer is cryptobiosis, from the Greek for hidden life.","A cryptobiotic animal is not dormant like a hibernating mammal: hibernation is a slowed metabolism, and cryptobiosis is, as far as any instrument can tell, no metabolism at all.","Biologists sort it by trigger: freezing gives cryobiosis, lack of oxygen gives anoxybiosis, and a sudden rise in outside salt gives osmobiosis.","Drying gives anhydrobiosis, the one the tardigrade performs best."],["The change into the dried form, called a tun, is orderly and takes hours.","The animal pulls its head and legs inward, folds its cuticle into lengthwise creases, and contracts until its surface area drops by roughly half, slowing the escape of the water still inside.","Body water falls from about eighty percent of fresh mass to a few percent or less.","Measured metabolic rate falls below a hundredth of one percent of the active rate, below the detection limit of the instruments.","A tardigrade dried gradually, over several hours in fairly humid air, usually survives.","The same animal dropped into dry air and desiccated within minutes usually does not, because the tun is a program that needs time to run, not a passive collapse."],["Ordinary drying wrecks an ordinary cell.","A phospholipid membrane holds its spacing because water molecules hydrogen bond to the lipid head groups.","Pull the water out and the heads crowd together, the sheet stiffens into a different phase, and when water is added back the membrane cracks along the boundaries between phases and leaks its contents."],["The first molecule offered as an answer was trehalose, two glucose units joined head to head.","Because both anomeric carbons are tied up in that linkage, trehalose is nonreducing: it has no free aldehyde group waiting to react with the amino groups of nearby proteins, so it can be packed around delicate machinery at high concentration without browning it.","Under the water replacement idea, its hydroxyl groups take over the hydrogen bonds departing water leaves behind, propping membrane head groups apart near their normal spacing."],["The second proposal is that trehalose turns the cell interior into a glass, not a windowpane but a physical state: a liquid dried or cooled so far past easy molecular motion that it seizes up without ever arranging itself into a crystal.","It has the disorder of a liquid and the rigidity of a solid.","Diffusion through it is slowed by many orders of magnitude, so two molecules that would ruin each other on contact never get far enough to meet.","A tun does not so much repair damage as refuse to let the reactions that cause it get started."],["Trehalose, though, cannot carry the whole account.","Brine shrimp cysts and anhydrobiotic nematodes load themselves with the sugar, in some cases to a fifth of their dry weight.","Tardigrades accumulate far less, and in several species that dry and revive as reliably as any, trehalose is present only in traces or cannot be detected at all.","A molecule missing from an animal that performs the trick cannot be the whole explanation of the trick."],["What fills the gap appears to be proteins that break the usual rule of one fold and one job.","Intrinsically disordered proteins have no stable fold, and they are promiscuous: rather than matching one partner the way a key matches one lock, they drape themselves over many different partners and take whatever form suits the one they meet.","Three families are abundant in tardigrades, named for where each sits: the cytoplasmic, secretory, and mitochondrial abundant heat soluble proteins.","They stay in solution when boiled, which is itself a signature of disorder."],["As water leaves, the cytoplasmic family condenses into fibers and a gel that stiffens into the glassy state.","The clearest sign they do real work is that the trick travels.","Move the tardigrade genes for them into yeast or bacteria, organisms that ordinarily die when dried, and the engineered cells survive desiccation at rates their unmodified relatives do not."],["Tardigrades carry a chromatin binding protein nicknamed the damage suppressor, which associates with DNA and appears to shield it from the hydroxyl radicals that radiation and oxidative stress generate.","When a tun takes up water it switches on a vigorous repair response, and part of what looks like invulnerability from outside is a first hour of frantic proofreading within."],["Here the popular version drifts away from the biology.","Anhydrobiosis suspends metabolism, not chemistry, and certainly not time.","Inside a tun, slowly and without enzymes, oxygen still attacks lipids, sugars still condense onto proteins, and the DNA backbone still breaks.","The glass slows all of this by orders of magnitude.","It does not stop it.","The consequence is measurable: the longer a population stays dry, the smaller the fraction that walks away on rewetting, and the slower the survivors get moving.","Revival after several years in a dry moss sample is documented.","The much repeated figure of more than a century rests on one report that no one has reproduced, and it is better read as folklore than as data."],["The rest of the folklore has the same shape: a real measurement with its conditions stripped off.","Tuns have been carried into low Earth orbit, exposed to vacuum and solar ultraviolet, and some came back.","Tuns have been held near absolute zero and above the boiling point of water, and some came back.","Both are true, and both describe a tun, briefly, with a survival fraction well short of everything.","The active, hydrated animal is far less hardy.","Left in water a little above normal human body temperature it can die within a day."],["Nothing in a tardigrade's history has ever asked it to survive orbit.","What its history asks, over and over, is that a film of water on a moss leaf vanish on Tuesday and return with Friday's rain.","The machinery that answers that, a cell interior converted into inert glass, is also indifferent to vacuum, cold, and heat, because all three do damage by moving molecules around and there are no molecules free to move.","The tardigrade is not built to be indestructible.","It is built to be dry, and indestructibility is a side effect."],["That side effect is the part with an address in medicine.","Vaccines, antibody drugs, clotting factors, and living cells all depend on refrigeration, and the cold chain is expensive, power hungry, and the first thing to fail in a rural clinic or disaster zone.","A preparation that could sit on a shelf as dry powder and be revived with sterile water would change the logistics of global immunization."],["The tardigrade suggests how to build one, and why the obvious attempt falls short.","Trehalose is already a common stabilizer in freeze dried products, but sugar alone gives a brittle glass that crystallizes over time or pulls moisture from the air and softens, and a softened glass lets molecules move again.","The lesson of the tun is that the sugar was never working alone.","Adding a tardigrade disordered protein to a human clotting factor preparation lets it be dried and rewetted with much of its activity intact.","The unsolved problems are the ones the animal faces too: the drying has to be slow and controlled, and anything thicker than half a millimeter dries from the outside in.","An organ is not a tun, and no one should promise that it will become one soon."],["Back on the roof tile, the moss is gray again by afternoon.","The animals in it are not waiting, in any sense that involves an experience of waiting.","They are glass, and the glass keeps until the rain comes."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p01-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the water content of a tardigrade in the tun state falls to","choices":["a few percent of its fresh mass or less","one fifth of its fresh mass","less than a hundredth of one percent of its fresh mass","roughly half of its fresh mass","about eighty percent of its fresh mass"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"to a few percent or less"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 gives the number outright: body water drops from about eighty percent of fresh mass to a few percent or less. The other figures in that same paragraph belong to different measurements, which is what makes this one worth reading slowly.","distractorNotes":[null,"A fifth of dry weight is the trehalose load of brine shrimp cysts in paragraph 8, a different animal and a different quantity.","Below a hundredth of one percent is the metabolic rate inside a tun, not its water content.","Roughly half is how much the animal's surface area shrinks, not how much water it keeps.","Eighty percent is the starting point, the water content of an active animal, not where it ends up."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that a tardigrade dried within minutes in very dry air usually dies because","choices":["sudden drying tears the cuticle along its lengthwise creases","rapid water loss strips away the trehalose already stored in its tissues","tun formation is a program the animal needs hours to run","quick drying leaves too much water behind for a glass to form","the animal cannot take in enough oxygen while a tun is forming"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"the tun is a program that needs time to run"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 sets the two schedules side by side. Dried gradually over several hours in humid air, the animal usually survives; dropped into dry air and desiccated in minutes, it usually does not, because the tun is a program that needs time to run rather than a passive collapse.","distractorNotes":["Those creases are part of normal tun formation in paragraph 4, not a failure point the passage describes.","The passage never ties drying speed to trehalose loss, and paragraph 8 says several species get by with almost none of the sugar.",null,"Leftover water is a problem for dried medical preparations in paragraph 16, not the reason fast drying kills the animal.","Oxygen supply is not the issue here; lack of oxygen appears in paragraph 3 only as a separate trigger, for anoxybiosis."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says trehalose is nonreducing, and treats that as important because the sugar therefore","choices":["can be stored at a fifth of the animal's total dry weight","has no free aldehyde group to react with protein amino groups","resists being broken down by the ordinary enzymes of the cell","arranges itself into a crystal as the last water is removed","cannot form hydrogen bonds with the head groups of a phospholipid membrane"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"no free aldehyde group waiting to react with the amino groups"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 defines the term as it goes: both anomeric carbons are tied up in the linkage, so there is no free aldehyde waiting to react with the amino groups of nearby proteins. That is what lets the sugar be packed in at high concentration without browning the machinery it surrounds.","distractorNotes":["A fifth of dry weight describes brine shrimp cysts in paragraph 8, and it is a quantity rather than a consequence of being nonreducing.",null,"Enzyme resistance is never mentioned; the passage ties the nonreducing character to reactivity with proteins.","Paragraph 7 says the opposite, that the glassy state forms without ever arranging into a crystal.","This reverses paragraph 6, which says the sugar's hydroxyl groups take over exactly those hydrogen bonds."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q04","type":"detail","stem":"Which of the following does the passage offer as evidence that trehalose alone cannot account for tardigrade survival?","choices":["Trehalose crystallizes inside a tun within a few years of drying","Some tardigrade species revive reliably with little or no detectable trehalose","Trehalose is absent from the dried cysts and seeds of every other organism studied","Tardigrades store more trehalose than brine shrimp cysts do","Trehalose cannot enter the cytoplasm until a tun has already formed"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"trehalose is present only in traces or cannot be detected at all"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"cannot be the whole explanation of the trick"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 makes the argument directly. Brine shrimp cysts and anhydrobiotic nematodes load up on the sugar, tardigrades accumulate far less, and in several species that dry and revive as well as any, trehalose is present only in traces or cannot be found at all.","distractorNotes":["Crystallizing sugar is a problem for dried medical formulations in paragraph 16, not something reported inside a tun.",null,"The passage names two other organisms that are full of the sugar, so this is contradicted on the page.","Paragraph 8 says the reverse: the cysts load themselves with the sugar and tardigrades accumulate far less.","Nothing in the passage describes trehalose being kept out of the cytoplasm until a tun forms."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The protein nicknamed the damage suppressor is described as acting on","choices":["DNA, by binding chromatin and shielding it","the trehalose stored in the cytoplasm","the phospholipids of the cell membrane","the repair enzymes switched on at rewetting","the folded shapes of the heat soluble proteins"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"which associates with DNA and appears to shield it"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 introduces it as a chromatin binding protein that associates with DNA and appears to shield it from the hydroxyl radicals that radiation and oxidative stress generate.","distractorNotes":[null,"This protein binds DNA, and the passage never connects it to the sugar.","Membranes are handled by trehalose in paragraph 6, not by this protein.","Repair does switch on at rewetting in paragraph 11, but that is a separate response rather than what this protein binds.","The heat soluble families in paragraph 9 have no stable folded shape at all, and this protein is not described as acting on them."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, \"glass\" most nearly means","choices":["a rigid solid state that keeps a liquid's disorder","a small vessel used for holding and viewing a specimen","a crystalline solid formed as the last water is removed","a thin protective coating laid down over a membrane","a hard, transparent material of the kind used in windows"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"the disorder of a liquid and the rigidity of a solid"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"without ever arranging itself into a crystal"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 defines the word for the reader before using it: not a windowpane but a physical state, with the disorder of a liquid and the rigidity of a solid, in which diffusion is slowed by many orders of magnitude.","distractorNotes":[null,"A watch glass shows up in paragraph 1, but that is a piece of labware and has nothing to do with the state described here.","Paragraph 7 says the material seizes up without ever arranging itself into a crystal, so crystalline is the opposite of the sense meant.","Coating surfaces is what trehalose does in paragraph 6, while the glass is a state of the whole cell interior.","This is the everyday meaning, and the passage rules it out in the very sentence that introduces the term."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 9, \"promiscuous\" most nearly means","choices":["changing chemically whenever the surrounding water leaves","moving freely between the cytoplasm and the mitochondria","present in unusually large amounts inside the cell","able to bind many different partners rather than one","reproducing without regard to a particular mate"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"they drape themselves over many different partners"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 explains the word in the same sentence it appears: instead of matching one partner the way a key matches one lock, these proteins drape over many different partners and take whatever shape suits the one they meet.","distractorNotes":["The proteins do condense on drying in paragraph 10, but that is a change of state, not what the word means here.","The three family names say where each protein sits, not that any of them travels between compartments.","Abundance is built into the family names in that paragraph, but it is a separate property from the binding behavior this word describes.",null,"This is the everyday sense of the word, and the paragraph is describing molecular binding rather than mating."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which statement about a tardigrade while it is in the tun state?","choices":["It repairs oxidative damage more slowly than an active animal does","It is chemically inert until water is added back to it","It loses the ability to revive at a steady, predictable rate","It accumulates damage that it cannot fix until water returns","It will survive indefinitely as long as it is kept dry"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"oxygen still attacks lipids"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"It does not stop it."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"When a tun takes up water it switches on a vigorous repair response"}],"why":"Two paragraphs combine here. Paragraph 12 says damage keeps building in the dry state even though metabolism is suspended, and paragraph 11 says the repair response switches on when a tun takes up water. So the fixing waits for rehydration.","distractorNotes":["This assumes repair is still happening, only slower, but the passage places metabolism, and with it repair, at zero until water returns.","Paragraph 12 says the opposite: chemistry continues in the dry state even though metabolism does not.","Paragraph 12 says the surviving fraction falls the longer a sample stays dry, but it never claims the decline is steady or predictable.",null,"Paragraph 12 rules this out, since survival drops with time dried and the century long claim is treated as folklore."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on the passage, a hydrated, active tardigrade exposed to high heat would most likely be","choices":["less well protected than a tun, because its molecules are free to move","protected about as well as a tun, since the damage suppressor is always present","killed outright, since active animals carry no protective proteins","protected only if it had stored trehalose beforehand","unaffected, because heat damage requires a dry cell interior"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"there are no molecules free to move"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"The active, hydrated animal is far less hardy."}],"why":"Paragraph 14 gives the reason the tun shrugs off vacuum, cold, and heat: all three do their damage by moving molecules, and in a glass nothing is free to move. A hydrated animal has no glass, and paragraph 13 says it is far less hardy.","distractorNotes":[null,"The damage suppressor of paragraph 11 guards DNA; paragraph 14 credits the tun's indifference to heat to the glassy state, which a hydrated animal does not have.","This overshoots. Paragraph 11 describes DNA shielding in tardigrade cells without restricting it to tuns.","Trehalose belongs to the drying response, and paragraph 8 notes that some species manage with almost none of it.","Reversed: paragraph 14 says a dry glass is what holds off heat damage, and paragraph 13 has the active animal dying in merely warm water."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Tardigrades are the most durable animals known, and no laboratory has yet matched their tolerances","Surviving drying is a chemical strategy that slows damage rather than a form of invulnerability","Experiments in low Earth orbit have produced most of what is known about drying","The tardigrade genome should become the standard model for studying radiation damage","A single sugar, trehalose, accounts for the ability of small animals to survive without water"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"The tardigrade is not built to be indestructible."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"indestructibility is a side effect"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"The glass slows all of this by orders of magnitude."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"refuse to let the reactions that cause it get started"}],"why":"The whole passage builds toward this. The tun works by turning the cell interior into a glass that keeps damaging reactions from getting started, which is a specific and limited trick, and paragraphs 15 and 16 argue that the trick, not toughness, is what dried medicines might borrow.","distractorNotes":["Paragraphs 12 and 13 argue against this framing, calling the famous tolerances real measurements with their conditions stripped off.",null,"Orbital exposure appears once, in paragraph 13, as an example of overstated folklore rather than as a main source of knowledge.","The passage never recommends a model organism, and radiation is a side topic inside the drying story.","Paragraph 8 takes this apart, since several species dry and revive with almost no detectable trehalose."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 8 serves mainly to","choices":["establish trehalose as the central molecule of anhydrobiosis","argue that brine shrimp survive drying better than tardigrades do","compare the drying speed of tardigrades with that of nematodes","introduce the three families of heat soluble proteins by name","show that the leading single molecule explanation is incomplete"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"cannot carry the whole account"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"cannot be the whole explanation of the trick"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 exists to open a gap. It grants that trehalose does real work in other animals, then points out that tardigrades carry far less and some carry none, which is exactly what makes the disordered proteins of paragraph 9 necessary.","distractorNotes":["That is paragraph 6's job, and paragraph 8 pulls in the opposite direction.","The comparison is about how much sugar each organism stores, not about which one dries better.","Drying speed is a paragraph 4 topic and is not the comparison being drawn here.","The three families are named in paragraph 9, after this paragraph has cleared the ground for them.",null]},{"id":"rc-p01-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A medical problem, then a survey of the organisms that might solve it, then a proposed trial","A definition, then a debate between two research camps, then a verdict in favor of one camp","An observation, then its mechanism, then a correction of a popular overstatement, then a use for it","A popular claim, then the experiments that were said to confirm it, then a call for more study","A history of the field, then the single discovery that changed it, then the questions that remain open"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"Under a dissecting scope the crust becomes a crowd."},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":1,"quote":"The answer is cryptobiosis"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"Here the popular version drifts away from the biology."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"That side effect is the part with an address in medicine."}],"why":"The passage opens with moss on a roof tile, works through the tun, trehalose, the glassy state, and the disordered proteins, spends paragraphs 12 through 14 walking the indestructibility story back, and closes on dried medicines.","distractorNotes":["The cold chain problem arrives late, in paragraph 15, rather than at the start, and no trial is proposed.","Trehalose and the disordered proteins are presented as partial and complementary answers, not as rival camps with a winner named.",null,"The passage does the reverse of confirming the popular claim, and it ends with an application rather than a call for study.","There is no history of the field, and no single discovery is presented as the turning point."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the claim that tardigrades have revived after more than a century is best described as","choices":["cautiously hopeful","angrily dismissive","openly admiring","plainly skeptical","entirely neutral"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":8,"quote":"better read as folklore than as data"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":8,"quote":"no one has reproduced"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 grants that revival after several years is documented, then says the century long figure rests on one report nobody has reproduced and is better read as folklore than as data. That is doubt, stated calmly.","distractorNotes":["Hope would suggest the author expects the claim to hold up, but the passage files it under folklore.","The tone stays measured, and the author faults the evidence rather than the people repeating the claim.","Admiration is not the note here; the author sets the claim aside rather than celebrating it.",null,"Neutrality would mean reporting the claim without judging it, and the author judges it in the same sentence."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In discussing what the tardigrade's chemistry might do for medicine, the author's stance is best described as","choices":["doubtful that dried preparations will ever be more than a curiosity","indifferent to whether the approach ever reaches patients","interested in the approach and candid about its limits","convinced that room temperature storage of tissue is close at hand","impatient with researchers who have failed to solve the problem"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"would change the logistics of global immunization"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":6,"quote":"no one should promise that it will become one soon"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 treats the sugar plus protein route as worth building on, reports a clotting factor dried and rewetted with much of its activity intact, then lists what is unsolved and ends by saying an organ is not a tun and nobody should promise that it will be one soon.","distractorNotes":["Doubt that deep does not fit a paragraph reporting a clotting factor dried and rewetted with much of its activity intact.","Paragraph 15 spends its length on rural clinics and disaster zones, which shows the author cares about reaching patients.",null,"The closing sentence of paragraph 16 refuses exactly this optimism about anything thicker than half a millimeter.","No blame is placed on researchers; the obstacles are described as the same ones the animal itself faces."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Some tuns have survived exposure to the vacuum of low Earth orbit","An active tardigrade can be killed by water a little warmer than the human body","Certain tardigrade species contain little or no detectable trehalose","Yeast or bacteria given tardigrade genes can survive drying better than usual","A tun protects itself by running its metabolism at a low but steady rate"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"exposed to vacuum and solar ultraviolet, and some came back","choice":0},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"it can die within a day","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"trehalose is present only in traces or cannot be detected at all","choice":2},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"the engineered cells survive desiccation at rates their unmodified relatives do not","choice":3},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"no metabolism at all","choice":4},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"below the detection limit of the instruments","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraphs 3 and 12 are explicit that cryptobiosis is no measurable metabolism at all rather than a slowed one, and paragraph 7 credits the protection to a glass that keeps reactions from starting. Each of the other four statements sits on the page.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 13 reports tuns carried into low Earth orbit and exposed to vacuum, with some coming back.","Paragraph 13 says a hydrated animal left in water a little above normal body temperature can die within a day.","Paragraph 8 says trehalose is present only in traces, or not detectable at all, in several reliable species.","Paragraph 10 says engineered yeast and bacteria survive desiccation at rates their unmodified relatives do not.",null]},{"id":"rc-p01-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for the physical reason a dried membrane leaks when water is added back to it should turn to","choices":["paragraph 3","paragraph 14","paragraph 7","paragraph 11","paragraph 5"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"the membrane cracks along the boundaries between phases and leaks its contents"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 walks through the whole sequence: water normally hydrogen bonds to the lipid head groups, removing it lets the heads crowd together and the sheet shift phase, and on rewetting the membrane cracks at the phase boundaries and leaks.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 defines cryptobiosis and its varieties without touching membranes.","Paragraph 14 argues that the extreme tolerances are a side effect of drying, with no membrane detail.","Paragraph 7 describes the glassy state, which concerns diffusion through the cell interior rather than membrane structure.","Paragraph 11 is about DNA shielding and the repair response at rewetting.",null]},{"id":"rc-p01-q17","type":"application","stem":"A laboratory wants to store a fragile protein drug as a dry powder that stays active for months on a shelf. Based on the passage, which preparation is most likely to succeed?","choices":["trehalose alone, dried in minutes as a thin film","trehalose alone, dried slowly into a thick block","trehalose and a disordered protein, dried slowly as a thin film","the damage suppressor protein alone, dried in minutes as a thick block","a disordered protein alone, dried slowly into a thick block"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"sugar alone gives a brittle glass that crystallizes over time or pulls moisture from the air and softens"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"Adding a tardigrade disordered protein to a human clotting factor preparation lets it be dried and rewetted with much of its activity intact"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"the drying has to be slow and controlled, and anything thicker than half a millimeter dries from the outside in"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 hands you the recipe and its limits. Sugar alone gives a glass that crystallizes or softens with time, a disordered protein added alongside the sugar keeps a dried protein active, and the drying has to be slow and no thicker than half a millimeter. Only one preparation respects all three.","distractorNotes":["Sugar alone is the obvious attempt paragraph 16 says falls short, and drying in minutes breaks the slow and controlled rule in the same paragraph.","Slow drying is right, but sugar alone still crystallizes or softens with time, and anything thicker than half a millimeter dries from the outside in.",null,"The damage suppressor shields DNA in paragraph 11 and is never offered as a glass former, and a thick block dried in minutes breaks both drying rules in paragraph 16.","The protein is the right ingredient, but paragraph 16 says anything thicker than half a millimeter dries from the outside in, so the block is the problem."]}]},"rc-p02":{"id":"rc-p02","title":"How Concrete Sets and How It Fails","topic":"the chemistry of Portland cement and concrete","field":"materials chemistry","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1384,"paragraphs":[["By weight, people make more concrete than any other manufactured thing, and most never ask how it works.","Concrete does not dry into a solid.","It grows one."],["The story starts in a quarry and a kiln.","The binder in ordinary concrete is Portland cement, made by burning roughly four parts limestone to one part clay or shale.","Limestone is calcium carbonate, and the clay supplies silica, alumina, and iron.","That mixture, called raw meal, goes into the kiln, and near nine hundred degrees Celsius the limestone gives up its carbon dioxide and becomes calcium oxide, or quicklime.","This step is called calcination, and it is the source of both the usefulness and the trouble of cement."],["Farther down the kiln, at about fourteen hundred and fifty degrees, the quicklime and the clay's silica and alumina fuse into new compounds and emerge as dark gravel sized nodules called clinker.","Four phases matter.","Tricalcium silicate, called alite, makes up half to two thirds of the clinker and does most of the early work.","Dicalcium silicate, called belite, is about a fifth and works slowly.","Tricalcium aluminate and a calcium aluminoferrite fill out the rest, the first alarmingly quick to react and the second responsible for the gray color."],["Clinker is cooled and ground finer than flour, with three to five percent gypsum by mass ground in.","The gypsum is not filler.","Without it the tricalcium aluminate would react within minutes and seize the batch into a useless stiff mass, called flash set.","Sulfate from the gypsum reaches the aluminate first and builds needles of ettringite around each grain, slowing the aluminate and buying the hours a crew needs to place the batch."],["Add water and the real reaction begins.","The calcium silicates react with the water and precipitate a new solid, a calcium silicate hydrate that chemists write as C-S-H and everyone else calls the glue.","It grows outward from every cement grain as a tangle of sheets and fibers that locks sand and stone together.","The same reaction releases calcium hydroxide, a mineral called portlandite, which is not much of a glue but matters enormously later."],["The two silicates set the pace.","Alite hydrates quickly and supplies most of the strength gained in the first week.","Belite hydrates slowly and keeps adding strength for months and years, so a structure quietly gets stronger long after the forms come off.","A cement heavier in belite runs cool and gains late, an advantage in a massive dam, where the heat of reaction would crack the mass as it cooled."],["Here the common intuition fails.","Concrete does not harden by drying out.","Water is a reactant, built into the C-S-H, and hydration stops when the water runs out, whether or not the surface looks dry.","Curing, on a site, means keeping the water in: misting, wet burlap, ponding.","Concrete that is still green, soft enough to take a thumbprint and not yet carrying load, is most easily ruined by a hot dry afternoon.","Crews routinely add more water than hydration needs, because a wetter mix is easier to place, and every extra measure leaves a capillary pore when it evaporates.","Those pores are where strength goes, and they are the roads by which everything that later attacks the concrete gets in."],["The kiln, meanwhile, has a problem no engineer can design away.","Roughly half a tonne of carbon dioxide leaves the limestone for every tonne of clinker, purely as stoichiometry, before any fuel is counted.","Firing the kiln and grinding the product push the total near eight tenths of a tonne.","More than half of cement's emissions are therefore chemical rather than combustion: the carbon was in the rock, and making cement means taking it out.","Cement production is commonly put at seven or eight percent of human carbon dioxide emissions, well above aviation."],["Concrete is strong in compression and weak in tension, so it is nearly always cast around steel bars.","The pairing works partly because both expand and contract with temperature at almost the same rate, and partly because of the portlandite.","The pore water in hardened concrete is saturated with calcium hydroxide and alkali, giving it a pH between about twelve and a half and thirteen and a half.","In liquid that alkaline, steel grows a thin, tight oxide film and stops corroding, a state engineers call passive.","The concrete is not merely armor.","It is a chemical environment that keeps the bar from rusting."],["Air undoes this slowly.","Carbon dioxide dissolves in the pore water and reacts with the calcium hydroxide to make calcium carbonate.","Carbonated concrete is slightly harder and slightly denser.","What it has lost is alkalinity: the pH behind the carbonation front falls below about nine.","As long as unreacted portlandite remains the pH holds, so the portlandite acts as an alkaline reserve, a stock of protection that the air spends.","The front moves inward roughly in proportion to the square root of time, so a depth reached in ten years takes forty to double.","That arithmetic is the logic behind the specified cover, the twenty to fifty millimeters between the outer face and the nearest bar."],["When the front reaches the steel, the film goes and the bar begins to rust.","Rust is the slow failure mode of the built environment, and it is mechanical as much as chemical: iron oxides occupy several times the volume of the metal they replace.","The expanding rust cracks the cover along the bar and pushes slabs of it off.","Chloride from sea spray or road salt does the same work faster and more locally, breaking the passive film in pits without waiting for the pH to fall.","A bridge deck rarely fails because the concrete was crushed.","It fails because the steel inside it grew."],["Because the carbon is in the limestone, the most direct way to cut emissions is to use less clinker.","Industry has long done this by blending in supplementary materials, cementitious or pozzolanic.","Fly ash from coal boilers and slag from blast furnaces both react with water and lime to make more C-S-H.","A newer blend grinds together clinker, limestone, and clay calcined at roughly eight hundred degrees, too cool to decompose carbonate, and can replace about half the clinker.","Roman builders made durable marine mortar from lime and volcanic ash on much the same principle, with no steel in it, and some of their harbor works still stand."],["The catch is chemical, and it deserves more attention than it usually gets.","A pozzolan makes its extra glue by consuming calcium hydroxide.","The reaction that adds strength is the one that spends the alkaline reserve, so a binder with less portlandite has less protection to give the steel when the carbonation front arrives.","Against that, the same blends usually produce a finer, less connected pore structure, which slows carbon dioxide on its way in.","Which effect wins is not a matter of principle but of the mix, the cover, the curing, and the exposure, and for a given structure it has to be worked out rather than assumed."],["Supply is the other limit.","Fly ash and slag exist because coal is burned and iron is smelted, so the two established substitutes are byproducts of other high-carbon industries and grow scarcer as those clean up or close down.","Suitable clay is common almost everywhere, but a plant has to be built to calcine it."],["Other routes are under development, none yet ordinary.","Capturing carbon dioxide at the kiln stack would address process and fuel emissions together, at a cost in energy and equipment.","Binders based on magnesium or alkali-activated slag avoid the limestone problem and raise their own unsettled questions."],["Underneath all of it sits an accounting question.","Emissions are counted per tonne of cement, but service is counted in years.","A binder that cuts emissions by a third and halves the structure's life has gained nothing, because the replacement pour will emit again, and a plain mix with generous cover and patient curing may beat a clever one that is rushed.","The chemistry of concrete is not a problem waiting for one substitute.","It is a set of trades among the heat that makes the binder, the water that grows it, and the air that slowly spends it."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p02-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, calcination is the step in which","choices":["limestone sheds its carbon dioxide and becomes quicklime","the clay contributes the silica, alumina, and iron the kiln will need","gypsum is interground so the aluminate will not stiffen the batch too quickly","the ground powder meets water and begins precipitating the binding solid","quicklime and silica fuse and crystallize into gravel-sized nodules"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"the limestone gives up its carbon dioxide and becomes calcium oxide, or quicklime"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"This step is called calcination"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 names this step outright. Near nine hundred degrees the limestone loses its carbon dioxide and becomes calcium oxide, or quicklime, and the passage calls that calcination.","distractorNotes":[null,"The clay does supply those oxides, but that is the makeup of the raw meal, not the reaction the passage names.","Interground gypsum belongs to paragraph 4, after the clinker has already been made and cooled.","That is hydration, which happens once water is added on the job, not inside the kiln.","That is the hotter clinkering step farther down the kiln, which paragraph 3 treats separately."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that gypsum is ground in with the clinker in order to","choices":["give the finished powder its characteristic gray color","hold water in the mix so that hydration can continue for longer","raise the proportion of alite so the cement gains strength sooner","keep the tricalcium aluminate from stiffening the batch almost at once","replace part of the clinker and lower the emissions of the finished cement"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"the tricalcium aluminate would react within minutes and seize the batch"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the gypsum is not filler. Without it the tricalcium aluminate would react within minutes and seize the batch, a failure the passage calls flash set.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 credits the gray color to the calcium aluminoferrite phase, not to the gypsum.","Keeping water available is curing, which paragraph 7 puts in the hands of the crew after the concrete is placed.","Alite content is set in the kiln, and paragraph 6 treats early strength as a matter of clinker composition rather than anything gypsum does.",null,"That describes the supplementary materials of paragraph 12, while the three to five percent of gypsum is there to control setting."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, most of the strength a concrete gains in its first week comes from","choices":["alite, the most abundant phase in clinker","the calcium aluminoferrite that colors the powder","belite, the slower of the two calcium silicates","portlandite crystallizing out of the pore water","ettringite needles built around the cement grains"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"supplies most of the strength gained in the first week"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"makes up half to two thirds of the clinker"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the two silicates set the pace and gives the first week to alite. Paragraph 3 has already put alite at half to two thirds of the clinker.","distractorNotes":[null,"The ferrite phase is mentioned only for the gray color it gives the powder.","Belite is the other silicate, but the passage gives it months and years rather than the first week.","Portlandite is released by the reaction, and paragraph 5 says plainly that it is not much of a glue.","Ettringite is what slows the aluminate down so the batch stays workable, and the passage never calls it a source of strength."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage puts the pH of the pore water in sound hardened concrete closest to","choices":["4.5","7.0","9.0","13.0","14.0"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"between about twelve and a half and thirteen and a half"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 gives a range of about twelve and a half to thirteen and a half for the pore water, and thirteen is the only value here inside it.","distractorNotes":["That is acidic, the opposite of the strongly alkaline liquid paragraph 9 describes.","Neutral water would not grow the protective film, and the passage stresses how alkaline the pore water is.","Below about nine is the value paragraph 10 gives for concrete that has already carbonated.",null,"The passage tops the range out near thirteen and a half, not at the extreme end of the scale."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q05","type":"detail","stem":"For every tonne of clinker produced, the passage says the limestone itself gives off about","choices":["eight tenths of a tonne of carbon dioxide","half a tonne of carbon dioxide","a full tonne of carbon dioxide","a fifth of a tonne of carbon dioxide","seven or eight percent of a tonne of carbon dioxide"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"Roughly half a tonne of carbon dioxide leaves the limestone"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 separates two sources. Roughly half a tonne comes out of the rock as stoichiometry alone, before any fuel is counted.","distractorNotes":["That is the total once the fuel for firing and the energy for grinding are added in, not the share from the rock.",null,"The passage keeps the share from the limestone well under a tonne, at roughly half.","The passage never gives this figure, and it would make the share coming from the rock look far smaller than it is.","Seven or eight percent is cement's share of human carbon dioxide emissions, a different figure entirely."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, \"green\" most nearly means","choices":["inexperienced or newly trained","rich in plant material or organic matter","freshly placed and not yet hardened","produced with unusually low emissions","tinted by the iron in the clinker"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"soft enough to take a thumbprint and not yet carrying load"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 defines the word in the same sentence: soft enough to take a thumbprint and not yet carrying load. It describes how young the pour is.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday sense of the word, and it does not fit a sentence about a slab that still takes a thumbprint.","Nothing organic is involved, since the passage is describing cement, sand, stone, and water.",null,"Emissions do come up, but not until paragraph 8, and here the word describes a slab that has just been placed.","Paragraph 3 credits the gray color to the ferrite phase, and nothing in the passage is green in color."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 10, \"reserve\" most nearly means","choices":["a rule written into a specification","a spare batch held back for repairs","an area of land protected from building","a cautious, understated manner","a stored supply that gets used up"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"a stock of protection that the air spends"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 supplies the sense itself: the portlandite is a stock of protection that the air spends. It is an amount on hand that carbonation draws down.","distractorNotes":["Cover depth is specified later in the paragraph, but the reserve is a quantity of portlandite rather than a written requirement.","Nothing is being held back for later, since the portlandite is already in place and is consumed where it sits.","The land sense is a real dictionary meaning, but the passage is describing a chemical stock inside the concrete.","That is the personality sense of the word, which has no place in a sentence about how long the pH holds.",null]},{"id":"rc-p02-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which statement about a fresh slab left uncovered on a hot, dry day?","choices":["It will carbonate before its steel has a chance to become passive","It will never set at all, since the cement cannot react without cover","It may stop gaining strength while some of its cement is still unreacted","It needs a larger share of gypsum to make up for the water it has lost to the air","It will set faster and therefore end up stronger than one kept damp"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"hydration stops when the water runs out, whether or not the surface looks dry"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"most easily ruined by a hot dry afternoon"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says hydration stops when the water runs out, whether or not the surface looks dry. A slab that loses its water early leaves cement unreacted and stops gaining.","distractorNotes":["Carbonation belongs to paragraph 10 and works over years, not over a single afternoon.","Hydration starts as soon as water is added, so the slab does set. The passage says the reaction stops early, not that it never begins.",null,"Gypsum controls how fast the aluminate reacts, and the passage never ties it to how much water a slab keeps.","This is the intuition the paragraph is written to correct, since drying is not hardening and losing water early costs strength."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage best supports which inference about the Roman marine mortar it mentions?","choices":["It was fired in kilns that reached the same temperature as a modern clinker kiln","It was immune to carbonation because volcanic ash blocks carbon dioxide entirely","Its long life shows that lime and ash binders are stronger than any modern cement","A loss of alkalinity would not have threatened it as it threatens modern members","Roman builders had worked out why steel stays passive in alkaline surroundings"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"some of their harbor works still stand"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"has less protection to give the steel"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"It is a chemical environment that keeps the bar from rusting."}],"why":"Paragraph 12 describes a binder of lime and volcanic ash, and paragraph 9 explains that alkalinity matters because of the steel cast inside modern concrete. A mortar with no steel in it has nothing for a falling pH to endanger.","distractorNotes":["Only the Portland kiln is given a temperature, about fourteen hundred and fifty degrees, and Roman firing is never described.","Nothing in the passage says volcanic ash blocks carbon dioxide, and paragraph 13 says only that finer pores slow it down.","The passage calls the mortar durable, which is a claim about lasting rather than about out-performing modern cement in strength.",null,"The passive film is presented as modern engineering language, and the passage credits the Romans with a working principle instead."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Concrete is overused, and the volume poured each year ought to be cut sharply","Corrosion of reinforcing steel is the leading cause of building collapse worldwide","Ancient builders understood durability far better than modern engineers manage to","Blended binders have made ordinary Portland cement obsolete for new construction","One chemistry sets cement's usefulness, its carbon cost, and its slow decay"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"the source of both the usefulness and the trouble of cement"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"not a problem waiting for one substitute"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"a set of trades among the heat that makes the binder"}],"why":"The passage follows one chain. The calcination that makes the binder is the step that releases the carbon dioxide, and the portlandite that protects the steel is the compound the air slowly spends. Every proposal at the end is weighed against that chain.","distractorNotes":["The passage never argues for building less, and the closing paragraph is about how binders should be compared.","Rust is called the slow failure mode of the built environment, which is not the same as a worldwide count of collapses.","The Roman mortar appears once to illustrate a principle, not as a verdict on modern engineering.","Paragraph 13 presents the blends as a trade with an unsettled outcome, not as a replacement that has already won.",null]},{"id":"rc-p02-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 7 is included mainly to","choices":["correct a common misunderstanding and show what it costs","compare the two calcium silicates that set the pace","introduce the carbonation reaction that arrives later on","explain why cement kilns have to be fired so hot","show that site crews are careless with the mixes they place"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"Here the common intuition fails."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"Concrete does not harden by drying out."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":7,"quote":"Those pores are where strength goes"}],"why":"The paragraph opens by saying the common intuition fails, replaces drying with hydration, and then follows the error to the extra water and the capillary pores it leaves behind.","distractorNotes":[null,"That comparison is the work of paragraph 6, which comes just before this one.","Carbonation is introduced in paragraph 10. What paragraph 7 introduces is the pore network that later lets it in.","Kiln temperature is handled in paragraphs 2 and 3, while paragraph 7 is about what happens after the cement leaves the plant.","The passage explains that a wetter mix is easier to place, describing the tradeoff rather than faulting anyone for it."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"The passage is organized mainly by","choices":["opening with a failure and working backward to its causes","moving through the four clinker phases one at a time","setting ancient construction against modern construction throughout","following the material forward in time, then testing proposals against it","alternating a chemical claim with a rebuttal of that claim in every paragraph"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"The story starts in a quarry and a kiln."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"Add water and the real reaction begins."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"Air undoes this slowly."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"Other routes are under development, none yet ordinary."}],"why":"The passage runs quarry, kiln, clinker, hydration, curing, then emissions and decades of slow carbonation. Only after all that does it take up substitutes, supply, and the accounting.","distractorNotes":["The failure arrives late, in paragraphs 10 and 11, after the chemistry that explains it has been laid out.","The four phases are named once, in paragraph 3, and two of them are barely mentioned again.","Roman mortar occupies a single sentence rather than framing the whole passage.",null,"There is pushback in paragraphs 7 and 13, but most paragraphs simply carry the account forward."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the carbon dioxide that comes out of the limestone is best described as","choices":["matter of fact about an unavoidable piece of chemistry","indignant at an industry that has refused to act","confident that cleaner fuel will shortly remove the whole problem","dismissive of a figure that is often exaggerated","puzzled by a source of emissions nobody has explained"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"a problem no engineer can design away"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"purely as stoichiometry"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 calls it a problem no engineer can design away and explains it as stoichiometry. The author gives the size of it plainly, without blaming anyone or promising a fix.","distractorNotes":[null,"The paragraph assigns no blame and says instead that the carbon was in the rock to begin with.","Fuel is only part of the total, and the passage says more than half of the emissions are chemical rather than combustion.","The author treats the figure as serious, comparing cement's share with that of aviation.","The origin is explained exactly, in one sentence about where the carbon was."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 13, the author's attitude toward blended binders is best described as","choices":["openly enthusiastic about a settled improvement","interested but unwilling to generalize from principle","resigned to the idea that nothing much can be improved here","hostile, on the grounds that they endanger structures","indifferent to which binder a given project chooses"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"not a matter of principle but of the mix"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"has to be worked out rather than assumed"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 gives the blends a real benefit, a finer pore structure, and a real cost, a smaller alkaline reserve, then says which effect wins has to be worked out for the particular mix and exposure.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph opens with a catch and never treats the question as settled.",null,"The author is weighing an option rather than giving one up, and the closing paragraph still looks for better practice.","The danger is conditional here, and the same paragraph credits the blends with slowing carbon dioxide down.","Indifference would not fit a paragraph that says the catch deserves more attention than it usually gets."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Chloride can break the protective film without any fall in pH","Carbonated concrete is denser than the concrete it formed from","Concrete reaches its final strength once its surface is dry","Belite goes on adding strength for months after a structure is finished","Cover depth is specified because the carbonation front advances slowly"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"keeps adding strength for months and years","choice":3},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"without waiting for the pH to fall","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"slightly harder and slightly denser","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":7,"quote":"That arithmetic is the logic behind the specified cover","choice":4},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"whether or not the surface looks dry","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"a structure quietly gets stronger long after the forms come off","choice":2}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the opposite. Water is a reactant, and hydration stops when the water runs out, whether or not the surface looks dry, so a dry surface is not a finished reaction.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 11 says chloride breaks the film in pits without waiting for the pH to fall, so this one is supported.","Paragraph 10 calls carbonated concrete slightly harder and slightly denser, so this one is supported.",null,"Paragraph 6 gives belite months and years of continued gain, so this one is supported.","Paragraph 10 ties the specified cover to a front that advances with the square root of time, so this one is supported."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for the reason a very large dam might be built with a slow-gaining cement would find it in which paragraph?","choices":["Paragraph 4","Paragraph 5","Paragraph 6","Paragraph 10","Paragraph 13"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"an advantage in a massive dam"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 contrasts the two silicates and closes on the dam: a cement heavier in belite runs cool, and the heat of reaction would otherwise crack a mass that size as it cooled.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 is about gypsum and flash set, a matter of minutes rather than of heat in a massive pour.","Paragraph 5 describes hydration and names its products, but the dam example is not there.",null,"Paragraph 10 covers carbonation and cover depth, long after any heat of reaction has gone.","Paragraph 13 weighs blended binders and says nothing about heat in a large pour."]}]},"rc-p03":{"id":"rc-p03","title":"How the Deep Ocean Makes Its Own Light","topic":"bioluminescence in the deep ocean","field":"marine biology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1375,"paragraphs":[["The deep ocean is usually described in the language of absence.","It is cold, black, and crushing, and no plant or alga can photosynthesize there.","Most of that holds up: below about 1,000 meters, sunlight is gone.","But the blackness deserves a second look.","The deep sea is not lightless.","It is a place where nearly all the light is made by the animals living in it."],["Sunlit water has a light source no animal controls; in the deep, light is a possession.","An animal that makes a photon chooses where, when, and what color, and every animal in range has to interpret it.","Four questions follow: how the light is made, why it keeps being invented, what it is for, and why the answers took so long to gather."],["A molecule called luciferin is the fuel, and an enzyme called luciferase is the machine.","The luciferase binds the luciferin and adds oxygen, producing a strained intermediate that comes apart on its own.","The product lands in an excited state, one electron briefly held higher than it wants to be.","When that electron falls back down, the energy leaves as a photon.","The payout is taken as light instead of heat."],["Because so little energy escapes as heat, bioluminescence is often called cold light, and the phrase is literal.","Some organisms run it as a steady enzymatic glow; others use a photoprotein, loaded in advance with luciferin and oxygen and held, cocked, until a trigger arrives.","The jellyfish protein aequorin works this way: when calcium floods in, the stored reaction fires at once and the animal flashes.","That same jellyfish supplies a green fluorescent protein, which absorbs the blue light made beside it and re-emits it as green."],["Bioluminescence is not a trait handed down from one ancient luminous ancestor.","It has been invented independently at least 40 times, in bacteria, dinoflagellates, jellyfish, worms, crustaceans, squid, and fish.","The luciferases are not related to one another.","They have been assembled again and again out of whatever enzymes happened to be present.","The luciferins, by contrast, are few.","One of them, coelenterazine, turns up in many unrelated phyla, and in a great many of them it is not made but eaten.","Animals route it from food to light organs, so the hard part of evolving light is often not the fuel but the machine that burns it."],["Why should the same solution keep arising?","Part of the answer is that the starting materials are ordinary.","Cells are already full of molecules that oxidize readily, and an oxidation leaving its product in an excited state is not exotic chemistry.","Any protein rigid enough to hold that excited state can be co-opted into a crude luciferase, which selection can sharpen.","Even so, making light is not free.","A flash spends fuel, oxygen, and protein, and for a small animal where meals are sparse, that is expensive.","It keeps evolving because in the dark it buys something worth the price."],["What it buys depends on the physics of seawater.","Clear water transmits blue and blue-green light far better than red or violet, so a signal near 470 to 490 nanometers travels the farthest, and most marine bioluminescence falls in that window.","Deep-sea eyes have followed, with pigments tuned to that band and little sensitivity outside it.","A few fish have exploited the gap: the stoplight loosejaw makes and sees red light, a range its prey cannot detect, using a pigment derived from ingested chlorophyll."],["The most widespread use of the light is not a signal at all but camouflage.","In the upper few hundred meters a faint blue haze of sunlight still filters down, and anything swimming in it is a silhouette from below.","Counterillumination erases the silhouette.","Rows of light organs along the belly emit light downward at the intensity of the water overhead, so a predator looking up sees only uniform dim blue.","The organs are small optical instruments, with reflectors behind the light source, lenses in front of it, and filters that adjust the color."],["The lie has to be a good one.","Too little light and the silhouette survives.","Too much and the animal becomes a lamp.","Several counterilluminating animals adjust their output as the surrounding light changes, dimming as a cloud crosses the surface or as they sink deeper.","The disguise also works in one direction only, because it is aimed down."],["Other uses of light are meant to be seen.","The burglar alarm hypothesis describes a defense that makes the attacker conspicuous.","A dinoflagellate grazed by a copepod flashes, and the flash does the copepod no harm.","It advertises the copepod to a fish, which eats it.","Larger prey may use the same logic: an animal seized in the dark throws a repeating display a bigger predator can home in on, so the captor's safest move is to let go."],["Some shrimp and squid discharge a luminous cloud instead of ink, leaving a glowing distraction behind.","Certain worms and brittle stars shed glowing body parts that flash after they detach."],["Light is also used for hunting and for conversation.","The deep-sea anglerfish dangles an illuminated lure in front of its mouth, turning darkness into a trap.","Small crustaceans release timed trains of glowing droplets in species-specific patterns, so a signal readable by the right mate is meaningless to the wrong one."],["Many animals do not make their own light at all.","They farm bacteria that do.","The Hawaiian bobtail squid hatches without a single symbiont and recruits its bacteria from the seawater around it within hours.","Cilia on the developing light organ sweep seawater toward pores, mucus traps passing cells, and chemical filters winnow the catch to one species.","Each bacterial cell releases a signaling molecule, and only when enough cells are packed together for it to build up do they switch on the luciferase genes.","This density check is called quorum sensing, and it keeps a drifting bacterium from spending energy to glow alone."],["The partnership is maintained rather than merely established.","Each dawn the squid expels about 95 percent of the bacteria in its light organ into the water, and the survivors regrow through the day to a full population by night.","Other partnerships have gone further still.","The bacteria in the lures of some deep-sea anglerfish have lost large stretches of their genomes, including genes they would need in order to live on their own, the mark of long confinement."],["That leaves the fourth question: how much of this is there?","The field spent decades underestimating it for reasons that had nothing to do with the animals.","Early sampling was done with nets, and nets are brutal to the animals most likely to be luminous.","Jellyfish and comb jellies arrive at the surface as shreds, and a shredded animal cannot be scored as luminous.","Animals that survive often exhaust their light after handling and arrive dark.","Submersibles brought a problem of their own, since floodlights bright enough to film by wash out any glow.","The resulting census tilted toward tough-bodied crustaceans and fish.","Once video surveys began counting animals in place under dim red light, the numbers moved sharply: roughly three quarters of the animals observed could make light."],["Caution is still warranted.","A display recorded once in the dark tells an observer what an animal did, not what it was for, and a plausible story about function is easy to construct and hard to test.","Laboratory work supports the burglar alarm in certain pairings, yet many deep-sea displays have never been watched by anyone who knew which predator was nearby.","The claim worth defending is the narrow one, that light is common and is made for reasons, rather than a confident purpose assigned to every flash."],["What survives the hedging is still a reversal of the ordinary picture.","The largest habitat on the planet is not an empty dark space with occasional sparks.","It is a place where most of the animals carry their own light, where the same few fuels have been harnessed by unrelated machinery over and over, and where a photon is the currency of hiding, hunting, warning, and courtship.","The deep sea is dark only in the sense that its light has to be earned."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p03-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the photon in a bioluminescent reaction leaves the molecule at the moment when","choices":["oxygen is first bound and added by the luciferase enzyme","calcium floods in and reaches the luciferin stored in a photoprotein","a green fluorescent protein absorbs blue light and re-emits it as green light","an electron in the reaction product falls back to its lower energy state","heat generated by the reaction escapes into the surrounding water"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"When that electron falls back down, the energy leaves as a photon."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 walks the reaction in order: oxygen is added, a strained intermediate comes apart, the product lands in an excited state, and the light leaves when that electron falls back down. The photon is the last step, not the first.","distractorNotes":["Oxygen goes in earlier in the sequence, which sets the reaction up rather than releasing the light.","Calcium is the trigger for the photoprotein in paragraph 4, so it starts the stored reaction instead of being the instant light departs.","That is the color shift in paragraph 4, which recolors light that already exists.",null,"Paragraph 3 says the payout is taken as light instead of heat, so escaping heat is not where the photon comes from."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage gives which reason that most marine bioluminescence falls in the blue-green range?","choices":["Blue-green light carries farther than other colors through clear seawater","Blue-green light is the cheapest color for a luciferase to produce","Blue-green light is invisible to the majority of deep-sea predators","Blue-green light is the color of the sunlight that still reaches 1,000 meters","Blue-green light can be produced without using any oxygen"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"transmits blue and blue-green light far better than red or violet"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"travels the farthest"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 ties the color straight to the physics of water: blue and blue-green travel far better than red or violet, so signals near 470 to 490 nanometers reach the farthest.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage never ranks colors by what they cost to make; the reason it gives is how far the light travels.","The opposite holds in paragraph 7, where deep-sea eyes are tuned to that same band, which is why one fish using red is remarkable.","Paragraph 1 says sunlight is gone below about 1,000 meters, so there is no sunlight down there to match.","Oxygen is required by the reaction in paragraph 3 no matter what color comes out."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage describes the luciferases of different luminous groups as","choices":["variations on a single enzyme inherited from one ancient luminous ancestor","proteins that most animals obtain from their food rather than build themselves","unrelated proteins assembled again and again from enzymes already present","identical in structure but different in the kind of fuel they will accept","made only by bacteria and passed to animals through symbiosis"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"The luciferases are not related to one another."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"assembled again and again out of whatever enzymes happened to be present"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says plainly that the luciferases are not related to one another and have been put together repeatedly out of whatever enzymes were on hand. The fuel is what gets shared, not the enzyme.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 opens by rejecting a single luminous ancestor.","Diet supplies coelenterazine, the fuel, in paragraph 5, not the enzyme that burns it.",null,"Not being related to one another is exactly the point, so identical structure is what they lack.","Bacterial partnership is one route among several in paragraphs 13 and 14, not the only source."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q04","type":"detail","stem":"Quorum sensing, as the passage presents it, keeps symbiotic bacteria from","choices":["being taken up by a host species other than the one they belong in","growing beyond the space that is available inside the light organ","losing the genes they would need in order to live independently","being expelled from the light organ when morning comes","making light when they are too sparse for it to be worth the cost"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"keeps a drifting bacterium from spending energy to glow alone"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 describes a density check: the signaling molecule builds up only when cells are crowded, and only then do the luciferase genes switch on, which spares a lone drifting cell the cost of glowing.","distractorNotes":["Host selection is handled earlier in paragraph 13 by the mucus and the chemical filters, not by the density check.","Paragraph 13 links the signal to switching on light genes, not to limiting how many cells there are.","Genome loss belongs to the anglerfish symbionts in paragraph 14 and is not governed by this signal.","The dawn expulsion in paragraph 14 is something the squid does, and the density check does not prevent it.",null]},{"id":"rc-p03-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Each dawn, the light organ of the Hawaiian bobtail squid","choices":["vents most of its bacteria into the water and regrows them through the day","takes in an entirely fresh population of bacteria from the surrounding seawater","digests the bacteria it holds and absorbs the nutrients they contain","swaps its bacteria for a second species that is better suited to daylight","shuts its bacteria down by withdrawing the oxygen they need"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"expels about 95 percent of the bacteria in its light organ"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"the survivors regrow through the day"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 puts a number on it: about 95 percent of the bacteria are expelled each dawn, and the survivors regrow during the day so the organ is full again by night.","distractorNotes":[null,"Recruitment from seawater happens once, in the hours after hatching, as paragraph 13 describes.","Paragraph 14 says the bacteria are expelled into the water and says nothing about digesting them.","The winnowing in paragraph 13 leaves essentially one species, and no second species appears anywhere in the passage.","Oxygen withdrawal is never mentioned; the reset described there is a physical expulsion."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, \"expensive\" most nearly means","choices":["commanding a high price in a market","unusually difficult for an animal to obtain","demanding in energy and materials","wasteful of the heat a reaction produces","uncommon among the smallest animals"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"A flash spends fuel, oxygen, and protein"}],"why":"The sentence lists what a flash spends, which is fuel, oxygen, and protein, and then calls that expensive for an animal where meals are sparse. The cost is metabolic, not monetary.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday money sense of the word, and no money is involved anywhere in paragraph 6.","Paragraph 6 calls the starting materials ordinary, so the cost is in spending them, not in getting hold of them.",null,"Paragraph 3 makes the point that this reaction gives up very little heat, so waste heat is not the cost being named.","The sentence is about what one flash costs, not about how many animals have the trait."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 13, \"recruits\" most nearly means","choices":["trains for a specialized task","enlists by force","produces internally from its own tissue","draws in from an outside supply","selects from among its own offspring"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"recruits its bacteria from the seawater around it"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"hatches without a single symbiont"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says the squid hatches with no symbionts and gathers its bacteria out of the seawater around it within hours. The word is about taking in cells from outside the body.","distractorNotes":["No training happens, since the bacteria already carry the machinery to make light.","Nothing in paragraph 13 suggests force; the squid draws in cells that are already drifting past.","The squid hatches without a single symbiont, so the bacteria cannot come from its own tissue.",null,"The bacteria are free-living cells in the seawater, not the squid's young."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 9 most strongly suggests that an animal using counterillumination must also be able to","choices":["see the light that its own belly organs produce","sense how bright the water above it is","recognize the species of a predator approaching it","produce red light in addition to blue-green light","stop glowing entirely whenever it is threatened"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"adjust their output as the surrounding light changes"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"at the intensity of the water overhead"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says these animals adjust their output as the surrounding light changes, dimming as a cloud passes or as they sink. Matching a background that shifts means the animal has to register what the background is doing.","distractorNotes":["The organs aim light downward, away from the animal's own eyes, and the passage never asks it to view its own output.",null,"Paragraph 9 is about matching background light; recognizing a species belongs to the courtship signals in paragraph 12.","Red light is the unusual case of one fish in paragraph 7, not a requirement for this disguise.","Going dark would restore the very silhouette the disguise exists to erase."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q09","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from paragraph 15 that counts of luminous animals based on net collections","choices":["were reliable for jellyfish but not for fish","were abandoned as soon as submersibles became available","overstated the trait, because handled animals flash repeatedly","matched the later video counts once the badly damaged specimens were set aside","understated how common light is, especially among soft-bodied animals"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"The field spent decades underestimating it"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"a shredded animal cannot be scored as luminous"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":7,"quote":"tilted toward tough-bodied crustaceans and fish"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 says nets shred the animals most likely to be luminous and that a shredded animal cannot be scored as luminous, which tilted the census toward tough-bodied groups. Those counts had to come in low.","distractorNotes":["Jellyfish and comb jellies are precisely what the nets destroy, so those counts are the least trustworthy.","The passage says submersibles brought a problem of their own and gives no timeline for retiring nets.","Repeated handling leaves animals dark, which pushes a count down rather than up.","The numbers moved sharply once video counting began, which is the opposite of a match.",null]},{"id":"rc-p03-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Light production is chemically simpler than the variety of its biological uses would suggest.","The deep ocean is a hostile place that only a few highly specialized animals have managed to colonize.","Partnership with luminous bacteria is the main way that deep-sea animals obtain the light they use.","Making light is normal in the deep sea, and what is known about it has been shaped by how it was sampled.","Improvements in underwater imaging have overturned most of what biologists once believed about the open ocean."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":6,"quote":"nearly all the light is made by the animals living in it"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"for reasons that had nothing to do with the animals"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":3,"quote":"most of the animals carry their own light"}],"why":"The passage opens by correcting the picture of a lightless deep sea, works through how light is made, why it keeps appearing, and what it does, then explains in paragraphs 15 and 16 why the count was long too low. Both halves of that answer are the spine of the piece.","distractorNotes":["True as far as it goes, and it is one thread in the middle of the passage rather than the point the whole piece builds toward.","The first paragraph rejects exactly this empty-desert picture of the deep sea.","Symbiosis gets two paragraphs among many routes to light and is treated as one arrangement, not the main one.",null,"Video methods revise one number in paragraph 15, and the passage claims nothing that sweeping."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q11","type":"function","stem":"The main purpose of paragraph 15 is to","choices":["argue that nets should no longer be used in deep-sea research","explain why the collecting methods, not the animals, produced a low early count","describe the physical damage that deep-sea animals suffer during capture and handling","show that submersibles solved the problems the earlier net surveys had created","introduce the claim that most luminous animals in the ocean are gelatinous"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"for reasons that had nothing to do with the animals"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"nets are brutal to the animals most likely to be luminous"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":6,"quote":"Submersibles brought a problem of their own"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 asks how much bioluminescence there is and answers that the field undercounted it for reasons that had nothing to do with the animals. Everything in it, the nets, the handling, the floodlights, serves that explanation.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph explains what nets distort, but it never calls for retiring them.",null,"The damage is evidence inside the argument rather than the reason the paragraph exists.","The paragraph says submersible floodlights created a problem of their own.","Gelatinous animals appear as an example within the argument, not as the paragraph's claim."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["An assumption is corrected, four questions are posed and answered, and the conclusions are then qualified.","A single hypothesis is introduced, weighed carefully against two competing explanations, and then rejected on the evidence.","One organism is followed closely from start to finish, and general principles are drawn from it at the end.","A history of deep-sea exploration is traced from the earliest nets through modern video methods.","The uses of light are listed one after another, each one supported by a separate experiment."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"But the blackness deserves a second look."},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"Four questions follow"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"That leaves the fourth question"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"Caution is still warranted."}],"why":"Paragraph 1 corrects the picture of a lightless deep sea, paragraph 2 sets out four questions, the body answers them in that order, and paragraph 16 pulls back on how far the answers can be pushed.","distractorNotes":[null,"No hypothesis is rejected; the closing paragraphs keep the general claim while limiting the specific ones.","The bobtail squid gets close attention in the middle, but the passage is built around questions rather than around one animal.","Methods come up only near the end, and only to explain why one number was wrong.","The uses are described, but no experiments are reported for any of them."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude in paragraph 16 toward explanations of what a particular flash is for is best described as","choices":["dismissive of the idea that these displays serve any biological function at all","confident that the purpose of each display has now been worked out","careful, holding the general claim while reserving judgment case by case","frustrated with the researchers who study deep-sea behavior","indifferent to whether such explanations can ever be tested"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"The claim worth defending is the narrow one"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"easy to construct and hard to test"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 keeps the broad claim that light is common and made for reasons, and it withholds only the confident purpose assigned to any single flash. That is caution, not doubt about the whole subject.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph keeps the claim that light is made for reasons; what it resists is naming the reason for every flash.","The paragraph says a plausible story is easy to construct and hard to test, which is the opposite of confidence.",null,"No researcher is criticized; the difficulty belongs to the setting, since displays happen unobserved in the dark.","Testability is precisely what the paragraph cares about."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 6, the author treats the repeated independent evolution of light as","choices":["a coincidence that remains unexplained","an outcome that ordinary cell chemistry makes unsurprising","evidence that the trait must have a single ancient origin after all","a puzzle that undermines current evolutionary thinking","a rare event that required unusual conditions each time it happened"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"the starting materials are ordinary"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"is not exotic chemistry"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 answers its own question by pointing to ordinary ingredients: cells are already full of molecules that oxidize readily, and any protein rigid enough to hold the excited state can be pressed into service. The repetition is presented as expected, not mysterious.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph offers an explanation rather than leaving the pattern unaccounted for.",null,"Paragraph 5 rules out a single luminous ancestor, and paragraph 6 explains why none is needed.","Nothing is presented as overturning evolutionary thinking; building a trait from available parts is treated as routine.","The paragraph stresses that the starting materials are ordinary, which is the argument against rarity."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements about light organs EXCEPT:","choices":["Some contain reflectors, lenses, and filters that shape the light leaving them.","Some are stocked with bacteria instead of making the light themselves.","The output of some can be adjusted as conditions around the animal change.","Some must shed the considerable heat their reaction produces.","Some are aimed downward so that an animal's silhouette disappears."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"with reflectors behind the light source, lenses in front of it, and filters","choice":0},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"They farm bacteria that do.","choice":1},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"adjust their output as the surrounding light changes","choice":2},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"because it is aimed down","choice":4},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"Because so little energy escapes as heat","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraphs 3 and 4 make the point that almost no energy leaves as heat, which is why the passage calls it cold light. There is no meaningful heat to shed, so that statement is the one the passage does not support.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 8 lists these optical parts by name.","Paragraphs 13 and 14 describe light organs filled with symbiotic bacteria.","Paragraph 9 describes animals dimming and brightening as the surrounding light shifts.",null,"That is the counterillumination described in paragraph 8."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to know why the bacteria inside some anglerfish lures could not survive on their own would find that explained in","choices":["paragraph 5","paragraph 9","paragraph 11","paragraph 13","paragraph 14"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"including genes they would need in order to live on their own"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 is where the anglerfish symbionts appear, and it says they have lost large stretches of their genomes, including the genes they would need in order to live on their own.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 covers the independent origins of luminous chemistry, not any particular partnership.","Paragraph 9 is about matching the background light while counterilluminating.","Paragraph 11 covers luminous clouds and shed glowing body parts.","Paragraph 13 explains how a squid gathers and screens its bacteria, but the genome loss appears in the paragraph after it.",null]}]},"rc-p04":{"id":"rc-p04","title":"How Resistance Genes Travel","topic":"antibiotic resistance and horizontal gene transfer","field":"microbiology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1375,"paragraphs":[["Penicillin reached general clinical use in the early 1940s, and within a decade hospitals were reporting infections that shrugged it off.","That reversal set the pattern for nearly every antibiotic since: a compound arrives, works spectacularly, and then loses ground, sometimes within years.","The usual explanation, that bacteria evolve quickly because they divide quickly, is true as far as it goes and incomplete in a way that matters for how the problem is managed."],["The familiar account runs through mutation and selection.","A bacterium copying its chromosome makes occasional errors; most are harmful or meaningless, but a few alter a drug's target, the envelope's permeability, or a pump that expels foreign molecules.","In the presence of the drug, that cell leaves more descendants than its neighbors.","Because a population in rich medium can double every twenty minutes, a rare variant can dominate within days.","This is ordinary vertical descent: the gene passes from parent to offspring and the lineage grows."],["Vertical descent alone, though, cannot account for what clinicians actually see.","Resistance to a new drug often appears in several unrelated species at once, in the same ward or stretch of river, carrying the same gene with the same sequence.","Independent mutation does not produce identical answers in lineages that have not shared an ancestor for hundreds of millions of years.","Something is moving the gene itself."],["Bacteria pass genes sideways as well as down, a process called horizontal gene transfer.","Three routes do most of the work.","In conjugation, a donor carrying a conjugative plasmid extends a pilus that binds a recipient and draws the cells into direct contact.","A relaxase enzyme then nicks one strand of the plasmid at a sequence called the origin of transfer, and that single strand is threaded through a channel into the recipient.","Both cells synthesize the complementary strand, and the recipient becomes a donor in its own right."],["These plasmids are circular DNA molecules separate from the chromosome, replicating on their own schedule.","A single plasmid may carry a dozen resistance determinants plus genes for metal tolerance, virulence, and its own transfer machinery.","Some live within one genus; others are far less particular, establishing themselves in hosts as distantly related as Escherichia and Pseudomonas.","A cell that receives one acquires an entire package at once, so a susceptible strain can become multidrug resistant with no intermediate stage."],["The second route requires no contact at all.","Dying bacteria release DNA into their surroundings, and some species can take that naked DNA across the envelope and recombine it into their own chromosome.","The capacity is called competence, and in several species it switches on under stress or at a certain population density."],["The third route conscripts viruses.","Bacteriophages package their genome into new particles, and occasionally the machinery takes a piece of host DNA by mistake.","When such a particle infects another cell, it injects bacterial genes instead of viral ones.","Generalized transduction moves more or less any fragment of the host chromosome; specialized transduction moves the segments that lie alongside a phage's integration site.","Neither is efficient per particle, but phages are so abundant in seawater and sediment that rare events become routine."],["Arriving is not the same as being used.","A gene that lands in a new cell without a promoter in front of it sits inert.","Integrons solve that problem, and they explain how resistance accumulates rather than merely appears.","An integron is a genetic filing system: an integrase gene, a recombination site for new material, and a single promoter aimed at that site.","Incoming gene cassettes, small circular units carrying one gene apiece, are cut in under the resident promoter.","A fresh cassette inserts nearest the promoter, so the most recent addition is expressed most strongly, while older ones sit further back in the array."],["Integrons themselves are not mobile, but they ride on things that are.","Class 1 integrons in particular sit inside transposons that hop onto plasmids, and those plasmids conjugate.","Arrays holding several cassettes have been found in clinical isolates."],["This raises an obvious question: where do the genes come from in the first place?","The intuitive answer, that antibiotics created them, is simply wrong.","Bacteria from a deep cave chamber in New Mexico, cut off from the surface for something on the order of four million years, carry resistance to multiple modern drug classes, including compounds first made in a laboratory.","DNA from permafrost tens of thousands of years old contains recognizable genes for enzymes that destroy penicillin type drugs.","The reason is not mysterious: many antibiotic scaffolds in clinical use came from soil microbes, which must protect themselves from what they make."],["The standing pool of such genes in soil and gut communities is large enough to have earned a name, the resistome, and it is ancient.","The antibiotic era did not invent resistance.","It applied intense selection to a reservoir that was already there, and it rearranged the contents of that reservoir, pulling genes out of soil chromosomes, onto plasmids, and into the species that infect people."],["This distinction, between resistance arising and resistance spreading, is the one most often collapsed in public discussion, and collapsing it leads to bad predictions.","Arising is chemistry and chance, and has gone on for as long as microbes have made antibiotics.","Spreading is a matter of ecology: how much drug is present, how often cells meet, how mobile the element is, and how many hosts it tolerates.","Only the second of those is something human behavior can push on.","Hospitals, feedlots, and sewage systems are not laboratories where resistance is invented but mixing vessels where it is concentrated and moved."],["There is a tempting corollary: resistance is supposed to be costly.","A pump that expels molecules burns energy, an altered ribosome may translate less efficiently, and a plasmid adds DNA that must be copied at every division.","In the absence of the drug, a resistant cell should therefore fall behind a susceptible one, and resistance should drain out of a population once prescribing stops.","The reasoning is sound and the premise is often true.","The conclusion, unfortunately, does not follow as reliably as it should."],["Several things blunt it.","Compensatory mutations elsewhere in the genome restore the lost efficiency while leaving the resistance gene intact, so the cost can shrink toward nothing within a few hundred generations.","Co-selection links otherwise independent determinants: a plasmid carrying resistance to an abandoned drug may also carry resistance to one still in use, so ordinary practice keeps selecting for the whole package.","Some plasmids carry toxin antitoxin systems, pairing a durable toxin with a short lived antidote, so a daughter cell that fails to inherit the plasmid poisons itself.","And some resistance genes appear to cost very little from the beginning."],["The empirical record matches that picture rather than the tidy one.","Where national programs have sharply and durably cut a whole drug class, resistance in some target organisms has declined measurably.","Elsewhere, the same kind of restriction has produced years of flat numbers.","A gene that has become fixed in a population, with its cost paid down and its carriage tied to other advantages, does not vanish because prescribing habits change.","Reversal happens, but it is slow, partial, and organism specific, and no one should plan around it as though it were a wall switch."],["None of this argues against stewardship.","It argues for a more honest version of it.","If the aim is to restore an earlier state, stewardship will look like failure.","If the aim is to slow the movement of genes that already exist, then the tools follow: diagnostics that separate bacterial from viral illness before prescribing, narrow agents rather than broad ones, courses no longer than necessary, infection control that keeps resistant strains from new hosts, and sanitation that keeps gut plasmids out of the water supply.","These measures buy time rather than turning back the clock, and time is what a slow drug development pipeline needs most."],["Treating resistance as a contaminant to be cleaned up misreads what it is.","It is a standing feature of microbial life, older than medicine by a wide margin, and the realistic goal is to manage the traffic rather than to clear the road."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p04-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, what happens immediately after the relaxase nicks one strand of a conjugative plasmid?","choices":["One strand of the plasmid passes into the recipient through a channel","The recipient degrades the incoming DNA before replication begins","The pilus retracts and the donor permanently loses its plasmid","The plasmid inserts itself into the recipient chromosome at the origin of transfer site","A virus particle packages the plasmid and carries it across"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"that single strand is threaded through a channel into the recipient"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 walks through conjugation in order: contact, pilus, relaxase nick at the origin of transfer, then the single nicked strand threaded through a channel into the recipient. The answer is the next step in that sequence.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage never mentions the recipient destroying incoming DNA during conjugation; it says the recipient builds the complementary strand and becomes a donor itself.","The passage says the recipient becomes a donor in its own right, which means the donor keeps a copy rather than losing the plasmid.","The origin of transfer is where the relaxase cuts, not a site where the plasmid integrates into the chromosome; plasmids in this passage replicate separately from the chromosome.","Packaging into a virus particle is transduction, described in paragraph 7, not conjugation."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that the gene cassette most recently added to an integron is","choices":["silenced until the older cassettes are excised","placed farthest from the promoter and expressed weakly","expressed most strongly because it sits closest to the promoter","copied onto a separate plasmid before any of it can be expressed","given a dedicated promoter of its own by the integrase"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"A fresh cassette inserts nearest the promoter"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"the most recent addition is expressed most strongly"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 explains that a fresh cassette inserts nearest the single resident promoter, so the newest arrival is expressed most strongly and older cassettes sit further back in the array.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in the passage requires older cassettes to be removed; they simply sit further back and are expressed less strongly.","This reverses the arrangement in paragraph 8. The newest cassette goes nearest the promoter, and the older ones are the ones sitting further away.",null,"The passage says cassettes are filed into the integron and expressed there. Movement onto a plasmid happens to the whole integron, and it is not a requirement for expression.","The point of the integron is that one shared promoter serves the array. A cassette arrives without a promoter of its own, which is exactly the problem integrons solve."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q03","type":"detail","stem":"As the passage describes them, toxin antitoxin systems on a plasmid work because","choices":["the toxin kills neighboring susceptible cells and clears competitors from the surrounding population","the toxin speeds the transfer of the plasmid into new hosts","the system lowers the energy cost of carrying the plasmid","the toxin destroys any competing plasmid that enters the cell","the antidote is short lived, so a cell losing the plasmid dies"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"a durable toxin with a short lived antidote"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"a daughter cell that fails to inherit the plasmid poisons itself"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 pairs a durable toxin with a short lived antidote. When a daughter cell fails to inherit the plasmid, the antidote disappears first and the lingering toxin kills that cell, which is why the plasmid is hard to shed.","distractorNotes":["The passage describes the toxin acting on the cell that lost the plasmid, not on neighboring cells.","Moving a plasmid into new hosts is the work of the conjugation machinery in paragraph 4; paragraph 14 ties the toxin only to what happens in a cell that loses the plasmid.","Paragraph 13 states that a plasmid adds DNA that must be copied at every division, so it adds cost rather than lowering it. Compensatory mutations, not toxin systems, are what reduce cost here.","Nothing in the passage describes the toxin attacking other plasmids; the target is the cell that failed to inherit this one.",null]},{"id":"rc-p04-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, specialized transduction moves","choices":["any fragment of the host chromosome, with roughly equal probability for each","the chromosomal segments lying next to where the phage integrates","only plasmids that carry resistance cassettes","naked DNA released into the surroundings by cells that have died","the phage genome alone, with no bacterial DNA"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"specialized transduction moves the segments that lie alongside a phage's integration site"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 draws the contrast directly: generalized transduction moves more or less any fragment, while specialized transduction moves the segments that lie alongside a phage's integration site.","distractorNotes":["That describes generalized transduction, which the passage names in the same sentence as the contrast.",null,"Plasmids move by conjugation in paragraph 4. The passage does not restrict transduction to plasmids.","Uptake of naked DNA from the surroundings is competence, described in paragraph 6.","If only the phage genome moved, no bacterial gene would travel at all, and the passage is explaining how bacterial genes ride along."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Which of the following is offered in the passage as evidence that resistance genes existed before the antibiotic era?","choices":["Soil bacteria shed their resistance genes within a few generations once antibiotics are withdrawn","Integrons holding several cassettes have been found in clinical isolates","Bacterial populations in rich medium can double every twenty minutes","Cave bacteria sealed off for millions of years resist several modern drug classes","Permafrost bacteria grow faster than bacteria taken from the surface"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"cut off from the surface for something on the order of four million years"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"carry resistance to multiple modern drug classes"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives two pieces of evidence, and this is one of them: bacteria from a New Mexico cave chamber cut off from the surface for roughly four million years resist multiple modern drug classes.","distractorNotes":["The passage argues the opposite in paragraphs 13 through 15. Resistance often persists after a drug is withdrawn rather than disappearing quickly.","That detail appears in paragraph 9, but it describes how much resistance accumulates in clinical strains, not how old resistance is.","That figure comes from paragraph 2 and explains how fast a variant can spread, which is a different question.",null,"The passage mentions permafrost DNA carrying penicillin destroying enzyme genes, not anything about growth rates."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 11, \"reservoir\" most nearly means","choices":["a tank constructed to hold water for later use","a species that carries a pathogen without ever becoming ill from it","an existing supply of genes that selection can draw from","a laboratory collection of preserved bacterial strains","a barrier that keeps genes from moving between species"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"applied intense selection to a reservoir that was already there"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"The standing pool of such genes in soil and gut communities"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 calls the standing pool of resistance genes in soil and gut communities the resistome and says the antibiotic era applied selection to a reservoir that was already there. The word names a pre existing supply, not a container.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday dictionary sense. Nothing is being stored as water here, and the passage is talking about genes in living communities.","This is a real biological use of the word in the study of infection, but the passage is describing a pool of genes, not a host species that stays healthy.",null,"The pool described sits in soil and gut communities in the wild, not in a curated laboratory collection.","The paragraph says the era pulled genes out of soil chromosomes and into human pathogens, so the reservoir is a source of movement, not a barrier against it."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 15, \"fixed\" most nearly means","choices":["repaired after having been damaged","held at a single location on the chromosome","set in advance by the prescribing physician","established across a population and not easily lost","attached permanently to a plasmid so that it cannot be shed"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"A gene that has become fixed in a population"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"does not vanish because prescribing habits change"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 says a gene that has become fixed in a population, with its cost paid down, does not vanish when prescribing habits change. The word describes a gene that has become a settled part of the population.","distractorNotes":["This is the most common everyday meaning, but the sentence is about a gene spreading through a population, not about something broken being mended.","This confuses the gene's position in a genome with its frequency in a population, which is what the sentence is tracking.","The paragraph is about what happens in bacterial populations regardless of prescribing, and prescribing habits are named as the thing that changes without dislodging the gene.",null,"Plasmid carriage is one reason a gene persists, described in paragraph 14, but the sentence is about the gene's standing in the population as a whole, not its physical attachment."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that if resistance genes moved only from parent to offspring, then","choices":["resistance would appear far sooner after a drug is introduced","plasmids would carry more cassettes than they currently do","different lineages would be expected to arrive at different genetic solutions","resistance would still be expected to appear as the same sequence in distant species","resistance would be confined to the species that manufacture antibiotics"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"Independent mutation does not produce identical answers in lineages"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"carrying the same gene with the same sequence"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Something is moving the gene itself."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 argues that independent mutation cannot produce identical answers in unrelated lineages, which is why the shared sequence points to transfer. Turn that around and vertical descent alone would leave each lineage with its own separate solution.","distractorNotes":["Nothing suggests vertical descent alone would be faster. Paragraph 5 notes that transfer can deliver a whole package at once, which is the faster route.","Cassette counts come from paragraph 9 and depend on integrons capturing incoming genes, which is horizontal movement, not vertical descent.",null,"This is exactly the observation paragraph 3 says vertical descent cannot explain, which is why the author concludes the gene itself is moving.","Paragraph 10 says producer organisms protect themselves, but the passage never claims resistance would be limited to them without transfer."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on the passage, what can be inferred about a resistance gene that carries almost no fitness cost?","choices":["It must have arrived by transduction rather than by conjugation or by transformation","It requires a compensatory mutation before it can be expressed at all","It will spread in hospital wards but not in soil communities","It cannot be carried on a plasmid alongside other resistance genes","Withdrawing the drug would be an unusually weak way to reduce it"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"some resistance genes appear to cost very little from the beginning"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"resistance should drain out of a population once prescribing stops"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"with its cost paid down"}],"why":"Paragraphs 13 and 14 hang the whole case for reversal on the idea that resistance is costly, and paragraph 14 lists low cost genes as one reason withdrawal disappoints. If there is little cost to escape, removing the drug removes little pressure against the gene.","distractorNotes":["Cost has nothing to do with which of the three transfer routes delivered the gene; the passage never links the two.","Compensatory mutations reduce an existing cost, as paragraph 14 says. A gene that already costs little has no need of one, and the passage never ties them to expression.","The passage places resistance genes in soil communities from the start, in paragraphs 10 and 11, so this contradicts the text.","Paragraph 5 describes plasmids carrying a dozen determinants at once, and nothing excludes a low cost gene from riding along.",null]},{"id":"rc-p04-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Agricultural antibiotic use is the single cause of resistance in human infections, and restricting it would solve the problem","Resistance genes are ancient and spread chiefly by gene transfer, so stewardship should aim at slowing that spread rather than reversing it","Because bacteria divide quickly, mutation and selection within single lineages account for nearly all the resistance clinicians encounter","Conjugation, transformation, and transduction are the three routes of horizontal gene transfer, and each contributes to the spread of resistance","Since resistance cannot be reversed once it appears, the only realistic response is to keep developing new antibiotics faster than the old ones fail"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"and it is ancient"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"rearranged the contents of that reservoir"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"slow the movement of genes that already exist"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":2,"quote":"manage the traffic rather than to clear the road"}],"why":"The passage moves from a puzzle about speed, through the transfer mechanisms, to the evidence that resistance is ancient, and lands in paragraphs 15 and 16 on stewardship as a way to slow movement rather than restore an earlier state. That arc is the main idea.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 names feedlots as one mixing vessel among several, alongside hospitals and sewage, so the passage never makes agriculture the single cause.",null,"Paragraph 1 calls this explanation true as far as it goes but incomplete, and paragraph 3 says it cannot account for what clinicians see.","This is accurate and occupies several paragraphs, but it is the machinery the argument uses, not the conclusion the passage reaches.","Paragraph 15 says reversal does happen, though slowly and partially, and paragraph 16 endorses stewardship rather than abandoning it for new drugs."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author's main purpose in describing the cave bacteria and the permafrost DNA is","choices":["to correct the assumption that resistance genes arose in the antibiotic era","to show that resistance is more severe in natural settings than it is in hospitals","to argue that soil bacteria should be mined for the next generation of drugs","to explain how integrons capture and file incoming gene cassettes","to warn that thawing permafrost will release new pathogens"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"where do the genes come from in the first place"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"that antibiotics created them, is simply wrong"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 opens by asking where the genes come from and says flatly that the intuitive answer, that antibiotics created them, is wrong. The cave and the permafrost are the evidence brought in to overturn that assumption.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage does not compare severity between settings. Paragraph 12 calls hospitals and feedlots mixing vessels, which is about concentration, not severity.","Paragraph 10 notes that many antibiotic scaffolds came from soil microbes, but that is background, not a proposal for drug discovery.","Integrons are explained in paragraph 8, several paragraphs earlier, and for a different reason.","Nothing in the passage predicts a release of pathogens from thawing. The permafrost appears only as a record of ancient DNA."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes the relationship between paragraph 13 and paragraph 14?","choices":["Paragraph 13 gives a single example and paragraph 14 generalizes from it to a broader rule","Both paragraphs describe the same mechanism from two different angles","Paragraph 13 raises a question that paragraph 14 declines to answer","Paragraph 14 supplies fresh experimental evidence for the claim in paragraph 13","Paragraph 13 builds a sound expectation and paragraph 14 explains why it often fails"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"resistance should drain out of a population once prescribing stops"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"does not follow as reliably as it should"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":1,"quote":"Several things blunt it."}],"why":"Paragraph 13 builds the fitness cost argument and grants that its reasoning is sound, then says the conclusion does not follow reliably. Paragraph 14 opens with the phrase about what blunts it and lists the specific mechanisms that do so.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 13 presents a general argument rather than a single example, and paragraph 14 narrows to specific mechanisms rather than widening.","The two paragraphs describe opposing forces, not one mechanism seen twice.","Paragraph 14 answers the question paragraph 13 leaves open, in four distinct ways.","Paragraph 14 offers mechanisms that undercut the expectation, not evidence that supports it. The empirical record arrives in paragraph 15.",null]},{"id":"rc-p04-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the common habit of treating resistance arising and resistance spreading as one thing is best described as","choices":["amused by it","indifferent, since the distinction makes no practical difference to patients","approving, since simplification helps public understanding","critical, because merging the two leads to unreliable expectations","alarmed, predicting an imminent epidemic because of the confusion"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"collapsing it leads to bad predictions"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says the distinction is the one most often collapsed in public discussion and that collapsing it leads to bad predictions. That is measured criticism, not amusement or panic.","distractorNotes":["There is no humor in the paragraph. The language is corrective and plainly serious.","The paragraph argues the opposite, that the distinction determines what human action can actually change.","The author treats the simplification as a source of bad predictions, so approval is the reverse of the stance taken.",null,"The tone is analytical rather than alarmed, and the passage makes no prediction of an imminent epidemic."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward antibiotic stewardship is best described as","choices":["skeptical that stewardship is worth the effort it requires","supportive, provided its goal is understood as slowing spread rather than reversing it","confident that strict stewardship will restore susceptibility within about a decade","neutral, presenting stewardship without offering any evaluation of it","dismissive of diagnostics and infection control as minor measures next to new drug development"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"None of this argues against stewardship."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"It argues for a more honest version of it."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"slow the movement of genes that already exist"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 states outright that none of this argues against stewardship and that it argues for a more honest version, then says the measures buy time rather than turning back the clock. That is support with a corrected goal.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph opens by denying exactly this reading and goes on to list the tools stewardship should use.",null,"Paragraph 15 says reversal is slow, partial, and organism specific, which rules out confidence in a decade long restoration.","The author evaluates stewardship directly, calling for a more honest version of it and naming which aims will and will not work.","Diagnostics and infection control head the author's own list of tools, and new drug development is presented as needing the time stewardship buys."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Some plasmids can establish themselves in distantly related host genera","A phage particle can inject bacterial DNA into a new host cell","Competence allows a cell to take up DNA released into its surroundings by dying bacteria","Integrons move between cells on their own, without help from other elements","Compensatory mutations can reduce the cost of carrying a resistance gene"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"Integrons themselves are not mobile, but they ride on things that are.","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"hosts as distantly related as Escherichia and Pseudomonas","choice":0},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"it injects bacterial genes instead of viral ones","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"take that naked DNA across the envelope","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"The capacity is called competence","choice":2},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"the cost can shrink toward nothing within a few hundred generations","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says plainly that integrons themselves are not mobile and that they ride on transposons which hop onto plasmids. The other four statements each restate something the passage asserts.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 says some plasmids establish themselves in hosts as distantly related as Escherichia and Pseudomonas, so this is supported.","Paragraph 7 describes a particle that injects bacterial genes instead of viral ones, so this is supported.","Paragraph 6 defines competence as the capacity to take up naked DNA released by dying bacteria, so this is supported.",null,"Paragraph 14 says compensatory mutations restore lost efficiency so the cost shrinks toward nothing, so this is supported."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wants to know how a resistance gene that arrives without a promoter can still be switched on should look in the paragraph that discusses","choices":["the integron and its gene cassettes","the steps of conjugation and the origin of transfer","the cave and permafrost evidence","the difference between resistance arising and resistance spreading","the stewardship measures near the end"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"without a promoter in front of it sits inert"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"Integrons solve that problem"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 raises exactly that problem, saying a gene that lands without a promoter sits inert, and then presents the integron with its single shared promoter as the solution.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 4 explains how DNA physically gets into a new cell, which is the step before the expression problem arises.","Paragraph 10 addresses the age and origin of resistance genes, not how an arriving gene is expressed.","Paragraph 12 separates two processes conceptually and does not deal with promoters.","Paragraph 16 lists practical measures and says nothing about gene expression."]}]},"rc-p05":{"id":"rc-p05","title":"Volcanic Ash and the Zero Threshold","topic":"volcanic ash and aviation safety","field":"geology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1371,"paragraphs":[["The word ash does more harm than good.","It invites a picture of the soft gray powder left in a fireplace.","Volcanic ash is none of that, and nothing in it has burned.","A pilot who treats ash as smoke will reason about it wrongly.","An engineer who treats it as pulverized rock thrown to cruising altitude will reason about it correctly."],["What a volcano ejects is magma torn apart by its own gas.","Dissolved water and carbon dioxide come out of solution as the magma rises, and the bubbles expand until the liquid between them shatters.","The fragments cool within seconds, far too quickly for their atoms to settle into the ordered lattice of a crystal, so they are quenched into glass, a liquid structure frozen in place.","Fragments under two millimeters across are called ash.","They are angular, hard, and abrasive enough to frost a windscreen in minutes."],["Size decides how far ash travels.","Coarse fragments fall out near the vent.","A particle ten micrometers across settles at a few millimeters per second, so a cloud lofted to nine kilometers can drift for days and cross an ocean.","Aviation's problem lies almost entirely in this fine tail, which arrives where nobody expects it."],["Inside a jet engine the trouble starts with temperature.","Air compressed at the front is burned with fuel in the combustor, where the gas exceeds 1,400 degrees Celsius before it reaches the turbine.","Depending on its composition, volcanic ash begins to soften and flow near 1,100 degrees.","It does not burn.","It melts."],["Molten ash does not stay molten.","It is carried into the first stage of the turbine, where the metal is held cooler than the gas by air bled from the compressor and blown out through hundreds of small holes in every vane and blade.","Glass touching those cooler surfaces sticks and freezes onto them.","The cooling holes plug, so the metal beneath runs hotter than designed.","The deposit also narrows the passage the gas must squeeze through, and a narrowed throat pushes back against the compressor.","The airflow breaks down, the flame goes out, and the engine stops."],["The recovery is the strange part.","A dead engine cools.","Glass and metal contract at different rates, the brittle layer cracks and flakes away in the airflow, and a shaft still turning in the wind can be relit.","The procedure this implies runs against instinct: descend out of the cloud, wait, and try again."],["On 24 June 1982 a British Airways 747 bound for Perth entered ash from Mount Galunggung in Java at night.","The weather radar showed nothing, because such radar is built to detect water droplets and is effectively blind to dry ash.","The crew saw a glow on the leading edges, smelled something acrid, and watched all four engines flame out.","The airplane glided for about sixteen minutes and lost 25,000 feet before the engines relit one at a time.","It landed at Jakarta with a windscreen sandblasted nearly opaque."],["On 15 December 1989 a new 747-400 descending toward Anchorage flew into a cloud from Mount Redoubt that had been reported but had not reached the crew usefully.","All four engines failed again.","The airplane fell about 14,000 feet before the crew got them running and landed safely.","The repair bill, including four replaced engines, came to roughly 80 million dollars."],["The institutional answer took shape from the late 1980s into the 1990s as the International Airways Volcano Watch.","Nine Volcanic Ash Advisory Centres divide the globe between them.","When a volcano erupts, the responsible center issues an advisory: polygons showing where ash is expected at given flight levels now and at six, twelve and eighteen hours ahead."],["The polygons come from a dispersion model, which is only as good as its inputs.","It needs three source parameters: column height, the mass leaving the vent each second, and the range of particle sizes.","Only height is observed directly.","The mass rate is usually inferred from height through an empirical relation: height rises as roughly the fourth root of the rate.","Read the other way, that relation punishes error: mass goes as the fourth power of height, so a column overestimated by a quarter overstates the ash by nearly a factor of two and a half.","The model then releases millions of notional particles into the winds of a weather forecast and follows them."],["Direct observation is thinner than it sounds.","The standard satellite technique compares brightness at two infrared wavelengths; the difference comes out positive over water cloud and negative over ash.","The method goes blind when ash lies beneath a meteorological cloud, when ice condenses onto the particles and dresses them as weather, and when the layer is thin.","It also says little about how much ash sits at any altitude, because a satellite looks down through the whole column and cannot resolve it into layers."],["The rule built on all this was short and, for a long time, unarguable: avoid ash.","It was written when nobody could say what exposure an engine could survive, and without a number the only defensible number is zero.","The people who wrote it deserve sympathy.","They had two total power losses, no laboratory data, and a duty not to gamble with passengers.","But a rule of that shape quietly makes a claim it cannot support.","The model produces a field of concentrations fading from thick to nothing, with no edge in it, and the rule needs an edge.","So one was drawn at the outermost contour, everything inside forbidden and everything outside clear.","The rule was conservative in the engineer's sense: it moved the whole weight of the uncertainty onto the side of not flying, and left the question of how much ash is too much permanently unasked."],["For nearly two decades nobody had much reason to ask it.","Certifying an engine against ash would mean expensive testing to publish a number that could mainly expose the manufacturer to blame.","While eruptions closed a thinly flown corner of Alaska or the Pacific, the cost of not knowing stayed small."],["Iceland ended that.","In April 2010 an eruption at Eyjafjallajokull shifted beneath the summit ice cap.","Meltwater reaching the magma chilled and shattered it, producing an unusually large share of very fine ash, and the weather carried it southeast toward the world's busiest airspace.","Applying the rule as written, authorities closed most of European airspace from 15 to 23 April.","About 100,000 flights were canceled, more than ten million travelers were stranded, and the industry put its losses at roughly 1.7 billion dollars.","No airplane was damaged, because almost none flew."],["The closure achieved what two engine failures had not.","Within days, research aircraft were sampling the plume and lidar stations were measuring the ash layers passing over Europe.","Much of the closed airspace turned out to hold concentrations far below anything the accident record suggested was dangerous.","Engine manufacturers, pressed to state a tolerance they had never been obliged to state, produced one.","Within weeks the binary chart had become a graded one: below two milligrams of ash per cubic meter of air, ordinary operations; between two and four, flight permitted with the manufacturer's agreement and added inspections; above four, closed."],["Two ideas came with the numbers, and both outlast them.","The first is that what harms an engine is a dose, the concentration multiplied by the minutes spent inside it, so a brief crossing of a thick layer and a long cruise through a thin one are not different in kind.","The second is that an advisory should show its uncertainty, so centers now run their models repeatedly and publish the spread.","When Grimsvotn erupted in May 2011 with a taller column than Eyjafjallajokull's, cancellations ran into the hundreds rather than the hundreds of thousands.","The science had not become certain.","It had become quantitative, which is a different and more useful thing.","The lesson worth keeping is not that the 2010 closure was an overreaction.","It is that a threshold of zero is not the absence of a judgment about risk.","It is a judgment made silently, by people who had not been given the data to make it any other way."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p05-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, ash particles have the structure of a glass rather than a crystal because","choices":["they are ground against one another inside the vent until their edges become rounded","dissolved gas keeps crystals from forming at any temperature","meltwater quenches them at the moment they leave the vent","they are melted a second time inside a jet engine","they cool too fast for their atoms to reach an ordered lattice"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"far too quickly for their atoms to settle into the ordered lattice of a crystal"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says the fragments cool within seconds, far too quickly for their atoms to settle into the ordered lattice of a crystal, so they freeze as glass. Speed of cooling is the whole reason.","distractorNotes":["The passage calls the fragments angular, not rounded, and never mentions grinding inside the vent.","Dissolved gas is what tears the magma apart in paragraph 2. It is not offered as a reason crystals fail to form.","Meltwater appears only in the 2010 account in paragraph 14, where it explains fine fragmentation rather than glassy structure.","Melting inside an engine comes in paragraph 4, long after the particle is already glass.",null]},{"id":"rc-p05-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage gives the temperature, in degrees Celsius, at which volcanic ash begins to soften and flow as approximately","choices":["400","1,400","2,000","1,100","700"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"begins to soften and flow near 1,100 degrees"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 states that, depending on composition, ash begins to soften and flow near 1,100 degrees Celsius. The 1,400 figure in the same paragraph belongs to the gas in the combustor.","distractorNotes":["Far too low. Paragraph 4 puts the softening point up near 1,100 degrees.","This is the combustor gas, which paragraph 4 says exceeds 1,400 degrees. That is the number the softening point is contrasted against, not the softening point itself.","No figure this high appears anywhere, and the point of the paragraph is that the engine is already hotter than ash needs.",null,"Paragraph 4 gives one specific number for softening, and it is not this one."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 5, a glass deposit in the first turbine stage does which two things?","choices":["Cooling holes become blocked and the gas passage is narrowed","The blades melt outright and the shaft seizes","The fuel supply is contaminated and the flame is put out directly","The compressor blades are worn thin and the intake is choked","The turbine spins faster and overspeeds the shaft"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"The cooling holes plug"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"narrows the passage the gas must squeeze through"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the cooling holes plug, so the metal beneath runs hotter than designed, and the deposit narrows the passage the gas must squeeze through. Those are the two effects named.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage says the metal runs hotter than intended. It never says the blades melt or the shaft seizes.","Fuel appears in paragraph 4 only as what burns in the combustor, and the flame goes out because the airflow breaks down.","Wear on the compressor is not what paragraph 5 describes. The damage there is glass sticking to hot turbine parts.","The paragraph describes the opposite. A narrowed throat chokes the flow until the engine stops."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the weather radar on the 1982 flight showed nothing because","choices":["it had been switched off earlier in the flight to save electrical power","ash gives back a signal identical to heavy rain","it is built to detect water droplets and cannot see dry ash","the ash cloud was still below the airplane","the crew were watching the engine instruments instead"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"built to detect water droplets and is effectively blind to dry ash"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 explains that weather radar is designed to detect water droplets and is effectively blind to dry ash. The radar was working. It simply cannot see this hazard.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in paragraph 7 says the radar was off. The limitation described is what the radar can detect.","If ash looked like rain, the radar would have shown something. Paragraph 7 says it showed nothing at all.",null,"Paragraph 7 says the airplane entered the ash, so the cloud was not beneath it.","The crew noticed the glow and the smell. The radar limit is stated as a property of the radar, not of their attention."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Under the graded scheme in paragraph 15, flight through air carrying three milligrams of ash per cubic meter is","choices":["prohibited outright","treated exactly as ordinary operations","allowed only above a stated flight level","permitted with the manufacturer's agreement and added inspections","permitted only for aircraft fitted with one particular type of engine"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":5,"quote":"between two and four, flight permitted with the manufacturer's agreement and added inspections"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 sets ordinary operations below two milligrams per cubic meter and closure above four. Three falls in the middle band, where flight is allowed with the manufacturer's agreement and extra inspections.","distractorNotes":["Prohibition begins above four milligrams per cubic meter, and three is below that.","Ordinary operations apply below two milligrams per cubic meter, and three is above that.","The bands in paragraph 15 are set by concentration, not by altitude.",null,"No such restriction appears. The condition named is the manufacturer's agreement, not the engine model."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 2, \"quenched\" most nearly means","choices":["soaked in water so that it cools evenly all the way through","extinguished, the way a flame is put out by smothering","satisfied, the way a thirst is relieved by drinking","hardened by repeated cycles of heating and hammering","cooled so fast that a disordered structure is locked in"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"quenched into glass, a liquid structure frozen in place"}],"why":"In paragraph 2 the word sits between rapid cooling and a liquid structure frozen in place, so it means cooling so fast that the disorder of the melt is trapped rather than resolving into crystals.","distractorNotes":["Nothing soaks the fragments. Paragraph 2 says they cool within seconds after the melt shatters, and the result is glass, not something wetted.","This is the everyday sense. The paragraph is about cooling, and the passage insists nothing in ash is burning.","A real dictionary sense that has nothing to do with a sentence about atoms failing to reach a crystal lattice.","Repeated heating is the opposite of what happens. The fragment gets one very fast cooling and keeps what it leaves.",null]},{"id":"rc-p05-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 12, \"conservative\" most nearly means","choices":["careful with money and therefore unwilling to pay for engine testing","placing the whole burden of uncertainty on the restrictive side","resistant to new technology of any kind","cautious in a way that is always correct","traditional, in keeping with long established practice"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":8,"quote":"moved the whole weight of the uncertainty onto the side of not flying"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 defines the word inside the sentence: the rule moved the whole weight of the uncertainty onto the side of not flying. That is the engineering sense the author is naming.","distractorNotes":["The cost of testing is raised in paragraph 13, which is a separate point from what the word means here.",null,"Paragraph 12 is about how a safety margin was set, not about attitudes toward technology.","The paragraph separates caution from this word and argues the rule was not simply correct.","Long practice is not the sense here. The author supplies the meaning in the same sentence."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 6 most strongly supports which conclusion about ash damage to an engine?","choices":["Some of it can undo itself once the engine stops making heat","It is unlikely to occur if the engine is throttled back in advance","It is permanent, so a damaged engine must be replaced before flight continues","It comes mainly from abrasion rather than from melting","It grows worse the longer an engine is left to cool"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"A dead engine cools."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"the brittle layer cracks and flakes away in the airflow"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says a dead engine cools, the glass and metal contract at different rates, and the brittle layer cracks and flakes away so the engine can be relit. The damage partly reverses itself.","distractorNotes":[null,"Throttle setting is never discussed. Paragraph 6 describes what happens after the engines have already failed.","Engines were replaced after the 1989 flight, but paragraph 6 explains why a deposit can shed in flight and allow a relight.","Abrasion damages the windscreen in paragraphs 2 and 7. The engine damage in paragraphs 5 and 6 comes from melted glass.","Paragraph 6 says a cooling engine sheds its deposit, so cooling time helps rather than harms."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Which conclusion about advisory boundaries follows most directly from paragraph 10?","choices":["They are measured directly by radar and satellite at each flight level","They are drawn deliberately wider than the model output to add a safety margin","They are recalculated only after an eruption has ended","Particle size has no measurable effect on where the model sends the ash","A small error in column height can shift the forecast ash load sharply"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"mass goes as the fourth power of height"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"overstates the ash by nearly a factor of two and a half"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says mass goes as the fourth power of height, so a column overestimated by a quarter overstates the ash by nearly a factor of two and a half. An uncertain input becomes a much more uncertain output.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 10 says only the height is observed directly. The mass and the spread are modeled.","Widening for safety is not described in paragraph 10. The question of where the edge falls comes later, in paragraph 12.","The advisories in paragraph 9 look six, twelve and eighteen hours ahead, so they are issued while the eruption continues.","Particle size is listed in paragraph 10 as one of the source parameters the model requires.",null]},{"id":"rc-p05-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Volcanic ash is dangerous to aircraft chiefly because it is abrasive enough to blind a crew in flight","Aviation met an unmeasured hazard with a blanket ban until a costly closure forced measurement","Dispersion models are now accurate enough to make ash advisories reliable","The 1982 and 1989 failures show that flying near any volcano is unsafe","Iceland's volcanoes pose a uniquely severe threat to European air travel"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":8,"quote":"left the question of how much ash is too much permanently unasked"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"The closure achieved what two engine failures had not."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":8,"quote":"a threshold of zero is not the absence of a judgment about risk"}],"why":"The passage moves from what ash is, to what it does inside an engine, to a rule of avoid all ash written without data, to the 2010 closure that forced concentration numbers into existence. That arc is the point.","distractorNotes":["Abrasion is real in the passage, but it is one detail. The engine failures and the rule built on them carry the argument.",null,"Paragraph 10 stresses how sensitive the models are to an uncertain input, and paragraph 16 says the science became quantitative rather than certain.","The two flights are used to explain where the rule came from, not to argue that flight near volcanoes is unsafe.","Iceland is the case that forced the change, but nine centers cover the whole globe and the problem is framed as a global one."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q11","type":"function","stem":"The first paragraph serves mainly to","choices":["establish that ash clouds are visible to a crew well before they are entered","clear away an everyday association before the real material is described","introduce the two accidents that the rest of the passage explains","argue that pilots are less well informed than engineers about volcanoes","explain why ash is hard to detect from a satellite"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":1,"quote":"The word ash does more harm than good."},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"It invites a picture of the soft gray powder"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"will reason about it correctly"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 spends its whole length separating volcanic ash from the fireplace ash the word suggests, so that the reader stops picturing something burned and soft before paragraph 2 describes pulverized rock.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the opposite in paragraph 7, where the radar showed nothing and the encounter happened at night.",null,"The accidents arrive in paragraphs 7 and 8. The opening is about what ash is and is not.","The pilot and engineer sentences contrast two mental pictures of ash, not the competence of two professions.","Satellite detection is the subject of paragraph 11 and is not raised in the opening."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A list of eruptions arranged from earliest to most recent","A comparison of two competing explanations for the engine failures","A single problem stated at the start and then deliberately left unresolved at the end","A substance, then a mechanism, then a rule, then the event that revised it","A history of one regulatory body told through its nine centers"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"What a volcano ejects is magma torn apart by its own gas."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"Inside a jet engine the trouble starts with temperature."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"The rule built on all this was short"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":1,"quote":"Iceland ended that."}],"why":"Paragraphs 1 through 3 describe the material, 4 through 6 the mechanism inside an engine, 7 through 13 the accidents and the rule they produced, and 14 through 16 the eruption that forced the rule to be rewritten.","distractorNotes":["Eruptions do appear in date order, but they occupy a small part of a passage that moves from substance to mechanism to regulation.","Only one mechanism is offered anywhere: melted silicate glass freezing onto cooler turbine metal.","The closing paragraphs resolve the problem with graded thresholds and the idea of a dose.",null,"The nine centers occupy one paragraph. This is not an institutional history."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude in paragraph 12 toward the people who wrote the avoid all ash rule is best described as","choices":["openly scornful of their unwillingness to gather evidence","indifferent, since the rule has since been replaced","understanding of their position while critical of what the rule hid","admiring, with no reservations about the rule itself","puzzled that anyone would restrict flight over a hazard they could not see"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"The people who wrote it deserve sympathy."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"quietly makes a claim it cannot support"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says those people deserve sympathy and had a duty not to gamble with passengers, then argues in the same breath that the rule makes a claim it cannot support and leaves a question permanently unasked.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 states plainly that these people deserve sympathy and faced a real duty.","Indifference does not fit a paragraph that argues at length about what the rule left unasked.",null,"The sympathy is genuine, but the same paragraph says the rule quietly makes a claim it cannot support.","The author treats the hazard as real throughout and questions only the shape of the rule."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In the final paragraph, the author's attitude toward the 2010 closure is best described as","choices":["unwilling to call it a mistake, since the number needed was missing","firmly convinced that it was a clear overreaction by regulators who were too cautious","certain that it prevented a disaster that would otherwise have taken place","neutral, offering the reader no view of the episode at all","regretful that airlines were not consulted before the airspace was closed"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":7,"quote":"not that the 2010 closure was an overreaction"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":9,"quote":"had not been given the data to make it any other way"}],"why":"The last paragraph says directly that the lesson worth keeping is not that the closure was an overreaction, and then reframes a threshold of zero as a silent judgment made without data.","distractorNotes":[null,"The final paragraph rejects this reading in so many words.","The passage says no airplane was damaged because almost none flew. It does not claim a disaster was averted.","The author does take a position, calling a threshold of zero a judgment made silently.","Consultation is never raised. The gap the author names is data, not process."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage presents all of the following as limits on knowing where ash is and how much of it is there EXCEPT","choices":["Weather radar returns no signal from dry ash","A satellite cannot separate a column into layers","The mass leaving the vent is inferred rather than measured","Forecast winds are unavailable to the modeling centers","The satellite method fails when ash sits under ordinary cloud"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"into the winds of a weather forecast","choice":3},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"effectively blind to dry ash","choice":0},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"cannot resolve it into layers","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"The mass rate is usually inferred from height","choice":2},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"when ash lies beneath a meteorological cloud","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the model releases its particles into the winds of a weather forecast, so forecast winds are exactly what the centers do have. The other four limits appear in paragraphs 7, 10 and 11.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 7 gives this as the reason the radar showed nothing in 1982.","Paragraph 11 says a satellite looks down through a whole column and cannot resolve layers.","Paragraph 10 says the mass rate is inferred from column height rather than observed.",null,"Paragraph 11 lists ash beneath a meteorological cloud as a case where the method goes blind."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to find out how air taken from the compressor is used to keep turbine metal cool should look in which paragraph?","choices":["Paragraph 2","Paragraph 4","Paragraph 5","Paragraph 9","Paragraph 11"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"air bled from the compressor and blown out through hundreds of small holes"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 is the one that describes air bled from the compressor and blown out through hundreds of small holes in every vane and blade to hold the metal cooler than the gas.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 is about how ash forms and what it is made of.","Paragraph 4 gives the temperatures, but the cooling air and the holes appear one paragraph later.",null,"Paragraph 9 covers the advisory centers, not engine hardware.","Paragraph 11 is about satellite detection."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q17","type":"application","stem":"An airline weighs two routings past an eruption. One crosses a layer holding four milligrams of ash per cubic meter and spends five minutes inside it; the other stays in air holding one milligram per cubic meter for twenty minutes. The reasoning in paragraph 16 would most likely treat the two routes as","choices":["roughly equivalent, since each route delivers about the same dose to the engines","different in kind, since only the first enters a band needing the manufacturer's agreement","different in kind, since ash that thin never gets hot enough to melt onto the turbine","equivalent only if both are flown by day, when a crew can see and avoid the layer","impossible to compare until the column height behind the advisory has been verified"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"the concentration multiplied by the minutes spent inside it"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"are not different in kind"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 says what harms an engine is a dose, the concentration multiplied by the minutes spent inside it, so a brief crossing of a thick layer and a long cruise through a thin one are not different in kind. Four times five and one times twenty come to the same twenty, so the two routes carry about the same risk.","distractorNotes":[null,"The bands in paragraph 15 are the numbers, and paragraph 16 says the dose idea outlasts them. A dose counts minutes as well as concentration.","Paragraph 4 puts the combustor far above the softening point of ash, so whatever enters melts. A thinner layer changes the amount, not the physics.","Nothing in paragraph 16 ties the dose to daylight, and paragraphs 7 and 8 show a hazard that neither radar nor the crew saw coming.","Column height is a modeling uncertainty from paragraph 10. Here both exposures are already stated as a concentration and a time, which is all a dose needs."]}]},"rc-p06":{"id":"rc-p06","title":"Plaque as a Community, Not Debris","topic":"the oral microbiome and the biofilm model of dental caries","field":"microbiology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1376,"paragraphs":[["A few hours after brushing, a fingernail drawn along the gumline comes away with a soft, pale smear.","For most of the twentieth century, dentistry treated it as debris whose only interesting property was that it could be scraped off.","Dental plaque is not a pile of leftovers.","It is a community with architecture, and the disease it can produce is better understood as a change in local conditions than as an invasion."],["A biofilm is a population of microorganisms attached to a surface and held in a matrix that the cells themselves secrete.","In plaque that matrix is mostly polysaccharide, with protein and extracellular DNA woven through it.","It fixes cells at stable addresses and slows the movement of small molecules through the film.","It turns a smear of bacteria into a structure with an inside and an outside."],["Assembly follows a reliable order.","Within minutes of a cleaning, salivary proteins adsorb onto the enamel and form the acquired pellicle, a film a fraction of a micrometer thick.","The first colonizers, mostly oral streptococci along with Actinomyces species, do not bind bare enamel at all.","They bind receptors displayed on the pellicle.","Later arrivals bind poorly to pellicle and instead attach to cells already in place.","Fusobacterium nucleatum is the great connector, able to bind both early streptococci and late colonizers that bind almost nothing else."],["Sucrose holds a special place in this system.","Bacterial enzymes called glucosyltransferases split the sugar and polymerize its glucose into glucans, sticky branched polymers that make up much of the matrix.","The reaction needs no cellular energy, because the energy is already stored in the bond joining the two halves of sucrose.","Glucans glue cells to the tooth and to one another.","A sucrose-rich diet therefore does two things at once: it feeds acid production, and it builds the scaffolding that holds the acid producers against the enamel."],["Because the matrix slows diffusion, a film far thinner than a millimeter develops steep internal gradients.","Oxygen is consumed near the outer surface, leaving the depths anaerobic and hospitable to organisms that could not survive in saliva.","Acid made in those depths is not immediately washed away.","Microelectrodes show that the pH inside plaque can sit more than a full unit below the pH of the saliva a millimeter away.","The consequence is the central fact of the model: the tooth surface does not experience the chemistry of the mouth but that of the film pressed against it."],["Once plaque was seen as a community, the next question was which members matter.","The specific plaque hypothesis, dominant from the 1970s onward, answered that only a few do, and named Streptococcus mutans and the lactobacilli.","The case was reasonable.","Both are strongly acidogenic, making acid quickly from sugar, and aciduric, still growing at pH values that stall their neighbors."],["The evidence never quite cooperated.","Lesions appear in mouths from which mutans streptococci cannot be recovered, and plenty of decay-free mouths carry them in abundance.","When sequencing replaced culturing, other capable acid producers turned up in active lesions, among them bifidobacteria and the yeast Candida albicans.","Streptococcus mutans remains a competent troublemaker and a serviceable marker of risk, but it proved neither necessary nor sufficient.","A model resting on one organism cannot absorb that many exceptions."],["The ecological plaque hypothesis offered a different account.","Its claim is that local conditions decide which species dominate a site, and that caries follows a shift in those conditions rather than the arrival of a villain.","Frequent fermentable carbohydrate means frequent acid, and the acid is itself the selective pressure.","Species that tolerate low pH keep growing while acid-sensitive neighbors decline or are crowded out.","The community is plastic, and its makeup at any moment is a record of the conditions that have been acting on it."],["What is lost in that shift matters as much as what is gained.","Several acid-sensitive residents are actively protective.","Some run the arginine deiminase system or produce urease, pathways that release ammonia and push local pH upward.","Others generate hydrogen peroxide that holds competitors in check.","A sustained acid challenge therefore removes brakes at the same time that it adds accelerators, which is part of why the shift tends to hold."],["After sugar, plaque pH falls within minutes to a minimum of roughly 4.5 to 5.5, then climbs back toward a resting value near 6.7 as saliva buffers and clears the acid, a recovery that commonly takes twenty to sixty minutes.","Enamel begins to dissolve below a critical pH near 5.5.","Exposed root dentin, which carries less mineral, begins to dissolve closer to 6.2.","That curve explains why frequency outranks quantity.","Sweets eaten at one sitting drive the pH down once.","The same sweets nibbled across an afternoon restart the descent repeatedly and hold the film under the threshold for hours."],["Underneath the microbiology sits a plain chemistry.","Enamel is about ninety-six percent mineral by weight, a form of hydroxyapatite.","When plaque fluid falls below the critical pH it is undersaturated with respect to that crystal, and mineral dissolves out of the surface.","When pH recovers and the fluid is again supersaturated, calcium and phosphate move back in.","Neither direction is an event.","Both run all day, in every mouth, and what decides the fate of a tooth is the budget, the running difference between the two.","An early lesion, the chalky white spot, is subsurface mineral loss beneath an intact surface layer, and while that layer holds, the loss can be repaid."],["Saliva is the other half of the ledger.","Its bicarbonate and phosphate buffer acid, and its flow clears sugar.","It carries calcium and phosphate at levels that keep it supersaturated with respect to enamel, while statherin and proline-rich proteins keep that fluid from precipitating mineral where none is wanted.","When flow fails, through medication, radiation to the head and neck, or autoimmune disease of the glands, decay can become rapid and widespread in a person whose diet and bacteria have not changed."],["Fluoride was long explained as something built into enamel during development; the part of the story that carries the most weight happens at the crystal surface, largely after the tooth has erupted.","When fluoride is present in the fluid around a crystal during recovery, it is taken up as new mineral forms, producing a fluoridated apatite that does not dissolve until roughly pH 4.5.","Lowering the threshold by about a unit shortens the part of each acid cycle in which the crystal loses ground, and fluoride also speeds repair.","What the chemistry recommends is a low concentration present often."],["All of this bears on the common phrase that tooth decay is an infection a person catches.","The phrase is not entirely wrong.","Strains do move between people, and caregiver-to-infant transmission is well documented.","But the organisms involved are ordinary residents of nearly every human mouth, and their mere presence predicts very little.","Calling it an infection aims treatment at eradication, and no one can sterilize a mouth, nor would clearing out the resident community be desirable, since much of it competes with the acid makers and buffers their acid.","The more defensible statement is that the cast is already assembled, and that diet, saliva, and fluoride decide which members prosper."],["The ecological account should not be oversold in its turn.","It describes a mechanism convincingly and still predicts an individual's next five years poorly.","Enamel structure, salivary flow, genetics, fluoride exposure, and the community a person started with all contribute, and no risk assessment yet separates high from low risk with confidence.","The model has earned its standing less by settling questions than by changing which questions look worth asking."],["Those questions now shape practice.","If a lesion is a balance that has tipped rather than a hole, the earliest lesions are candidates for repair rather than removal, and arresting one counts as a result.","Advice shifts from scrubbing harder toward reducing how often the film is fed, keeping fluoride present, and protecting saliva.","The soft film that returns by evening is not failure but the ordinary state of a warm wet surface that is never sterile.","The useful question is not how to abolish it, but what it is being given, and how often."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p06-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the first bacteria to settle on a freshly cleaned tooth attach to","choices":["glucans left behind from earlier sucrose exposure","receptors displayed on a film of adsorbed salivary proteins","strands of extracellular DNA within the plaque matrix","bare enamel crystal uncovered by the cleaning","the surfaces of Fusobacterium nucleatum cells already in place"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"do not bind bare enamel at all"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"They bind receptors displayed on the pellicle."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the first colonizers do not bind bare enamel at all. They bind receptors displayed on the acquired pellicle, the film of salivary proteins that adsorbs within minutes of a cleaning.","distractorNotes":["Glucans are built later from dietary sucrose and are matrix material, not the surface the first colonizers land on.",null,"Extracellular DNA is listed in paragraph 2 as part of the matrix, not as the attachment site for the first colonizers.","The passage says the opposite in the same sentence: those species do not bind bare enamel, which is why the pellicle matters.","That reverses the order in paragraph 3: later arrivals bind cells already in place, and Fusobacterium nucleatum is the connector between early and late groups."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that exposed root dentin begins to lose mineral","choices":["only once plaque pH drops below about 4.5","near pH 6.7, the resting value of plaque","at a pH above the threshold for enamel","only where salivary flow has already failed","at the same pH as enamel, since both are apatite"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"begins to dissolve closer to 6.2"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"below a critical pH near 5.5"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives two thresholds: enamel near pH 5.5 and exposed root dentin closer to 6.2. Since 6.2 is the higher number, root dentin starts dissolving earlier in the pH drop.","distractorNotes":["About 4.5 is the figure from paragraph 13 for fluoridated apatite, not for root dentin.","6.7 is the resting pH plaque climbs back toward, not a point at which mineral is lost.",null,"Paragraph 12 links failed flow to rapid decay, but paragraph 10 sets the root dentin threshold without any such condition.","The passage gives root dentin a different threshold and explains it by its lower mineral content."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, bacteria can build glucans without spending cellular energy because","choices":["the glucose is taken from proteins already in the pellicle","saliva supplies the phosphate the reaction consumes","the bond joining sucrose already holds the needed energy","glucosyltransferases work only in the anaerobic depths of the film","the acid released during the reaction drives the polymer forward"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"the energy is already stored in the bond joining the two halves of sucrose"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 states it plainly: the reaction needs no cellular energy because the energy is already stored in the bond that joins the two halves of sucrose.","distractorNotes":["The pellicle is a protein film; paragraph 4 says the glucose comes from sucrose.","Salivary phosphate appears in paragraph 12 in connection with buffering and mineral supply, not with glucan synthesis.",null,"Anaerobic depths come from paragraph 5 and describe oxygen gradients, not the energetics of glucan building.","Acid is a separate product of sugar fermentation, and the passage never makes it the driver of glucan formation."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q04","type":"detail","stem":"Which of the following does the passage describe as a way resident bacteria push the local pH upward?","choices":["releasing ammonia through urease or arginine deiminase","generating hydrogen peroxide","polymerizing glucose from sucrose into branched glucans","consuming oxygen in the outer layer of the film","adsorbing fluoride onto the enamel crystal surface"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"pathways that release ammonia and push local pH upward"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 names the arginine deiminase system and urease as pathways that release ammonia and push local pH upward.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 9 does credit some residents with hydrogen peroxide, but its stated job is holding competitors in check, not raising pH.","Glucan building is described in paragraph 4 as matrix construction, with no effect on pH given.","Oxygen consumption in paragraph 5 creates anaerobic depths; the passage ties it to which species can live there, not to pH.","Fluoride uptake belongs to paragraph 13 and is chemistry at the crystal surface rather than something the bacteria do."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage states that mineral containing fluoride resists dissolving until the pH falls to roughly","choices":["4.5","5.0","5.5","6.2","6.7"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"does not dissolve until roughly pH 4.5"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says fluoride taken up during repair produces a fluoridated apatite that does not dissolve until roughly pH 4.5, which shortens the part of each acid cycle that costs mineral.","distractorNotes":[null,"5.0 sits inside the range plaque pH dips to after sugar, but the passage never uses it as a dissolution threshold.","5.5 is the threshold for ordinary enamel, the number fluoride improves on.","6.2 is the threshold paragraph 10 gives for exposed root dentin.","6.7 is the resting pH plaque returns to after a sugar exposure, from paragraph 10."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 8, \"plastic\" most nearly means","choices":["able to be molded by hand into a chosen shape","made of a synthetic polymer","fixed in form once it has assembled","reshaped by the conditions acting on it","artificial rather than naturally occurring"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"The community is plastic"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"a record of the conditions that have been acting on it"}],"why":"The sentence right after the word explains it: the community's makeup at any moment is a record of the conditions that have been acting on it. The word points to something shaped by its surroundings.","distractorNotes":["This keeps the molding idea but adds someone doing the molding, while the passage credits the local conditions.","The literal material sense does not fit; the matrix described in paragraph 2 is a polysaccharide the cells make themselves.","This is the reverse of the point, since the whole paragraph describes a membership that changes as conditions change.",null,"That is a common dictionary sense of the word, but nothing in paragraph 8 contrasts natural with artificial."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 11, \"budget\" most nearly means","choices":["a fixed limit on how much mineral a tooth is allowed to lose","a schedule setting when demineralization happens","the total mineral a tooth carries when it erupts","the running net balance between two opposing processes","an allowance of sugar a person can take in each day"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"the budget, the running difference between the two"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says mineral loss and mineral return both run all day, and then calls the budget the running difference between the two. It is a net balance, not a cap or a plan.","distractorNotes":["A cap implies some allowance being enforced, and the passage describes a running total instead.","The passage explicitly says neither direction is an event, which rules out a set schedule.","The starting amount of mineral is not what the word tracks; the sentence is about the difference between loss and return over time.",null,"The passage does discuss how often sugar arrives in paragraph 10, but the word here labels a mineral balance, not a diet limit."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The description of gradients in paragraph 5 most strongly supports which conclusion?","choices":["Oxygen is absent from every layer of a mature plaque film","The species inside plaque are spread evenly through the film","Plaque must grow thicker than a millimeter before enamel is at risk","Saliva loses the ability to buffer acid once a biofilm has formed","A saliva pH reading can understate the acid at the tooth surface"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"more than a full unit below the pH of the saliva a millimeter away"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"the tooth surface does not experience the chemistry of the mouth"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 reports that pH inside plaque can sit more than a full unit below the saliva a millimeter away, and it closes by saying the tooth meets the chemistry of the film rather than of the mouth. A saliva reading would therefore look better than conditions at the surface.","distractorNotes":["Oxygen is consumed near the outer surface, which leaves the depths anaerobic; the passage does not empty the whole film of oxygen.","Gradients are the point of the paragraph, and they mean the film is not uniform.","The paragraph says the gradients form in a film far thinner than a millimeter, so thickness of that scale is not required.","Paragraphs 10 and 12 both show saliva buffering and clearing acid even though a film is present.",null]},{"id":"rc-p06-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that an approach aimed at wiping out as much of the plaque community as possible would risk","choices":["leaving enamel permanently unable to take mineral back up","converting sucrose into a form bacteria cannot use","stopping the pellicle from forming after a cleaning","removing residents that raise pH and check acid makers","raising the critical pH of enamel above 6.2"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"nor would clearing out the resident community be desirable"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"removes brakes at the same time that it adds accelerators"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 describes acid-sensitive residents that release ammonia and hydrogen peroxide, and paragraph 14 says clearing out the resident community would not be desirable because much of it competes with the acid makers and buffers their acid.","distractorNotes":["Remineralization in paragraph 11 depends on pH and on calcium and phosphate in the fluid, not on which bacteria are present.","Nothing in the passage connects removing bacteria to changing the chemistry of sucrose itself.","The pellicle is made of salivary proteins and forms within minutes of any cleaning, so bacteria are not required for it.",null,"Critical pH is a property of the mineral in paragraph 11, and 6.2 belongs to root dentin rather than enamel."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Decay reflects a shift in a resident community, not the arrival of a pathogen","Streptococcus mutans is the one organism responsible for nearly all tooth decay","Brushing the film away is the most effective defense a person has against decay","Salivary flow by itself decides whether a given tooth will decay","Fluoride has made the study of oral bacteria largely unnecessary in dentistry"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"a change in local conditions than as an invasion"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"rather than the arrival of a villain"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":6,"quote":"diet, saliva, and fluoride decide which members prosper"}],"why":"The passage moves from plaque as a structured community, through the failure of a single-organism explanation, to the ecological account and the running mineral balance it implies. The closing paragraphs make that reframing explicit.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 7 shows this claim failing, calling the organism neither necessary nor sufficient.","Paragraph 16 shifts advice away from scrubbing harder and toward how often the film is fed.","Saliva is one contributor among several, and paragraph 15 lists others that matter as well.","Fluoride gets one paragraph as surface chemistry, and the passage never sets it against studying the community."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 7 functions primarily to","choices":["recommend that every patient be tested for Candida albicans","argue that culturing gives more reliable results than sequencing","identify the organism most useful for judging a person's risk","explain how the matrix keeps acid pressed against the enamel","show why an explanation built on one organism could not hold"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The evidence never quite cooperated."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"A model resting on one organism cannot absorb that many exceptions."}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lists findings the earlier model could not absorb: lesions without mutans streptococci, decay-free mouths full of them, and other acid producers found by sequencing. It closes by saying a model resting on one organism cannot carry that many exceptions.","distractorNotes":["Candida albicans appears only in a list of acid producers, and the passage makes no testing recommendation.","The passage credits sequencing with revealing what culturing missed, which is the reverse of this claim.","The paragraph does call the organism a serviceable marker, but that is a concession made in passing, not the work the paragraph does.","That is the job of paragraph 5, not paragraph 7.",null]},{"id":"rc-p06-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how paragraphs 6 through 8 are arranged?","choices":["A chemical mechanism is laid out and then applied to one patient's history","A clinical recommendation is given and then supported with laboratory measurements","A popular belief is stated and then shown to be entirely false","Two rival explanations are described and then combined into a single model","An earlier explanation is presented, tested against evidence, and replaced"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"The specific plaque hypothesis, dominant from the 1970s onward"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The evidence never quite cooperated."},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"The ecological plaque hypothesis offered a different account."}],"why":"Paragraph 6 presents the specific plaque hypothesis and grants that its case was reasonable, paragraph 7 sets the evidence against it, and paragraph 8 supplies the ecological account in its place.","distractorNotes":["The passage never follows an individual patient.","No recommendation appears in these paragraphs; advice waits until paragraph 16.","Too strong: the passage keeps Streptococcus mutans as a real troublemaker and a serviceable marker of risk.","The ecological account replaces the earlier one rather than merging with it.",null]},{"id":"rc-p06-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the specific plaque hypothesis is best described as","choices":["fair to its reasoning yet unconvinced","dismissive of it as careless thinking","alarmed that it still directs most treatment","eager to see it restored to favor","indifferent to whether it was ever accepted"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"The case was reasonable."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"it proved neither necessary nor sufficient"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the case was reasonable and lays out the evidence behind it, and paragraph 7 then explains calmly why that case does not cover the findings. The author gives the idea its due and still sets it aside.","distractorNotes":[null,"Too harsh: the passage says outright that the case was reasonable.","No claim is made that this model still governs treatment; paragraph 16 describes practice shaped by the newer account.","The passage moves past this model rather than arguing for its return.","The author spends two full paragraphs weighing the idea, which is not indifference."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 15, the author's stance toward the ecological account is best described as","choices":["neutral, since the paragraph makes no judgment either way","supportive but careful not to overstate what it can do","impatient with researchers who keep studying saliva","doubtful that it captures any real mechanism","confident that it now predicts an individual's risk accurately"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"should not be oversold in its turn"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"describes a mechanism convincingly and still predicts an individual's next five years poorly"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 opens by warning against overselling the account, grants that it describes a mechanism convincingly, notes that it predicts an individual's next five years poorly, and credits it with changing which questions look worth asking.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph offers a clear judgment: valuable, and not yet predictive.",null,"Saliva appears in the list of contributors, and nothing in the paragraph criticizes studying it.","The same paragraph says the account describes a mechanism convincingly, so doubt about the mechanism is the wrong read.","This is the claim the paragraph explicitly denies."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements about saliva EXCEPT:","choices":["It contains proteins that keep mineral from precipitating where none is wanted.","It neutralizes plaque acid about as fast as bacteria can produce it.","Its flow clears sugar from the mouth.","It carries calcium and phosphate at levels that keep it supersaturated with respect to enamel.","Its bicarbonate and phosphate buffer acid."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"a recovery that commonly takes twenty to sixty minutes","choice":1},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"Its bicarbonate and phosphate buffer acid","choice":4},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"its flow clears sugar","choice":2},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"keep it supersaturated with respect to enamel","choice":3},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"keep that fluid from precipitating mineral where none is wanted","choice":0}],"why":"Paragraph 12 supports the first four points directly. The fifth runs against the passage: paragraph 5 says acid made in the depths of the film is not immediately washed away, and paragraph 10 gives recovery times of twenty to sixty minutes.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 credits statherin and proline-rich proteins with exactly this, so it is supported.",null,"Paragraph 12 states this, so it is supported and not the exception.","Paragraph 12 states this, so it is supported and not the exception.","Paragraph 12 states this, so it is supported and not the exception."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for why sucrose, more than sugar in general, helps hold the film against the tooth should turn to","choices":["paragraph 4","paragraph 3","paragraph 10","paragraph 2","paragraph 13"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"Sucrose holds a special place in this system."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"builds the scaffolding that holds the acid producers against the enamel"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 is the one that singles out sucrose, explaining how glucosyltransferases turn it into glucans that glue cells to the tooth and to one another.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 3 covers the order in which species arrive, not what diet contributes.","Paragraph 10 follows pH after a sugar exposure, which is about acid rather than attachment.","Paragraph 2 defines a biofilm and its matrix in general terms and never names a dietary sugar.","Paragraph 13 is about fluoride at the crystal surface."]}]},"rc-p07":{"id":"rc-p07","title":"What Dissolving Carbon Does To Shells","topic":"ocean acidification and calcifying organisms","field":"ocean chemistry","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1367,"paragraphs":[["Roughly a quarter of the carbon dioxide human activity has released into the atmosphere crossed the sea surface and dissolved.","That transfer is usually described as a favor, the ocean slowing the warming of the air, but the ocean does the favor by chemistry, and the chemistry does not leave the water unchanged."],["Carbon dioxide dissolves in seawater, and a small fraction combines with water to form carbonic acid.","Carbonic acid is weak and promptly sheds a hydrogen ion, leaving bicarbonate.","A much smaller fraction of that bicarbonate sheds a second hydrogen ion and becomes carbonate.","In pure water that would simply make the water more acidic; in seawater the part that matters begins one step later."],["Seawater already carries a standing stock of carbonate ions, and carbonate scavenges hydrogen eagerly: nearly every hydrogen ion that dissolving carbon dioxide releases is intercepted by a carbonate ion, and the two combine into bicarbonate.","Total dissolved inorganic carbon goes up.","Bicarbonate goes up sharply.","Hydrogen ion concentration goes up slightly, which is what a falling pH records.","And carbonate ion concentration goes down.","Adding carbon to seawater subtracts carbonate from it, and that inversion is the engine of the whole problem."],["Carbonate matters because many marine organisms build their hard parts out of it.","A calcifier draws a calcium ion and a carbonate ion out of solution and lays the pair down as calcium carbonate.","Scarcer carbonate means a crystal that once assembled readily must be coaxed into place."],["Chemists summarize that difficulty with one number, the saturation state, written as omega.","It compares the actual product of calcium and carbonate concentrations against the product at equilibrium with solid calcium carbonate.","Where omega exceeds one the water is supersaturated and a crystal tends to grow; below one it is undersaturated and an unprotected crystal tends to dissolve.","Calcium is abundant and varies little, so omega rises and falls with carbonate."],["Deeper water is colder, under greater pressure, and loaded with carbon dioxide from decomposing matter, so omega falls with depth.","The depth at which it passes through one is the saturation horizon.","Above that boundary shells are thermodynamically safe; below it only living tissue keeps a shell intact.","In the North Atlantic, whose deep water sank recently and carries little respired carbon, the horizon lies thousands of meters down.","In the North Pacific, whose deep water is the oldest in the world ocean and has spent centuries collecting respired carbon dioxide, it can sit within a few hundred meters of the surface."],["That boundary has been moving in one direction.","As atmospheric carbon dioxide works into the upper ocean, the depth at which omega falls below one shoals, migrating upward toward the light.","In parts of the North Pacific the change has been measured in tens of meters over a few decades.","Where seasonal upwelling drags deep water onto a continental shelf, water corrosive to shell arrives in the sunlit shallows where larvae are trying to build one."],["Calcium carbonate is also not a single material: two crystal forms dominate marine biology, calcite and aragonite.","They share an identical chemical formula and differ in the internal geometry of the crystal, which sets the solubility.","Aragonite is roughly half again as soluble as calcite in seawater, so in any water column the aragonite saturation horizon sits shallower than the calcite horizon.","An organism's choice of crystal decides which portion of the ocean is hospitable to it at all."],["Reef building corals lay down aragonite.","So do pteropods, small swimming snails eaten by everything from herring to whales.","Coccolithophores, single celled algae, build calcite, as do the foraminifera whose shells accumulate on the seafloor.","A third form, calcite with magnesium substituted into part of the crystal, is more soluble still than aragonite, and it is what coralline algae and sea urchins use."],["Here the most common phrase in public discussion does damage.","The ocean is repeatedly said to be turning to acid.","It is not.","Surface seawater has a pH near 8.1, comfortably on the alkaline side of neutral, and it has fallen from roughly 8.2 since the industrial era began.","No plausible emissions trajectory takes the open ocean below 7.","Acidification names a direction of travel, not a destination, the way a bath cooling from scalding to hot is cooling.","The imprecision is not harmless: a reader who pictures vinegar and later learns the truth may throw out the subject entirely.","But the correction is no reprieve.","Shells do not wait for pH 7.","They answer to a saturation state that can cross below one while the water is still alkaline."],["Nor do organisms sit passively.","Most calcifiers wall off a small internal space, a calcifying fluid held between tissue and skeleton, and pump hydrogen ions out of it, holding its pH well above the pH outside.","These mechanisms are genuinely effective.","Laboratory populations of some corals, coccolithophores and crustaceans have held calcification rates steady under carbon dioxide far beyond what the coming century should deliver, and a few have raised them."],["Such results are sometimes reported as evidence that the concern was overblown.","They are better read as evidence that calcification has a price and that the price is climbing.","Pumping protons against a steeper gradient is expensive, and the energy must come from somewhere in an animal that is also feeding, growing, defending itself and reproducing.","The trade shows up in quieter measurements: shells of normal size but lower density, larvae slower to settle, adults that keep their skeletons and release fewer eggs.","A Pacific Northwest oyster hatchery can lose most of a batch of larvae when upwelled, undersaturated water enters its intake, not because they dissolve but because they cannot build fast enough when they must."],["The picture is uneven rather than uniform.","Response varies between species, between populations of one species, and between seasons.","Seagrass meadows and kelp forests draw down carbon dioxide during daylight and can lift the local saturation state enough to shelter the animals among them.","Estuaries swing across a wider chemical range in a day than the open ocean is projected to cross in a century, and the organisms there are built for the ride.","None of this contradicts the chemistry; it means the chemistry is a boundary condition ecology plays out against, not a script it has to read."],["To see what happens when that boundary condition shifts quickly, look to an interval about 56 million years ago known as the Paleocene Eocene Thermal Maximum.","A large quantity of carbon entered the ocean and atmosphere, the planet warmed, and the seafloor recorded the consequence with unusual clarity.","In deep sea sediment cores the pale carbonate ooze that had piled up for millions of years gives way abruptly to a band of brown clay, then returns over tens of thousands of years.","Carbonate did not stop being produced.","It dissolved on the seafloor as the saturation horizon rose through the sediment, and a substantial fraction of deep dwelling foraminifera species went extinct."],["The event is instructive, and it is also asked with some regularity to carry more weight than it can bear.","It shows that the carbonate system responds to a large carbon injection as theory predicts, and that recovery is slow.","What it does not show is the present rate.","Estimates put that ancient release at several thousand years, against roughly two centuries for the modern one, and rate is nearly the whole question, because the ocean's slow buffers only help if given time.","Nor can the record resolve a decade: sediment accumulates slowly and burrowing animals stir it, so a fifty year event can smear into invisibility.","The pH values come from proxies such as boron isotopes in fossil shells, which carry real uncertainty."],["What survives the caveats is a narrower claim than the headlines make and a firmer one.","The chemistry is not in dispute: carbon dioxide entering seawater raises dissolved carbon and lowers carbonate, and it is doing so faster than anything the record can resolve.","What remains open is the biology: which organisms will pay the climbing price out of reserves they can spare, which out of growth and reproduction they cannot, and which will simply move."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p07-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, aragonite differs from calcite in that aragonite","choices":["carries magnesium substituted into part of its crystal","has a chemical formula unlike that of calcite","dissolves more readily in seawater than calcite does","is the material of coccolithophore plates and foraminifera shells","stays saturated to a greater depth in the water column"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"roughly half again as soluble as calcite in seawater"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 makes the whole comparison turn on solubility. The two forms share a formula, differ in the internal geometry of the crystal, and aragonite is roughly half again as soluble in seawater.","distractorNotes":["That describes the third form the passage names later, magnesium bearing calcite, not aragonite.","The passage says the two share an identical chemical formula and differ only in crystal geometry.",null,"Those are the calcite builders the passage lists, which is the opposite grouping.","This reverses the result: because aragonite is more soluble, its saturation horizon sits shallower, not deeper."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage attributes the shallow saturation horizon of the North Pacific mainly to","choices":["the unusually high pressure of that particular ocean basin","the long residence of its deep water, which has collected respired carbon dioxide","the seasonal upwelling that drags deep water up onto the continental shelf each summer","the number of aragonite building organisms living in its surface water","a calcium concentration much lower there than in other basins"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"has spent centuries collecting respired carbon dioxide"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 contrasts the two basins directly. North Pacific deep water is the oldest in the world ocean and has spent centuries collecting carbon dioxide from decomposition, so its saturation horizon can sit within a few hundred meters of the surface.","distractorNotes":["Pressure rises with depth everywhere, so it cannot explain why one basin differs from another.",null,"Upwelling is what carries that water onto a shelf, not what made the water corrosive in the first place.","The passage never connects how many calcifiers live above a spot to how deep its horizon lies.","The passage says calcium is abundant and varies little, which is why omega tracks carbonate instead."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage names which organisms as builders of the magnesium bearing form of calcite?","choices":["reef building corals","pteropods, the small swimming snails of polar water","coccolithophores","foraminifera","coralline algae and sea urchins"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"it is what coralline algae and sea urchins use"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 closes by naming a third crystal form, calcite with magnesium substituted into part of it, and says coralline algae and sea urchins are the organisms that use it.","distractorNotes":["Corals are listed as aragonite builders at the start of that paragraph.","Pteropods are grouped with corals as aragonite builders.","Coccolithophores are given as builders of ordinary calcite.","Foraminifera are also placed in the ordinary calcite group.",null]},{"id":"rc-p07-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, deep sea sediment cores record the ancient warm interval as","choices":["a thickening of the pale carbonate ooze","an abrupt band of brown clay set between layers of carbonate ooze","a dense layer of intact foraminifera shells","a slow darkening spread evenly across many millions of years of accumulated sediment","a gap in which no sediment accumulated at all"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"gives way abruptly to a band of brown clay"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 describes the core signature plainly. Pale carbonate ooze that had piled up for millions of years gives way abruptly to brown clay, then returns over the following tens of thousands of years, because the carbonate was dissolving on the seafloor.","distractorNotes":["This reverses what happened: the ooze stops and clay takes its place.",null,"The passage says the opposite, that a substantial fraction of deep dwelling foraminifera species went extinct.","The passage calls the change abrupt, with the recovery, not the onset, taking tens of thousands of years.","Sediment kept accumulating. What changed is that the carbonate in it dissolved."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage gives the pH of present day surface seawater as","choices":["slightly below 7","exactly 7, the neutral point","about 7.5, down from roughly 8 over the last century","near 8.1, down from roughly 8.2","near 8.2 and essentially unchanged"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"a pH near 8.1"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"it has fallen from roughly 8.2"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives both numbers. Surface seawater sits near 8.1, down from roughly 8.2 since the industrial era began, which keeps it on the alkaline side of neutral.","distractorNotes":["That would put seawater on the acid side, which the passage says no plausible trajectory reaches.","Neutral is exactly what the passage says surface seawater is not.","Both numbers are wrong and they overstate the shift the passage actually reports.",null,"The passage reports a drop of about a tenth of a unit, so the value has not held steady."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, \"shoals\" most nearly means","choices":["gathers into dense groups","becomes sandy and shallow, like a sandbar","splits apart into separate layers","slows to a gradual crawl","moves nearer to the surface"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"shoals, migrating upward toward the light"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 is tracking a depth. The boundary is migrating upward toward the light, measured in tens of meters over a few decades, so the word means climbing toward the surface.","distractorNotes":["That is the fish sense of the word, and nothing in the sentence concerns animals grouping together.","A shoal as a sandbar is a different sense, and the sentence is about a chemical boundary, not sediment.","The passage describes one boundary moving, not a boundary dividing in two.","The sentence measures distance, tens of meters, rather than any rate of motion being slow.",null]},{"id":"rc-p07-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 12, \"expensive\" most nearly means","choices":["scarce and hard to obtain","demanding of the animal's energy","costly in terms of money and trade","elaborate in its design","damaging to nearby tissue"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"the energy must come from somewhere"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 defines the word in the very next clause: the energy must come from somewhere in an animal that is also feeding, growing, defending itself and reproducing. The cost is metabolic.","distractorNotes":["Scarcity in this passage belongs to carbonate in the water, not to the pumping the word describes.",null,"The monetary sense is the dictionary one, but the sentence is about where an animal's energy comes from.","The passage is not commenting on how intricate the mechanism is.","The passage never says the pumping harms tissue, only that it draws on a limited energy supply."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which statement about a parcel of seawater that has taken up extra carbon dioxide?","choices":["It holds more dissolved carbon overall yet less of the ion a shell builder needs","It holds less dissolved carbon, because the carbon departs as bicarbonate","It holds more carbonate than before, since carbonate is one of the products of the reaction","It has turned acidic and will dissolve any shell that is placed into it","Its calcium concentration has fallen in step with its carbonate concentration"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"Total dissolved inorganic carbon goes up."},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"carbonate ion concentration goes down"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"many marine organisms build their hard parts out of it"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 walks the bookkeeping through one line at a time. Total dissolved inorganic carbon rises and bicarbonate rises sharply, while carbonate, the ion a calcifier draws on, goes down.","distractorNotes":[null,"Total dissolved inorganic carbon goes up in that paragraph, and bicarbonate is where most of it ends up rather than leaving.","Carbonate is consumed in the passage's account, because it mops up the freed hydrogen ions.","The water stays alkaline, and whether a shell dissolves depends on saturation state rather than on the water being acidic.","The passage says calcium is abundant in seawater and varies little."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q09","type":"inference","stem":"A species is found in the laboratory to hold its shell growth rate unchanged under high carbon dioxide. Based on the passage, the author would most likely treat that result as","choices":["proof that the species is untouched by the change in seawater chemistry","probably a measurement error, given what the chemistry predicts","leaving open what the species gave up to hold that rate","evidence that the species has permanently adapted to any future condition","a result that will hold equally for every species sharing its habitat"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"calcification has a price and that the price is climbing"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"shells of normal size but lower density"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 handles exactly this case. The author accepts steady laboratory rates and reads them as a cost met somewhere else, showing up in shell density, larval timing, or eggs released.","distractorNotes":["The author treats a steady rate as a sign that a cost is being met elsewhere, not as a sign of no effect.","The author accepts these laboratory results and calls the mechanisms behind them genuinely effective.",null,"The passage frames a steady rate as a cost being paid now, out of energy the animal needs elsewhere, not as a permanent adaptation to whatever comes.","The passage says response varies between species and even between populations of one species."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Added carbon dioxide lowers the carbonate shell builders need, a settled chemical result whose biological effects remain open","The ocean is on course to become acidic enough that marine shells will dissolve outright in open water within a century","Marine calcifiers have evolved mechanisms that will carry them through the change without meaningful cost to growth or reproduction","The record of an ancient warming interval shows in fine detail what the coming century will do to the ocean and its marine life","Concern about seawater chemistry has been exaggerated, largely by the imprecise language that reporters and scientists use in public"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"Adding carbon to seawater subtracts carbonate from it"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"raises dissolved carbon and lowers carbonate"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"What remains open is the biology"}],"why":"The passage runs one chemical result, less carbonate, out through organisms, depth, popular wording, coping mechanisms and the fossil record. The closing paragraph states the balance directly: the chemistry is not in dispute, the biology is what remains open.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage denies this outright, saying no plausible trajectory takes the open ocean below 7 and tying shell trouble to saturation state instead.","Coping mechanisms are described, but the passage insists they carry a cost that is climbing.","The passage says the ancient record cannot show what happens at the present rate.","The author does criticize the phrasing, then refuses to let the correction count as reassurance."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author's main purpose in the paragraph that takes up the phrase about acid is to","choices":["show that popular reporting on seawater chemistry is on the whole trustworthy and precise","argue that pH is a misleading way to describe any chemical change in a solution","correct a common misstatement while denying that the correction is cause for relief","establish that the open ocean will eventually fall below neutral on the pH scale","explain why a cooling bath and a warming ocean behave in much the same way"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"It is not."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":8,"quote":"But the correction is no reprieve."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 spends half its length correcting the slogan and the other half refusing the comfort that correction might offer, closing with shells answering to a saturation state while the water is still alkaline.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph faults the popular phrasing rather than defending it.","The author uses pH values freely and objects only to one careless slogan.",null,"The passage states the opposite, that no plausible emissions trajectory takes the open ocean below 7.","The bath appears only as an analogy for direction of travel, not as a claim about baths."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"How does the paragraph on the energy cost of calcification relate to the paragraph on internal chemical control that comes just before it?","choices":["It supplies the laboratory evidence for the mechanism just described","It grants the evidence and rereads what that evidence means","It disputes the accuracy of the measurements just reported","It shifts the subject from living organisms to the fossil record","It restates the earlier point using a different set of organisms"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"sometimes reported as evidence that the concern was overblown"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"They are better read as evidence"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 reports that some corals, coccolithophores and crustaceans hold calcification rates steady. Paragraph 12 does not deny those results, it rereads them as evidence that calcification has a price and that the price is climbing.","distractorNotes":["The laboratory evidence sits in the earlier paragraph, and the later one reads it differently.",null,"The author accepts the measurements and questions only the conclusion drawn from them.","The fossil record does not enter the passage until two paragraphs later.","The later paragraph adds a new claim about cost rather than repeating the old one."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the claim that the ocean is turning to acid is best described as","choices":["accepting it as a simplification that serves the public well","dismissing the whole subject as overstated","indifferent to how the chemistry gets described","alarmed that the phrase badly understates the danger","correcting the wording but insisting the worry stands"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"It is not."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":8,"quote":"But the correction is no reprieve."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":9,"quote":"Shells do not wait for pH 7."}],"why":"In paragraph 10 the author calls the phrase false and calls the imprecision harmful, then immediately says the correction is no reprieve. Both halves of that move belong to the attitude.","distractorNotes":["The author calls the imprecision harmful rather than useful.","The author rejects the slogan yet explicitly refuses to treat the correction as a reprieve.","The author gives a full paragraph to why the wording matters, which is not indifference.","The objection is that the phrase overstates what is happening to pH, not that it understates it.",null]},{"id":"rc-p07-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In the discussion of the ancient warming interval, the author's attitude toward that record as evidence is best described as","choices":["appreciative of what it shows and wary of how far it is stretched","convinced that it settles the question of how fast the present change is moving","skeptical that an event of that kind occurred in the way described","impatient with the scientists who spend their careers reading sediment cores","confident that modern proxies have removed what uncertainty once remained"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"The event is instructive"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"asked with some regularity to carry more weight than it can bear"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"What it does not show is the present rate."}],"why":"Paragraph 15 opens by calling the event instructive and also asked to carry more weight than it can bear, then lists what it cannot show: the present rate, a single decade, and pH values free of proxy uncertainty.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage says the opposite, that what the record does not show is the present rate.","The author treats the event as well recorded and genuinely instructive.","The author draws on that work respectfully and questions only how it gets used.","The passage says the proxy values carry real uncertainty."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Dissolved calcium in seawater changes far less than dissolved carbonate does","A shell can be at risk in water that is still alkaline by ordinary measures","Open ocean pH is expected to fall below neutral within the coming century","Sediment cores cannot resolve a change that lasts only a few decades","Some organisms raise the pH of an internal fluid in order to build their skeletons"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"Calcium is abundant and varies little","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":10,"quote":"can cross below one while the water is still alkaline","choice":1},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":5,"quote":"a fifty year event can smear into invisibility","choice":3},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"holding its pH well above the pH outside","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"No plausible emissions trajectory takes the open ocean below 7.","choice":2}],"why":"Paragraph 10 states the opposite of this choice: no plausible emissions trajectory takes the open ocean below 7. Each of the other four traces back to a specific line elsewhere in the passage.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 says calcium is abundant and varies little, which is why omega tracks carbonate.","Paragraph 10 makes exactly this point when it says shells do not wait for pH 7.",null,"Paragraph 15 says a fifty year event can smear into invisibility in a core.","Paragraph 11 describes calcifiers pumping hydrogen ions out of a calcifying fluid to hold its pH up."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q16","type":"detail","stem":"In which paragraph does the passage describe habitats whose surrounding water chemistry departs from the open ocean pattern?","choices":["paragraph 6","paragraph 9","paragraph 11","paragraph 13","paragraph 15"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"can lift the local saturation state enough to shelter the animals"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"Estuaries swing across a wider chemical range in a day"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 is the one about local settings. Seagrass meadows and kelp forests lift the saturation state around them during daylight, and estuaries swing across a wider chemical range in a day than the open ocean is projected to cross in a century.","distractorNotes":["That paragraph maps how saturation changes with depth and between ocean basins, not between habitats.","That paragraph sorts organisms by which crystal form they build.","That paragraph covers chemistry an organism controls inside itself, not the water around it.",null,"That paragraph weighs what the geological record can and cannot show."]}]},"rc-p08":{"id":"rc-p08","title":"Sleep and Waste Clearance in the Brain","topic":"sleep and brain waste clearance","field":"neuroscience","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1374,"paragraphs":[["Every organ faces the same housekeeping problem: cells burn fuel, discard proteins, and leave behind molecules with no further purpose.","In most of the body the lymphatic system handles it, thin vessels that return leaked fluid to the blood.","The brain is the conspicuous exception.","No lymphatic vessels run through the tissue itself, yet the brain is the most metabolically expensive organ a person owns, consuming roughly a fifth of the body's energy while making up about a fiftieth of its mass.","Something has to take out the trash."],["Cerebrospinal fluid is made mainly by the choroid plexus, a ribbon of tissue inside the ventricles.","That fluid fills the ventricles and bathes the outer surface of the brain and cord, but it does not obviously reach the inside.","Between the cells lies a maze of gaps tens of nanometers wide, and for most of the twentieth century anything crossing it was assumed to move by diffusion alone."],["A route to the interior had long been visible.","When an artery dives from the surface into the brain, it carries a sleeve of the surface space with it, so the vessel travels inside a channel of its own.","That channel's outer wall is built from astrocytes, glial cells whose flattened endfeet tile the vessel surface almost completely.","Studded into the endfoot membrane facing the vessel is aquaporin-4, a channel protein that speeds water across the astrocyte.","The circuit proposed from this anatomy runs as follows: cerebrospinal fluid driven inward along the sleeves around arteries, pushed through the tissue, and collected along the channels around veins, carrying dissolved waste out."],["That the idea waited until this century to be tested is a matter of method, not imagination.","Nearly everything known about the space between brain cells came from electron microscopy, which requires tissue to be fixed, dehydrated, and sliced.","Fixation collapses those spaces, and the earliest measurements accordingly put the extracellular fraction near zero.","A hardened slab cannot show fluid moving either, because in a dead brain none is.","Two methods changed that.","The first, real-time iontophoresis, releases a charged tracer from a pipette tip and records its arrival at a second tip nearby, and the shape of that arrival curve reports how much open space the tracer had to cross and how twisted its path was.","The second, two-photon microscopy, sends infrared light through a window in the skull and follows fluorescent tracers in a living animal whose heart is beating and whose brain waves are being recorded."],["The result, when it came, was arresting.","In mice, extracellular space made up about 14 percent of brain volume during quiet waking and about 23 percent during natural sleep, an increase of roughly 60 percent.","Tracer in the cerebrospinal fluid spread into sleeping brains far more readily than waking ones, and labeled amyloid-beta, the protein fragment that builds up in Alzheimer's disease, cleared about twice as fast.","Waking mice given drugs that block adrenergic receptors showed much of the same expansion, which pointed at norepinephrine, the arousal signal broadcast by a small brainstem nucleus called the locus coeruleus, as a candidate controller of the space."],["Aquaporin-4 supplied the molecular hook.","Mice lacking the channel took up less tracer and cleared labeled protein more slowly, and in aged or injured brains the channel drifts off the endfoot membrane and scatters over the cell.","A story with a named protein, a named cell, a named transmitter, and a named disease is a story that travels."],["Human work followed the same arc.","Magnetic resonance imaging of sleeping volunteers has captured slow pulses of cerebrospinal fluid surging into the fourth ventricle during deep non-rapid-eye-movement sleep, each locked to a slow electrical wave in the cortex and to a dip in blood volume.","Keeping people awake for a single night raises the concentration of amyloid-beta and of tau in their cerebrospinal fluid the next morning.","And poor sleep, tracked over years, is associated with a higher risk of dementia."],["From there the account escaped into public speech in a compressed form: the brain washes itself at night, a rinse cycle.","The image is vivid, memorable, and further ahead of the evidence than most of those repeating it realize."],["Begin with what the central experiments measure.","A tracer gets farther into a sleeping brain than a waking one in the same interval.","That is a difference in transport, and transport is not the same thing as flow.","A tracer can outrun diffusion along a channel without any net movement of fluid: an artery that widens and narrows with every heartbeat sloshes the fluid around it back and forth, and that sloshing mixes a tracer outward even though the fluid ends each cycle where it began.","Calculations of what it would take to push fluid in bulk through the dense extracellular mesh return pressure differences larger than any measured in a living brain."],["Then there is the anesthesia.","Much of the evidence compares waking animals with anesthetized ones, on the premise that a drug producing slow waves is a fair stand-in for sleep.","It is not one.","Anesthetics change heart rate, blood pressure, breathing, body temperature, and vessel diameter, and each of those can move a tracer on its own.","Slow waves are something sleep and certain anesthetics happen to share, not proof of a shared mechanism."],["The tracer is a third complication.","Getting a labeled molecule into the cerebrospinal fluid usually means injecting liquid into a closed skull, which raises the pressure inside.","Varying the injected volume and speed changes the result, as one would expect if part of the signal came from the needle."],["Replication has been uneven in a way a settled finding would not be.","Independent laboratories have both confirmed and failed to find the dependence on aquaporin-4, and the disagreement traces partly to mouse strain, anesthetic, and what counted as clearance.","At least one group, measuring a dye's disappearance rather than its entry, reported clearance slower during sleep and anesthesia than during waking.","That disagreement is live."],["The last stretch of the popular story is the longest leap.","Suppose sleep really does speed the removal of amyloid-beta.","The inference that poor sleep therefore causes Alzheimer's disease still needs the arrow to point one way, and the disease can point it the other.","Alzheimer's pathology reaches the cell groups that build sleep early, often years before memory fails, so broken sleep in a person later diagnosed may be a symptom rather than a cause.","Rising amyloid after a sleepless night fits more of the protein being made during the extra hours awake just as well as less of it being removed."],["None of this makes the underlying observation wrong, and it is worth being exact about what stands.","That the extracellular space changes size with behavioral state is a real measurement, made with more than one method, and a genuinely surprising one.","What has not been shown is the part the metaphor supplies for free: a directed, pressure-driven current that sweeps waste out of the brain and whose failure produces disease."],["Saying what would settle a question is a better test of a hypothesis than listing what supports it.","Three measurements would do most of the work.","The first is a direct reading of fluid speed and direction in a perivascular channel of an awake, undisturbed animal, with nothing injected, fine enough to tell a steady current from sloshing that averages to zero.","The second is an intervention that changes perivascular transport and nothing else, leaving sleep, blood pressure, breathing, and vessel tone untouched, then watching whether protein builds up.","The third is a measurement in people: the disappearance rate of one labeled protein across a night of sleep and a matched day of waking in the same person."],["Until those exist, the honest version of the finding is narrower than the headline and more interesting than it sounds.","The brain's fluid economy has a daily rhythm nobody had documented twenty years ago, tied to the arousal chemistry that governs attention, and what it accomplishes is an open question.","The appetite for a tidy story is the real hazard here.","Sleep does a great many things for a brain, and it does not need a rinse cycle to earn its place."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p08-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the brain accounts for roughly what share of the body's energy use?","choices":["About one fiftieth","About one twentieth","About one tenth","About one fifth","About one half"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"consuming roughly a fifth of the body's energy"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 gives two numbers side by side: the brain uses roughly a fifth of the body's energy while making up about a fiftieth of its mass. The question asks about energy, so a fifth is the one to take.","distractorNotes":["That is the share of body mass the passage gives the brain, not its share of energy.","No such figure appears, and it is smaller than the share the passage actually states.","This is in the neighborhood of the real answer but not the number given, which is one fifth.",null,"The passage never puts the brain's energy use anywhere near half of the body's total."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage describes the increase in extracellular space between quiet waking and sleep in mice as roughly","choices":["twofold","23 percent","14 percent","9 percent","60 percent"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"an increase of roughly 60 percent"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 reports about 14 percent during quiet waking and about 23 percent during sleep, then names the change directly as an increase of roughly 60 percent.","distractorNotes":["Twofold is how much faster labeled amyloid-beta cleared from a sleeping brain, not how much the space grew.","That is the size of the space during sleep, not the size of the change.","That is the size of the space during quiet waking, not the size of the change.","This is the arithmetic gap between the two percentages, but the passage reports the change as a percentage of the waking value.",null]},{"id":"rc-p08-q03","type":"detail","stem":"Which structure does the passage name as the source of the arousal signal implicated in setting the size of the extracellular space?","choices":["The choroid plexus in the ventricles","The fourth ventricle","A small brainstem nucleus","The astrocyte endfeet","The arachnoid membrane"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"a small brainstem nucleus called the locus coeruleus"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 names norepinephrine, broadcast across the brain by a small brainstem nucleus called the locus coeruleus, as a candidate controller of the space itself.","distractorNotes":["The choroid plexus makes cerebrospinal fluid, which is paragraph 2, and it is never described as releasing an arousal signal.","The fourth ventricle is where slow fluid pulses were imaged in sleeping people, not a source of norepinephrine.",null,"Endfeet build the wall of the perivascular channel and carry aquaporin-4; they do not broadcast the signal.","The passage never mentions an arachnoid membrane, and nothing at the brain surface is described as sending the signal."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q04","type":"detail","stem":"In the imaging of sleeping volunteers, each slow pulse of cerebrospinal fluid coincided with","choices":["a rise in body temperature","a burst of rapid eye movement","a rise in heart rate","a dip in blood volume","an injection of tracer into the skull"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"and to a dip in blood volume"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says each pulse into the fourth ventricle was locked to a slow electrical wave in the cortex and to a dip in blood volume.","distractorNotes":["Body temperature appears in paragraph 10 as something anesthetics change, not as part of the human imaging result.","The pulses were recorded during deep non-rapid-eye-movement sleep, which is the opposite of what this choice names.","Heart rate is listed in paragraph 10 among the variables anesthetics change; the human result names blood volume.",null,"Injected tracer is a complication of the animal work in paragraph 11, not a feature of the human imaging."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the earliest measurements of the space between brain cells were misleading because","choices":["the tracer molecules used were too large to enter the gaps","chemical fixation collapsed the spaces being measured","the animals studied were anesthetized rather than asleep","the samples came only from aged brains","the microscope could not detect fluorescent labels"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"Fixation collapses those spaces"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 explains that electron microscopy requires fixing the tissue, that fixation collapses those spaces, and that early measurements therefore put the extracellular fraction near zero.","distractorNotes":["Tracer size is never raised as the problem; the trouble was what preparing the tissue did to it.",null,"Anesthesia is a separate objection, raised in paragraph 10 about much later experiments.","Aged brains come up in paragraph 6, where aquaporin-4 shifts position, not as the source of the early error.","Fluorescent labels belong to the two-photon method described afterward, not to the early electron-microscope work."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 5, \"arresting\" most nearly means","choices":["striking","halting","preliminary","disputed","reassuring"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"The result, when it came, was arresting."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"a genuinely surprising one"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 uses the word to introduce a set of numbers the author plainly finds remarkable, so the sense is striking rather than anything to do with stopping.","distractorNotes":[null,"This is the everyday sense of arrest, but nothing in the sentence stops or is stopped.","The paragraph reports finished measurements, not a first look.","Disagreement arrives much later in the passage; here the author is describing how the result landed.","The result is offered as surprising, not as comforting."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, \"a story that travels\" most nearly describes a story that","choices":["moves physically from one tissue to another","becomes harder to test as time passes","changes shape every time it is retold","spreads easily and gets repeated widely","applies just as well to other organs"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"is a story that travels"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"the account escaped into public speech in a compressed form"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 lists what the account has going for it, a named protein, a named cell, a named transmitter, and a named disease, and then says it travels, meaning it catches on and gets repeated.","distractorNotes":["The sentence is about the account of aquaporin-4, not about anything moving through tissue.","Testability is the worry of later paragraphs; this sentence is about how readily the story caught on.","Distortion in retelling is a reasonable guess, but the sentence stresses the appealing parts that carry the story along.",null,"Nothing in the paragraph extends the account beyond the brain."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 9 suggests that a tracer reaching deeper into a sleeping brain is compatible with which of the following?","choices":["Fluid that oscillates with the heartbeat and returns to where it began","Fluid that has stopped moving entirely during sleep","A tracer that binds to the walls of the channel","A pressure difference across the tissue larger than any yet measured in a living brain","Diffusion running at exactly the rate seen in waking"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"the fluid ends each cycle where it began"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 explains that a pulsating artery makes the fluid around it slosh back and forth, and that sloshing mixes a tracer outward even though the fluid ends each cycle where it began. Deeper spread therefore does not require net flow.","distractorNotes":[null,"If nothing moved, the tracer would not get farther in; the paragraph offers back-and-forth motion, not stillness.","Binding is never mentioned, and a tracer stuck to a wall would spread less, not more.","The paragraph says pressures that large have not been measured, which argues against bulk flow rather than supporting the observation.","The point of the paragraph is that the tracer outruns plain diffusion, so an unchanged diffusion rate cannot account for it."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage's treatment of amyloid-beta levels after a sleepless night implies that measuring a concentration by itself cannot separate","choices":["one protein from another in the same fluid","sleep from anesthesia as a brain state","how much is produced from how much is removed","cortical waves from brainstem activity","a measurement in mice from a measurement in people"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"fits more of the protein being made during the extra hours awake"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"just as well as less of it being removed"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says a rise after a sleepless night fits more of the protein being made during the extra hours awake just as well as less of it being removed, so the number alone does not settle which is happening.","distractorNotes":["The passage names both amyloid-beta and tau, but the ambiguity it raises is about where a change in amount comes from.","That confusion is real and appears in paragraph 10, but it is a different problem from what a concentration can show.",null,"No such contrast is drawn anywhere in the passage.","Species differences are not what this paragraph is weighing."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Sleep clears waste from the brain, and disrupted sleep therefore causes Alzheimer's disease","Electron microscopy is an unreliable way to study any living tissue","A real finding about sleep and brain fluid has been stretched into a mechanism it has not demonstrated","The brain makes up for its lack of lymphatic vessels through the channels that run around its arteries and veins","Popular writing about science cannot be trusted to describe laboratory work"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"further ahead of the evidence than most of those repeating it realize"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"What has not been shown is the part the metaphor supplies for free"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"narrower than the headline"}],"why":"The passage builds the case for the finding in paragraphs 1 through 7, works through the objections in paragraphs 9 through 13, marks off what stands in paragraph 14, and then names what would settle the rest.","distractorNotes":["This is the compressed public version the passage sets out to qualify, not the author's own position.","Paragraph 4 makes a narrower version of this point in passing, and it serves the larger argument rather than being it.",null,"This is the proposal under examination, and the passage spends most of its length on how much of it has actually been shown.","The author objects to one particular overstatement, not to science writing in general."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 4 is included mainly in order to","choices":["show that electron microscopy has no remaining use in biology","argue that the two techniques it describes give identical results","establish that the extracellular space is far larger than anyone had expected it to be","introduce the tracer injection problem that paragraph 11 develops","explain why the proposal could not be tested until living tissue could be watched"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"a matter of method, not imagination"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"because in a dead brain none is"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 opens by saying the delay was a matter of method rather than imagination, then shows why fixed tissue could not answer the question and what two live-tissue techniques made possible.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph criticizes what fixation does to fluid spaces, not the method as a whole.","The two are described as measuring different things, one the open space and the path, the other tracer movement in a living animal.","The measured sizes arrive in paragraph 5; this paragraph is about how measurement became possible at all.","Injection artifacts are a separate objection raised later, not the work this paragraph is doing.",null]},{"id":"rc-p08-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A history of one laboratory, told in the order in which its experiments were run","Two rival theories compared point by point throughout","A definition of terms followed by one extended example","A case built for a proposal, then objections to it, then what would decide it","A popular claim refuted, with a replacement theory offered in its place"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"The circuit proposed from this anatomy runs as follows"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"Begin with what the central experiments measure."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"Saying what would settle a question is a better test"}],"why":"Paragraphs 1 through 7 assemble the case, paragraphs 9 through 13 raise the objections one at a time, paragraph 14 separates what stands from what does not, and paragraph 15 names three measurements that would settle it.","distractorNotes":["No laboratory or researcher is named anywhere, and the order is logical rather than chronological.","Only one proposal is under examination, and nothing is set up as its rival.","Terms are defined along the way, but the passage never settles into a single example.",null,"The author questions how far the claim has been carried and offers no competing mechanism."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the rinse cycle image in paragraph 8 is best described as","choices":["contempt for the researchers who produced the underlying data","wariness that it claims more than the evidence supports","confidence that it captures the finding accurately","indifference to how the public describes science","regret that the finding was ever published"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"further ahead of the evidence than most of those repeating it realize"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 grants that the image is vivid and memorable, then says it runs further ahead of the evidence than most of those repeating it realize. That is caution about the phrasing, not hostility toward the work.","distractorNotes":["The author treats the underlying research respectfully; the complaint is aimed at the compressed retelling.",null,"The paragraph ends by saying the image gets ahead of the evidence.","The author cares enough about the framing to spend most of the passage correcting it.","Paragraph 14 calls the observation real and surprising, so there is no regret about the work itself."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 14, the author's attitude toward the measured change in the size of the extracellular space is best described as","choices":["treating it as solid and genuinely surprising","dismissing it as an artifact of anesthesia","accepting it only if the link to disease holds up","regarding the change as too small to matter","withholding judgment until other laboratories confirm it"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"a real measurement, made with more than one method, and a genuinely surprising one"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 calls the state-dependent change a real measurement, made with more than one method, and a genuinely surprising one, while reserving the author's doubt for the directed current the metaphor adds.","distractorNotes":[null,"Anesthesia is a complaint about certain comparisons in paragraph 10, and the author does not apply it to this measurement.","The author keeps the two apart: this measurement stands on its own, and the disease claim is the part not shown.","The reported change is roughly 60 percent, and the author calls the result arresting.","The paragraph says the measurement has already been made with more than one method."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Aquaporin-4 sits in the astrocyte membrane that faces the vessel","No lymphatic vessels run through brain tissue itself","Drugs that block adrenergic receptors put waking mice to sleep","A single night without sleep raises amyloid-beta in the cerebrospinal fluid","Two-photon microscopy can follow tracers in an animal whose heart is beating"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Studded into the endfoot membrane facing the vessel is aquaporin-4","choice":0},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"No lymphatic vessels run through the tissue itself","choice":1},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"raises the concentration of amyloid-beta","choice":3},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":7,"quote":"a living animal whose heart is beating","choice":4},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"showed much of the same expansion","choice":2}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says those drugs produced much of the same expansion of the extracellular space in waking mice, which is not the same as making the animals sleep. The other four statements are stated outright in paragraphs 1, 3, 4, and 7.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 places aquaporin-4 in the endfoot membrane facing the vessel.","Paragraph 1 states this as the brain's peculiarity.",null,"Paragraph 7 reports this for people kept awake a single night.","Paragraph 4 describes the method working in a living animal with a beating heart."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for the passage's explanation of how the arrival curve of a charged tracer reveals the amount of open space would turn to","choices":["paragraph 2","paragraph 4","paragraph 6","paragraph 9","paragraph 15"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"that arrival curve reports how much open space the tracer had to cross"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 describes real-time iontophoresis, where a charged tracer is released from one pipette tip and detected at a second, and says the shape of that arrival curve reports the open space and how twisted the path was.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 covers where cerebrospinal fluid is made and where it goes, not how the space is measured.",null,"Paragraph 6 is about aquaporin-4 and why the account caught on.","Paragraph 9 discusses what tracer movement does and does not prove, not the measuring technique.","Paragraph 15 lists measurements that have not been made yet."]}]},"rc-p09":{"id":"rc-p09","title":"Intercalation and the Runaway Cell","topic":"lithium ion batteries and thermal runaway","field":"electrochemistry","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1377,"paragraphs":[["Cut away the steel can of a spent cell and almost nothing inside looks like a source of power.","Out comes a tight spiral of foil, two coated metal ribbons with a sheet of white plastic between them, damp with a clear organic liquid."],["Every rechargeable battery that came before worked by taking metal apart and putting it back together.","In a lead acid cell the active material turns to lead sulfate on discharge and back again on charge; a nickel cadmium cell does much the same with cadmium.","The first rechargeable lithium cells pushed the idea to its limit with a sheet of lithium metal as the negative electrode."],["Plating is untidy: metal does not return as a smooth layer but builds up wherever the surface already bulges, and each cycle leaves the surface rougher.","With lithium the roughness grows into whiskers and mossy growths called dendrites.","Some keep going until they touch the opposite electrode, shorting a sealed can of lithium metal and flammable solvent."],["The idea that rescued the technology was to stop making and unmaking metal: in an intercalation electrode lithium arrives as an ion, slots into a gap waiting inside a crystal, and later leaves the same way.","The crystal is called a host, not anything living but a rigid framework that takes in guest ions between its layers and gives them back without being consumed.","Graphite, its carbon sheets stacked like the pages of a closed book, is the host on the negative side and a layered metal oxide on the positive.","The cell runs by rocking lithium from one host to the other and back."],["A fully charged graphite electrode holds one lithium for every six carbon atoms, and that much lithium swells the graphite by roughly a tenth of its volume.","A common positive electrode is a layered oxide of nickel, manganese, and cobalt; another is lithium iron phosphate.","A cell built from graphite and a layered oxide rests near 3.7 volts and is charged to about 4.2.","The powders are spread onto thin foils, copper on the negative side and aluminum on the positive."],["None of this settles what the lithium travels through.","Water is out of the question.","Every solvent has a voltage window, the span between the voltage at which it begins to break down at one electrode and the voltage at which it breaks down at the other, and the window for water is a little over one volt wide.","A lithium ion cell runs at nearly four.","So the electrolyte is a lithium salt, usually lithium hexafluorophosphate, dissolved in organic carbonate solvents that tolerate high voltage but are flammable."],["A carbonate electrolyte should not survive charged graphite either, and strictly speaking it does not.","During the first charge, performed at the factory, a thin skin of decomposition products builds up on the graphite surface.","This layer, the solid electrolyte interphase, conducts lithium ions but not electrons, so once it covers the surface the reaction that built it shuts itself off.","Forming it permanently locks away a few percent of the cell's lithium.","The layer is nanometers thick, chemically fragile, and the first thing to give way when a cell gets hot."],["The white film is the separator.","It must keep the electrodes apart without obstructing the ions, so it is a porous polyolefin membrane, about forty percent empty space by volume.","Many are built as three bonded layers, polypropylene outside and polyethylene in the middle, so that the polyethylene melts near 130 degrees Celsius and its pores close, choking off ion flow, while the polypropylene holds the sheet together for another thirty degrees."],["Intercalation was supposed to retire dendrites, and in ordinary use it does.","But intercalation has a speed limit.","Ions must not only reach the graphite but work their way in, which is slow when the cell is cold, when the current is high, and when the graphite is nearly full.","If ions arrive faster than the graphite can take them in, they accept an electron at the surface instead and plate out as lithium metal after all.","Given enough of those mossy deposits, some will reach across."],["What follows is a well characterized sequence.","Somewhere between roughly 80 and 120 degrees Celsius the solid electrolyte interphase begins to break down, exposing charged graphite to liquid electrolyte and giving off heat.","Near 130 degrees the separator's shutdown layer closes its pores, which helps only if the cell can now cool faster than it warms.","If it cannot, the separator itself melts and shrinks back from the edges above about 160 degrees, and the electrodes meet across an area far larger than any dendrite could bridge.","The internal short dumps the cell's stored charge as heat in seconds."],["Above roughly 200 degrees the positive electrode starts to come apart, and a layered oxide that comes apart gives off oxygen.","The cell now holds fuel, an oxidizer, and a heat source, and no longer needs the room's air to burn.","Pressure climbs until a scored disc in the can lets go and the cell vents a jet of flammable gas: hydrogen, carbon monoxide, hydrocarbons, vaporized solvent, and hydrogen fluoride from the fluorine bearing salt.","Crews flood such fires mainly to cool the neighboring cells."],["The word runaway is doing precise work there.","Heat production climbs steeply with temperature, while heat loss grows only in proportion to how much hotter the cell is than the room.","Below a certain temperature removal wins and a warm cell cools off.","Above it production wins, every degree of warming buys further warming, and nothing done from outside can reach the reaction."],["It is tempting to read that cascade as a malfunction, and popular coverage usually does.","The framing is misleading.","Every step is the same chemistry the cell performs on purpose, running with nothing left to hold it back.","A charged cell is by design a metastable arrangement, a strong reducing agent and a strong oxidizing agent parked tens of micrometers apart and kept from reacting by a plastic film and a few nanometers of surface chemistry.","Engineers do not claim to have removed the hazard.","They claim something more modest, that they have made its onset rare and its spread slow."],["The familiar complaint, that these cells are dangerous because they store so much energy, is only half right.","A good lithium ion cell holds something like 250 to 300 watt hours per kilogram, far more than lead acid, and that figure is why the technology displaced everything else.","The stored electrical energy is not even the largest term: when a cell burns, combustion of its electrolyte and electrodes releases several times more energy than the charge it held.","Density and severity are two readings of a single property."],["One response is a less energetic chemistry: lithium iron phosphate binds its oxygen far more tightly than a layered oxide does, so the onset of runaway sits at a markedly higher temperature and such cells are genuinely harder to set off.","They also sit at a lower voltage and store less energy for their mass, which in a vehicle means a shorter range or a heavier pack.","The safer chemistry is not simply the better one."],["Most of the remaining work happens at the pack.","A management system watches each group of cells and refuses to charge a cold one quickly, because cold and fast is exactly when plating begins.","Cells are spaced apart, wrapped in mats that swell and insulate when heated, and seated on cooling plates, and vents are aimed so that hot gas leaves through the floor of a vehicle rather than into the cabin.","Each costs volume and mass that could have carried more cells, so every firebreak is paid for out of the range the chemistry was chosen to deliver."],["The newest packs bond cells directly into the structure and delete the module walls between them, recovering volume and removing a firebreak at a stroke.","Designers accept that trade only when a test that sets one cell alight shows its neighbors stay cool.","The goal is narrower than the public version of it: not a cell that cannot burn, but a pack in which one cell's bad day stays one cell's bad day."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p09-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, taking lithium into graphite changes the graphite's volume in which way?","choices":["It swells by roughly a tenth.","It shrinks by roughly a tenth.","It swells by about half.","It roughly doubles.","It stays exactly the same, which is the whole point of intercalation."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"swells the graphite by roughly a tenth of its volume"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 gives the figure outright: one lithium for every six carbons at full charge, and that much lithium swells the graphite by roughly a tenth of its volume.","distractorNotes":[null,"Right size, wrong direction. Lithium moving in swells the graphite rather than shrinking it.","Half is the wrong number. Paragraph 5 says roughly a tenth.","Too extreme. Nothing in the passage describes a volume change anywhere near that large.","Intercalation does keep the framework intact, but the passage still reports a real swelling of about a tenth."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says lead acid cells and nickel cadmium cells share which feature?","choices":["They use a lithium salt dissolved in carbonate solvents.","They hold lithium ions between stacked carbon sheets.","They shut down when a plastic membrane melts near 130 degrees Celsius.","They depend on a thin surface layer that conducts ions but blocks electrons.","Their active material is broken down on discharge and rebuilt on charge."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"taking metal apart and putting it back together"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"turns to lead sulfate on discharge and back again on charge"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 groups the older chemistries by one habit. The electrode material is chemically taken apart as the cell discharges and put back together as it charges.","distractorNotes":["A carbonate electrolyte belongs to the lithium ion cell described in paragraph 6.","That is intercalation, the later idea introduced in paragraph 4, not how these older cells work.","The melting membrane is the separator of a lithium ion cell in paragraph 8.","The surface layer is the solid electrolyte interphase of paragraph 7, part of the lithium ion design.",null]},{"id":"rc-p09-q03","type":"detail","stem":"In the three layer separator the passage describes, the polyethylene layer is responsible for","choices":["keeping the membrane about forty percent empty by volume","closing its pores near 130 degrees and choking off ion flow","raising the temperature at which the positive electrode gives off oxygen","carrying electrons between the two electrodes","holding the sheet together for about thirty degrees more"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"the polyethylene melts near 130 degrees Celsius and its pores close, choking off ion flow"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 splits the two jobs. The polyethylene in the middle melts near 130 degrees Celsius so its pores close and ion flow stops, while the outer polypropylene keeps the sheet intact longer.","distractorNotes":["Forty percent empty space describes the membrane as a whole, not something one layer contributes.",null,"Oxygen release above roughly 200 degrees is a property of the positive electrode in paragraph 11, not of the separator.","A separator exists to keep the electrodes from touching, so carrying electrons between them is exactly what it prevents.","That is the polypropylene's role, which holds on for roughly another thirty degrees."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, forming the solid electrolyte interphase costs the cell","choices":["a few percent of its lithium, permanently","the ability to use a carbonate electrolyte after the first charge","roughly a tenth of its volume","about forty percent of its internal space","most of its capacity to take charge in the cold"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"permanently locks away a few percent of the cell's lithium"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the layer builds during the factory's first charge and that making it permanently locks away a few percent of the cell's lithium.","distractorNotes":[null,"The interphase is what lets the carbonate electrolyte keep working, so it does not retire it.","A tenth of the volume is graphite's swelling in paragraph 5, a different number in a different place.","Forty percent is the separator's empty space in paragraph 8.","Trouble charging in the cold comes from intercalation's speed limit in paragraph 9, not from forming this layer."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that once the separator melts and pulls back from the edges, what happens next is that","choices":["a scored disc opens and the cell vents","the pores close and ion flow stops","charged graphite is exposed to liquid electrolyte for the very first time","the electrodes meet across a much larger area","the positive electrode begins giving off oxygen"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"the electrodes meet across an area far larger than any dendrite could bridge"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 runs the steps in order. The shutdown layer closes near 130 degrees, and if the cell keeps warming the separator itself melts above about 160, letting the electrodes meet over an area no dendrite could match.","distractorNotes":["Venting is driven by rising pressure in paragraph 11, after the internal short has already dumped the cell's charge.","That is the earlier step near 130 degrees, and it is the opposite effect.","Charged graphite meets electrolyte first, when the interphase breaks down between about 80 and 120 degrees.",null,"Oxygen release comes later, above roughly 200 degrees, in paragraph 11."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 4, \"host\" most nearly means","choices":["a large number of something","an organism that shelters another organism","a framework that holds ions without being consumed","a liquid in which a salt is dissolved","an electrode made of metal that gives up its electrons readily"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"a rigid framework that takes in guest ions"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"gives them back without being consumed"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 defines the word on the spot: not anything living, but a rigid framework that takes in guest ions between its layers and gives them back without being used up.","distractorNotes":["A real dictionary sense of the word, but the passage is naming a crystal, not a quantity.","The passage rules this sense out in the same sentence where it introduces the word.",null,"That describes the carbonate solvent of paragraph 6, not the crystal.","That describes the lithium metal electrode of paragraph 2, which is exactly what intercalation replaced."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, \"window\" most nearly means","choices":["a pore in the separator that ions pass through","the gap between two stacked sheets of carbon","the voltage range a solvent can survive","an opening in the steel can that relieves pressure","a short stretch of time in which something is possible"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"Every solvent has a voltage window"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"the span between the voltage at which it begins to break down"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 builds the meaning as it uses the term: the span between the voltage where a solvent starts breaking down at one electrode and where it breaks down at the other. Water's span is a little over a volt and the cell needs nearly four.","distractorNotes":["The separator's pores appear in paragraph 8 and are never called a window.","The space between carbon sheets is where lithium sits in paragraph 4.",null,"That is the scored vent disc of paragraph 11.","The everyday sense of the word, but this paragraph is measuring volts rather than minutes."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly suggests that charging a cold cell at a high current is risky because","choices":["the interphase thickens and locks away more of the cell's lithium","lithium plates onto the graphite instead of entering it","the separator's pores close before the cell is full","the electrolyte freezes and stops carrying ions","the positive electrode starts releasing oxygen at low temperature"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"plate out as lithium metal after all"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"cold and fast is exactly when plating begins"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 supplies the mechanism. Working into graphite is slow when the cell is cold and slow when the current is high, and ions that arrive faster than the graphite can take them in accept an electron at the surface and plate out as metal.","distractorNotes":["The interphase is built during the factory's first charge in paragraph 7, not during cold fast charging.",null,"Pore closure is a heat triggered shutdown near 130 degrees, which is the opposite situation.","The passage never mentions the electrolyte freezing. The limit it describes is how fast lithium can work its way into graphite.","Oxygen release needs roughly 200 degrees, nowhere near a cold cell."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on paragraph 13, a fully charged cell sitting idle on a shelf is best described as","choices":["held apart from a reaction its own chemistry favors","already undergoing slow thermal runaway at room temperature","gradually consuming its separator with every passing day","chemically stable, since its reactants cannot reach one another","safe because its electrolyte will not burn"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"a metastable arrangement"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"kept from reacting by a plastic film"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 calls a charged cell metastable: a strong reducing agent and a strong oxidizing agent kept from each other by a plastic film and a few nanometers of surface chemistry. Blocked is not the same as having no reason to react.","distractorNotes":[null,"Runaway in this passage is a rapid cascade above a crossing temperature, not something a room temperature cell is doing.","Nothing in the passage describes the separator wearing away during storage or ordinary use.","Metastable is the passage's own word, and it means the reaction is favored but blocked, not that there is nothing to block.","Paragraph 6 says plainly that the carbonate solvents are flammable."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the main point of the passage?","choices":["Thermal runaway begins with the interphase and ends when the cell vents.","Lithium cells fail mainly because manufacturers economize on the separator film that keeps the electrodes apart.","Lithium iron phosphate should replace layered oxides everywhere they are now used.","Intercalation cured the plating problem, but the energy it unlocked is also what makes failure severe.","The separator is the only thing standing between a cell and a fire."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"Intercalation was supposed to retire dendrites, and in ordinary use it does."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"that figure is why the technology displaced everything else"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"Density and severity are two readings of a single property."}],"why":"The passage moves from why intercalation replaced plating to what a cell does when it overheats, then states the link in paragraph 14: density and severity are two readings of a single property.","distractorNotes":["That is a fair summary of paragraphs 10 and 11 alone, not of the whole passage.","The passage argues the opposite, that the cascade is ordinary chemistry running unchecked rather than a cut corner.","Paragraph 15 says the safer chemistry is not simply the better one and names what it costs.",null,"Too narrow and too absolute. The interphase, the management system, cell spacing and vent paths are all described as barriers too."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 12 is included mainly in order to","choices":["explain why the failure keeps going on its own","describe how a management system tracks cell temperature","show that reaction rates can be measured with precision","catalog the gases a venting cell releases","recommend lithium iron phosphate for vehicles"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"The word runaway is doing precise work there."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"every degree of warming buys further warming"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 sets heat produced against heat removed. Above a certain temperature production wins and every degree of warming buys further warming, which is what makes the word runaway accurate.","distractorNotes":[null,"Management systems appear in paragraph 16.","The temperature dependence is used to explain a feedback, not to make a point about measurement.","The gases are listed in paragraph 11.","No such recommendation is made, and paragraph 15 is careful to name what that chemistry costs."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"How do the early paragraphs of the passage relate to the later ones?","choices":["The early ones give the history and the later ones give a procedure the reader is meant to follow step by step.","The early ones build the cell part by part; the later ones follow those same parts failing.","The early ones describe one chemistry and the later ones describe a rival chemistry.","Both stretches make the same argument from two different sets of numbers.","The early ones raise a problem the later ones report as solved."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"The cell runs by rocking lithium from one host to the other and back."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"the solid electrolyte interphase begins to break down"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"the separator itself melts"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"the positive electrode starts to come apart"}],"why":"The passage introduces the foils, the hosts, the electrolyte, the interphase and the separator in turn, then walks those same components through the heating sequence in paragraphs 10 and 11.","distractorNotes":["There is history in paragraph 2, but the passage never hands the reader a procedure.",null,"Lithium iron phosphate gets one paragraph near the end and is not what the later paragraphs are about.","The two stretches do different work, one descriptive and one causal.","Nothing is reported as solved. The passage closes on a compromise and a deliberately narrow goal."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the popular habit of calling thermal runaway a malfunction is best described as","choices":["admiring of the clarity it brings","neutral, taking no position","corrective but not scornful","alarmed","dismissive of the concern itself"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"The framing is misleading."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"Every step is the same chemistry the cell performs on purpose"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says the framing is misleading and then explains what is actually going on, calmly and without mocking anyone. That is a correction rather than an attack.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in the paragraph praises the popular framing.","Calling a framing misleading is taking a position.",null,"The paragraph is measured and explanatory rather than alarmed.","The author corrects how the hazard is described, not the idea that there is a hazard."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In reporting what engineers claim about the hazard, the author comes across as","choices":["doubtful that any of the safety work accomplishes much","certain that the hazard has been engineered out of existence","indifferent to whether the claim turns out to hold","impatient with how slowly the field is moving","approving of a claim kept deliberately narrow"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"They claim something more modest"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":3,"quote":"The goal is narrower than the public version of it"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 calls the engineers' claim more modest and lets it stand: onset made rare, spread made slow. That word reads as approval of a claim that does not overreach.","distractorNotes":["The passage treats those measures as real and effective within limits, not as empty.","The paragraph states directly that engineers do not claim to have removed the hazard.","Indifference would not single the claim out as modest, which is a favorable word here.","The passage says nothing at all about the pace of the work.",null]},{"id":"rc-p09-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage describes all of the following as pack level measures EXCEPT","choices":["mats that swell and insulate as they heat","aiming the vents so that hot gas leaves through the floor rather than the cabin","setting cells apart from one another","refusing to charge a cold pack quickly","flooding the pack with inert gas"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"Cells are spaced apart","choice":2},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"wrapped in mats that swell and insulate when heated","choice":0},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"hot gas leaves through the floor of a vehicle rather than into the cabin","choice":1},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"refuses to charge a cold one quickly","choice":3},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"Crews flood such fires mainly to cool the neighboring cells.","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 16 lists spacing, swelling mats, cooling plates, aimed vents and a management system that limits cold fast charging. An inert gas flood never appears; the only flooding in the passage is crews cooling the neighboring cells in paragraph 11.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 16 describes mats that swell and insulate when heated.","Paragraph 16 says vents are aimed so hot gas leaves through the floor of a vehicle.","Paragraph 16 lists cell spacing directly.","Paragraph 16 says the management system refuses to charge a cold cell quickly.",null]},{"id":"rc-p09-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to know why a lithium ion cell cannot use water as its electrolyte should look in","choices":["paragraph 5","paragraph 11","paragraph 2","paragraph 6","paragraph 4"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"Water is out of the question."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"the window for water is a little over one volt wide"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 rules water out and gives the reason: water's voltage window is a little over one volt wide and the cell runs at nearly four.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 gives the cell's numbers, including its voltages, but not why water is unsuitable.","Paragraph 11 covers the positive electrode giving off oxygen and the cell venting.","Paragraph 2 covers the plating chemistries that came before lithium ion.",null,"Paragraph 4 explains intercalation and what a host is."]}]},"rc-p10":{"id":"rc-p10","title":"The Trade Beneath the Forest Floor","topic":"mycorrhizal fungi and nutrient exchange in forests","field":"ecology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1375,"paragraphs":[["Pull a fine root from a forest floor, rinse it, and set it under a microscope.","The last few millimetres are wrapped in fungal tissue or riddled with it, and that boundary is where most of the tree's mineral nutrition takes place.","The arrangement is called a mycorrhiza, and most land plant families form one.","For most trees, the organ that takes up phosphate from soil is not a root.","It is a partnership."],["Botanists recognised the relationship in the 1880s, reinterpreting the pale sheath on pine and beech root tips as a partner rather than an infection.","Plant remains roughly four hundred million years old contain branched structures resembling the feeding organs modern fungi build inside plant cells.","The symbiosis looks about as old as land plants themselves; plants seem to have reached dry ground with fungi rather than recruiting them later."],["Two associations dominate.","In an arbuscular mycorrhiza the fungus breaches the wall of a cortex cell and grows inside it, branching into a shape like a miniature tree.","That structure is the arbuscule, the exchange surface.","The plant cell does not simply give way.","Its own membrane stretches inward around every branch, so the two organisms stay separated by two membranes and never share cytoplasm.","Other hyphae swell into vesicles that store lipid."],["An ectomycorrhiza never enters a living cell.","The fungus instead sheathes the root tip in a dense mantle of hyphae, then pushes a lattice of flattened hyphae between the outer root cells.","That lattice is the Hartig net, and it does the arbuscule's job by wrapping cells rather than entering them.","Thousands of fungal species form ectomycorrhizas, and many are the familiar mushrooms of forest floors: boletes, amanitas, chanterelles and truffles.","Only about two percent of seed plant species take this route, but they include the pines, oaks, beeches and birches that build most of the world's forests."],["The reason a tree pays for either arrangement comes down to geometry and to one stubborn nutrient.","Phosphate barely moves in soil.","It binds to mineral surfaces and diffuses so slowly that a root strips the millimetre or two around itself within days, then sits in its own depletion zone.","A fine root is perhaps two to five hundred micrometres across and a root hair ten to twenty.","A fungal hypha is two to ten micrometres across, finds purchase in pores far too narrow for a root hair to enter, and extends centimetres past the depleted shell.","Per unit of carbon spent, hyphae buy far more absorbing surface than roots do."],["What travels back is, above all, phosphate, strung into chains called polyphosphate for the journey and released at the exchange interface.","Nitrogen moves too, as ammonium and as amino acids.","Many ectomycorrhizal fungi secrete enzymes that break down protein in leaf litter, which lets their trees tap nitrogen locked inside dead tissue, while arbuscular fungi rely on nitrogen already freed into the soil."],["The plant pays in carbon, and it pays twice over.","Sugar crosses the interface as simple six carbon units.","Lipid crosses as well, which is the more surprising half of the bill.","Arbuscular fungi cannot manufacture their own fatty acids and must be handed them by a host, which is largely why no one could grow one to completion on a plate without a plant until a fatty acid was supplied directly."],["That bill is not trivial.","Estimates of the share of a plant's photosynthetic output that ends up in fungal partners run from four to twenty percent, highest in seedlings and poor soils.","Experiments that sever the phloem by girdling a trunk show the flow falling away within days, which means the fungi live on sugar made that week, not on stored reserves.","A colonised root system is a powerful sink, and sugar made up in the leaves is drawn down to it rather than kept in the shoot."],["The obvious question is where the carbon ends up, and the tool that answers it is the labelled atom.","Seal a seedling in a bag, feed it carbon dioxide built from an uncommon isotope, and every sugar it makes for the next several hours carries a tag instruments can detect at very low concentrations.","Wait, then harvest the plants nearby and look for the tag."],["Separating the fungal route from the soil takes a second trick.","Investigators grow donor and receiver in compartments divided by a mesh whose pores admit hyphae but exclude roots, and sometimes rotate the mesh cylinder to shear any hyphae that cross.","Run these designs and labelled carbon does turn up in neighbouring plants, across species as well as within them, and shaded receivers often show more than sunlit ones."],["This is the point where care is required, because a tag in a neighbour is not automatically carbon in a neighbour.","Much of what the instruments detect sits in hyphae wrapped around and threaded through the receiving root, which is fungal tissue, not plant tissue.","The stricter tests look for the tag above ground, in stem and leaves, and by that measure the quantity that clearly crosses into plant tissue is usually a small fraction of what the receiver fixes for itself."],["The network is also not the only road.","Labelled carbon can leak from roots into soil water and be taken up by a neighbour, be respired underground as carbon dioxide and fixed again above ground, travel in fallen litter, or cross a graft where two roots have fused.","Each of these is real, none requires a fungal conduit, and pulling them apart in a forest rather than a pot is genuinely difficult."],["Suppose all of that is handled and real transfer is shown.","A further claim is smuggled in when the result is described as one tree feeding another, because purpose is a separate question from movement.","The fungus is not an inert pipe but a third organism with an interest of its own, usually joined to many hosts at once, and it can hold carbon in its hyphae, spend it exploring new soil, or pour it into spores.","Flow may be set by the fungus, or simply by which end of the connection is hungrier."],["Evolution sharpens the objection.","Natural selection acts on the donor, and a trait that costs a tree carbon while benefiting a competitor should be whittled down over generations unless the donor gets something back: a better fungal partner, a return flow later, or the survival of a relative.","No such account is needed for leakage down a gradient, which happens whenever a tissue rich in sugar is plumbed to one short of it.","The plain explanation is the one to beat."],["One group of plants shows how far the conduit can go.","Ghost pipe and a number of orchids carry no usable chlorophyll and make no sugar at all.","They live on carbon drawn from fungi attached at the same time to photosynthetic trees, as clear a demonstration as anyone could want that plant carbon can cross a fungus into another plant.","It also cuts the other way.","These plants return nothing.","The route says nothing about generosity at either end."],["That distinction has largely been lost outside the laboratory.","The image of the forest as a web of nurture, old trees knowingly provisioning seedlings and warnings passing from root to root, has become the standard telling, and the appeal is easy to see.","Some of it is defensible as metaphor.","But the confident language of intention has run well ahead of the measurements, and recent surveys have found that several of the most repeated claims rest on a handful of studies, and that sources cited for the strongest statements often do not say what those statements report."],["None of this diminishes the science.","What is not in doubt is plain enough: the symbiosis is ancient, nearly universal, and structurally elegant.","The trade of mineral nutrients for sugar and fat is measured and quantified.","Carbon does move through fungal tissue between root systems.","What stays open is how much of that movement matters to a growing forest, and whether anything resembling provisioning happens at all.","The gap is where the interesting work sits, and a bargain that old was never going to need the decoration."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p10-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when a fungus builds an arbuscule inside a cell of the root cortex, the plant cell","choices":["dissolves its own wall so the two organisms can mix freely","wraps the fungus in a dense sheath of its own tissue","folds its membrane inward around every fungal branch","fills with stored lipid that the fungus later draws on","dies off, leaving a cavity that the fungus then occupies"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"Its own membrane stretches inward around every branch"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"The plant cell does not simply give way."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the plant cell does not simply give way: its own membrane stretches inward around every branch, which keeps the two organisms separated by two membranes. The fungus gets inside the wall, but never inside the cytoplasm.","distractorNotes":["The fungus does breach the wall, but paragraph 3 is explicit that the two never share cytoplasm, so nothing mixes freely.","A sheath around the outside is the ectomycorrhizal mantle from paragraph 4, and that mantle is fungal tissue, not plant tissue.",null,"Lipid storage happens in vesicles, which paragraph 3 names as a separate structure formed by other hyphae.","Nothing in paragraph 3 says the colonised cell dies, and a dead cell could not stretch its membrane around the branches."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage describes the Hartig net as","choices":["a lattice of flattened hyphae between the outer root cells","a strand of hyphae running from a root tip out into open soil","a dense fungal sheath covering the outside of a short root tip","a swollen hypha that stores lipid on behalf of the fungus","a branched fungal organ that grows inside a cortex cell"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"That lattice is the Hartig net"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"pushes a lattice of flattened hyphae between the outer root cells"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 names the Hartig net as the lattice of flattened hyphae the fungus pushes between the outer root cells, and points out that it does the arbuscule's job by wrapping cells instead of entering them.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 5 does describe hyphae reaching out into soil, but the passage never gives that reach the name Hartig net.","That is the mantle, which paragraph 4 describes as a separate structure sitting on the outside of the root tip.","That is a vesicle, which paragraph 3 attributes to the arbuscular type rather than the ectomycorrhizal one.","That is the arbuscule from paragraph 3, and paragraph 4 opens by saying an ectomycorrhiza never enters a living cell."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage gives the diameter of a fungal hypha as roughly","choices":["20 to 100 micrometres","10 to 20 micrometres","2 to 10 micrometres","1 to 2 millimetres","200 to 500 micrometres"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"A fungal hypha is two to ten micrometres across"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 lines the three figures up in a row, and the hypha is the smallest of them at two to ten micrometres. That is the whole point of the comparison.","distractorNotes":["No structure in paragraph 5 is given this width, so this choice has nothing behind it.","Ten to twenty micrometres is the figure paragraph 5 gives for a root hair, not for a hypha.",null,"A millimetre or two is the width of the depletion zone around a root in paragraph 5, not the width of a hypha.","Two to five hundred micrometres is the figure paragraph 5 gives for a fine root."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, many ectomycorrhizal fungi can reach nitrogen held in dead tissue because they","choices":["release enzymes that break down protein in leaf litter","capture nitrogen from air trapped inside the root mantle","take up ammonium faster than a root hair can take it up","move nitrogen along the hypha as chains of polyphosphate","trade nitrogen with arbuscular fungi on neighbouring roots"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"secrete enzymes that break down protein in leaf litter"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 draws the line between the two association types right here: ectomycorrhizal fungi secrete enzymes that break down protein in litter, while arbuscular fungi lean on nitrogen already freed into the soil.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 6 never mentions nitrogen being taken from air, and no part of the passage describes nitrogen fixation.","Paragraph 6 does say nitrogen moves as ammonium, but that is the form it travels in, not the reason litter nitrogen becomes available.","Polyphosphate is how paragraph 6 says phosphate travels, and swapping it onto nitrogen is a one word change.","No exchange between the two fungal types appears anywhere in the passage, so this is outside its scope."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage explains that an arbuscular fungus has never been grown to completion on a plate without a plant because the fungus","choices":["can store lipid only while it is attached to a living root","loses the ability to take up phosphate when no root is present","needs a mantle of root tissue in order to produce its spores","cannot make fatty acids and must be handed them","depends on carbon dioxide that living roots release into soil"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"cannot manufacture their own fatty acids and must be handed them by a host"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 calls lipid the more surprising half of the bill. Arbuscular fungi cannot manufacture their own fatty acids, and that dependence is given as a large part of why they cannot be raised to completion without a host.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 mentions vesicles storing lipid but never makes storage itself conditional on a living root.","Phosphate uptake is the fungus's own job in paragraph 6, and nothing ties it to the presence of a root.","Mantles belong to the ectomycorrhizal type in paragraph 4, and the passage never links them to spore production.",null,"Soil carbon dioxide appears in paragraph 12 as a route a label can take, not as something a fungus must be fed."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 5, \"purchase\" most nearly means","choices":["a payment made for a service","a claim on a shared resource","an advantage won by bargaining","something acquired by buying","a secure physical hold"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"finds purchase in pores far too narrow for a root hair to enter"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 is about size and reach: the hypha finds purchase in pores too narrow for a root hair. The word is being used for getting a grip on a space, not for buying anything.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday meaning of the word, and the surrounding trade language makes it tempting, but pores are not paid for.","The passage does describe an exchange, but this particular sentence is purely about the size of the openings a hypha can enter.","Paragraph 5 is describing physical access to soil pores, not negotiation between the partners.","Nothing is being acquired here. The sentence is about where the hypha can fit.",null]},{"id":"rc-p10-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 8, \"sink\" most nearly means","choices":["a basin into which a liquid drains away and is lost","a steady decline in value over a long period","a destination that draws supply in","a slow settling downward through soil","a place where unwanted material collects"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"a powerful sink, and sugar made up in the leaves is drawn down to it"}],"why":"Watch what the word is doing in paragraph 8. Sugar made up in the leaves is drawn down to the colonised root rather than kept in the shoot, so a sink here is the end of the line that demand pulls supply toward.","distractorNotes":["This is the common household meaning, and paragraph 8 is not describing plumbing or drainage.","Nothing in paragraph 8 is losing value. Sugar is being pulled toward the root, not devalued.",null,"The movement in paragraph 8 is set by demand at the root, not by anything sifting downward through soil.","Sugar arriving at the root is the tree's own product being spent on a partner, not waste being dumped."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that finding a tag in a neighbouring plant's root is weaker evidence than finding it in that plant's leaves because","choices":["a root carries fungal tissue that may hold the tag itself","the tag breaks down before it can travel up into a shoot","the fungus strips the tag out of a root before it is harvested","leaves are the only tissue instruments can measure accurately","a root holds far less carbon overall than a leaf holds"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"which is fungal tissue, not plant tissue"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"The stricter tests look for the tag above ground"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 makes the distinction that drives the whole second half of the passage: much of what the instruments pick up in a receiving root sits in fungal hyphae wrapped around and through it. Tissue above ground cannot be fungal, so it is the stricter test.","distractorNotes":[null,"Nothing in the passage says the tag degrades, and the isotope is described as detectable at very low concentrations.","No removal of the tag by the fungus appears anywhere in the passage, so this invents a mechanism.","Paragraph 9 says instruments detect the tag at very low concentrations, with no suggestion that roots are unreadable.","The passage never compares how much carbon a root and a leaf hold, and that would not settle whose tissue the tag is in."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The example of ghost pipe and certain orchids most strongly supports which conclusion?","choices":["Chlorophyll turns out to be unnecessary for the majority of land plants","Fungi favour hosts that have given up photosynthesis","Trees raise their sugar output when a network drains carbon from them","A plant can take from a network without giving back","Most plants in a forest obtain their carbon from other plants"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":5,"quote":"These plants return nothing."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":6,"quote":"The route says nothing about generosity at either end."}],"why":"Paragraph 15 says these plants make no sugar and return nothing, and the author uses them to show that carbon crossing a fungus says nothing about generosity at either end. A route can be used in one direction only.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 15 limits the missing chlorophyll to ghost pipe and a number of orchids, not to most land plants.","The passage never says fungi prefer these plants, only that these plants can live off fungi already attached to trees.","Nothing in the passage describes a donor increasing its output in response to a drain.",null,"These plants are presented as one unusual group, and paragraph 1 says most plants take up their own nutrients through a partnership."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Ectomycorrhizal and arbuscular partners differ mainly in their fungal lineages","The trade is well documented, the sharing between trees far less so","Isotope labelling has settled the question of how carbon moves between trees","Fungal networks join the trees of a forest into a single system of mutual care","Phosphate scarcity is the one force that has shaped the evolution of land plants"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":17,"sentence":3,"quote":"The trade of mineral nutrients for sugar and fat is measured and quantified."},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":5,"quote":"What stays open is how much of that movement matters to a growing forest"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"the confident language of intention has run well ahead of the measurements"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"It is a partnership."}],"why":"The passage builds the partnership carefully, then spends its second half separating what the measurements show from what the popular story claims. Paragraph 17 states the split directly.","distractorNotes":["The structural contrast is real, but it fills two paragraphs and is not what the passage is arguing.",null,"Paragraph 11 and paragraph 17 both say the opposite: labelling raises a question the field has not closed.","This is the popular account the author pushes back on in paragraph 16, not the position the passage argues for.","Phosphate gets one paragraph as the reason the partnership pays, and the passage never claims it is the single driver of plant evolution."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 12 is included mainly in order to","choices":["argue that soil water supplies most of the nutrients in a forest","point out other ways a tag could reach a neighbouring plant","explain why root grafts form between trees of the same species","describe the way fallen leaves decompose on a forest floor","show that the fungal route carries more carbon than the soil route does"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"The network is also not the only road."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"none requires a fungal conduit"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 lists soil water, respired carbon dioxide, litter and root grafts, then says none of them requires a fungal conduit. Its job is to keep a tag in a neighbour from being read automatically as network traffic.","distractorNotes":["Soil water is named as one possible path for a tag, not as the forest's main nutrient supply.",null,"Grafts appear as one item on a list of alternative routes, and the passage never explains how or why they form.","Litter is mentioned in a single clause as a route, and paragraph 6 covers what litter contributes without describing decay.","Paragraph 12 makes no comparison of quantities. It says the routes are hard to tell apart, which is the opposite of ranking them."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"The passage as a whole is organized as","choices":["a problem raised in the opening and fully solved by the closing paragraph","a comparison of two fungal groups, followed by a defence of one of them","a series of experiments presented in the order they were carried out","a chronological history of research into the fungi of forest soils","an account of a partnership, then a limit on what it shows"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"It is a partnership."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"The reason a tree pays for either arrangement"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"This is the point where care is required"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":5,"quote":"What stays open is how much of that movement matters"}],"why":"The first eight paragraphs build the symbiosis and price it. Paragraphs 9 and 10 give the method. From paragraph 11 on, the passage tests how far the results can be pushed, and paragraph 17 leaves the question open.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 17 says plainly that the central question stays open, so nothing is solved at the close.","The two groups are compared, but the author never takes a side between them.","The labelling work fills two paragraphs and is described by design rather than by date.","Only paragraph 2 is historical, and the rest of the passage is not arranged by date.",null]},{"id":"rc-p10-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the popular image of the forest as a web of nurture is best described as","choices":["alert to its appeal but unconvinced by it","indifferent to whether the image is accurate","fully persuaded by the case that has been made for it","openly contemptuous of everyone who repeats it","amused that researchers have allowed the phrase to spread so widely"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"the appeal is easy to see"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"the confident language of intention has run well ahead of the measurements"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 grants that the appeal is easy to see and that some of the image is defensible as metaphor, then says the language of intention has run well ahead of the measurements. That is acknowledgement followed by doubt.","distractorNotes":[null,"Accuracy is the author's central concern, which is what paragraphs 11 through 14 are built to test.","Paragraph 16 states the opposite, that the confident language has outrun what has been measured.","The author is critical of the claims, not of the people, and paragraph 16 concedes the image has real appeal.","Paragraph 16 treats the overreach as a serious problem with the literature rather than as something funny."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In the final paragraph, the author treats the unanswered questions about carbon sharing as","choices":["a sign that the whole field has been oversold","evidence that the symbiosis itself has been misdescribed","a reason to set the subject aside for the time being","a problem that no instrument will ever be able to settle","an opening for further work rather than a flaw"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":17,"sentence":1,"quote":"None of this diminishes the science."},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":6,"quote":"The gap is where the interesting work sits"}],"why":"Paragraph 17 lists what is not in doubt, names what stays open, and then says the gap is where the interesting work sits. The uncertainty is framed as the place to go next.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 17 opens by saying none of this diminishes the science, so the criticism is aimed at one claim, not the field.","Paragraph 17 calls the symbiosis ancient, nearly universal and structurally elegant, which is the part it treats as settled.","The closing sentence points toward more work on the question, not away from the subject.","No such limit is claimed. The passage describes methods that are getting stricter, not methods that are failing.",null]},{"id":"rc-p10-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements EXCEPT","choices":["Some of the fungi that form mycorrhizas produce mushrooms on a forest floor","Fungi steer carbon toward the seedlings most closely related to the donor","Phosphate moves through soil slowly enough to leave a root short of it","There are plants that live without making any sugar of their own","A share of what a tree makes in a given week ends up in fungal tissue"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"diffuses so slowly that a root strips the millimetre or two around itself within days","choice":2},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"many are the familiar mushrooms of forest floors","choice":0},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"the fungi live on sugar made that week","choice":4},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"make no sugar at all","choice":3},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"Flow may be set by the fungus, or simply by which end of the connection is hungrier.","choice":1},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"several of the most repeated claims rest on a handful of studies","choice":1}],"why":"Paragraph 14 raises a return to a relative only as one of the things a donor would need to get back for the behaviour to persist. The passage never reports that fungi actually route carbon by kinship, and paragraph 13 says flow may be set by the fungus or by simple demand.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 supports this by naming boletes, amanitas, chanterelles and truffles as ectomycorrhizal fungi.",null,"Paragraph 5 supports this: phosphate diffuses so slowly that a root creates its own depletion zone within days.","Paragraph 15 supports this with ghost pipe and a number of orchids, which make no sugar at all.","Paragraph 8 supports this with the girdling result, which shows the fungi living on sugar made that week."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wants to know how investigators let hyphae, but not roots, pass between two plants should look in which paragraph?","choices":["Paragraph 5","Paragraph 8","Paragraph 9","Paragraph 10","Paragraph 12"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"a mesh whose pores admit hyphae but exclude roots"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 is the one that describes the mesh whose pores admit hyphae and exclude roots, along with the rotating cylinder used to shear the hyphae that cross.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 compares the widths of roots, root hairs and hyphae, but describes no apparatus.","Paragraph 8 covers the carbon budget and the girdling result, not the design of a transfer experiment.","Paragraph 9 explains how a plant is labelled with an isotope, which is the step before the barrier is set up.",null,"Paragraph 12 lists routes other than the fungal one, which is a different concern from blocking roots."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q17","type":"application","stem":"A birch seedling, potted alone with an established fungal partner, is kept in a dark room for ten days so that it makes no new sugar. Based on the passage, the flow of carbon to its fungal partner would most likely","choices":["continue unchanged, since the phosphate trade is unaffected","fall away within days, long before the ten days are up","reverse, with the fungus now supplying sugar to the seedling","rise, as the seedling leans harder on its partner for nutrients","taper off slowly over weeks as stored reserves are drawn down"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"show the flow falling away within days"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"the fungi live on sugar made that week, not on stored reserves"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 gives the rule: when girdling cuts off the sugar supply, the flow to the fungi falls away within days, because the fungi live on sugar made that week, not on stored reserves. A seedling making no sugar at all is the same situation, so expect the flow to drop within days.","distractorNotes":["Phosphate moving up is the fungus's side of the trade. Paragraph 8 is about the sugar moving down, and that supply depends on sugar made that week.",null,"Nothing in the passage has a fungus feeding sugar to an ordinary green plant, and the seedling is potted alone, so there is no neighbouring tree for the fungus to draw on.","The flow in paragraph 8 is set by what the leaves make. A seedling making nothing has nothing extra to send, however much it needs its partner.","Paragraph 8 says the flow falls within days, not weeks, and that the fungi do not live on stored reserves."]}]},"rc-p11":{"id":"rc-p11","title":"From Bacterial Memory to Gene Editing","topic":"bacterial adaptive immunity and gene editing","field":"molecular biology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1373,"paragraphs":[["In the late 1980s a team sequencing a bacterial gene ran past its end and found something nobody could explain.","Downstream sat a short sequence, roughly thirty base pairs long, repeated again and again.","Between one copy and the next lay a stretch of similar length that was not repeated, and every intervening stretch was different.","The finding was published, noticed by a few specialists, and then left alone for fifteen years."],["As sequencing grew cheap the same pattern surfaced everywhere: about four in ten bacteria carried such an array, and most archaea did too.","Two details hinted that the arrays were doing work.","They sat beside a tight cluster of genes encoding proteins that looked like nucleases and helicases.","And the nonrepeating stretches, called spacers, differed from strain to strain within one species."],["The answer arrived around 2005, when several groups began comparing spacers with everything else in the sequence databases.","The matches were not bacterial.","They came from bacteriophages, the viruses that prey on bacteria, and from plasmids, the small circles of DNA that pass between cells.","A spacer was a fragment of something that had once tried to get in.","An array was a ledger of past intrusions, kept in the order they happened."],["A ledger implies a use, and within two years the use was demonstrated.","In a dairy bacterium, cultures were challenged with a phage and the survivors sequenced.","The survivors had added new spacers that matched the attacking phage.","Deleting a single spacer from a resistant strain cost it protection against precisely the phage that spacer matched, and nothing else.","Adding a spacer to a strain that had never met the phage handed it protection.","The defense was sequence specific, heritable, and acquired during an encounter rather than carried from birth."],["That last property is what made the result startling.","Bacteria already defended themselves, mainly with restriction enzymes, which cleave any DNA carrying a particular short sequence unless the cell has chemically marked its own copies as friendly.","That defense is blunt and fixed: the same weapon, aimed the same way, in every generation.","The arrays described something else.","A cell met a specific virus, kept a souvenir of it, and passed that souvenir to descendants who would know the virus on sight."],["The cycle has three stages.","In adaptation, the cell samples an invader and files a new spacer.","In expression, the array is transcribed and cut into small guide RNAs.","In interference, a guide loaded into a protein finds the matching sequence in an invading genome and cuts it."],["Adaptation is the least understood of the three.","A pair of proteins, Cas1 and Cas2, captures a short piece of foreign DNA and installs it in the array.","The insertion is not random.","New spacers go in at one particular end, beside a stretch called the leader, so the array accumulates chronologically with the most recent threat at the front.","Each insertion also duplicates one copy of the repeat, which is why the alternating pattern survives every time the record grows."],["Expression begins with one long transcript of the whole array, which the cell then chops into units.","Each mature guide carries about twenty nucleotides of spacer derived sequence, which will pair with a target, attached to repeat derived sequence that the protein grips.","In the best studied version a second small RNA pairs with the repeat portion and is required for the guide to function."],["Interference is where the numbers get specific.","The loaded protein does not scan the genome end to end.","It collides with DNA at random, checking at each collision for a motif just two or three bases long.","Only when it finds one does it pry the helix open and offer its guide to the exposed strand.","Pairing begins at the ten or twelve bases nearest the motif, called the seed, and if those fail to match the protein lets go within milliseconds.","If they match, pairing runs the length of the guide and two nuclease domains inside the protein cut one strand each."],["That short motif is the hinge of the whole system.","In the most widely used version it is any base followed by two guanines, immediately beside the target on the strand the guide does not pair with.","The motif is no part of the guide, and the requirement looks at first like a design flaw, since only sequences sitting next to the right two letters can be attacked.","It is not a flaw.","The spacer stored in the array is flanked by repeat sequence rather than by the motif, so a guide never finds the motif beside the cell's own copy of the record.","The motif licenses the cut, and by licensing it only outside the array it keeps the cell from shredding its own memory.","The same rule guides adaptation, where the capture complex prefers fragments carrying the motif."],["Popular accounts reach immediately for scissors, and the metaphor does more harm than good.","In a bacterium the cut really is the end of the story: the break destroys the invading genome, and most bacteria have no pathway for rejoining it.","In a nucleated cell the break is the beginning.","The cell repairs the damage, and what the experimenter gets is not the cut but whatever the repair machinery decides to do with it.","The scissors image describes the easy half of the job and skips the half that settles the outcome."],["The step that turned a defense system into an instrument was smaller than the publicity suggests.","By 2012 it was clear that the two RNAs could be fused into a single synthetic guide and the protein would work just as well.","That left an enzyme whose specificity lived entirely in twenty nucleotides of RNA.","Earlier targeting tools read DNA with protein, which meant building a new protein for every new site.","Retargeting the newer system means ordering a different short strand of RNA."],["What happens next depends on which repair pathway arrives first.","The fast route rejoins the ends directly and often loses or gains a base or two, which shifts the reading frame and silences the gene.","The accurate route copies from a supplied template and can install a chosen change, but it works mainly in dividing cells and is far less efficient.","One response has been to avoid double strand breaks entirely, with editors that nick one strand and chemically convert a single base."],["Specificity is the other honest worry.","Twenty bases is long enough to be unique in a mammalian genome in principle, but pairing tolerates mismatches, particularly at the end farthest from the motif.","A guide that behaves this way is promiscuous: it pairs where it was not sent and cuts sites that resemble its target without matching it.","The countermeasures are real and partial.","Engineered variants hold DNA less tightly and so demand a better match, shortened guides are less forgiving of mismatches, and a pair of nicking enzymes aimed at opposite strands severs the helix only where both bind.","Finding rare off target events among billions of cells remains a measurement problem of its own."],["Delivery is the harder and less glamorous problem.","Editing cells in a dish and returning them to the patient works, and the first approved uses have landed there.","Editing a tissue inside the body is another matter.","The most common viral vector carries roughly four and a half kilobases, and the gene for the standard enzyme alone runs about four, leaving no room for the promoter and guide it needs.","Lipid particles sidestep the size limit but drain overwhelmingly to the liver.","And because these enzymes come from bacteria most people have already met, many patients carry antibodies primed against them."],["A pattern that looked like sequencing noise turned out to be a filing system, the filing system a memory, and the memory a program that anyone can retype in twenty letters.","None of it was designed for human purposes, which is why it resists domestication.","Phages already carry proteins that shut the system down, a reminder of how long it has been under test.","The instrument on the bench is a borrowed one, still carrying the constraints of the cell it came from."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p11-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, where does a newly captured spacer end up in the array?","choices":["At one end of the array, beside the leader sequence","At the end of the array farthest from the leader sequence","Inside the repeat that sits closest to the neighboring genes","In a second array kept separate from the original one","Distributed at random among the spacers already stored there"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"New spacers go in at one particular end, beside a stretch called the leader"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says outright that new spacers go in at one particular end, beside a stretch called the leader, which is what makes the array chronological.","distractorNotes":[null,"Half right: it is one particular end, but paragraph 7 places it beside the leader rather than away from it.","Spacers sit between repeats, and paragraph 7 has each insertion duplicate a repeat rather than break into one.","No second array appears anywhere in the passage; the record grows inside the one array.","Paragraph 7 states directly that the insertion is not random, and then names the position."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage states that when the bases nearest the motif fail to pair with the guide, the protein","choices":["lets go of the DNA within milliseconds","files the mismatched sequence away as a new spacer","calls in a second protein to finish the search for a target","opens a longer stretch of the helix and tries the match again","cuts one strand of the helix and leaves the other intact"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"the protein lets go within milliseconds"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 describes the seed, the ten or twelve bases nearest the motif, and says that if those fail to match the protein lets go within milliseconds.","distractorNotes":[null,"Filing a spacer belongs to adaptation in paragraph 7 and is carried out by a different pair of proteins.","No second protein is described at this step, and the two nuclease domains named in paragraph 9 are inside the same protein.","Paragraph 9 runs the other way: the helix is opened first, and a failed seed ends the attempt.","Single strand nicking shows up in paragraph 13 as a deliberate laboratory design, not as the response to a failed seed match."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q03","type":"detail","stem":"In the phage experiment described, removing a single spacer from a resistant strain caused that strain to","choices":["lose protection against every phage it had previously resisted","resist a wider range of phages than it had resisted before","stop adding any new spacers to its array from then on","take up a replacement spacer from the surrounding medium","lose protection only against the phage that spacer matched"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"cost it protection against precisely the phage that spacer matched, and nothing else"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 reports that deleting one spacer cost the strain protection against precisely the phage that spacer matched, and nothing else.","distractorNotes":["Too sweeping: paragraph 4 stresses that the loss was confined to the matching phage.","This reverses the finding: the deletion cost protection instead of broadening it.","The experiment measured protection, and the passage never claims that deletion shuts adaptation down.","Paragraph 4 has spacers added during infection or by the experimenters, never picked up to fill a deletion.",null]},{"id":"rc-p11-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the trouble with the most common viral vector is that","choices":["its capacity leaves no room for the promoter and guide beside the enzyme gene","it works only in cells that are actively dividing","it delivers almost all of its cargo to the liver","patients have usually been exposed already to the bacteria it comes from","it has to be given over and over again before a useful number of cells are edited"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"leaving no room for the promoter and guide it needs"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 gives the numbers: the vector carries roughly four and a half kilobases and the enzyme gene alone runs about four, so the promoter and guide do not fit.","distractorNotes":[null,"Dependence on dividing cells belongs to the template copying repair route in paragraph 13.","Draining to the liver is the drawback of lipid particles in that same paragraph, offered as the alternative.","Prior exposure in paragraph 15 is to the bacteria the enzymes come from, not to the vector.","Repeat dosing is never mentioned; the stated obstacle is size."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage reports that the repeat arrays sit next to","choices":["a long stretch of DNA that encodes no protein at all","the genes a cell uses to mark its own DNA as friendly rather than foreign","a cluster of genes for proteins resembling nucleases and helicases","a duplicated copy of the whole bacterial chromosome","the plasmids that carry DNA between bacterial cells"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"a tight cluster of genes encoding proteins that looked like nucleases and helicases"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 names this as one of the two details that hinted the arrays were doing work: they sat beside a tight cluster of genes for proteins that looked like nucleases and helicases.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 says those neighboring genes encode proteins, which is exactly why they were suggestive.","Marking one's own DNA as friendly belongs to the restriction enzyme system in paragraph 5, used there as a contrast.",null,"No duplicated chromosome appears in the passage; paragraph 7 duplicates a single repeat.","Plasmids appear in paragraph 3 as a source of spacer sequence, not as a neighbor of the array."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 10, \"licenses\" most nearly means","choices":["explains","postpones","measures","conceals","permits"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"The motif licenses the cut, and by licensing it only outside the array"}],"why":"In paragraph 10 the motif licenses the cut, meaning it gives the cut permission to proceed, and withholding that permission inside the array is what protects the cell's own record.","distractorNotes":["The motif does not account for the cut, it allows it; the sentence is about permission rather than explanation.","The motif does not delay the cut, it is the condition that lets the cut happen at all.","Nothing is being quantified in that sentence, which is about whether cutting may go ahead.","The motif sits in plain view beside the target, and hiding it would defeat the checkpoint entirely.",null]},{"id":"rc-p11-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 14, \"promiscuous\" most nearly means","choices":["present in many copies","shortened at one end","not selective enough","easily broken down","unusually quick to act"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"it pairs where it was not sent and cuts sites that resemble its target without matching it"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 defines the word in the same sentence: a promiscuous guide pairs where it was not sent and cuts sites that merely resemble its target.","distractorNotes":["Copy number is never discussed; the sentence concerns a single guide pairing in the wrong place.","Shortened guides turn up in the same paragraph as one of the fixes, so this trades the problem for the remedy.",null,"Nothing about the guide breaking down appears in that paragraph, which is describing binding behavior.","Speed is not at issue there; the sentence is about where a guide binds, not how fast."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 10 most strongly supports which conclusion about a bacterium whose stored spacer happened to sit beside the recognition motif?","choices":["It would need a second copy of the motif on the opposite strand before cutting","It would lose the ability to recognize the matching phage","It could no longer transcribe its array into guide RNAs","It would risk having its own record cut by one of its own guides","It would stop capturing new spacers from invading DNA"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"a guide never finds the motif beside the cell's own copy of the record"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"it keeps the cell from shredding its own memory"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 explains that the stored spacer is flanked by repeat sequence rather than by the motif, which is what keeps a guide from attacking the array. Put the motif there and that protection is gone.","distractorNotes":["The passage describes one motif, on the strand the guide does not pair with, and never a second.","Recognition comes from the twenty nucleotide guide, which would be unchanged; what changes is whether the array can be attacked.","Transcription is described in paragraph 8 and does not depend on the motif in any way.",null,"Capture is described as preferring fragments that carry the motif, and nothing ties it to what is already stored."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Which conclusion about the same enzyme acting in a bacterium and in a nucleated cell does the passage best support?","choices":["A bacterium repairs a double strand break more precisely than a nucleated cell does","An identical cut can lead to different outcomes depending on the cell that receives it","The enzyme cuts less accurately once it is working inside a nucleated cell","The enzyme would require a different recognition motif in order to work inside a nucleated cell","A nucleated cell destroys the enzyme before it can ever reach its target"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"In a bacterium the cut really is the end of the story"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"In a nucleated cell the break is the beginning."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"whatever the repair machinery decides to do with it"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the cut ends the story in a bacterium but only begins it in a nucleated cell, where the repair machinery decides what the experimenter actually gets.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 11 says most bacteria have no pathway for rejoining such a break, so they do not repair it more precisely.",null,"Paragraph 11 is about what follows the cut, not about cutting accuracy, which is raised separately in paragraph 14.","The motif is a property of the enzyme and its guide, and the passage never makes it depend on the host cell.","The immune recognition in paragraph 15 concerns patients carrying antibodies, not a cell destroying the enzyme at the target."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Gene editing will stay impractical until viral vectors can be engineered to carry a much larger cargo","Repeated sequences found in bacterial genomes had no known function for fifteen years after they were first reported","A bacterial record of past infection was decoded and then borrowed as a tool that still carries its original constraints","Bacterial defenses turn out to be more sophisticated than the immune memory found in vertebrate animals","The recognition motif is the single feature that matters most in the whole system, in bacteria and in the laboratory alike"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"An array was a ledger of past intrusions"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"the memory a program that anyone can retype in twenty letters"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"The instrument on the bench is a borrowed one, still carrying the constraints of the cell it came from."}],"why":"The passage moves from an unexplained pattern to a memory system to a laboratory instrument, and closes in paragraph 16 by calling that instrument borrowed and still bound by the cell it came from.","distractorNotes":["Vector capacity is one obstacle named in paragraph 15, not the claim the passage is built to make.","True, and drawn from paragraphs 1 and 2, but far too narrow to carry sixteen paragraphs.",null,"The passage compares the two kinds of defense without ranking bacteria above animals.","Paragraph 10 does call the motif the hinge of the system, but one mechanism is not the point of the whole piece."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 5 is included mainly in order to","choices":["establish that bacteria are attacked by plasmids as well as by viruses","explain how restriction enzymes pick out the sequences they cut","argue that restriction enzymes protect a cell less effectively than arrays do","introduce the three stages of the cycle that the paragraph immediately after it goes on to list","show what sets the array system apart from the defense bacteria were already known to have"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"That last property is what made the result startling."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"That defense is blunt and fixed"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"The arrays described something else."}],"why":"Paragraph 5 sets a blunt, fixed, inherited defense against a specific memory of a specific attacker, and that contrast is what makes the earlier result startling.","distractorNotes":["That the invaders include both phages and plasmids is already established back in paragraph 3.","Recognition gets one clause there, as setup for the contrast rather than as the point of the paragraph.","The paragraph draws a difference in kind, blunt and fixed against specific and remembered, and never ranks the two.","The three stages arrive in paragraph 6, and paragraph 5 does not preview them.",null]},{"id":"rc-p11-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes the way the passage is organized?","choices":["A laboratory technique is described first, then traced back to the organism it came from, then set against the rival techniques it displaced","A controversy is introduced, the evidence on both sides is weighed, and one side is finally endorsed","A series of experiments is presented in the order performed, each one correcting an error made in the one before it","An unexplained observation is reported, its function is worked out, its mechanism is detailed, and its move into the laboratory is assessed","A general principle is stated at the outset and then illustrated with examples of steadily increasing difficulty"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":1,"quote":"found something nobody could explain"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"A ledger implies a use, and within two years the use was demonstrated."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"The cycle has three stages"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"The step that turned a defense system into an instrument"}],"why":"The passage opens on a puzzle in paragraph 1, settles what the arrays are for by paragraph 4, spends paragraphs 6 through 10 on mechanism, and then weighs the laboratory version in paragraphs 11 through 16.","distractorNotes":["The passage runs the other way, from the bacterial observation forward to the technique, and rival tools get a single sentence in paragraph 12.","The author does push back on a popular metaphor in paragraph 11, but no two sided controversy is set up and weighed.","Experiments appear early, and the later work builds on the earlier work rather than correcting it.",null,"The opening offers a puzzle rather than a principle, and nothing is laid down as a rule to be illustrated."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the habit of comparing the protein to a pair of scissors is best described as","choices":["impatient with it, because it drops the part that settles the result","neutral, on the grounds that the comparison is accurate as far as it goes","alarmed by it, as a claim that has pushed researchers into unsafe work","amused by a figure of speech that no specialist takes seriously","enthusiastic about a phrase that makes a hard idea accessible to readers"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"the metaphor does more harm than good"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"skips the half that settles the outcome"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the metaphor does more harm than good and ends by charging it with describing the easy half of the job and skipping the half that settles the outcome.","distractorNotes":[null,"Calling the image harmful is the opposite of neutral.","Too strong: the complaint is that the image is incomplete, and no researcher is accused of unsafe work.","Nothing in paragraph 11 is playful, and the author treats the metaphor as a live problem.","The author presents the phrase as a source of confusion, not as a helpful simplification."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In describing the measures taken against cutting at unintended sites, the author is best described as","choices":["dismissive, treating the measures as amounting to very little in practice","resigned, treating further progress on the problem as unlikely","puzzled, treating the measures as working for reasons nobody has established","candid, treating the measures as genuine and the problem as still open","confident, treating the problem as one the measures have brought under control"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"The countermeasures are real and partial."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":6,"quote":"remains a measurement problem of its own"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 calls the countermeasures real and partial in the same breath, lists three of them, and then notes that finding rare off target events is still a measurement problem of its own.","distractorNotes":["The author calls the countermeasures real and describes three of them without belittling any.","The author leaves the problem open rather than predicting that it will not improve.","Paragraph 14 explains exactly why each measure works, so nothing there is presented as mysterious.",null,"The word partial, and the unsolved detection problem at the end, rule out any sense of control."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Cutting both strands of the helix is not required in order to change a single base.","A guide that matches its target perfectly will cut that sequence wherever it occurs.","The order of the spacers in an array carries information about when each threat was met.","Some viruses that infect bacteria can shut the system down.","The same protein can be aimed at a new target without altering the protein itself."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"the array accumulates chronologically with the most recent threat at the front","choice":2},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"Retargeting the newer system means ordering a different short strand of RNA.","choice":4},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"editors that nick one strand and chemically convert a single base","choice":0},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"Phages already carry proteins that shut the system down","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"only sequences sitting next to the right two letters can be attacked","choice":1}],"why":"Paragraph 10 makes a perfect match insufficient on its own: only sequences sitting next to the motif can be attacked, so a flawless guide is stopped wherever the motif is missing.","distractorNotes":["Supported, so not the exception: paragraph 13 describes editors that nick one strand and convert a base.",null,"Supported, so not the exception: paragraph 7 has new spacers enter at the leader end, making the array chronological.","Supported, so not the exception: paragraph 16 notes that phages carry proteins that shut the system down.","Supported, so not the exception: paragraph 12 says retargeting means ordering a different short strand of RNA."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for an explanation of why the repeats stay evenly spaced as the record grows should turn to","choices":["paragraph 13","paragraph 7","paragraph 1","paragraph 2","paragraph 9"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"which is why the alternating pattern survives every time the record grows"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 supplies the reason: each insertion also duplicates one copy of the repeat, so the alternating pattern survives every time a spacer is added.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 13 concerns what a cell does after a break, a different subject altogether.",null,"Paragraph 1 describes the alternating pattern as it was first seen, without saying what maintains it.","Paragraph 2 reports how widespread the arrays are and what sits beside them.","Paragraph 9 is about finding and cutting a target, not about the structure of the array."]}]},"rc-p12":{"id":"rc-p12","title":"The Heart's Own Current and the Pacemaker","topic":"cardiac conduction and the artificial pacemaker","field":"physiology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1383,"paragraphs":[["A heart lifted out of the body, kept warm and oxygenated, goes on beating with no brain attached.","That observation contains the whole subject.","The signal behind each beat is manufactured inside the heart, by a small population of cells that behave nothing like the muscle around them.","Every cardiac device ever built stands in for that internal source."],["Ordinary working muscle cells are electrically patient.","Each holds a steady negative voltage and waits for a neighbor's wave of charge to shove it past threshold.","Such cells make an excellent relay and a hopeless starter."],["Pacemaker cells never settle.","Their membranes are leaky in a particular way: the moment one reaches its most negative point, channels swing open that admit a slow trickle of positive charge, and the cell starts climbing again.","At roughly minus forty millivolts the cell fires, its upstroke carried mostly by calcium rather than by the fast sodium rush that drives working muscle.","Then it falls back and begins again."],["Several tissues do this at different speeds.","The sinoatrial node, in the wall of the right atrium, drifts to threshold sixty to one hundred times a minute.","The atrioventricular junction manages forty to sixty.","The Purkinje fibers in the ventricular walls are slower still, twenty to forty.","Since all of them are excitable, the fastest wins: its wave resets the others before they finish.","The node leads by speed alone, not by rank, and the slower sites wait underneath as backups."],["The wave leaving the node spreads across both atria, then meets a wall.","A ring of fibrous tissue separates atria from ventricles and carries no current.","One door leads through it, the atrioventricular node, low in the right atrium.","Beyond that door the signal enters the bundle of His, splits into two bundle branches, and fans through the Purkinje network, which hands it to ventricular muscle near the apex.","The ventricles therefore contract from the bottom upward, wringing blood toward the great vessels."],["At the door the signal slows.","Conduction through the atrioventricular node takes about a tenth of a second, and the slowness is built in.","Its cells are thin, they are joined by comparatively few of the protein channels that pass current from cell to cell, and their upstroke depends on calcium.","The pause buys the ventricles their last measure of filling."],["The delay does a second job that is easy to overlook.","It rations.","Flutter runs the atria at about three hundred impulses a minute, and fibrillation is faster still, with no order at all.","The ventricles cannot follow either rhythm and survive, and nothing in the ventricles is equipped to refuse.","The node refuses on their behalf, passing only a fraction.","A narrow, slow, easily exhausted passage is poor design for speed and excellent design for protection.","The most conspicuous bottleneck in the system is also its finest safety feature."],["When conduction along that route falters, clinicians call it a block, and the word misleads anyone who hears it as a plug in a pipe.","Nothing is obstructed.","A block is a failure of the signal to arrive, late or not at all, at tissue perfectly able to contract.","The grades matter, because they are separate problems sharing a name."],["In first degree block every impulse gets through, only late: the atrial to ventricular interval stretches past two hundred milliseconds.","In the second degree pattern known as Wenckebach, that interval lengthens with each beat until one impulse fails and a beat is dropped.","Both usually sit in the node itself, both turn up in healthy people, particularly in athletes at rest and in almost anyone asleep, and neither alone is a reason to act.","Most blocks are a finding, not a disease."],["The dangerous ones look different.","In the second degree pattern called Mobitz type II, beats drop without warning, which places the fault below the node, in the His and Purkinje wiring, where tissue tends to fail further.","In complete block nothing crosses.","The atria carry on at their own pace while the ventricles fall back on whatever escape rhythm they can raise, often only twenty to forty beats a minute, prone to pausing long enough that the person faints where they stand.","That is the problem the artificial pacemaker was built to solve."],["Nineteenth century investigators had driven an animal heart at any rate they chose, and by the early 1930s a physician had built a hand cranked device he called an artificial pacemaker.","Practical machines arrived in the 1950s, and they worked, which was the remarkable part.","They were also brutal.","Pacing ran through electrodes on the chest wall, so the current crossed skin and muscle, with burns and pain severe enough that patients were sedated.","The units were the size of a cabinet on wheels and ran on wall current."],["Wall current was the flaw that broke the design open.","In 1957 a power failure cut electricity to a hospital where children were being paced, and a surgeon asked a local repairman to build something independent of the grid.","The answer was a battery powered box small enough to wear, wired through the chest wall to the heart."],["The first fully implanted unit went into a patient in Stockholm in 1958.","It failed within hours and a second within days.","The limit was now chemical.","Those units ran on zinc and mercuric oxide cells that gave twelve to twenty four months of service and vented hydrogen gas as they discharged, so the case could not be sealed.","Body fluid crept in and shorted the circuits."],["Two answers were tried.","One was nuclear: a pellet of plutonium warm from its own decay driving a thermoelectric converter, which lasted decades but carried a regulatory burden.","The other, introduced in 1972, was the lithium iodine cell, which used a solid electrolyte, produced no gas, and could therefore be sealed inside a welded titanium can.","Within a decade the unit could sense the patient's own beats and hold its pulse, be reprogrammed through the skin, and report on its own condition."],["Here the story takes an unexpected turn.","The generator, the part that looked hard, became the reliable part: batteries now announce their decline months ahead.","The stubborn problem turned out to be the lead, the insulated wire running from the can through a vein into the heart, the one component that has never stopped failing.","A lead must flex with every heartbeat, on the order of one hundred thousand times a day and forty million times a year.","Conductors fracture, classically where the lead is pinched between collarbone and first rib, and insulation cracks."],["The tip has troubles of its own.","A pacing pulse counts only if it is strong enough to capture, meaning that it actually provokes a contraction rather than fading at the electrode, and the energy needed is not fixed.","Scar tissue forms around a fresh tip over the first weeks and drives the requirement up.","The fixes were small and elegant.","Tips shrank to a few square millimeters, concentrating the same current into a higher density, and the metal was made to release a steroid slowly into the surrounding tissue.","Lower capture energy meant smaller pulses, and smaller pulses meant a longer battery."],["Taking an old lead out is harder than putting a new one in.","The tissue reaction that anchors the tip also binds the lead to the vein wall, and after a few years the wire is no longer a separate object.","Locking stylets and cutting sheaths exist for the job, but the vein the lead has fused to tears easily.","This is much of why self contained capsules, with no wire leaving the heart, have drawn such interest, and equally why enthusiasm for them has stayed measured rather than triumphant.","A device with no lead cannot suffer a lead fracture.","It will still have to come out someday."],["A dual chamber pacemaker senses activity in the atrium, waits, and then paces the ventricle, and the wait is programmable, usually between about one hundred twenty and two hundred milliseconds.","Engineers spent five decades building a machine that could reproduce, in titanium and software, the tenth of a second that a knot of unremarkable tissue on the floor of the right atrium has been giving away for nothing."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p12-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Purkinje fibers in the ventricular walls reach threshold on their own at a rate of about","choices":["sixty to one hundred times a minute","one hundred twenty to two hundred times a minute","forty to sixty times a minute","twenty to forty times a minute","three hundred times a minute"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"The Purkinje fibers in the ventricular walls are slower still, twenty to forty."}],"why":"Paragraph 4 lists the pacemaker sites in order from fastest to slowest, and the Purkinje fibers come last at twenty to forty times a minute.","distractorNotes":["That is the sinoatrial node's rate, given first in the same list.","Those numbers are the programmable waiting interval in paragraph 18, which is measured in milliseconds rather than beats.","That is the rate of the atrioventricular junction, the middle member of the list.",null,"Three hundred a minute is the atrial rate in flutter from paragraph 7, not any pacemaker site's own pace."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage explains that conduction through the atrioventricular node is slow partly because","choices":["its cells are joined by comparatively few of the channels that pass current between cells","its cells cannot reach threshold on their own and must wait to be driven from above","a ring of fibrous tissue runs through the middle of the node and blocks part of the current","its cells depend on the same fast inward sodium rush that drives ordinary working muscle","the wave has to cross the whole of both atria before any part of it arrives there"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"joined by comparatively few of the protein channels that pass current from cell to cell"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 gives three built in reasons for the delay: the cells are thin, they are joined by comparatively few of the channels that pass current from cell to cell, and their upstroke depends on calcium.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 4 says the atrioventricular junction reaches threshold on its own at forty to sixty a minute, so this reverses the passage.","The fibrous ring in paragraph 5 surrounds the route and leaves one door through it, and the passage never places it inside the node.","Paragraph 6 says the opposite, that the upstroke there depends on calcium rather than on the fast sodium rush.","That spread across the atria is described in paragraph 5, but it happens before the node and is not offered as a reason the node itself is slow."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the zinc and mercuric oxide cells in the earliest implanted units created a design problem because they","choices":["could not deliver enough current to trigger a contraction of the chamber","gave off a gas as they discharged, so the case could not be sealed","lost all of their remaining capacity suddenly and with no warning at all","needed a thermoelectric converter to turn their heat into usable current","could only be recharged by a coil held against the skin for hours"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"vented hydrogen gas as they discharged, so the case could not be sealed"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says those cells vented hydrogen gas as they discharged, which is why the case could not be sealed. Body fluid then crept in and shorted the circuits.","distractorNotes":["Capture and the energy a pulse needs come up in paragraph 16, and the passage never blames the early cells for weak output.",null,"Paragraph 13 describes those cells as short lived, twelve to twenty four months, rather than abrupt, and paragraph 15 says modern batteries give months of notice.","The thermoelectric converter belongs to the nuclear unit in paragraph 14, which was a different answer to the same problem.","Recharging never appears in the passage. Paragraph 14 mentions reprogramming through the skin, which is a different thing entirely."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage states that a pacing lead is flexed roughly","choices":["one thousand times a day","one million times a day","forty million times a day","one hundred thousand times a year","one hundred thousand times a day"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"on the order of one hundred thousand times a day and forty million times a year"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 gives both figures in one sentence, about one hundred thousand flexes a day and forty million a year. The daily figure is the one asked for here.","distractorNotes":["That is far below the number given, and the passage ties the flexing to every single heartbeat.","No such figure appears, and a million beats a day is far beyond any rate the passage describes.","Forty million is the yearly figure from that same sentence, not the daily one.","This swaps the two units. One hundred thousand is the daily count in paragraph 15.",null]},{"id":"rc-p12-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Which two changes to the electrode tip does the passage credit with lowering the energy a pulse needs?","choices":["a welded titanium can and a solid electrolyte sealed inside it","a locking stylet and a sheath that cuts a path along the body of the lead","a larger tip surface and a stiffer, thicker conductor running to it","a much smaller tip and the slow release of a steroid into nearby tissue","an atrial sensor and a waiting interval the operator can program"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"Tips shrank to a few square millimeters"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"the metal was made to release a steroid slowly into the surrounding tissue"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 names both fixes: tips shrank to a few square millimeters, which concentrates the same current into a higher density, and the metal was made to release a steroid slowly into the surrounding tissue.","distractorNotes":["Those belong to the battery and its case in paragraph 14, which is a different part of the device.","Those are the lead extraction tools from paragraph 17 and have nothing to do with the energy a pulse needs.","Paragraph 16 says the tips got smaller rather than larger, precisely so the same current is concentrated into a higher density.",null,"That describes the dual chamber timing in paragraph 18 rather than anything done to the tip."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, \"leaky\" most nearly means","choices":["damaged in a way that allows charge to escape","unable to hold any voltage across the membrane at all","joined to neighboring cells by channels that stay open","steadily admitting a small inward current","liable to tear open under mechanical pressure"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"channels swing open that admit a slow trickle of positive charge"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 uses the word for a membrane that admits a slow trickle of positive charge as soon as the cell reaches its most negative point. It is a normal working feature of these cells, not damage.","distractorNotes":["The everyday sense of a leak is something escaping through a flaw, but in paragraph 3 the charge moves inward and nothing is broken.","The cell does hold a voltage. It simply will not hold still at one value, since it climbs toward threshold instead.","Channels between neighboring cells appear in paragraph 6, where their scarcity slows the node, and that is a separate idea.",null,"This borrows the plumbing sense of a leak. Nothing in paragraph 3 involves pressure or tearing."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 16, \"capture\" most nearly means","choices":["to record the chamber's electrical activity for later review","to hold the tip of the lead firmly against the chamber wall","to succeed in triggering a contraction","to soak up the energy of the pulse without responding to it","to take over pacing duty from the sinoatrial node permanently"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"meaning that it actually provokes a contraction rather than fading at the electrode"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 defines the word right where it uses it: a pulse captures when it actually provokes a contraction rather than fading at the electrode.","distractorNotes":["Recording is a fair everyday sense of the word, but the passage is describing a pulse the device sends out, not data it takes in.","Anchoring the tip is discussed in paragraph 17, where scar tissue binds the lead, and the word capture is never used for it.",null,"This is the opposite of the passage's meaning, since a pulse that fades at the electrode is exactly one that failed to capture.","Taking over from the sinoatrial node describes an escape rhythm in paragraph 10, while capture in paragraph 16 refers to a single pulse."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Suppose the sinoatrial node in a particular heart slowed to about thirty impulses a minute. The passage most supports which expectation?","choices":["The ventricles would stop entirely until the node recovered its usual speed.","The atrioventricular junction would be expected to set the rate instead.","The fibrous ring would begin to carry current from the atria downward.","The Purkinje fibers would speed up to about sixty impulses a minute.","The heart would by definition be in complete block from that moment on."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"Since all of them are excitable, the fastest wins"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"The atrioventricular junction manages forty to sixty."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"the slower sites wait underneath as backups"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the fastest site wins and the slower sites wait underneath as backups. At thirty a minute the node is no longer fastest, so the junction, listed at forty to sixty, would reach threshold first.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 describes the slower sites as backups, which is exactly why a slowed node does not stop the heart.",null,"Paragraph 5 says the fibrous ring carries no current, and nothing in the passage suggests that ever changes.","The rates in paragraph 4 are each site's own pace, and nothing lets a slower site run faster than its own.","Paragraph 10 defines complete block as nothing crossing from atria to ventricles, which is a failure to conduct rather than a slow node."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which statement about a person whose atria are firing about three hundred times a minute?","choices":["The Purkinje network will turn away the extra impulses on its own.","The ventricles will follow every one of those impulses unless the atria are stopped.","The sinoatrial node will take control back as soon as the fibrous ring conducts.","Any degree of block at the node would make the situation more dangerous.","The ventricles are spared largely because the node passes only a fraction of them."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"The node refuses on their behalf, passing only a fraction."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"nothing in the ventricles is equipped to refuse"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the ventricles cannot follow either of those atrial rhythms and survive, and that nothing in them is equipped to refuse, so the node refuses on their behalf and passes only a fraction.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 7 says nothing in the ventricles is equipped to refuse, which is why the node has to do the refusing.","This ignores the rationing in paragraph 7, which is precisely what keeps the ventricles from following the atria.","Paragraph 5 says the fibrous ring carries no current, and paragraph 7 gives the node no role in restoring a normal rhythm.","Paragraph 7 treats the node's habit of holding impulses back as protective here, so slowing at the node is the opposite of dangerous.",null]},{"id":"rc-p12-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Better battery chemistry is the single main reason implanted pacemakers eventually became practical devices.","Most conduction abnormalities that turn up on a tracing are harmless findings that call for no treatment of any kind.","The heart's own signal proved hard to imitate, and the effort to replace it kept relocating the hardest problem.","The atrioventricular node is the most fragile structure in the heart and the usual site of serious disease.","Self contained capsules placed inside the heart have now resolved the problems that every earlier design could not."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"Every cardiac device ever built stands in for that internal source."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"The stubborn problem turned out to be the lead"},{"paragraph":18,"sentence":2,"quote":"Engineers spent five decades building a machine that could reproduce"}],"why":"The passage runs from how the heart makes and routes its own signal, through what happens when that routing fails, to the devices built to stand in, and it ends with the hard problem moving from the power source to the wire.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 14 does credit the lithium iodine cell, but paragraph 15 says the battery stopped being the hard part, so this covers only one stretch of the story.","Paragraph 9 makes that point about first degree block and Wenckebach, and paragraph 10 turns immediately to the blocks that do need a device.",null,"Paragraph 7 treats the node's narrowness as a safety feature, and the passage never calls it the most fragile structure or the usual site of disease.","Paragraph 17 says enthusiasm for those capsules has stayed measured, and that a capsule will still have to come out someday."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 7 is included mainly in order to","choices":["show that a feature that looks like a weakness is also the system's best protection","introduce the grading of blocks that the next two paragraphs go on to lay out in detail","explain why a disorganized atrial rhythm has to be treated as a medical emergency","argue that the heart would be better served by a faster route through the fibrous ring","prepare the reader for the external devices of the 1950s and the trouble they caused"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":7,"quote":"The most conspicuous bottleneck in the system is also its finest safety feature."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"poor design for speed and excellent design for protection"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 explains the delay in terms of filling. Paragraph 7 adds the second job, rationing, and closes by saying the most conspicuous bottleneck in the system is also its finest safety feature.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 8 does that work, and paragraph 7 is still describing the normal node rather than any block.","Flutter and fibrillation are mentioned only to show what the node protects against, and treatment of them never comes up.","This inverts the paragraph, which praises the narrow slow passage instead of wishing it were faster.","Those devices arrive in paragraph 11, and paragraph 7 gives no hint of the history that follows."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["It alternates between individual patient cases and the laboratory findings that stand behind each of them.","It lays out a natural system, how it fails, the devices built to replace it, and where the difficulty settled.","It states an argument at the very start and then spends the rest of its length answering objections raised to it.","It follows one strict chronology from the nineteenth century forward to the present day without any digression.","It compares two competing devices point by point and then recommends one of them over the other."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"The signal behind each beat is manufactured inside the heart"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"When conduction along that route falters, clinicians call it a block"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"That is the problem the artificial pacemaker was built to solve."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"Here the story takes an unexpected turn."}],"why":"The first half builds the natural conduction system and the blocks that interrupt it, the middle traces the devices built to replace it, and paragraphs 15 through 17 show the hard problem moving from the generator to the lead.","distractorNotes":["No individual patient cases are presented anywhere in the passage.",null,"The passage is explanatory rather than argumentative, and no objection is ever raised and then answered.","The history in paragraphs 11 through 14 is chronological, but the first ten paragraphs are not a history at all.","Only one kind of device is traced here, and the passage recommends nothing to anyone."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's treatment of the external pacing machines of the 1950s is best described as","choices":["admiring of how elegant and comfortable they had become by then","dismissive of the physicians who were willing to use them on patients","candid about how crude they were while granting that they worked","neutral throughout, offering no evaluation of them in either direction","regretful that anyone attempted such a thing before better tools existed"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"they worked, which was the remarkable part"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"They were also brutal."}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the machines worked and calls that the remarkable part, then in the next breath calls them brutal and describes burns, sedation, and a cabinet on wheels.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph describes burns, pain, sedation and a unit the size of a cabinet, none of which reads as elegant.","No criticism is aimed at the people using the machines. The judgment falls on the machines themselves.",null,"Words like brutal and remarkable are plain evaluations, so the paragraph is anything but neutral.","The passage treats these machines as a real step forward, and paragraph 12 builds directly on them."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward self contained capsules placed inside the heart is best described as","choices":["confident that they remove the last serious weakness left in the system","dismissive of them as a novelty with no real advantage to offer anyone","openly doubtful that they can pace a chamber reliably at all","interested while holding back full endorsement","worried that they will make lead extraction a far more common operation"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":17,"sentence":4,"quote":"enthusiasm for them has stayed measured rather than triumphant"}],"why":"Paragraph 17 explains why the capsules are attractive, since a device with no lead cannot suffer a lead fracture, and then notes that enthusiasm has stayed measured rather than triumphant and that the capsule will still have to come out someday.","distractorNotes":["The closing lines of paragraph 17 pull back from exactly that confidence by raising the question of retrieval.","The paragraph says the capsules have drawn such interest, which is not dismissal, and it names a genuine advantage.","Pacing reliability is never questioned. The reservation on offer is about getting the device out later.",null,"The passage suggests the opposite, since a capsule has no lead at all to be extracted."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["The sinoatrial node leads because it reaches threshold first, not because of any special standing.","A dropped beat can show up in a person with a perfectly healthy heart.","A ventricular escape rhythm is as steady and dependable as the node it replaces.","A cell with a solid electrolyte made a fully sealed pacemaker case possible.","A pacing lead has to bend with every beat for as long as it stays in place."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"The node leads by speed alone, not by rank","choice":0},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"one impulse fails and a beat is dropped","choice":1},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"both turn up in healthy people","choice":1},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"could therefore be sealed inside a welded titanium can","choice":3},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"A lead must flex with every heartbeat","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"prone to pausing long enough that the person faints where they stand","choice":2}],"why":"Paragraph 10 calls the escape rhythm slow, often only twenty to forty a minute, and prone to pausing long enough that the person faints, which is the opposite of dependable. The other four statements come from paragraphs 4, 9, 14 and 15.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 says the node leads by speed alone and not by rank, so this one is supported.","Paragraph 9 says the Wenckebach pattern, in which a beat is dropped, turns up in healthy people, so this one is supported.",null,"Paragraph 14 credits the solid electrolyte of the lithium iodine cell with allowing a welded titanium can, so this one is supported.","Paragraph 15 gives the flexing figures for a lead, about one hundred thousand times a day, so this one is supported."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wants to know why the ventricles begin contracting near the bottom of the heart rather than at the top should look in","choices":["paragraph 3","paragraph 5","paragraph 7","paragraph 10","paragraph 16"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"hands it to ventricular muscle near the apex"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"The ventricles therefore contract from the bottom upward"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 traces the signal past the bundle of His and through the Purkinje network to ventricular muscle near the apex, which is why the squeeze runs from the bottom upward.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 is about how a single pacemaker cell drifts to threshold, not about where the wave travels.",null,"Paragraph 7 is about the node holding back a flood of atrial impulses.","Paragraph 10 covers the dangerous blocks and the escape rhythms that follow them.","Paragraph 16 is about the electrode tip and the energy a pulse needs."]}]},"rc-p13":{"id":"rc-p13","title":"The Bond That Made Nitrogen Scarce","topic":"nitrogen fixation and industrial ammonia synthesis","field":"chemistry","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1373,"paragraphs":[["Roughly seventy eight percent of the air is nitrogen, and yet nitrogen is the nutrient that most often decides how much a field can yield.","The line from one chemical bond to a coastal dead zone is shorter than it looks."],["Begin with the bond.","The two atoms in nitrogen gas are held by a triple bond, three shared pairs of electrons, and pulling it apart costs roughly nine hundred and forty five kilojoules per mole.","The molecule is also symmetrical end to end, with no partially positive or partially negative region where a reagent could get a grip.","Nitrogen gas is therefore not unreactive because reacting is impossible.","Most of its reactions release energy overall.","It is unreactive because the first step, breaking that bond, sits behind a wall almost nothing at ordinary temperatures can climb."],["Lightning supplies enough local energy to force the issue, tearing nitrogen and oxygen apart to recombine as oxides that rain out as nitrate.","Measured against what the world's crops pull from the soil each season, that natural supply is a rounding error."],["The route that mattered for most of life's history is biological.","Scattered bacteria and archaea, called diazotrophs, carry an enzyme named nitrogenase that does at ordinary temperature and pressure what industry still cannot do without extreme conditions.","At its center a cluster of iron and molybdenum atoms grips the nitrogen molecule and feeds electrons into it one at a time, weakening the bond in stages.","The accounting is brutal.","Reducing one molecule of nitrogen gas to two molecules of ammonia consumes sixteen molecules of ATP, the cell's energy currency."],["Sixteen ATP is an enormous bill, and the cheapest way a cell can pay it is aerobic respiration, which requires oxygen.","Yet oxygen poisons nitrogenase.","Not in the mild, reversible way a competing molecule slows an enzyme and then lets go: oxygen attacks the exposed metal cluster and wrecks it, and the cell must build a replacement from scratch.","A fixing organism therefore needs oxygen present in one compartment and absent in another, micrometers apart.","Everything interesting about the biology follows from that one conflict."],["Different lineages settled it differently.","Filamentous cyanobacteria build heterocysts, thick walled cells that slow gas entry and lack the oxygen producing half of photosynthesis, so the fixing cell makes none of the gas that would destroy its enzyme.","Legumes, including peas and clover, strike a bargain with soil bacteria instead.","The plant grows a nodule on its root, houses the bacteria inside, and floods it with leghemoglobin, an oxygen binding protein like the one that reddens blood.","Leghemoglobin does something more elegant than shutting oxygen out: it holds free oxygen in the nodule at a vanishingly low concentration while still ferrying a steady supply to the bacteria, so the bill gets paid and the enzyme survives."],["Farmers exploited the arrangement for thousands of years with no idea it existed.","Rotating a cereal with a legume, or plowing clover back into the ground, restored something to a tired field.","For most of agricultural history the usable nitrogen supply was capped by whatever those bacteria, plus manure and a little lightning, happened to deliver."],["By the nineteenth century that cap was being felt.","European agriculture leaned on Peruvian seabird guano and Chilean desert nitrate, deposits that were rich and finite.","In 1898 the president of the British Association for the Advancement of Science devoted his annual address to the arithmetic: wheat consumption was climbing, the nitrate beds would not last, and the shortfall would have to come from the air.","Within fifteen years the chemists had answered him."],["Fritz Haber demonstrated the reaction on a laboratory bench in 1909, coaxing a slow drip of ammonia out of nitrogen and hydrogen passed over a heated catalyst.","Carl Bosch and his colleagues then spent four years turning that result into a factory, inventing steel vessels that held under sustained pressure and screening thousands of catalysts.","The modern version still runs near four hundred to five hundred degrees Celsius and one hundred and fifty to three hundred atmospheres, and still uses iron."],["Combining nitrogen with hydrogen to make ammonia gives off heat, and it turns four molecules of gas into two.","Both facts argue for running the reaction cold and squeezed, since low temperature favors a heat releasing product and high pressure favors the side with fewer gas molecules.","But a low temperature also means a bond nothing wants to break breaks even more slowly.","Engineers accept a hotter reactor than equilibrium would prefer, since a small share of a fast reaction beats a large share of one that never finishes, then raise the pressure to claw back yield."],["The catalyst is the other half of the bargain, and it acts on rate alone.","It cannot move where equilibrium lies; it only lowers the barrier to getting there.","The working material is iron promoted with small quantities of potassium and aluminum oxides, additives that carry out no reaction themselves but keep the iron surface open and lift its activity.","Even so, one pass converts only ten to twenty percent of the feed, so the unreacted gas is cooled, stripped of its ammonia, and sent around again."],["The consequences were not subtle.","World population stood near one and a half billion in 1900 and near eight billion now, and by common estimate close to half the nitrogen in the protein of a person alive today entered the food chain through an ammonia reactor rather than a root nodule."],["It is tempting to stop there and file the process under unambiguous triumph, and that reading is too clean.","The trouble is not the reaction, which is remarkably good at what it does.","The trouble is everything downstream of the reactor.","Across the world's farms, well under half of the nitrogen applied ends up in the harvested crop.","A technology can be an extraordinary piece of chemistry and a careless piece of plumbing at once, and the honest description of synthetic nitrogen is that it is both."],["The leaking has a chemical reason.","Soil particles carry a net negative charge, so positively charged ammonium is held in place by plain attraction.","Soil bacteria, though, convert ammonium to nitrate within days, and nitrate is negative.","The soil repels it, and the next heavy rain carries it past the roots into groundwater or a river.","Some of the surplus leaves as nitrous oxide instead, a gas that traps roughly three hundred times as much heat per molecule as carbon dioxide."],["River water arrives loaded with nutrients and fertilizes a bloom of algae at the surface.","The algae grow, they die, and they sink.","Bacteria near the bottom decompose the falling material and consume dissolved oxygen as they work.","Meanwhile the warm fresh river water floats on colder, saltier seawater in a layer that resists mixing, so surface oxygen cannot get down to replace what the bacteria are using.","The bottom water goes hypoxic.","Each summer the hypoxic zone at the mouth of the Mississippi covers several thousand square miles."],["The hydrogen for ammonia is stripped out of natural gas with steam, so most of the world's fertilizer begins as fossil carbon and releases carbon dioxide before it reaches a field.","Ammonia synthesis accounts for between one and two percent of global energy use and a comparable share of industrial carbon emissions.","That is a large bill for one reaction, and not one that can simply be refused, because the alternative to the fertilizer is not a cleaner fertilizer but a great deal less food."],["Which is why the promising work aims at the losses, not the reaction.","Applying fertilizer in several small doses timed to the crop's demand, instead of one heavy dose in spring, leaves less nitrate in wet soil.","Compounds that slow the bacterial conversion of ammonium to nitrate buy the roots more time.","Cover crops take up leftover nitrogen through the winter, and legume rotations supply part of the need biologically.","Making the hydrogen by splitting water with renewable electricity would address the fuel side without touching the chemistry.","None of it repeals the triple bond.","That bond is why nitrogen was scarce and why the remedy is expensive, and that much has not changed since 1909."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p13-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 2, nitrogen gas in the air resists reacting mainly because","choices":["its reactions with other elements take in more energy than they give off","breaking the bond between its two atoms demands an enormous first push","one end of the molecule is partially negative and pushes reagents away","it is far too dilute in the atmosphere for reactions to get started","its two atoms share two pairs of electrons instead of three"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"breaking that bond, sits behind a wall almost nothing at ordinary temperatures can climb"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"Most of its reactions release energy overall."},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"no partially positive or partially negative region"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 makes the point directly. Most nitrogen reactions release energy overall, so the block is not that reacting is impossible. The block is the first step, breaking a triple bond that costs roughly nine hundred forty five kilojoules per mole.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the opposite, that most of nitrogen's reactions release energy overall.",null,"Paragraph 2 calls the molecule symmetrical, with no partially positive or partially negative region at all.","Nitrogen is about seventy eight percent of the air, so being too dilute is not the problem.","A triple bond is three shared pairs rather than two, and that third pair is the whole difficulty."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q02","type":"detail","stem":"Paragraph 4 states that turning one molecule of nitrogen gas into ammonia costs the cell","choices":["two molecules of ATP, one for each ammonia made","eight molecules of ATP","sixteen molecules of ATP, but only in cells that lack oxygen","sixteen molecules of ATP","an amount of energy the passage treats as negligible"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"consumes sixteen molecules of ATP"}],"why":"This is a straight number check against paragraph 4. Reducing one nitrogen molecule to two ammonia molecules runs the cell sixteen ATP, which is why the passage calls the accounting brutal.","distractorNotes":["The passage never ties the ATP count to the number of ammonia molecules made, and the real figure is far higher.","Eight is not the number given, and it would make the cost sound modest rather than brutal.","The number is right, but the passage attaches no oxygen condition to it; the oxygen problem arrives in the next paragraph.",null,"Paragraph 4 calls the accounting brutal, which is the opposite of negligible."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 6, leghemoglobin serves the root nodule by","choices":["sealing the nodule so that no oxygen at all can get in","thickening the nodule wall so that gases enter slowly","holding free oxygen very low while still feeding oxygen to the bacteria","converting ammonium into nitrate that the plant can absorb","supplying the electrons that nitrogenase pushes into the nitrogen molecule"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"holds free oxygen in the nodule at a vanishingly low concentration while still ferrying"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"more elegant than shutting oxygen out"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 draws the distinction for you. Leghemoglobin does something more elegant than shutting oxygen out: it keeps free oxygen vanishingly low in the nodule while still ferrying a steady supply to the bacteria so they can pay the ATP bill.","distractorNotes":["This is the reading the paragraph specifically rules out, since the bacteria still need oxygen to make their ATP.","That is the heterocyst solution used by cyanobacteria, not what leghemoglobin does.",null,"Soil bacteria make that conversion in paragraph 14, out in the field rather than inside the nodule.","Paragraph 6 gives leghemoglobin an oxygen handling job only, and says nothing about where the electrons come from."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q04","type":"detail","stem":"Paragraph 11 reports that one pass of the feed gas through the reactor","choices":["turns nearly all of the nitrogen and hydrogen into ammonia","converts only about ten to twenty percent of it","ruins the iron catalyst, which then has to be replaced","shifts the position of equilibrium toward more ammonia","removes any need for high pressure later on"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"one pass converts only ten to twenty percent of the feed"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 gives the figure at the end. A single pass converts only ten to twenty percent of the feed, which is why the leftover gas is cooled, stripped of its ammonia and sent around again.","distractorNotes":["If that were true there would be no reason to recycle the leftover gas, which the paragraph says is standard.",null,"The passage never says the catalyst is used up; it says the catalyst lowers the barrier to reaction.","Paragraph 11 states plainly that a catalyst cannot move where equilibrium lies, only how fast the reaction gets there.","High pressure is still part of the design in paragraph 10, and the low conversion is exactly why it is needed."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 15, oxygen fails to reach the bottom water near the river mouth because","choices":["the algae at the surface use it up before any of it can sink","nitrate binds it chemically and pulls it out of solution","the bacteria near the bottom give off nitrous oxide in place of oxygen","wind stirs the surface layer downward only during winter","warm fresh water floats on colder salty water and resists mixing"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"floats on colder, saltier seawater in a layer that resists mixing"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 puts the layering in the middle of the sequence. Warm fresh river water sits on colder, saltier seawater, and that boundary keeps surface oxygen from getting down to replace what the bacteria are using.","distractorNotes":["The algae fuel the problem by dying and sinking, and the oxygen is consumed by bacteria on the bottom.","Nitrate in this passage feeds the algal bloom, and no chemical binding of oxygen is described.","Nitrous oxide appears in paragraph 14 as a loss from farm soil, not as something released on the sea floor.","The passage never mentions wind or a winter cycle, so this comes from outside the text.",null]},{"id":"rc-p13-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 5, \"poisons\" most nearly means","choices":["makes unfit to eat","slows for a while and then lets go","damages beyond repair","coats with a trace impurity","makes unpleasant to the taste"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"oxygen attacks the exposed metal cluster and wrecks it"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"Not in the mild, reversible way"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 defines the word by contrast. Oxygen does not slow nitrogenase the way a competing molecule does and then release it; it wrecks the metal cluster so thoroughly that the cell has to build a new one.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday sense of the word, and nothing in this sentence is being eaten.","The paragraph names that reversible behavior only in order to say this is not it.",null,"The passage describes real destruction of the metal cluster, not a film or a contaminant.","Taste plays no part here, since the subject is an enzyme being put out of action."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 11, \"promoted\" most nearly means","choices":["advertised to possible buyers","moved up to a higher rank","heated thoroughly before use","made more active by added substances","purified until no other element remains on the surface"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"keep the iron surface open and lift its activity"}],"why":"The sentence explains itself in paragraph 11. The iron carries small amounts of potassium and aluminum oxides, additives that run no reaction of their own but keep the surface open and lift the iron's activity.","distractorNotes":["That is the marketing sense of the word, which has no place in a description of a catalyst surface.","Rank belongs to people and jobs, while the paragraph is describing what additives do to a metal.","Heating is set by the operating temperature in paragraph 9, not by the word describing the additives.",null,"The additives are deliberately present, so the iron is not being cleaned of everything else."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which conclusion about a fertilizer that stayed in the ammonium form longer than usual?","choices":["It would wash out of the soil more easily during a heavy rain","It would linger in the soil and reach the water less readily","It would stop soil bacteria from growing anywhere near the roots","It would remove the need to fertilize that field in later seasons","It would keep nitrous oxide from forming under any conditions"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"positively charged ammonium is held in place by plain attraction"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"the next heavy rain carries it past the roots into groundwater"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":3,"quote":"buy the roots more time"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 supplies both halves. Soil particles are negatively charged, so positive ammonium is held in place, while nitrate is negative and gets repelled and carried off. Ammonium that lingers is ammonium that stays put.","distractorNotes":["That is the behavior of nitrate, the negatively charged form the soil pushes away.",null,"Nothing in the passage says ammonium harms bacteria; paragraph 14 says bacteria convert it within days.","The passage never claims one application could carry a field through later seasons.","Paragraph 17 describes slowing that conversion, which is not the same as shutting off nitrous oxide entirely."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q09","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from paragraph 16 that giving up synthetic fertilizer altogether would","choices":["leave the world with a great deal less food","have little effect on the food supply, since biology could take over at once","make the nitrogen in the air easier for crops to use directly","cut global energy use roughly in half","raise the share of applied nitrogen that crops take up"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"not a cleaner fertilizer but a great deal less food"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 closes on exactly this point. The bill for ammonia synthesis is large, but it cannot simply be refused, because the alternative to the fertilizer is not a cleaner fertilizer but a great deal less food.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 12 says close to half the nitrogen in human protein comes through the reactor, so the effect would be large.","Nothing about the triple bond changes, and paragraph 17 says none of the remedies repeal it.","The passage puts ammonia synthesis at one to two percent of global energy use, not half.","Uptake depends on how nitrate behaves in soil, and closing the factory would not change that chemistry."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Biological fixation in root nodules is more efficient than the industrial process and could replace it if farmers planted more legumes","Coastal dead zones are the single most serious environmental problem the world now faces","An unusually stable bond kept nitrogen scarce until industry learned to break it, a fix that feeds billions and leaks badly","Early twentieth century chemists solved a problem that farmers had already solved by rotating their fields","Ammonia synthesis should be shut down because its energy cost outweighs what it provides"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"The line from one chemical bond to a coastal dead zone is shorter"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"close to half the nitrogen in the protein of a person alive today"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"an extraordinary piece of chemistry and a careless piece of plumbing at once"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":7,"quote":"That bond is why nitrogen was scarce and why the remedy is expensive"}],"why":"The passage runs from the strength of the triple bond, through the biological and industrial ways around it, to the food it produces and the nitrogen that escapes. Paragraph 13 states the balanced verdict: extraordinary chemistry, careless plumbing.","distractorNotes":["The passage never ranks the two for efficiency, and paragraph 16 says removing the factory would mean far less food.","Dead zones get one paragraph as a consequence, and no claim is made about their rank among world problems.",null,"Farmers raised the ceiling without removing it, since paragraph 7 says the supply stayed capped by what the bacteria delivered.","Paragraph 16 makes the opposite point, that this particular bill cannot simply be refused."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 6 is included mainly to","choices":["show two different ways organisms settle the conflict raised just before it","prove that legumes fix more nitrogen than cyanobacteria do","explain why farmers began rotating cereal crops with legumes in the first place","describe the color of a healthy root nodule","argue that biological fixation ought to replace industrial ammonia"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"Everything interesting about the biology follows from that one conflict."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"Different lineages settled it differently."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"with no idea it existed"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 ends by saying everything interesting in the biology follows from one conflict, oxygen needed and oxygen fatal. Paragraph 6 then lays out two answers to it, the heterocyst and the nodule.","distractorNotes":[null,"The paragraph sets the two strategies side by side without comparing how much nitrogen either one fixes.","Crop rotation is taken up in the next paragraph, while paragraph 6 stays on the biology.","Nodule color is never mentioned, and a detail that small could not carry a paragraph.","The author never makes that argument, and paragraph 16 says the industrial supply cannot simply be given up."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes the way the passage is organized?","choices":["a list of the scientists who worked on nitrogen, in the order they worked","a comparison of two industrial processes that compete with each other today","a claim, a long objection raised against it, and a return to the claim unchanged","a series of regional case studies of how fertilizer is used","one chemical obstacle, its two answers, and the cost of the industrial one"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"Begin with the bond."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"The route that mattered for most of life's history is biological."},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"Within fifteen years the chemists had answered him."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"The trouble is everything downstream of the reactor."}],"why":"The passage opens on the triple bond in paragraph 2, works through biology in paragraphs 4 to 7 and industry in paragraphs 8 to 12, and spends paragraphs 13 to 17 on what the industrial answer costs.","distractorNotes":["Two chemists are named in passing, but the passage is built around a chemical problem rather than around people.","Only one industrial process is described, and nothing is set against it as a competitor.","The author does not return to the opening verdict unchanged, since paragraph 13 revises it on purpose.","No region is studied on its own, and the Mississippi appears once as an example of a general pattern.",null]},{"id":"rc-p13-q13","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 13, the author's attitude toward treating synthetic nitrogen as an unqualified success is best described as","choices":["fully in agreement with it","dismissive of the chemistry behind the process","alarmed enough to call for the process to be shut down","unwilling to accept it without naming what the process loses","indifferent to what happens to the nitrogen after it is spread"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"that reading is too clean"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"the honest description of synthetic nitrogen is that it is both"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"remarkably good at what it does"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 calls the triumphant reading too clean, then supplies the missing half, that well under half of applied nitrogen reaches the crop. The verdict at the end is both, not either.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph opens by calling that reading too clean, which is a refusal rather than agreement.","The reaction is called remarkably good at what it does, so the chemistry is not what draws criticism.","No shutdown is called for anywhere, and paragraph 16 says the fertilizer cannot simply be refused.",null,"Where the nitrogen goes afterward is the whole subject of the paragraph, so indifference does not fit."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude in paragraph 17 toward the measures described there is best characterized as","choices":["encouraged about the losses, clear that the chemistry is unchanged","confident that these steps will end coastal dead zones within a few years","doubtful that any of them is worth trying","irritated that farmers have not adopted them already","certain that they make the industrial process unnecessary"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":17,"sentence":1,"quote":"the promising work aims at the losses, not the reaction"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":6,"quote":"None of it repeals the triple bond."}],"why":"Paragraph 17 lists measures that all aim at the losses, which is a hopeful list, and then closes by saying none of it repeals the triple bond. Positive about the plumbing, honest about the chemistry.","distractorNotes":[null,"No timeline is offered, and the closing sentences hold back from promising a cure.","The paragraph calls this the promising work, which is the opposite of doubt.","Farmers are never faulted here, since the paragraph describes practices rather than blaming anyone.","Paragraph 16 says the fertilizer cannot simply be given up, so these measures work alongside the process."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements about biological nitrogen fixation EXCEPT:","choices":["It runs at ordinary temperature and pressure.","It costs the cell a large amount of energy.","Oxygen can wreck the enzyme that carries it out.","Legumes shelter the bacteria that carry it out inside nodules grown on their roots.","It now supplies most of the nitrogen used by the world's crops."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"does at ordinary temperature and pressure","choice":0},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"consumes sixteen molecules of ATP","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"oxygen attacks the exposed metal cluster and wrecks it","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"grows a nodule on its root, houses the bacteria inside","choice":3},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"through an ammonia reactor rather than a root nodule","choice":4},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":4,"quote":"legume rotations supply part of the need biologically","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraphs 4 through 7 support the other four statements. Paragraph 12 goes the other way on this one: by common estimate close to half the nitrogen in human protein now arrives through an ammonia reactor rather than a root nodule.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 says nitrogenase does at ordinary temperature and pressure what industry needs extreme conditions to do.","Paragraph 4 puts the cost at sixteen ATP for one nitrogen molecule and calls the accounting brutal.","Paragraph 5 says oxygen attacks the metal cluster and wrecks it, leaving the cell to build a replacement.","Paragraph 6 describes the legume nodule, with the bacteria housed inside it.",null]},{"id":"rc-p13-q16","type":"detail","stem":"In which paragraph would a reader find the source the hydrogen for ammonia is currently taken from?","choices":["paragraph 4","paragraph 9","paragraph 11","paragraph 16","paragraph 17"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"The hydrogen for ammonia is stripped out of natural gas with steam"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":5,"quote":"would address the fuel side"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 is where the fuel side is handled. The hydrogen is stripped out of natural gas with steam, which is why the fertilizer begins as fossil carbon.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 is about the enzyme nitrogenase, not about where industrial hydrogen comes from.","Paragraph 9 covers the history and the operating conditions, and mentions hydrogen only as a feed gas.","Paragraph 11 explains what the iron catalyst does, without saying where the hydrogen is obtained.",null,"Paragraph 17 offers splitting water as a possible future source, not the one in use now."]}]},"rc-p14":{"id":"rc-p14","title":"How Migrating Birds Find Their Way","topic":"avian migration and navigation","field":"animal behavior","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1371,"paragraphs":[["A blackpoll warbler weighs about twelve grams.","In autumn it leaves eastern Canada, crosses the Atlantic, and comes down three days later in northern South America.","Many of them hatched a few months earlier.","No parent leads them, and they travel at night over water with nothing to steer by.","The question is not whether such a bird navigates, but what it is reading while it does."],["The most informative early experiments simply moved the birds.","An animal that merely holds an inherited heading for a set time should be unaffected by being carried sideways.","It will fly the same direction for the same time and finish somewhere new.","An animal that knows where the goal sits relative to itself should notice the move and compensate."],["A mid twentieth century study did this with European starlings.","Thousands were caught in the Netherlands in autumn, marked, carried several hundred kilometers to Switzerland, and released.","Starlings from that population normally winter in Britain and northern France.","The recoveries split cleanly by age.","Adults that had made the trip before turned and made for the usual wintering range.","The young birds, on their first migration, held the heading they had started with and were recovered in southern France and Spain, shifted by about the distance and direction they had been carried."],["A bird on its first migration runs a short program: fly roughly this way, for roughly this long, then stop.","Heading and duration are inherited, and they suffice, because the route its ancestors flew ends somewhere a bird can survive.","After one full round trip the animal has something better.","It can be carried hundreds of kilometers off course and still correct, so it knows roughly where it is."],["That leaves the narrower question of how a bird points at all.","The oldest answer is the sun.","The sun's bearing shifts by roughly fifteen degrees an hour, so an animal treating it as fixed is off by ninety degrees after six hours.","Birds get around this with an internal clock, correcting the sun's bearing against the time of day."],["The clock can be fooled.","Hold a bird for several days where the lights come on and go off six hours early, and its clock resets.","Release it under the real sun and it departs about ninety degrees from its proper heading, the error running in the direction the shift predicts."],["Night migrants use the stars.","A caged migrant in the restless state of migration season hops and flutters toward the direction it would travel if freed.","In a funnel shaped cage lined with paper that records its footprints, the marks pile up on one side.","Under a planetarium sky these birds orient to the projected stars, and rotating the projection rotates the birds."],["Hand raised birds that never see the sky turn do not develop a usable star compass at all.","Birds raised under a planetarium sky that rotates around an ordinary star instead of Polaris treat that star's neighborhood as north.","What is inherited is not a picture of the constellations but a rule for pulling north out of them: watch the sky turn, find the point it turns around, call that the pole.","The pattern itself is learned."],["A third cue keeps working when the sky is covered.","Birds in closed cages with no view of the sky still orient, and coils that shift the field turn them predictably.","The avian magnetic compass does not behave like a hiker's.","It ignores polarity and reads inclination, the angle at which the field lines dip into the ground.","Such a bird tells not north from south but poleward, where the lines plunge steeply, from equatorward, where they flatten.","Reverse the vertical part of the field and the bird reverses.","Flip the whole field, reversing both parts at once, and it carries on as before."],["How a bird senses the field is where two accounts part company.","The first says iron.","Small crystals of magnetite, an iron oxide, have been reported in the upper beak, wired to the brain through the trigeminal nerve.","The prediction is sharp: a strong magnetic pulse should scramble the crystals and throw the sense off.","Pulsed birds do misorient, experienced ones more than young ones."],["The second account says light and chemistry.","When a photon strikes a retinal protein called cryptochrome, an electron jumps to a neighboring molecule, leaving a linked pair of unpaired electrons.","The surrounding field tips that pair between two states yielding different amounts of product.","Because the outcome depends on the molecule's angle in the field, the bird may see direction as faint shading.","The compass would then need light; in several species it works under blue and green light and fails under deep red.","It also runs on electron spins, which weak oscillating fields in the radio range can upset, fields far too feeble to move an iron crystal.","Birds exposed to them lose their bearings."],["It is tempting to treat these two as rivals awaiting a verdict.","That framing may be the trouble.","Nothing requires an animal to hold only one magnetic sense.","The eye based mechanism is good at angles and poor at field strength, a serviceable compass and a useless meter.","The beak and nerve mechanism responds to strength rather than angle, a poor compass but a plausible instrument for reading position.","A bird carrying both would own a compass and part of a map."],["That distinction is the one most often blurred.","A compass answers a single question, which way is north.","It says nothing about where the traveler is standing.","To register a sideways displacement and correct for it, a bird needs a fix: an estimate of its own position relative to the goal.","A map of that kind must be built from something that changes predictably across the ground."],["Several candidates exist and not one is settled.","Field strength climbs from the magnetic equator toward the poles, and inclination runs from zero degrees there to ninety at the poles, so two magnetic quantities vary with latitude.","The gradients are noisy, though: iron in the crust can bend the field within a few kilometers, so an animal reading strength alone would sometimes place itself well away from where it stands.","Smell is the other serious candidate: homing pigeons often fail to return from unfamiliar sites when their sense of smell is blocked."],["The neatest test of a magnetic map leaves the bird in place and moves the field.","A cage sits inside coils reproducing the strength and inclination of a place the animal has never been.","Some birds shift their heading as though they had been carried there, and in at least one species first time migrants do it too, suggesting a rough magnetic map is inherited.","Other trials, in other species and seasons, produce nothing."],["It is worth asking why a field this old, and this full of memorable results, has converged so slowly.","Part of the answer is redundancy.","A bird holding sun, star, and magnetic compasses can lose any one and keep flying, so removing a cue often changes nothing and proves nothing.","Part of it is effect size: a twenty degree shift in a dozen caged birds' mean heading is a small signal.","Part of it is that a striking result travels widely and a failed repetition rarely does."],["Shift the field around birds at sunset, when several laboratories believe the compasses are calibrated against one another, and they sometimes adopt the new heading, sometimes ignore it, and sometimes adopt it and drop it the next night.","Read generously, the pattern says birds weigh their cues rather than obeying any single one.","Read harshly, it says the experiments are not controlled well enough to tell those stories apart."],["The more useful question is probably not which cue a bird uses but how an animal carrying several imperfect instruments, each wrong in its own way, combines them into one decision made at dusk with a fixed amount of fuel.","Tracking tags light enough for a warbler are starting to return entire routes, which would settle most of these arguments.","Until then the fair position is that the compasses are largely understood and the map is not."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p14-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a bird whose light cycle has been moved six hours before release will","choices":["turn and fly in the direction opposite its normal heading","set off about ninety degrees away from its proper heading","keep its normal heading but leave several hours later than it should","orient normally as soon as the real sun becomes visible","wander without settling on any consistent direction at all"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"lights come on and go off six hours early"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"departs about ninety degrees from its proper heading"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 lays out the arithmetic for you: the sun moves about fifteen degrees an hour, six hours of clock shift adds up to about ninety degrees, and the bird departs that far off, in the direction the shift predicts.","distractorNotes":["A full reversal would be one hundred eighty degrees, and paragraph 6 gives about ninety.",null,"The shifted clock changes which way the bird points, not when it decides to leave.","Seeing the real sun is what creates the error, because the bird reads that sun against the wrong time of day.","The birds still pick a consistent direction after the shift, and only the direction is wrong."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q02","type":"detail","stem":"In the starling study, the young birds released in Switzerland","choices":["turned toward Britain the way the older birds did","were carried back to the Netherlands and released a second time","kept the original bearing and were found south of the usual wintering range","scattered so widely that no shared heading could be read from the recoveries","stopped migrating and stayed near the point where they were released"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"held the heading they had started with and were recovered in southern France and Spain"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"normally winter in Britain and northern France"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 splits the recoveries by age. The first time migrants held the heading they had started with and turned up in southern France and Spain, shifted by about the distance and direction of the truck ride.","distractorNotes":["Heading for the normal wintering area is what the adults did, and the age contrast is the whole point of the paragraph.","No bird in paragraph 3 is returned to the capture site; they were released in Switzerland and left there.",null,"The recoveries were consistent enough for the age split to be readable, so they were not scattered.","The young birds kept flying, which is why they were recovered hundreds of kilometers away."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that the magnetic compass of a bird","choices":["tells true north from magnetic north","reverses when the whole field is flipped at once","reads the dip of the field lines rather than their polarity","measures how strong the field is rather than which way it runs","works only when the bird can also see the sun or the stars"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"It ignores polarity and reads inclination"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the avian compass ignores polarity and reads inclination, the angle at which the field lines dip into the ground, which is why it sorts poleward from equatorward rather than north from south.","distractorNotes":["True north never comes up; the contrast the passage draws is between polarity and dip angle.","Paragraph 9 says the bird carries on when the whole field is flipped; it is the vertical part alone that turns it around.",null,"Reading strength is what the beak and nerve mechanism is credited with in paragraph 12, and the passage calls that a poor compass.","Paragraph 9 introduces this cue precisely because it keeps working when the sky is covered."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage reports that weak oscillating fields in the radio range","choices":["throw off the birds although they are too weak to move an iron crystal","restore orientation in birds tested under deep red light","make the crystals in the beak respond more strongly","serve as the cue birds use to calibrate their compasses against one another at sunset","leave caged migrants entirely unaffected"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"fields far too feeble to move an iron crystal"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":7,"quote":"Birds exposed to them lose their bearings."}],"why":"Paragraph 11 uses these fields as the sharp test of the light based account. They disturb electron spins, birds exposed to them lose their bearings, and they are far too feeble to move an iron crystal.","distractorNotes":[null,"Deep red light is where that compass fails in paragraph 11, and radio fields are not offered as a remedy.","The passage says the opposite: such fields are too weak to move an iron crystal, so the iron account predicts no effect.","Sunset calibration is a separate issue raised in paragraph 17, and nothing there involves radio fields.","Paragraph 11 says exposed birds lose their bearings in the cage, which is a measurable effect."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Birds hand raised without ever seeing the night sky turn","choices":["fall back on the magnetic compass twice as often as other birds","treat whichever star is brightest as the pole","pick up the star compass later, during the first migration","orient to Polaris without having to learn anything","never develop a usable star compass"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"do not develop a usable star compass at all"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 states it plainly: without seeing the sky rotate, the star compass does not develop at all, because what is inherited is the rule for finding the center of rotation rather than the pattern itself.","distractorNotes":["The passage never counts how often birds switch to the magnetic cue.","Brightness is never the criterion; the birds in paragraph 8 learn whichever star the artificial sky turns around.","Paragraph 8 says the compass does not develop at all, not that it shows up late.","Paragraph 8 says the pattern is learned, so no bird arrives already knowing which star marks the pole.",null]},{"id":"rc-p14-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 13, \"fix\" most nearly means","choices":["a workable solution to a difficult problem","a steady heading held without variation","a repair made to a sense that has been damaged","a landmark that stays in one place","an estimate of one's own position"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"an estimate of its own position relative to the goal"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 defines the word in the same sentence it uses it: an estimate of the bird's own position relative to the goal, which is the thing a compass alone cannot supply.","distractorNotes":["This general sense fits the sentence loosely, but the paragraph pins the word to position specifically.","A steady heading is what a compass gives, and paragraph 13 says that is exactly what is not enough.","This is an everyday meaning of the word, and nothing in paragraph 13 is broken or being mended.","Paragraph 1 points out there are no landmarks over open ocean, and the paragraph is about a measurement, not an object.",null]},{"id":"rc-p14-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 14, \"noisy\" most nearly means","choices":["loud enough to be heard over a distance","unusually strong in the neighborhood of the poles","shifting quickly from one season to the next","irregular enough in places to mislead a reading","carrying more information than an animal could possibly use"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"iron in the crust can bend the field within a few kilometers"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"would sometimes place itself well away from where it stands"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 explains the word right after using it: iron in the crust bends the field over a few kilometers, so a bird reading strength alone would sometimes place itself well away from where it actually stands.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday meaning, and the paragraph is describing a magnetic field rather than sound.","Strength does climb toward the poles earlier in the paragraph, but that is a separate property from the one this word names.","The passage never describes the field changing with the seasons; the problem paragraph 14 names is local distortion by iron in the crust.",null,"The problem described is that the field misleads, not that it offers too much to process."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which conclusion about the inherited program a first time migrant follows?","choices":["It is abandoned once the bird reaches the wintering grounds","It works only while the bird stays on the ancestral route","It depends on the bird seeing the sun at some point in the flight","It is more accurate than what an experienced bird relies on","It supplies a position but leaves the direction to be worked out"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"fly the same direction for the same time and finish somewhere new"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"because the route its ancestors flew ends somewhere a bird can survive"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"shifted by about the distance and direction they had been carried"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 describes the program as a heading plus a duration, which works because the ancestral route ends somewhere survivable. Paragraph 3 shows what happens when that assumption breaks: carried sideways, the young birds ran the same instructions and landed somewhere else.","distractorNotes":["The passage credits the adult with an added ability after one round trip, not with dropping the inherited heading.",null,"Night migrants in paragraphs 7 and 9 have stars and a magnetic cue as well, so the sun is not required.","Paragraph 4 says the adult has something better, since it can be displaced and still correct.","This reverses the passage: the program gives a direction, and position is the thing it cannot give."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q09","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from paragraph 12 that a bird equipped only with the eye based mechanism would","choices":["be unable to hold any heading after dark","lose all orientation under blue and green light","know which way to fly without knowing it had been moved","read its latitude more accurately than its direction","depend on other birds in the flock to correct its course"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"a serviceable compass and a useless meter"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"It says nothing about where the traveler is standing."}],"why":"Paragraph 12 calls the eye based mechanism a serviceable compass and a useless meter of field strength, and paragraph 13 says noticing a displacement takes a position estimate rather than a direction. Put together, that bird could point but could not place itself.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 calls that mechanism a serviceable compass, and paragraph 11 has it working under blue and green light.","Paragraph 11 reports that this compass works under blue and green light and fails under deep red.",null,"The paragraph says the reverse: it is poor at measuring field strength, which is what a latitude reading would need.","Nothing in the passage makes navigation a group effort, and paragraph 1 stresses that no parent leads these birds."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Birds use several imperfect cues, and the compasses are better understood than the map","Poor experimental control is the only reason the question remains open","The star compass is the single most important cue a night migrant has","Displacement experiments established that migration is entirely learned","Magnetic sensing in birds has now been traced conclusively to a single protein in the retina"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"what it is reading while it does"},{"paragraph":18,"sentence":1,"quote":"several imperfect instruments, each wrong in its own way"},{"paragraph":18,"sentence":3,"quote":"the compasses are largely understood and the map is not"}],"why":"The passage moves through sun, stars, and magnetism, then separates a compass from a map in paragraph 13 and closes in paragraph 18 by saying the compasses are largely understood and the map is not.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 16 puts redundancy and small effect sizes ahead of method, so control is only part of the story.","The stars get one stretch of the passage among several, and no cue is ever ranked first.","Paragraph 4 gives the first migration an inherited heading and duration, so the passage splits inheritance and learning rather than choosing one.","The retinal protein is one of two accounts in paragraphs 10 and 11, and paragraph 12 declines to declare a winner, so nothing has been settled conclusively."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 13 is included mainly to","choices":["summarize the evidence for a sense of smell in homing pigeons","separate knowing a direction from knowing a position","explain why the funnel cage produces a readable record of intent","show that a compass is of no use over open water","argue that the magnetic compass is the most dependable cue of all"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"That distinction is the one most often blurred."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"A compass answers a single question, which way is north."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"It says nothing about where the traveler is standing."}],"why":"Paragraph 13 draws the line the rest of the passage depends on: a compass answers which way is north, a map answers where you are, and correcting a displacement needs the second one. That sets up the unsettled map cues in paragraph 14.","distractorNotes":["Pigeons and smell appear in paragraph 14, one paragraph later.",null,"The funnel cage belongs to paragraph 7 and has nothing to do with this distinction.","The paragraph says a compass is not sufficient, not that it is useless, and paragraph 1 has birds crossing open ocean successfully.","No cue is ranked in paragraph 13, and paragraph 16 explains why ranking them is hard."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"The passage is organized mainly by","choices":["moving from laboratory work to field work and then back again","comparing birds with other migrating animals at each stage","listing the cues in the order they were discovered and ranking them by importance","opening with a puzzle, splitting it into two problems, then taking each in turn","presenting one decisive experiment and defending it against objections"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"not whether such a bird navigates, but what it is reading while it does"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"That leaves the narrower question of how a bird points at all."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"a bird needs a fix: an estimate of its own position relative to the goal"},{"paragraph":18,"sentence":3,"quote":"the compasses are largely understood and the map is not"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 poses the puzzle, paragraphs 2 through 4 use displacement to split it into holding a course and knowing a position, and the rest of the passage works through the compasses first and the map second.","distractorNotes":["Cages, coils, and planetariums run throughout, so the passage never divides itself that way.","No other migrating animal appears anywhere in the passage.","The cues do appear in a rough order, but the passage never ranks them, and paragraph 16 says redundancy makes ranking hard.",null,"Many experiments appear, and the passage weighs them against one another rather than defending any single one."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward treating the two magnetic accounts as rivals is best described as","choices":["enthusiastic agreement with that framing","confidence that the question has already been settled in one direction","indifference to which account proves correct","open hostility toward the iron based account","doubt that the contest is the right question to ask"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"It is tempting to treat these two as rivals awaiting a verdict"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"That framing may be the trouble."}],"why":"Paragraph 12 calls the rivalry tempting and then pushes back on it, pointing out that nothing requires one magnetic sense and that the two mechanisms suit different jobs.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 opens by calling that framing tempting and then argues against it.","Paragraph 12 ends with a proposal rather than a verdict, and paragraph 18 says the map is still unsettled.","The author is not indifferent; the paragraph proposes a specific division of labor between the two.","The iron based account is handed a serious role in paragraph 12, reading field strength for a map.",null]},{"id":"rc-p14-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In accounting for why the field has converged slowly, the author's tone is best described as","choices":["candid about the weaknesses without dismissing the work","defensive about criticism coming from outside the field","triumphant that the long argument has finally been won","unconcerned, on the view that answers will arrive on their own","scornful of the researchers doing the work"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"this full of memorable results"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"removing a cue often changes nothing and proves nothing"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"a striking result travels widely and a failed repetition rarely does"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 names real problems, including small effects and a published record tilted toward striking results, but it leads with redundancy in the birds themselves. The difficulty is treated as partly built into the subject.","distractorNotes":[null,"No outside critic appears, and paragraph 17 gives the harsh reading its own fair hearing.","Paragraph 18 closes by saying the map is still not understood.","The author treats the slowness as a real problem worth explaining rather than something to wait out.","Paragraph 16 puts redundancy and effect size ahead of any fault in the people doing the work."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Pigeons whose sense of smell is blocked often fail to return from unfamiliar sites","A bird that loses one compass can often keep going on another","Magnetic field strength varies with distance from the magnetic equator","A bird without its magnetic sense cannot orient at night","Adult starlings corrected course after being carried off their usual route"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"can lose any one and keep flying","choice":1},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"Field strength climbs from the magnetic equator toward the poles","choice":2},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"turned and made for the usual wintering range","choice":4},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"often fail to return from unfamiliar sites when their sense of smell is blocked","choice":0},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"Night migrants use the stars.","choice":3},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"A bird holding sun, star, and magnetic compasses can lose any one and keep flying","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 16 makes the opposite point: the compasses are redundant, so a bird can lose one and keep flying, which is part of why removing a cue often proves nothing. The other four statements all sit on the page.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 14 gives exactly this result as the leading evidence for a sense of smell.","Paragraph 16 says a bird holding sun, star, and magnetic compasses can lose any one and keep flying.","Paragraph 14 says field strength climbs from the magnetic equator toward the poles.",null,"Paragraph 3 says the adults turned and made for the usual wintering range."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for how a bird corrects for the sun's movement across the sky should turn to","choices":["paragraph 5","paragraph 11","paragraph 2","paragraph 3","paragraph 16"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"correcting the sun's bearing against the time of day"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 gives the problem and the solution together: the sun's bearing shifts about fifteen degrees an hour, and the bird corrects that bearing against an internal clock.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 11 covers the light based magnetic mechanism in the eye.","Paragraph 2 sets out the logic of moving birds and never mentions the sun.","Paragraph 3 reports the starling displacement and the split between adults and young birds.","Paragraph 16 explains why the field has been slow to settle."]}]},"rc-p15":{"id":"rc-p15","title":"The Calibration Problem in Radiocarbon Dating","topic":"radiocarbon dating and calibration","field":"history of science","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1379,"paragraphs":[["A few milligrams of charred wheat from a storeroom that burned three thousand years ago can name the century of the fire.","The mechanism is almost too tidy.","Living things take in carbon.","A tiny, constant fraction of that carbon is unstable.","When the organism dies the intake stops and that fraction thins out on a fixed schedule.","Measure what is left, work backward, and the grain gives up its date.","The word carrying the trouble in that summary is constant."],["Carbon fourteen is manufactured continuously overhead.","Cosmic rays smash into nuclei in the upper atmosphere and knock loose free neutrons.","A neutron that strikes a nitrogen fourteen nucleus can lodge there and expel a proton, leaving a nucleus of the same total mass but one fewer unit of positive charge: carbon fourteen.","It is soon oxidized to carbon dioxide, and within a few years mixing carries it worldwide."],["A plant takes in carbon fourteen in close to the proportion the air offers, animals acquire it by eating plants, and every living body tops up its supply as long as it feeds.","The result is an internal ratio near the atmosphere's own, roughly one carbon fourteen atom per trillion of ordinary carbon twelve."],["Death closes the account.","Nothing new comes in, and the carbon fourteen already present decays on its own timetable, an electron flying off as each atom turns back into nitrogen fourteen.","Half of any population of these atoms is gone in about 5,730 years.","There is a wrinkle, though.","The earliest measurements put the half-life near 5,568 years, and though the better figure has long been known, laboratories still report ages calculated from the original value, so dates published half a century apart stay comparable."],["After ten halvings only about a thousandth of the original carbon fourteen survives, which sets a practical ceiling near fifty thousand years.","The method is also particular about its subject, dating only carbon that once exchanged with the atmosphere: charcoal, wood, seed, bone, shell, cloth and parchment."],["All of this rests on knowing how much carbon fourteen the air held when the organism died, and the founding assumption was the simplest one available: that production and decay had long since balanced, leaving the atmospheric level fixed.","That is a sensible first approximation, and it is wrong.","Production depends on how many cosmic rays get through.","The earth's magnetic field deflects them, and its strength has drifted over the last ten thousand years.","The sun's outflowing wind deflects them as well, so an active sun suppresses production and a quiet sun raises it."],["Egyptian objects whose calendar dates were already fixed by written records came back from the laboratory too young, some by several centuries.","The conclusion was unavoidable: a radiocarbon year is not a calendar year, and the gap between them is not a constant."],["The correction came from trees.","A temperate tree lays down one ring a year, wide in a good season and narrow in a bad one, so a run of rings records fat and lean years.","Every tree alive through those seasons shares the pattern, so a living tree can be matched to an old beam, the beam to a buried log, and so on backward.","Stitched together, European oak and pine carry an unbroken year by year record back more than twelve thousand years."],["Measuring carbon fourteen in rings of known age turns the problem into a graph, and it is not the straight line the founding assumption predicted.","It wobbles.","Decades of weak solar activity leave bumps in it, and in places the curve flattens into a plateau, a stretch where the atmospheric level barely changed, so that many different calendar years produce nearly the same radiocarbon result.","One notorious flat stretch covers roughly four centuries, from about 800 to 400 before the common era.","A sample from anywhere inside it returns almost the same answer, and the calendar range that comes back is four centuries wide however careful the laboratory was."],["Trees run out before the method does.","For older material the curve is carried forward by other archives, among them corals dated independently by uranium and thorium.","The agreed curve now runs to about fifty five thousand years."],["Some carbon came from the air long ago or never at all, and organisms feeding on such a reservoir inherit its head start.","Deep ocean water has been out of contact with the atmosphere for centuries, so carbon from the sea is already old when a clam builds a shell from it.","Marine organisms therefore appear roughly four hundred years dead at the moment they actually die.","Fresh water can be worse: a lake fed by springs running through limestone carries dissolved carbonate with no carbon fourteen in it at all, and shells from such a lake have come back thousands of years too old."],["Human activity has since moved the level far more violently than the sun ever did.","Nuclear weapons tested in the open air released floods of neutrons, making carbon fourteen exactly as cosmic ray neutrons do.","By 1963 the northern hemisphere's atmospheric level had nearly doubled.","A treaty signed that year drove testing underground, and the excess has since drained away until the air is close to its level before the tests.","That steep decline is a gift.","Each year on the descending limb carries its own value, so tissue grown in the last seventy years can often be placed within a year or two, which is how tooth enamel, laid down in childhood and never rebuilt, has been used to estimate a person's year of birth."],["A second human signal runs the opposite way.","Coal, oil and gas formed so long ago that every carbon fourteen atom in them decayed away ages back, which makes their carbon dead in the only sense that matters here: it carries no radiocarbon whatever.","Burning them pours dead carbon into the air and dilutes the living fraction, so the atmosphere takes on an apparent age it did not earn.","With the bomb carbon nearly gone, dilution is now the dominant trend, and if emissions continue, cloth made later this century could carry the radiocarbon signature of something several hundred years old."],["The laboratory must also defend the sample, and the threats are lopsided.","Recent carbon is the dangerous intruder.","One percent of modern carbon mixed into a sample that is truly forty thousand years old brings back a reported age near thirty two thousand, an error of eight millennia.","One percent of dead carbon mixed into a modern sample shifts its reported age by about eighty years.","Rootlets, groundwater humic acids and old glues all deliver carbon of the wrong age, so pretreatment is no formality: acid and alkali washes strip the soluble intruders.","Sample choice matters just as much: the heartwood of a beam grew centuries before the tree was felled, and beams were often salvaged and reused, so a seed, which lives one season, answers a sharper question."],["The instrument changed as well.","Counting decays meant grams of purified carbon and days of waiting.","Accelerator mass spectrometry counts the atoms themselves, so a milligram is enough."],["What the laboratory hands back, then, is not a year.","It is a measurement, reported in a form such as 2450 plus or minus 30 before present, with present fixed by convention at 1950.","The plus or minus describes the precision of the counting, and it is tempting to read it as the calendar answer's error bar.","It is not.","That symmetrical bell has to be pushed through the wobbling calibration curve first, and what emerges is lumpy: sometimes one narrow interval, sometimes two or three humps with gaps between them.","The intuition that a better measurement always buys a better date fails outright on a plateau, where halving the counting error barely narrows the calendar range."],["An honest radiocarbon date is therefore a probability distribution, normally quoted as the intervals holding sixty eight and ninety five percent of it.","And the atmosphere's refusal to hold still, the flaw that made all of this necessary, turned out to be worth having.","The calibration curve is itself a record of the sun, the magnetic field and the ocean.","The correction and the archive are the same object."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p15-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, carbon fourteen forms in the upper atmosphere when","choices":["nitrogen fourteen absorbs a passing electron and grows heavier by one unit of mass","a cosmic ray proton is captured directly by a carbon twelve nucleus","high energy radiation splits carbon dioxide molecules apart in the stratosphere","a free neutron enters a nitrogen fourteen nucleus and drives out a proton","particles in the solar wind attach a neutron to a nucleus of carbon thirteen"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"A neutron that strikes a nitrogen fourteen nucleus can lodge there and expel a proton"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 walks the step through in order: cosmic rays knock free neutrons loose, and a neutron that strikes a nitrogen fourteen nucleus lodges there and expels a proton. Same total mass, one fewer unit of positive charge, which makes it carbon.","distractorNotes":["This runs the paragraph 4 decay step backward, and no electron is absorbed anywhere in the passage.","Cosmic rays start the chain, but the passage has them shattering nuclei to release neutrons rather than being captured by carbon.","Carbon dioxide is where the new atom ends up after it forms, not the raw material it is made from.",null,"Paragraph 6 gives the solar wind the opposite role, deflecting cosmic rays and lowering production rather than building the isotope."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says laboratories still calculate reported ages from a half-life near 5,568 years because","choices":["modern measurements have confirmed that this is the accurate figure","the shorter value offsets the effect of contamination by recent carbon","holding to the original value keeps dates published decades apart comparable","the calibration curve is built on the assumption that the atmosphere never changed","decay counting instruments cannot resolve the difference between the two figures"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"so dates published half a century apart stay comparable"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"though the better figure has long been known"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 calls it a wrinkle and gives the reason outright: the original value is kept so that dates published half a century apart stay directly comparable, even though about 5,730 years is the better figure.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 names 5,730 years as the better figure, so the value actually used is the one known to be slightly off.","Contamination is a paragraph 14 problem, handled by pretreatment, not by the choice of a half-life.",null,"Calibration exists precisely because the atmosphere did change, as paragraphs 6 and 9 both insist.","The passage never blames the instruments for this choice, since paragraph 4 gives comparability as the whole reason."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a sample that is truly forty thousand years old but carries one percent modern carbon comes back with a reported age of about","choices":["eight thousand years","thirty nine thousand years","forty eight thousand years","thirty two thousand years","forty thousand years, since a one percent intruder is too small to matter"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"brings back a reported age near thirty two thousand"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 supplies that number to show how lopsided the threat is. One percent of modern carbon drags a forty thousand year old sample down to roughly thirty two thousand, an error of eight millennia.","distractorNotes":["Eight thousand is the size of the error, not the age that comes back.","A thousand year error would be the mild outcome, and the passage puts the shift at eight thousand years.","Modern carbon adds carbon fourteen, so it always makes a sample look younger rather than older.",null,"This is the intuition paragraph 14 is written to overturn, since a tiny intruder produces an enormous shift."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage attributes the fact that shells from a limestone fed lake can come back thousands of years too old to","choices":["the unusual pressure under which lake shells build their carbonate","the slow turnover of the deep ocean water that feeds the lake","dissolved carbonate in the spring water with no carbon fourteen","a locally high rate of carbon fourteen production over inland regions","contamination of the shells by rootlets and by groundwater"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"dissolved carbonate with no carbon fourteen in it at all"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 treats it as a reservoir effect. Springs running through limestone carry dissolved carbonate with no carbon fourteen in it at all, so shells built from that carbon start out looking ancient.","distractorNotes":["Pressure plays no part in anything the passage describes.","Deep ocean water is the marine case in the same paragraph, and a spring fed lake is a separate reservoir.",null,"Paragraph 2 says mixing carries the isotope worldwide within a few years, so no region has its own elevated supply.","Rootlets and groundwater are paragraph 14 contaminants, and those tend to add recent carbon, which makes a sample look younger."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, by 1963 the northern hemisphere's atmospheric carbon fourteen level had","choices":["returned to where it stood before the weapons tests began","roughly doubled","fallen by about half","stopped changing in any measurable way","risen by the equivalent of four hundred years of decay"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"had nearly doubled"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 gives the figure plainly: neutrons from open air weapons testing had nearly doubled the northern hemisphere's atmospheric level by 1963.","distractorNotes":["The return toward the pre-test level came afterward, once the treaty drove testing underground.",null,"Nothing halved, since the tests added carbon fourteen to the air rather than removing it.","The level was moving faster than at any other point in the passage, which is what makes the descending limb useful.","Four hundred years is the marine offset from paragraph 11, a different quantity in a different context."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 9, \"plateau\" most nearly means","choices":["a flat stretch where many calendar years share one radiocarbon value","a gap in the tree ring record that no surviving piece of timber can bridge","a broad raised area of level ground in a landscape","the highest value that the curve ever reaches","the age past which the method stops working entirely"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"many different calendar years produce nearly the same radiocarbon result"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 defines the word where it uses it: a stretch of curve where the atmospheric level barely changed, so many different calendar years produce nearly the same radiocarbon result.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 8 describes the tree ring record as unbroken, so no such gap is at issue.","This is the everyday landform sense the passage is borrowing from, not the sense it puts to work.","A flat stretch is level, not high, and nothing in paragraph 9 makes it a maximum.","A sample inside a plateau still returns an answer; the answer is just spread across four centuries."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 13, carbon called \"dead\" is carbon that","choices":["comes from organisms that stopped feeding only recently","can no longer take part in any chemical reaction","has been stripped out of a sample during pretreatment","sits sealed away from the air while it goes on decaying","contains no detectable carbon fourteen"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"it carries no radiocarbon whatever"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 states the sense it intends in the same sentence: coal, oil and gas are so old that every carbon fourteen atom in them decayed away long ago, so their carbon carries no radiocarbon whatever.","distractorNotes":["Recently dead material is the opposite case, since its carbon is still rich in carbon fourteen.","Fossil carbon burns readily, which is the whole problem, so chemical inertness is not the point.","Pretreatment in paragraph 14 removes intruders; it does not create the carbon the passage calls dead.","The point is that the decay finished ages ago, not that it is still running out of sight.",null]},{"id":"rc-p15-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage's account of contamination most strongly supports which statement about an ancient sample that takes up a trace of recent carbon","choices":["no pretreatment can remove an intruder of this kind","the sample will come back much younger than it really is","the error will match what an equal trace of ancient carbon would cause","the sample will come back older than it really is","the error will stay small because the intruding amount is small"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"Recent carbon is the dangerous intruder."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"an error of eight millennia"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"shifts its reported age by about eighty years"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 runs the arithmetic in one direction only. Recent carbon adds carbon fourteen that the old sample should not have, so the reported age is pulled toward the present, by eight thousand years in the example given.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 14 describes acid and alkali washes stripping the soluble intruders, so such samples are not written off.",null,"The passage calls the threats lopsided and gives the two figures, eight thousand years against about eighty.","Adding carbon fourteen makes a sample look younger; only dead carbon can push an age older.","Paragraph 14 chooses the one percent example precisely because a tiny intruder produces an enormous shift."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on paragraph 9, two pieces of charcoal burned three hundred years apart, both inside that flat stretch, would most likely","choices":["come back three hundred years apart, matching their true separation","prove impossible for a laboratory to measure at all","differ by far more than the laboratory's stated uncertainty","land on two separate humps of one calibrated distribution","come back with radiocarbon results too close to tell apart"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"A sample from anywhere inside it returns almost the same answer"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says a sample from anywhere inside the flat stretch returns almost the same answer, which is why the calendar range stays four centuries wide. Two samples three hundred years apart in there would be very hard to tell apart.","distractorNotes":["That is what a straight calibration line would deliver, and paragraph 9 says the curve is flat across this span instead.","The measurement itself is fine; it is the translation onto a calendar that stays wide.","A plateau compresses real differences rather than exaggerating them.","Separate humps belong to paragraph 16, where a wiggle splits a distribution, not to a flat stretch.",null]},{"id":"rc-p15-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage","choices":["Tree rings are the most valuable archive available anywhere in the earth sciences","The chemistry behind carbon fourteen production is more intricate than most people realize","Nuclear weapons testing did permanent damage to the usefulness of radiocarbon dating","Radiocarbon dating has been compromised by human pollution to the point where recent material can no longer be trusted","A radiocarbon date is a corrected range rather than a year, because the method's founding assumption is false"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":7,"quote":"The word carrying the trouble in that summary is constant."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"That is a sensible first approximation, and it is wrong."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"What the laboratory hands back, then, is not a year."},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":1,"quote":"An honest radiocarbon date is therefore a probability distribution"}],"why":"The passage moves in one direction throughout: a tidy mechanism, the constant level it quietly assumes, the evidence that the level was never constant, and then the corrections and limits that follow. Paragraphs 16 and 17 land it, calling the output a probability distribution rather than a year.","distractorNotes":["Tree rings supply the correction, but the passage never claims anything about their value to other sciences.","The production chemistry is only the opening move, and most of the passage is about calibration.","Paragraph 12 presents the spike as a dating opportunity, and says the excess has largely drained away since.","Paragraph 12 treats the bomb curve as a gift for recent material, so the passage argues nearly the reverse.",null]},{"id":"rc-p15-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author pairs the weapons testing spike with fossil fuel dilution mainly in order to show that","choices":["people have pushed the level both ways, with opposite effects on dating","burning fossil fuels released the neutrons that the weapons tests had produced","human activity has made modern samples impossible to date","the sun influences production more than any human activity does","the calibration curve has to be rebuilt from fresh tree rings every few decades"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"A second human signal runs the opposite way."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"That steep decline is a gift."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"could carry the radiocarbon signature of something several hundred years old"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":6,"quote":"can often be placed within a year or two"}],"why":"Paragraphs 12 and 13 are deliberately mirrored. The tests raised the level and opened a way to date recent tissue to within a year or two, and fossil carbon lowers it and may take that ability away.","distractorNotes":[null,"The tests released the neutrons themselves, while fossil fuels release carbon that carries no radiocarbon at all.","Paragraph 12 makes recent material easier to date rather than impossible, so this overstates the case.","Paragraph 12 opens by saying human activity moved the level far more violently than the sun ever did.","Rebuilding the curve on a schedule is never mentioned anywhere in the passage."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes the way the passage is organized","choices":["a chronological history of the people who developed the technique","a mechanism, then the assumption behind it, then the corrections its failure forces","a list of laboratory procedures arranged from the least to the most expensive","a comparison of radiocarbon dating with two competing dating methods","a problem stated at the very outset and a proposed solution that the author finally rejects"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"The mechanism is almost too tidy."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"the founding assumption was the simplest one available"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"The correction came from trees."},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":2,"quote":"the flaw that made all of this necessary"}],"why":"Paragraphs 1 through 5 lay out a clean mechanism, paragraph 6 names the assumption it rests on and calls it wrong, and everything after that is correction and limitation. The last two paragraphs say what survives: a range with a probability attached.","distractorNotes":["Dates appear, but no researcher is named and the material is not arranged as a history.",null,"Pretreatment steps appear in paragraph 14, but cost is never discussed anywhere.","Uranium and thorium dating of corals appears once as a support for the curve, never as a rival.","The corrections are endorsed rather than rejected, and paragraph 17 calls the whole difficulty worth having."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the founding assumption that the atmospheric level never changed is best described as","choices":["convinced that it still holds well enough for most practical laboratory work","puzzled that anyone would ever have proposed it","accepting of it as a sensible start that evidence later overturned","scornful of the carelessness it represented","indifferent, since it has no bearing on how dates are produced now"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"That is a sensible first approximation, and it is wrong."}],"why":"Paragraph 6 gives both halves in one breath. It calls the assumption a sensible first approximation and then says flatly that it is wrong, which is respect for the reasoning together with a clear verdict on it.","distractorNotes":["The same paragraph says the assumption is wrong, and the rest of the passage corrects for it.","Calling something a sensible first approximation is the opposite of finding it baffling.",null,"The word sensible rules out scorn, and the passage treats the assumption as reasonable for its moment.","The failure of that assumption drives the entire calibration enterprise, so it could hardly matter more."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 16, the author's attitude toward the figure a laboratory reports is best described as","choices":["confident in the measurement and wary of how it gets read","impressed by how precisely a calendar answer can now be fixed","irritated with laboratories for publishing an uncertainty at all","doubtful that radiocarbon measurements carry real information","resigned to the method being replaced by something better"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"it is tempting to read it as the calendar answer's error bar"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"It is not."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"what emerges is lumpy"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 never questions the measurement. What it questions is the instinct to read the plus or minus as a calendar error bar, and it answers that instinct with three blunt words.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 16 argues the reverse, since better counting precision buys almost nothing on a plateau.","The uncertainty is welcomed as honest reporting, and paragraph 17 treats the honest date as a probability distribution.","The passage treats the measurement as sound, and its caution is about interpretation rather than content.","No replacement is proposed anywhere, and paragraph 17 closes by calling the difficulty worth having."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["The calibration curve reaches further back in time than the tree ring record does on its own","A milligram of material can be enough for a determination today","Carbon fourteen production rises and falls with the activity of the sun","Halving a laboratory's counting error always halves the width of the calendar range","An organism that feeds from the sea can seem centuries old on the day it dies"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"an active sun suppresses production and a quiet sun raises it","choice":2},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"appear roughly four hundred years dead at the moment they actually die","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"Trees run out before the method does.","choice":0},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"so a milligram is enough","choice":1},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":6,"quote":"halving the counting error barely narrows the calendar range","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 16 says the intuition that a better measurement always buys a better date fails outright on a plateau, where halving the counting error barely narrows the calendar range. The other four statements are stated in paragraphs 6, 11, 10 and 15.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 8 gives the trees about twelve thousand years, and paragraph 10 carries the curve out to about fifty five thousand.","Paragraph 15 credits accelerator mass spectrometry with bringing the sample down to a milligram.","Paragraph 6 says an active sun suppresses production and a quiet sun raises it, so this one is supported.",null,"Paragraph 11 puts marine organisms at roughly four hundred years dead at the moment they actually die."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for how radiocarbon has been used to estimate a person's year of birth should turn to","choices":["paragraph 12","paragraph 14","paragraph 3","paragraph 7","paragraph 16"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":6,"quote":"has been used to estimate a person's year of birth"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 closes with it. Because each year on the declining bomb curve carries its own value, tooth enamel laid down in childhood and never rebuilt can be matched to a year of birth.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 14 is about contamination, pretreatment and choosing a short lived sample.","Paragraph 3 covers how living tissue takes carbon fourteen in, not how recent tissue is dated.","Paragraph 7 is about Egyptian objects coming back too young, which is where the calibration problem first surfaced.","Paragraph 16 explains what a reported measurement means, not what has been dated with it."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q17","type":"application","stem":"A timber from a barn whose construction is documented in the year 1700 comes back about three centuries older than the barn. The passage's reasoning most directly supports which explanation?","choices":["Rootlets and groundwater delivered recent carbon into the timber over the centuries","The heartwood grew long before felling, or the timber was reused from an older building","The laboratory counted decays in the sample instead of counting its atoms directly","The laboratory calculated the age from the newer half-life rather than the original value","The timber's carbon was drawn from deep ocean water long out of contact with the atmosphere"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":6,"quote":"the heartwood of a beam grew centuries before the tree was felled, and beams were often salvaged and reused"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 gives the rule: the heartwood of a beam grew centuries before the tree was felled, and beams were often salvaged and reused, so a timber can easily be older than the building it holds up. That is exactly why the passage prefers a seed, which lives one season.","distractorNotes":["Rootlets and groundwater deliver recent carbon, and paragraph 14 shows recent carbon pulling a reported age toward the present, not away from it.",null,"Paragraph 15 says the two instruments differ in how much sample they need, not in the age they report.","Paragraph 4 says laboratories still report ages from the original value, so no switch of half-life is in play.","Paragraph 11 reserves the marine offset for organisms that build tissue from sea water, and paragraph 3 has plants taking their carbon from the air."]}]},"rc-p16":{"id":"rc-p16","title":"The Protein That Copies Its Own Shape","topic":"prions and templated protein misfolding","field":"history of science","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1371,"paragraphs":[["A ewe in a hill flock begins to rub.","Her gait goes wide, she startles at nothing, and within a few months she is dead.","Shepherds in Britain were describing this pattern by the mid eighteenth century, and they named it for the rubbing: scrapie.","Almost nothing about it looked like an infection: no fever, no swelling, no rise in antibodies, none of the noise a body makes against an intruder.","Under the microscope the brain was riddled with holes, like a sponge."],["In the 1930s a simple experiment settled one question and opened a harder one.","Brain material from a sick sheep, injected into healthy sheep, gave them the disease.","Scrapie was transmissible, but whatever was passed behaved like nothing in the microbiology textbooks.","It slipped through filters fine enough to hold back bacteria.","It provoked no immune response at any point.","And it took its time: injected animals stayed well for a year or more before the first symptom."],["Then came experiments designed to measure the agent rather than see it.","Radiation damages nucleic acid, so a larger genome offers a larger target.","Scrapie refused to cooperate.","Ultraviolet light at the wavelength nucleic acid absorbs most strongly, at doses that would cut the infectivity of an ordinary virus by a factor of thousands, barely touched it.","Enzymes that cut DNA and RNA did nothing measurable."],["The mirror image was just as striking.","Treatments aimed at protein worked.","Proteases lowered the titer.","Set the two results side by side and the conclusion was awkward but plain: whatever carried scrapie between animals was made of protein and carried no nucleic acid at all."],["Purification caught up in 1982, when fractions of infected hamster brain were enriched step by step and infectivity tracked with one small protein.","The particle was named prion, from proteinaceous and infectious, and the protein itself PrP.","The gene for PrP had not been smuggled in by the agent.","It sat in the animal's own chromosomes, and healthy uninfected animals transcribed it steadily, hanging the finished protein on the surface of their neurons."],["Infected and healthy brain held the same protein, from the same gene, with the same amino acids in the same order.","The difference was shape.","The normal cellular form is soluble, sits on the cell surface, is digested to nothing by a protease within minutes, and is roughly forty percent alpha helix with almost no beta sheet.","The disease form is insoluble, gathers into clumps and long fibrils, and runs near thirty percent helix with more than forty percent sheet.","Give it a protease and it does not disappear: the enzyme trims a loose tail and stops, leaving a resistant core of about twenty-seven to thirty kilodaltons out of a protein of thirty-three to thirty-five.","One chain, two folds, two fates."],["It is easy, looking back, to tell this as a story about a stubborn establishment and a lone heretic, and that telling is unfair.","The objection was not fussiness.","Biology had spent thirty years establishing that heredity lives in sequence, copied by base pairing, and that protein is the end of the line.","An infectious protein with no genome was not a small amendment: it asked for a second channel of heredity, one in which the thing copied was not a message at all.","Demanding extraordinary evidence for that was the correct first response, and the evidence took twenty years to assemble."],["What made the idea thinkable was a mechanism that copies no message.","The misfolded form is not a poison.","It is a mold.","A molecule already folded the wrong way binds a normal one, holds it against its own sheet face, and presses it into the same geometry.","The new convert is a mold in its turn.","Molecules stack into a fibril that grows at both ends, and when a fibril snaps, one growing surface becomes two."],["That mechanism also explains the strange clock.","The slow part is the beginning, the improbable first seed, whether it arrives by injection or forms on its own in one cell out of billions.","Once enough seeds exist the curve turns steep and the brain fills quickly, which is why these diseases seem to arrive all at once after years of nothing.","In kuru, a human prion disease, some incubation periods ran past forty years."],["The sharpest objection to the protein idea was never the sequence rule.","It was strains.","Isolates of scrapie taken from different sources and passaged in mice bred to be genetically identical behaved as distinct and stable varieties.","One strain killed at about 150 days and another at more than 300, reproducibly, in the same inbred line.","Each left its own signature map of which brain regions turned spongy and which were spared.","A strain bred true through passage after passage.","That is inheritance of a trait, and inheritance by an agent with no genes looked like a contradiction."],["The resolution is that the shape carries the trait.","A given strain is a particular fold, a particular way the sheet is stacked, and each fold templates that arrangement onto the next molecule.","The information is real and heritable.","It is simply written in geometry rather than in bases."],["Crossing between species revealed a second property.","Put a hamster prion into a mouse and most animals stay well, while the few that sicken take far longer than a mouse given a mouse prion.","Pass the agent again, now mouse to mouse, and the incubation shortens and settles at a new stable value.","The obstacle is real, but it is a steep hill rather than a wall, and it belongs to a pair rather than a species: what matters is the fit between the incoming shape and the host's own sequence."],["Two episodes showed how partial that protection is.","Cattle in Britain developed a spongiform disease recognized in 1986 and traced to feed containing rendered tissue from other ruminants.","Ten years later a new human disease appeared in people who had eaten beef, and fewer than two hundred died of it in Britain.","Kuru had spread decades earlier among the Fore of Papua New Guinea through mortuary practices in which the dead were eaten, and faded once those stopped."],["Hospitals inherited a practical problem.","Sterilization protocols destroy nucleic acid and unfold ordinary proteins.","A prion offers nothing of the first kind and is far too stable for the second.","A standard autoclave cycle, 121 degrees Celsius for fifteen minutes, does not reliably do it.","Published guidance calls instead for 134 degrees for eighteen minutes in a porous load autoclave, or an hour in one molar sodium hydroxide, or an hour in bleach at twenty thousand parts per million available chlorine.","Worse, the reagents that usually make a specimen safe do the opposite here.","Formaldehyde fixes the tissue, hardening it and sealing the infectivity inside, where it survives for years, and alcohol behaves much the same way.","Instruments have carried infectivity through ordinary cleaning, so high risk work leans on single use tools."],["For a long time this looked like one bizarre corner of pathology.","That has changed.","The same templating move turns up across a set of proteins with no relation to PrP.","Alpha synuclein in Parkinson disease, tau in Alzheimer disease, amyloid beta, and the polyglutamine tracts of Huntington disease all misfold into sheet rich aggregates that seed more of themselves.","Inject affected brain extract into a healthy animal and matching deposits appear, then spread along connected circuits."],["Two cautions attach to that list.","The word infectious is doing much less work there than it appears.","Nothing indicates that Alzheimer disease or Parkinson disease passes between people in ordinary life, and treating a shared molecular move as evidence of contagion is the error the field works hardest to avoid.","The second caution runs the other way: self templating is not only a failure.","Yeast carry protein based traits with no change to any gene, and a few animal proteins in memory and immune signaling switch on this way.","The sheep disease that broke the rules in the eighteenth century was the first visible case of something ordinary.","Many chains can fold more than one way, and one of those ways can copy itself."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p16-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, applying a protease to the disease form of PrP leaves behind","choices":["nothing, since the enzyme digests it completely","a soluble fragment that can no longer join a fibril","a resistant core of about twenty-seven to thirty kilodaltons","a fragment of thirty-three to thirty-five kilodaltons, the full length protein","a stretch that is roughly forty percent alpha helix"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"leaving a resistant core of about twenty-seven to thirty kilodaltons"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 states it directly: the enzyme trims a loose tail and stops, leaving a resistant core of about twenty-seven to thirty kilodaltons. That paragraph carries several numbers, so the trick is matching the right one to the right thing.","distractorNotes":["Complete digestion is what happens to the normal cellular form in paragraph 6, not to the disease form.","Solubility belongs to the normal form, while the disease form stays insoluble and keeps gathering into fibrils.",null,"Thirty-three to thirty-five kilodaltons is the size of the whole protein before the enzyme trims it, not what survives.","Forty percent helix describes the normal fold in paragraph 6, and it is a structural figure rather than a fragment."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage reports that ultraviolet light tuned to the wavelength nucleic acid takes up best","choices":["destroyed scrapie infectivity at low doses","left scrapie infectivity nearly untouched","was never actually tested on scrapie","reduced scrapie infectivity by a factor of thousands","worked only when combined with enzymes that cut DNA and RNA"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"barely touched it"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"at doses that would cut the infectivity of an ordinary virus by a factor of thousands"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says that doses high enough to cut an ordinary virus by a factor of thousands barely touched scrapie. That survival is the whole point of the experiment, since it argued against a nucleic acid genome.","distractorNotes":["Low doses did nothing and high doses did little more, which is the opposite of what this choice claims.",null,"Paragraph 3 describes that test being run, and its result is what pushed the field toward a protein.","A drop of that size is what the same dose did to an ordinary virus, which is the comparison the paragraph draws.","Enzymes that cut DNA and RNA had no measurable effect either, so pairing them changes nothing."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q03","type":"detail","stem":"Which treatment does the passage name as part of published guidance for prion contaminated instruments?","choices":["an autoclave cycle at 121 degrees for fifteen minutes","an hour in formaldehyde followed by alcohol","eighteen minutes at 134 degrees in a porous load autoclave","twenty minutes in bleach at two hundred parts per million chlorine","several rounds of ordinary cleaning between uses"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"134 degrees for eighteen minutes in a porous load autoclave"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"does not reliably do it"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 lists the accepted options, and 134 degrees for eighteen minutes in a porous load autoclave is the first of them. The others in that list are sodium hydroxide and much stronger bleach.","distractorNotes":["That is the standard cycle paragraph 14 names as the one that does not reliably work.","Formaldehyde and alcohol are named as making things worse, since they fix the tissue and seal infectivity inside.",null,"The bleach figure in paragraph 14 is twenty thousand parts per million for a full hour, far stronger and far longer.","Paragraph 14 says instruments have carried infectivity through ordinary cleaning, so that is the problem rather than the solution."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, one feature that made scrapie unlike a familiar infection was that it","choices":["produced a high fever within days","could not be transmitted by injection","was visible under an ordinary light microscope","was held back by filters that stop bacteria","provoked no immune response at any point"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"It provoked no immune response at any point."},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"It slipped through filters fine enough to hold back bacteria."}],"why":"Paragraph 2 lists the oddities, and the absence of any immune response is one of them. Paragraph 1 makes the same point from the clinical side, noting no fever, no swelling, and no rise in antibodies.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 1 lists the absence of fever among the things that made scrapie look unlike an infection.","Injection of brain material did transmit it, which is the finding paragraph 2 opens with.","The agent was never seen, and paragraph 3 says the experiments were designed to measure it rather than to see it.","It slipped through those filters, which is how investigators knew it was smaller than bacteria.",null]},{"id":"rc-p16-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The evidence from scrapie strains passaged in genetically identical mice included","choices":["different incubation periods and different damage maps","different amino acid sequences in the host protein","a steady loss of infectivity with each passage","the appearance of new genes in the mouse genome","identical incubation periods in every case once the host line was inbred"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"One strain killed at about 150 days and another at more than 300"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"Each left its own signature map of which brain regions turned spongy"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives both signatures: one strain killing near 150 days and another past 300 in the same inbred line, and each strain leaving its own map of which brain regions turned spongy.","distractorNotes":[null,"The host protein sequence was the same in every mouse, which is exactly what made the result so puzzling.","Strains bred true through passage after passage rather than fading out.","No genetic change in the mice was involved, since the whole point was that the mice were identical.","The strains differed sharply in incubation, by more than a hundred fifty days in the example given."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 10, \"strains\" most nearly means","choices":["forces that stretch or deform a molecule","efforts made under difficult conditions","versions of the agent whose properties reproduce reliably","lines of laboratory mice bred over generations to be genetically identical","samples taken from a single infected animal"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"behaved as distinct and stable varieties"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"A strain bred true through passage after passage."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 defines the word by what it describes: isolates that behave as distinct and stable varieties, each with its own incubation period and its own pattern of damage, holding true through passage after passage.","distractorNotes":["That is a mechanical sense of the word, and the paragraph is describing varieties of an agent rather than forces on a molecule.","The everyday sense does not fit a sentence about isolates that reproduce their own properties reliably.",null,"The inbred mice are the background the comparison was run in, not what the word names.","A single sample could contain any variety, so this names the act of collecting rather than the behavior being described."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 14, \"fixes\" most nearly means","choices":["repairs damage done to the tissue","settles on one value something that had been variable","attaches the specimen to a slide","hardens and preserves the tissue chemically","removes the infectious material from the tissue"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":7,"quote":"fixes the tissue, hardening it and sealing the infectivity inside"}],"why":"The same sentence in paragraph 14 supplies the meaning: formaldehyde hardens the tissue and seals the infectivity inside, where it survives for years. The word describes chemical preservation, not repair.","distractorNotes":["Repair is the everyday sense, and the paragraph is describing a treatment that makes the hazard worse rather than better.","That sense of the word belongs to prices and schedules, not to what formaldehyde does to a specimen.","Mounting is a real laboratory step, but the sentence is about hardening tissue and trapping what is inside it.",null,"The sentence says the reverse: the infectivity survives for years inside the treated tissue."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The account of incubation in paragraph 9 most strongly suggests that","choices":["symptoms track how much conversion has occurred, not time since exposure","the agent lies dormant until a fever activates it","younger animals incubate the disease longer than older ones do","most exposed animals never accumulate any misfolded protein","the immune system holds the agent in check for many years before it finally fails"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"The slow part is the beginning, the improbable first seed"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"Once enough seeds exist the curve turns steep and the brain fills quickly"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the slow step is forming the improbable first seed, and that once enough seeds exist the curve turns steep and the brain fills quickly. Timing therefore follows the amount of templating, which is why the disease seems to arrive all at once.","distractorNotes":[null,"No fever appears anywhere in this passage, and paragraph 1 lists its absence as one of the original puzzles.","Age is never raised as a variable in the account of incubation.","Paragraph 9 says the first seed is improbable, not that seeding usually fails outright.","Paragraph 2 says the agent provokes no immune response at all, so there is nothing holding it in check."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on paragraph 12, which step would most likely shorten the incubation period of a prion in a new host species?","choices":["raising the dose of the first inoculation until every animal sickens","passing the agent through several generations of that host","using a host whose own PrP gene has been deleted","selecting a strain that has never been passaged before","storing the inoculum at a lower temperature before use"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"Pass the agent again, now mouse to mouse, and the incubation shortens"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 describes exactly this: pass the agent again in the new host and the incubation shortens and settles at a new stable value. The shape adapts to the host sequence it is now copying itself onto.","distractorNotes":["Dose is not the variable paragraph 12 identifies, since the shortening comes with repeated passage in the new host.",null,"A host with no PrP of its own would supply nothing for the template to convert, which is the reverse of what is wanted.","Being new to the host is what makes a first passage long, so an unpassaged strain would not help.","Storage conditions of the inoculum are never mentioned as affecting incubation."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"The central point of the passage is that","choices":["scrapie has been recognized in British sheep since the eighteenth century and is still untreatable","sterilization practice in hospitals has had to change because of prion diseases","a protein can copy information in its shape, and this is not rare","the central dogma of molecular biology has turned out to be false in its essentials","an infectious agent can have strains without any genetic differences"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":7,"quote":"Many chains can fold more than one way, and one of those ways can copy itself."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"It is simply written in geometry rather than in bases."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"The same templating move turns up across a set of proteins with no relation to PrP."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":6,"quote":"the first visible case of something ordinary"}],"why":"The passage moves from one sheep disease to a general property of proteins, ending in paragraphs 15 and 16 with a list of other proteins that template their own shape and with a normal cellular version of the same trick.","distractorNotes":["Scrapie opens the passage as an entry point, and the argument travels well past sheep.","Sterilization gets one paragraph as a practical consequence rather than being the point the passage builds toward.",null,"Paragraph 7 treats the sequence rule as sound and hard won, and the passage adds a second channel rather than overturning the first.","Strains are one striking piece of evidence in paragraph 10, offered in support of the larger claim rather than as the claim."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 13 is included mainly in order to","choices":["show that the species barrier can be crossed under real conditions","argue that beef is unsafe to eat","explain how rendered animal feed is manufactured","date the first recognition of scrapie in Britain","establish that kuru and the cattle disease were caused by the very same strain"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"Two episodes showed how partial that protection is."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"it is a steep hill rather than a wall"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 ends by calling the barrier a steep hill rather than a wall, and paragraph 13 supplies two real episodes, cattle in Britain and kuru among the Fore, in which the agent did move. It is evidence for the point just made.","distractorNotes":[null,"The paragraph reports a past epidemic and its toll without making any recommendation about what to eat.","Feed is named as the route of spread in a single clause, and the manufacturing process is never described.","That date is in paragraph 1, and paragraph 13 deals entirely with the twentieth century.","The two episodes are presented as separate events in different species, with no claim that one strain caused both."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["a list of prion diseases followed by their treatments","a debate between two camps, with each given equal space","a chronological history of one laboratory's work","a practical guide to sterilization, with background supplied first","a puzzle set out, narrowed by evidence, then resolved and widened"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"settled one question and opened a harder one"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"the conclusion was awkward but plain"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"The resolution is that the shape carries the trait."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"The same templating move turns up across a set of proteins with no relation to PrP."}],"why":"The passage opens with a disease nothing explained, spends the middle paragraphs eliminating a genome and building a mechanism, then widens in paragraphs 15 and 16 to other proteins that do the same thing.","distractorNotes":["Treatments never appear, and the passage offers nothing on curing any of these diseases.","Only one position is developed, and the opposing view in paragraph 7 is explained rather than argued for at length.","No laboratory is followed, and the passage moves across decades, species, and proteins rather than tracking one group.","Sterilization occupies a single paragraph well after the argument is built, and it is a consequence rather than the destination.",null]},{"id":"rc-p16-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the scientists who first rejected the idea of an infectious protein is best described as","choices":["amused by their confusion","sharply critical of their caution","wholly indifferent to their position","admiring of their eventual conversion","sympathetic to their reasoning"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"that telling is unfair"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"Demanding extraordinary evidence for that was the correct first response"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 calls the heretic story unfair, says the objection was not fussiness, and ends by calling the demand for extraordinary evidence the correct first response. That is a defense of the doubters.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in paragraph 7 treats the objection as confusion, and amusement is not the note the paragraph strikes.","Criticism is what the paragraph sets out to correct, and it calls their caution correct.","Indifference would mean no judgment at all, and the paragraph makes one in its final sentence.","No conversion is described, and the paragraph praises the initial resistance rather than any later change of mind.",null]},{"id":"rc-p16-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 16, the author's attitude toward calling Alzheimer disease infectious is best described as","choices":["enthusiastic about a bold new framing","uneasy with the word and careful to limit it","confident that the disease will prove contagious","dismissive of the entire line of research as a distraction","neutral, reporting the claim without any comment"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"The word infectious is doing much less work there than it appears."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"treating a shared molecular move as evidence of contagion is the error the field works hardest to avoid"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 opens with a caution, says the word is doing much less work there than it appears, and calls the slide from shared mechanism to contagion the error the field works hardest to avoid.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph opens with a warning rather than with enthusiasm about the framing.",null,"The author states that nothing indicates these diseases pass between people in ordinary life.","Paragraph 15 treats the seeding work as solid and interesting, so the research itself is not being brushed aside.","Comment is exactly what the paragraph offers, naming the loose use of the word as an error."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Healthy animals that have never been exposed still make PrP","Formaldehyde treatment can leave infectivity intact for years","The disease form of PrP contains more beta sheet than the normal form","A prion strain carries a short stretch of its own nucleic acid","Breaking a fibril increases the number of surfaces that can convert"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"healthy uninfected animals transcribed it steadily","choice":0},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":7,"quote":"sealing the infectivity inside, where it survives for years","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"almost no beta sheet","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"more than forty percent sheet","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"when a fibril snaps, one growing surface becomes two","choice":4},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"carried no nucleic acid at all","choice":3}],"why":"The passage argues the opposite at length: paragraph 3 reports that nucleic acid damaging treatments did not touch the agent, and paragraph 11 says the strain information is written in geometry rather than in bases. The other four statements all sit on the page.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 says the gene sits in the animal's own chromosomes and that healthy uninfected animals transcribe it steadily.","Paragraph 14 says formaldehyde hardens the tissue and seals the infectivity inside, where it survives for years.","Paragraph 6 contrasts almost no sheet in the normal fold with more than forty percent in the disease fold.",null,"Paragraph 8 says that when a fibril snaps, one growing surface becomes two."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to know why a prion takes longer to cause disease the first time it enters a new species should turn to","choices":["paragraph 3","paragraph 8","paragraph 10","paragraph 12","paragraph 15"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"the few that sicken take far longer than a mouse given a mouse prion"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 is the one that crosses species: a hamster prion in a mouse, the long and incomplete first passage, and the shortening that follows on later passage in the new host.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 is the radiation work that argued against a genome, with no mention of species.","Paragraph 8 gives the templating mechanism in general terms and never brings in a second species.","Paragraph 10 concerns strains within one host species, mice bred to be identical.",null,"Paragraph 15 extends the mechanism to other proteins and does not return to crossing species."]}]},"rc-p17":{"id":"rc-p17","title":"What the Ice Remembers and What It Loses","topic":"ice cores as a record of past atmospheres","field":"earth science","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1364,"paragraphs":[["A drill through an ice sheet returns cylinders of ice about ten centimeters across, boxed for a freezer laboratory.","What arrives looks like plumbing stock.","What it holds is an archive.","Every cylinder holds snow from particular days, the dust that fell with it, and, sealed in small bubbles, a sample of the air the planet was breathing some time after that snow landed.","That last clause carries the first complication: the ice and the air trapped inside it are not the same age."],["Turning snow into ice is slow mechanical work.","Fresh polar snow has a density near one tenth that of water, mostly empty space between crystals.","Under the weight of each new season those crystals round off, press together, and weld.","The intermediate material is firn, granular and porous, and its pore space forms continuous channels that run all the way to the surface.","Solid glacial ice has a density of about 0.917 grams per cubic centimeter."],["Because those channels stay open, the upper firn breathes.","Air from above moves down through it and back up, so the gas in open pores thirty meters down is very nearly modern.","How deep that exchange reaches depends on temperature and snowfall: past a hundred meters in the cold, dry interior of East Antarctica, only fifty on a warm coastal dome with heavy snowfall.","At the base of the column sits a lock in zone, layers dense enough that vertical mixing effectively stops."],["Below the lock in zone, at a density near 0.83 grams per cubic centimeter, the remaining pores pinch shut and the air in them becomes a set of isolated bubbles.","That moment of closure, not the moment the snow fell, is the birthday of the gas sample.","Because the ice waited while the firn buried it, the air is always younger than the ice holding it.","That offset varies enormously, from a few decades at a wet coastal site to several thousand years at the coldest inland sites in a glacial period."],["The offset is the central obstacle to the question the cores are most often asked.","Anyone asking whether carbon dioxide rose before or after a temperature change is comparing a measurement on the gas with one on the ice, and the two sit on different clocks.","Correcting for it requires a model of the past firn column, which in turn requires estimates of past temperature and snowfall.","The uncertainty in that correction is often larger than the lead or the lag under debate."],["Closure also blurs.","Air in the open firn is stirred for years before sealing, and bubbles close at a range of depths, so a measurement averages a window of time, not one year.","A sharp spike lasting a decade is flattened into a gentle rise, or lost.","Mixing has a use too: methane spreads between the hemispheres within a couple of years, so its swings can be matched from core to core."],["The ice itself carries a second archive, independent of the bubbles.","Windblown dust records the dryness of distant continents.","Sea salt tracks storms and sea ice.","Sulfate spikes mark eruptions, and beryllium-10, produced when cosmic rays strike the upper atmosphere, tracks the sun's activity.","Greenland ice carries enough calcium carbonate dust to react with acids already in the ice and manufacture carbon dioxide inside the core itself, which is why Antarctic ice is the reference archive for past carbon dioxide."],["The best known measurement made on the ice is the isotope ratio of its water.","Most water is built from oxygen-16 and ordinary hydrogen, but a small fraction carries oxygen-18 or deuterium, the heavier hydrogen.","Heavy molecules evaporate slightly less readily and condense slightly more readily.","As an air mass travels from a warm ocean toward a cold pole, each round of condensation removes heavy molecules first, so the colder the final condensation, the lighter the snow that falls."],["The chain of reasoning turns a chemical ratio into a thermometer.","The calibration in common use is spatial: measure the isotope ratio of recent snow at many sites across an ice sheet, plot each against the site's mean annual temperature, and take the slope.","Applying that slope downward through a core converts an isotope profile into a temperature history."],["The step from a line across space to a line through time needs care, and it is worth being blunt about what the reading assumes.","It assumes that snow fell in the same season then as now, from the same oceans at the same temperatures, and that the ice surface stood at the same elevation.","None of these holds exactly.","If the ice sheet stood taller, its surface was colder for reasons unrelated to the climate at sea level.","The thermometer is not wrong, but it is conditional, and reading it as a direct account of air temperature claims too much."],["What rescues it is that the ice offers thermometers of other kinds.","An ice sheet conducts heat poorly, so a borehole holds a faint temperature profile left by past surface conditions.","During an abrupt warming, the temperature contrast across the firn sorts nitrogen and argon isotopes by mass, leaving a fingerprint in the trapped gas that records the size of the temperature jump directly.","Applied to Greenland, these methods put the change since the last glacial period close to twice what the spatial slope alone suggested."],["Dating begins with counting.","Near the top the layers are visible, coarse summer crystals alternating with fine winter ones.","Deeper down, chemistry keeps the calendar.","Dust and sodium peak in one season, hydrogen peroxide and the water isotopes in another, and a probe drawn along the core measures electrical conductivity, which traces acidity layer by layer.","Counting works where snowfall is heavy.","Central Greenland gets roughly twenty centimeters of ice a year, enough to resolve single years well back into the last glacial period."],["Counting alone would drift, so the count is pinned.","A large eruption spreads sulfate to both hemispheres within a couple of years, leaving one horizon that shows up in Greenland and Antarctic cores alike.","Eruptions with known historical dates anchor the recent count outright.","Farther back, sharp beryllium-10 peaks produced by extreme solar events appear in the ice and in the radiocarbon of tree rings at the same moment, tying two independent chronologies together."],["The deepest ice is the least trustworthy, and the reasons compound.","An ice sheet spreads under its own weight, so every annual layer is stretched sideways and thinned as it is buried.","Once layers are that thin, diffusion of water molecules through the solid smears the isotope cycles until annual structure is gone.","Geothermal heat can melt the bottom away, removing the oldest ice.","Worse, ice near bedrock flows around obstacles and can fold back over itself, stacking older ice above younger."],["Below the depth where counting fails, chronology comes from models of ice flow and from matching the record to Earth's orbital cycles.","This is reasonable but weaker, and the resulting ages carry uncertainties of thousands of years.","It is why the long Antarctic records, reaching back eight hundred thousand years, serve for the size and ordering of slow glacial cycles rather than the fine timing of a single event."],["All of this shapes what the record can say about rates.","Smoothing in the firn and thinning at depth push the same way: both hide fast changes and preserve slow ones.","The record therefore sets a floor under past rates of change rather than a ceiling, and a claim that no past rise matched the speed of the present one is only as strong as the resolution of the ice behind it.","That claim holds up because it does not rest on the deep cores.","Sites with heavy snowfall resolve the last two thousand years to a decade or better, and across that stretch the record is flat."],["None of this makes the archive less valuable.","Its limits are unusually well characterized.","The age offset can be modeled, the smoothing estimated, the thinning calculated, and the disturbed sections identified and set aside.","An archive whose distortions are known and bounded is worth far more than one that merely looks clean."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p17-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the air held inside a bubble in an ice core dates from","choices":["the season in which the snow now surrounding it first fell","the moment the pores in the firn pinched shut and sealed it off","the year the core was drilled and lifted to the surface","the time when the firn column at that site first began to form","the point at which the ice reached a density of 0.917 grams per cubic centimeter"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"That moment of closure, not the moment the snow fell, is the birthday of the gas sample."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"the remaining pores pinch shut and the air in them becomes a set of isolated bubbles"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the moment of closure, not the moment the snow fell, is the birthday of the gas sample. Below the lock in zone the pores pinch shut and the air becomes isolated bubbles.","distractorNotes":["The passage states the opposite, that the snow and the gas carry different dates and the gas is the younger of the two.",null,"Drilling only recovers the sample. The passage dates the gas to closure in the firn, far below the modern surface.","The firn column at a site is far older than any single bubble in it, and paragraph 4 ties the gas age to pore closure.","The number 0.917 is the density of solid glacial ice given in paragraph 2, not the close off density of about 0.83 named in paragraph 4."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says that acidity is traced layer by layer in a core by measuring","choices":["the density of each band of crystals in the ice","the concentration of sea salt held in the ice","the ratio of oxygen-18 to ordinary oxygen-16","the amount of windblown mineral dust deposited in each layer","electrical conductivity along the core"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"measures electrical conductivity, which traces acidity layer by layer"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 lists the seasonal markers and then says a probe drawn along the core measures electrical conductivity, which traces acidity layer by layer.","distractorNotes":["Density tells you how far snow has turned into ice, which paragraph 2 covers, not how acidic a layer is.","Sea salt appears in paragraph 7 as a tracer of storms and sea ice, not as the acidity measurement.","The isotope ratio is the temperature proxy and one of the seasonal markers, not the way acidity is read.","Dust is named as a seasonal marker and as a continental tracer, but the acidity reading is the conductivity probe.",null]},{"id":"rc-p17-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Antarctic ice rather than Greenland ice serves as the reference record for past carbon dioxide because","choices":["Greenland cores rarely reach back far enough to cover a full glacial cycle of the kind Antarctic ice records","the firn column in Greenland is too shallow to seal any gas at all","carbonate dust in Greenland ice reacts with acids in the ice and makes carbon dioxide inside the core","Greenland receives too little snowfall for its annual layers to be counted","carbon dioxide mixes between the hemispheres far too slowly to ever reach Greenland"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"react with acids already in the ice and manufacture carbon dioxide inside the core itself"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says Greenland ice carries enough calcium carbonate dust to react with acids already in the ice and manufacture carbon dioxide inside the core itself.","distractorNotes":["The passage never compares the two regions on length of record. The objection it raises is chemical contamination inside the core.","Greenland firn seals gas normally. Paragraph 7 objects to a reaction in the ice, not to a failure to trap bubbles.",null,"Paragraph 12 says the opposite, that central Greenland's snowfall is heavy enough to make layer counting work well.","Paragraph 6 says methane crosses between the hemispheres in a couple of years, so slow mixing is not the problem raised here."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage presents the sorting of nitrogen and argon isotopes in the firn as a way to","choices":["measure the size of an abrupt temperature jump directly","date the gas trapped at the bottom of the firn column","identify which season a given layer of snow fell in","detect the sulfate that a distant volcanic eruption left behind in the ice","estimate how much snow fell at a site during a glacial period"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"records the size of the temperature jump directly"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says that during an abrupt warming the temperature contrast across the firn sorts nitrogen and argon by mass, leaving a fingerprint in the trapped gas that records the size of the jump directly.","distractorNotes":[null,"The sorting records a temperature change, not an age. Dating comes from counting and flow models in paragraphs 12 through 15.","Seasonal identification comes from dust, sodium, hydrogen peroxide and the water isotopes in paragraph 12.","Volcanic sulfate is a dating tie point in paragraph 13 and has nothing to do with gas isotope sorting.","Snowfall estimates feed the firn model in paragraph 5. The isotope sorting described here reports a temperature change."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says that sharp peaks of beryllium-10 are useful for dating because they","choices":["appear only in ice that has never been disturbed by flow","mark the season in which each annual layer was deposited","record the total amount of sulfate a large eruption released","show up in the ice and in the radiocarbon of tree rings at the same moment","accumulate in the ice at a steady rate that can be converted directly into an age"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"appear in the ice and in the radiocarbon of tree rings at the same moment"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says beryllium-10 peaks from extreme solar events appear in the ice and in tree ring radiocarbon at the same moment, tying two independent chronologies together.","distractorNotes":["The passage never links beryllium-10 to undisturbed ice. Its value is that it shows up in two separate archives at once.","Seasonal marking is done by dust, sodium, hydrogen peroxide and the water isotopes, as paragraph 12 explains.","Sulfate is the volcanic marker described just before this. Beryllium-10 comes from cosmic rays and solar events.",null,"This describes a steady radiometric clock. The passage values beryllium-10 for matching two chronologies, not for decaying predictably."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, \"breathes\" most nearly means that the upper firn","choices":["expands and contracts as the temperature at the surface rises and falls","releases the gases that were sealed into it during earlier seasons of snowfall","exchanges air with the atmosphere through its connected pore spaces","supports living communities of microbes within its open pores","loses mass steadily to evaporation from the surface of the snow"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"Air from above moves down through it and back up"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"its pore space forms continuous channels that run all the way to the surface"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 explains the word right after using it: air moves down and back up through the open channels, so the gas thirty meters down is very nearly modern.","distractorNotes":["The word here is about air moving in and out, not about the firn changing volume.","Nothing is sealed in the upper firn yet. Paragraph 4 places trapping below the lock in zone.",null,"The passage never mentions anything living in the firn. The image is entirely about air moving in and out.","The passage describes firn gaining mass and compacting under new snow, not wasting away."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 16, \"floor\" most nearly means","choices":["an upper bound on how fast past change could have been","a lower bound on how fast change could have been","the lowest temperature ever reached in the polar regions","the base of the ice sheet where it rests on bedrock","the deepest section of a core that can still be counted"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"sets a floor under past rates of change rather than a ceiling"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"both hide fast changes and preserve slow ones"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 says smoothing and thinning hide fast change, so the record can show that change was at least this fast without ruling out something faster. A floor is the lower bound, and the paragraph sets it against a ceiling.","distractorNotes":["That is the ceiling the paragraph says the record does not give, and a floor works the other way.",null,"Here the word limits a rate of change, not a temperature.","This is the literal sense of the word, but the paragraph is discussing limits on a rate, not the bottom of an ice sheet.","Depth comes up nearby, though the phrase is about how fast past change could have been, not about where counting stops."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q08","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from paragraph 5 that a conclusion about whether carbon dioxide rose before or after a warming is most trustworthy when it is drawn from","choices":["the deepest ice available at a site, since it covers by far the longest span of time","a single core, so that only one chronology is involved","gas measurements alone, with the ice measurements set aside entirely","an interior site where the firn column is unusually deep and cold","a site where the ice age and the gas age are close together"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"the two sit on different clocks"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"The uncertainty in that correction is often larger than the lead or the lag under debate."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"from a few decades at a wet coastal site to several thousand years"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the correction for the age offset is often larger than the lead or lag being argued about, so the smaller that offset is, the less the answer depends on the model.","distractorNotes":["Length of record does not help with sequencing, and paragraph 14 adds that the deepest ice is the least trustworthy of all.","Using one core does not shrink the offset between the two clocks inside that same core.","The comparison needs both, since the temperature signal is read from the ice and the gas is read from the bubbles.","A deeper firn column takes longer to seal air, which makes the offset larger and the correction harder.",null]},{"id":"rc-p17-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 10 suggests that a temperature history built from water isotopes could be misleading if","choices":["the core was drilled at a site with unusually heavy snowfall","the laboratory measured deuterium instead of oxygen-18","the surrounding ice contained unusually large amounts of volcanic sulfate","the height of the ice sheet surface changed over the period covered","the bubbles in the core closed off at a shallower depth than usual"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"If the ice sheet stood taller, its surface was colder for reasons unrelated to the climate at sea level."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"that the ice surface stood at the same elevation"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 lists constant surface elevation as one of the assumptions behind the reading, then notes that a taller ice sheet had a colder surface for reasons unrelated to the climate at sea level.","distractorNotes":["Heavy snowfall helps the record by improving resolution. The worry in this paragraph is season, moisture source, and elevation.","The passage treats deuterium and oxygen-18 as two versions of the same signal, not as a source of error.","Sulfate is a dating marker in paragraph 13 and is never said to distort the isotope thermometer.",null,"Close off depth changes the age of the gas, which is paragraph 4, not the isotope ratio measured in the ice itself."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Ice cores have proven beyond argument that carbon dioxide and temperature have always risen and fallen together","The water isotope thermometer should be abandoned in favor of borehole and trapped gas methods","Ice holds a usable record of past air, but each step from snowfall to number adds a limit to account for","Deep drilling in Antarctica has extended the climate record much further back than drilling in Greenland","Layer counting is the only dependable way to assign an age to anything found inside an ice core"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"What it holds is an archive."},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"That last clause carries the first complication"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"The thermometer is not wrong, but it is conditional"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":4,"quote":"An archive whose distortions are known and bounded is worth far more than one that merely looks clean."}],"why":"The passage traces snow into ice, then into a readable record, and at every stage names what is lost or shifted. Paragraph 17 states the point outright: the archive is valuable because its distortions are known and bounded.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the ordering of the two is exactly what the age offset makes hard to establish.","Paragraph 11 presents those methods as checks that correct the isotope reading, not as replacements for it.",null,"The length of the Antarctic record is one detail in paragraph 15, not the argument the whole passage builds.","Paragraphs 13 and 15 add volcanic horizons, solar events and flow models as further ways of assigning ages."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q11","type":"function","stem":"The main function of paragraph 11 in the passage is to","choices":["introduce a second and more reliable method for dating the layers in a core","show that the record carries its own checks on the isotope reading","argue that measurements made on the ice are more reliable than measurements made on the gas","explain why the deepest sections of a core cannot be counted year by year","describe how heat from the interior of the Earth reaches the base of an ice sheet"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"What rescues it is that the ice offers thermometers of other kinds."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"close to twice what the spatial slope alone suggested"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 ends by calling the isotope thermometer conditional. Paragraph 11 opens with what rescues it and gives two other thermometers, one of which revised the Greenland estimate by about a factor of two.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 11 is about temperature, not age. Dating begins in the paragraph that follows it.",null,"One of the two checks it describes, the isotope sorting of nitrogen and argon, is a measurement made on the trapped gas.","That problem belongs to paragraph 14, which takes up the difficulties of the deepest ice.","Geothermal heat appears in paragraph 14 as a way the oldest ice is lost, not as the subject of this paragraph."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["It traces snow into ice, then how the record is read and dated, and finally what it can claim","It compares the Greenland and Antarctic drilling programs and judges which of them is better","It states a conclusion about past climate and then works backward through the evidence supporting it","It moves through the seasons of a single year of snowfall and then repeats the cycle at greater depth","It alternates between describing a measurement and describing an eruption that confirms it"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"Turning snow into ice is slow mechanical work."},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"The chain of reasoning turns a chemical ratio into a thermometer."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"Dating begins with counting."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"All of this shapes what the record can say about rates."}],"why":"The passage runs in order: snow to firn to sealed bubbles in paragraphs 2 through 6, then the isotope thermometer and the dating methods, then the deepest ice, and finally what the record can and cannot say about rates.","distractorNotes":[null,"Both regions appear, but as sources of different kinds of information, and they are never ranked against each other.","No conclusion is stated up front. The passage builds its cautions as it goes and gathers them at the end.","Seasonal layering appears only as a dating method in paragraph 12, not as the plan of the passage.","Volcanic eruptions appear once, as a way of pinning a layer count, not as a recurring pattern."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the water isotope thermometer in paragraph 10 is best described as","choices":["dismissive of a method that rests on assumptions that no one is able to test","enthusiastic about a technique that requires no further checking","indifferent to whether the method gives an accurate temperature","puzzled by a result that nobody working in the field has managed to explain","accepting of the method while insisting that its assumptions be stated"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"The thermometer is not wrong, but it is conditional"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"it is worth being blunt about what the reading assumes"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 lists what the reading assumes, says none of it holds exactly, and then says plainly that the thermometer is not wrong but conditional. That is acceptance with conditions attached.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph says the thermometer is not wrong, and the next paragraph shows the assumptions being tested.","The paragraph spends most of its length on what the reading takes for granted, which is not enthusiasm.","Accuracy is the entire concern of the paragraph, and the author cares enough to list what can bias it.","Nothing here is left unexplained. The author names each assumption and shows how a failed one can skew the result.",null]},{"id":"rc-p17-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 16, the author treats the claim that no past rise matched the speed of the present one as","choices":["an overstatement the ice record cannot support at all","a conclusion that the deepest Antarctic cores establish most firmly","a question that further deep drilling will have to settle one way or another","sound, but only because well resolved records stand behind it","less interesting than the question of how the layers are counted"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"That claim holds up because it does not rest on the deep cores."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"resolve the last two thousand years to a decade or better"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 says the claim is only as strong as the resolution of the ice behind it, then says it holds up because it rests on heavy snowfall sites that resolve the last two thousand years to a decade or better.","distractorNotes":["The author says the claim holds up, and names the evidence that carries it.","The paragraph says the claim survives precisely because it does not rest on the deep cores.","The author treats the question as already answered by the well resolved recent record.",null,"Counting is discussed earlier as a tool. Here the author is judging the rate claim itself, not ranking the two topics."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage names all of the following as reasons the deepest ice is hard to trust EXCEPT","choices":["layers are thinned by the spreading of the ice sheet until single years are very thin","the bubbles at that depth have leaked their gas into the nearby ice","diffusion of water molecules through solid ice erases the annual isotope cycles","heat from below can melt away the oldest ice at the base","flow around obstacles near bedrock can place older ice above younger ice"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"every annual layer is stretched sideways and thinned as it is buried","choice":0},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"diffusion of water molecules through the solid smears the isotope cycles","choice":2},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"Geothermal heat can melt the bottom away","choice":3},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"stacking older ice above younger","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 14 lists thinning, isotope diffusion, basal melting and folding near bedrock. Leaking bubbles are never mentioned anywhere in the passage.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 14 says the spreading ice sheet stretches every annual layer sideways and thins it as it is buried.",null,"Paragraph 14 names this smearing as one reason annual structure disappears at depth.","Paragraph 14 says geothermal heat can melt the bottom away and remove the oldest ice.","Paragraph 14 describes ice folding back over itself near bedrock and stacking older ice above younger."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for an explanation of how a volcanic eruption helps fix the age of a layer would find it in","choices":["paragraph 3, on how deep the open firn column runs","paragraph 7, on what the ice carries besides trapped air","paragraph 10, on the assumptions that sit behind the isotope thermometer","paragraph 13, on the tie points that keep a layer count from drifting","paragraph 14, on the problems that affect the deepest ice"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"Eruptions with known historical dates anchor the recent count outright."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"leaving one horizon that shows up in Greenland and Antarctic cores alike"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 explains that a large eruption lays down one sulfate horizon in both hemispheres, and that eruptions with known historical dates anchor the recent count.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 is about how far the firn exchanges air with the atmosphere, not about dating.","Paragraph 7 mentions sulfate as a marker of eruptions but does not explain how it pins a chronology.","Paragraph 10 is about reconstructing temperature, not about assigning ages to layers.",null,"Paragraph 14 explains why deep layers are unreliable, not how eruptions anchor a count."]}]},"rc-p18":{"id":"rc-p18","title":"What Venom Has Actually Given Medicine","topic":"venom as a source of medicines","field":"pharmacology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1374,"paragraphs":[["Every few years a headline announces that the next great painkiller is swimming in a tide pool or coiled under a rock.","Venom is chemistry built to shut down a nervous or circulatory system in seconds, and it is extraordinarily good at the job.","Researchers like to call venom a library that evolution has already screened against the very proteins medicine wants to control.","The premise is sound.","The output is thin."],["First, the singular noun: there is no such thing as the toxin in a snake.","A venom is a mixture of dozens to hundreds of components: enzymes that break down tissue, small molecules that cause pain, and, of most interest here, short folded peptides that bind ion channels and receptors.","Cone snails are the extreme case: one species may produce over a hundred peptides, and the mixture shifts with whether the animal is hunting or defending."],["The peptides are small, ten to forty amino acids, and what makes them durable is not length but stitching.","Cysteine residues pair off into disulfide bonds that lash distant parts of the chain together, and in one common arrangement, the inhibitor cystine knot, three such bonds build a compact core in which one bond threads through the ring formed by the other two.","The result is less a floppy string than hardened resin, resistant to heat, acid, and the enzymes that destroy ordinary peptides."],["That rigid core explains the selectivity that first drew pharmacologists in.","The cysteines and the fold stay conserved while venom genes duplicate readily and the copies accumulate changes on the exposed loops between them.","Prey evolve resistance, predators need fresh keys, and the cycle repeats.","What comes out is a set of peptides that can tell nearly identical proteins apart."],["Humans carry nine subtypes of voltage-gated sodium channel, built alike yet sitting in different tissues, and a drug that blocks all of them stops the heart.","Chemists have spent decades trying to build small molecules that touch one subtype and spare the rest, with limited success, because the pockets they bind are so alike.","Venom peptides sidestep that by not using a pocket: they lie across the outer face of the channel and read a patch of its surface, where small differences decide whether they stick."],["Binding tightly to a human channel is a laboratory result, not a therapy, and the distance between them is where nearly every venom program has died.","Ask what the animal was selecting for: a dose delivered by needle, acting within seconds, on prey it will eat and never see again.","Nothing pressed it toward a molecule safe for months at a steady dose or able to survive a stomach.","Evolution screened for the predator's problem.","The patient's problem was never on the test."],["The practical form of that mismatch is the window.","Every drug has a range between the amount that helps and the amount that harms, and usefulness depends far more on the width of that range than on potency.","Venom peptides tend to have narrow ones.","Several candidates worked in patients and were dropped anyway, because the helpful dose sat close to the one that brought dizziness or a fall in blood pressure."],["Against that, the record of approved drugs is short but real.","Captopril, approved in 1981 to block angiotensin converting enzyme, began with peptides in a pit viper's venom.","Chemists did not develop the peptide; they worked out how it met the enzyme, then built a small molecule that met it the same way and could be swallowed.","Eptifibatide, approved in 1998, is a ring modeled on a rattlesnake protein that stops platelets clumping; tirofiban hits the same target with a synthetic molecule of similar shape.","Exenatide, approved in 2005, is a peptide from Gila monster saliva; it lowers blood sugar through the same receptor as a human hormone that survives about two minutes, and because the lizard version resists the enzyme that clips the human one, it lasts for hours."],["The purest case is ziconotide, a peptide with three disulfide bonds, identical to one made by a cone snail.","Approved in the United States in 2004, it eases pain when opioids have failed.","It is also close to unusable.","Swallowed, it is destroyed.","In a vein, it is filtered out by the kidney within minutes, and could not reach the central nervous system anyway.","The only route that works is continuous infusion through a catheter threaded into the fluid around the spinal cord, fed by a pump implanted under the skin."],["So the tally is roughly a dozen, most of them injected, and several not peptides at all but small molecules designed after a peptide showed the way.","Set that against the pool: some two hundred thousand species are thought venomous, and distinct venom peptides have been put in the millions.","Well under one percent have ever been tested against a human target.","Those numbers get quoted to argue a breakthrough is near, but read the other way, an enormous input has produced a small output for reasons that are not mysterious."],["Most concern getting the molecule where it must go.","A peptide taken by mouth meets stomach acid and then a wall of digestive enzymes, and whatever survives is too large and polar to cross the gut.","Injection solves that and creates a second problem: small peptides slip through the filter in the kidney and are gone from the blood in minutes.","Targets inside the brain or spinal cord sit behind a barrier peptides do not cross.","Any foreign sequence may also be recognized by the immune system, which can neutralize the drug or attack the patient.","None of these are failures of potency."],["There is a tool for each problem.","A peptide can be closed into a ring, hung with a polymer tail, or rebuilt from mirror-image amino acids, each change buying time against enzymes or the kidney.","Each change also alters the surface that made the peptide selective, so the rebuilt molecule often has to prove itself again."],["It is worth being blunt about the framing that keeps the field oversold: calling venom a library is a metaphor doing a great deal of unearned work.","A library implies that the hard part, generating good candidates, is finished, and that what remains is retrieval.","What evolution supplied is a vast set of molecules optimized for an unrelated purpose, in which a handful are useful and there is no index.","Counting sequences is not counting leads.","The gap between a peptide that exists and one that can be given to a patient is where the work lives, and being natural does not narrow it."],["For most of the twentieth century the limit was supply: a researcher milked animals, separated the venom, and needed a great deal of material to isolate one component.","Sequencing the venom gland changed that: reading the messenger RNA there yields the sequence of everything the animal is building, including peptides far too scarce to isolate by hand.","The bottleneck moved rather than vanished.","Thousands of sequences now arrive with no indication of what any of them binds, and testing one peptide against a panel of ion channels stays slow and costly even with automated instruments."],["The pain channel called Nav1.7 shows the pattern in miniature.","People born without a working copy feel no pain and are otherwise largely ordinary, about as well validated a target as exists.","Spider and scorpion venoms gave up potent blockers, candidates entered trials, and the results have been repeatedly disappointing.","The channel may yet yield.","What it has not done is yield easily."],["The fair summary is neither the press release nor the dismissal.","Venom has given medicine a small number of genuinely useful drugs and far more research tools: a peptide that blocks one channel subtype and nothing else is how a physiologist proves what that subtype does.","Its most reliable contribution has been information rather than product: proof that a protein can be hit selectively, which chemists can then pursue with molecules that behave better in a body.","Captopril is the template, not ziconotide.","The animal did not make a drug; it made an argument that a drug was possible, and the rest of the work belongs to people."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p18-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the only delivery route that works for ziconotide is","choices":["a tablet taken by mouth on a regular daily schedule","an injection into a vein, repeated several times through the day","a steady infusion into the fluid that bathes the spinal cord","a patch worn on the skin above the part of the body that hurts","a single injection into the muscle at the site of the pain"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"continuous infusion through a catheter threaded into the fluid around the spinal cord"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"it is filtered out by the kidney within minutes"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 walks through the routes that fail and lands on the one that works: a catheter threaded into the fluid around the spinal cord, fed by a pump implanted under the skin.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 9 says that swallowed, the peptide is destroyed.","The same paragraph rules a vein out, since the kidney clears the peptide within minutes and it cannot reach the central nervous system.",null,"No skin patch appears anywhere in the passage; the routes weighed are the mouth, a vein, and the space around the spinal cord.","A muscle injection is never mentioned, and any injection into the body still leaves the peptide facing the kidney and the barrier around the nervous system."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says the peptide found in Gila monster saliva outlasts the human hormone it mimics because it","choices":["resists the enzyme that clips the human version","binds a different and considerably more durable receptor","is far smaller than the human hormone it stands in for","is taken by mouth rather than injected into the patient","carries three disulfide bonds that hold its shape together"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"the lizard version resists the enzyme that clips the human one"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"through the same receptor as a human hormone"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 gives the reason outright: the human hormone survives about two minutes, and the lizard version lasts for hours because it resists the enzyme that clips the human one.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 8 states that it works through the same receptor as the human hormone, not a different one.","Size is never offered as the reason, and the passage never compares the two molecules by size.","Nothing says this drug is swallowed, and paragraph 10 notes that most venom-derived drugs are injected.","Three disulfide bonds belong to ziconotide in paragraph 9, not to the peptide from the lizard."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q03","type":"detail","stem":"As the passage describes it, the inhibitor cystine knot consists of","choices":["a single disulfide bond looping the two ends of the chain together","three disulfide bonds lying side by side along the outer surface of the peptide","a ring of cysteines that grips the ion channel directly","three disulfide bonds, one threaded through the ring made by the other two","a knot of hydrogen bonds formed between neighboring amino acids"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"one bond threads through the ring formed by the other two"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 defines the arrangement exactly: three disulfide bonds build a compact core, and one of them threads through the ring formed by the other two.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 describes three bonds in this arrangement, not one.","The number is right and the geometry is not, since one bond passes through the ring the other two make.","The cysteines hold the peptide's own shape in paragraph 3; the surface that meets a channel is described in paragraph 5.",null,"Paragraph 3 names disulfide bonds between cysteine residues, not hydrogen bonds."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage attributes the trouble chemists have had in targeting a single sodium channel subtype to","choices":["the small number of subtypes they have available to work with","the close similarity of the pockets that small molecules bind","the barrier that keeps drugs out of the brain and spinal cord","the speed with which the body clears small molecules from the blood","the immune response that a foreign molecule provokes in the patient"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"because the pockets they bind are so alike"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says chemists have had limited success because the pockets those molecules bind are so alike from one subtype to the next.","distractorNotes":["Nine subtypes is a fact from paragraph 5, but their number is never treated as the obstacle.",null,"That barrier is a peptide delivery problem in paragraph 11, not the reason a small molecule cannot single out one subtype.","Rapid clearance in paragraph 11 applies to small peptides in the kidney, and it is a dosing problem rather than a targeting one.","Immune recognition appears in paragraph 11 as a hazard for foreign sequences, not as the reason subtypes are hard to tell apart."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, sequencing the venom gland made it possible to obtain","choices":["a finished drug with no further chemistry required of anyone","a reliable prediction of which channel each peptide will block","a short and manageable list of the most promising candidates","a way to test many peptides at once against a panel of channels","the sequences of peptides too scarce to isolate by hand"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"including peptides far too scarce to isolate by hand"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"with no indication of what any of them binds"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"stays slow and costly even with automated instruments"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 says that reading the gland's messenger RNA yields the sequence of everything the animal is building, including peptides far too scarce to isolate by hand.","distractorNotes":["The same paragraph says the bottleneck moved rather than vanished, and a sequence still has to be made and tested.","Paragraph 14 says the opposite, that thousands of sequences arrive with no indication of what any of them binds.","Sequencing produced thousands of candidates, which paragraph 14 presents as the new problem rather than a solution.","Testing against a panel of ion channels is called slow and costly in paragraph 14, even with automated instruments.",null]},{"id":"rc-p18-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, \"window\" most nearly means","choices":["the span between a dose that helps and a dose that harms","the period during which a drug stays active inside the body","the opening in a channel through which a peptide passes","the brief chance a research team has to publish a finding","the range of patients for whom a treatment has been approved"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"a range between the amount that helps and the amount that harms"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The practical form of that mismatch is the window."}],"why":"Paragraph 7 defines the term as it goes: every drug has a range between the amount that helps and the amount that harms, and venom peptides tend to have narrow ones.","distractorNotes":[null,"A stretch of time is the everyday sense of the word, but paragraph 7 is measuring amounts of drug rather than hours.","Paragraph 5 has peptides lying across a channel's outer face rather than passing through an opening, and paragraph 7 is about dosing.","Nothing in the passage concerns publishing; the paragraph is about the gap between a helpful dose and a harmful one.","Which patients may receive a drug never comes up in paragraph 7, which is about how much of it is safe to give."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 13, \"library\" most nearly means","choices":["a place where research findings are stored and looked up","an orderly record of every venom peptide yet reported","a ready stock of candidate molecules to be drawn on","a set of instructions an organism follows to build a protein","a collection of research tools kept on hand for teaching"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"generating good candidates, is finished, and that what remains is retrieval"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"a library that evolution has already screened"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 takes the word apart as the field uses it: a library implies that good candidates have already been generated and that what remains is retrieval, which is what a waiting stock of compounds would mean.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday sense, and the author's complaint is precisely that venom is not a place where usable answers sit waiting.","An orderly record is the part the author denies, since the same paragraph says there is no index.",null,"The passage never uses the word for genetic instructions; the metaphor concerns a supply of compounds.","Research tools do appear in paragraph 16, but the metaphor in paragraph 13 is about candidate drugs, not teaching."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The account of captopril and tirofiban in paragraph 8 most strongly supports which conclusion?","choices":["Most of the drugs that trace back to venom can be taken by mouth.","A venom molecule can lead to a medicine containing none of the venom.","Small molecules reach targets that venom peptides are unable to reach.","Peptides are on the whole safer for patients than synthetic molecules.","Copying a venom peptide exactly is the surest route to an approved drug."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"Chemists did not develop the peptide"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"built a small molecule that met it the same way and could be swallowed"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"hits the same target with a synthetic molecule of similar shape"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"most of them injected"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the chemists did not develop the pit viper peptide but built a small molecule that met the enzyme the same way, and tirofiban does the same at the platelet target, so the venom supplied the model rather than the product.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 10 says most of the approved drugs are injected.",null,"Tirofiban aims at the same target as the peptide-based drug beside it, so the two are not reaching different places.","Paragraph 7 gives venom peptides narrow windows and paragraph 11 lists the trouble they bring, so the passage does not treat them as the safer option.","Paragraph 8 makes the opposite point, since the chemists set the peptide aside and built something that could be swallowed."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 12 suggests that after a peptide has been closed into a ring to protect it from enzymes, a researcher would most likely have to","choices":["raise the dose to make up for the potency lost in the change","set the molecule aside, since a closed ring cannot bind a channel","repeat the gland sequencing to confirm the change was made","check whether it still tells its target from related proteins","move the drug from injection to some form taken by mouth"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"alters the surface that made the peptide selective"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"the rebuilt molecule often has to prove itself again"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says each such change alters the surface that made the peptide selective, so the rebuilt molecule has to prove itself again, and paragraph 4 explains that this surface is what does the discriminating.","distractorNotes":["Lost potency is not what paragraph 12 warns about; the risk it names is lost selectivity.","That goes well past the passage, which presents ring closure as a tool that works and carries a cost worth checking.","Sequencing in paragraph 14 is a way of learning what an animal makes, not a way of confirming a chemical modification.",null,"Nothing in paragraph 12 promises that these changes let a peptide survive the gut, and paragraph 11 names more than one obstacle there."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Venom research has absorbed decades of effort and should now give way to other approaches.","The chief obstacle to venom-derived medicine has always been the difficulty of collecting enough raw venom to work with.","Cone snails and spiders offer the most promising starting points for the drugs of the future.","Venom peptides matter chiefly because they showed that ion channels come in many related forms.","Venom offers unusually selective molecules, yet the gap between potency and medicine keeps the drug list short."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"The premise is sound."},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"The output is thin."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"Binding tightly to a human channel is a laboratory result, not a therapy"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"The gap between a peptide that exists and one that can be given to a patient"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"The bottleneck moved rather than vanished."}],"why":"The passage moves from why venom peptides are so selective, through why potency alone does not make a drug, to the short list of approvals and the reasons behind it, which is what this choice says.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 16 refuses that dismissal and credits venom with real drugs and a larger number of research tools.","Paragraph 14 says supply was the old limit and that gland sequencing removed it, so the current bottleneck lies elsewhere.","Cone snails and spiders appear as examples, and the passage never ranks sources by how promising they are.","Paragraph 16 gives research tools a supporting role, and the point there is that a peptide proves what a subtype does.",null]},{"id":"rc-p18-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author's main purpose in describing ziconotide at length is to","choices":["show a peptide that works as intended and is still barely usable","argue that opioids ought to be replaced with peptides wherever that is possible","demonstrate that cone snail venom is the most potent venom yet measured","illustrate how quickly gland sequencing can turn a peptide into a medicine","explain why disulfide bonds leave a peptide too rigid to act on a channel"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"it eases pain when opioids have failed"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"It is also close to unusable."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"Captopril is the template, not ziconotide."}],"why":"Paragraph 9 grants that the drug eases pain when opioids have failed, then lists everything that makes it close to unusable, which makes it the clearest case of the gap the passage is built around.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 9 mentions opioids only to say who the drug is for, and it makes no recommendation about replacing them.","The passage never ranks venoms by strength, and potency is not why this drug is featured.","Gland sequencing belongs to paragraph 14, and ziconotide is presented as a problem case rather than a quick success.","Paragraph 3 presents disulfide bonds as what makes a peptide durable, and ziconotide, with three of them, is approved and eases pain."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A history of one drug, followed by predictions about several others to come","A popular claim, the biology behind it, the obstacles and the record, a revised verdict","A list of unsolved problems, each paired with a solution already in use today","A comparison of two competing schools of thought, with the evidence for each weighed in turn","A summary of laboratory methods, followed by instructions for improving on them"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":1,"quote":"Every few years a headline announces"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"That rigid core explains the selectivity"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"where nearly every venom program has died"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"the record of approved drugs is short but real"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"The fair summary is neither the press release nor the dismissal."}],"why":"Paragraph 1 opens with the familiar claim, paragraphs 2 through 5 explain why the peptides are so selective, the middle paragraphs turn to what stands in the way and what has actually been approved, and paragraph 16 delivers a reworked judgment.","distractorNotes":["Captopril and ziconotide are examples along the way, and the passage makes no predictions about drugs to come.",null,"Paragraph 12 does pair tools with problems, but that is one paragraph, and most of what the passage raises goes unsolved.","No two camps are set against each other; the author is arguing with one widely repeated framing.","Methods appear only in paragraph 14, and the passage never tells researchers how to do their work."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the claim that evolution has already screened venom for useful drugs is best described as","choices":["openly contemptuous of the researchers who reach for the phrase","untroubled, since the phrase captures the situation well enough","bluntly skeptical of a phrase claiming more than it earns","puzzled by a comparison whose meaning is difficult to make out","enthusiastic about the promise the phrase is meant to convey"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"a metaphor doing a great deal of unearned work"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"the framing that keeps the field oversold"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"The premise is sound."}],"why":"Paragraph 13 calls the metaphor a great deal of unearned work and says counting sequences is not counting leads, which is criticism of the phrase rather than of the science or the people using it.","distractorNotes":["The criticism falls on the framing, not the people, and paragraph 16 credits the field with real results.","Paragraph 13 is built entirely on the objection that the phrase is misleading.",null,"The author states plainly what the phrase implies and why that implication is wrong, so nothing here is unclear.","Paragraph 1 reports that enthusiasm as something other people express, and paragraph 13 pushes back against it."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In the final paragraph, the author's assessment of what venom has given medicine is","choices":["confident that a major new drug is now close at hand","resigned to the view that nothing further will come of the work","nostalgic for the days of milking animals and running bioassays","defensive about the money and effort the field has already absorbed","measured, crediting real gains and leaving the rest to chemists"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"neither the press release nor the dismissal"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"a small number of genuinely useful drugs"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"which chemists can then pursue with molecules that behave better in a body"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"the rest of the work belongs to people"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 sets aside both the press release and the dismissal, credits venom with useful drugs and research tools, and ends by saying the remaining work belongs to people.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph makes no prediction of a breakthrough and explicitly sets the press release aside.","The same sentence sets the dismissal aside, and the paragraph goes on to list genuine contributions.","Paragraph 14 treats milking animals and separating venom by hand as a limit that was overcome, with no fondness attached.","The passage mentions that testing is slow and costly, but the final paragraph never defends what the field has spent; it weighs results.",null]},{"id":"rc-p18-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage names all of the following as obstacles to using a venom peptide as a medicine EXCEPT","choices":["destruction by acid and enzymes along the digestive tract","rapid removal from the blood by the filter in the kidney","the barrier that surrounds the brain and the spinal cord","the difficulty of working out a peptide's sequence","recognition of the peptide by the patient's immune system"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"meets stomach acid and then a wall of digestive enzymes","choice":0},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"slip through the filter in the kidney and are gone from the blood in minutes","choice":1},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"sit behind a barrier peptides do not cross","choice":2},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"recognized by the immune system","choice":4},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"yields the sequence of everything the animal is building","choice":3},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"Thousands of sequences now arrive","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 11 lists digestion, kidney clearance, the barrier around the nervous system, and immune recognition, while paragraph 14 says sequence is now the easy part, since reading the gland's messenger RNA supplies it.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 11 opens with stomach acid and a wall of digestive enzymes.","Paragraph 11 says small peptides slip through the kidney's filter and are gone from the blood in minutes.","Paragraph 11 says targets in the brain or spinal cord sit behind a barrier peptides do not cross.",null,"Paragraph 11 warns that a foreign sequence may be recognized by the immune system."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for the year a venom-derived treatment for blood sugar was approved should turn to","choices":["paragraph 2","paragraph 5","paragraph 8","paragraph 11","paragraph 14"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"Exenatide, approved in 2005"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"it lowers blood sugar"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 is the roll call of approved drugs with their years, and it places the peptide from Gila monster saliva, which lowers blood sugar, at 2005.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 describes what a venom contains, with cone snails as its example.","Paragraph 5 is about sodium channel subtypes and why they are hard to tell apart.",null,"Paragraph 11 lists the delivery problems peptides face and names no drug at all.","Paragraph 14 covers how venom peptides are found, from milking animals to sequencing the gland."]}]},"rc-p19":{"id":"rc-p19","title":"Why Anaesthesia Still Lacks an Explanation","topic":"the unsettled mechanism of general anaesthesia","field":"neuroscience","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1365,"paragraphs":[["Every year tens of millions of people receive a drug, lose consciousness within seconds, lie still while their bodies are opened, and wake with nothing to report.","The procedure is also remarkably safe, with deaths attributable to the anaesthetic counted in cases per hundreds of thousands.","What is strange about this success is that no one can say, as a pharmacologist can say how a blood pressure drug works, what general anaesthesia is at the level of molecules."],["At the turn of the twentieth century, Hans Horst Meyer and Charles Ernest Overton separately noticed that if one lines up the available anaesthetic agents and measures how readily each dissolves in olive oil, that ranking predicts almost exactly how little of each is needed to put an animal under.","The relationship held across more than four orders of magnitude of potency.","A gas that barely dissolves in oil, such as nitrous oxide, needs enormous concentrations; one that dissolves greedily works at a whisper."],["The inference seemed forced: if oil solubility predicts potency, the drug must work somewhere oily, and the oiliest structure in a neuron is its lipid membrane.","The idea hardened into a unified account: anaesthetics dissolve into the bilayer, disorder it, and the ion channels there stop working.","One observation appeared to seal the case.","Animals anaesthetized and held under helium at roughly a hundred atmospheres begin to move again, as though the pressure were squeezing the membrane back to its original volume.","One site, one mechanism, no receptors required."],["The account began to fail on its own terms.","Warming a membrane by a single degree disorders the bilayer more than a clinical dose of anaesthetic does, yet a mildly feverish patient does not fall asleep.","Isoflurane exists as two mirror image forms, identical in every property a bath of oil could distinguish.","In animals the two differ in potency, one being roughly twice as effective as the other.","Oil cannot tell left from right.","Something handed, which in a cell means a protein, must be reading the molecule."],["Then came compounds that refused to cooperate: chemists built halogenated molecules whose oil solubility predicted that they ought to be powerful anaesthetics, and several of them are not.","An animal breathing one still withdraws from a surgical stimulus.","These non-immobilizers are not inactive: several impair an animal's memory for what happened while it breathed them.","A compound that erases the record of an event without abolishing the response to it is itself an argument that the old correlation was never a law."],["The gentlest blow was the most damaging.","Firefly luciferase, the enzyme behind a firefly's glow, is a soluble protein with no membrane near it.","Anaesthetics switch it off, and they do so in nearly the order of their clinical potency.","If a lone protein in a test tube reproduces the century old correlation, the correlation never pointed at lipid in particular.","They are small, greasy and weakly sticky, and lodge in any oily pocket, whether in a bilayer or in the folded interior of a protein.","After that, the lipid theory lost its purchase."],["The field turned to proteins and found too many of them.","Propofol, etomidate, the barbiturates and the volatile ethers all enhance the GABA type A receptor, which carries most of the brain's inhibition, so each pulse lasts longer than it should.","Glycine receptors, which do similar work in the cord, are enhanced too.","Ketamine, nitrous oxide and xenon leave GABA largely alone and instead block the NMDA glutamate receptor, cutting excitation rather than boosting inhibition.","Volatile agents also open two pore domain potassium channels, which make a neuron harder to fire, and suppress the pacemaker channels of thalamic cells."],["Set the drugs side by side and the problem is visible.","Xenon is a single atom that forms almost no chemical bonds, and it is a complete anaesthetic.","Nitrous oxide is three atoms in a line.","Propofol is an oily phenol delivered as a white emulsion.","Etomidate is a small ring, alphaxalone a steroid, and the volatile agents short carbon chains hung with fluorine and chlorine.","In any other drug class the list would be absurd, since molecules that act at one receptor usually share a recognizable shape.","These share almost nothing beyond a mild reluctance to stay dissolved in water."],["Depth, the operating room's word, is a metaphor.","Its most precise definition is minimum alveolar concentration, or MAC: the concentration of inhaled agent at which half of patients will not move when the skin is cut.","For isoflurane that is a little over one percent, for sevoflurane about two percent, for desflurane about six, and for xenon roughly two thirds of the breath.","For nitrous oxide it is slightly more than a whole atmosphere, so that gas cannot carry an anaesthetic alone.","MAC is both useful and blunt.","It describes a population rather than the patient on the table, and the only thing it measures is whether a body moves."],["Whether a body moves is decided largely below the neck.","In animal preparations that separated the brain's blood supply from the body's, the brain alone needed far more agent to stop movement, and animals whose cords were cut off from the brain needed about the same as intact ones.","Immobility is mostly a spinal effect.","Unresponsiveness to speech, which a patient would call being under, depends on loops between thalamus and cortex and appears at perhaps a third to a half of MAC.","Amnesia appears earlier still, at something like a quarter of MAC, and leans on the hippocampus and the amygdala.","Three effects, three doses, three places."],["General anaesthesia, then, is not one state but a bundle of them, assembled from several drugs: one for unconsciousness, an opioid for the reflex responses to injury, and often a muscle relaxant for stillness.","The relaxant is the awkward member: it paralyzes the patient and so removes the very sign, movement, on which the standard measure of depth was built."],["This is why monitoring depth is harder than it looks.","No instrument reads experience.","The available monitors compress the electroencephalogram from a few forehead electrodes through an algorithm fitted to particular drugs and return one number from zero to one hundred.","Those numbers behave well for propofol and the ethers and badly for the rest.","Ketamine, nitrous oxide and xenon can drive the index toward apparent wakefulness in a patient who is entirely unresponsive."],["Before the relaxant is given, a cuff on the upper arm keeps it from one hand, so the anaesthetist can ask the patient to squeeze.","A minority of patients squeeze on command during surgery, and almost none of them remember anything afterward.","Whether such a patient is conscious in any sense that ought to trouble us is not something a monitor can settle, and honest disagreement about it runs through the literature.","Reports of awareness with recall run about one in twenty thousand when patients must volunteer the experience, and twenty or thirty times higher when every patient is asked directly.","The distance between those two figures is itself a finding."],["Why has a mechanism been so slow to arrive?","Partly because the targets are redundant.","Delete one in a mouse and the anaesthetic usually still works, shifted a little along the dose axis, as expected of a drug that binds many things weakly.","There is a celebrated exception.","Etomidate acts at a pocket in one GABA type A subunit, and changing a single amino acid there leaves mice the drug can no longer keep still.","That is as clean a result as the field has produced, and it holds for one drug, not for the state."],["None of this unsettles the practice, and it should not.","Anaesthesia is a recipe refined over a century and a half by watching patients rather than deducing from theory, and the refinement has been extraordinarily successful.","The incompleteness is a research program rather than a warning.","The situation does have a particular flavor.","The field has learned to produce on demand, by the millions, a condition it cannot yet define, and the most economical description of what these drugs do is still a list of what a patient cannot do."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p19-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, what did Meyer and Overton find when they compared the anaesthetic agents available in their day?","choices":["Agents that dissolved most readily in water were the ones that could be given to an animal in the smallest doses","How readily an agent dissolved in olive oil predicted how little of it was needed to anaesthetize an animal","Heavier molecules were consistently more potent than lighter ones","Every agent worked by binding the same receptor in the nerve membrane","The agents varied so widely that no single ordering of them could be found"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"how readily each dissolves in olive oil, that ranking predicts almost exactly how little"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 states it directly: ranking the agents by how readily each dissolves in olive oil predicts almost exactly how little of each is needed. The measurement was solubility in oil, and the finding was a prediction of potency.","distractorNotes":["The passage names olive oil, not water, and it later calls these molecules reluctant to stay dissolved in water.",null,"Molecular weight never enters the comparison; the only property measured was solubility.","The lipid account that grew out of the finding was attractive precisely because it needed no receptor at all.","The opposite is stated. The ranking held across more than four orders of magnitude of potency."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage reports that anaesthetized animals held under helium at roughly a hundred atmospheres","choices":["stopped breathing and had to be supported by a ventilator","needed a far larger dose of agent for the rest of the experiment","showed no measurable change of any kind","began to move again, which was read as pressure restoring the membrane's volume","lost the response to a surgical stimulus that they had still shown a moment earlier"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"begin to move again, as though the pressure were squeezing the membrane back"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 gives this as the observation that seemed to seal the lipid case: under high pressure the animals begin to move again, as though the membrane were being squeezed back toward its original volume.","distractorNotes":["Breathing is not what the passage describes. The reported change was the return of movement.","No later dosing is mentioned. The observation was about what the pressure did at the time.","The whole force of the result was that something did change, which is why it looked like proof.",null,"This runs the effect backwards. Pressure brought movement back rather than taking a response away."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 9, which agent has a minimum alveolar concentration greater than a whole atmosphere?","choices":["isoflurane","sevoflurane","desflurane","the noble gas xenon","nitrous oxide"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"For nitrous oxide it is slightly more than a whole atmosphere"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"for xenon roughly two thirds of the breath"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the MAC of nitrous oxide is slightly more than a whole atmosphere, which is the reason that gas cannot carry an anaesthetic by itself.","distractorNotes":["Isoflurane is a little over one percent, the smallest figure in the list.","Sevoflurane is about two percent, far below a full atmosphere.","Desflurane is about six percent, still a small fraction of the breath.","Xenon is roughly two thirds of the breath, high but under one atmosphere.",null]},{"id":"rc-p19-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage groups ketamine, nitrous oxide and xenon together because these three","choices":["block a glutamate receptor instead of strengthening inhibition","make each pulse of the brain's inhibition last longer than it should","act on the glycine receptors of the spinal cord","open potassium channels that make a neuron harder to fire","are the only agents that can be used without a muscle relaxant"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"instead block the NMDA glutamate receptor, cutting excitation rather than boosting inhibition"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 sets these three apart from the rest: they leave GABA largely alone and block the NMDA glutamate receptor, cutting excitation rather than boosting inhibition.","distractorNotes":[null,"That is what the GABA type A group does, and the passage contrasts these three against that group.","Glycine receptors appear in the same paragraph, but they are enhanced by the other group of drugs.","Two pore domain potassium channels are attributed to the volatile agents, not to these three.","The passage never connects any drug to doing without a relaxant."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, unresponsiveness to speech appears at roughly what fraction of MAC?","choices":["about a quarter","about one tenth","somewhere between a third and a half","the full value of MAC and nothing less","roughly twice the value of MAC"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"appears at perhaps a third to a half of MAC"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"Amnesia appears earlier still, at something like a quarter of MAC"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 places the three effects on a dose ladder. Unresponsiveness to speech appears at perhaps a third to a half of MAC, above amnesia and below immobility.","distractorNotes":["A quarter of MAC is where amnesia appears, one rung lower on the same ladder.","No figure that low is given for any of the three effects.",null,"Full MAC is the immobility figure, the concentration at which half of patients do not move.","Nothing in the passage puts any of the three effects above MAC."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, the statement that the lipid theory lost its purchase most nearly means that it lost its","choices":["commercial backing from the companies that made the drugs","physical grip on the membranes it described","place in the historical record of the field","power to explain what the evidence showed","supporters among the chemists who first proposed it"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"the correlation never pointed at lipid in particular"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"the lipid theory lost its purchase"}],"why":"The sentence closes a paragraph about what the old correlation could and could not account for. Purchase here is the hold an idea has on the evidence, so losing it means losing explanatory power.","distractorNotes":["Purchase can mean something bought, but no money appears anywhere in this paragraph.","A theory is an idea and not an object, and what slipped was its hold on the evidence.","Paragraph 6 is about whether the theory still fit the evidence, not whether it was remembered; the history of the idea is not at issue.",null,"Who supported the theory is never the subject here. What it could account for is."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 9, \"blunt\" most nearly means","choices":["worn down by repeated use","incapable of fine distinctions","plainly and even harshly worded","slow to take effect in the body","not sharp enough to cut tissue"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"It describes a population rather than the patient on the table"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"the only thing it measures is whether a body moves"}],"why":"The next sentence in paragraph 9 explains the word: MAC describes a population rather than the patient on the table, and it measures only whether a body moves. That is a measure too coarse to separate cases.","distractorNotes":["Nothing suggests the measure has eroded over time. It is coarse from the start.",null,"That is the everyday sense of blunt applied to speech, and here the word is judging a measurement.","Speed of onset is not what the sentence weighs. It weighs how much MAC can tell you.","The literal edge of a blade has no place here, since the word describes a criterion."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The firefly luciferase result best supports which conclusion about the old solubility correlation?","choices":["It rested on measurements that later turned out to be mistaken","It applies only to agents that are gases at room temperature","It shows that enzymes rather than ion channels are the real targets","It holds for proteins in a test tube but breaks down in the tissue of a living animal","It fits protein binding just as well, so it cannot single out lipid"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"the correlation never pointed at lipid in particular"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"whether in a bilayer or in the folded interior of a protein"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 draws exactly this lesson. A protein with no membrane near it reproduces the correlation, so the correlation was never evidence for lipid in particular, only for these molecules lodging in any oily pocket.","distractorNotes":["The passage never doubts the measurements. It doubts what they were pointing at.","Nothing restricts the correlation to gases, and propofol is described as an oily liquid.","Luciferase is used as a demonstration, not offered as a target in the brain.","The passage does not set the test tube against the animal here. It uses the test tube to reread the correlation.",null]},{"id":"rc-p19-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 11 suggests that a paralyzed patient creates a particular difficulty because","choices":["the sign that the standard measure of depth depends on can no longer be seen","muscle relaxants lower the concentration of agent that a patient turns out to need","relaxants are known to erase the memory of the procedure","the opioid in the mixture becomes unnecessary once movement has stopped","paralysis makes the electroencephalogram impossible to record"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"removes the very sign, movement, on which the standard measure of depth was built"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 links two facts: MAC is built on movement, and the relaxant removes movement. With the sign gone, the standard measure has nothing left to read.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage says nothing about relaxants changing how much agent is required.","Amnesia is credited to the anaesthetic at low doses, not to the relaxant.","Each drug in the bundle keeps its own job, and the opioid's job is the reflex response to injury.","The monitors described next read the brain's electrical signal, which paralysis does not silence."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["The lipid membrane theory has been replaced by a settled account built on one protein target","Anaesthesia carries hidden dangers that patients are never told about, and the profession has been slow to admit it","Anaesthesia works safely at scale yet is not fully understood, with many targets and effects that come apart","The main obstacle to understanding anaesthesia is that operating room monitors are not accurate enough","Because different drugs act at different sites, anaesthetists ought to give only one agent at a time"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"what general anaesthesia is at the level of molecules"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The field turned to proteins and found too many of them."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"Three effects, three doses, three places."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":5,"quote":"a condition it cannot yet define"}],"why":"Every section of the passage feeds this claim: the safety record in paragraph 1, the collapse of one theory, the crowd of protein targets, and the separation of immobility, unconsciousness and amnesia, closing with a procedure that works without being defined.","distractorNotes":["Half right. The lipid theory did fall, but the passage insists that no single settled target replaced it.","The passage says the opposite about safety and treats the incompleteness as ordinary rather than concealed.",null,"Monitoring is one section of the argument rather than the argument. The trouble starts with the drugs.","The passage treats the multi drug bundle as normal practice and never recommends dropping it."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author lists xenon, nitrous oxide, propofol, etomidate, alphaxalone and the volatile agents in paragraph 8 mainly to","choices":["rank the agents by how safe each one is in practice","show that molecules with nothing structurally in common produce the same state","explain why the volatile agents became the most widely used members of the group","argue that only one drug on the list is a true anaesthetic","demonstrate that every agent named acts at the GABA type A receptor"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"molecules that act at one receptor usually share a recognizable shape"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":7,"quote":"These share almost nothing beyond a mild reluctance to stay dissolved in water."}],"why":"Paragraph 8 ends by noting that in any other drug class such a list would be absurd, because molecules acting at one receptor usually share a shape. The list exists to make the chemical variety impossible to miss.","distractorNotes":["Safety appears in the first paragraph, and no ranking of these drugs is given anywhere.",null,"Popularity is never the subject. The paragraph is about chemical variety.","Xenon is called a complete anaesthetic in this very paragraph, so no member is being disqualified.","Paragraph 7 sorts the drugs by target and places ketamine, nitrous oxide and xenon somewhere else."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A puzzle is posed, one explanation rises and collapses, a messier replacement is laid out, and the consequences are weighed","Two rival drugs are compared point by point and one is judged the better choice","A single patient's case is described and then generalized to the whole of surgery","A chronological list of discoveries is given with no argument running through it","A problem in the operating room is described in detail and then a particular piece of monitoring equipment is recommended to solve it"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"What is strange about this success"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"One site, one mechanism, no receptors required."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"The account began to fail on its own terms."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The field turned to proteins and found too many of them."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"not one state but a bundle of them"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 poses the puzzle, paragraphs 2 and 3 build the lipid account, paragraphs 4 through 6 dismantle it, paragraphs 7 through 13 lay out the crowded replacement, and paragraphs 14 and 15 weigh what the gap means.","distractorNotes":[null,"No two drugs are set against each other as choices. The drugs are grouped by target and by chemistry.","There is no case history anywhere in the passage.","The dates do run forward, but each step is used as evidence in an argument about mechanism.","The monitors are described and criticized, and no particular device is endorsed."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q13","type":"tone","stem":"In the final paragraph, the author's attitude toward the gap between practice and explanation is best described as","choices":["alarmed at what the gap means for patients","dismissive of the research that seeks to close it","indifferent to whether patients are safe","untroubled by it and curious about what remains","triumphant about how much has been settled"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"None of this unsettles the practice, and it should not."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"The incompleteness is a research program rather than a warning."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"The situation does have a particular flavor."}],"why":"Paragraph 15 opens by saying none of this unsettles the practice and calls the incompleteness a research program rather than a warning, while still pointing out that the field cannot yet define what it produces.","distractorNotes":["Alarm is ruled out by the first line of the paragraph, which says the practice is not unsettled.","Calling the gap a research program endorses the work rather than waving it off.","The success of the practice is the first thing the paragraph credits, so indifference does not fit.",null,"The paragraph ends on what the field still cannot define, which is not a victory lap."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's handling of the two awareness figures in paragraph 13 is best described as","choices":["confident that the lower figure is the accurate one","convinced that the higher figure proves patients are routinely awake and aware during surgery","impatient with colleagues who keep raising the question","certain that the difference is a simple error of record keeping","deliberate about leaving the disagreement open and treating the gap as informative"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"honest disagreement about it runs through the literature"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"The distance between those two figures is itself a finding."}],"why":"Paragraph 13 gives both numbers, says honest disagreement about the question runs through the literature, and then calls the distance between the two figures a finding in its own right.","distractorNotes":["Both numbers are given without either being endorsed, and the disagreement is called honest.","Almost none of the patients who respond remember anything, and the author declines to say what that amounts to.","No irritation toward anyone appears here. The open question is presented as worth having.","The gap is called a finding, which makes it informative rather than a mistake in the records.",null]},{"id":"rc-p19-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage presents all of the following as trouble for the lipid membrane theory EXCEPT","choices":["a single degree of warming disorders a bilayer more than a clinical dose does","two mirror image forms of one agent differ in potency","pressure restores movement in an animal that has been anaesthetized","a soluble enzyme with no membrane is switched off in the order of clinical potency","some highly oil soluble compounds fail to keep an animal from moving"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"Warming a membrane by a single degree disorders the bilayer more than a clinical dose","choice":0},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"the two differ in potency, one being roughly twice as effective","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"they do so in nearly the order of their clinical potency","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"ought to be powerful anaesthetics, and several of them are not","choice":4},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"One observation appeared to seal the case.","choice":2}],"why":"Pressure reversal is the one item on this list that the passage offers as support for the lipid theory, in paragraph 3, not as evidence against it. The other four all appear in paragraphs 4 through 6 as failures.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 gives this as the first failure of the theory, since a mild fever would otherwise put a patient to sleep.","Paragraph 4 gives this as a failure of the theory, since oil cannot tell one mirror form from the other.",null,"Paragraph 6 uses luciferase to show that the correlation never pointed at lipid in particular.","Paragraph 5 gives the non-immobilizers, whose oil solubility predicts a potency they do not have."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to find out which part of the nervous system decides whether a surgical patient moves should turn to","choices":["paragraph 3","paragraph 7","paragraph 9","paragraph 10","paragraph 14"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"Whether a body moves is decided largely below the neck."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"Immobility is mostly a spinal effect."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 is where the three effects are assigned to places. It says immobility is mostly a spinal effect and describes the preparations that separated brain from cord.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 covers the lipid theory and the pressure observation.","Paragraph 7 lists molecular targets rather than regions of the nervous system.","Paragraph 9 defines MAC by whether a body moves, but it never says where that decision is made.",null,"Paragraph 14 is about redundant targets and the etomidate exception."]}]}},"order":["rc-o01","rc-o02","rc-o03","rc-o04","rc-o05","rc-o06","rc-o07","rc-o08","rc-o09","rc-o10","rc-o11","rc-o12","rc-o13","rc-o14","rc-o15","rc-o16","rc-o17","rc-o18","rc-o19","rc-o20","rc-o21","rc-o22","rc-o23","rc-o24","rc-o25","rc-o26","rc-p01","rc-p02","rc-p03","rc-p04","rc-p05","rc-p06","rc-p07","rc-p08","rc-p09","rc-p10","rc-p11","rc-p12","rc-p13","rc-p14","rc-p15","rc-p16","rc-p17","rc-p18","rc-p19"],"sections":[{"id":"rc-s1","title":"Altitude, Stripes, Deep-Sea Light","passages":["rc-o01","rc-o06","rc-p03"]},{"id":"rc-s2","title":"Mirror Molecules, Wolves, Prions","passages":["rc-o04","rc-o02","rc-p16"]},{"id":"rc-s3","title":"Phages, Insulin, Nitrogen","passages":["rc-o05","rc-o03","rc-p13"]},{"id":"rc-s4","title":"Ulcers, Echoes, Concrete","passages":["rc-o12","rc-o13","rc-p02"]},{"id":"rc-s5","title":"Superconductors, Blood Groups, Resistance Genes","passages":["rc-o11","rc-o22","rc-p04"]},{"id":"rc-s6","title":"Ozone, Camouflage, Plaque","passages":["rc-o17","rc-o21","rc-p06"]},{"id":"rc-s7","title":"Smallpox, Enzymes, Shells","passages":["rc-o08","rc-o24","rc-p07"]},{"id":"rc-s8","title":"C4 Plants, Fission, Sleep","passages":["rc-o09","rc-o23","rc-p08"]},{"id":"rc-s9","title":"Endosymbiosis, Spontaneous Generation, Batteries","passages":["rc-o14","rc-o20","rc-p09"]},{"id":"rc-s10","title":"Antifreeze Fish, Cholera, Gene Editing","passages":["rc-o16","rc-o10","rc-p11"]},{"id":"rc-s11","title":"Islands, Lead, Pacemakers","passages":["rc-o25","rc-o26","rc-p12"]},{"id":"rc-s12","title":"Telomeres, Ash, Migration","passages":["rc-o07","rc-p05","rc-p14"]},{"id":"rc-s13","title":"Smell, Tardigrades, Ice Cores","passages":["rc-o18","rc-p01","rc-p17"]},{"id":"rc-s14","title":"Bone, Radiocarbon, Venom","passages":["rc-o19","rc-p15","rc-p18"]},{"id":"rc-s15","title":"Photo 51, Mycorrhizae, Anesthesia","passages":["rc-o15","rc-p10","rc-p19"]}]};
